window.ANAT_IDX=window.ANAT_IDX||{};window.ANAT_IDX["full"]=[{"id":"t-I-1","p":"I","n":1,"t":"General features and classification of bones. Types of ossification.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer A bone is a composite organ built of bone tissue plus cartilage, marrow, vessels, nerves and periosteum. Bones are classified by shape (long, short, flat, irregular, sesamoid, pneumatic), by structure (compact and spongy) and by development (membranous, cartilaginous, mixed). Bone tissue forms by two mechanisms — intramembranous (desmal) ossification directly from mesenchyme and endochondral (chondral) ossification on a hyaline cartilage model; both add bone by apposition, and endochondral ossification additionally grows by interstitial proliferation at the epiphyseal plate. 1. Bone as a tissue and as an organ; functions Bone ( os , ossis ) is both a tissue and an organ. As a tissue it is a specialised connective tissue whose extracellular matrix is mineralised — type I collagen reinforced with calcium hydroxyapatite. As an organ it consists of bone tissue together with cartilage, fibrous tissue, bone marrow, blood and lymphatic vessels and nerves; it is covered by periosteum ( periosteum ) everywhere except the articular surfaces and the sites of tendon insertion, and is lined internally by endosteum ( endosteum ). Mechanical — support of the body, protection of viscera (skull, thorax, pelvis), and levers on which muscles act. Metabolic — the body's calcium and phosphate reservoir; about 99 % of body calcium is stored in bone as hydroxyapatite and is exchanged continuously with the plasma. Haemopoietic — red marrow of spongy bone produces red cells, white cells and platelets; yellow marrow is a fat depot. Other — conduction of sound (ossicles), and shaping of the body contour. Bones are vascular and innervated . A nutrient artery , usually one per bone, enters through the nutrient foramen and supplies the marrow, the spongy bone and the inner part of the cortex; periosteal vessels supply the outer cortex and are essential for appositional growth and for fracture callus. Nerves follow the vessels: most intramedullary fibres are vasomotor, whereas the periosteum is richly supplied with sensory fibres and is the source of the deep pain of a fracture. A bone stripped of its periosteum does not survive. 2. Classification by shape Morphological classification of bones (Terminologia Anatomica) Type Latin Morphology and mode of ossification Examples Long (tubular) os longum Diaphysis with a medullary cavity and two expanded epiphyses; length far exceeds width. Endochondral. Humerus, radius, ulna, femur, tibia, fibula, metacarpals, metatarsals, phalanges Short os breve Roughly cuboidal; a thin compact shell around spongy bone. Endochondral. Carpal bones, tarsal bones Flat os planum Two compact plates (tabulae) enclosing spongy bone (diploë). Intramembranous or endochondral. Parietal, frontal, occipital squama, scapula, sternum, ribs, ilium Irregular os irregulare Complex, non-geometric shape; mixed ossification. Vertebrae, sacrum, sphenoid, temporal, maxilla, hip bone Sesamoid os sesamoideum Develops inside a tendon; improves the moment arm and protects the tendon. Ossifies late. Patella (the largest), pisiform, sesamoids of the thumb and big toe Pneumatic os pneumaticum Contains an air-filled, mucosa-lined cavity continuous with the nasal cavity (in the temporal bone, with the middle ear). Maxilla, frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, mastoid part of temporal Accessory bones ( ossa accessoria ) are supernumerary bones present as normal variants — for example the os trigonum of the talus and an accessory navicular bone — and must not be mistaken for fractures. 3. Gross structure of a long bone and surface markings Part Description and functional note Diaphysis (corpus) Tubular shaft with a thick compact cortex and a medullary cavity ( cavitas medullaris ) containing yellow marrow in the adult; primary ossification centre. Epiphysis Expanded end; thin cortex over spongy bone filled with red marrow; the articular surface is covered by hyaline cartilage and has no periosteum. Secondary ossification centre. Metaphysis Flared junction of diaphysis and epiphysis; contains the epiphyseal plate in the growing bone and is a site of haematogenous osteomyelitis in children. Epiphyseal plate (lamina epiphysialis) Hyaline cartilage disc responsible for growth in length; after closure it persists as the epiphyseal line. Apophysis Bony projection with its own ossification centre, but not contributing to the articular surface or to growth in length (tubercles, trochanters). Compact bone ( substantia compacta ) forms the cortex; its structural unit is the osteon (Haversian system) — concentric lamellae around a central Haversian canal that carries a neurovascular bundle. Osteons are joined by oblique Volkmann's canals and bounded by cement lines. Spongy bone ( substantia spongiosa ) is a three-dimensional lattice of trabeculae whose orientation follows the trajectories of load, so that the same bone mass resists force with the minimum of material. Surface markings are either elevations ( eminentiae ) — tuberosity, tubercle, tuber, line ( linea ), crest ( crista ), trochlea, head, capitulum, process — or depressions ( impressiones ) — sulcus, fossa, foramen, hiatus, incisure. They mark tendon and ligament attachments, joint surfaces and passages for vessels and nerves. 4. Woven and lamellar bone Primary (woven, immature) bone is the first bone to form, in the embryo, in the fracture callus and in tumours: it is more cellular, less calcified and its collagen bundles are randomly arranged. Secondary (lamellar, mature) bone has precisely ordered lamellae and is stronger. Except in the alveoli of the teeth and at tendon insertions, primary bone is always replaced by lamellar bone. 5. Intramembranous (desmal) ossification Mesenchyme condenses in a highly vascular membrane; mesenchymal cells express the osteogenic transcription factors Runx2 and osterix and differentiate into osteoblasts . Osteoblasts secrete osteoid , which calcifies; spicules of primary bone form trabeculae — this is the primary ossification centre. Trabeculae thicken and fuse; some osteoblasts are trapped in lacunae and become osteocytes , connected through canaliculi. The surfaces consolidate into compact plates (tabulae) on the outside and inside, with diploë between them. Remaining mesenchyme becomes periosteum and endosteum. Sites: calvarial vault and facial skeleton, most of the mandible and clavicle. The same mechanism is responsible for appositional growth — increase in width, with periosteal apposition and simultaneous endosteal resorption — of every bone throughout life. 6. Endochondral (chondral) ossification A miniature hyaline cartilage model forms from mesenchyme; its perichondrium becomes vascularised and turns into periosteum. Osteoblasts lay down a subperiosteal bone collar (intramembranous formation) around the middle of the model; the collar cuts off diffusion, so the central chondrocytes hypertrophy, die and the matrix calcifies. Osteoclasts perforate the collar; an osteogenic bud of vessels and osteoprogenitor cells invades the calcified cartilage, and bone is deposited on the calcified cartilage septa — the primary ossification centre in the diaphysis. Osteoclasts enlarge the cavity while the collar grows towards the ends; the diaphysis is replaced by bone. Secondary ossification centres appear in the epiphyses after birth, without a bone collar; the epiphyseal plate persists between the two centres and is the engine of growth in length. At the end of growth the plate stops proliferating and is replaced by bone — epiphyseal closure . Five zones are described in the plate, from the epiphyseal to the diaphyseal side: reserve (resting) cartilage → proliferation (columns of dividing chondrocytes) → maturation and hypertrophy → calcification (chondrocytes die by apoptosis) → ossification (osteoblasts deposit bone on the calcified septa). Intramembranous versus endochondral ossification Feature Intramembranous Endochondral Precursor Mesenchyme (fibrous membrane) Hyaline cartilage model First tissue formed Woven bone directly Woven bone on calcified cartilage Growth mode Appositional only Appositional + interstitial (epiphyseal plate) Typical sites Calvaria, facial skeleton, clavicle, mandible; periosteal growth of every bone Limb bones, vertebrae, ribs, sternum, skull base Failure Craniosynostosis, cleidocranial dysplasia Rickets, achondroplasia, growth-plate injury Must-memorise Six shape classes: long, short, flat, irregular, sesamoid, pneumatic — plus accessory bones as variants. Only two ossification mechanisms: desmal from mesenchyme, chondral on a cartilage model; appositional growth everywhere, interstitial growth only in the epiphyseal plate. Epiphyseal plate has five zones; the plate is the site of growth in length and of growth-plate fractures. Periosteum = fibrous outer layer + cellular inner (cambium) layer; it provides appositional growth, callus and cortical blood supply, and it is very sensitive. Primary (woven) bone is replaced everywhere except at tendon insertions and tooth alveoli. Clinical / radiological correlation Fracture healing: haematoma → granulation tissue → soft (fibrocartilaginous) callus → hard callus of woven bone (intramembranous and endochondral) → remodelling into lamellar bone along lines of stress. Rickets / osteomalacia: defective mineralisation with a widened, irregular epiphyseal plate and bowing of weight-bearing bones; in adults the same defect is osteomalacia. Achondroplasia: impaired endochondral ossification with short limbs but normally developed membranous bones. Scurvy: vitamin C deficiency blocks collagen synthesis, so bone matrix and callus cannot be formed properly. Growth-plate injury: Salter–Harris types I–V; compression of the plate may cause asymmetrical growth and deformity. Osteoporosis / avascular necrosis: loss of trabecular bone predisposes to insufficiency fractures; regions with a retrograde intra-osseous supply (femoral head, scaphoid, talus) are prone to avascular necrosis after fracture. Radiology: on plain films the cortex appears dense, spongy bone trabecular, and articular cartilage radiolucent — hence the apparent joint space. Likely follow-up questions What is the difference between an epiphysis, a metaphysis and an apophysis? — The apophysis contributes neither to the articular surface nor to growth in length. Why does a fractured bone unite but a destroyed articular surface heal poorly? — Periosteum and endosteum provide osteoprogenitor cells; articular cartilage is avascular and has no perichondrium. How does a bone grow in width while its marrow cavity enlarges? — Periosteal apposition plus endosteal resorption, i.e. continuous remodelling. Which bones ossify intramembranously? — Calvarial vault, facial skeleton, most of the mandible and clavicle."},{"id":"t-I-2","p":"I","n":2,"t":"Connections between bones. Classification and general description of joints.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer A joint ( articulatio ) is any connection between skeletal elements. There are two great classes: solid (continuous) joints , in which the surfaces are united by connective tissue or cartilage with no cavity, and synovial (cavitated) joints , in which the surfaces are covered by hyaline cartilage and separated by a fluid-filled cavity. Solid joints are fibrous (suture, syndesmosis, gomphosis) or cartilaginous (synchondrosis, symphysis), and may ossify into a synostosis. Synovial joints consist of articular cartilage, capsule, cavity with synovial fluid, ligaments and accessory structures, and are classified by the shape of their surfaces and by the number of axes of movement. 1. Terminology and functional classification The study of joints is arthrology or syndesmology . A joint is the site where two or more skeletal elements meet. Classically the criterion is continuity: in solid joints ( juncturae fibrosae et cartilagineae ) the adjacent surfaces are linked by continuous tissue, whereas in synovial joints they are separated by a narrow cavity filled with synovial fluid. Functionally, joints are graded by the amount of movement they allow: synarthrosis — practically immobile; amphiarthrosis — slight movement only; diarthrosis — free movement. The two classifications are matched in the table below. Some plane synovial joints behave functionally as amphiarthroses because their range is small. Structural class Subtype Functional equivalent Fibrous Suture Synarthrosis Syndesmosis Synarthrosis (little movement) Gomphosis Synarthrosis Cartilaginous Synchondrosis Synarthrosis Symphysis Amphiarthrosis Bony Synostosis No movement Synovial All shapes Diarthrosis 2. Solid joints Type Uniting tissue Description Examples Suture (sutura) Thin layer of dense fibrous tissue — the sutural ligament Occurs only in the skull; the edges interlock and allow growth of the vault in childhood. Sagittal, coronal, lambdoid sutures Syndesmosis Ligament or interosseous membrane Two bones linked by a ligamentous band; slight movement. Interosseous membrane of the forearm and leg, ligamentum flavum, inferior tibiofibular syndesmosis Gomphosis Periodontal ligament Dentoalveolar syndesmosis: the tooth root is fixed in its socket by short collagen fibres. All teeth Synchondrosis Hyaline cartilage A cartilage plate between two ossification centres; allows growth and then ossifies. Epiphyseal plate of a growing long bone, sternocostal joint Symphysis Fibrocartilage Two separate bones joined by a fibrocartilaginous disc, usually in the midline; slight movement. Pubic symphysis, intervertebral discs Synostosis Bone The final stage of a solid joint after ossification; no movement. Fused sutures of the adult skull, sacral segments 3. General structure of a synovial joint Hyaline articular cartilage covers both surfaces, so the bones never touch directly; it is avascular, aneural and has no perichondrium over the articulating surface, and is nourished by diffusion from synovial fluid and from the subchondral bone. The joint capsule has two layers: an outer fibrous membrane of dense connective tissue, continuous with the periosteum and thickened in places into capsular ligaments, and an inner synovial membrane that attaches at the cartilage–bone junction, closes the cavity and secretes synovial fluid. Synovial fluid is a plasma filtrate rich in hyaluronic acid and containing lubricin ; it lubricates the surfaces, nourishes the cartilage and is cleared by the synovial membrane, which contains macrophage-like type A cells and fluid-secreting type B cells. Component Structure and function Articular surface Hyaline cartilage over the bone end; distributes load and reduces friction. A few joints use fibrocartilage instead — for example the sternoclavicular joint. Articular capsule Membrana fibrosa (mechanical restraint) plus membrana synovialis (secretion and resorption). Articular cavity Narrow space containing a film of synovial fluid; a negative intra-articular pressure helps hold the surfaces together. Ligaments Capsular thickenings or separate extracapsular/intracapsular bands; guide and limit movement and carry the reflex apparatus. Accessory structures Articular disc, meniscus, labrum, fat pads, bursae, synovial sheaths of tendons. 4. Accessory structures An articular disc ( discus articularis ) is a fibrocartilaginous plate that completely divides the cavity — for example in the temporomandibular and sternoclavicular joints. A meniscus ( meniscus articularis ) is an incomplete fibrocartilaginous crescent, as in the knee. Both improve congruence, absorb compression and widen the range of movement. A labrum deepens a shallow socket (glenoid, acetabulum). Fat pads lie between the synovial membrane and the capsule and move in and out as the joint's contour changes. Bursae and synovial tendon sheaths are closed sacs of synovial membrane outside the joint that reduce friction between tendon and bone, tendon and tendon, or skin and bone; some bursae communicate with the cavity through apertures in the fibrous membrane. 5. Classification of synovial joints Shape Axes Movements Example Plane (articulatio plana) Functional amphiarthrosis Sliding or gliding of one flat surface on another. Acromioclavicular, intercarpal joints Hinge (ginglymus) Uniaxial Flexion and extension in one plane. Elbow (humeroulnar), interphalangeal Pivot (trochoidea) Uniaxial Rotation about a longitudinal axis. Atlanto-axial, proximal radioulnar Bicondylar Mainly uniaxial with limited rotation Flexion and extension with a little rotation. Knee Condylar (ellipsoid) Biaxial Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, circumduction. Wrist, metacarpophalangeal Saddle (sellaris) Biaxial The same movements on reciprocally concave–convex surfaces; opposition. Carpometacarpal joint of the thumb Ball and socket (spheroidea) Multiaxial Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, circumduction. Shoulder, hip 6. Blood supply, innervation and clinical anatomy Articular branches arise from the vessels that cross or surround the joint and form a periarticular anastomotic network; the capsule and synovial membrane are richly vascular, whereas articular cartilage is avascular. The nerve supply follows the same rule now called Hilton's law : the nerves that innervate the muscles moving a joint also supply the joint itself and the skin over it, so pain from a joint is referred to the muscles that move it and to the overlying skin. Proprioceptive endings in the capsule and ligaments, together with muscle spindles and tendon organs, protect the joint reflexively. Must-memorise Two structural classes: solid (fibrous, cartilaginous, bony synostosis) and synovial (cavitated). Fibrous: suture, syndesmosis, gomphosis. Cartilaginous: synchondrosis, symphysis. Six components of a synovial joint: articular cartilage, capsule (fibrous + synovial), cavity with synovial fluid, ligaments, articular disc, meniscus. Seven shapes: plane, hinge, pivot, bicondylar, condylar (ellipsoid), saddle, ball and socket. Hilton's law: the nerve of the muscle moving a joint also supplies the joint and the overlying skin. Clinical / radiological correlation Osteoarthritis: loss of articular cartilage with subchondral sclerosis, osteophytes, cysts and narrowing of the radiological joint space. Rheumatoid arthritis: primary destruction of the synovial membrane with pannus formation and secondary erosion of cartilage. Joint effusion: excess synovial fluid distends the capsule; on plain films only the soft-tissue swelling is seen, on MRI the fluid is bright on fluid-sensitive sequences. Arthroscopy: a telescope through a small incision allows direct inspection and treatment, most often of the knee, shoulder, ankle and hip. Dislocation and sprain: loss of congruity of the surfaces, or rupture of ligaments and capsule; ligamentous laxity predisposes to recurrent dislocation. Intra-articular injection of corticosteroid or local anaesthetic is used for both diagnosis and treatment of a painful joint. Likely follow-up questions Why are joint surfaces not in direct contact? — Hyaline articular cartilage is interposed; it distributes load and is nourished by synovial fluid. What is the difference between a disc and a meniscus? — A disc divides the cavity completely; a meniscus is an incomplete crescent. Give an example of a synchondrosis that persists in the adult. — The sternocostal joint of the first rib; elsewhere synchondroses ossify. Which joints contain fibrocartilage instead of hyaline cartilage? — The sternoclavicular joint is one of them."},{"id":"t-I-3","p":"I","n":3,"t":"General features of skeletal muscles and fasciae. Types of muscles. Sensory and motor innervation of muscles.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer Skeletal muscle is the active organ of movement: striated, voluntary, multinucleated fibres arranged in fascicles, invested by three connective tissue sheaths and attached to bone through tendons or aponeuroses. It accounts for about 40 % of body mass, has its own blood and nerve supply and works in lever systems between an origin (fixed point) and an insertion (mobile point). Fasciae are superficial, deep and subserous. Muscles are classified by shape, by number of heads or bellies and by function. Each muscle receives somatic motor fibres (alpha and gamma motoneurons, forming motor units), sensory fibres (muscle spindles, tendon organs) and autonomic vasomotor fibres. 1. The skeletal muscle as an organ; general terminology Skeletal muscle ( musculus ) is an independent anatomical and functional unit: it has its own shape and name, its own innervation, its own vessels and lymphatics, and it is attached to bones by tendons. It accounts for the largest share of body mass (about 40 %) and is a major source of body heat during activity. It determines the external contours of the body. Origin ( origo ) — the more proximal, relatively fixed attachment; insertion ( insertio ) — the more distal attachment that is moved. The terms fixed point and mobile point are preferred, because in some movements they reverse (e.g. climbing). Venter (belly) — the fleshy, contractile part; tendon ( tendo ) — dense regular connective tissue continuing the sheaths to the bone; aponeurosis — a flat, broad tendon (external oblique, epicranial aponeurosis). Tendinous intersections ( intersectiones tendineae ) — flat tendons interrupting the belly of the rectus abdominis. Sphincter — a circular muscle closing an opening; synergist, antagonist, fixator — muscles that assist, oppose or stabilise the prime mover (agonist). Muscles work in lever systems . In a first-class lever the fulcrum lies between effort and load (triceps brachii extending the elbow); in a second-class lever the load lies between fulcrum and effort (triceps surae in a calf raise); in a third-class lever the effort lies between fulcrum and load (biceps brachii, brachialis). Third-class levers favour speed and range at the expense of force, and they are the commonest arrangement in the limbs. 2. Connective tissue framework: from myoblast to tendon In development, hundreds of myoblasts fuse into a myotube — a long, multinucleated syncytium — which then manufactures myofibrils. Between the basal lamina and the sarcolemma persist satellite cells , undifferentiated cells that can re-enter the myoblast pool and support regeneration. The connective tissue sheaths arrange the muscle in a hierarchy: each fibre is wrapped by endomysium , a bundle of fibres (a fascicle ) by perimysium , and the whole muscle by epimysium , which is continuous with the deep fascia and with the tendon. Within a tendon the same connective tissue continues between the collagen bundles as endotenon (endotenonium), carrying vessels and nerves; this continuum is what transmits the force of contraction from the sarcolemma to the bone. Sheath Latin What it encloses Endomysium endomysium A single muscle fibre; reticular fibres interweave with those of neighbouring fibres and with the fluted ends of the fibre at the myotendinous junction. Perimysium perimysium A fascicle; carries the larger vessels and nerve branches. Epimysium epimysium The whole muscle; continuous with the deep fascia and with the tendon or aponeurosis. Endotenon endotenonium Fine connective tissue between collagen bundles within a tendon, continuous with perimysium. 3. Fasciae Fascia ( fascia ) is a sheet or band of connective tissue that envelops, separates and connects muscles and organs. Three layers are distinguished. Superficial fascia ( fascia superficialis ) — loose connective and adipose tissue immediately beneath the skin, present everywhere except the face; it contains cutaneous vessels, cutaneous nerves and, in the neck, the platysma. Deep fascia ( fascia profunda ) — dense irregular connective tissue covering and separating muscle groups, sending intermuscular septa to the bone and so creating osteofascial compartments . Named examples: fascia brachii, fascia lata, thoracolumbar fascia, fascia of the leg. Subserous fascia ( fascia subserosa ) — connects the serous lining of a body cavity to the wall (endothoracic fascia, fascia transversalis). Local specialisations of deep fascia act as mechanical accessories: retinacula hold tendons against bone, fibrous sheaths form tunnels for tendons, and bursae reduce friction where a tendon crosses bone. Deep fascia also gives attachment to muscle fibres (intermuscular septa, iliotibial tract) and shapes the venous return of the limbs by compressing the deep veins during contraction. 4. Types of muscles Criterion Types Examples and note Fascicular arrangement Parallel (fusiform, strap-like), unipennate, bipennate, multipennate, circular Biceps brachii (fusiform, long range), flexor pollicis longus (unipennate), dorsal interossei (bipennate — fascicles insert into a thin tendon in the middle), deltoid (multipennate), orbicularis oculi (circular). The more oblique the fascicles, the greater the force and the shorter the range of shortening. Number of heads Two, three, four heads Biceps brachii, triceps brachii, quadriceps femoris Number of bellies One, two (digastric), many Digastric, rectus abdominis with its tendinous intersections Shape Flat, quadrate, triangular, spindle-shaped Occipitofrontalis (flat), pronator quadratus, temporalis Function Flexor, extensor, abductor, adductor, rotator, supinator, sphincter, dilator, levator, depressor, tensor The name usually states origin, insertion, action or position (sternocleidomastoid, flexor carpi radialis) Histochemistry Red (type I-like), white (type II-like), intermediate fibres Red fibres contract more slowly but resist fatigue; white fibres are fast and easily fatigued, with fewer mitochondria and less myoglobin. The nerve supply determines the fibre type. 5. Motor innervation Every skeletal muscle receives one or more somatic motor nerves , each containing myelinated axons of alpha motoneurons . A single muscle fibre forms a synapse with only one motoneuron, while one motoneuron innervates many fibres: the motoneuron together with all the fibres it supplies is a motor unit . The degree of divergence matches the precision of the movement — a few fibres per motoneuron in the extraocular muscles, several hundred in the back muscles. All fibres of a motor unit contract together, and the strength of a whole muscle is graded by the number of motor units recruited and by their firing frequency. The contact is the neuromuscular junction ( junctio neuromuscularis ), a specialised synapse on the motor end plate . The presynaptic terminal contains synaptic vesicles of acetylcholine ; the postsynaptic membrane carries nicotinic acetylcholine receptors in junctional folds. Acetylcholine released into the primary synaptic cleft depolarises the sarcolemma, and acetylcholinesterase in the basal lamina of the folds then hydrolyses it, so that one nerve impulse gives one contraction. The gamma motoneurons are a separate set: they do not innervate extrafusal fibres but the contractile poles of the intrafusal fibres of muscle spindles, and thereby set the sensitivity of the proprioceptive system. 6. Sensory and autonomic innervation Receptor Afferent Location and function Muscle spindle Group Ia (dynamic) and group II (static) Encapsulated bundle of intrafusal fibres lying parallel to the extrafusal fibres; signals the length of the muscle and the rate of change of length, and drives the stretch reflex. Nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibres; motor supply from gamma motoneurons. Tendon organ (Golgi) Group Ib Lies at the muscle–tendon interface in series with the fibres; monitors tensile force and inhibits the motor neurons when the load threatens the muscle, tendon or bone. Free nerve endings Thin myelinated and unmyelinated fibres In fascia, tendon and periosteum: pain and crude touch; the source of muscle and fascial pain in strain and compartment syndrome. Autonomic fibres Postganglionic sympathetic Vasomotor supply of the muscle vessels; adjust blood flow to the working muscle. Proprioceptive information ascends to the cerebellum for subconscious control and to the cortex, where it contributes to conscious position sense. Muscle tone — the continuous low-level motor discharge that keeps a muscle firm at rest — depends on the spindle–gamma loop and is lost in lower motoneuron lesions. Must-memorise Three sheaths: endomysium (fibre), perimysium (fascicle), epimysium (whole muscle); endotenon continues into the tendon. Three fasciae: superficial (fat and cutaneous structures), deep (compartments, septa, retinacula), subserous (lining of cavities). Motor unit = one alpha motoneuron + all the muscle fibres it innervates; the smaller the unit, the finer the control. Muscle spindle = length and rate of change (Ia, II); tendon organ = tension (Ib); both are proprioceptive and protective. Motor end plate transmitter: acetylcholine on nicotinic receptors, broken down by acetylcholinesterase. Clinical / radiological correlation Lower motoneuron lesion: flaccid paralysis, atrophy, loss of reflexes and fasciculations; an upper motoneuron lesion gives spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia. Muscle strain: tearing of fibres and endomysium, typically at the myotendinous junction; MRI shows high signal from oedema and haemorrhage. Myasthenia gravis: autoantibodies against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor produce fatigable weakness, worst in the evening. Compartment syndrome: raised pressure inside a closed osteofascial compartment compromises the capillary circulation and the nerves; deep fascia and intermuscular septa define the compartments. Denervation and reinnervation: the nerve determines the fibre type, so cross-innervation changes the histochemical and functional character of the muscle. Likely follow-up questions What is the difference between origin and insertion? — Origin is the relatively fixed proximal attachment, insertion the moved distal one; when the body moves instead, the roles reverse (fixed and mobile points). Why are pennate muscles stronger but shorter in range? — Fascicles insert obliquely into the tendon: more fibres per unit length, but each shortens obliquely, so the effective excursion is smaller. What happens to a muscle after its nerve is cut? — Paralysis, loss of tone and reflexes, then atrophy and fatty replacement. What are satellite cells? — Quiescent myogenic cells under the basal lamina that regenerate muscle fibres after injury."},{"id":"t-I-4","p":"I","n":4,"t":"Biomechanism of the muscle contraction. Structural and functional features of myo- and osteotendinous junctions, tendon sheaths. Intratendinous torsion and compression during exercise.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer Contraction is a sliding-filament process: myosin heads of the thick filaments form cyclic cross-bridges with actin of the thin filaments and pull them towards the M line, so the sarcomere shortens while the filaments themselves keep their length. It is triggered by excitation–contraction coupling — nerve impulse, acetylcholine at the motor end plate, depolarisation of the T-tubules, calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, calcium binding to troponin C. Force is transmitted from the sarcomere through the series-elastic component to the tendon. The force leaves the muscle at the myotendinous junction , where the folded sarcolemma and its basal lamina interlock with collagen, and enters bone at the osteotendinous junction , where tendon collagen continues through fibrocartilage and calcified fibrocartilage into bone as Sharpey's fibres. Tendon sheaths (synovial and fibrous) reduce friction, and in exercise tendons also undergo intratendinous torsion and compression , to which they adapt by fibrocartilaginous metaplasia. 1. The sarcomere and the sliding filament theory The sarcomere ( sarcomerum ) extends from one Z disc to the next and is the contractile unit. Its alternating bands — anisotropic A band , isotropic I band , the lighter H band flanked by the M line , and the Z line — arise from the regular overlap of two sets of filaments: thin filaments of F-actin (about 1 μm long, 7 nm thick) and thick filaments of myosin II (about 1.5 μm long, 15 nm thick, roughly 300 myosin molecules). Each thick filament is surrounded by six thin filaments at 60° intervals. The filaments themselves do not shorten; the thin filaments slide past the thick ones, so the Z discs approach, the I band narrows, the H band disappears and the A band stays constant . Structure Composition and role Thin filament F-actin (two twisted chains of G-actin), tropomyosin in the groove masking the myosin-binding site, and the tripartite troponin : TnC binds calcium, TnT binds tropomyosin, TnI inhibits the actin–myosin interaction. Thick filament Myosin II: two heavy chains forming a rod and two globular S1 heads , each with an ATP-binding site and an actin-binding site; the heads project at the two ends, leaving a smooth central region. Z disc Alpha-actinin anchors the thin filaments; desmin links adjacent myofibrils and dystrophin links desmin to the sarcolemma at the costameres. M line C protein and myomesin interconnect the thick filaments in register. Titin Very large elastic protein running from the Z disc to the M line; centres the thick filament and is the main intracellular passive elastic element . Nebulin Runs along the thin filament and defines its length. The cross-bridge cycle runs in four steps. ATP is hydrolysed on the S1 head and the head binds to the exposed actin site; release of inorganic phosphate strengthens the bond and tilts the head — the power stroke — dragging the thin filament towards the sarcomere centre; ADP is released; a new ATP binds and detaches the head, which is then re-cocked. Each cycle consumes one ATP and the cycle must repeat a few hundred times for a full contraction. When ATP is absent the heads cannot detach: this is the molecular basis of rigor mortis . 2. Excitation–contraction coupling An action potential in the alpha motoneuron depolarises the presynaptic terminal and voltage-gated calcium channels open; synaptic vesicles release acetylcholine into the primary synaptic cleft. Acetylcholine binds nicotinic receptors of the motor end plate; ligand-gated sodium channels open and the sarcolemma depolarises. Acetylcholinesterase in the basal lamina of the junctional folds terminates the signal. The depolarisation spreads along the sarcolemma and down the T-tubules (invaginations of the sarcolemma filled with extracellular fluid), which lie at the I–A junction, two per sarcomere. In the triad (one T-tubule + two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum) the voltage-sensitive dihydropyridine receptor of the T-tubule activates the ryanodine receptor calcium-release channel of the terminal cisterna, and calcium floods the sarcoplasm. Calcium binds troponin C ; the troponin–tropomyosin complex rolls deeper into the actin groove, exposing the myosin-binding sites, and the cross-bridge cycle begins. Relaxation follows when the stimulus stops: calcium-release channels close, calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum by calcium-ATPase and sequestered by calsequestrin, troponin C releases its calcium, tropomyosin again masks the actin sites and the muscle returns passively to its resting length, aided by the elastic recoil of titin and of the tendon. 3. Motor unit, series-elastic component and whole-muscle mechanics The motor unit is one alpha motoneuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates; all its fibres contract together, and a single muscle fibre obeys the all-or-none law. The force of a whole muscle is therefore graded in two ways: by recruitment of more motor units (small units first) and by summation of twitches into a fused tetanus as the firing frequency rises. Depending on the load, the contraction is isometric (tension rises, length unchanged), isotonic (the muscle shortens against a constant load) or eccentric (the muscle lengthens while producing tension). The length–tension relationship follows from filament overlap: tension is greatest at an intermediate sarcomere length and falls when the muscle is over-shortened or over-stretched. Contractile force has to reach the bone through elastic material arranged in series with the sarcomeres — the series-elastic component . It consists of the intracellular titin and the cytoskeleton, the tendon and aponeurosis, and the cross-bridges themselves. Because this component is extensible, it stores elastic energy when stretched and returns it during the next contraction: this is the stretch-shortening cycle that makes a counter-movement jump more powerful than a squat jump from rest. Clinically the same property explains why a tendon can be pre-loaded by a short counter-movement, and why a stiff, unyielding tendon transmits force quickly but tolerates less elongation. 4. Myotendinous and osteotendinous junctions 4.1 Myotendinous junction At the myotendinous junction ( junctio myotendinea ) the end of the muscle fibre is not flat: the sarcolemma and its basal lamina form deep folds and finger-like processes into which the tendon collagen bundles insert, so that the surface area for force transmission is greatly increased. Reticular fibres link the collagen bundles to the basal lamina, and dystrophin, desmin and the costameres anchor the myofibrils to the sarcolemma. Two consequences follow: the junction is the site where force concentration is highest, and it is a weak point in eccentric loading — hence most muscle strains occur here or just proximal to it. 4.2 Osteotendinous junction (enthesis) Where a tendon meets bone the tissue changes step by step: tendon → uncalcified fibrocartilage → calcified fibrocartilage → bone. The fibrocartilage — produced when fibroblasts under tensile and compressive load differentiate into chondrocytes — buffers the stiffness mismatch between soft tendon and rigid bone. The tendon collagen does not simply stop at the bone surface: it continues into the bone matrix as Sharpey's fibres (perforating fibres), exactly as the periosteum is anchored; the woven bone laid down around these fibres persists here, whereas elsewhere it is replaced by lamellar bone. Clinically, this zone is the enthesis , the seat of enthesopathy and of avulsion fractures, and in children the apophyseal equivalent is the growth plate. Feature Myotendinous junction Osteotendinous junction Interface Muscle fibre with tendon collagen Tendon with bone Specialisation Folded sarcolemma and basal lamina, reticular fibres, dystrophin–desmin–costamere complex Fibrocartilage, calcified fibrocartilage, Sharpey's fibres Function Amplifies the attachment area and transmits sarcomere force Buffers the stiffness mismatch and anchors the tendon Typical failure Muscle strain, partial or complete tear Avulsion fracture, enthesopathy, insertional tendinopathy 5. Tendon sheaths: synovial and fibrous A tendon that must run over a long distance, or around a bony angle, is provided with a synovial tendon sheath ( vagina synovialis tendinis ): a closed sac of synovial membrane with a parietal layer lining the fibrous tunnel and a visceral layer attached to the surface of the tendon, the two separated by a thin film of synovial fluid. The visceral layer is continuous with the parietal layer through a mesotendon , which carries vessels and nerves to the tendon. The fibrous sheath ( vagina fibrosa tendinis ) is the dense connective tissue tunnel around it — retinacula and the fibrous digital sheaths strengthened by annular and cruciform ligaments. The two work together: the fibrous sheath is the pulley that prevents bowing, the synovial sheath is the bearing that makes gliding almost frictionless. Feature Synovial sheath Fibrous sheath Tissue Synovial membrane (parietal and visceral layers) Dense connective tissue (retinaculum, annular and cruciform ligaments) Contents Film of synovial fluid No cavity; encloses the synovial sheath and tendon Function Lubrication, nourishment of the tendon, friction-free gliding Holds the tendon to bone, prevents bowing, forms a pulley Examples Common flexor sheath of the fingers, sheath of flexor pollicis longus, sheaths of the extensor compartments of the wrist Flexor and extensor retinacula, fibrous digital sheaths of the fingers Details worth remembering: the sheaths of the thumb and little finger are continuous with the carpal sheaths, while those of the second to fourth fingers are interrupted in the palm and recommence on the fingers; the tendon of palmaris longus has no synovial sheath. Tenosynovitis is inflammation of tendon plus sheath; fibrosis and thickening produce trigger finger , and inflammation of the first extensor compartment is De Quervain's syndrome . 6. Intratendinous torsion and compression during exercise Tendon is not a straight cable. Its collagen bundles follow the line of pull of their muscle fibres, and when two or more muscles with different directions of pull share one tendon, the bundles must rotate about the long axis of the tendon to reach their insertion — this is intratendinous torsion . The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon is the classic example: the fibres of the gastrocnemius and soleus converge and rotate as they descend, so that fibres that begin on one side of the leg end on another side of the calcaneus. The rotator cuff tendons show the same phenomenon on a smaller scale, twisting as they wrap round the humeral head before inserting on the tubercles; the long head of the biceps brachii, spiralling through the intertubercular sulcus, is a further example. Torsion matters mechanically. It distributes the load across the tendon in a spiral rather than in one plane, allows the tendon to change direction without a sharp kink, and lets the tendon store and release elastic energy during the stretch-shortening cycle. During exercise, however, it produces shear between bundles, and repetition under shear is one route to tendinopathy. The second loading mode is compression : wherever a tendon passes under a retinaculum, over a bony prominence or through a narrow osteofibrous tunnel, the tendon is pressed against bone or pulley. Examples are the supraspinatus tendon under the acromion and acromioclavicular ligament in the subacromial space, the flexor tendons in the carpal tunnel, the tibialis posterior and flexor tendons behind the medial malleolus and the peroneal tendons behind the lateral malleolus. The tendon adapts to these loads. Sustained compression, and excessive tensile load, make fibroblasts differentiate into chondrocytes and lay down cartilage matrix, so that part of the tendon becomes fibrocartilage — the same adaptation that creates the fibrocartilaginous pads at tendon insertions and under pulleys. This makes the tendon better able to resist compression, but it also changes its material properties. Regular loading increases collagen turnover, tendon stiffness and cross-sectional area; sudden unaccustomed loading, or loading in a tendon already weakened by age or tendinopathy, may exceed the tendon's tolerance. Must-memorise Sliding filament theory: filaments keep their length; A band constant, I band narrows, H band disappears. Coupling chain: nerve → acetylcholine → end plate → T-tubule → DHP receptor → ryanodine receptor → Ca²⁺ → troponin C → tropomyosin moves. Relaxation: calcium re-sequestered by the sarcoplasmic reticulum; ATP is needed both for the power stroke and for detachment. Myotendinous junction: folded sarcolemma, basal lamina, reticular fibres, dystrophin. Osteotendinous junction: fibrocartilage, calcified fibrocartilage, Sharpey's fibres. Synovial sheath lubricates; fibrous sheath is the pulley. Achilles and rotator cuff tendons are twisted (intratendinous torsion); tendons under retinacula are compressed and adapt by fibrocartilage. Clinical / radiological correlation Muscle strain: failure at or near the myotendinous junction after an eccentric contraction; MRI shows fluid and oedema in the muscle–tendon unit. Tendinopathy and rupture: the calcaneal tendon ruptures typically in a tendon already weakened by overuse or degeneration, with a palpable gap in the tendon; complete rotator-cuff tears may even be asymptomatic. Subacromial impingement: the supraspinatus tendon passes through a space of fixed dimensions, so swelling, bursal fluid or bony spurs compress it when the arm is abducted; the tendon's blood supply is relatively poor and calcific deposits may form. Tenosynovitis, trigger finger and De Quervain's syndrome: inflammation and fibrosis of the tendon–sheath unit interfere with gliding and cause pain and triggering. Carpal tunnel syndrome: swelling of the flexor tendons and their sheaths raises the pressure on the median nerve in the carpal tunnel. Rigor mortis is the clinical demonstration that ATP is required for cross-bridge detachment. Likely follow-up questions Why does the A band not change during contraction? — It is the length of the thick filament, which does not shorten; the thin filaments only slide over it. What is the series-elastic component? — The elastic material in series with the sarcomeres (titin, tendon, aponeurosis) that stores and returns elastic energy. Why do tendons pass through synovial sheaths only in certain places? — Where they must glide over long distances or around angles; a sheath reduces friction and nourishes the tendon. Why is the myotendinous junction a weak point? — It is the site of greatest force concentration, where a soft contractile tissue meets a stiff collagenous one. What is the functional significance of intratendinous torsion? — It distributes load spirally, permits a change of direction and contributes to elastic energy storage."},{"id":"t-I-5","p":"I","n":5,"t":"The bones, joints and movements of the shoulder girdle. Radiological anatomy of the shoulder girdle.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The shoulder girdle is built from two bones — the clavicle and the scapula . Its only true articulation with the axial skeleton is the sternoclavicular joint ; the clavicle and scapula are joined to each other by the acromioclavicular joint ; and the scapula glides on the thoracic wall through the scapulothoracic mechanism , which is not a true joint. Elevation–depression, protraction–retraction and upward–downward rotation of the scapula are produced by the trapezius, levator scapulae, rhomboids, serratus anterior, pectoralis minor and subclavius, and together these movements greatly increase the reach of the arm. 1. Bones of the girdle The clavicle ( clavicula ) is a subcutaneous, S-shaped strut lying across the root of the neck. Its expanded sternal (medial) end articulates with the manubrium of the sternum; its flattened acromial (lateral) end articulates with the acromion. The inferior surface carries the subclavian groove (for the subclavius), the conoid tubercle and the trapezoid line (for the coracoclavicular ligament). It is a mixed bone — cartilaginous medially, membranous laterally — and it is the first bone in the body to begin ossification ; it is also the only long bone with a sternal epiphysis , which appears late and fuses last of all epiphyses, so the medial end of the clavicle is still useful for age estimation in the third decade. The scapula ( scapula ) is a flat, triangular bone with three borders (superior, medial, lateral), three angles (superior, inferior, lateral) and two surfaces. The costal surface bears the subscapular fossa; the dorsal surface is divided by the spine into the supraspinous and infraspinous fossae. The spine ends in the acromion , which is subcutaneous and forms the point of the shoulder; the coracoid process projects forwards above the glenoid. Other landmarks: the suprascapular notch (converted into a foramen by the superior transverse scapular ligament), the shallow, pear-shaped glenoid cavity with its supraglenoid and infraglenoid tubercles , and the subscapular fossa on the costal surface. 2. Sternoclavicular joint — surfaces, capsule, ligaments, synovium The sternoclavicular joint ( articulatio sternoclavicularis ) is a synovial saddle-shaped joint between the sternal end of the clavicle, the clavicular notch of the manubrium and the superior part of the first costal cartilage. A complete articular disc attaches above to the interclavicular ligament and below to the first costal cartilage and divides the cavity into two compartments ; because of this disc the joint behaves functionally like a double ball-and-socket joint. It is the only true joint between the upper limb and the axial skeleton , so the whole weight of the limb is transmitted through it. Capsule: fibrous sleeve attached to the margins of the articular surfaces, lined by synovial membrane in both compartments. Anterior and posterior sternoclavicular ligaments: thickenings of the capsule in front of and behind the joint. Interclavicular ligament: links the two clavicular ends across the jugular notch and to the superior surface of the manubrium. Costoclavicular (rhomboid) ligament: from the first rib and costal cartilage to the inferior surface of the medial clavicle; the main weight-bearing support of the joint, restricting elevation. Close-packed position: full retraction; nerve supply: medial supraclavicular nerve (C3–C4) and the nerve to subclavius (C5–C6); blood supply: internal thoracic and suprascapular arteries. 3. Acromioclavicular joint — surfaces, capsule, ligaments, synovium The acromioclavicular joint ( articulatio acromioclavicularis ) is a small synovial plane joint between an oval facet on the medial surface of the acromion and a matching facet on the acromial end of the clavicle. A small, variable and usually incomplete fibrocartilaginous disc lies between the surfaces; the capsule is reinforced by: the acromioclavicular ligament — a weak thickening of the capsule superiorly; the coracoclavicular ligament — not attached to the joint at all, but the chief suspensory ligament of the limb on the clavicle . It consists of the anterior trapezoid ligament (trapezoid line) and the posterior conoid ligament (conoid tubercle). Movements are small glides in the anteroposterior and vertical planes plus some axial rotation; they are only visible clinically as a step between clavicle and acromion. Close-packed position: full elevation of the arm (scapula fully rotated upwards). Nerve supply: suprascapular nerve (C5–C6) and branches of the lateral pectoral nerve; blood supply: suprascapular and thoraco-acromial arteries. 4. Scapulothoracic mechanism and the movements of the girdle The scapula has no direct joint with the thorax. Its costal surface rests on the serratus anterior , which itself glides on the thoracic wall over loose areolar tissue — the scapulothoracic “joint” , a functional sliding plane, not a true joint. All girdle movement is therefore a combination of movement at the sternoclavicular and acromioclavicular joints. The scapula can be elevated , depressed , protracted (drawn forwards), retracted (drawn backwards) and rotated upwards or downwards around an axis perpendicular to its dorsal surface; the clavicle rotates around its long axis to allow the last movement. Movement Principal muscles Nerve supply Elevation Trapezius (upper fibres), levator scapulae; rhomboids assist Accessory n. [XI] + C3, C4; dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5) and C3, C4 Depression Trapezius (lower fibres), serratus anterior (lower slips), pectoralis minor, subclavius; gravity Accessory n.; long thoracic n. (C5–C7); medial pectoral n. (C8–T1); nerve to subclavius (C5–C6) Protraction Serratus anterior, pectoralis minor Long thoracic n. (C5–C7); medial pectoral n. (C8–T1) Retraction Trapezius (middle fibres), rhomboid major and minor Accessory n. [XI] + C3, C4; dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5) Upward rotation Trapezius (upper and lower fibres as a force couple) with serratus anterior (lower slips) Accessory n.; long thoracic n. (C5–C7) Downward rotation Rhomboid major and minor, levator scapulae; gravity Dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5); C3, C4 + dorsal scapular n. (C5) Upward rotation is the clinically most important movement: without it the arm cannot be elevated above the horizontal, because the greater tubercle would impinge on the acromion. It is produced by a force couple between the upper trapezius and the lower serratus anterior, which pull on the lateral end of the clavicle and on the inferior angle of the scapula respectively. 5. Radiological anatomy of the shoulder girdle The girdle is examined by an anteroposterior (AP) shoulder radiograph , an AP and lateral (scapular Y) view of the scapula , an axillary view and dedicated acromioclavicular views (usually bilateral, sometimes with weights). The scapular Y view shows the body of the scapula as a Y: the humeral head must lie centred in the glenoid, in front of the acromion and coracoid. On the AP view the medial end of the clavicle should be at the same level as the contralateral one, the inferior cortex of the clavicle should be aligned with the inferior cortex of the acromion , and the coracoclavicular distance should be symmetric and equal on both sides. The joint spaces that must be inspected are the sternoclavicular , the acromioclavicular and the glenohumeral spaces. Ossification: the clavicle is the first bone to ossify and is largely ossified at birth; the scapula ossifies from one centre for the body and separate centres for the coracoid, glenoid, acromion, medial border and inferior angle , which fuse during adolescence. An unfused acromial apophysis ( os acromiale ) is a normal variant and must not be mistaken for a fracture; equally, the separate apophyses of the coracoid and acromion in a child are not fractures. Ultrasound and MRI are the methods for the rotator cuff, the subacromial–subdeltoid bursa, the labrum and the acromioclavicular ligaments; CT is used for complex scapular or clavicular fractures. 6. Clinical anatomy of the girdle Fracture of the clavicle — the typical site is the middle third . The clavicle is the only bony connection between the upper limb and the trunk, and fractures are common. The medial fragment is pulled upwards by the sternocleidomastoid, the lateral fragment is drawn downwards by the weight of the arm; the bone is subcutaneous, so the fracture is palpable, and non-union is common because the fragments move. Acromioclavicular separation — a minor injury tears the capsule and acromioclavicular ligament; more severe trauma disrupts the conoid and trapezoid ligaments, and the clavicle rides upwards (step deformity). This is a girdle injury, not a dislocation of the shoulder joint. Sternoclavicular dislocation — anterior is more common; a posterior dislocation is dangerous because the medial clavicle can compress the great vessels or the trachea in the root of the neck. Winging of the scapula — injury to the long thoracic nerve (C5–C7) paralyses the serratus anterior; the medial border and inferior angle lift away from the chest wall, most obvious when the patient pushes forwards against a wall. Accessory nerve palsy — trapezius paralysis gives a drooping shoulder, inability to shrug against resistance and weakness of abduction above 90 degrees. Must-memorise Girdle = clavicle + scapula; two true joints (sternoclavicular, acromioclavicular) + one functional joint (scapulothoracic). The sternoclavicular joint is the only true articulation of the upper limb with the axial skeleton ; its disc divides it into two compartments; the costoclavicular ligament carries the weight. The coracoclavicular ligament (trapezoid + conoid) is the chief suspensory ligament of the limb on the clavicle — it is why the acromioclavicular joint does not give way easily. Six girdle movements: elevation, depression, protraction, retraction, upward rotation, downward rotation — know one prime mover and its nerve for each. Close-packed: sternoclavicular = full retraction; acromioclavicular = full elevation of the arm. Clavicle fracture = middle third; scapular winging = long thoracic nerve; drooping shoulder = accessory nerve. Clinical / radiological correlation On an AP shoulder radiograph the humeral head overlaps the glenoid ; on the scapular Y view it must sit in the centre of the Y. Displacement out of the Y indicates glenohumeral dislocation. Bilateral acromioclavicular views are needed because the normal coracoclavicular distance varies between individuals; asymmetry is the sign of injury. Do not diagnose a fracture through the acromion or coracoid in a child: those are separate ossification centres that fuse in adolescence. Clavicular fractures may injure the underlying brachial plexus and subclavian vessels ; always test the radial pulse, sensation and hand movement. Likely follow-up questions What is the only bony connection between the upper limb and the trunk? — the clavicle, through the sternoclavicular joint. Why does the shoulder droop when the accessory nerve is injured? — the trapezius is the powerful elevator and rotator of the scapula; without it the scapula descends and cannot be rotated upwards. Why is upward rotation of the scapula necessary? — it tilts the glenoid upwards and moves the acromion away from the greater tubercle, allowing elevation above the horizontal. Which ligaments resist elevation of the clavicle? — the costoclavicular ligament, with the acromioclavicular and coracoclavicular ligaments."},{"id":"t-I-6","p":"I","n":6,"t":"The gross and radiological anatomy and movements of the shoulder joint and the participating muscles. The morphological features and biomechanics of the rotator cuff.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The shoulder (glenohumeral) joint is a synovial ball-and-socket (spheroidal) joint between the head of the humerus and the glenoid cavity of the scapula. It is multiaxial and the most mobile joint of the body, and it buys that mobility with a small, shallow socket; stability comes instead from the glenoid labrum, the capsule and its ligaments, the long head of biceps, the coraco-acromial arch and above all the rotator cuff — supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis. Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, medial and lateral rotation and circumduction are each produced by a defined set of muscles, all of them innervated by branches of the brachial plexus. 1. Articular surfaces and type of joint The glenohumeral joint ( articulatio humeri ) is a synovial ball-and-socket joint, multiaxial, between the large spherical head of the humerus (about one third of a sphere) and the small, shallow, pear-shaped glenoid cavity of the scapula. Both surfaces are covered by hyaline cartilage. The socket is far smaller than the ball, so only a fraction of the head is in contact with the glenoid at any moment — this is the anatomical basis both of the enormous range of movement and of the joint's instability. The glenoid is deepened and widened at its margin by the glenoid labrum ( labrum glenoidale ), a fibrocartilaginous collar; superiorly the labrum is continuous with the origin of the long head of the biceps brachii at the supraglenoid tubercle, and that tendon passes through the joint cavity above the head. 2. Capsule, ligaments and synovial features Fibrous capsule: attaches to the margin of the glenoid outside the labrum and to the anatomical neck of the humerus; on the medial side the attachment is lower, extending onto the shaft, and the capsule is here redundant (folded) so that it can unfold in abduction. Glenohumeral ligaments — superior, middle and inferior thickenings of the anterior capsule running from the supraglenoid margin to the lesser tubercle and adjacent neck. The inferior one is the main static restraint against anteroinferior dislocation when the arm is abducted and laterally rotated. Coracohumeral ligament — from the base of the coracoid process to the greater tubercle; strengthens the capsule superiorly and bridges the rotator interval between supraspinatus and subscapularis, which transmits the long biceps tendon. Transverse humeral ligament — a band between the greater and lesser tubercles which holds the long biceps tendon in the intertubercular sulcus. Coraco-acromial arch — coracoid process, acromion and the coraco-acromial ligament form a secondary, bony–ligamentous roof over the head; it is the static ceiling against which the supraspinatus tendon works. Synovial membrane: lines the capsule and reflects around the long biceps tendon as a synovial sheath extending into the intertubercular sulcus; it protrudes through apertures in the capsule to form bursae, the most constant being the subtendinous bursa of subscapularis . Bursae not communicating with the joint: the subacromial (subdeltoid) bursa between acromion, deltoid, supraspinatus tendon and capsule; also bursae between acromion and skin, between coracoid and capsule, and around the tendons of coracobrachialis, teres major, long head of triceps and latissimus dorsi. Close-packed position: full abduction with lateral rotation — the position in which the capsule and ligaments are most taut and the joint most congruent; open-packed (resting) position: the arm hanging by the side in slight abduction. 3. Movements and participating muscles Movements are described in the anatomical position: flexion carries the arm forwards, extension backwards, abduction away from the trunk in the coronal plane, adduction towards the trunk, medial (internal) and lateral (external) rotation around the long axis of the humerus, and circumduction is the combination of all of them. Every movement is accompanied by movement of the scapula; above the horizontal plane, abduction is no longer possible without upward rotation of the scapula (see I.5). Movement Principal muscles Nerve supply Flexion Deltoid (anterior/clavicular part), pectoralis major (clavicular head), coracobrachialis; biceps brachii assists Axillary n. (C5, C6); lateral and medial pectoral nn. (C5–T1); musculocutaneous n. (C5–C7) Extension Latissimus dorsi, teres major, deltoid (posterior part), long head of triceps Thoracodorsal n. (C6–C8); lower subscapular n. (C5–C7); axillary n. (C5, C6); radial n. (C6–C8) Abduction Supraspinatus (initiates), deltoid (middle part) — the main abductor; serratus anterior and trapezius rotate the scapula for the part above 90 degrees Suprascapular n. (C5, C6); axillary n. (C5, C6); long thoracic n. (C5–C7); accessory n. [XI] Adduction Pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi, teres major, coracobrachialis; gravity Pectoral nn. (C5–T1); thoracodorsal n.; lower subscapular n.; musculocutaneous n. (C5–C7) Medial rotation Subscapularis (prime), teres major, latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major, deltoid (anterior part) Upper and lower subscapular nn. (C5, C6); lower subscapular n.; thoracodorsal n.; pectoral nn.; axillary n. Lateral rotation Infraspinatus, teres minor, deltoid (posterior part) Suprascapular n. (C5, C6); axillary n. (C5, C6); axillary n. 4. The rotator cuff — morphology The rotator cuff ( musculotendinosus cuff) is a collar of four musculotendinous units that arise from the scapula and blend with the fibrous capsule of the glenohumeral joint before inserting on the tubercles of the humerus. Together they surround the joint posteriorly, superiorly and anteriorly — only inferiorly is the capsule uncovered, and that is the weakest point (the site of inferior and anteroinferior dislocation). Muscle Origin Insertion Action Nerve Supraspinatus (m. supraspinatus) Medial two-thirds of the supraspinous fossa and the overlying deep fascia Superior facet of the greater tubercle, passing beneath the acromion and the subacromial bursa Initiates abduction; compresses the head into the glenoid Suprascapular n. (C5, C6) Infraspinatus (m. infraspinatus) Medial two-thirds of the infraspinous fossa and its deep fascia Middle facet of the greater tubercle Lateral rotation; stabilisation Suprascapular n. (C5, C6) Teres minor (m. teres minor) Upper two-thirds of a flattened strip along the lateral border of the scapula Inferior facet of the greater tubercle Lateral rotation; stabilisation Axillary n. (C5, C6) Subscapularis (m. subscapularis) Medial two-thirds of the subscapular fossa Lesser tubercle of the humerus Medial rotation; stabilisation Upper and lower subscapular nn. (C5, C6) The tendons of the cuff fuse with the capsule and with each other, forming a continuous musculotendinous ring ; the gap between the supraspinatus and subscapularis tendons, closed by the coracohumeral ligament, is the rotator interval and is the exit route of the long head of biceps. Note the tendon of the long head of biceps inside the joint: it is an intracapsular but extrasynovial structure, and it helps to hold the head down. 5. Biomechanics of the rotator cuff Dynamic stabilisation by compression (“concavity–compression”). The cuff pulls the head into the glenoid cavity; because the glenoid is deepened by the labrum, this compression resists translation in every direction and lets the deltoid move the arm without the head riding upwards. Balancing the deltoid — a force couple. The deltoid pulls the humerus almost vertically upwards; the inferior part of the cuff (infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) pulls it downwards and medially. The two opposite forces produce a couple that rotates the humerus while keeping the head centred in the glenoid, and thereby prevents the head from impinging on the acromion. Rotation couples. In lateral rotation the posterior deltoid, infraspinatus and teres minor work against the subscapularis; in medial rotation the subscapularis, pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi and teres major work against the posterior deltoid. The antagonist partners co-contract, so the head stays centred while it rotates. Fine adjustment to the instantaneous centre of rotation. Because each cuff muscle has a slightly different line of pull, the central nervous system selects the combination that keeps the head congruent throughout the whole arc — the cuff is therefore a steering mechanism, not a prime mover. Static restraints: the capsular ligaments (especially the inferior glenohumeral ligament), the coraco-acromial arch and the negative intra-articular pressure supplement the cuff at the end of the range. 6. Radiological anatomy AP radiograph: the humeral head overlaps the glenoid; the glenohumeral joint space should be uniform and the head spherical; the acromiohumeral interval (the space between the acromion and the head, occupied by the supraspinatus tendon and the subacromial bursa) is narrowed when the cuff is torn and the head rides upwards. Additional views: axillary view (joint space, anterior/posterior dislocation), scapular Y view (head centred), and Stryker notch or Garth views for Hill–Sachs lesions. MRI: the method of choice for the tendons, labrum and capsule; MR arthrography distends the capsule and is best for labral tears; ultrasound is excellent for cuff tears, calcific deposits and the subacromial bursa and allows dynamic examination. Ossification centres of the proximal humerus: the head appears in the first year, the greater tubercle later, the lesser tubercle later still, and the three fuse with the shaft around the end of growth (about 20 years); the epiphyseal plate is therefore a normal radiolucent line in adolescents and must not be called a fracture. Dislocation: with anterior (anteroinferior) dislocation the head lies below and medial to the glenoid; a compression fracture of the posterolateral head ( Hill–Sachs ) and a tear of the anteroinferior labrum ( Bankart ) are the classic associated lesions. 7. Clinical anatomy Anterior dislocation is the commonest; the axillary nerve may be compressed as the head passes through the quadrangular space, and the radial nerve may be stretched, so sensation over the deltoid and the hand extensors must be tested before and after reduction. Rotator cuff disease — impingement and tendinopathy, most often involving supraspinatus as it passes under the acromion; the subacromial space is of fixed size, so swelling, fluid in the bursa or bony spurs cause impingement in abduction. Degenerative change predisposes to partial- and full-thickness tears, with pain on combing the hair or abducting against resistance. Subacromial (subdeltoid) bursitis — inflammation of the bursa makes every glenohumeral movement painful; treated by corticosteroid injection under ultrasound guidance. Suprascapular nerve entrapment at the suprascapular notch gives pain and weakness of supraspinatus and infraspinatus with atrophy of the infraspinous fossa. Adhesive capsulitis (“frozen shoulder”) — thickening and contraction of the capsule, with loss of lateral rotation first; the radiological picture is normal, which is itself an exam point. Must-memorise Type: synovial ball-and-socket, multiaxial; surfaces = head of humerus + glenoid cavity; deepened by the labrum. Rotator cuff = SITS : supraspinatus (abduction), infraspinatus (lateral rotation), teres minor (lateral rotation), subscapularis (medial rotation); all compress and stabilise the head. Nerves of the cuff: suprascapular (C5, C6) for supra- and infraspinatus; axillary (C5, C6) for teres minor; upper and lower subscapular (C5, C6) for subscapularis. The capsule is weak inferiorly, where dislocation occurs; the coraco-acromial arch is the protective roof. Deltoid is the main abductor, supraspinatus initiates abduction; above 90 degrees the scapula must rotate upwards. Close-packed: abduction + lateral rotation; the acromiohumeral interval narrows in cuff tear. Clinical / radiological correlation Painful arc between about 60 and 120 degrees of abduction suggests subacromial impingement; weakness without pain suggests a cuff tear. Drop arm test — a complete supraspinatus tear prevents the patient from holding the abducted arm against gravity. Frozen shoulder has a normal radiograph; cuff arthropathy shows a high-riding head with secondary arthritic change — the two must be told apart. Axillary nerve injury after dislocation paralyses deltoid and teres minor and causes numbness over the lateral aspect of the deltoid. Likely follow-up questions Why is the shoulder the most mobile but least stable joint? — the glenoid is small and shallow, the capsule is lax and the joint relies on the cuff and labrum for stability. What is the force couple of abduction? — deltoid and supraspinatus elevate the arm; the inferior cuff (infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) pulls the head downwards, so the resultant keeps the head centred in the glenoid. Which muscles produce lateral rotation and how are they tested? — infraspinatus and teres minor (suprascapular and axillary nerves); tested with the elbow at the side and the forearm rotated outwards against resistance. What is the rotator interval and why does it matter? — the gap between supraspinatus and subscapularis containing the long biceps tendon and coracohumeral ligament; it is a weak spot and a common site of pathology."},{"id":"t-I-7","p":"I","n":7,"t":"The gross and radiological anatomy and movements of the elbow joint and the participating muscles.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The elbow is a compound synovial joint in which three articulations share one capsule and one synovial cavity: the humeroulnar and humeroradial joints, which are functionally a hinge for flexion and extension, and the proximal radio-ulnar joint , a pivot joint for pronation and supination. The capsule is reinforced by the ulnar and radial collateral ligaments and by the anular ligament of the radius ; fat pads in the coronoid, radial and olecranon fossae are the radiological key to an effusion. Flexion is produced mainly by brachialis and biceps brachii, extension by triceps brachii and anconeus. 1. The three articulations and their types The elbow joint ( articulatio cubiti ) is a complex (compound) joint : one fibrous capsule encloses three separate articulations that share a common synovial cavity. The articular surfaces of all three are covered by hyaline cartilage. Articulation Type Articular surfaces Movement Humeroulnar (articulatio humeroulnaris) Synovial hinge (trochlear, ginglymus ) Trochlea and trochlear groove of the humerus; trochlear notch of the ulna with its olecranon and coronoid processes Flexion–extension Humeroradial (articulatio humeroradialis) Synovial spheroidal, but constrained to a hinge Rounded capitulum of the humerus; concave fovea of the head of the radius Flexion–extension; the radial head also spins here during pronation–supination Proximal radio-ulnar (articulatio radioulnaris proximalis) Synovial pivot (trochoid) Cylindrical side of the radial head; radial notch of the ulna plus the anular ligament Pronation–supination (see I.8) 2. Articular surfaces in detail The distal humerus carries the trochlea medially — a pulley-shaped surface with a central groove, extending further distally than the capitulum — and the rounded capitulum laterally. Above them are the coronoid fossa (anteriorly, receiving the coronoid process in flexion), the radial fossa (anteriorly, receiving the radial head in flexion) and the olecranon fossa (posteriorly, receiving the olecranon in extension). The ulna contributes the trochlear notch , whose upper part is formed by the olecranon and whose lower part is formed by the coronoid process; the radial notch lies on the lateral side of the coronoid process. The radius contributes its disc-shaped head , whose concave upper surface articulates with the capitulum and whose cylindrical rim articulates with the radial notch and the anular ligament. The trochlear notch is divided by a transverse non-articular groove into an olecranon part and a coronoid part; flexion is stopped by bone and by the contact of the forearm with the arm. 3. Capsule, ligaments and synovial features Fibrous capsule — attached anteriorly to the margins of the coronoid and radial fossae and to the front of the medial epicondyle, posteriorly to the margins of the olecranon fossa, and distally to the coronoid process and the olecranon; on the lateral side its free inferior margin passes around the neck of the radius (it does not attach to the radius), which is what allows the radial head to rotate. Ulnar collateral ligament ( lig. collaterale ulnare ) — triangular thickening from the medial epicondyle to the coronoid process and the olecranon; the anterior band is the strongest and tightens in extension. Radial collateral ligament ( lig. collaterale radiale ) — from the lateral epicondyle, blending with the anular ligament; it does not attach to the radius, so the head can rotate underneath it. Anular ligament of the radius ( lig. anulare radii ) — a strong fibrous cuff around the radial head, attached to the anterior and posterior margins of the radial notch; forms about four-fifths of the osseo-fibrous ring in which the head rotates, and is lined internally by cartilage. Synovial membrane — arises from the edges of the articular cartilages, lines the three fossae and the deep surface of the capsule, and sends a sacciform recess under the free inferior margin of the capsule to allow rotation of the radial head. Fat pads — pads of fat between the synovial and fibrous membranes over the coronoid, radial and olecranon fossae; they fill the empty fossae and are pulled out of the way by the brachialis and triceps as the bony processes enter the fossae. Close-packed position: full extension with supination — the olecranon is locked in its fossa and all ligaments are taut; open-packed (resting) position: about 70 degrees of flexion with slight supination. 4. Movements and participating muscles The elbow works as a hinge: flexion brings the forearm against the arm and extension returns it to the straight position. The arc is roughly 140–150 degrees, with a few degrees of hyperextension in some people. The transverse axis of flexion–extension is not exactly right-angled to the long axis of the humerus, so full extension is accompanied by slight lateral rotation of the forearm: in extension the forearm therefore deviates laterally — the carrying angle — and this angle disappears when the forearm is fully pronated. Pronation and supination occur at the proximal radio-ulnar joint and are dealt with in I.8. Muscle Origin Insertion Action at the elbow Nerve Brachialis (m. brachialis) Distal half of the anterior surface of the humerus and the adjacent intermuscular septa Tuberosity of the ulna Main flexor ; works in every position of the forearm Musculocutaneous n. (C5, C6); small radial contribution (C7) Biceps brachii (m. biceps brachii) Long head — supraglenoid tubercle; short head — apex of the coracoid process Radial tuberosity (and the bicipital aponeurosis into the deep fascia) Powerful flexor, strongest with the forearm supinated; also a supinator Musculocutaneous n. (C5, C6) Brachioradialis (m. brachioradialis) Proximal part of the lateral supra-epicondylar ridge of the humerus Lateral surface of the distal radius Accessory flexor, most efficient in mid-pronation Radial n. (C5, C6) Triceps brachii (m. triceps brachii) Long head — infraglenoid tubercle; medial and lateral heads — posterior surface of the humerus Superior surface of the olecranon Main extensor ; the long head also extends and adducts the arm Radial n. (C6, C7, C8) Anconeus (m. anconeus) Lateral epicondyle of the humerus Olecranon and the proximal posterior surface of the ulna Accessory extensor; abducts the ulna during pronation Radial n. (C6, C7, C8) 5. Nerve and blood supply The flexors of the elbow are supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5, C6) — brachialis and biceps — with the radial nerve (C5, C6) for brachioradialis and a small lateral part of brachialis. The extensors are supplied by the radial nerve (C6, C7, C8). The joint itself is innervated predominantly by branches of the radial and musculocutaneous nerves , with some contribution from the ulnar and median nerves (Hilton's law — the nerves of the muscles acting on the joint also supply the joint). The blood supply comes from an anastomotic network around the elbow formed by collateral branches of the brachial and profunda brachii arteries with recurrent branches of the radial and ulnar arteries. 6. Radiological anatomy The elbow is examined by an anteroposterior and a lateral radiograph (in a child often both sides for comparison). The normal relationships to check are: Radiocapitellar line — a line drawn through the centre of the radial neck must pass through the centre of the capitulum in every projection; if it does not, the radial head is dislocated or subluxated. Anterior humeral line — a line down the anterior cortex of the humerus should pass through the middle third of the capitulum; in a supracondylar fracture the capitulum lies behind it. Joint space — the humeroulnar and humeroradial joint spaces should be uniform; the olecranon lies in the olecranon fossa in extension. Fat pads — a small anterior fat pad seen as a teardrop of lucency is normal; a posterior fat pad is never normal and means an intra-articular effusion, hence a fracture (the “fat pad sign”). Ossification centres — these appear in a characteristic order and are a common trap in children. Ossification centre Approximate age of appearance Comment Capitulum 1 year First to appear — remember the mnemonic CRITOE Head of the radius 5 years Looks like a fragment of bone beside the radial neck Medial (internal) epicondyle 5 years An apophysis; may be avulsed with the ulnar collateral ligament Trochlea 11 years Multiple centres give a fragmented appearance Olecranon 12 years Apophysis; must not be read as a fracture Lateral (external) epicondyle 13 years Last to appear; fusion occurs around puberty 7. Clinical anatomy Supracondylar fracture (children) — a transverse fracture above the epicondyles; the distal fragment is pulled posteriorly by the triceps and the brachial artery is “bowstrung” over the proximal fragment, so the forearm flexors may become ischaemic and undergo Volkmann's ischaemic contracture . Pulled elbow — in children under about five years the radial head subluxates from under the lax anular ligament after a sharp pull on the hand; supination and compression reduce it immediately. Fracture of the head of the radius — after a fall on the outstretched hand; often visible only through the fat pad sign , and typically limits full extension. Fracture of the olecranon — the triceps can avulse the fragment; the elbow cannot be actively extended. Epicondylitis — lateral (“tennis elbow”, common extensor origin) and medial (“golfer's elbow”, common flexor origin) overuse strains. Arthritis — loose bodies lodge in the olecranon and coronoid fossae and block extension. Must-memorise Three articulations, one capsule: humeroulnar (hinge), humeroradial (spheroidal but hinge-constrained), proximal radio-ulnar (pivot). Ligaments: ulnar collateral (anterior band strongest), radial collateral, anular ligament of the radius (forms most of the ring around the radial head). The capsule does not attach to the radius — that is why the radial head can rotate; the sacciform recess allows the rotation. Flexors: brachialis (main), biceps brachii, brachioradialis — musculocutaneous and radial nerves. Extensors: triceps (main), anconeus — radial nerve (C6–C8). Close-packed: extension + supination. Carrying angle is present in extension and disappears in full pronation. Radiology: radiocapitellar line, anterior humeral line, posterior fat pad = effusion = fracture; CRITOE order of ossification. Clinical / radiological correlation Always look for the posterior fat pad on the lateral view: it is the most sensitive plain-radiographic sign of an occult intra-articular fracture in the adult elbow. In children, always compare with the other elbow and know the age of the patient — the ossification centres are symmetric and have sclerotic, rounded margins, whereas fractures are sharp and irregular. A supracondylar fracture may threaten the brachial artery and the median nerve: check the radial pulse and the capillary refill before and after treatment. After any fracture of the radius or ulna, image the elbow and the wrist, because a fracture of one bone may be accompanied by dislocation of the other (see I.8). Likely follow-up questions Why is the elbow described as a compound joint? — three articulations with one capsule and one synovial cavity. Why does the elbow not dislocate easily? — the deep trochlear notch grips the trochlea, and the strong collateral ligaments and the bony fossae limit movement. What limits flexion and extension? — extension is stopped by the olecranon in the olecranon fossa and by the anterior band of the ulnar collateral ligament; flexion by the contact of the forearm muscles with the arm and by the coronoid process in the coronoid fossa. Why is the carrying angle clinically important? — it is increased after a malunited supracondylar fracture (cubitus valgus) or decreased (cubitus varus), and both may stretch the ulnar nerve."},{"id":"t-I-8","p":"I","n":8,"t":"Pronation and supination in the forearm: participating joints and muscles.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer Pronation and supination are rotation of the radius around the ulna and occur only in the forearm. Two synovial pivot (trochoid) joints participate: the proximal radio-ulnar joint , where the radial head turns within the ring formed by the radial notch and the anular ligament, and the distal radio-ulnar joint , where the distal end of the radius moves anteromedially over the relatively fixed head of the ulna, the triangular articular disc holding the two together. The interosseous membrane holds the two bones together and transmits force between them. Supination is produced by biceps brachii and supinator, pronation by pronator teres and pronator quadratus, with brachioradialis bringing the forearm back to the mid-position. 1. Definition, axis and range In the anatomical position the palm faces forwards and the radius and ulna are parallel — this is supination . Pronation turns the palm backwards, and the distal end of the radius crosses in front of the ulna. The movement is a rotation around an axis that passes from the centre of the radial head to the ulnar styloid process ; the ulna stays almost still and the radius swings around it, carrying the hand with it. The total arc between full supination and full pronation is about 180 degrees (roughly 80–90 degrees in each direction, with individual variation); the range is greatest with the elbow flexed at a right angle, because in full extension the collateral ligaments and the shape of the joint restrict rotation. 2. Proximal radio-ulnar joint The proximal radio-ulnar joint is a synovial pivot (trochoid) joint . Its articular surfaces are the cylindrical rim of the head of the radius and the radial notch of the ulna , which is lined by cartilage; the rest of the ring is completed by the inner surface of the anular ligament , which is also cartilage-lined. The joint has no capsule of its own — it is part of the elbow joint cavity, and its synovial membrane is continuous with that of the elbow. The radial head is held in the ring by the anular and radial collateral ligaments. Superiorly, the head also spins on the convex capitulum, so the humeroradial and proximal radio-ulnar articulations work together. Close-packed position: full supination. 3. Distal radio-ulnar joint and the articular disc The distal radio-ulnar joint is a synovial pivot joint between the ulnar notch of the radius , the head of the ulna and the triangular articular disc ( discus articularis ), which is attached by its apex to the depression between the ulnar styloid process and the articular surface of the ulnar head (the fovea) and by its base to the angular margin of the radius between the ulnar notch and the carpal articular surface. The disc separates the distal radio-ulnar joint from the wrist joint and is therefore the structure that prevents the ulna from articulating directly with the carpal bones. The joint is held by the palmar and dorsal radio-ulnar ligaments and by the disc; movement is a glide of the ulnar notch over the head of the ulna in an anteromedial direction during pronation. Close-packed position: full pronation. 4. Interosseous membrane and oblique cord The interosseous membrane ( membrana interossea antebrachii ) is a thin fibrous sheet between the medial border of the radius and the lateral border of the ulna. Its fibres run predominantly inferomedially from the radius to the ulna , which is exactly the direction needed to transfer force from the radius (which carries the hand) to the ulna, and then to the humerus. It has a free upper margin just below the radial tuberosity and a small aperture in its distal third ; the anterior interosseous vessels pass between the two bones through these gaps. It provides attachment for muscles of both compartments and — importantly — it does not restrict pronation and supination. The oblique cord ( chorda obliqua ) is a narrow band running from the radial tuberosity to the coronoid process of the ulna; it is taut in supination. 5. Muscles of pronation and supination Two muscles supinate and two pronate the forearm; a fifth, brachioradialis, returns it to the mid-position and is the reason the limb rests with the thumb up. Muscle Origin Insertion Action Nerve and root value Biceps brachii (m. biceps brachii) Supraglenoid tubercle (long head) and apex of the coracoid process (short head) Radial tuberosity and bicipital aponeurosis Supination — strongest supinator when the elbow is flexed; also flexes the elbow Musculocutaneous n. (C5, C6) Supinator (m. supinator) Humeral layer — lateral epicondyle, radial collateral and anular ligaments; ulnar layer — supinator crest of the ulna Lateral surface of the radius superior to the anterior oblique line Supination in every position of the elbow, especially in unresisted movement Posterior interosseous n. (C6, C7) Pronator teres (m. pronator teres) Humeral head — medial epicondyle and supra-epicondylar ridge; ulnar head — medial side of the coronoid process Roughening on the lateral surface of the midshaft of the radius Pronation, especially rapid and resisted; also flexes the elbow Median n. (C6, C7) Pronator quadratus (m. pronator quadratus) Linear ridge on the distal anterior surface of the ulna Distal anterior surface of the radius Pronation — the prime mover in slow, unresisted pronation; stabilises the distal radio-ulnar joint Anterior interosseous n. (median) (C7, C8) Brachioradialis (m. brachioradialis) Proximal part of the lateral supra-epicondylar ridge Lateral surface of the distal radius Returns the forearm to mid-pronation from either extreme; accessory elbow flexor Radial n. (C5, C6) Mechanism: in full pronation the biceps tendon and the supinator are wrapped around the proximal radius ; when they contract they unwrap and so rotate the bone back into supination. In pronation the pronator teres pulls the radius medially across the ulna, and the pronator quadratus pulls the distal radius anteriorly and medially, so the two ends of the radius rotate in the same direction. 6. Radiology and clinical correlations Radiology: on an AP forearm in supination the radius and ulna are parallel; in pronation the radius crosses the ulna and the two bones overlap at their middle thirds. The distal radio-ulnar joint is therefore never seen en face ; its alignment is judged indirectly (ulnar variance — the relative length of the distal radius and ulna — and the congruity of the ulnar head in the sigmoid notch), and CT is used for subtle subluxation. Monteggia's fracture — fracture of the proximal third of the ulna with anterior dislocation of the radial head. Galeazzi's fracture — fracture of the distal third of the radius with subluxation of the ulnar head at the wrist. Colles' fracture — fracture of the distal radius with dorsal displacement of the distal fragment, producing a “dinner-fork” deformity; the distal radio-ulnar joint may be disrupted so supination is lost. Pulled elbow — subluxation of the radial head under the anular ligament in small children; reduced by supination and compression. Pronator syndrome — compression of the median nerve between the two heads of pronator teres, with pain in the proximal forearm and sensory loss in the median distribution. Must-memorise Two pivot joints: proximal radio-ulnar (radial head in the ring of the radial notch + anular ligament) and distal radio-ulnar (ulnar head in the ulnar notch + articular disc). Axis: centre of the radial head to the ulnar styloid; the ulna is the fixed unit and the radius rotates around it. The articular disc separates the distal radio-ulnar joint from the wrist joint ; the ulna therefore does not articulate with the carpal bones. Supination — biceps brachii (elbow flexed) and supinator (any position); pronation — pronator teres (fast, resisted) and pronator quadratus (slow, unresisted). Close-packed: proximal joint in full supination, distal joint in full pronation. Nerves: biceps = musculocutaneous (C5, C6); supinator = posterior interosseous (C6, C7); pronator teres = median (C6, C7); pronator quadratus = anterior interosseous (C7, C8). Clinical / radiological correlation Loss of supination after a distal radial fracture means the distal radio-ulnar joint has been disrupted, not just the bone broken. In a true lateral radiograph of the elbow the radial head must point to the capitulum; a radial head that does not is dislocated (Monteggia equivalent). Pronation is the position of comfort after most forearm fractures; supination is the position in which the forearm is functionally strongest, which is why biceps is tested with the elbow flexed and the forearm supinated. Forearm rotation is a good functional test: the patient should be able to turn the palm fully up and fully down with the elbow at the side. Likely follow-up questions Where does pronation and supination take place? — only in the forearm, at the two radio-ulnar joints, not at the wrist. Why does the supinator work better than biceps when the elbow is extended? — the biceps is at a mechanical disadvantage in extension and is also an elbow flexor; the supinator wraps directly around the radius. What happens to the interosseous membrane during rotation? — its fibres wind and unwind; it never restricts the movement but always transmits force from radius to ulna. Which structure prevents the ulna from touching the carpus? — the triangular articular disc of the distal radio-ulnar joint."},{"id":"t-I-9","p":"I","n":9,"t":"The gross and radiological anatomy and movements of the wrist joint and the participating muscles.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The wrist (radiocarpal) joint is a synovial ellipsoid (condyloid) joint between the distal end of the radius and the triangular articular disc proximally, and the scaphoid, lunate and triquetrum distally; the ulna takes no direct part. It is biaxial — flexion–extension around a transverse axis and radial–ulnar deviation around an anteroposterior axis — and its capsule is reinforced by palmar radiocarpal, palmar ulnocarpal, dorsal radiocarpal and radial and ulnar collateral ligaments. The flexors are flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris, palmaris longus and the long finger flexors; the extensors are extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis and extensor carpi ulnaris. 1. Classification and articular surfaces The wrist joint ( articulatio radiocarpalis ) is a synovial ellipsoid (condyloid) joint, biaxial — movement occurs around two axes at right angles to each other, and circumduction is the combination of both. Proximally the articular surface is formed by the distal end of the radius , which bears the scaphoid and lunate facets, and by the triangular articular disc overlying the distal end of the ulna. Distally the surface is formed by the proximal row of carpal bones — scaphoid, lunate and triquetrum — whose convex surfaces together form an oval, egg-shaped head. The ulna does not articulate with the carpal bones: it is separated from them by the articular disc of the distal radio-ulnar joint. The radiocarpal cavity is therefore a closed space, separate from the distal radio-ulnar joint and normally from the intercarpal joints. 2. Capsule, ligaments and synovial features The capsule surrounds the joint and is attached above to the distal radius and ulna (through the disc) and below to the proximal carpal row. Its palmar part is much thicker and stronger than the dorsal part , which explains why extension is limited sooner than flexion and why the wrist is the commonest site of a sprain after a fall on the extended hand. The ligaments are: Ligament Attachments and course Function Palmar radiocarpal From the anterior margin of the distal radius, passing distally and medially to the scaphoid, lunate and triquetrum; its fibres are continuous with the palmar intercarpal ligaments Strongest ligament of the joint; limits extension, and because it resists torsion it also brings the carpus back with the radius in pronation and supination Palmar ulnocarpal From the ulnar styloid process and the palmar radioulnar ligament to the lunate and triquetrum Supports the ulnar side of the carpus Dorsal radiocarpal From the dorsal margin of the radius to the triquetrum and lunate Thin and weak; limits flexion Radial collateral (radial side) From the radial styloid process to the scaphoid (and a band to the trapezium) Resists ulnar deviation and stabilises the radial side with the scaphoid Ulnar collateral (ulnar side) From the ulnar styloid process to the triquetrum and pisiform; blends with the articular disc Resists radial deviation The synovial membrane lines the capsule and is reflected onto the non-articular parts of the bones. There are no fat pads of the kind seen at the elbow, so an effusion is not detectable on a plain film of the wrist in the same way. Close-packed position: full extension — the position in which the palmar ligaments and both collateral ligaments are taut and the carpus is most congruent with the radius; open-packed position: neutral with slight ulnar deviation. 3. Movements and participating muscles The hand can be flexed , extended , abducted (radial deviation) , adducted (ulnar deviation) and circumducted at the wrist. Two practical points: the range of flexion is slightly greater than that of extension, and ulnar deviation is greater than radial deviation — because the radial styloid process extends further distally and strikes the carpus earlier. In full flexion the hand also deviates to the ulnar side, and in full extension to the radial side; the wrist is therefore a “link” joint whose pure movements are rarely used. Movement Muscles Nerve supply Flexion Flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris, palmaris longus; assisted by flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus, flexor pollicis longus Median n. (C6, C7); ulnar n. (C7, C8, T1); median n. (C7, C8); median and ulnar nn. for FDS and FDP; anterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) for FPL Extension Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor carpi ulnaris; assisted by extensor digitorum Radial n. (C6, C7); deep branch of radial n. (C7, C8); posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) Abduction (radial deviation) Flexor carpi radialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis; abductor pollicis longus assists Median n. (C6, C7); radial n. (C6, C7) and its deep branch (C7, C8); posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) Adduction (ulnar deviation) Flexor carpi ulnaris, extensor carpi ulnaris Ulnar n. (C7, C8, T1); posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) Circumduction Combined action of the above in sequence — Practical ranges (individual variation is considerable): roughly 85° of flexion and 80° of extension; the ranges of deviation are smaller. ⇒ Ulnar deviation is greater than radial deviation , because the radial styloid process projects further distally and stops the carpus earlier. 4. Nerve and blood supply The wrist joint is innervated by branches of the anterior interosseous nerve (from the median nerve) and the posterior interosseous nerve (from the radial nerve), with a small contribution from the ulnar nerve. Its blood supply is derived from the palmar and dorsal carpal arches , which are fed by the radial, ulnar and anterior interosseous arteries and lie on the palmar and dorsal aspects of the carpus; this rich anastomosis is why the wrist tolerates ligation of one of the forearm arteries. 5. Functional (“coupled”) movements of the wrist Wrist extension amplifies finger flexion. The long flexor tendons are at their optimal length for force production when the wrist is extended by about 20 degrees, and their weakest action is at about 40 degrees of wrist flexion. This is why a patient with a painful wrist cannot grip strongly. The tenodesis effect: passive wrist extension in a paralysed hand causes the finger flexors to be pulled tight and the fingers to flex; passive wrist flexion opens the fingers. The hand and wrist work synergistically. Functionally sufficient range: a 45-degree arc of flexion–extension is enough for most everyday use; personal support needs about 10 degrees of flexion and 15 degrees of extension, while eating, drinking and reading need about 5 degrees of flexion and 35 degrees of extension. This explains why extension of the wrist is functionally the more important direction. Compensation: joints proximal to the wrist (the intercarpal and midcarpal joints, and pronation–supination of the forearm) can compensate for a considerable loss of radiocarpal movement. 6. Radiological anatomy Projections: a posteroanterior (PA) and a lateral radiograph; the PA view is taken with the forearm in pronation so that the radius and ulna do not overlap. Joint space: the radiocarpal joint space should be uniform, and the three carpal arcs described by Gilula must be smooth and unbroken — (1) the proximal convexity of the proximal carpal row, (2) the distal concavity of the proximal row, (3) the proximal convexity of the distal row (capitate and hamate). A step in any arc means carpal dislocation or instability. Scapholunate interval: widening between scaphoid and lunate suggests scapholunate ligament tear and dissociation. Ossification: all carpal bones are cartilaginous at birth. The capitate and hamate ossify first, in the first year; the triquetrum, lunate, scaphoid, trapezium and trapezoid follow; the pisiform is the last, shortly before puberty. The carpus is therefore the standard region for bone-age estimation from a left-hand radiograph. Fractures to look for: the scaphoid waist, the distal radius (Colles'/Smith), the triquetrum, and the base of the first metacarpal. 7. Clinical anatomy Fracture of the scaphoid — the commonest carpal injury, across the waist. Its blood supply enters distally in a proportion of people, so the proximal fragment can undergo avascular necrosis ; a clinically suspected fracture with a normal radiograph is treated as a fracture until proven otherwise. Kienböck's disease — avascular necrosis of the lunate with sclerosis on the radiograph, pain and later arthritis. Colles' fracture — distal radius fracture with dorsal displacement; the wrist looks like a dinner fork, and wrist and hand function are both impaired. Carpal tunnel syndrome — compression of the median nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum: numbness of the radial three and a half digits, thenar wasting, and a positive Tinel's sign. Guyon's tunnel — the ulnar nerve and artery pass radial to the pisiform between the palmar aponeurosis and the palmaris brevis; compression here causes ulnar-sided sensory loss and weakness of the intrinsic muscles. Scapholunate dissociation — a fall on the extended wrist tears the scapholunate ligament, the scaphoid rotates (rotary subluxation) and the wrist develops progressive arthritis. Must-memorise Type: synovial ellipsoid, biaxial; surfaces = distal radius + articular disc proximally, scaphoid + lunate + triquetrum distally. The ulna does not articulate with the carpus — the triangular articular disc intervenes. Ligaments: palmar radiocarpal (strongest), palmar ulnocarpal, dorsal radiocarpal (weak), radial and ulnar collateral. Flexion = FCR, FCU, palmaris longus (+ long finger flexors); extension = ECRL, ECRB, ECU; radial deviation = FCR, ECRL, ECRB; ulnar deviation = FCU, ECU. Close-packed: full extension. Ulnar deviation is greater than radial deviation because the radial styloid projects further distally. Radiology: three carpal arcs; scaphoid fracture and lunate avascular necrosis are the classic films. Clinical / radiological correlation A painful wrist with a normal radiograph still needs a scaphoid view and repeat films: the fracture may be invisible for days. Weakness of grip in a patient with wrist pain is mechanical, not neurological: the flexor tendons lose their optimal length when the wrist is flexed. Tinel's sign at the flexor retinaculum and thenar wasting point to the median nerve; ulnar-sided symptoms with clawing point to the ulnar nerve at Guyon's tunnel. After a distal radial fracture always check the distal radio-ulnar joint and forearm rotation; a stiff wrist is often really a stiff radio-ulnar joint. Likely follow-up questions Why is the wrist joint called ellipsoid and not hinge? — it has two axes of movement (flexion–extension and radial–ulnar deviation) and allows circumduction. Why can the hand be ulnar-deviated more than radially deviated? — the radial styloid process extends further distally, so the carpus abuts it sooner on radial deviation. Which carpal bone is most often fractured and why is it dangerous? — the scaphoid, because its blood supply enters distally and the proximal fragment may become avascular. What are the three carpal arcs used for? — to check carpal alignment and detect dislocation or instability on the PA radiograph."},{"id":"t-I-10","p":"I","n":10,"t":"Joints and movements of the hand. Radiological anatomy of the hand.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The hand contains three groups of synovial joints: the carpometacarpal joints — a biaxial saddle joint for the thumb and plane joints for the fingers — the metacarpophalangeal joints , which are condylar (ellipsoid) joints allowing flexion–extension, abduction–adduction and circumduction, and the interphalangeal joints , which are pure hinges. The thumb is set apart: its saddle carpometacarpal joint, its independence from the deep transverse metacarpal ligaments and its 90-degree rotation give it opposition, the movement that makes the human hand a precision tool. 1. Classification of the joints of the hand The joints of the hand are the intercarpal joints, the five carpometacarpal joints ( articulationes carpometacarpales ), the five metacarpophalangeal joints ( articulationes metacarpophalangeae ) and the nine interphalangeal joints ( articulationes interphalangeae ). Their type determines what each joint can do, and therefore which muscles are needed to move it. Joint group Type Surfaces Movements Intercarpal joints (including midcarpal) Synovial plane; share one common cavity Adjacent carpal bones of the two rows Small glides that add to wrist range, especially extension Carpometacarpal (CMC) I Synovial saddle Base of metacarpal I and the trapezium Flexion–extension, abduction–adduction, rotation, circumduction; opposition Carpometacarpal (CMC) II–V Synovial plane Bases of metacarpals II–V and the distal carpal row Gliding only, increasing from II to V; metacarpal V is the most mobile Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) Synovial condylar (ellipsoid) Head of the metacarpal and the base of the proximal phalanx Flexion–extension, abduction–adduction, circumduction, limited rotation Interphalangeal (IP) Synovial hinge Head of the more proximal and base of the more distal phalanx Flexion–extension only 2. Carpometacarpal joints There are five carpometacarpal joints. The saddle joint of the thumb (metacarpal I and trapezium) is the most important: its two saddle-shaped surfaces are set at right angles, so the thumb can be moved in two planes and rotated. Its movements are flexion–extension (in a plane at right angles to that of the fingers, because the thumb is rotated 90 degrees in relation to them), abduction–adduction , and rotation ; opposition is the composite movement that brings the thumb pad to face the pads of the fingers, and it is the key to precision handling and to power grip. The capsule is reinforced by strong ligaments, especially on the palmar side, and the joint is a common site of osteoarthritis. In contrast, the carpometacarpal joints of the fingers are plane synovial joints with only limited gliding; their mobility increases from the second to the fifth, so that metacarpal V slides most, which can be seen on the dorsum when the hand makes a fist. Together with the intermetacarpal joints and the deep transverse metacarpal ligaments, they form a relatively fixed skeletal unit for the palm , against which the thumb and fingers can work. 3. Metacarpophalangeal joints The MCP joints are condylar joints between the head of a metacarpal and the base of a proximal phalanx. Each capsule is reinforced by a palmar ligament (a thick fibrocartilaginous plate fused to the capsule and to the base of the phalanx) and by medial and lateral collateral ligaments . The collateral ligaments are lax in extension and taut in flexion , which is why the fingers can be spread out when straight but cannot be moved sideways when fully flexed. The palmar plates of the MCP joints of the four fingers are linked to each other by the deep transverse metacarpal ligaments , which restrict independent movement of the metacarpal heads and unify the palm. There is no deep transverse metacarpal ligament between the thumb and the index finger — one of the anatomical reasons for the independence of the thumb. 4. Interphalangeal joints and thumb The interphalangeal joints are hinge joints with medial and lateral collateral ligaments and palmar ligaments; they allow only flexion and extension. Each finger has a proximal and a distal interphalangeal joint; the thumb has a single interphalangeal joint and therefore only two phalanges. The close-packed positions are: full flexion at the MCP joints, full extension at the interphalangeal joints, and full opposition at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb. 5. Movements and participating muscles Movement Muscles Nerve supply MCP flexion Flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus; lumbricals and interossei also flex the MCP while extending the IP joints Median n. (C7–T1) for FDS; anterior interosseous (median) and ulnar nn. (C8, T1) for FDP; median and ulnar (C8, T1) for the intrinsics MCP extension Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis, extensor digiti minimi, with the extensor hood Posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) Abduction of fingers Dorsal interossei (three-finger rule: DAB — dorsal abduct); abductor digiti minimi; abductor pollicis brevis for the thumb Deep branch of ulnar n. (C8, T1); recurrent branch of median n. (C8, T1) Adduction of fingers Palmar interossei (PAD — palmar adduct); adductor pollicis for the thumb Deep branch of ulnar n. (C8, T1) PIP flexion Flexor digitorum superficialis Median n. (C7–T1) DIP flexion Flexor digitorum profundus Anterior interosseous (median) for the index and middle; ulnar n. (C8, T1) for the ring and little fingers IP extension Extensor digitorum via the extensor hood, with the lumbricals and interossei Posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8); median and ulnar nn. (C8, T1) for the intrinsics Thumb opposition Opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, with the deep head of flexor pollicis brevis and the adductor pollicis Recurrent branch of median n. (C8, T1); deep branch of ulnar n. (C8, T1) for adductor pollicis and the deep head of FPB Thumb extension (reposition) Extensor pollicis longus and brevis, abductor pollicis longus Posterior interosseous n. (C7, C8) Two functional patterns are worth remembering: the power grip , in which the object is held between the flexed fingers and the palm with the thumb in a vice-like opposition, and precision handling , in which a small object is held between the tips of the thumb, index and middle finger (the “dynamic tripod”), with the ring and little fingers providing static support. The wrist is slightly extended and ulnar-deviated in a power grip. 6. Radiological anatomy of the hand Projections: a posteroanterior view of the whole hand with the fingers extended, an oblique view and a lateral view. The PA view is the standard for bone age, joint spaces and erosions. Joint spaces: the carpometacarpal, metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joint spaces should be uniform and congruent, with no step in the cortex at the joint margins; the joint surfaces of adjacent phalanges should be parallel. Sesamoid bones: two constant sesamoids lie on the palmar aspect of the MCP joint of the thumb (over the head of metacarpal I); they may also occur at the MCP joint of the index or little finger. They are normal and must not be read as fractures. The pisiform is a sesamoid in the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris. Growth: each metacarpal has a distal epiphysis (except the first, whose epiphysis is proximal), and each phalanx has a proximal epiphysis; the plates close in adolescence. The order and symmetry of epiphyses, together with the carpal ossification centres, are the basis of bone-age assessment . Alignment: on the PA view a line drawn along the axis of a metacarpal should continue into its phalanx; dislocation at the MCP joint is recognised by loss of this line and by overlap of the joint surfaces on the lateral view. 7. Clinical anatomy Bennett's fracture — an intra-articular fracture of the base of metacarpal I with subluxation, caused by the pull of the abductor pollicis longus; the CMC joint of the thumb is also the site of osteoarthritis. Boxer's fracture — fracture of the neck of metacarpal V after a punch, with the head angulated palmarly; the knuckle disappears. Gamekeeper's thumb — rupture of the ulnar collateral ligament of the MCP joint of the thumb after forced abduction; pinch grip becomes weak. Mallet finger — avulsion of the extensor tendon from the base of the distal phalanx, so the DIP joint cannot be actively extended. Rheumatoid arthritis — marginal erosions of the MCP and PIP joints with ulnar drift of the fingers; osteoarthritis affects the DIP and PIP joints (Heberden and Bouchard nodes) with joint-space narrowing and osteophytes. Dupuytren's contracture — fibrosis of the palmar aponeurosis, most often affecting the ring and little fingers, pulling them into flexion. Must-memorise CMC I = saddle (opposition); CMC II–V = plane, gliding, most mobile at V; MCP = condylar; IP = hinge. Thumb: 2 phalanges, 1 IP joint, saddle CMC, no deep transverse metacarpal ligament to the index finger — the anatomical basis of independence. Collateral ligaments of the MCP joints are taut in flexion and lax in extension. Close-packed: MCP in full flexion, IP in full extension, CMC I in full opposition. Intrinsic muscles: the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles and the lateral two lumbricals (mnemonic LOAF ); the ulnar nerve supplies everything else in the hand, including all the interossei ( mm. interossei ) and the medial two lumbricals ( mm. lumbricales ). Radiology: PA, oblique and lateral views; two sesamoids at MCP I; distal metacarpal epiphyses except in metacarpal I. Clinical / radiological correlation In a lateral radiograph of the hand the metacarpals overlap, so a dislocated MCP joint is best confirmed on the PA and oblique views together. Do not mistake the sesamoids of the thumb or the epiphyses of children for fractures; look for a sharp, non-sclerotic line breaking the cortex instead. Loss of opposition is the most disabling deficit of the thumb: the patient cannot pinch a key or hold a pen, even when flexion and extension are intact. The flexor tendon sheaths and their annular pulleys (A1–A5) keep the tendons close to the bone; infection or a divided pulley lets the tendon “bowstring” away from the bone and abolishes full flexion. Likely follow-up questions What is the difference between the thumb and the fingers in terms of joints? — saddle CMC with three planes of movement versus plane CMC; two phalanges versus three; independent ligaments. What are the movements of the MCP joint and which muscle groups produce them? — flexion (long flexors and intrinsics), extension (long extensors), abduction (dorsal interossei), adduction (palmar interossei). Which nerve would be damaged in a deep laceration of the palm and what would be lost? — the deep branch of the ulnar nerve: all interossei, the medial two lumbricals, adductor pollicis and the hypothenar muscles. Why is opposition so important? — it rotates metacarpal I so that the thumb pad faces the finger pads, which is the basis of precision grip."},{"id":"t-I-11","p":"I","n":11,"t":"The anatomy, innervation and function of the spinohumeral and thoracohumeral muscles.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The spinohumeral and thoracohumeral muscles are the muscles that connect the axial skeleton to the shoulder region . The spinohumeral group arises from the vertebral column — trapezius, levator scapulae, rhomboid major, rhomboid minor, serratus anterior and latissimus dorsi; the thoracohumeral group arises from the thoracic wall — pectoralis major, pectoralis minor and subclavius. The trapezius is supplied by the accessory nerve (XI) with C3 and C4; the levator scapulae by direct C3 and C4 branches together with the dorsal scapular nerve (C4–C5, chiefly C5); the remaining muscles by branches of the brachial plexus (dorsal scapular, long thoracic, thoracodorsal and pectoral nerves). They position the scapula on the thorax and power the shoulder joint, and they are the muscles examined clinically in winging of the scapula and in shoulder droop. 1. Definition and place in the classification of the upper limb muscles The muscles of the upper limb are conveniently classified by their proximal attachment : muscles arising from the trunk and inserting on the shoulder region, muscles of the shoulder girdle itself (deltoid, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and teres major), muscles of the arm, forearm and hand. Spinohumeral muscles — arise from the vertebral column and attach to the shoulder region: trapezius, levator scapulae, rhomboid minor, rhomboid major, serratus anterior, latissimus dorsi . Note that the group name follows the old teaching that these muscles run from the spine to the humerus; in fact only latissimus dorsi reaches the humerus, the others insert on the scapula (and clavicle) and act on the shoulder girdle. Thoracohumeral muscles — arise from the thoracic cage and attach to the shoulder region: pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, subclavius . Again, only pectoralis major reaches the humerus, pectoralis minor inserting on the coracoid process and subclavius on the clavicle. Functionally all nine are positioners of the scapula and prime movers of the arm : they cross the sternoclavicular, acromioclavicular, scapulothoracic and glenohumeral joints, so their contraction can move the scapula, the clavicle or the humerus, depending on which segment is fixed. 2. The six spinohumeral muscles Muscle Origin Insertion Function Nerve Trapezius (m. trapezius) Superior nuchal line and external occipital protuberance, ligamentum nuchae, spinous processes of C7–T12 and the supraspinous ligaments Superior edge of the crest of the spine of the scapula, acromion, posterior border of the lateral third of the clavicle Elevates the scapula; upper and lower fibres rotate it upwards; middle fibres retract it; lower fibres depress it Accessory n. [XI] (motor); C3, C4 (proprioception) Levator scapulae (m. levator scapulae) Transverse processes of C1 and C2, posterior tubercles of the transverse processes of C3 and C4 Posterior surface of the medial border of the scapula, from the superior angle to the root of the spine Elevates the scapula and draws it medially (with the rhomboids, rotates it downwards) Direct branches of C3, C4 + branches of the dorsal scapular n. (C5) Rhomboid minor (m. rhomboideus minor) Lower end of the ligamentum nuchae, spinous processes of C7 and T1 Posterior surface of the medial border of the scapula at the root of the spine Elevates and retracts the scapula; assists downward rotation Dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5) Rhomboid major (m. rhomboideus major) Spinous processes of T2–T5 and the intervening supraspinous ligaments Posterior surface of the medial border of the scapula, from the root of the spine to the inferior angle Elevates and retracts the scapula; presses it against the thoracic wall Dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5) Serratus anterior (m. serratus anterior) Lateral surfaces of the upper eight to nine ribs and the deep fascia of the corresponding intercostal spaces Costal surface of the medial border of the scapula Protracts the scapula and rotates it upwards (lower slips); keeps the scapula applied to the thoracic wall Long thoracic n. (C5, C6, C7) Latissimus dorsi (m. latissimus dorsi) Spinous processes of the lower six thoracic vertebrae, thoracolumbar fascia (lumbar spinous processes and iliac crest), lower three or four ribs Floor of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus Adducts, medially rotates and extends the arm; also depresses the raised arm Thoracodorsal n. (C6, C7, C8) 3. The three thoracohumeral muscles Muscle Origin Insertion Function Nerve Pectoralis major (m. pectoralis major) Clavicular head — anterior surface of the medial half of the clavicle; sternocostal head — sternum, first seven costal cartilages, aponeurosis of the external oblique Lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus Adducts, medially rotates and flexes the arm; the clavicular head flexes an extended arm, the sternocostal head extends a flexed arm Lateral and medial pectoral nn. (clavicular C5, C6; sternocostal C6–T1) Pectoralis minor (m. pectoralis minor) Anterior surfaces and superior borders of ribs III–V and the overlying deep fascia Medial border and upper surface of the coracoid process of the scapula Protracts the scapula and pulls the tip of the shoulder down (depresses the lateral angle) Medial pectoral n. (C8, T1) Subclavius (m. subclavius) First rib at the junction of rib and costal cartilage Groove on the inferior surface of the middle third of the clavicle Depresses the shoulder and pulls the clavicle medially, stabilising the sternoclavicular joint Nerve to subclavius (C5, C6) 4. Innervation — the summary that the examiner wants All of these muscles are supplied by branches that leave the brachial plexus proximal to the cords of the free limb , except the pectoral nerves, which leave the cords but do not reach the free upper limb. The pattern is: Supraclavicular branches : dorsal scapular n. (C4, C5) → rhomboids, levator scapulae; long thoracic n. (C5–C7) → serratus anterior; nerve to subclavius (C5, C6) → subclavius; suprascapular n. (C5, C6) → supra- and infraspinatus. Infraclavicular branches : thoracodorsal n. (C6–C8) → latissimus dorsi; lateral pectoral n. (C5–C7) and medial pectoral n. (C8, T1) → pectoralis major and minor. ⇒ Only the trapezius is not supplied by the brachial plexus: it receives the accessory nerve [XI] plus proprioceptive fibres from C3 and C4. 5. Function and biomechanics The scapula is the mobile base of the arm. Spinohumeral and thoracohumeral muscles fix the scapula on the thorax so that the deltoid and the rotator cuff have a stable origin; when they are paralysed, abduction above the horizontal becomes impossible. Force couples of the scapula. Upward rotation is produced by the upper trapezius and the lower serratus anterior working as a couple; downward rotation by the rhomboids and levator scapulae with gravity. The stabilising couple of the scapula on the thorax is the trapezius (medial pull) against the serratus anterior (lateral pull). Both ends can move. When the arm is fixed (as in climbing or pushing up from a chair), the same muscles move the trunk: latissimus dorsi and pectoralis major pull the body towards the fixed hand. Arm power and posture. Pectoralis major and latissimus dorsi are the great adductors and medial rotators of the arm; trapezius and the rhomboids set the height and retraction of the shoulder, and hence the posture of the whole upper limb. Breathing accessory: the lower slips of serratus anterior and the costal attachments of pectoralis minor can elevate the ribs when the scapula is fixed. 6. Clinical anatomy Winging of the scapula — paralysis of serratus anterior after injury to the long thoracic nerve (radical neck dissection, axillary surgery, stab wound, or repetitive strain). The medial border and inferior angle lift off the chest wall, most obvious when the patient pushes forwards against a wall with straight arms. Trapezius palsy — accessory nerve injury (neck surgery, trauma) produces a drooping shoulder, inability to shrug against resistance and weakness of abduction above 90 degrees; the scapula slides laterally and downwards. Dorsal scapular nerve palsy — weakness of the rhomboids and slight lateral displacement of the scapula, with aching between the shoulder blades. Thoracodorsal nerve palsy — latissimus dorsi weakness after axillary dissection; the patient cannot pull the raised arm down against resistance (tested with the arm abducted and the elbow extended). Pectoralis major rupture — usually at its humeral insertion during heavy bench pressing: the anterior axillary fold is lost and adduction is weak. Surgical use: the latissimus dorsi myocutaneous flap and the pectoralis major flap are standard reconstructive options after breast and chest-wall surgery, which is why their nerve and blood supply must be known exactly. Must-memorise Spinohumeral (6): trapezius, levator scapulae, rhomboid minor, rhomboid major, serratus anterior, latissimus dorsi. Thoracohumeral (3): pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, subclavius. Nerves: accessory [XI] + C3, C4 for trapezius; dorsal scapular (C4, C5) for rhomboids; C3, C4 + dorsal scapular (C5) for levator scapulae; long thoracic (C5–C7) for serratus anterior; thoracodorsal (C6–C8) for latissimus dorsi; lateral and medial pectoral for pectoralis major; medial pectoral (C8, T1) for pectoralis minor; nerve to subclavius (C5, C6). Only latissimus dorsi and pectoralis major of these nine reach the humerus; the rest insert on the scapula or clavicle. Force couple of upward rotation: upper trapezius + lower serratus anterior. Two exam lesions: scapular winging (long thoracic) and shoulder droop (accessory nerve). Clinical / radiological correlation Test serratus anterior by asking the patient to push against a wall with straight arms: the scapula should stay flat on the chest. Test trapezius by asking the patient to shrug against resistance and to hold the arm abducted above the horizontal. On MRI or CT, atrophy of the serratus anterior (loss of the normal serrated appearance on the lateral thoracic wall) supports a long thoracic nerve lesion; fatty replacement of the rhomboids suggests chronic dorsal scapular palsy. After axillary lymph node dissection, both the long thoracic nerve and the thoracodorsal nerve are at risk — always document shoulder function pre- and post-operatively. Likely follow-up questions What exactly do the terms spinohumeral and thoracohumeral mean? — muscles from the vertebral column and from the thoracic wall respectively, acting on the shoulder region; the names describe the proximal attachment, not the insertion. Why is the trapezius not innervated by the brachial plexus? — it is a branchial (pharyngeal arch) muscle, supplied by the accessory nerve, with only proprioceptive fibres from C3 and C4. Which muscles retract the scapula and which protract it? — retraction: trapezius (middle) and rhomboids; protraction: serratus anterior and pectoralis minor. What are the consequences of a long thoracic nerve injury? — winging of the scapula, weakness of protraction and loss of abduction above the horizontal. How does the scapula rotate upwards and why is it necessary? — trapezius and serratus anterior as a force couple; the rotation turns the glenoid upwards and moves the acromion away from the greater tubercle."},{"id":"t-I-12","p":"I","n":12,"t":"Classification (types) and innervation of medium and large blood vessels. Functional and structural features of the arterioles and venules. Types of vascular anastomoses. Structure, function and types of capillaries.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer Every blood vessel has three tunics — tunica intima (endothelium, subendothelial layer, internal elastic lamina), tunica media (smooth muscle and elastic fibres) and tunica adventitia (fibroelastic coat carrying the vasa vasorum and nervi vasorum). Arteries are classified as elastic (conducting) , muscular (distributing) and arterioles (resistance) ; veins as venules , medium and small veins and large veins (capacitance) . The arteriole is the resistance vessel that maintains mean arterial pressure and distributes cardiac output, the postcapillary venule is the main exchange and leukocyte-emigration segment, and capillaries are continuous , fenestrated or sinusoidal . Vessels are joined by arterial, venous, collateral and arteriovenous anastomoses. 1. Common plan: the three tunics Tunica intima ( tunica intima ) — a simple squamous endothelium on a basal lamina, a subendothelial layer of loose connective tissue, and in arteries the internal elastic lamina ( lamina elastica interna ), a fenestrated elastic sheet marking the intima/media boundary. Endothelial cells contain Weibel–Palade bodies , the storage site of von Willebrand factor; their luminal membrane carries angiotensin-converting enzyme and lipoprotein lipase, and they secrete nitric oxide, prostacyclin and endothelin. Tunica media — the thickest coat of an artery: helically arranged smooth muscle cells with elastic fibres, type I and III collagen and ground substance. In muscular arteries its outer border is marked by the external elastic lamina . The smooth muscle is the effector of vasoconstriction and vasodilatation. Tunica adventitia — fibroelastic connective tissue anchoring the vessel to its surroundings; it carries the vasa vasorum (small arteries supplying the outer media and adventitia, more prominent in veins) and the nervi vasorum (vasomotor nerves). In large vessels the wall is too thick for nutrition from the lumen, hence the vasa vasorum. Capillaries have no media at all; their contractile elements are pericytes , which share the capillary basal lamina. 2. Classification of arteries and of veins Arteries are classified by calibre and by the relative amount of elastic tissue and smooth muscle. Three structural types are described — elastic, muscular and arteriolar — and the smallest arterial segment, the metarteriole , has a discontinuous muscle coat that forms the precapillary sphincter . Vessel Tunica intima Tunica media Tunica adventitia / example Elastic artery (conducting) Endothelium, basal lamina, subendothelial layer, incomplete internal elastic lamina 40–70 fenestrated elastic membranes with smooth muscle cells between them; thin external elastic lamina; vasa vasorum in the outer half Thin fibroelastic coat; aorta and pulmonary trunk Muscular artery (distributing) Endothelium, basal lamina, subendothelial layer, thick internal elastic lamina Up to 40 layers of smooth muscle cells; thick external elastic lamina Thin fibroelastic coat, vasa vasorum inconspicuous; carotid, femoral, subclavian, axillary, brachial arteries Arteriole Endothelium and basal lamina; subendothelial layer not prominent; scattered elastic fibres instead of a defined internal elastic lamina 1–3 layers of smooth muscle cells (fewer in the smallest arterioles) Loose connective tissue and nerve fibres; terminal branch of every artery Metarteriole Endothelium, basal lamina Smooth muscle cells forming the precapillary sphincter Sparse loose connective tissue; arterial end of a capillary bed In functional terms the same vessels are grouped by the task they perform in the circuit: Functional class Vessels Function Elastic / pressure chamber Aorta, pulmonary trunk, proximal large arteries Windkessel effect: the wall stretches in systole and recoils in diastole, converting intermittent ejection into continuous flow Distributing Large muscular arteries (subclavian, axillary, brachial) Direct blood to a region; calibre adjusted by smooth muscle Resistance Small muscular arteries and arterioles Produce most of the peripheral resistance, maintain mean arterial pressure, control organ perfusion Exchange Capillaries and venules Transendothelial transport of gases, water and solutes Capacitance / reservoir Venules, small and large veins Hold most of the blood volume; their tone sets venous return and the filled state of the circulation Veins possess the same three tunics but a thin, poorly demarcated media, a relatively thicker adventitia, a larger and flattened lumen, and valves in many medium-sized veins. They are grouped into venules , medium and small veins and large veins (longitudinal smooth-muscle bundles in the adventitia, cardiac muscle near the heart) and contain about 70 % of the blood volume. 3. Innervation of medium and large blood vessels The wall contains both effector and sensory nerve endings; the vasomotor nerves enter with the adventitia as the nervi vasorum . Sympathetic noradrenergic vasoconstrictor fibres are the principal efferent supply. Postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline , which diffuses to the smooth muscle of the media; contraction spreads from cell to cell through gap junctions . Reduced noradrenaline release, or antagonism of its receptors, produces vasodilatation. Parasympathetic vasodilator fibres releasing vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) supply salivary glands and erectile tissue. Sensory endings: baroreceptors of the carotid sinus (glossopharyngeal nerve, IX) and of the aortic arch (vagus nerve, X) monitor pressure; peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) monitor blood gas composition. Peptidergic, CGRP-containing afferents serve nociception. Humoral and local control: the smooth muscle is relaxed by endothelial mediators (nitric oxide, prostacyclin) and by mast-cell products, and constricted by endothelin and angiotensin II; circulating vasopressin and the renin–angiotensin system act on the same cells. 4. Arteriole and venule — structure and function The arteriole ( arteriola ) is the last arterial segment before the capillary bed, less than 0.3 mm in diameter, built of endothelium, basal lamina and only one to three layers of smooth muscle. Because resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius, a small change in arteriolar calibre produces a large change in resistance: the arterioles are the resistance vessels . They hold the main part of the total peripheral resistance, maintain mean arterial pressure and distribute cardiac output between organs. The last arteriole continues as a metarteriole whose muscle forms the precapillary sphincter — the switch that admits blood to the capillary bed or diverts it along the thoroughfare channel. The postcapillary venule is the venous counterpart of the capillary: endothelium, basal lamina and a few pericytes , with no true media; collecting and muscular venules acquire a thin smooth-muscle layer. Venules are low-pressure, highly distensible capacitance vessels — most of the blood volume lies on the venous side — and the postcapillary venule is the most permeable segment of the microcirculation, hence the main site of fluid exchange and of leukocyte emigration (diapedesis) in inflammation. In lymphoid organs postcapillary venules become high endothelial venules , through which lymphocytes re-enter the circulation. Feature Arteriole Postcapillary venule Wall Endothelium, basal lamina, 1–3 layers of smooth muscle Endothelium, basal lamina, pericytes; no media Calibre Less than 0.3 mm Slightly larger than a capillary, widening into collecting venules Pressure and flow High, pulsatile; flow set by smooth-muscle tone Low, non-pulsatile; flow set by downstream pressure Main function Resistance vessel: peripheral resistance, arterial pressure, organ perfusion Capacitance and exchange vessel: fluid exchange, diapedesis, lymphocyte recirculation 5. Capillaries — structure, function and types Capillaries are the smallest and thinnest-walled vessels (a few micrometres wide; sinusoids wider). They are a tube of simple squamous endothelium with a basal lamina; the endothelial cell is extremely attenuated (less than 0.2 μm thick), its nucleus bulges into the lumen, and neighbouring cells overlap as marginal folds sealed by tight junctions. The cytoplasm is rich in pinocytotic vesicles — the basis of transcytosis — and pericytes share the basal lamina, regulate flow and take part in angiogenesis and repair. Capillaries lie between arterioles and venules and form a capillary bed . Exchange takes place across the endothelial cell, through junctional pores of about 9–11 nm and through fenestrae and vesicles forming large pores of about 50–70 nm. Type Ultrastructure Organs Function Continuous No fenestrae; numerous pinocytotic vesicles; junctions sealed by tight junctions (fasciae occludentes) Connective tissue and skin, skeletal and cardiac muscle, lung, nerve tissue; brain capillaries are a modified continuous type Barrier; carrier-mediated transport of amino acids, glucose and nucleosides; blood–brain barrier in the CNS Fenestrated Fenestrae, pores of 60–80 nm, closed by a diaphragm Kidney, endocrine glands, pancreas, intestinal mucosa Rapid exchange of fluid and small solutes — filtration, secretion, absorption Sinusoidal (discontinuous) Large fenestrae without diaphragms; discontinuous endothelium and basal lamina; wide irregular channels Liver, red bone marrow, spleen, lymph nodes, some endocrine glands Free exchange of macromolecules and even cells between blood and tissue Flow through the bed is controlled at two levels: the central channel (metarteriole plus thoroughfare channel) with its precapillary sphincters, and arteriovenous anastomoses that bypass the bed entirely. 6. Types of vascular anastomoses Type Arrangement Examples Arterial — arch (end-to-end) Two arteries of equal order meet in an arch, so either can supply the whole territory Superficial and deep palmar arches; arterial arcades of the mesentery (Riolan's arch) Arterial — convergence Two arteries join to form a single trunk Right and left vertebral arteries unite to form the basilar artery Collateral (circum-articular) network Descending and ascending branches meet around a joint, so flow can reverse when the main trunk is occluded Shoulder (scapular arcade), elbow, wrist, knee Venous Venous plexuses and perforating veins connect the superficial and deep systems, so drainage never depends on one channel Perforating veins of the limbs; pampiniform plexus Arteriovenous (AVA) A direct vessel from a small artery to a small vein bypasses the capillary bed; its intermediate segment has a thickened media of modified smooth muscle and a dense adrenergic and cholinergic nerve supply Acral skin of fingers, toes, lips, nose and ears; thermoregulation Where such channels are absent the supply is functionally end-arterial (retina, and the segmental arteries of kidney and spleen), so occlusion there produces infarction rather than reversible ischaemia. Must-memorise Three tunics: intima (endothelium, internal elastic lamina), media (smooth muscle and elastic), adventitia (vasa vasorum and nervi vasorum). Arterial ladder: elastic aorta with 40–70 elastic membranes → muscular artery with up to 40 smooth-muscle layers → arteriole with 1–3 layers and a calibre under 0.3 mm. Capillary types and organs: continuous (muscle, lung, nerve, brain), fenestrated (kidney, endocrine glands, gut), sinusoidal (liver, marrow, spleen, lymph node). Arteriole = resistance; venule = capacitance, exchange and diapedesis; veins hold about 70 % of the blood volume. Four anastomotic types: arterial (arch, convergence), collateral, venous (perforating), arteriovenous (thermoregulation). Clinical / radiological correlation Raynaud phenomenon — abnormal spasm of digital arteries and arteriovenous anastomoses gives the three-phase colour change: white (ischaemia), blue (cyanosis), red (reactive hyperaemia) on exposure to cold. Oedema — histamine and bradykinin raise capillary permeability, so fluid leaves the vessels and the extracellular volume increases. Atherosclerosis — lipid plaques in the intima of elastic and large muscular arteries; arteriosclerosis is hyaline thickening of the media of small arteries and arterioles, with rigidity and calcification. Aneurysm — ballooning from weakness of the media, most often in the abdominal aorta; varicose veins follow valve incompetence and loss of venous wall tone. Blood–brain barrier — the sealed continuous capillaries of the CNS: only lipid-soluble and carrier-transported substances cross freely. Likely follow-up questions Why is the aorta elastic and a distributing artery muscular? — The aorta must smooth the pulse (Windkessel effect); a distributing artery must change its calibre. Where is the chief site of peripheral resistance, and why? — Arterioles: their small radius and thick muscular wall allow large resistance changes for small changes in diameter. How is a tissue supplied after ligation of its main artery? — Through pre-existing anastomoses: arch anastomoses and collateral networks around joints. What distinguishes a metarteriole from an arteriole? — A discontinuous muscle coat forming the precapillary sphincter, continuing into the thoroughfare channel."},{"id":"t-I-13","p":"I","n":13,"t":"The systemic circulation: the large branches of the aorta and the great veins.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The systemic circulation carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle through the aorta and its branches to all tissues and returns it through the superior and inferior venae cavae to the right atrium. It is arranged in series with the pulmonary circulation but in parallel between individual organs, and it is linked to the tissues by the microcirculation. The aorta is described in four parts — ascending aorta, arch, descending thoracic aorta and abdominal aorta — and the great veins are the brachiocephalic veins, the superior vena cava, the azygos system and the inferior vena cava. The hepatic portal vein is the one important exception, passing through the liver before it reaches the caval system. 1. The circuit and its functional design Systemic circuit : left ventricle → ascending aorta → arch → descending aorta → muscular arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins → superior and inferior venae cavae → right atrium. Series arrangement of the systemic and pulmonary circuits: the whole cardiac output of the left ventricle passes through the systemic circuit, then through the right heart and the lungs. Parallel arrangement of the organs within the systemic circulation: each organ receives blood of identical composition, and the arterioles of each organ set its share of the output. Microcirculation — arteries and veins are connected only through the capillary beds, where exchange occurs. Hepatic portal circulation — the venous blood of the abdominal gut, spleen, pancreas and gallbladder passes first through the liver; only the hepatic veins open into the inferior vena cava. Plasma filtration and reabsorption across the capillary wall are not exactly balanced; the surplus is returned as lymph through the thoracic duct and the right lymphatic duct to the great veins of the neck. 2. The aorta — parts, course and levels Part Course and relations Beginning and end Ascending aorta Arises from the aortic orifice of the left ventricle, passes superiorly, anteriorly and to the right within the pericardial sac, and leaves the sac to become the arch. Begins at the aortic orifice; becomes the arch behind the right half of the manubrium of the sternum. Arch of the aorta Courses upwards, backwards and to the left over the left main bronchus; initially anterior and finally lateral to the trachea. Its three branches are all crossed anteriorly by the left brachiocephalic vein. From the end of the ascending aorta to the left side at about the level of vertebra TIV, where the thoracic aorta begins. Descending thoracic aorta Lies to the left of the vertebral column above and approaches the midline below, anterior to the thoracic vertebral bodies, in the posterior mediastinum. From the lower edge of vertebra TIV to the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at the lower edge of vertebra TXII. Abdominal aorta Midline structure on the anterior surface of vertebrae LI–LIV, covered anteriorly by the prevertebral nerve plexus. From the aortic hiatus at TXII to the level of vertebra LIV, where it divides into the two common iliac arteries. 3. Large branches of the aorta Segment Branches Territory Ascending aorta Right and left coronary arteries from the aortic sinuses — the only branches of this segment Heart Arch Brachiocephalic trunk (dividing at the right sternoclavicular joint into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries); left common carotid artery; left subclavian artery Head and neck on the right, right upper limb, head and neck on the left, left upper limb Descending thoracic aorta Pericardial, bronchial, oesophageal, mediastinal branches; posterior intercostal arteries (usually nine pairs); superior phrenic arteries; subcostal artery Pericardium, lungs, oesophagus, posterior mediastinum, thoracic wall, diaphragm, abdominal wall Abdominal aorta — anterior unpaired Coeliac trunk; superior mesenteric artery; inferior mesenteric artery Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivatives Abdominal aorta — lateral paired Middle suprarenal arteries; renal arteries; testicular or ovarian arteries Suprarenal glands, kidneys, gonads Abdominal aorta — posterior Inferior phrenic arteries; four pairs of lumbar arteries; median sacral artery Diaphragm, posterior abdominal wall, sacrum and coccyx Terminal Right and left common iliac arteries, each dividing into an external iliac artery (to the lower limb) and an internal iliac artery (to the pelvis) Pelvis and lower limb The external iliac artery continues as the femoral artery and is the caudal partner of the subclavian artery in the collateral circulation of the body wall (topic I.14). The first two posterior intercostal spaces are supplied not by the thoracic aorta but by the supreme intercostal artery of the costocervical trunk. 4. The great veins Vein Formation Main tributaries Termination Brachiocephalic veins (right and left) Form at the junction of the internal jugular and subclavian veins behind the medial end of the clavicle Vertebral, first posterior intercostal, internal thoracic, inferior thyroid and thymic veins; on the left also the left superior intercostal vein Unite behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage to form the superior vena cava Superior vena cava Union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins Azygos vein (immediately before entering the pericardium), pericardial and mediastinal veins Right atrium at the lower edge of the right third costal cartilage; its lower half is inside the pericardial sac Azygos system Azygos vein formed by the union of the right ascending lumbar and right subcostal veins; enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus Right superior intercostal vein, fifth to eleventh right posterior intercostal veins, hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins, oesophageal, mediastinal, pericardial and right bronchial veins Azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung at about TIV and joins the superior vena cava Inferior vena cava Union of the two common iliac veins at the level of vertebra LV, just to the right of the midline Common iliac, lumbar, right testicular or ovarian, renal, right suprarenal, inferior phrenic and hepatic veins Right atrium after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at about vertebra TVIII Pulmonary veins Two right and two left veins from the lungs Intrapulmonary veins Left atrium (pulmonary circuit, not systemic) The inferior vena cava receives no tributaries from the abdominal gastrointestinal tract, spleen, pancreas or gallbladder , because these drain into the portal vein. Of the lumbar veins, the fifth usually drains into the iliolumbar vein, the first and second may drain into the ascending lumbar veins, and the third and fourth usually enter the inferior vena cava directly. 5. The azygos system as a caval collateral The azygos system is the longitudinal venous pathway of the body wall on each side of the vertebral column. On the left the hemiazygos vein collects the lowest four or five left posterior intercostal veins and crosses to the azygos vein at about TIX; the accessory hemiazygos vein collects the middle left posterior intercostal veins and crosses at about TVIII. Below, the system is continuous through the ascending lumbar veins with the iliolumbar and common iliac veins, and above with the superior vena cava. It is therefore an important anastomotic pathway: if the inferior vena cava is blocked, blood from the lower body can still reach the heart through the ascending lumbar → azygos → superior vena cava route. 6. Clinical and radiological correlations Coarctation of the aorta — congenital narrowing just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery; the abdomen and lower limbs are underperfused while the upper body is hypertensive. Collateral flow enlarges the intercostal arteries, which erode the inferior margins of the ribs and produce rib notching on the chest radiograph; cardiac failure may follow. Aortic dissection — a longitudinal split between intima and media creates a false channel; in the ascending aorta or arch it may occlude the coronary or cerebral arteries and cause myocardial infarction or stroke. Traumatic aortic injury — deceleration tears the aorta at its three fixed points: the aortic valve, the ligamentum arteriosum, and the point where it passes behind the median arcuate ligament of the diaphragm. Abdominal aortic aneurysm — dilation, usually infrarenal, treated by open or endovascular grafting because of the risk of rupture. Superior vena cava obstruction — facial and upper limb oedema with dilated collateral veins of the chest wall; the azygos system takes over the drainage. Venous access — central lines are passed through the subclavian, internal jugular or axillary vein into the distal superior vena cava; the straight superior vena cava → right atrium → inferior vena cava axis is used for transjugular liver biopsy, TIPS and inferior vena cava filters. Must-memorise Aorta in four parts: ascending (coronary arteries only), arch (brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian), descending thoracic (TIV to TXII), abdominal (TXII to LIV, then two common iliac arteries). Levels: aortic hiatus TXII; oesophagus TX; inferior vena cava through the central tendon at about TVIII. Superior vena cava = union of the brachiocephalic veins; formed behind the right first costal cartilage, ends at the right third costal cartilage; receives the azygos vein. Inferior vena cava = union of the common iliac veins at LV; tributaries: lumbar, right gonadal, renal, right suprarenal, inferior phrenic and hepatic veins. No caval tributaries from the gut, spleen, pancreas or gallbladder — these belong to the portal system. Clinical / radiological correlation Rib notching in coarctation is the radiological trace of dilated posterior intercostal arteries acting as collaterals. Radio-femoral delay — the femoral pulse is delayed and weaker than the radial pulse distal to a coarctation. Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism — thrombus from the lower limb veins reaches the right heart and the pulmonary arteries; an inferior vena cava filter traps large emboli. Right-sided aortic arch — the arch passes to the right of the trachea; it may be asymptomatic or associated with an aberrant left subclavian artery forming a vascular ring around the trachea and oesophagus. Likely follow-up questions Which are the only branches of the ascending aorta? — The right and left coronary arteries. Where does the thoracic aorta begin and end? — At the lower edge of TIV and at the aortic hiatus, at the lower edge of TXII. How is the superior vena cava formed and what is its main tributary? — By union of the two brachiocephalic veins; the azygos vein joins it just before it enters the pericardium. Why does the inferior vena cava not receive the splenic or mesenteric veins? — They belong to the hepatic portal system, which passes through the liver first. What is the collateral route when the inferior vena cava is blocked? — Common iliac → ascending lumbar → azygos → superior vena cava."},{"id":"t-I-14","p":"I","n":14,"t":"Branching system of the subclavian artery. Anastomoses between subclavian and external iliac arteries.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The right subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk behind the sternoclavicular joint, the left directly from the arch of the aorta; both arch over the cervical pleura and the apex of the lung, pass posterior to the anterior scalene muscle and end at the lateral border of rib I, where they become the axillary artery. The anterior scalene divides the artery into three parts. Its branches are the vertebral artery , the thyrocervical trunk (inferior thyroid, transverse cervical, suprascapular arteries), the internal thoracic artery and the costocervical trunk (deep cervical and supreme intercostal arteries), with a dorsal scapular artery that may arise from the third part. Through the internal thoracic artery, the superior epigastric artery and the inferior epigastric artery the subclavian artery anastomoses with the external iliac artery, which is the main collateral route of the anterior abdominal wall. 1. Origin, course and termination Right subclavian artery — begins posterior to the sternoclavicular joint as one of the two terminal branches of the brachiocephalic trunk; arches superiorly and laterally, anterior to the cervical pleura and posterior to the anterior scalene muscle. Left subclavian artery — begins lower in the thorax as a direct branch of the arch of the aorta, posterior to the left common carotid artery and lateral to the trachea; ascends and arches laterally in the same relation to the pleura and the anterior scalene muscle. Termination — both arteries groove the superior surface of rib I and end at its lateral border, where the axillary artery begins. The artery, the brachial plexus and the subclavian vein all pass between the neck and the upper limb over rib I, through the axillary inlet . Relations — the subclavian vein lies anterior to the artery and anterior to the anterior scalene muscle; the artery runs in the scalene triangle (interscalene space) between the anterior and middle scalene muscles. The phrenic nerve crosses anterior to the anterior scalene; on the left the thoracic duct and the vagus nerve are related medially; the sympathetic trunk lies posteriorly. 2. Three parts and their branches The anterior scalene muscle divides the artery into a first part (origin to the muscle), a second part (behind the muscle) and a third part (from the muscle to the lateral border of rib I). All the named branches arise from the first part except the costocervical trunk, which arises from the second part on the right side. Part Branch Course and territory First part Vertebral artery ( a. vertebralis ) First and largest branch, medial to the anterior scalene; enters the transverse foramen of vertebra CVI and ascends through the foramina of CV to CI; turns medially over the posterior arch of CI, enters the posterior cranial fossa through the foramen magnum and unites with the opposite vertebral artery to form the basilar artery. Gives spinal branches, meningeal branches and cerebellar branches. Thyrocervical trunk ( truncus thyrocervicalis ) Divides into the inferior thyroid artery (with the ascending cervical artery) for the thyroid gland and prevertebral muscles, the transverse cervical artery (superficial branch to trapezius; deep branch, the dorsal scapular artery, along the medial border of the scapula) and the suprascapular artery for the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles. Internal thoracic artery ( a. thoracica interna ) Descends posterior to the clavicle and anterior to the cervical pleura, posterior to the costal cartilages of the upper six ribs about 1 cm lateral to the sternum and anterior to the transversus thoracis; gives anterior intercostal arteries and perforating branches; at the level of the sixth intercostal space divides into the superior epigastric artery and the musculophrenic artery . Second part Costocervical trunk ( truncus costocervicalis ) From the first part on the left but from the second part on the right; passes posterolaterally over the dome of the pleura and divides into the deep cervical artery (to the back of the neck, anastomosing with the descending branch of the occipital artery) and the supreme intercostal artery (to the first two posterior intercostal spaces). Third part Dorsal scapular artery (variable) May arise from the third part; passes posterolaterally to the superior angle of the scapula and descends along the medial border, deep to the rhomboid muscles. The branches of the thyrocervical trunk and the suprascapular artery form the scapular arcade together with the circumflex scapular and subscapular arteries of the axillary artery — the collateral network of the shoulder. 3. Anastomoses between the subclavian and external iliac arteries The subclavian artery and the external iliac artery are the cranial and caudal arterial trunks of the body wall; they meet through a continuous longitudinal chain that is the anatomical basis of the collateral supply of the trunk (and of lower-limb perfusion in coarctation of the aorta). Route Chain of vessels Where the anastomosis lies Deep abdominal route (main) Subclavian artery → internal thoracic artery → superior epigastric artery → inferior epigastric artery → external iliac artery Inside the rectus sheath, where the superior and inferior epigastric arteries anastomose with each other behind the rectus abdominis muscle Superficial abdominal route Subclavian artery → internal thoracic artery → musculophrenic and anterior intercostal branches → superficial epigastric and superficial circumflex iliac arteries → femoral artery → external iliac artery In the superficial fascia of the anterolateral abdominal wall Segmental lateral route Subclavian → costocervical trunk → supreme intercostal artery → posterior intercostal arteries → thoracic aorta → lumbar arteries; the tenth and eleventh intercostal and subcostal arteries anastomose with the deep circumflex iliac artery of the external iliac artery In the muscles and fascia of the lateral abdominal wall, segment by segment Key chain : a. subclavia → a. thoracica interna → a. epigastrica superior → (anastomosis in the rectus sheath) → a. epigastrica inferior → a. iliaca externa → a. femoralis. Both arteries send branches into the thoracic and abdominal wall — the subclavian through the anterior intercostal arteries of the internal thoracic artery, the external iliac through the deep circumflex iliac artery — so the two territories overlap segment by segment. The internal thoracic–epigastric chain is the shortest and the largest-calibre of the routes, which is why it is the first to dilate when the aortic lumen is narrowed. 4. Clinical and radiological correlations Coarctation of the aorta — the narrowing lies just distal to the left subclavian artery. Blood reaches the lower body through the internal thoracic → superior epigastric → inferior epigastric → external iliac chain and through dilated intercostal arteries, which erode the ribs ( rib notching ). The upper limbs are hypertensive and the femoral pulse is weak and delayed. Subclavian steal syndrome — stenosis or occlusion of the proximal subclavian artery makes blood flow retrogradely down the ipsilateral vertebral artery during exercise of the arm, producing vertebrobasilar insufficiency (dizziness, visual disturbance). Thoracic outlet syndrome — the subclavian artery and the brachial plexus may be compressed in the scalene triangle, between the clavicle and rib I, or beneath the pectoralis minor. Central venous catheterisation — the subclavian vein is punctured in the infraclavicular fossa; it lies anterior to the artery, separated from it by the anterior scalene muscle, and joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. Brachial plexus block — local anaesthetic injected into the interscalene groove, where the plexus and the subclavian artery lie, anaesthetises the whole limb. Clinical / radiological correlation Pulses and blood pressure — the subclavian pulse is felt above the clavicle; comparison of the two radial pulses and of the two arms is the first bedside test of a subclavian stenosis. Radiology — the artery is shown by subclavian or aortic arch angiography; in coarctation the dilated internal thoracic and intercostal collaterals produce rib notching on the chest radiograph. Complications of subclavian vein puncture — the adjacent artery or the apical pleura may be injured, producing haemothorax or pneumothorax. Must-memorise Right subclavian from the brachiocephalic trunk; left subclavian from the arch of the aorta; both end at the lateral border of rib I. Three parts divided by the anterior scalene; all branches from the first part except the costocervical trunk (second part on the right). Branches: vertebral artery, thyrocervical trunk (inferior thyroid, transverse cervical, suprascapular), internal thoracic artery, costocervical trunk (deep cervical, supreme intercostal); dorsal scapular artery from the third part. Internal thoracic artery ends at the sixth intercostal space as the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. Main subclavian–external iliac anastomosis: internal thoracic → superior epigastric → inferior epigastric → external iliac. Likely follow-up questions How would you describe the subclavian artery in one sentence? — The trunk from the brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch to the lateral border of rib I, divided into three parts by the anterior scalene muscle. Which vessel passes through the transverse foramen of CVI and why does it matter? — The vertebral artery; it is the first branch of the subclavian and a channel of the posterior cerebral circulation. What are the branches of the thyrocervical trunk? — Inferior thyroid, transverse cervical and suprascapular arteries. Why is the internal thoracic artery clinically important? — It is the cranial half of the main collateral route to the lower body and is used as a coronary bypass graft. Why can the subclavian artery be ligated without loss of the limb? — The scapular arcade connects it with the axillary artery, and the intercostal and thoracic branches provide further collaterals."},{"id":"t-I-15","p":"I","n":15,"t":"The branching system and anastomoses of the axillary artery.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The axillary artery is the continuation of the subclavian artery from the lateral border of rib I to the lower border of the teres major muscle, where it becomes the brachial artery. It lies in the axilla with the brachial plexus and the axillary vein inside the axillary sheath , and its relations change as it passes behind the pectoralis minor muscle, which divides it into three parts. It gives six named branches: superior thoracic from the first part; thoraco-acromial and lateral thoracic from the second part; subscapular , anterior circumflex humeral and posterior circumflex humeral arteries from the third part. Its anastomoses — the scapular arcade and the acromial network — connect it with the subclavian artery and secure the blood supply of the shoulder. 1. Origin, course and sheath Origin — at the lateral border of rib I, as the continuation of the subclavian artery. Course — enters the axilla through the axillary inlet, runs along its medial wall and then turns laterally below the shoulder joint; its distal (third) part lies on the medial side of the coracobrachialis muscle, and it continues into the interval between the coracobrachialis and the long head of the triceps. Termination — at the lower border of the teres major muscle, where it becomes the brachial artery. Axillary sheath — a sleeve of prevertebral fascia enclosing the axillary artery, the axillary vein and the brachial plexus. This is why a single injection of local anaesthetic into the sheath produces a brachial plexus block for the whole limb. 2. Three parts and relations to the brachial plexus The pectoralis minor crosses anteriorly to the artery and divides it into a first part (proximal to the muscle), a second part (behind the muscle) and a third part (distal to the muscle). The three cords of the brachial plexus are named from their relation to the second part: the lateral cord lies lateral, the medial cord medial and the posterior cord posterior to it. First part — lies in the axillary inlet, related anteriorly to the clavicle and the subclavius muscle and medially to the first rib and the cervical pleura; the cords of the brachial plexus lie lateral to it. Second part — posterior to the pectoralis minor and surrounded by the cords of the brachial plexus; the axillary vein lies anterior and medial to the artery. Third part — related anteriorly to the roots of the median nerve (which embrace it), medially to the ulnar nerve and the medial cutaneous nerves of the arm and forearm, laterally to the musculocutaneous nerve and coracobrachialis, and posteriorly to the radial and axillary nerves. 3. Branches by part Part Branch Course and territory First part Superior thoracic artery ( a. thoracica superior ) Small branch from the anterior surface; supplies the upper parts of the medial and anterior axillary walls. Second part Thoraco-acromial artery ( a. thoracoacromialis ) Short branch from the anterior surface; curves round the superior margin of the pectoralis minor and divides into pectoral, deltoid, clavicular and acromial branches for the anterior axillary wall and shoulder. The pectoral branch also supplies the breast; the deltoid branch runs with the cephalic vein in the clavipectoral triangle. Lateral thoracic artery ( a. thoracica lateralis ) Follows the lateral margin of the pectoralis minor to the thoracic wall; supplies the medial and anterior axillary walls and, through branches around the pectoralis major, the breast. Third part Subscapular artery ( a. subscapularis ) — largest branch Runs along the inferior margin of the subscapularis and divides into the circumflex scapular artery (through the triangular space into the infraspinous fossa) and the thoracodorsal artery (along the lateral border of the scapula to its inferior angle). Supplies the posterior wall of the axilla and the posterior scapular region. Anterior circumflex humeral artery ( a. circumflexa humeri anterior ) Passes anterior to the surgical neck of the humerus; supplies the glenohumeral joint and the head of the humerus; anastomoses with the posterior circumflex humeral artery. Posterior circumflex humeral artery ( a. circumflexa humeri posterior ) Leaves the axilla through the quadrangular space with the axillary nerve; curves round the surgical neck; supplies the deltoid, teres minor and the shoulder joint; anastomoses with the anterior circumflex, profunda brachii, suprascapular and thoraco-acromial arteries. Six branches, 1-2-3: ST / TA + LT / SS + AC + PC — superior thoracic; thoraco-acromial, lateral thoracic; subscapular, anterior and posterior circumflex humeral. 4. Anastomoses of the axillary artery Network Vessels that meet Region supplied / note Scapular arcade Suprascapular artery (thyrocervical trunk, subclavian) and dorsal scapular artery (transverse cervical, subclavian) above; circumflex scapular artery (from the subscapular, axillary) below Around the scapula and its muscles; the main route by which blood reaches the limb after ligation or occlusion of the axillary artery Acromial network Acromial branch of the thoraco-acromial artery, suprascapular artery, posterior circumflex humeral artery Over the acromion and the shoulder joint Circumflex humeral ring Anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries Around the surgical neck of the humerus Deltoid-posterior scapular connections Posterior circumflex humeral artery with the profunda brachii and with branches of the suprascapular and thoraco-acromial arteries Deltoid and posterior shoulder Thoracic wall connections Lateral thoracic and superior thoracic arteries with intercostal arteries and the internal thoracic artery Axillary walls and the breast Collateral loop around the shoulder : a. subclavia → truncus thyrocervicalis → a. suprascapularis → (scapular arcade) → a. circumflexa scapularis → a. subscapularis → a. axillaris. 5. Clinical and radiological correlations Axillary artery injury — the third part is stretched over the head of the humerus in anterior dislocation of the shoulder joint and may be torn by a fracture of the surgical neck; the expanding haematoma may compress the brachial plexus. Ligation of the axillary artery — tolerated because the scapular arcade and the acromial network maintain the blood supply of the distal limb. Crutch compression — prolonged pressure in the axilla can compress the axillary artery and the brachial plexus. Quadrangular space syndrome — hypertrophy or fibrosis of the boundaries of the quadrangular space compresses the axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery; weakness and atrophy of the deltoid and teres minor result. Angiography and catheterisation — the axillary artery is a common access route for upper limb angiography and for the axillary brachial-plexus block; the artery is palpable in the axilla when the arm is abducted. Must-memorise Boundaries: lateral border of rib I → lower border of the teres major. Three parts divided by the pectoralis minor; cords of the brachial plexus lie around the second part. Six branches: superior thoracic (1st); thoraco-acromial and lateral thoracic (2nd); subscapular, anterior and posterior circumflex humeral (3rd). Subscapular artery divides into the circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal arteries; the posterior circumflex humeral artery runs with the axillary nerve through the quadrangular space. Scapular arcade = suprascapular + dorsal scapular + circumflex scapular arteries: the collateral route around the shoulder. Clinical / radiological correlation The axillary artery is surrounded by the brachial plexus, so a single sheath injection anaesthetises the whole upper limb. Anterior shoulder dislocation in an elderly patient may rupture the axillary artery; check the radial pulse before and after reduction. Selective angiography of the axillary artery demonstrates the subscapular, circumflex humeral and thoraco-acromial branches. The axillary vein lies anterior and medial to the artery and is the target for central venous access from the arm. Likely follow-up questions Why is the axillary artery divided into three parts? — Because the pectoralis minor crosses it and each part has a different set of branches and relations. Which branch of the axillary artery is the largest and what does it supply? — The subscapular artery; it supplies the posterior wall of the axilla and scapular region. Which artery accompanies the axillary nerve? — The posterior circumflex humeral artery, through the quadrangular space. How does blood reach the arm if the axillary artery is ligated? — Through the scapular arcade from the thyrocervical and transverse cervical branches of the subclavian artery into the circumflex scapular artery."},{"id":"t-I-16","p":"I","n":16,"t":"The branching system of the brachial artery; collateral circulation of the elbow.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The brachial artery is the continuation of the axillary artery from the lower border of the teres major muscle and runs through the anterior compartment of the arm to the cubital fossa, where it divides at the level of the neck of the radius into the radial and ulnar arteries. Its main branches are the profunda brachii artery (which gives the radial and middle collateral arteries), the superior ulnar collateral artery , the inferior ulnar collateral artery , muscular branches and the humeral nutrient artery. Around the elbow these descending collaterals anastomose with the ascending recurrent branches of the forearm — radial recurrent, interosseous recurrent, and anterior and posterior ulnar recurrent arteries — forming the collateral circulation of the elbow , which secures the supply of the forearm when the brachial artery is occluded. 1. Course and relations Begins at the lower border of the teres major muscle ; lies in the anterior compartment of the arm. Proximally it is on the medial side of the arm, related medially to the ulnar nerve and the basilic vein, and laterally to the median nerve. Distally it moves laterally to lie midway between the medial and lateral epicondyles of the humerus, crosses anterior to the elbow joint and lies immediately medial to the tendon of the biceps brachii in the cubital fossa. It is palpable along its whole length and can be compressed against the medial side of the humerus in the proximal arm. The median nerve is its constant companion: lateral to the artery proximally, crossing to its medial side distally. It ends in the cubital fossa deep to the bicipital aponeurosis by dividing into the radial artery and the ulnar artery . 2. Branches of the brachial artery Branch Origin Course and contribution Profunda brachii artery (deep artery of the arm) (a. profunda brachii) Largest branch; arises proximally from the posteromedial aspect Enters the posterior compartment through the triangular interval together with the radial nerve and runs in the radial groove; supplies the triceps brachii, the humerus and the lateral side of the arm; gives the radial and middle collateral arteries. Radial collateral artery (a. collateralis radialis) From the profunda brachii Descends laterally with the radial nerve; anastomoses with the radial recurrent artery of the radial artery. Middle collateral artery (a. collateralis media) From the profunda brachii Descends in the lateral intermuscular septum towards the olecranon fossa; anastomoses with the interosseous recurrent artery. Superior ulnar collateral artery (a. collateralis ulnaris superior) From the brachial artery in the middle of the arm Accompanies the ulnar nerve, pierces the medial intermuscular septum and descends behind the medial epicondyle; anastomoses with the posterior ulnar recurrent artery. Inferior ulnar collateral artery (a. collateralis ulnaris inferior) From the brachial artery just above the medial epicondyle Passes in front of the medial epicondyle; anastomoses with the anterior ulnar recurrent artery. Muscular, nutrient and articular branches Along the whole course Muscular branches to biceps brachii, brachialis and coracobrachialis; the humeral nutrient artery enters the humerus through a foramen in the anteromedial surface of its shaft; periosteal and articular branches. Terminal branches In the cubital fossa Radial artery (lateral) and ulnar artery (medial). 3. The cubital fossa and termination The cubital fossa is a triangular space anterior to the elbow joint, bounded by the brachioradialis muscle laterally, the pronator teres muscle medially and a line between the two epicondyles above. Its floor is the brachialis and supinator muscles; its roof is the skin and superficial fascia containing the median cubital vein , and the bicipital aponeurosis separates that vein from the deeper structures. The contents, from lateral to medial, are the biceps brachii tendon, the brachial artery and the median nerve ( TAN ); the radial nerve lies just under the lip of the brachioradialis and divides into superficial and deep branches, and the ulnar nerve does not enter the fossa — it passes behind the medial epicondyle. 4. Collateral circulation of the elbow The elbow has a ring of anastomoses formed by descending collateral branches from above and ascending recurrent branches from below. Each descending vessel has its named partner, and the pairs are named after the artery that gives the recurrent branch. Descending (collateral) Ascending (recurrent) Source of the recurrent Region Radial collateral artery (from the profunda brachii) Radial recurrent artery (a. recurrens radialis) Radial artery Lateral epicondyle and lateral compartment Middle collateral artery (from the profunda brachii) Interosseous recurrent artery (a. recurrens interossea) Posterior interosseous artery Olecranon fossa and posterior elbow Superior ulnar collateral artery Posterior ulnar recurrent artery (a. recurrens ulnaris posterior) Ulnar artery Behind the medial epicondyle Inferior ulnar collateral artery Anterior ulnar recurrent artery (a. recurrens ulnaris anterior) Ulnar artery In front of the medial epicondyle How the loop works : a. axillaris → a. brachialis → a. profunda brachii → a. collateralis radialis → (elbow anastomosis) → a. recurrens radialis → a. radialis; the same pattern repeats through the middle collateral with the interosseous recurrent and through the two ulnar collaterals with the ulnar recurrent arteries. Both recurrent ulnar arteries arise from the ulnar artery ; the radial recurrent from the radial artery ; the interosseous recurrent from the posterior interosseous artery (a branch of the common interosseous artery). After ligation or slow occlusion of the brachial artery, the forearm survives on this network — but the network is only as good as its calibre, so an abrupt tear may still cause ischaemia. 5. Clinical and radiological correlations Blood pressure measurement — the cuff compresses the brachial artery against the humerus and the stethoscope is placed over the artery in the cubital fossa; the sounds are the Korotkov sounds of the returning pulse. Supracondylar fracture of the humerus — the classic arterial injury of the upper limb; the brachial artery may be torn or compressed by the displaced distal fragment, and the resulting ischaemia can produce Volkmann ischaemic contracture of the forearm flexors. The radial pulse must be checked before and after reduction. Arterial blood gas sampling — the brachial artery is punctured medial to the biceps tendon in the cubital fossa, a site with good collateral support. Arteriovenous fistula — a brachiocephalic (brachial artery to cephalic vein) fistula at the elbow is a standard vascular access for haemodialysis. Venesection and cannulation — the median cubital vein lies in the roof of the fossa, superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis, which protects the brachial artery from a misdirected needle. Must-memorise Brachial artery: lower border of teres major → neck of the radius, where it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. Branches: profunda brachii (largest, with radial and middle collaterals), superior and inferior ulnar collateral arteries, muscular and humeral nutrient branches. Collateral pairs: radial collateral → radial recurrent; middle collateral → interosseous recurrent; superior ulnar collateral → posterior ulnar recurrent; inferior ulnar collateral → anterior ulnar recurrent. Cubital fossa contents lateral to medial: biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve. The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle and does not enter the cubital fossa. Clinical / radiological correlation Supracondylar fracture in a child with an absent radial pulse and a cold hand is a vascular emergency; the elbow collateral network may not compensate for an acute tear. The brachial artery is the vessel auscultated during sphygmomanometry, and its pulse is felt medial to the biceps in the mid-arm. Radial artery cannulation is preceded by the Allen test to confirm that the ulnar artery and palmar arches can supply the hand. A brachial artery puncture may be complicated by a median nerve haematoma because the nerve lies immediately medial to the artery. Likely follow-up questions What is the largest branch of the brachial artery and where does it run? — The profunda brachii; through the triangular interval into the posterior compartment, in the radial groove with the radial nerve. Name the four collateral arteries of the elbow and their partners. — Radial collateral with the radial recurrent, middle collateral with the interosseous recurrent, superior ulnar collateral with the posterior ulnar recurrent, inferior ulnar collateral with the anterior ulnar recurrent. Why is the cubital fossa a safe site for venepuncture? — The median cubital vein lies in the roof, separated from the brachial artery and median nerve by the bicipital aponeurosis. Which nerve is at risk in a supracondylar fracture, and which artery? — The median nerve and the brachial artery, both passing anterior to the elbow."},{"id":"t-I-17","p":"I","n":17,"t":"Topography and branches of the palmar arterial arches.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The hand is supplied by two anastomotic arches formed by the radial and ulnar arteries. The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by the ulnar artery and is completed laterally by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery; it lies deep to the palmar aponeurosis and superficial to the long flexor tendons and gives three common palmar digital arteries and a proper digital artery to the little finger. The deep palmar arch is formed mainly by the terminal part of the radial artery and is completed medially by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery; it lies deeper in the palm on the metacarpals and gives three palmar metacarpal arteries, three perforating branches, the princeps pollicis artery and the radialis indicis artery. Together the two arches secure a dual arterial supply to the hand. 1. General arrangement of the arterial supply of the hand The ulnar artery enters the hand on the medial side of the wrist, lateral to the pisiform and superficial to the flexor retinaculum; it is the main supply of the medial three and one-half digits. The radial artery leaves the forearm around the lateral side of the wrist, crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox , and reaches the deep plane of the palm by passing between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle; it is the main supply of the thumb and the lateral side of the index finger. The two arteries are interconnected by the superficial and deep palmar arches , by the dorsal carpal arch and its dorsal metacarpal arteries, and by the perforating branches — so either artery alone can supply the whole hand. 2. The superficial palmar arch Formation — mainly the continuation of the ulnar artery, completed on the lateral side of the palm by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery . Position — superficial in the palm: it lies deep to the palmar aponeurosis and superficial to the long flexor tendons of the digits; it is more distal than the deep arch. Branches: a palmar digital artery to the medial side of the little finger ; three large common palmar digital arteries , which supply the lateral side of the little finger, both sides of the ring and middle fingers and the medial side of the index finger. Before bifurcating into the proper palmar digital arteries they are joined by the palmar metacarpal arteries from the deep arch. 3. The deep palmar arch Formation — mainly the terminal part of the radial artery, completed on the medial side of the palm by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery , which curves round the hook of the hamate into the deep plane. Position — lies proximal to the superficial arch, on the palmar surfaces of the metacarpal bones and deep to the long flexor tendons; it is the deeper and more proximal of the two arches. Branches: three palmar metacarpal arteries (which join the common palmar digital arteries); three perforating branches (which pass dorsally between the heads of the dorsal interossei to anastomose with the dorsal metacarpal arteries); and, arising from the radial artery at this level, the princeps pollicis artery (main supply of the thumb) and the radialis indicis artery (lateral side of the index finger). 4. Comparison of the two arches Feature Superficial palmar arch Deep palmar arch Main contributor Ulnar artery Radial artery Completing vessel Superficial palmar branch of the radial artery Deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery Plane and level Deep to the palmar aponeurosis, superficial to the flexor tendons; more distal On the metacarpals, deep to the flexor tendons; more proximal Main branches Palmar digital artery to the little finger; three common palmar digital arteries Three palmar metacarpal arteries; three perforating branches; princeps pollicis and radialis indicis arteries Chief territory Medial three and one-half digits Thumb and lateral side of the index finger, plus the metacarpal and dorsal connections 5. Branches of the arches in summary Arch Branch Distribution Superficial Palmar digital artery to the medial side of the little finger Medial border of the little finger Three common palmar digital arteries Lateral side of the little finger, both sides of ring and middle fingers, medial side of the index finger; each divides into proper palmar digital arteries Anastomosis with the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery Completes the arch on the lateral side of the palm Deep Three palmar metacarpal arteries Join the common palmar digital arteries in the intermetacarpal spaces Three perforating branches Pass dorsally to anastomose with the dorsal metacarpal arteries of the dorsal carpal arch Princeps pollicis artery Main supply of the thumb Radialis indicis artery Lateral side of the index finger Recurrent and articular branches Wrist joint and carpal bones (with the palmar carpal network) The dorsal carpal arch on the back of the wrist gives three dorsal metacarpal arteries, which divide into dorsal digital arteries; these anastomose with the palmar arches through the perforating branches, completing the double supply of each finger. 6. Clinical and radiological correlations Allen test — both arteries are compressed at the wrist and then released one at a time; if the palm does not flush when the ulnar artery is released, the arches are not communicating and the hand depends on a single artery. Transection of the radial or ulnar artery — these arteries are subcutaneous at the wrist and may be cut; because of the dual supply, either can usually be ligated without loss of the hand. Radial artery cannulation and harvesting — the radial artery is used for arterial lines and as a coronary bypass conduit; the arches must be tested beforehand (Allen test). Palpation sites — the radial pulse at the wrist lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon and in the floor of the anatomical snuffbox; the ulnar pulse lateral to the pisiform. Angiography — the palmar arches are demonstrated by contrast injection; the deep arch is visible on a standard palmar angiogram as the arcade between the radial and ulnar arteries. Must-memorise Superficial arch: mainly ulnar + superficial palmar branch of radial; superficial to the flexor tendons; gives three common palmar digital arteries and the digital artery of the little finger. Deep arch: mainly radial + deep palmar branch of ulnar; on the metacarpals; gives three palmar metacarpal arteries, three perforating branches, princeps pollicis and radialis indicis arteries. The deep arch lies deeper and more proximal than the superficial arch. The princeps pollicis artery is the main artery of the thumb; the radialis indicis supplies the lateral side of the index finger. Dual supply: radial and ulnar arteries are joined at both arches, through the perforating branches and at the dorsal carpal arch. Clinical / radiological correlation An incomplete superficial arch (ending as a branch to the thumb and index finger) is a normal variant that makes the hand dependent on the radial artery. Hand ischaemia after radial artery cannulation is rare but is the reason for the Allen test. Laceration of the palm may divide both the superficial arch and the median nerve branches, so a careful vascular and neurological examination is needed. Contrast angiography of the hand demonstrates filling of the superficial and deep arches and their digital branches. Likely follow-up questions Which artery forms the superficial arch and which completes it? — The ulnar artery, completed by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. Which artery forms the deep arch and which completes it? — The radial artery, completed by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery. Where does the radial artery pass from the forearm into the deep palm? — Through the anatomical snuffbox and between the heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle. What is the arterial supply of the thumb? — Mainly the princeps pollicis artery from the deep arch, with the first dorsal metacarpal artery and perforating branches. Why does the hand survive ligation of the radial artery? — Because the deep and superficial arches connect it with the ulnar artery, which supplies the same territory."},{"id":"t-I-18","p":"I","n":18,"t":"The venous and lymphatic drainage of the upper limb.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The upper limb is drained by two interconnected venous systems. The superficial veins lie in the subcutaneous tissue: the dorsal venous network of the hand drains into the cephalic vein laterally and the basilic vein medially, and the two are connected in the cubital fossa by the median cubital vein . The deep veins are paired venae comitantes that accompany the arteries — radial, ulnar and interosseous veins, then the brachial veins, the axillary vein and the subclavian vein, which becomes the brachiocephalic vein. Most lymph from the limb passes to the axillary lymph nodes ; superficial lymphatics run with the superficial veins through the cubital nodes (those of the thumb, index finger and dorsum of the hand may bypass them), deep lymphatics follow the arteries, and from the apical group the subclavian trunk carries the lymph into the thoracic duct on the left or the right lymphatic duct on the right. 1. General plan Two venous systems — superficial (subcutaneous, highly variable, used for venepuncture) and deep (paired, accompanying the arteries) — connected by perforating veins at many levels, so obstruction of one route is compensated by the other. Both systems contain valves that direct blood towards the heart; the flow is assisted by the contraction of the muscles of the limb (muscle pump). Lymphatics form a superficial plexus with the superficial veins and a deep plexus with the arteries; the two communicate freely. 2. Superficial veins Vein Origin Course and termination Dorsal venous network (rete venosum dorsale manus) Dorsal digital and dorsal metacarpal veins on the back of the hand Lies over the metacarpal bones; gives origin to the cephalic and basilic veins on its lateral and medial sides. Cephalic vein (v. cephalica) Lateral (radial) side of the dorsal venous network Crosses the anatomical snuffbox, ascends on the lateral side of the forearm and the anterolateral aspect of the arm, runs in the deltopectoral groove and pierces the clavipectoral fascia to enter the axillary vein through the anterior wall of the axilla. Basilic vein (v. basilica) Medial (ulnar) side of the dorsal venous network Ascends on the posteromedial aspect of the forearm and the medial side of the arm, pierces the deep fascia in the middle of the arm (the basilic hiatus ) and joins the brachial veins, becoming the axillary vein at the lower border of the teres major muscle. Median cubital vein (v. mediana cubiti) A communicating vein of the cubital fossa Passes diagonally across the roof of the cubital fossa and connects the cephalic with the basilic vein; it is separated from the brachial artery and median nerve by the bicipital aponeurosis . Median antebrachial vein (v. mediana antebrachii) Venous network of the palm and forearm Ascends in the midline of the anterior forearm; may divide into a median cephalic and a median basilic vein. The arrangement of the cubital veins is very variable (M-shaped, absent median cubital vein, accessory cephalic vein). 3. Deep veins The deep veins follow the arteries and are paired (venae comitantes) with numerous valves; they are the veins that must be considered when a deep venous thrombosis is suspected after a fracture or immobility. Deep vein Accompanying artery Drains into Palmar digital and palmar metacarpal veins, deep palmar venous arch Palmar arches Radial and ulnar veins Radial veins (paired) Radial artery Brachial veins Ulnar veins (paired) Ulnar artery Brachial veins Interosseous veins (anterior and posterior, paired) Interosseous arteries Ulnar veins Brachial veins (paired) Brachial artery Axillary vein (they join the basilic vein in the arm) Axillary vein Axillary artery Subclavian vein at the lateral border of rib I Subclavian vein Subclavian artery Brachiocephalic vein at the medial end of the clavicle 4. Lymphatic drainage of the upper limb Superficial lymphatics lie in the subcutaneous tissue with the superficial veins. Most ascend with the basilic vein and pass through one or two cubital (supratrochlear) lymph nodes at the elbow, then continue to the humeral (lateral) group of axillary nodes . Lymphatics from the thumb, index finger and the dorsum of the hand may run with the cephalic vein directly to the infraclavicular and apical axillary nodes without passing through the cubital nodes. Deep lymphatics accompany the arteries and their deep veins and drain into the humeral group of axillary nodes. All lymph from the upper limb therefore passes to the axillary nodes ; no other node group receives the limb's lymph — but not every vessel passes through the cubital nodes. Axillary group Position Drains Humeral (lateral) Posteromedial to the axillary vein Most of the upper limb Pectoral (anterior) Along the inferior margin of the pectoralis minor, with the lateral thoracic vessels Anterior chest wall, breast, upper anterolateral abdominal wall Subscapular (posterior) Posterior axillary wall, with the subscapular vessels Back, shoulder and neck Central Embedded in the axillary fat Receives from the humeral, subscapular and pectoral groups Apical Most superior group, at the apex of the axilla All other axillary groups; lymphatics running with the cephalic vein; superior part of the mammary gland For surgical staging in breast cancer the axillary nodes are also divided into three levels by their relation to the pectoralis minor: level I lateral to it, level II behind it and level III medial to it. 5. Termination of the lymph trunks Limb lymphatics (most) → cubital nodes → humeral axillary nodes → central nodes → apical nodes → subclavian trunk → on the right: right lymphatic duct (or the junction of the right subclavian and internal jugular veins); on the left: thoracic duct. The thoracic duct receives, in most cases, the left jugular trunk and the left subclavian trunk and empties into the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. On the right side a right lymphatic duct may form and open at the corresponding junction; when it is absent the right subclavian trunk opens directly into the vein. 6. Clinical and radiological correlations Venepuncture — the superficial veins of the cubital fossa (median cubital, cephalic, basilic) are the standard sites for blood sampling and cannulation; a tourniquet proximal to the elbow makes them prominent. Lymphoedema — mastectomy, axillary node clearance or radiotherapy to the axilla destroys the lymphatics of the limb and causes swelling of the arm. Sentinel node biopsy — the first node draining a breast cancer is identified in the axilla; the levels of axillary nodes determine the surgical approach. Cubital (supratrochlear) node enlargement — a palpable node at the elbow indicates an infection of the hand or forearm on the same side. Arteriovenous fistula — the cephalic vein is joined to the radial or brachial artery for haemodialysis; the cephalic vein becomes arterialised and dilates over about six weeks. Superficial thrombophlebitis — the cephalic vein in the arm and the long saphenous vein in the leg are the usual sites; the vein becomes a palpable, tender cord. Must-memorise Superficial veins: dorsal venous network → cephalic (lateral) and basilic (medial) veins, linked by the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa. Cephalic vein ends in the axillary vein after running in the deltopectoral groove; the basilic vein pierces the deep fascia at the basilic hiatus and becomes the axillary vein at the lower border of the teres major. Deep veins are paired venae comitantes: radial, ulnar and interosseous → brachial → axillary → subclavian → brachiocephalic vein. Most lymph from the limb reaches the axillary nodes after the cubital nodes; lymphatics of the thumb, index finger and dorsum of the hand may bypass the cubital nodes. Axillary groups: humeral (lateral), pectoral (anterior), subscapular (posterior), central, apical; the apical group forms the subclavian trunk. Clinical / radiological correlation Axillary node clearance for breast cancer removes the main drainage route of the limb and is the commonest cause of upper limb lymphoedema. The median cubital vein is separated from the brachial artery by the bicipital aponeurosis — a safeguard during venepuncture. Deep vein thrombosis of the upper limb may follow central venous catheterisation; the axillary and subclavian veins are the usual sites. Lymphoscintigraphy and sentinel node biopsy demonstrate the lymphatic pathway from the hand and breast to the axilla. Likely follow-up questions Which superficial vein is on the lateral side of the limb and where does it end? — The cephalic vein; it ends in the axillary vein after crossing the deltopectoral groove. What is the basilic hiatus? — The opening in the deep fascia of the middle of the arm through which the basilic vein passes to join the brachial veins. Why are the deep veins of the limb paired? — They are venae comitantes that accompany the corresponding artery, a pattern that helps the muscle pump and heat exchange. Where does lymph from the upper limb go? — Through the cubital nodes to the axillary nodes, then by the subclavian trunk to the thoracic duct (left) or right lymphatic duct (right). Which axillary node group forms the subclavian trunk? — The apical group."},{"id":"t-I-19","p":"I","n":19,"t":"The organization of spinal cord segments and spinal nerves. The general organization and features of the cranial nerves.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The spinal cord is built metamerically: each spinal segment emits one pair of spinal nerves through a dorsal (sensory) root carrying the spinal ganglion and a ventral (motor) root, and the two unite in the intervertebral foramen into a mixed spinal nerve that divides into dorsal, ventral and communicating rami. There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves; the ventral rami of adjacent segments form the cervical, brachial, lumbar and sacral plexuses, while thoracic ventral rami stay segmental as intercostal nerves. The 12 pairs of cranial nerves belong to the PNS, leave (or enter) the skull through named foramina, and carry general somatic and visceral as well as special sensory and branchial motor fibres. 1. The spinal cord and its segments The spinal cord ( medulla spinalis ) is continuous with the medulla oblongata at the foramen magnum and tapers into the cone-shaped conus medullaris at the level of the disc between vertebrae LI and LII in the adult (in the neonate it reaches approximately LIII). A spinal segment is the length of cord that gives origin to one pair of spinal nerves by its rootlets; externally the segments are indicated by two fusiform swellings, the cervical enlargement (C5–T1, for the upper limb) and the lumbosacral enlargement (for the lower limb). 31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal. Because the cord is much shorter than the vertebral canal, the lumbosacral roots run obliquely below the conus as the cauda equina ; a pial filum terminale continues from the apex of the conus to the coccyx. The dural sac and subarachnoid space extend down to the SII vertebra, which is why CSF is sampled below the LII level. Meninges: dura mater (separated from bone by the epidural space), arachnoid mater, pia mater; the pia forms the segmentally arranged denticulate ligaments that anchor the cord within the dural sleeve. Region Nerve pairs Exit from the vertebral canal Ventral rami form Cervical 8 (C1–C8) C1–C7 emerge above their own vertebra; C8 emerges between vertebrae CVII and TI Cervical plexus (C1–C4), brachial plexus (C5–T1), phrenic nerve (C3–C5) Thoracic 12 (T1–T12) Below the corresponding vertebra No plexus: segmental intercostal and subcostal nerves Lumbar 5 (L1–L5) Below the corresponding vertebra Lumbar plexus (T12–L4) Sacral 5 (S1–S5) Through the sacral foramina Sacral plexus (L4–S4) Coccygeal 1 (Co1) Through the sacral hiatus region Coccygeal plexus 2. Internal organization of a segment Grey matter is H-shaped: anterior (ventral) horn = motor neuron cell bodies; posterior (dorsal) horn = sensory relay neurons; the intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) between T1 and L2 holds the preganglionic sympathetic neurons. Cytologically the grey matter is divided into Rexed laminae I–X ; a small central canal runs the length of the cord. White matter forms three funiculi on each side: posterior (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus — fine touch, vibration, conscious proprioception), lateral (corticospinal, rubrospinal, spinothalamic, spinocerebellar) and anterior (anterior corticospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal). Surface grooves: anterior median fissure, posterior median sulcus and the posterolateral sulcus where the posterior rootlets enter. 3. Formation of a spinal nerve and its rami Motor axons leave the anterior horn as anterior rootlets that collect into the ventral (anterior) root ; sensory axons enter as posterior rootlets that collect into the dorsal (posterior) root , which bears the fusiform spinal (sensory) ganglion . The two roots unite in the intervertebral foramen to form the short mixed spinal nerve , which immediately divides. anterior horn → anterior rootlets → ventral root + spinal ganglion → dorsal root → spinal nerve (mixed) → ramus dorsalis + ramus ventralis + rami communicantes → dorsal ramus: back muscles and skin; ventral ramus: limbs and anterolateral body wall (plexuses) Part Fibre content Destination Ramus dorsalis General somatic afferent + efferent, plus postganglionic sympathetic fibres Thinner and shorter; runs posteriorly to the deep muscles of the back and the overlying skin (segmental, never forms a plexus) Ramus ventralis Same plus, at T1–L2, preganglionic sympathetic fibres Thicker and longer; supplies the anterolateral trunk and limbs; in the limbs it exchanges fibres in a plexus Ramus communicans albus Myelinated preganglionic sympathetic Present only at T1–L2, to the sympathetic trunk Ramus communicans griseus Unmyelinated postganglionic sympathetic Present at every spinal nerve: vasomotor, sudomotor and pilomotor fibres for the periphery Meningeal branch Sensory Recurrent branch to the dura and vertebral periosteum (basis of meningeal pain) 4. Segmental versus plexus distribution In the trunk the ventral rami remain segmental (intercostal nerves), so each skin strip is a dermatome and each muscle block a myotome . At the limb buds the ventral rami mix into plexuses, so a peripheral nerve carries fibres of several segments to a territory that cuts across dermatomes — the reason a root lesion and a nerve lesion give different sensory maps. Plexuses: cervical C1–C4; brachial C5–T1; lumbar T12–L4; sacral L4–S4 plus the coccygeal nerve. Autonomic fibres are distributed through the plexus: postganglionic sympathetic from the sympathetic chain supplies vasomotor, sudomotor and pilomotor functions of the limb. 5. General organization of the cranial nerves The 12 pairs of cranial nerves belong to the peripheral nervous system and leave the cranial cavity through foramina and fissures. All except the accessory nerve [XI] arise from the brain or brainstem. Unlike spinal nerves they may carry special sensory (smell, vision, hearing, balance, taste) and branchial motor components, because the muscles of the head and neck derive from the pharyngeal arches. Four cranial nerves (III, VII, IX, X) also carry parasympathetic (general visceral efferent) fibres, relayed in the four cranial parasympathetic ganglia (ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, otic). Pharyngeal arch motor supply: 1st arch — mandibular division of V; 2nd arch — VII; 3rd arch — IX; 4th arch — superior laryngeal branch of X; 6th arch — recurrent laryngeal branch of X; posterior arches — XI. No. Name Modality Main function Exit from the skull I Olfactory (n. olfactorius) Special afferent (SA) Smell; central processes end in the olfactory bulb. Developmentally a CNS tract. Cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone II Optic (n. opticus) SA Vision; axons of retinal ganglion cells. Developmentally a CNS tract. Optic canal III Oculomotor (n. oculomotorius) GSE + GVE Levator palpebrae superioris, superior, inferior and medial rectus, inferior oblique; parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle Superior orbital fissure IV Trochlear (n. trochlearis) GSE Superior oblique muscle; the only nerve leaving the dorsal surface of the brainstem Superior orbital fissure V Trigeminal (n. trigeminus) GSA + BE General sensation of face, teeth, oral and nasal mucosa, dura; motor to muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric V1 superior orbital fissure; V2 foramen rotundum; V3 foramen ovale VI Abducent (n. abducens) GSE Lateral rectus muscle Superior orbital fissure VII Facial (n. facialis) GSA + SA + GVE + BE Muscles of facial expression, stapedius, posterior belly of digastric, stylohyoid; taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue; lacrimal, submandibular and sublingual glands Internal acoustic meatus, facial canal, then stylomastoid foramen VIII Vestibulocochlear (n. vestibulocochlearis) SA Cochlear part — hearing; vestibular part — balance Internal acoustic meatus IX Glossopharyngeal (n. glossopharyngeus) GVA + GSA + SA + GVE + BE Taste from the posterior third of the tongue; sensation of the oropharynx and tonsil; carotid body and sinus; motor to stylopharyngeus; secretomotor to the parotid gland Jugular foramen X Vagus (n. vagus) GSA + GVA + SA + GVE + BE Motor and sensory supply of pharynx and larynx; taste from epiglottis; thoracic and abdominal viscera of foregut and midgut Jugular foramen XI Accessory (n. accessorius) BE Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; the cranial part arises from the medulla, the spinal part from the upper cervical cord — the only cranial nerve not arising from the brain alone Jugular foramen XII Hypoglossal (n. hypoglossus) GSE All intrinsic muscles of the tongue plus hyoglossus, genioglossus and styloglossus Hypoglossal canal Must-memorise 31 pairs: 8 C, 12 T, 5 L, 5 S, 1 Co. Cervical enlargement C5–T1; conus medullaris at LI–LII; dural sac to SII. C1–C7 exit above their vertebra, C8 between CVII and TI, all others below their vertebra. Ramus dorsalis = thin, to back muscles and skin; ramus ventralis = thick, to limbs and body wall, forms the plexuses. Intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) T1–L2 only = preganglionic sympathetic; white rami communicantes exist only here. Cranial nerve foramina: I cribriform plate, II optic canal, III/IV/VI superior orbital fissure, V1 superior orbital fissure, V2 foramen rotundum, V3 foramen ovale, VII stylomastoid, VIII internal acoustic meatus, IX/X/XI jugular foramen, XII hypoglossal canal. Clinical / radiological correlation Lumbar puncture is safe below LI–LII because the cord has ended; the needle enters the subarachnoid space of the cauda equina (dural sac to SII). Cauda equina syndrome (large disc herniation, tumour): bilateral flaccid paralysis of the legs with saddle anaesthesia and sphincter disturbance — a surgical emergency, in contrast to a single root lesion. Erb–Duchenne lesion = upper brachial plexus roots C5–C6; Klumpke = lower roots C8–T1, which also interrupts the sympathetic fibres to the face (Horner syndrome). Shingles : varicella-zoster virus reactivates in a single spinal or cranial ganglion and erupts along the corresponding dermatome. Cranial nerve lesions are localised by their exit: III/IV/VI within the orbit, V through the skull base, VII after the stylomastoid foramen (facial palsy), XI in the posterior triangle (trapezius wasting). Likely follow-up questions What exactly is a spinal segment? — The transverse slice of cord giving one pair of spinal nerves; there are 31, not 33–35, because the homologous segments have fused. Why can a cervical disc herniation injure a root a level below the vertebra? — The cord ends at LII, so the lumbar and sacral roots run down the canal as the cauda equina. Where do the sympathetic fibres for the upper limb originate and travel? — Preganglionic neurons in the intermediolateral column of T1–L2, via white rami to the sympathetic trunk, then postganglionic via grey rami onto the roots of the brachial plexus. Which cranial nerves are mixed? — V, VII, IX, X; I, II and VIII are purely sensory, III, IV, VI, XI, XII purely motor (III, VII, IX, X additionally autonomic)."},{"id":"t-I-20","p":"I","n":20,"t":"Organization and supply areas of the brachial plexus.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The brachial plexus is the somatic nerve plexus of the upper limb, formed by the anterior rami of C5–C8 and most of T1 . It is built in five stages — roots → trunks → divisions → cords → branches — begins in the neck between the scalene muscles, crosses the first rib and enters the axilla, where the three cords surround the second part of the axillary artery. Its branches supply every muscle and skin area of the shoulder girdle and limb, plus the scapular region and upper thoracic wall. 1. Roots and their topography Roots = anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and most of T1 , emerging from the intervertebral foramina. Close to their origin the roots receive grey rami communicantes from the sympathetic trunk, which carry postganglionic sympathetic fibres for the limb. The roots pass posterior to the vertebral artery and between the anterior and middle scalene muscles through the scalene hiatus ( hiatus scaleni ), lying superior and posterior to the subclavian artery. C5 also contributes a small branch to the phrenic nerve (C3–C5). 2. Trunks, divisions and cords Stage Composition Position and note Roots C5, C6, C7, C8, T1 Intervertebral foramina to scalene hiatus; anterior rami Superior trunk C5 + C6 Crosses rib I above the subclavian artery Middle trunk C7 alone Continuation of the C7 root Inferior trunk C8 + T1 Lies on rib I, posterior to the subclavian artery Divisions Each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division Situated in the space between the clavicle and rib I; no peripheral nerve arises directly from a division Lateral cord Anterior divisions of superior + middle trunks C5–C7; lateral to the 2nd part of the axillary artery Medial cord Anterior division of inferior trunk C8, T1; medial to the 2nd part of the axillary artery Posterior cord All three posterior divisions C5–T1; posterior to the 2nd part of the axillary artery Terminal branches Musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, axillary, radial (± medial cutaneous nerves) Arise around the 3rd part of the axillary artery roots C5–T1 → superior / middle / inferior trunks → anterior + posterior divisions → lateral, medial, posterior cords → terminal nerves. Mnemonic for the cords around the artery: L M P around the 2nd part 3. Branches of every part of the plexus Branch Origin Root value Supply / destination Dorsal scapular n. C5 root C5 Rhomboid major and minor (also levator scapulae via C5 branches) — does not reach the free limb Long thoracic n. C5–C7 roots C5, C6, C7 Serratus anterior; runs down the medial wall of the axilla on its superficial surface Nerve to subclavius Superior trunk C5, C6 Subclavius muscle Suprascapular n. Superior trunk C5, C6 Supraspinatus and infraspinatus; through the suprascapular foramen, the suprascapular artery passing above the superior transverse scapular ligament Lateral pectoral n. Lateral cord C5–C7 Pectoralis major; pierces the clavipectoral fascia with the thoraco-acromial artery Musculocutaneous n. Lateral cord C5–C7 All muscles of the anterior compartment of the arm; ends as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm Lateral root of median n. Lateral cord C5–C7 Joins the medial root to form the median nerve Medial pectoral n. Medial cord C8, T1 Pectoralis minor and (through it) pectoralis major Medial cutaneous n. of arm Medial cord C8, T1 Skin of the medial side of the distal third of the arm and floor of the axilla Medial cutaneous n. of forearm Medial cord C8, T1 Skin of the medial (ulnar) side of the forearm down to the wrist Medial root of median n. Medial cord C8, T1 Joins the lateral root anterior to the 3rd part of the axillary artery Ulnar n. Medial cord C8, T1 FCU, medial half of FDP, most intrinsic hand muscles; skin of the medial one and a half digits Superior subscapular n. Posterior cord C5, C6 Subscapularis Thoracodorsal n. Posterior cord C6–C8 Latissimus dorsi; the longest of the three subscapular-group nerves Inferior subscapular n. Posterior cord C5, C6 Subscapularis and teres major Axillary n. Posterior cord C5, C6 Deltoid, teres minor and the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm; leaves through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery Radial n. Posterior cord C5–C8 (T1) All muscles of the posterior compartments of arm and forearm; skin of the posterior arm and forearm and dorsolateral hand 4. Supply areas of the terminal nerves Nerve Cord Motor territory Cutaneous territory Musculocutaneous (C5–C7) Lateral Anterior compartment of the arm: biceps brachii, brachialis, coracobrachialis Lateral side of the forearm (lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm) Axillary (C5, C6) Posterior Deltoid, teres minor Upper lateral part of the arm Radial (C5–C8, T1) Posterior Triceps, anconeus, brachioradialis, all extensors of the forearm Posterior arm and forearm, lower lateral arm, dorsolateral hand Median (C5–C8, T1) Lateral + medial All forearm flexors except FCU and the medial half of FDP; three thenar muscles; two lateral lumbricals Palmar lateral three and a half digits, lateral palm, middle of the wrist Ulnar (C7, C8, T1) Medial FCU, medial half of FDP, all intrinsic hand muscles except the thenar group and two lateral lumbricals Palmar and dorsal medial one and a half digits and the adjacent hand 5. Topography and surgical landmarks Supraclavicular part (roots and trunks): in the posterior triangle of the neck, in the scalene hiatus, above and behind the subclavian artery. Retroclavicular part (divisions): in the costoclavicular space between clavicle and rib I — a site of compression in thoracic outlet syndromes. Infraclavicular part (cords and terminal branches): in the axilla, wrapped in the axillary sheath with the axillary artery and vein. The musculocutaneous nerve, the two roots of the median nerve and the ulnar nerve form an M over the third part of the axillary artery; penetration of the coracobrachialis by the musculocutaneous nerve identifies the plexus. Root values are the basis of the Plexus anaesthesia: an interscalene block targets the roots and trunks, an axillary block the terminal nerves around the axillary artery. Must-memorise Roots C5–C8 + most of T1; superior trunk C5+C6, middle C7, inferior C8+T1; lateral cord C5–C7, medial C8–T1, posterior C5–T1. Only two nerves arise from the roots (dorsal scapular, long thoracic) and two from the trunks (nerve to subclavius, suprascapular). Nerves that never reach the free limb: dorsal scapular, long thoracic, nerve to subclavius, pectoral nerves, subscapulars, thoracodorsal. Nerves of the posterior cord: upper subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular, axillary, radial — ULTAR . Root values: axillary C5–C6, musculocutaneous C5–C7, radial C5–C8 (T1), median C5–C8 + T1, ulnar C8–T1. Clinical / radiological correlation Erb–Duchenne (upper plexus) palsy — roots C5–C6, e.g. birth injury or a fall on the shoulder: deltoid, supraspinatus, biceps and brachioradialis are lost, so the limb hangs adducted and medially rotated with the forearm pronated and the hand flexed (waiter’s tip). Klumpke (lower plexus) palsy — roots C8–T1: paralysis of the small muscles of the hand (claw hand) and sensory loss on the medial forearm and hand; T1 sympathetic fibres may be involved giving a Horner syndrome. Winged scapula — long thoracic nerve injury (mastectomy, axillary surgery, stab wound): serratus anterior fails and the medial border of the scapula stands off the chest wall. Axillary nerve is injured in shoulder dislocation and surgical neck fractures of the humerus: deltoid paralysis and a patch of numbness over the lower deltoid. Plexus injuries are assessed by nerve conduction studies and electromyography; severe traction lesions may require nerve transfer. Likely follow-up questions Why does the C7 root form a single middle trunk yet reach two cords? — Because its anterior division joins the lateral cord and its posterior division joins the posterior cord; C7 does not enter the medial cord, which is formed by C8 and T1 only. Which nerve is endangered in an axillary lymph node clearance and with what deformity? — The long thoracic nerve; serratus anterior paralysis gives a winged scapula. Where exactly do the cords get their names? — From their relation to the second part of the axillary artery: lateral, medial and posterior. What is the clinical value of the “M” in the axilla? — It marks the union of the two median roots and identifies the standard site for an axillary brachial plexus block."},{"id":"t-I-21","p":"I","n":21,"t":"Branches of the median nerve, functional loss of the median nerve.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The median nerve (C5–C8, T1) is formed by the union of a lateral and a medial root in the axilla anterior to the third part of the axillary artery. It runs with the brachial artery, passes between the two heads of pronator teres into the forearm, supplies most of the flexor compartment (all except FCU and the medial half of FDP) through its muscular branches , and enters the hand through the carpal tunnel . In the hand it gives the recurrent branch to the three thenar muscles and the palmar digital nerves to the lateral three and a half digits, so a lesion produces thenar wasting with loss of thumb opposition and a characteristic sensory loss. 1. Origin and course The median nerve ( n. medianus ) is formed anterior to the third part of the axillary artery by the union of the lateral root (from the lateral cord, C5–C7) and the medial root (from the medial cord, C8–T1). It enters the arm at the inferior margin of teres major, lies first lateral and then crosses anterior (medial) to the brachial artery, and reaches the cubital fossa medial to the biceps tendon and brachial artery. It leaves the fossa between the two heads of pronator teres, then runs deep to the flexor digitorum superficialis, becoming superficial in the distal forearm between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis, and enters the palm deep to the flexor retinaculum. 2. Branches Branch Level Root value Structures supplied Articular branches Arm, elbow — Capsule of the elbow joint; no major muscular branch in the arm Branch to pronator teres Just above the elbow C6, C7 Pronator teres Muscular branches (superficial group) Distal to the elbow, medial side C6–T1 Flexor carpi radialis (C6, C7), palmaris longus (C7, C8), flexor digitorum superficialis (C8, T1) Anterior interosseous n. (n. interosseus anterior) Arises between the two heads of pronator teres; runs on the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous artery C7, C8 Flexor pollicis longus, lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus, pronator quadratus; terminal articular branches to the wrist Palmar branch (ramus palmaris) Distal forearm, just proximal to the flexor retinaculum C8, T1 Skin over the base and central palm; passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum, so it is spared in carpal tunnel syndrome Recurrent branch (ramus recurrens) In the palm, from the lateral side of the nerve at the distal edge of the flexor retinaculum C8, T1 Opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis (superficial head) Palmar digital nerves Palm, deep to the palmar aponeurosis and superficial palmar arch C8, T1 Skin of the palmar lateral three and a half digits and the nail beds of the same digits; also the first and second lumbrical muscles 3. Muscles supplied and their action Muscle Branch Action blocked by a lesion Pronator teres, pronator quadratus Median / anterior interosseous Pronation — tested with the elbow flexed to exclude gravity-assisted movement Flexor carpi radialis Median Wrist flexion and radial abduction (FCU is intact and pulls the wrist ulnar-ward) Palmaris longus Median Tensing of the palmar aponeurosis (absent in part of the population anyway) Flexor digitorum superficialis Median Flexion of the proximal interphalangeal joints of the four fingers Flexor digitorum profundus (lateral half) Anterior interosseous Flexion of the distal interphalangeal joint of index and middle fingers Flexor pollicis longus Anterior interosseous Flexion of the interphalangeal joint of the thumb Thenar muscles (opponens, abductor, flexor pollicis brevis) Recurrent branch Opposition and abduction of the thumb — the cardinal median deficit First and second lumbricals Palmar digital nerves Flexion of the metacarpophalangeal and extension of the interphalangeal joints of index and middle fingers 4. Functional loss — level by level The deficit depends entirely on where the nerve is interrupted. A high (supracondylar) lesion removes all median-supplied forearm flexors plus the thenar muscles; a low (wrist) lesion removes only the thenar group and the sensory territory, while the long flexors are spared. Level of lesion Motor loss Sensory loss Axilla / arm (rare; ligament of Struthers) All median muscles: pronators, FCR, palmaris longus, FDS, FPL, lateral FDP, thenar group and two lateral lumbricals Palmar lateral three and a half digits, lateral palm, nail beds Elbow / proximal forearm (pronator syndrome) As above, but the branch to pronator teres may be spared; aching forearm pain and a positive Tinel sign over the pronator As above Anterior interosseous nerve (pure motor branch) FPL, lateral FDP and pronator quadratus only: the patient cannot flex the IP joint of the thumb or the DIP joint of the index finger, so the pinch ( OK sign ) becomes a flat pinch None — no cutaneous fibres Wrist / carpal tunnel (commonest) Thenar muscles only: thenar wasting, loss of opposition (ape hand); no long-flexor weakness Palmar lateral three and a half digits, sparing the central palm (palmar branch spared) 5. The two classic deformities Hand of benediction ( manus benedicta ) — on attempting to make a fist, the index and middle fingers stay extended because the median nerve supplies FDS, the lateral FDP and the lateral two lumbricals of those digits; the ring and little fingers flex normally. The posture resembles a blessing hand. Ape hand — wasting of the thenar eminence with the thumb lying flat beside the palm, because opposition, abduction and the superficial head of the short flexor of the thumb are lost while the adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve) is intact and pulls the thumb into the palm. Sensory loss is functionally more disabling than the motor loss: the thumb and index pulp are the chief tactile organs, so precision grip and stereognosis fail. Vasomotor and trophic changes : dry, glossy skin and brittle nails in the median territory, from loss of sympathetic fibres carried with the nerve. Must-memorise Median nerve = two roots (lateral C5–C7 + medial C8–T1) = C5–C8, T1 overall; no major branches in the arm (small articular twigs to the elbow joint may be given off). It supplies everything in the anterior forearm except FCU and the medial half of FDP (ulnar), plus the three thenar muscles and the two lateral lumbricals. The anterior interosseous nerve is purely motor: FPL, lateral FDP, pronator quadratus. The palmar branch leaves before the carpal tunnel and is therefore spared in carpal tunnel syndrome — the single most useful localising point. Recurrent branch: loops around the distal flexor retinaculum, so it is at risk in carpal tunnel release. Clinical / radiological correlation Carpal tunnel syndrome — compression of the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum: nocturnal burning pain and paraesthesia in the lateral three and a half digits, thenar wasting and loss of opposition; a positive Tinel sign on tapping over the retinaculum; treated by division of the flexor retinaculum. Supracondylar fracture of the humerus in children — the commonest high median lesion; the anterior interosseous branch is often the first to fail. Pronator syndrome — compression between the two heads of pronator teres or under the FDS arch; pain is provoked by resisted forearm pronation, and there is no thenar wasting until late. Ligament of Struthers — an occasional fibrous band from the humerus that compresses the nerve high in the arm, giving weakness of the forearm flexors and the thenar muscles. Wounds at the wrist — even a small laceration over the thenar crease may divide the recurrent branch and permanently abolish opposition. Likely follow-up questions Why is the hand of benediction a median, not an ulnar, sign? — Because the flexors of the index and middle fingers (FDS, lateral FDP) plus their lumbricals are median; the ulnar-supplied ring and little fingers still flex. How do you distinguish an anterior interosseous lesion from a carpal tunnel lesion? — AIN: pure motor, no sensory loss, weak pinch with a flat OK sign. Carpal tunnel: sensory loss plus thenar wasting, long flexors normal. Why is the central palm spared in carpal tunnel syndrome? — The palmar branch arises in the distal forearm and enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum. What does Tinel’s sign test? — Ectopic firing of regenerating or irritated axons: gentle percussion along the nerve reproduces the tingling in its territory."},{"id":"t-I-22","p":"I","n":22,"t":"Branches of the ulnar nerve, functional loss of the ulnar nerve.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The ulnar nerve (C8, T1, with a variable contribution from C7) is the terminal branch of the medial cord. It has no branch in the arm , gives muscular and cutaneous branches in the forearm (FCU, medial half of FDP, palmar and dorsal branches), and enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum and lateral to the pisiform, where it divides into a superficial (sensory) and a deep (motor) branch. It supplies nearly all intrinsic muscles of the hand, so its loss produces a claw hand , loss of finger abduction and adduction, and a very characteristic medial sensory loss. 1. Origin and course The ulnar nerve ( n. ulnaris ) arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8, T1, often with C7 via a communication from the lateral root of the median nerve). It enters the arm with the median nerve and axillary artery, runs medial to the axillary and brachial arteries, pierces the medial intermuscular septum in the middle of the arm, lies anterior to the medial head of triceps, and passes posterior to the medial epicondyle in the cubital tunnel. It then enters the anterior forearm between the humeral and ulnar heads of FCU, descends on the medial side between FCU and FDP under the lateral lip of the FCU tendon, and reaches the wrist lateral to the pisiform and superficial to the flexor retinaculum. 2. Branches Branch Level Root value Structures supplied Muscular branches Soon after entering the forearm C7, C8, T1 (FCU); C8, T1 (FDP) Flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (ring and little fingers) Palmar branch (ramus palmaris) Middle of the forearm C8, T1 Skin of the medial side of the palm Dorsal branch (ramus dorsalis) Distal forearm, deep to the FCU tendon C8, T1 Skin of the posteromedial hand and the dorsal surfaces of the medial one and a half digits Superficial branch Hand, distal to the pisiform C8, T1 Palmaris brevis and skin of the palmar little finger and medial half of the ring finger Deep (muscular) branch Hand; with the deep branch of the ulnar artery, through Guyon’s canal C8, T1 Hypothenar muscles (opponens, flexor, abductor digiti minimi), adductor pollicis, all dorsal and palmar interossei, the two medial lumbricals, and small articular branches to the wrist 3. Muscles supplied Muscle group Nerve branch Action lost Flexor carpi ulnaris Main trunk, forearm Wrist flexion and ulnar deviation Flexor digitorum profundus, medial half Main trunk, forearm Distal interphalangeal flexion of ring and little fingers Abductor, flexor and opponens digiti minimi Deep branch Abduction, flexion and opposition of the little finger Adductor pollicis Deep branch Adduction of the thumb; the pinch grip becomes weak Dorsal interossei (4) Deep branch Abduction of index, middle and ring fingers Palmar interossei (3) Deep branch Adduction of the fingers Third and fourth lumbricals Deep branch Flexion of MCP and extension of IP joints of ring and little fingers Palmaris brevis Superficial branch Deepening of the palmar cup (minor) 4. Functional loss — the two levels The ulnar nerve is most often injured where it is superficial: behind the medial epicondyle and at the wrist in Guyon’s canal. Because the forearm muscles are supplied above the wrist, a wrist lesion spares FCU and the medial half of FDP. Feature Lesion at the elbow Lesion at the wrist (Guyon) Forearm muscles FCU and medial FDP paralysed Intact Claw hand Present but less marked — the FDP of the ring and little fingers is also paralysed, so the distal interphalangeal joints cannot flex Most marked — the long flexors still flex the DIP joints while the intrinsic muscles cannot extend them, so the ring and little fingers are hyperextended at the MCP and flexed at the IP joints (ulnar claw) Hand muscles All ulnar-supplied intrinsic muscles lost All ulnar-supplied intrinsic muscles lost (deep branch) Sensory loss Palmar and dorsal medial one and a half digits (both branches affected) Palmar medial one and a half digits; the dorsal branch is spared if it arises above the lesion Localising test Guttering of the interosseous spaces and a hollow between the metacarpals Compare sensation on the dorsum of the little finger (dorsal branch) — spared in a low lesion 5. Signs on examination Clawing of the ring and little fingers (hyperextension at the MCP joints, flexion at the IP joints) — the “ulnar claw”. Loss of finger abduction and adduction — the patient cannot spread the fingers against resistance; the interosseous spaces look hollow (wasting of the dorsal interossei is visible and palpable on the dorsum). Froment’s sign — with loss of adductor pollicis the patient pinches paper by flexing the thumb IP joint (flexor pollicis longus, median nerve) instead of adducting the thumb. Little finger held abducted at rest ( Wartenberg's sign ): the extensor digiti minimi (radial nerve, still working) is unopposed once the ulnar-innervated palmar interossei and hypothenar muscles are lost — not because abductor digiti minimi is unopposed. Thenar eminence is spared (median nerve) — the contrast between a full thenar and a wasted hypothenar is diagnostic. Vasomotor changes and, in high lesions, wasting of the medial forearm. Must-memorise Ulnar nerve = C8, T1 (with occasional C7); medial cord; no branch in the arm . Forearm: FCU and medial half of FDP only. Hand: all intrinsic muscles except the three thenar muscles and the two lateral lumbricals. Runs behind the medial epicondyle (cubital tunnel) and superficial to the flexor retinaculum beside the pisiform. The deep branch passes through Guyon’s canal between the hook of the hamate and the flexor tendons — a site of entrapment by ganglia. Claw hand is worse with a wrist lesion than with an elbow lesion (the ulnar paradox) because the long flexors of the ring and little fingers still work. Clinical / radiological correlation Cubital tunnel syndrome — compression behind the medial epicondyle from leaning on the elbow or from a fracture; numbness of the little finger is often the first symptom, with wasting of the interossei later. Guyon’s canal syndrome — compression at the wrist by a ganglion, a hook-of-hamate fracture or prolonged pressure (cycling, crutch use): intrinsic muscle wasting with the long flexors intact. Fractures of the medial epicondyle and supracondylar fractures may stretch or entrap the nerve; the resulting deformity is the ulnar claw with sensory loss on the medial hand. Leprosy classically attacks the ulnar nerve at the elbow and produces the same claw with painless burns of the little finger. Lacerations at the wrist (suicide attempt, glass) divide the nerve just proximal to its bifurcation, combining the deep and superficial deficits. Likely follow-up questions Why is the little finger clawed more than the index? — The two lateral lumbricals (median) still extend the interphalangeal joints of the index and middle fingers, so the imbalance is greatest in the little and ring fingers. Name the two ulnar-innervated forearm muscles. — Flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (ring and little fingers). What is the ulnar paradox? — A more proximal lesion produces a less obvious claw, because the long flexors that would flex the distal interphalangeal joints are themselves paralysed. Which muscles of the hand are not ulnar? — Opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis (superficial head) and the first two lumbricals — all median."},{"id":"t-I-23","p":"I","n":23,"t":"Branches of the radial nerve, functional loss of the radial nerve.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The radial nerve (C5–C8, with an inconsistent T1 contribution) is the largest terminal branch of the posterior cord. It leaves the axilla through the triangular interval with the profunda brachii artery, spirals in the radial groove of the humerus directly on bone, crosses the lateral intermuscular septum into the anterior compartment, and divides at the lateral margin of the cubital fossa into a deep branch (which becomes the posterior interosseous nerve) and a superficial branch . It is the nerve supply of the entire extensor musculature of the upper limb, so its lesion produces wrist drop , finger drop and a small dorsolateral sensory loss. 1. Origin and course The radial nerve ( n. radialis ) arises from the posterior cord behind the third part of the axillary artery and enters the arm at the inferior margin of teres major. It passes through the triangular interval (inferior margin of teres major, lateral margin of the long head of triceps, humeral shaft) accompanied by the profunda brachii artery , then runs diagonally from medial to lateral in the radial groove between the medial and lateral heads of the triceps, tightly bound with the artery. On the lateral side of the arm it pierces the lateral intermuscular septum and enters the anterior compartment between brachialis and brachioradialis, then enters the forearm anterior to the lateral epicondyle, deep to brachioradialis, and divides into its two terminal branches under the margin of that muscle. 2. Branches Branch Level Root value Structures supplied Muscular branches to the triceps Axilla and radial groove C6, C7, C8 Long, lateral and medial heads of triceps brachii; one branch to the medial head arises before the nerve enters the posterior compartment Branch to anconeus Distal posterior arm C6, C7, C8 Anconeus (through the branch to the medial head of triceps) Posterior cutaneous n. of arm Axilla C5–C8 Skin of the posterior surface of the arm Inferior lateral cutaneous n. of arm Posterior compartment C5, C6 Skin of the lower lateral surface of the arm Muscular branches: brachioradialis, ECRL, lateral part of brachialis Distal arm / elbow C5, C6 (brachioradialis); C6, C7 (ECRL) Brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus, lateral part of brachialis Posterior cutaneous n. of forearm Posterior compartment of the arm; pierces the lateral head of triceps C5–C8 Skin of the posterior surface of the forearm Deep branch (ramus profundus) Under the margin of brachioradialis C7, C8 Passes between the two heads of supinator to supply it, then continues as the posterior interosseous nerve Posterior interosseous n. (n. interosseus posterior) After emerging from supinator C7, C8 Extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis Superficial branch (ramus superficialis) Under the margin of brachioradialis; runs with the radial artery then passes deep to the brachioradialis tendon C5–C8 Purely sensory: skin of the dorsolateral hand and the dorsal lateral three and a half digits to about the terminal interphalangeal joints; palpable as cords over the extensor pollicis longus tendon in the snuffbox 3. Muscles supplied and their action Muscle Branch Action lost Triceps brachii, anconeus Trunk and radial-groove branches Extension of the elbow; the arm cannot be held up against gravity Brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus Radial nerve proper before division Elbow flexion in midprone position; wrist extension with radial deviation (ECRL is spared in a posterior interosseous lesion) Extensor carpi radialis brevis Deep branch Wrist extension — its loss with the other wrist extensors gives wrist drop Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis, extensor digiti minimi Posterior interosseous Extension of the MCP joints and of the fingers Extensor carpi ulnaris Posterior interosseous Wrist extension with ulnar deviation Supinator Deep branch Supination (biceps still supinates with the elbow flexed) Abductor pollicis longus, extensors pollicis brevis and longus Posterior interosseous Extension and abduction of the thumb — the thumb falls into the palm 4. Functional loss — level by level Site of lesion Motor loss Sensory loss Axilla (crutch pressure, shoulder dislocation) All radial muscles including the triceps: complete wrist drop and finger drop with weak elbow extension Posterior arm and forearm, dorsolateral hand Radial groove / midshaft humerus (commonest) Triceps largely spared (its branches arise proximally); brachioradialis, supinator and all wrist and finger extensors lost → wrist drop , inability to extend the fingers and thumb, weak supination Reduced sensation over the dorsum of the hand, mainly the dorsolateral part and the dorsal lateral three and a half digits Elbow / posterior interosseous nerve No wrist drop — ECRL is supplied by the radial nerve proper, so wrist extension is retained (often with radial deviation) while the fingers and thumb cannot be extended; supinator may be spared Usually none , since the superficial branch is unaffected Superficial branch only (wrist) None Small area of numbness over the dorsolateral hand; of little functional consequence 5. The two classic pictures Wrist drop ( manus pendula ) — the hand hangs flexed at the wrist because all wrist extensors are paralysed; the fingers and thumb cannot be extended at the MCP joints. If the triceps is intact, elbow extension and the triceps jerk are preserved. Saturday-night palsy — prolonged compression of the radial nerve in the radial groove while the arm hangs over the back of a chair during sleep (often after alcohol); the classic cause of a painless wrist drop that recovers spontaneously over weeks. Finger drop with preserved wrist extension points to the posterior interosseous nerve, not to the radial groove — an important localising distinction. Trophic and vasomotor changes are mild; the sensory deficit is smaller than the motor deficit because the radial territory is mainly dorsal. Must-memorise Radial nerve = posterior cord, C5–C8 (T1); the largest terminal branch of the brachial plexus. Passes through the triangular interval with the profunda brachii artery and lies on bone in the radial groove — hence the risk in midshaft humeral fractures. It supplies all muscles of the posterior compartments of arm and forearm, and only skin in the hand. Terminal branches: deep (motor, becomes posterior interosseous) and superficial (sensory). Key localisers: triceps power (proximal lesion), wrist extension (ECRL spared distal to the elbow), sensory loss on the dorsum of the hand. Clinical / radiological correlation Midshaft fracture of the humerus — the nerve is tethered to bone by the profunda brachii artery, so it is stretched or divided; wrist drop and numbness on the dorsum of the hand must always be documented before and after reduction. Saturday-night palsy — a compression neuropathy with a good prognosis; management is splinting of the wrist and hand in extension and physiotherapy while re-myelination occurs. Posterior interosseous nerve entrapment at the arcade of Frohse or by a radial head dislocation (Monteggia injury) gives finger drop with a normal wrist extension and no sensory loss. Radial nerve block for surgery on the dorsum of the hand is placed in the axilla or at the elbow; the superficial branch can be blocked in the anatomical snuffbox. Likely follow-up questions Why is wrist extension preserved in a posterior interosseous nerve lesion? — Because extensor carpi radialis longus is innervated by the radial nerve proper, before it divides. Which muscle's paralysis causes the wrist to deviate radially when the patient attempts extension? — Loss of extensor carpi ulnaris (posterior interosseous nerve) with intact ECRL. How would you distinguish a radial nerve lesion from a C7 root lesion? — A root lesion also affects the triceps jerk, other C7 muscles and gives a dermatomal sensory loss; a radial nerve lesion affects all extensors below its site and has a peripheral sensory pattern. What are the cutaneous branches of the radial nerve? — Posterior cutaneous nerve of arm, inferior lateral cutaneous nerve of arm, posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm and the superficial branch."},{"id":"t-I-24","p":"I","n":24,"t":"Skin innervation of the upper limb.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The skin of the upper limb is innervated by two overlapping systems: the dermatomes , which are the segmental territories of the spinal nerves (C4–T2) distributed through the brachial plexus, and the cutaneous (peripheral) nerves , which are the named branches of the plexus and carry fibres of several segments. Because the two maps cut across each other, the pattern of sensory loss distinguishes a root lesion (dermatomal, “band-like”, with minimal overlap) from a peripheral nerve lesion (territory of one named nerve). 1. General principles Dermatome = the area of skin supplied by the sensory fibres of one spinal segment, i.e. one dorsal root and its spinal ganglion. Adjacent dermatomes overlap, so division of a single root causes only a narrow strip of sensory loss. Peripheral (cutaneous) nerve territory = the skin area supplied by one named nerve (e.g. lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm); it may be much larger than a dermatome and crosses several dermatome boundaries. The upper limb dermatomes are carried by the ventral rami of C5–T1 (plus C3, C4 for the shoulder and T2 for the axilla), all mixed in the brachial plexus. The dorsal rami supply only the paravertebral skin of the back — they do not reach the limb. Autonomic (sympathetic) fibres travel with every cutaneous nerve: vasomotor, sudomotor and pilomotor. Their loss explains the dry, warm, glossy skin of a nerve lesion. 2. Cutaneous nerves of the upper limb Cutaneous nerve Parent nerve / source Root value Skin area Supraclavicular nerves Cervical plexus C3, C4 Skin over the shoulder and the upper pectoral and scapular region (“cape” area) Superior lateral cutaneous n. of arm Axillary nerve C5, C6 Skin over the lower part of the deltoid and the upper lateral arm Posterior cutaneous n. of arm Radial nerve C5–C8 Posterior surface of the arm Inferior lateral cutaneous n. of arm Radial nerve C5, C6 Lower lateral surface of the arm Posterior cutaneous n. of forearm Radial nerve C5–C8 Posterior surface of the forearm Lateral cutaneous n. of forearm Terminal branch of the musculocutaneous nerve C5–C7 Anterolateral surface of the forearm Medial cutaneous n. of arm Medial cord C8, T1 Medial side of the distal third of the arm and the floor of the axilla Medial cutaneous n. of forearm Medial cord C8, T1 Medial (ulnar) surface of the forearm down to the wrist Intercostobrachial n. Lateral cutaneous branch of T2 (not from the plexus) T2 Skin of the axilla and the medial upper arm; communicates with the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm Superficial branch of the radial n. Radial nerve C5–C8 Dorsolateral hand and the dorsal surface of the lateral three and a half digits as far as the terminal interphalangeal joints Palmar branch of the median n. Median nerve (proximal to the flexor retinaculum) C8, T1 Skin over the base and centre of the palm Palmar digital nerves of the median n. Median nerve C8, T1 Palmar surface of the lateral three and a half digits and the nail beds of the same digits Palmar branch of the ulnar n. Ulnar nerve, forearm C8, T1 Medial side of the palm Dorsal branch of the ulnar n. Ulnar nerve, distal forearm C8, T1 Posteromedial surface of the back of the hand and the dorsal medial one and a half digits Superficial branch of the ulnar n. Ulnar nerve, palm C8, T1 Palmar little finger and medial half of the ring finger 3. Dermatomes of the upper limb The dermatomes run as oblique bands down the limb: C5–C6 on the lateral (preaxial) border, C7 down the middle, C8–T1 on the medial (postaxial) border. Areas of minimal overlap — the ones used for testing — are given in the table. Segment Dermatome Reliable test area (minimal overlap) C3, C4 Neck, supraclavicular fossa and shoulder Skin over the top of the shoulder C5 Lateral aspect of the arm and shoulder Upper lateral region of the arm over the deltoid C6 Lateral forearm and the thumb Palmar pad of the thumb C7 Middle of the palm and the index and middle fingers Pad of the index finger C8 Medial forearm and hand, ring and little fingers Pad of the little finger T1 Medial aspect of the arm and elbow Skin on the medial aspect of the elbow T2 Floor of the axilla and medial upper arm Axilla (intercostobrachial nerve) 4. Myotomes and reflexes (a segment test) Segment Movement tested Tendon reflex C5 Abduction of the arm at the glenohumeral joint Biceps reflex (C5–C6) C6 Flexion of the forearm at the elbow Biceps jerk (mainly C6) C7 Extension of the forearm at the elbow Triceps jerk (mainly C7) C8 Flexion of the fingers Finger flexor reflex T1 Abduction and adduction of the index, middle and ring fingers Interosseous wasting and finger abduction 5. Clinical use Root versus nerve: a C6 root lesion affects the thumb dermatome plus the biceps reflex and elbow flexion; a median nerve lesion at the wrist affects the palmar lateral three and a half digits plus the thenar muscles but spares the C6 dermatome of the thumb dorsum and the biceps jerk. Cervical radiculopathy from a disc herniation gives pain and tingling in one dermatome, aggravated by neck movement, with a reduced reflex of the same segment. Sparing patterns are diagnostic: in carpal tunnel syndrome the central palm (palmar branch) and the dorsal hand (superficial radial) are spared because those branches arise proximal to the compression. Peripheral neuropathy (diabetes) produces a glove-like loss, which crosses several dermatomes and nerve territories — unlike a root or a single nerve lesion. Must-memorise Upper limb dermatomes: C5 lateral arm, C6 thumb, C7 index, C8 little finger, T1 medial elbow. Cutaneous nerves and their parents: axillary → superior lateral cutaneous of arm; radial → posterior cutaneous of arm, inferior lateral cutaneous of arm, posterior cutaneous of forearm, superficial branch; musculocutaneous → lateral cutaneous of forearm; medial cord → medial cutaneous nerves of arm and forearm; T2 → intercostobrachial. Palmar lateral three and a half digits = median; palmar medial one and a half = ulnar; dorsal lateral three and a half = superficial radial; dorsal medial one and a half = dorsal branch of ulnar. Reflexes: biceps C6, triceps C7, finger flexors C8. Clinical / radiological correlation Testing sensation is done with cotton wool (light touch) and a pin (pain) in the exclusive zones, comparing both sides; vibration and joint position test the posterior columns. Axillary nerve injury after shoulder dislocation: a small numb patch over the lower deltoid is the only sensory clue. Medial cord lesions (e.g. thoracic outlet compression) numb the medial arm and forearm through the medial cutaneous nerves. Herpes zoster of a cervical root, or a thoracic root (intercostobrachial nerve) in the axilla, follows exactly one dermatome. Likely follow-up questions What is the difference between a dermatome and a cutaneous nerve territory? — A dermatome is a segmental (root) area with overlap; a cutaneous territory is the whole area of one named nerve, often spanning several dermatomes. Which two nerves supply the dorsum of the little finger? — The dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve; distally the palmar digital nerves also send dorsal branches to the nail bed. Why is the skin of the shoulder supplied from the neck? — The shoulder region derives from the same segmental level as the lower neck (C3–C4), so the supraclavicular nerves, not the brachial plexus, supply it. Which nerve supplies the skin of the axilla? — The intercostobrachial nerve (T2), which is the lateral cutaneous branch of the second intercostal nerve."},{"id":"t-I-25","p":"I","n":25,"t":"Axillary fossa, triangular and quadrangular axillary spaces.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The axillary fossa (axilla) is an irregularly shaped pyramidal space between the neck and the arm, with four walls, an inlet (apex) at the clavicle and first rib, and a floor of skin and fascia. All major structures passing into and out of the upper limb travel through it: the axillary artery and vein, the cords and branches of the brachial plexus, and the axillary lymph nodes. Openings between the muscles of its posterior wall — the quadrangular space , triangular space and triangular interval — provide the routes to the posterior scapular and deltoid regions. 1. Shape, inlet and floor The axilla is a pyramidal space : four sides, an inlet (apex) directed superiorly towards the neck, and a floor (base) directed inferiorly. Inlet (apex): margins are entirely bony — the posterior surface of the clavicle anteriorly, the lateral border of rib I medially and the superior border of the scapula (as far as the coracoid process) posteriorly; the apex itself points laterally and is formed by the medial aspect of the coracoid process. Floor: a dome of skin and deep fascia between the anterior and posterior axillary folds; the anterior fold lies more superiorly than the posterior. The lateral part of the floor opens into the arm. The axillary sheath (a prolongation of the prevertebral fascia) wraps the axillary artery, vein, cords and terminal branches. 2. Walls Wall Formed by Notes and surface landmark Anterior Pectoralis major (superficial); pectoralis minor, subclavius and the clavipectoral fascia (deep) The anterior axillary fold is the lower border of pectoralis major; the pectoralis minor crosses the axillary artery and divides it into three parts Posterior Subscapularis, teres major and latissimus dorsi, plus the long head of triceps running vertically through it The posterior axillary fold is formed by teres major laterally and latissimus dorsi medially; its muscles form the three gateways to the posterior scapular and deltoid regions Medial Upper thoracic wall (ribs and intercostal muscles) covered by serratus anterior The long thoracic nerve runs vertically on the superficial surface of serratus anterior, just anterior to the posterior axillary fold Lateral The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus, with coracobrachialis and the short head of biceps Narrow wall; the intertubercular sulcus transmits the long head of biceps 3. Contents Axillary artery — continuation of the subclavian at the lateral margin of rib I, becoming the brachial artery at the inferior margin of teres major. Divided by pectoralis minor into three parts: first (proximal), second (posterior to the muscle, related to the cords) and third (distal, related to the terminal branches). Axillary vein — medial and anterior to the artery; begins at the lower margin of teres major and becomes the subclavian vein at rib I. Brachial plexus — cords surround the second part of the artery; the terminal branches and their M-shaped union lie around the third part. Axillary lymph nodes — about 20–30 nodes in five groups (humeral, pectoral, subscapular, central, apical), draining the upper limb, the chest wall and most of the mammary gland. Muscles: the proximal parts of coracobrachialis and the short head of biceps brachii pass through the axilla; the long head of biceps lies in the intertubercular sulcus. Loose connective tissue and fat, which allow the neurovascular bundle to glide. 4. The three gateways of the posterior wall Gateway Boundaries Contents Quadrangular space (foramen quadrilaterum) Inferior margin of subscapularis; surgical neck of the humerus; superior margin of teres major; lateral margin of the long head of triceps Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery and vein Triangular space (foramen trilaterum) Medial margin of the long head of triceps; superior margin of teres major; inferior margin of subscapularis Circumflex scapular artery and vein — the scapular anastomotic pathway Triangular interval Lateral margin of the long head of triceps; shaft of the humerus; inferior margin of teres major Radial nerve and the profunda brachii artery , passing from the axilla into the posterior compartment of the arm 5. Clinical correlations Axillary lymph node dissection for breast cancer removes the nodes and may injure the long thoracic nerve (winged scapula) or the intercostobrachial nerve (numbness of the medial arm) and produces lymphoedema of the limb. Quadrangular space syndrome — hypertrophy or fibrosis of the boundary muscles compresses the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery, giving deep shoulder pain and deltoid weakness. Crutch palsy — pressure in the axilla on the radial nerve (or on the whole posterior cord) produces a wrist drop. Shoulder dislocation — the axillary nerve is stretched as it passes around the surgical neck, so deltoid function and the skin over the lower deltoid must be tested before and after reduction. Axillary artery is compressed between the clavicle and rib I in the costoclavicular space (thoracic outlet syndrome) — the pulse may disappear on arm abduction. Must-memorise Axilla = four walls + bony inlet + skin floor; inlet margins: clavicle (anterior), lateral border of rib I (medial), superior border of scapula to the coracoid (posterior). Anterior wall: pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, subclavius, clavipectoral fascia. Posterior wall: subscapularis, teres major, latissimus dorsi, long head of triceps. Quadrangular space = axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral vessels. Triangular space = circumflex scapular vessels. Triangular interval = radial nerve + profunda brachii artery. Axillary artery parts are separated by pectoralis minor; the cord names follow the second part. Clinical / radiological correlation Sentinel node biopsy uses the lymphatic drainage of the mammary gland through the pectoral (anterior) group of axillary nodes — the anatomical basis of axillary staging. Axillary vein puncture for central lines uses the vein in the axilla; the axillary sheath explains why arterial and venous bleeding in this region is poorly contained. MRI and CT show the axilla as a fat-filled space; the cords and axillary artery stand out against the fat, and lymph nodes larger than normal are easily identified. Likely follow-up questions Which structure divides the axillary artery into three parts? — The pectoralis minor muscle. What is the difference between the triangular space and the triangular interval? — Both are bordered by the long head of triceps and teres major; the triangular space is closed superiorly by subscapularis and transmits the circumflex scapular vessels, whereas the triangular interval is closed laterally by the humerus and transmits the radial nerve and profunda brachii artery. Which nerve is at risk during axillary surgery and what is the consequence? — The long thoracic nerve; serratus anterior paralysis gives a winged scapula."},{"id":"t-I-26","p":"I","n":26,"t":"Topography of the volar and dorsal wrist regions: tendons, tendon sheaths, osteofibrous compartments, vessels and nerves. The carpal tunnel.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer At the wrist the carpal bones form a deep carpal arch whose medial and lateral prominences are spanned by the flexor retinaculum , converting the arch into the carpal tunnel (long flexor tendons plus the median nerve) and, on the medial side, the ulnar canal (ulnar nerve and artery). On the dorsum the extensor retinaculum holds the extensor tendons in six osteofibrous compartments , each with its own synovial sheath. The tunnels and compartments explain the entrapment syndromes of the wrist. 1. Bony framework: the carpal arch The carpal arch is open anteriorly (palmar). Its medial base is the pisiform and the hook of the hamate ; its lateral base is the tubercles of the scaphoid and trapezium . The proximal carpal row is formed by the scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum and pisiform; the distal row by the trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate. Palpable landmarks at the wrist: pisiform (medial), tubercle of the scaphoid (lateral, in the floor of the snuffbox), styloid processes of radius and ulna. 2. Volar (anterior) wrist: fascial layers and compartments Structure Attachments and relations Contents / note Palmar carpal ligament Thickening of the antebrachial fascia, superficial to the flexor retinaculum Forms the roof of the ulnar canal (Guyon’s tunnel) ; the ulnar nerve and artery enter the palm deep to it Flexor retinaculum (retinaculum flexorum) Bridges the carpal arch: laterally the tubercles of scaphoid and trapezium, medially the pisiform and hook of hamate Roof of the carpal tunnel; converts the arch into an osteofibrous compartment and prevents bowing of the tendons Carpal tunnel Between the flexor retinaculum and the floor of the carpal arch 4 FDS tendons, 4 FDP tendons, FPL tendon and the median nerve Compartment of the FCR tendon Tubular passage between the lateral part of the retinaculum and a groove on the trapezium Flexor carpi radialis tendon with its own synovial sheath — outside the carpal tunnel Ulnar canal (Guyon) Roof: palmar carpal ligament and palmaris brevis; floor: flexor retinaculum; walls: pisiform medially, hook of hamate laterally Ulnar nerve and ulnar artery — outside the carpal tunnel Structures passing superficial to the retinaculum — Ulnar artery, ulnar nerve and the tendon of palmaris longus (which has no synovial sheath) 3. Synovial sheaths of the flexor tendons All four FDP tendons and all four FDS tendons are enclosed in a single common synovial sheath within the carpal tunnel. A separate sheath surrounds the tendon of flexor pollicis longus. The median nerve lies anterior (superficial) to the tendons in the tunnel, immediately under the retinaculum — hence its vulnerability to raised pressure. In the digits the tendons continue in the fibrous digital sheaths with their annular and cruciate pulleys (anti-bowstringing mechanism) and a synovial sheath; the sheath of the little finger and of the thumb may communicate with the common carpal sheath. 4. Dorsal wrist: the extensor retinaculum and six compartments The extensor retinaculum ( retinaculum extensorum ) stretches across the back of the wrist and holds the extensor tendons against the bones; its septa divide the space into six osteofibrous compartments , each lined by a synovial sheath. No. Tendons in the compartment Note 1 Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis Over the radial styloid; their tendons form the lateral border of the anatomical snuffbox 2 Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis Over the distal radius; their tendons form part of the floor of the snuffbox 3 Extensor pollicis longus Passes around the dorsal tubercle of the radius; forms the medial border of the snuffbox 4 Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis The largest compartment, sharing one sheath 5 Extensor digiti minimi Separate compartment medial to the fourth 6 Extensor carpi ulnaris In the groove between the head and styloid process of the ulna 5. Vessels and nerves at the wrist Radial artery — lies immediately lateral to the FCR tendon in the distal forearm, then passes dorsally around the lateral side of the wrist over the scaphoid and through the anatomical snuffbox to reach the deep palm. It gives a palmar carpal branch, a dorsal carpal branch and the superficial palmar branch. Ulnar artery — runs under the lateral lip of the FCU tendon, enters the hand lateral to the pisiform and superficial to the flexor retinaculum, and forms the superficial palmar arch. Median nerve — lies between the tendons of palmaris longus and FCR just above the wrist, then enters the carpal tunnel. Ulnar nerve — medial to the ulnar artery, passes superficial to the retinaculum into the ulnar canal. Superficial veins : the cephalic vein begins at the lateral end of the dorsal venous network and crosses the snuffbox; the basilic vein begins medially. Cutaneous nerves: the superficial branch of the radial nerve and the dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve cross the dorsum; the palmar branch of the median and the palmar branch of the ulnar supply the volar skin. 6. Carpal tunnel syndrome Mechanism: any increase in the volume of the tunnel contents or decrease in its size raises the pressure, causing venous congestion, oedema and ischaemic damage of the median nerve. Causes: overuse, swelling of the tendons and tendon sheaths (rheumatoid arthritis), cysts arising from the carpal joints, ganglion, previous wrist fracture, pregnancy-related fluid retention. Symptoms: burning pain and pins-and-needles in the median distribution (lateral three and a half digits), often nocturnal and waking the patient; later weakness and wasting of the thenar muscles with loss of opposition. Signs: Tinel sign on tapping over the flexor retinaculum; sensory loss in the median digits with sparing of the central palm (palmar branch arises proximally); thenar wasting. Investigation and treatment: nerve conduction studies to confirm entrapment; initial conservative treatment of the inflammation and removal of repetitive insults, then surgical decompression of the flexor retinaculum. Must-memorise Carpal tunnel contents: 9 tendons + 1 nerve — 4 FDS, 4 FDP, 1 FPL and the median nerve. Outside the tunnel: FCR (own compartment), ulnar artery and nerve (ulnar canal), palmaris longus tendon (superficial, no sheath). Retinaculum attachments: scaphoid and trapezium laterally; pisiform and hook of hamate medially. Six dorsal compartments in order (radial to ulnar): APL+EPB, ECRL+ECRB, EPL, ED+EI, EDM, ECU. Two synovial sheaths in the carpal tunnel: one common for FDS and FDP, one for FPL. Clinical / radiological correlation Thenar wasting is a late sign; sensory symptoms precede it, so early decompression prevents permanent loss of opposition. Recurrent branch injury during retinaculum release abolishes opposition while leaving sensation intact — a well-known surgical complication, since the branch curves around the distal edge of the retinaculum. Guyon’s canal syndrome at the same level gives ulnar intrinsic wasting with no sensory loss on the dorsum if the dorsal branch is spared. De Quervain tenosynovitis — stenosing inflammation of the first dorsal compartment (APL and EPB) gives pain over the radial styloid on thumb movement. Radiology: MRI of the wrist in the axial plane shows the retinaculum, the tendons and the median nerve; the nerve is flattened in carpal tunnel syndrome. Likely follow-up questions Why is the median nerve the most vulnerable structure in the carpal tunnel? — It is the most superficial structure under the retinaculum and has no sheath or tendon to cushion it. Where does the ulnar nerve run relative to the carpal tunnel? — Superficial to the flexor retinaculum, in the ulnar canal, between the pisiform and the hook of the hamate. Which tendon has its own compartment outside the carpal tunnel? — The flexor carpi radialis tendon, in a groove on the trapezium. Which compartment of the extensor retinaculum is affected in De Quervain disease? — The first, containing abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis."},{"id":"t-I-27","p":"I","n":27,"t":"Dorsum of the hand. The anatomical snuffbox (foveola radialis).","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The dorsum of the hand is covered by thin skin and loose subcutaneous tissue containing the dorsal venous network (origin of the cephalic and basilic veins) and the terminal branches of the superficial radial and dorsal ulnar nerves. Deep to the extensor tendons lie the dorsal interossei . Between the tendons of the thumb, at the base of the first metacarpal, is the triangular anatomical snuffbox ( foveola radialis ), whose floor is the scaphoid and trapezium and which transmits the radial artery — the region is examined clinically for scaphoid fracture and for the radial pulse. 1. Skin and superficial fascia The skin is thin, loosely attached and hair-bearing, with the subcutaneous tissue arranged in loose areolar planes — hence the easy spread of oedema and the mobility of the skin. Dorsal venous network lies over the metacarpals; its lateral end continues as the cephalic vein (crossing the anatomical snuffbox into the forearm) and its medial end as the basilic vein . Cutaneous nerves: the dorsolateral area from the superficial branch of the radial nerve , the dorsomedial area from the dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve ; both give dorsal digital nerves that reach approximately the terminal interphalangeal joints, the nail beds being supplied by dorsal branches of the palmar digital nerves. Lymphatics of the dorsum run with the cephalic and basilic veins to the axillary and, for the medial side, sometimes the infraclavicular nodes. 2. Deep fascia and the extensor apparatus The extensor tendons, held in their six compartments at the wrist, fan out over the metacarpals, interconnected by oblique intertendinous connections on the dorsum — which is why the fingers tend to move together in extension. Over each MCP joint the extensor tendon expands into the triangular dorsal digital expansion (extensor hood) : its apex attaches to the distal phalanx, its central part to the middle phalanx (index to little fingers) or proximal phalanx (thumb), and its base wraps the sides of the MCP joint and attaches mainly to the deep transverse metacarpal ligaments. The lumbricals, interossei and abductor digiti minimi insert into the free margins of the hoods, and in the thumb the adductor pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis do so. Through the hood the intrinsic muscles can flex the MCP joint while extending the interphalangeal joints — the mechanism of the “intrinsic-plus” hand. Extensor digiti minimi, extensor indicis and extensor pollicis brevis also join the hoods of their digits. 3. Muscles and deep structures of the dorsum Structure Features Dorsal interossei (4) Bipennate muscles between the shafts of adjacent metacarpals; the most dorsal of all intrinsic muscles and palpable through the skin. All insert into the base of the proximal phalanx and the extensor hood. First = largest, to the lateral side of the index; second and third to the middle finger; fourth to the medial side of the ring finger. They abduct the index, middle and ring fingers. Nerve: deep branch of ulnar (C8, T1). Extensor tendons Extensor digitorum to digits 2–5, extensor indicis and extensor digiti minimi as accessory extensors; extensor pollicis longus and brevis and abductor pollicis longus for the thumb Metacarpals and interosseous spaces Second to fifth metacarpals; the spaces between them are occupied by the dorsal interossei and are hollowed in ulnar nerve lesions (guttering) Deep transverse metacarpal ligaments Connect the heads of metacarpals II–V and give attachment to the extensor hoods and the palmar plates 4. Vessels of the dorsum The dorsal carpal arch is formed by the dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery and the dorsal carpal branch of the ulnar artery; it lies on the carpal bones deep to the extensor tendons. From it arise the dorsal metacarpal arteries , which divide into dorsal digital arteries for the adjacent sides of the fingers; they anastomose with dorsal branches of the proper palmar digital arteries. The first dorsal metacarpal artery arises from the radial artery before it penetrates the back of the hand and supplies the adjacent sides of the thumb and index finger. Deep veins follow the arteries; the superficial dorsal venous network is the clinically important venous access on the dorsum. 5. The anatomical snuffbox (foveola radialis) The snuffbox is the triangular depression on the posterolateral side of the wrist and base of the first metacarpal, bounded by the tendons of the thumb; it is most obvious when the thumb is extended and abducted. The base of the triangle is at the wrist and the apex points into the thumb. Feature Structures Lateral border Tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis (first dorsal compartment) Medial border Tendon of extensor pollicis longus (third dorsal compartment) Floor Scaphoid and trapezium , with the distal ends of the tendons of extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis Contents Radial artery passing obliquely through, deep to the thumb tendons and adjacent to the scaphoid and trapezium; terminal parts of the superficial branch of the radial nerve running subcutaneously; the origin of the cephalic vein from the dorsal venous network 6. Clinical importance Scaphoid fracture — tenderness in the snuffbox after a fall on the outstretched hand. The scaphoid is best palpated within the snuffbox when the hand is in ulnar deviation. Because its blood supply enters distally and runs retrogradely, a fracture through the waist risks avascular necrosis of the proximal pole and non-union. Radial pulse — the radial artery can be felt in the floor of the snuffbox in the relaxed hand, and its terminal branches can be rolled against the extensor pollicis longus tendon. Venous access — the cephalic vein adjacent to the snuffbox is a preferred site for a short-term intravenous cannula. Superficial radial nerve — its branches lie subcutaneously over the snuffbox and can be damaged by tight wristbands or cannulation, producing numbness of the dorsolateral hand. Kienböck disease — avascular necrosis of the lunate, a neighbouring carpal bone, shows sclerosis of the lunate on radiographs and causes wrist pain and stiffness. Must-memorise Snuffbox: lateral border = APL + EPB; medial border = EPL; floor = scaphoid + trapezium (+ ECRL/ECRB tendons); content = radial artery, superficial radial nerve, cephalic vein. Dorsal interossei (4) abduct the index, middle and ring fingers; all intrinsic muscles of the hand are ulnar except the three thenar muscles and two lateral lumbricals (median). Cephalic vein from the lateral end of the dorsal venous network; basilic vein from the medial end. Dorsal cutaneous supply: superficial radial nerve laterally, dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve medially. The extensor hood (dorsal digital expansion) is the insertion site of the intrinsic muscles and the mechanism of IP joint extension. Clinical / radiological correlation Scaphoid fracture is notoriously occult on early radiographs; snuffbox tenderness with a normal film is treated as a fracture until proven otherwise (repeat views, CT or MRI). Avascular necrosis of the proximal scaphoid pole, and of the lunate (Kienböck), are the classic examples of retrograde intra-osseous blood supply in the carpus. Guttering of the interosseous spaces and a hollow dorsum are early signs of wasting of the dorsal interossei in ulnar nerve lesions — best seen and palpated on the dorsum, not the palm. Extensor tendon injury over the dorsum (laceration, mallet finger) must be tested by asking the patient to extend each finger against resistance at the MCP joint. Likely follow-up questions Why is a scaphoid fracture so important? — Its blood supply enters distally, so a waist fracture can cut off the proximal pole and cause avascular necrosis and non-union. What lies in the floor of the snuffbox? — The scaphoid and trapezium, with the tendons of ECRL and ECRB distally. Which structures cross the snuffbox superficially? — The terminal branches of the superficial branch of the radial nerve and the origin of the cephalic vein. Why does a lesion of the dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve not affect the little finger tip? — The distal pulp is supplied by palmar digital nerves, which send dorsal branches to the nail bed."},{"id":"t-I-28","p":"I","n":28,"t":"Palm of the hand: muscles, fasciae, compartments, vessels and nerves.","u":"part-1.html","x":"Core answer The palm is a layered region: skin, superficial fascia, the palmar aponeurosis , then the superficial palmar arch with the digital nerves, the flexor tendons with the lumbricals, the deep palmar arch with the deep branch of the ulnar nerve, and finally the interossei and adductor pollicis against the metacarpals. Its muscles are the thenar , hypothenar and metacarpal (lumbrical and interosseous) groups, supplied by the median and ulnar nerves; the blood supply comes from the superficial (ulnar) and deep (radial) palmar arches. 1. Fasciae of the palm Palmar aponeurosis ( aponeurosis palmaris ) — a triangular thickening of the deep fascia over the palm: its apex is continuous with the palmaris longus tendon (or anchored to the flexor retinaculum) and its fibres radiate to the bases of the digits, with transverse fibres interconnecting them. It is firmly attached to the skin distally, and the vessels, nerves and long flexor tendons lie deep to it. Palmaris brevis — a small quadrangular subcutaneous muscle over the hypothenar eminence, from the palmar aponeurosis and flexor retinaculum to the dermis of the medial margin; it deepens the cup of the palm and improves grip. Nerve: superficial branch of the ulnar (C8, T1). Fibrous digital sheaths — begin anterior to the MCP joints and extend to the distal phalanges, holding the flexor tendons against the phalanges; the annular (A1–A5) and cruciate (C1–C3) pulleys prevent bowstringing. Their synovial sheaths allow frictionless gliding and are the site of flexor tenosynovitis. Deep fascia of the palm sends lateral and medial septa to the third metacarpal and to the fifth metacarpal, dividing the palm into fascial compartments. 2. Layers of the palm (superficial to deep) Layer Contents 1 Skin, with its dense fibrous attachments to the palmar aponeurosis (skin creases, glabrous skin with thick keratin) 2 Palmar aponeurosis and palmaris brevis 3 Superficial palmar arch and the common and proper palmar digital nerves 4 Flexor tendons (FDS then FDP) and the lumbrical muscles 5 Deep palmar arch and the deep branch of the ulnar nerve 6 Interosseous muscles and the adductor pollicis , resting on the metacarpals 3. Muscles of the palm Muscle Origin Insertion Nerve Action Thenar group (median nerve, recurrent branch, C8, T1) Abductor pollicis brevis Scaphoid and trapezium tubercles, flexor retinaculum Lateral side of base of proximal phalanx of thumb, extensor hood Median (recurrent br.), C8, T1 Abducts the thumb at the MCP joint Flexor pollicis brevis Trapezium tubercle and flexor retinaculum; deep head from capitate and trapezium Lateral side of base of proximal phalanx of thumb (often with a sesamoid) Median, superficial head; deep head often by deep branch of ulnar nerve, C8, T1 Flexes the thumb at the MCP joint Opponens pollicis Trapezium tubercle and flexor retinaculum Lateral border and lateral palmar surface of metacarpal I Median (recurrent br.), C8, T1 Rotates metacarpal I towards the palm = opposition Hypothenar group (deep branch of ulnar nerve, C8, T1) Abductor digiti minimi Pisiform, pisohamate ligament, tendon of FCU Medial side of base of proximal phalanx of little finger, extensor hood Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Abducts the little finger Flexor digiti minimi brevis Hook of hamate and flexor retinaculum Medial side of base of proximal phalanx of little finger Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Flexes the little finger at the MCP joint Opponens digiti minimi Hook of hamate and flexor retinaculum Medial aspect of metacarpal V Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Rotates metacarpal V towards the palm Metacarpal (central) group Lumbricals (4) Tendons of flexor digitorum profundus (unipennate 1st and 2nd, bipennate 3rd and 4th) Extensor hoods of digits 2–5 1st and 2nd: median (digital branches); 3rd and 4th: ulnar (deep br.); C8, T1 Flex the MCP joints while extending the IP joints Dorsal interossei (4) Adjacent sides of the shafts of two metacarpals Base of proximal phalanx and extensor hood of index, middle and ring fingers Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Abduct the index, middle and ring fingers; flex MCP, extend IP joints Palmar interossei (3–4) Palmar surfaces of metacarpals Base of proximal phalanx and extensor hood of thumb, index, ring and little fingers Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Adduct the fingers; flex MCP, extend IP joints Adductor pollicis Transverse head: metacarpal III; oblique head: capitate and bases of metacarpals II–III Base of proximal phalanx and extensor hood of thumb Ulnar, deep br., C8, T1 Adducts and opposes the thumb in gripping Palmaris brevis Palmar aponeurosis and flexor retinaculum Dermis of the medial margin of the hand Ulnar, superficial br., C8, T1 Deepens the palmar cup, improves grip 4. Compartments and fascial spaces of the palm Thenar compartment — the three thenar muscles, separated by the lateral septum; it is the site of thenar wasting in median nerve lesions. Hypothenar compartment — the three hypothenar muscles, medial to the medial septum. Central (midpalmar) compartment — the flexor tendons, their sheaths and the lumbricals, between the two septa. Adductor compartment — adductor pollicis, deepest and most lateral, against metacarpal III. Interosseous compartment — the interossei between the metacarpals, closed by the deep transverse metacarpal ligaments and the palmar fascia. Between these compartments run the thenar (lateral) and midpalmar (medial) fascial spaces , potential spaces deep to the flexor tendons which become the site of pus from a penetrating injury or a suppurative tenosynovitis; because they communicate with the digital sheaths, infection can spread proximally into the forearm through the carpal tunnel. 5. Vessels of the palm Arch Main vessel Course and branches Superficial palmar arch Mainly the ulnar artery , completed laterally by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery Lies superficial to the long flexor tendons and just deep to the palmar aponeurosis; gives a proper palmar digital artery to the medial side of the little finger and three common palmar digital arteries, which join palmar metacarpal arteries and divide into proper palmar digital arteries Deep palmar arch Mainly the radial artery , completed medially by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery Passes between the metacarpals and the long flexor tendons; gives three palmar metacarpal arteries and three perforating branches that anastomose with dorsal metacarpal arteries, plus the princeps pollicis and radialis indicis arteries Supply: mainly the medial three and a half digits from the ulnar side and the thumb with the lateral side of the index from the radial side — the anatomical basis of Allen’s test for the adequacy of the anastomosis between the two arches. Pulse points: the ulnar artery is lateral to the pisiform; the deep arch is not palpable. 6. Nerves of the palm Median nerve — enters through the carpal tunnel, gives the recurrent branch to the thenar muscles and the palmar digital nerves to the lateral three and a half digits; the digital nerves also supply the first and second lumbricals. Ulnar nerve — enters superficial to the flexor retinaculum, divides at the pisiform into the superficial branch (palmaris brevis and skin of the medial one and a half digits) and the deep branch (hypothenar muscles, adductor pollicis, all interossei, the two medial lumbricals, and articular branches to the wrist). Radial nerve — only the superficial branch reaches the hand, and it supplies skin (dorsolateral) only. Communicating branches between the median and ulnar nerves in the palm are common, which makes the territory of the superficial ulnar branch variable. Digital anaesthesia (Oberst block) — the palmar digital nerves run on the sides of the digits, so a local anaesthetic is injected at the base of the finger on both sides, blocking the whole digit distal to the injection. Must-memorise All intrinsic muscles of the hand are ulnar except the three thenar muscles and the two lateral lumbricals (median) — and the deep head of flexor pollicis brevis may also be ulnar. Thenar = abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis. Hypothenar = abductor, flexor and opponens digiti minimi. Lumbricals: lateral two median, medial two ulnar. Interossei: all ulnar. Dorsal interossei abduct (DAB), palmar interossei adduct (PAD). Superficial arch mainly ulnar (superficial to flexor tendons); deep arch mainly radial (deep to flexor tendons). Six layers of the palm: skin → palmar aponeurosis → superficial arch and digital nerves → flexor tendons and lumbricals → deep arch and deep ulnar nerve → interossei and adductor pollicis. Clinical / radiological correlation Dupuytren contracture — degenerative thickening of the palmar aponeurosis, most often affecting the fibres to the ring and little fingers, pulls them into fixed flexion and requires surgical removal of the abnormal tissue. Palmar space infection — pus in the thenar or midpalmar space tracks along the flexor sheaths through the carpal tunnel into the forearm; the hand is held in a characteristic posture with loss of the palmar concavity. Combined ulnar and median nerve lesions cause atrophy of all the small muscles of the hand, with a flat, “skeleton” hand and clawing of all digits. Allen’s test — both arteries are compressed at the wrist and released one at a time to check the anastomoses of the arches before harvesting the radial artery or cannulating it. Penetrating wounds of the palm may divide the recurrent branch of the median nerve or the deep branch of the ulnar nerve, leaving a small skin wound but a permanent loss of opposition or of intrinsic muscle function. Likely follow-up questions Which muscles form the thenar eminence and which nerve supplies them? — Abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis; the recurrent branch of the median nerve (C8, T1). What is the action of the lumbricals and why is it special? — They flex the MCP joints while extending the interphalangeal joints, because they insert into the extensor hood — an action no other muscle can produce. How do you test the deep branch of the ulnar nerve? — Test finger abduction (dorsal interossei) and the pinch grip (adductor pollicis, Froment’s sign); the hypothenar muscles and interosseous wasting appear early. What is the anatomical basis of Dupuytren contracture? — Fibrosis of the palmar aponeurosis, mainly of the longitudinal bands to the fourth and fifth digits, which tethers the MCP and PIP joints in flexion. Where does the palmaris longus insert and what happens if it is absent? — Into the palmar aponeurosis (with the flexor retinaculum); it is absent in part of the population and has no functional consequence."},{"id":"t-II-1","p":"II","n":1,"t":"The bony pelvis: hip bone, sacrum and coccyx.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The bony pelvis ( pelvis ossea ) is a ring formed by the two hip bones ( os coxae ), the sacrum and the coccyx , united by the two sacroiliac joints and the pubic symphysis, and completed by the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments. Each hip bone is a composite of three bones — ilium, ischium and pubis — which meet in the acetabulum and fuse there during puberty and early adult life. The sacrum is five fused sacral vertebrae; the coccyx is three to four rudimentary vertebrae. The ring transmits the weight of the trunk to the lower limbs, protects the pelvic viscera and gives attachment to the muscles of the trunk, gluteal region and thigh. 1. General plan, orientation and functions The pelvis is the part of the trunk below the abdomen. Its skeleton is a bony ring : behind, the sacrum and coccyx are wedged between the two hip bones; in front the hip bones meet at the pubic symphysis; the ring is closed posterolaterally by the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments , which convert the sciatic notches of the hip bone into the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. The internal (pelvic) surfaces face the pelvic cavity, the external surfaces belong to the gluteal region of the lower limb. In the anatomical position the pelvis is oriented so that the anterior superior iliac spines and the front edge of the top of the pubic symphysis lie in the same vertical plane. Therefore the plane of the pelvic inlet is tilted and faces anterosuperiorly, while the bodies of the pubic bones and the pubic arch lie almost horizontally, facing the ground. This orientation explains why the inlet is a sloping plane (see II.2) and why the pelvic floor is concave downwards. Transmission of load: body weight passes through the sacrum to the acetabula and the femora; in sitting, through the ischial tuberosities. Protection: of the bladder, rectum and genital organs. Attachment: abdominal wall, back and gluteal muscles, thigh muscles, pelvic floor and perineal muscles. Obstetric function: the bony birth canal is the least distensible part of the passage of the fetus. 2. The hip bone (os coxae) The hip bone is a flat, irregular bone formed by the fusion of three primary bones. At birth the three parts are separate and meet in the triradiate cartilage inside the acetabulum; fusion starts in puberty and is completed in early adult life, leaving the acetabular floor as a thin plate of bone. The ilium lies superiorly, the pubis anteroinferiorly and the ischium posteroinferiorly. Part Main features Principal attachments Ilium os ilium Body (lower, forms the upper acetabulum) and ala. Iliac crest with outer and inner lips and an intermediate line; anterior superior and anterior inferior iliac spines; posterior superior and posterior inferior iliac spines; gluteal surface with inferior, anterior and posterior gluteal lines; iliac fossa; auricular surface; iliac tuberosity; arcuate line Iliac crest and gluteal lines — abdominal wall muscles and gluteus maximus, medius and minimus; iliac fossa — iliacus; anterior superior iliac spine — sartorius and inguinal ligament; anterior inferior iliac spine — rectus femoris (straight head); auricular surface and iliac tuberosity — sacroiliac ligaments Ischium os ischii Body (lower and posterior acetabulum), ramus, ischial spine, lesser sciatic notch, ischial tuberosity divided into upper and lower areas Ischial tuberosity — semitendinosus and long head of biceps femoris (upper medial part), semimembranosus (lateral part), adductor magnus (lower lateral part), sacrotuberous ligament (medial ridge); ischial spine — sacrospinous ligament and gemellus superior; ramus — adductor brevis and gracilis Pubis os pubis Body, superior ramus (with pecten pubis, pubic crest, pubic tubercle, iliopubic eminence), inferior ramus, symphyseal surface, obturator groove Pubic tubercle and crest — inguinal ligament, rectus abdominis, conjoined tendon; pectineal line — pectineus; inferior ramus — adductors and gracilis; bodies and rami — obturator externus and internus around the obturator foramen The margins of the hip bone are important landmarks: the superior margin is the iliac crest; the anterior margin runs from the anterior superior iliac spine through the iliopubic eminence and the pubic crest to the symphysis; the posterior margin runs from the posterior superior iliac spine through the greater sciatic notch, the ischial spine and the lesser sciatic notch to the ischial tuberosity; the inferior margin is the ischiopubic ramus, which arches around the obturator foramen. 3. The acetabulum and the obturator foramen The acetabulum is the cup-shaped socket on the lateral surface of the hip bone, formed where ilium, ischium and pubis fuse. Its wall has a nonarticular and an articular part. The nonarticular part is the rough acetabular fossa in the central and inferior part of the floor, continuous with the acetabular notch at the lower margin of the cup. The articular part is the smooth crescent-shaped lunate surface , broadest superiorly where most body weight is transmitted; it is deficient inferiorly at the notch. The notch is bridged by the transverse acetabular ligament, which turns it into the acetabular foramen. The fossa gives attachment to the ligament of the head of the femur; vessels and nerves reach the joint through the notch. The obturator foramen is a large, roughly oval opening enclosed by the pubis and ischium. It is closed by the obturator membrane except at its superolateral corner, where a gap between the membrane and the obturator groove of the superior pubic ramus forms the obturator canal for the obturator nerve and vessels. The membrane gives origin to the obturator internus (deep surface) and obturator externus (external surface) muscles. 4. The sacrum The sacrum ( os sacrum ) is a single wedge-shaped bone formed by the fusion of five sacral vertebrae; it is the keystone of the pelvic ring. Its base faces upwards and articulates with the fifth lumbar vertebra through the L5/S1 intervertebral disc and the superior articular processes. The anterior projecting upper margin of the base is the sacral promontory , an important obstetric landmark. The lateral expansions of the base are the alae , which carry the auricular surfaces for the sacroiliac joints. The apex faces downwards and articulates with the coccyx. Pelvic surface: concave, smooth, with the four pairs of anterior sacral foramina for the anterior rami of S1–S4 and the lateral sacral vessels; the ridges between them are fused intervertebral discs. Dorsal surface: convex, with the median sacral crest (fused spinous processes), the paired lateral sacral crests (fused transverse processes) and the four pairs of posterior sacral foramina . Sacral canal: the continuation of the vertebral canal; it ends below as the sacral hiatus , where the laminae of the lowest sacral vertebra are missing. Auricular surface: the ear-shaped articular area on the lateral part, facing the ilium; behind it is the rough sacral tuberosity for the interosseous sacroiliac ligaments. Lateral margin: forms part of the margin of the greater sciatic foramen and gives attachment to the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments. The number of sacral vertebrae may vary: the first sacral vertebra can remain separate from the rest ( lumbarisation of S1), or the fifth lumbar vertebra may be fused to the sacrum ( sacralisation of L5). Such variants alter the radiological count of vertebrae and the apparent level of the sacroiliac joints. The sacral hiatus is the route of caudal epidural anaesthesia ; it is closed by the sacrococcygeal membrane and flanked by the sacral cornua. 5. The coccyx The coccyx ( os coccygis ) is the remains of the tail: three to four rudimentary vertebrae, usually fused into one bone. Its base bears the coccygeal cornua , which articulate with the sacral cornua, and transverse processes; its apex is free. The first coccygeal vertebra may remain separate. The coccyx gives attachment to the anococcygeal ligament, the levator ani and coccygeus muscles, part of the gluteus maximus and the sacrotuberous ligament. It is freely movable and is pushed backwards during defecation and childbirth; a fall onto the buttocks may fracture or dislocate it, causing coccygodynia. Feature Female pelvis Male pelvis False (greater) pelvis Wide and shallow Narrow and deep Pelvic inlet (brim) Transversely oval or circular Heart-shaped, prominent promontory Pelvic outlet Roomy, rounded Narrow, oblong Pelvic cavity Roomy and shallow Narrow and deep Ischial tuberosities Everted Inverted Subpubic (pubic arch) angle Wide, about 80–85° Narrow, about 50–60° Sacrum Short, wide, flat Long, narrow, convex Must-memorise Pelvic ring = 2 hip bones + sacrum + coccyx; closed by sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments. Hip bone = ilium + ischium + pubis, fused in the acetabulum (triradiate cartilage in childhood). Acetabulum: lunate surface (articular, broadest superiorly), acetabular fossa (nonarticular), acetabular notch bridged by the transverse acetabular ligament. Obturator foramen is closed by the obturator membrane; the superolateral gap is the obturator canal. Sacrum = 5 fused vertebrae; coccyx = 3–4 rudimentary vertebrae; sacral hiatus = caudal anaesthesia. Ischial tuberosity: semitendinosus + long head of biceps (medial), semimembranosus (lateral), adductor magnus (lower). Clinical / radiological correlation Pelvic fractures are classified by the number of breaks in the bony ring: type 1 = no ring disruption (iliac wing); type 2 = single break (e.g. pubic ramus fracture with symphyseal diastasis); type 3 = double break; type 4 = acetabular fractures. Concealed blood loss is the main early danger. Iliac crest is a standard site for autologous bone graft (cancellous bone and marrow); the apophysis of the crest appears and fuses in adolescence and must not be mistaken for a fracture. Sacral hiatus may be absent or closed, and the sacrum may show spina bifida occulta; a sacral fracture is easily missed on plain films, so CT is used. On an AP pelvic radiograph the ring, the sacroiliac joints, the symphysis, the obturator foramina, the teardrop and the iliopectineal line are checked systematically; asymmetry of the obturator foramina suggests a ring injury. Likely follow-up questions Which structures pass through the greater sciatic foramen? — Above piriformis: superior gluteal nerve and vessels; below piriformis: sciatic nerve, inferior gluteal nerve and vessels, pudendal nerve, internal pudendal vessels, posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh, nerves to obturator internus and quadratus femoris. Why is the acetabular notch important? — It transmits vessels and nerves to the joint and is closed by the transverse acetabular ligament; the lunate surface is deficient here. Difference between the greater and lesser sciatic foramina — the greater is above the sacrospinous ligament and ischial spine, the lesser is below them, between the sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments. How do you sex a pelvis? — Inlet shape, subpubic angle, ischial spines, sacral proportions, outlet and false pelvis proportions; the female pelvis is built for parturition."},{"id":"t-II-2","p":"II","n":2,"t":"The structure and diameters of the bony pelvis.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The pelvic brim ( linea terminalis ) divides the pelvis into a greater (false) pelvis above and a lesser (true) pelvis below. The true pelvis has an inlet , a cavity and an outlet , and it is described by three sets of diameters — anteroposterior, transverse and oblique. In the inlet the narrowest anteroposterior diameter is the obstetric conjugate (about 11 cm), the transverse diameter is the largest (about 13 cm) and the oblique diameters are about 12 cm; the diagonal conjugate (about 12.5 cm) is the clinically measurable diameter from which the obstetric conjugate is estimated. The narrowest part of the canal is at the ischial spines (interspinous distance, about 10 cm). 1. Division of the pelvis: false and true pelvis The linea terminalis is the oblique line that runs on each side from the sacral promontory across the ala of the sacrum and the sacroiliac joint to the arcuate line of the ilium, then along the pecten pubis (pectineal line) to the pubic crest and the pubic symphysis. It is the bony margin of the pelvic inlet ( aditus pelvis ). Greater (false) pelvis ( pelvis major ): the space above the inlet, between the iliac alae and below the abdominal cavity. It contains abdominal organs (caecum, sigmoid colon, loops of small intestine) and is not part of the birth canal. Lesser (true) pelvis ( pelvis minor ): the bony-walled canal between the inlet and the outlet. It contains the pelvic viscera and the pelvic floor closes its outlet. The margin of the inlet is: promontory and ala of the sacrum posteriorly, arcuate line and pecten pubis laterally, pubic crest and pubic symphysis anteriorly. The margin of the outlet is: the tip of the coccyx and the inferior margin of the sacrotuberous ligament posteriorly, the ischial tuberosities laterally, and the pubic arch (inferior pubic ligament) anteriorly. Between inlet and outlet the true pelvis has a wall made of the sacrum, coccyx, hip bones below the linea terminalis, the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments and the obturator internus and piriformis muscles. 2. Diameters of the pelvic inlet All diameters are measured in the plane of the inlet. The table gives the conventional average values for the adult female pelvis, which are the figures used in the Szeged examination and in obstetrics; the male pelvis is uniformly narrower and its values are correspondingly smaller. Diameter Landmarks Average Exam note Anatomic conjugate conjugata anatomica Sacral promontory to the upper border of the pubic symphysis About 11.5 cm Anatomical, not obstetrically decisive True (obstetric) conjugate conjugata vera / obstetrica Sacral promontory to the most prominent point of the posterior surface of the pubic symphysis About 11 cm The narrowest anteroposterior diameter of the canal; the limiting diameter of the inlet Diagonal conjugate conjugata diagonalis Sacral promontory to the inferior border of the pubic symphysis About 12.5 cm Measured per vaginam; the true conjugate is estimated by subtracting about 1.5–2 cm Transverse diameter diameter transversa The greatest distance between the linea terminalis of the two sides About 13 cm Largest diameter of the inlet; the head usually engages in this direction Oblique diameters diameter obliqua dextra / sinistra Sacroiliac joint of one side to the iliopubic eminence of the opposite side (right and left diameter) About 12 cm Two oblique diameters cross at the middle of the inlet Because the promontory protrudes into the inlet, the anteroposterior diameters are shorter than the transverse one; this is why the fetal head enters the inlet with its longest diameter transversely and rotates later in the cavity. 3. Diameters of the cavity and of the outlet In the cavity three levels are distinguished: the plane of the inlet, the plane of greatest dimensions (at the level of the middle of the sacrum, where the canal is widest), and the plane of least dimensions at the level of the ischial spines. The outlet plane passes through the lower border of the symphysis, the ischial tuberosities and the tip of the coccyx. Level and diameter Landmarks Average Exam note Interspinous distance (plane of least dimensions) Between the tips of the two ischial spines About 10 cm Narrowest transverse diameter of the birth canal; also called the bispinous diameter Anteroposterior diameter of the cavity Mid-sacrum to the middle of the posterior surface of the symphysis About 11.5–12 cm Measured at the plane of greatest dimensions Transverse diameter of the outlet (intertuberous) Between the medial surfaces of the two ischial tuberosities About 11 cm Reduced by the ischial spines and the sacrotuberous ligaments Sagittal (anteroposterior) diameter of the outlet Tip of the coccyx to the inferior margin of the pubic symphysis About 12.5 cm Increases when the coccyx is pushed backwards during delivery 4. Planes and axes of the pelvis The plane of the pelvic inlet is inclined at about 60° to the horizontal in the standing position, so that the inlet looks anterosuperiorly. The plane of the pelvic outlet is inclined at about 15° to the horizontal; the two planes are therefore not parallel. The axis of the inlet is perpendicular to the inlet plane and points posteroinferiorly; the axis of the outlet is perpendicular to the outlet plane and points anteroinferiorly. The pelvic axis is the curve joining these axes — it is concave posteriorly and is followed by the fetal head (the obstetric curve). The pelvic inclination is described clinically by the relation of the inlet plane to the horizontal; it can be altered by posture, hip flexion and lumbar lordosis, which is why the lithotomy or squatting position widens the effective outlet. 5. Clinical use and measurement The diameters cannot be measured directly through the intact soft tissues, so clinical pelvimetry estimates them from palpable landmarks (diagonal conjugate, ischial spines, sacral curve, subpubic angle) and imaging measures them exactly. The diagonal conjugate is the only diameter that can be reached digitally; the obstetric conjugate is then calculated. MRI gives the diameters without ionising radiation and is therefore preferred in pregnancy; CT is used after trauma. A pelvis whose diameters are below the conventional values is called a contracted pelvis and predisposes to obstructed labour, while a very wide subpubic angle and a roomy outlet predispose to rapid delivery. Must-memorise (female averages) Obstetric conjugate 11 cm; anatomic conjugate 11.5 cm; diagonal conjugate 12.5 cm. Transverse diameter of the inlet 13 cm (largest); oblique diameters 12 cm. Interspinous distance 10 cm — the narrowest part of the canal. Outlet: intertuberous 11 cm, sagittal 12.5 cm. Inlet plane about 60° to the horizontal, outlet plane about 15°. Sequence inlet → cavity → outlet: the canal is narrowest at the inlet (AP) and at the ischial spines (transverse). Clinical / radiological correlation Obstetric conjugate is the single most important diameter: it is the narrowest fixed AP measurement the fetal head must pass. Contracted pelvis — one or more diameters below the normal range; causes prolonged labour, arrest of descent and fetal distress; assessed by clinical pelvimetry, MRI or CT. Sex determination from the dried pelvis uses the inlet shape, subpubic angle, ischial spines and sacral proportions (see II.1). On a plain radiograph the diameters are distorted by magnification, so CT or MRI with a scale is used when exact values are needed. Likely follow-up questions Why is the transverse diameter of the inlet larger than the AP? — Because the promontory projects into the inlet in the midline, shortening the AP diameters while the linea terminalis is wide transversely. How do you measure the true conjugate clinically? — By the diagonal conjugate on vaginal examination, then subtracting about 1.5–2 cm. Where is the narrowest part of the pelvic canal? — At the level of the ischial spines (interspinous distance, about 10 cm). What closes the pelvic outlet? — The pelvic floor (levator ani and coccygeus with their fasciae), not bone."},{"id":"t-II-3","p":"II","n":3,"t":"The joints and ligaments of the pelvis. The statics and radiological anatomy of the pelvis.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The pelvic ring contains three joints: the two sacroiliac joints (a synovial joint anteriorly combined with a very strong posterior syndesmosis), the pubic symphysis (secondary cartilaginous) and the sacrococcygeal joint . They are reinforced by the interosseous, anterior and posterior sacroiliac ligaments, the iliolumbar, sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments, and the superior and inferior pubic ligaments. Together they make the ring a self-locking arch: the sacrum is suspended between the ilia, body weight drives it into nutation , and the ligaments convert this into a stable, elastic transmission of load to the acetabula. Radiology uses the AP pelvic film (teardrop, Shenton's line, iliopectineal line), inlet and outlet views, oblique views, CT and MRI. 1. The sacroiliac joint The sacroiliac joint is the articulation of the auricular surface of the sacrum with the matching auricular surface of the ilium. It is a hybrid: a narrow synovial cavity exists in the anterior part (diarthrosis), while the posterior and superior part is a syndesmosis filled by the strongest of its ligaments. The surfaces are ear-shaped and irregularly ridged, which gives the joint a high resistance to shear. The capsule is short and tight. Ligament Attachments / course Function Interosseous sacroiliac Fills the gap posterosuperior to the joint, between the rough areas of sacrum and ilium Largest and strongest; the chief restraint of movement, resists shear and rotation Posterior sacroiliac (short and long parts) From the iliac crest and posterior iliac spines to the lateral sacral crest; covers the interosseous ligament Reinforces the syndesmosis from behind; limits nutation Anterior sacroiliac Thin band on the pelvic surface from the ala of the sacrum to the auricular margin of the ilium Reinforces the anterior capsule Iliolumbar Transverse process of L5 to the iliac crest and iliac tuberosity Stabilises L5 on the ilium Sacrotuberous Posterior superior iliac spine, dorsal sacrum and coccyx → medial margin of the ischial tuberosity Resists upward tilting of the lower sacrum; converts the sciatic notches into foramina Sacrospinous Lateral margin of sacrum and coccyx → ischial spine Same accessory role; lies deep to the sacrotuberous ligament Movement at the sacroiliac joint is small — a few degrees of nutation (the promontory moves anteriorly and inferiorly) and counternutation (the opposite), with slight gliding. It increases in pregnancy under hormonal softening of the ligaments. 2. Pubic symphysis, sacrococcygeal joint and obturator membrane The pubic symphysis ( symphysis pubica ) is a secondary cartilaginous joint between the symphyseal surfaces of the two pubic bones. Each surface is covered by hyaline cartilage and the two are united by a fibrocartilaginous interpubic disc , which is often a slit-like cavity in its upper part. It is reinforced above by the superior pubic ligament and below by the inferior (arcuate) pubic ligament , which runs across the subpubic angle and forms the upper border of the pubic arch. Movement is minimal (a few millimetres), but during pregnancy relaxation allows a small separation; a gap above about 10 mm is called diastasis . The sacrococcygeal joint is a symphysis between the apex of the sacrum and the base of the coccyx, with an intervening fibrocartilaginous disc. It is held by anterior, posterior and lateral sacrococcygeal ligaments, and it allows the coccyx to be flexed forwards (defecation) and pushed backwards (childbirth). The obturator membrane is a fibrous sheet closing the obturator foramen; its superolateral defect, with the obturator groove, forms the obturator canal for the obturator nerve and vessels. 3. Statics of the pelvis The pelvis behaves as an arch or ring: the sacrum is the keystone, the hip bones are the side pillars and the pubic symphysis is the tie-beam. Under vertical load the sacrum tends to move downwards, forwards and into nutation between the ilia; the sacroiliac ligaments and the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments resist this and give the joint its self-locking, force-closing character. The load path is therefore: Body weight → L5/S1 → sacrum → sacroiliac joints → arcuate line and ilium → acetabulum → femoral head ⇒ standing and walking; when sitting → ischial tuberosities. Trabecular lines in the ilium follow the two force paths: one from the sacroiliac joint to the acetabulum (along the arcuate line) and one from the sacroiliac joint to the ischial tuberosity; in the sacrum the trabeculae run from the upper end plate towards the auricular surfaces. Compression and tension: the upper part of the pelvic ring is compressed while the lower part, especially the pubic region, is kept in tension by the arcuate ligament. Muscles assist statics: the pelvic floor and abdominal wall act as a closed pressure chamber; the gluteus maximus and hamstrings extend the hip and stabilise the sacroiliac joint; the iliopsoas and rectus femoris pull anteriorly; the iliofemoral ligament prevents the pelvis from tilting backwards in the upright stance without muscular effort. Hip flexion and lumbar lordosis alter the pelvic inclination; in the later months of pregnancy the centre of gravity shifts forward and lumbar lordosis increases to keep the trunk balanced over the hip joints. 4. Radiological anatomy of the pelvis AP radiograph: the intact ring should be traced — iliac crests, sacroiliac joints (their width and symmetry), symphysis pubis, obturator foramina (symmetrical in shape and size), sacral foramina and arcuate lines. The iliopectineal line (from the arcuate line to the pubic crest) and the ilioischial line outline the acetabulum; Köhler's teardrop is the U-shaped shadow of the acetabular floor and the medial wall of the acetabulum. Shenton's line: the continuous curve formed by the medial border of the femoral neck and the superior border of the obturator foramen; a break indicates a fracture of the neck or a dislocation. Inlet and outlet views: the inlet view (tube tilted caudally) shows the pelvic ring and the AP displacement of fractures; the outlet view (tube tilted cranially) shows the sacrum, the symphysis and vertical displacement. Oblique (Judet) views of the acetabulum and the Lauenstein view of the hip (limb abducted and laterally rotated) show the acetabular rim, the fovea and the femoral head in profile. CT is the method of choice for pelvic ring and acetabular fractures and for sacral fractures; MRI shows the bone marrow, the sacroiliac joints in inflammation and is used for obstetric pelvimetry; ultrasound is the screening method for the infant hip. In the child the triradiate cartilage of the acetabulum is radiolucent and closes during puberty; in the adolescent the iliac crest apophysis is visible. Must-memorise Sacroiliac joint = synovial anteriorly + syndesmosis posteriorly; strongest ligament = interosseous sacroiliac. Pubic symphysis = secondary cartilaginous joint; superior and inferior (arcuate) pubic ligaments. Sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments resist upward tilting of the sacrum and create the sciatic foramina. Weight path: vertebral column → sacrum → sacroiliac joint → ilium → acetabulum → femur; sitting → ischial tuberosities. Radiology: teardrop, Shenton's line, iliopectineal and ilioischial lines; inlet and outlet views. Clinical / radiological correlation Sacroiliac dysfunction causes low back and buttock pain, often referred to the groin or posterior thigh; it is a common cause of pain in pregnancy. Ankylosing spondylitis (HLA-B27-associated) begins with sacroiliitis: blurring, erosions, sclerosis and finally fusion of the joint — a key radiological sign on the AP film. Symphyseal diastasis after childbirth or trauma widens the symphysis and destabilises the ring; disruption of the ring can be double (pubic rami plus posterior injury) and is associated with major retroperitoneal bleeding. Coccygodynia after a fall on the buttocks; the coccyx is examined on a lateral radiograph. Likely follow-up questions What kind of joint is the sacroiliac joint? — A synovial joint in front combined with a strong posterior syndesmosis; the auricular surfaces are covered by fibrocartilage and the interosseous ligament is the strongest bond. What is nutation? — Anterior/inferior movement of the promontory with posterior movement of the apex, decreasing the AP diameter of the inlet; counternutation is the reverse. Which ligaments stabilise the pelvis in standing? — Interosseous and posterior sacroiliac, sacrotuberous and sacrospinous; the iliofemoral ligament prevents the pelvis from dropping backwards. How does the pelvis transmit weight when sitting? — Through the ischial tuberosities and their ligaments, not through the acetabula."},{"id":"t-II-4","p":"II","n":4,"t":"The hip joint: gross and radiological anatomy, movements and the participating muscles.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The hip joint ( articulatio coxae ) is a synovial ball-and-socket (spheroid) joint between the spherical head of the femur and the acetabulum of the hip bone. It is multiaxial — flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, medial and lateral rotation and circumduction — but it is built for stability and weight-bearing rather than mobility: the acetabulum is deep, the labrum and transverse acetabular ligament enlarge its rim, and a strong capsule is reinforced by the iliofemoral, pubofemoral and ischiofemoral ligaments. Its arterial supply comes mainly from the medial circumflex femoral artery and its innervation from the femoral, obturator and superior gluteal nerves and the nerve to quadratus femoris. 1. Type and articular surfaces Femoral surface: the head of the femur is spherical and covered by hyaline cartilage except at the fovea ( fovea capitis ) on its medial aspect, where the ligament of the head attaches. Acetabular surface: the crescent-shaped lunate surface , broadest superiorly where the load is greatest; the nonarticular acetabular fossa lies centrally and inferiorly and contains loose connective tissue and fat. The acetabular labrum is a fibrocartilaginous collar on the rim which deepens the socket; inferiorly it bridges the acetabular notch as the transverse acetabular ligament and turns the notch into a foramen. The acetabulum covers nearly the whole hemisphere of the head, so the joint is intrinsically stable; the articular cavity is a narrow slit because the capsule and labrum are tight against the head. 2. Capsule, ligaments and synovial membrane The fibrous membrane is thick and strong. Medially it attaches to the margin of the acetabulum, the transverse acetabular ligament and the adjacent margin of the obturator foramen; laterally to the intertrochanteric line in front and to the neck just proximal to the intertrochanteric crest behind. The synovial membrane lines the capsule, covers the nonarticular neck inside the joint and forms a sleeve around the ligament of the head; it does not cross the articular cartilage. Ligament Attachments Function Iliofemoral (Y-shaped, of Bigelow) Apex: ilium between the anterior inferior iliac spine and the acetabular margin; base: intertrochanteric line, thicker above and below the line Strongest ligament of the joint; limits extension and lateral rotation, checks adduction of the extended limb, prevents posterior pelvic tilt in standing Pubofemoral Iliopubic eminence, adjacent bone and obturator membrane → blends with the capsule and the deep surface of the iliofemoral ligament Limits abduction and extension Ischiofemoral Ischium just posteroinferior to the acetabulum → greater trochanter deep to the iliofemoral ligament Reinforces the posterior capsule, limits medial rotation and extension Zona orbicularis Circular fibres encircling the femoral neck, not visible externally Constricts the neck like a buttonhole and tightens the capsule Ligament of the head of femur (round ligament) Fovea of the head → acetabular fossa, transverse acetabular ligament and margins of the notch Weak mechanical role; carries a small branch of the obturator artery to the head The fibres of the three main ligaments spiral around the joint so that they become taut in extension : in the upright position the iliofemoral ligament takes the weight of the trunk off the hip extensors and stabilises the pelvis on the stance limb. In flexion the ligaments unwind and the joint becomes looser. 3. Movements and participating muscles Because the femoral neck projects at about 125° and slightly forwards, rotation of the femur is best understood as the greater trochanter moving forwards (medial rotation) or backwards (lateral rotation). Ranges: flexion is the most extensive movement (about 120° with the knee flexed), extension is limited to about 10–20° by the iliofemoral ligament, abduction and rotation each reach roughly 30–45°, and adduction is limited by the opposite limb. Movement Main muscles Nerve (root value) Flexion Iliopsoas; rectus femoris; sartorius; pectineus; tensor fasciae latae; assisted by adductor longus Psoas major: anterior rami L1–L3; iliacus: femoral n. (L2–L3); rectus femoris: femoral n. (L2–L4); sartorius and pectineus: femoral n. (L2–L3); TFL: superior gluteal n. (L4–S1) Extension Gluteus maximus; semitendinosus, semimembranosus, biceps femoris (long head); hamstring part of adductor magnus Inferior gluteal n. (L5–S2); sciatic n. (L5–S2); hamstring part: sciatic n. (tibial division) Abduction Gluteus medius and minimus; tensor fasciae latae; piriformis (with the hip flexed); sartorius Superior gluteal n. (L4–S1); piriformis: branches of S1–S2 Adduction Adductor longus, brevis and magnus; gracilis; pectineus Obturator n. (L2–L4); pectineus: femoral n. (L2–L3) Medial rotation Anterior fibres of gluteus medius and minimus; tensor fasciae latae Superior gluteal n. (L4–S1) Lateral rotation Piriformis; obturator internus with gemellus superior; gemellus inferior and quadratus femoris; obturator externus; gluteus maximus Piriformis: S1–S2; obturator internus and gemellus superior: nerve to obturator internus (L5–S1); gemellus inferior and quadratus femoris: nerve to quadratus femoris (L5–S1); obturator externus: obturator n. (L3–L4); gluteus maximus: inferior gluteal n. (L5–S2) Circumduction Sequential combination of the above in a cone — 4. Blood supply and innervation Arteries: branches of the obturator artery, the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries, the superior and inferior gluteal arteries and the first perforating branch of the deep artery of the thigh form a network around the joint. The retinacular (ascending cervical) branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery, running up the neck under the capsule, are the main supply of the adult femoral head in addition to the small artery of the ligament of the head; this is why an intracapsular neck fracture threatens the head. Nerves: articular branches of the femoral nerve, the obturator nerve, the superior gluteal nerve and the nerve to quadratus femoris — a classical illustration of Hilton's law (the nerves crossing a joint supply it and the muscles acting on it). 5. Radiological anatomy AP radiograph of the hip/pelvis: the femoral head sits concentrically in the acetabulum with a uniform joint space; the neck-shaft angle is about 125°; the greater trochanter is level with the centre of the head; the fovea, the acetabular rim and the teardrop (Köhler) are visible; Shenton's line is continuous. Lauenstein view (hip abducted and laterally rotated) profiles the femoral neck and the fovea and is used for neck fractures. Ultrasound is the screening method for the infant hip (dysplasia, effusion); the cartilaginous femoral head is invisible on early radiographs, whereas the ossific nucleus appears in the first months of life. CT with 3D reconstruction is used for acetabular and complex femoral fractures; MRI demonstrates avascular necrosis, labral tears, marrow oedema and joint effusion; arthrography outlines the capsule and the labrum. 6. Clinical correlations Fracture of the femoral neck: intracapsular fractures interrupt the retinacular supply and cause avascular necrosis and non-union; intertrochanteric fractures are extracapsular and heal better. The limb is shortened and laterally rotated. Dislocation: posterior dislocation is the commoner type after a dashboard injury — the limb is shortened, adducted and medially rotated; sciatic nerve injury may occur. Trendelenburg sign: a lesion of the superior gluteal nerve (gluteus medius and minimus) makes the pelvis drop on the swing side during single-leg stance. Trendelenburg gait is a common exam question. Developmental dysplasia of the hip is screened by ultrasound; Legg–Calvé–Perthes disease and slipped capital femoral epiphysis are childhood hip disorders causing limping and pain. Osteoarthritis of the hip produces narrowing of the superior joint space, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts; it is a common indication for joint replacement. Must-memorise Ball-and-socket joint; articular surfaces = head of femur (fovea nonarticular) + lunate surface of the acetabulum. Three ligaments: iliofemoral (strongest), pubofemoral, ischiofemoral; spiral fibres are taut in extension. Main blood supply of the head: retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery; the artery of the ligament of the head is small. Innervation: femoral, obturator, superior gluteal nerves and nerve to quadratus femoris (Hilton's law). Lateral rotators: piriformis, obturator internus + gemelli, quadratus femoris, obturator externus, gluteus maximus. Abduction and medial rotation: superior gluteal n. (L4–S1). Clinical / radiological correlation Shenton's line broken = fracture of the femoral neck or dislocation. Intracapsular neck fracture → avascular necrosis of the head; an extracapsular (intertrochanteric) fracture preserves the blood supply. Hip effusion is best shown by ultrasound; the joint is the deepest joint of the body and is not easily palpated. Congenital dislocation: limitation of abduction, asymmetry of the gluteal folds, Ortolani/Barlow tests, ultrasound screening. Likely follow-up questions Why is the hip joint stable? — Deep acetabulum, labrum and transverse acetabular ligament, tight capsule reinforced by three strong ligaments, atmospheric pressure and the large articular surface. Which structures limit extension, adduction and abduction? — Extension by the iliofemoral (with the ischiofemoral) ligament; abduction by the pubofemoral ligament; adduction of the extended limb by the iliofemoral ligament; medial rotation by the ischiofemoral ligament. What happens to the obturator internus tendon? — It bends 90° around the ischium between the ischial spine and tuberosity and passes through the lesser sciatic foramen to the greater trochanter. Where is the fovea and what attaches there? — On the medial surface of the head; the ligament of the head of the femur."},{"id":"t-II-5","p":"II","n":5,"t":"The femur, tibia, fibula. Connections between tibia and fibula.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The femur is the longest and strongest bone of the body: head, neck, greater and lesser trochanters, a shaft with the linea aspera and two condyles distally. The tibia is the medial, weight-bearing bone of the leg, with a plateau proximally, a sharp anterior border and the medial malleolus distally; the fibula is the slender, non-weight-bearing lateral bone, ending as the lateral malleolus. Tibia and fibula are joined by the proximal tibiofibular joint (synovial), the interosseous membrane and the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis ; together these hold the two bones as one unit and form the ankle mortise. 1. The femur The femur ( os femoris ) articulates with the acetabulum proximally and with the tibia and patella distally. Its proximal end has a spherical head with the nonarticular fovea for the ligament of the head, a cylindrical neck projecting superomedially at about 125° and slightly forwards, and the greater and lesser trochanters joined anteriorly by the intertrochanteric line and posteriorly by the intertrochanteric crest , which bears the quadrate tubercle . The trochanteric fossa (medial surface of the greater trochanter) lodges the insertion of obturator externus; the lesser trochanter receives the iliopsoas. The shaft is bowed forwards and is triangular in cross-section, with anterior, medial and lateral surfaces and medial, lateral and posterior borders; the posterior border is the broad rough linea aspera , which splits above into the pectineal line, gluteal tuberosity and the two lips. Distally the femur expands into the medial and lateral condyles separated by the intercondylar fossa , above which are the medial and lateral epicondyles ; the adductor tubercle lies just posterosuperior to the medial epicondyle and receives the hamstring part of adductor magnus. Anteriorly the condyles form the patellar surface . The patella , the largest sesamoid bone, lies in the quadriceps tendon: its base receives the quadriceps tendon, its apex gives attachment to the patellar ligament, and its posterior surface has larger lateral and smaller medial facets separated by a ridge. Bone / part Key features Main attachments Femur — head and neck Head with fovea; neck at about 125°; capsule attaches to the intertrochanteric line in front Ligament of the head (fovea); retinacular vessels run on the neck Femur — trochanters Greater trochanter with trochanteric fossa and ridges; lesser trochanter; intertrochanteric line and crest with quadrate tubercle Greater: gluteus medius (lateral ridge), gluteus minimus (anterior ridge), piriformis, obturator internus + gemelli; lesser: iliopsoas; quadrate tubercle: quadratus femoris; trochanteric fossa: obturator externus Femur — shaft Linea aspera with medial and lateral lips; pectineal line; gluteal tuberosity; supracondylar lines Linea aspera: adductors, biceps femoris (short head), vastus medialis and lateralis; gluteal tuberosity: gluteus maximus; supracondylar lines: vasti and medial head of gastrocnemius Femur — distal end Medial and lateral condyles, intercondylar fossa, epicondyles, adductor tubercle, patellar surface Adductor tubercle: adductor magnus (hamstring part); facets for the cruciate ligaments and for the gastrocnemius heads; epicondyles: collateral ligaments Tibia Medial and lateral condyles forming the plateau; intercondylar region with intercondylar eminence; tibial tuberosity; anterior, interosseous and medial borders; soleal line; fibular notch; medial malleolus Tibial tuberosity: patellar ligament; plateau: menisci and cruciate ligaments; pes anserinus (sartorius, gracilis, semitendinosus) on the medial surface; soleal line: soleus; medial malleolus: deltoid ligament Fibula Head with styloid process and facet for the tibia, neck, shaft with three borders and surfaces (medial crest on the posterior surface), lateral malleolus with malleolar fossa and groove for the fibular tendons Head: biceps femoris and fibular collateral ligament; neck: common fibular nerve lies against it; lateral malleolus: lateral ligaments of the ankle 2. The tibia The tibia is the medial and much larger bone of the leg and the only one that transmits weight from the knee to the ankle. Proximally it is expanded into medial and lateral condyles whose superior surfaces form the tibial plateau ; between them is the intercondylar region with the intercondylar eminence and its medial and lateral tubercles, which gives attachment to the menisci and cruciate ligaments. The medial condyle is larger and better supported over the shaft; its posterior surface bears a groove for semimembranosus. Below the condyles, anteriorly, is the palpable tibial tuberosity for the patellar ligament. The shaft is triangular with three borders (anterior — the sharp, subcutaneous shin; interosseous; medial) and three surfaces (medial — subcutaneous; lateral; posterior — crossed by the oblique soleal line ). Distally the tibia expands into the box-like distal end whose inferior surface is the tibial plafond and whose medial projection is the medial malleolus ; the posterior surface of the distal end has a vertical groove for the tendon of tibialis posterior, and the lateral surface has the fibular notch for the distal fibula. 3. The fibula The fibula is the lateral bone of the leg. It is much thinner than the tibia, is enclosed by muscles except at its ends, and does not take part in the knee joint or in weight-bearing — its role is to give attachment to muscles and to form the lateral wall of the ankle mortise. Its proximal end is the head , which bears a facet for the lateral tibial condyle, the styloid process and an impression for the biceps femoris; the neck separates it from the shaft, and the common fibular nerve lies against the posterolateral aspect of the neck. The shaft has anterior, posterior and interosseous borders and lateral, posterior and medial surfaces; the posterior surface is divided by the medial crest . Distally the fibula ends in the spade-shaped lateral malleolus , whose medial surface bears the facet for the lateral talus, whose posteromedial aspect has the malleolar fossa for the posterior talofibular ligament, and whose posterior surface has a groove for the tendons of fibularis longus and brevis. 4. Connections between the tibia and fibula Connection Type Ligaments / structure Movement Proximal tibiofibular joint Synovial, plane; flat circular surfaces Anterior and posterior tibiofibular ligaments; capsule; common peroneal (fibular) nerve passes just lateral to the neck Very little — slight gliding during ankle movement Interosseous membrane of the leg Fibrous syndesmosis Collagen fibres running obliquely downwards from the tibia to the fibula; apertures above for the anterior tibial vessels and below for the perforating branch of the fibular artery None; increases attachment area and distributes load between the bones Distal tibiofibular syndesmosis Fibrous syndesmosis Anterior tibiofibular, posterior tibiofibular and interosseous (transverse) tibiofibular ligaments; distal end of the interosseous membrane None in health; it is the hinge of the ankle mortise and is slightly widened by dorsiflexion The distal tibiofibular joint is essential for ankle stability : the mortise must hold the talus firmly, and the syndesmosis is the only elastic link between the two leg bones. If it tears, the fibula separates from the tibia ( diastasis ) and the ankle becomes unstable — this is the rationale for the syndesmotic screw in ankle fracture surgery. 5. Clinical correlations Femoral shaft fracture: can lose a large volume of blood into the thigh, causes shortening and external rotation, and may be complicated by fat embolism and by compartment syndrome of the thigh. Femoral neck fracture: intracapsular fractures threaten the retinacular blood supply of the head (see II.4). Tibial plateau fracture: often intra-articular, split or depressed, with associated meniscal and ligamentous damage; CT is used when plain films are equivocal. Tibial shaft fracture: the thin subcutaneous anteromedial surface makes it a common site of an open fracture; the tibia has a poor blood supply in its lower third, so union is slow. Fibular neck injury: the common fibular nerve is damaged by a blow, cast or fracture, producing foot drop and loss of sensation over the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot. Maisonneuve fracture of the proximal fibula is associated with a distal tibiofibular syndesmosis tear and an unstable ankle. Osgood–Schlatter disease: traction apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity in adolescents. Must-memorise Femur: head, fovea, neck (about 125°), greater and lesser trochanters, intertrochanteric line and crest, linea aspera, condyles, intercondylar fossa, adductor tubercle. Tibia: plateau, intercondylar eminence, tibial tuberosity, anterior border, soleal line, fibular notch, medial malleolus. Fibula: head, styloid process, neck (common fibular nerve), lateral malleolus, malleolar fossa. Three connections: proximal tibiofibular joint (synovial), interosseous membrane (with two apertures), distal tibiofibular syndesmosis. Patella = largest sesamoid bone, inside the quadriceps tendon, largest lateral facet. Clinical / radiological correlation Radiology of the knee: the tibial plateau, intercondylar tubercles, patella and fibular head are identified on the AP film; the epiphyseal plates close in late adolescence and must not be mistaken for fractures. Lateral film of the leg: shows the tibial shaft, the fibula and the interosseous membrane; the joint space of the proximal tibiofibular joint is seen. Fibular graft is harvested from the middle third after subperiosteal dissection, preserving the common fibular nerve. Ankle film: the distal tibiofibular overlap and clear space judge the syndesmosis; widening means diastasis. Likely follow-up questions Why does the fibula not transmit weight? — It has a thin shaft, is enclosed by muscles, and its distal end is tethered to the tibia by the syndesmosis; only a small fraction of the load passes through it. What is the linea aspera and what attaches to it? — A broad posterior crest on the femur: adductors, short head of biceps femoris, vastus medialis and lateralis, and the intermuscular septa. Which nerve is related to the fibular neck? — The common fibular nerve, which is palpable and vulnerable there. What are the apertures of the interosseous membrane for? — The anterior tibial vessels above and the perforating branch of the fibular artery below."},{"id":"t-II-6","p":"II","n":6,"t":"The knee joint: gross and radiological anatomy, movements and participating muscles.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The knee joint ( articulatio genus ) is the largest synovial joint of the body. It is a compound joint: a tibiofemoral part (a modified, bicondylar hinge) and a patellofemoral part (a plane joint between patella and femur). Two fibrocartilaginous menisci improve congruence; the joint is stabilised by the tibial and fibular collateral ligaments and by the anterior and posterior cruciate ligaments. Its main movements are flexion and extension, with rotation of the flexed leg; in full extension the knee is locked by medial rotation of the femur on the tibia and by the change in shape of the femoral articular surfaces, and the popliteus muscle unlocks it. 1. Type and articular surfaces Femoral surfaces: the rounded posterior parts of the two condyles articulate in flexion, the broad flat inferior parts in extension — so the contact area increases and the joint becomes more stable as it extends. Anteriorly the condyles form a V-shaped patellar surface (trochlear groove). Tibial surfaces: the superior surfaces of the medial and lateral condyles (tibial plateau), concave centrally and flatter at the margins; the medial is oval and larger, the lateral circular; between them is the intercondylar region. Patellar surfaces: the posterior surface of the patella with a larger lateral and a smaller medial facet separated by a ridge; the patella directs the pull of the quadriceps anteriorly, preventing tendon wear on the femur. All three articular surfaces share a single articular cavity ; the menisci and the cruciate ligaments are inside it or enclosed by its capsule. 2. The menisci The menisci are C-shaped fibrocartilaginous discs, one on each side, attached by their horns to facets in the intercondylar region of the tibia. The medial meniscus is attached peripherally to the capsule and to the tibial collateral ligament, so it is less mobile and more often injured; the lateral meniscus is not attached to the capsule, is more mobile, and is connected to the popliteus tendon, which passes between it and the capsule. The two are joined anteriorly by the transverse ligament of the knee . The menisci improve congruence between the changing femoral surfaces and the flat tibial plateau, share the load, absorb shock and help to distribute synovial fluid; after meniscectomy the cartilage of the compartment degenerates. 3. Capsule, synovial membrane and bursae The fibrous membrane is extensive and is partly formed by tendinous expansions. Medially it blends with the tibial collateral ligament and is attached to the medial meniscus; laterally it is separated from the fibular collateral ligament by a bursa and does not attach to the lateral meniscus. Anteriorly it is attached to the margins of the patella and is reinforced by the vastus muscles; posteromedially by the oblique popliteal ligament (an expansion of semimembranosus); anterolaterally by a fibrous expansion of the iliotibial tract. The popliteus tendon passes through an aperture in the posterolateral capsule. The synovial membrane attaches to the margins of the cartilage and to the upper and lower margins of the menisci. It reflects round the cruciate ligaments, excluding them from the cavity, and anteriorly it covers the infrapatellar fat pad , forming the fringed alar folds and the midline infrapatellar fold . It forms two pouches: the suprapatellar bursa , a large superior extension between femur and quadriceps (so an effusion collects there and distends the suprapatellar region), and the small subpopliteal recess between the lateral meniscus and the popliteus tendon. Separate, non-communicating bursae include the subcutaneous prepatellar and the deep and subcutaneous infrapatellar bursae. 4. Ligaments Ligament Attachments Function Patellar ligament Margins and apex of patella → tibial tuberosity; continuation of the quadriceps tendon Part of the extensor mechanism; its tap tests the L3–L4 reflex Tibial (medial) collateral Medial femoral epicondyle just below the adductor tubercle → medial margin of tibia above and behind the pes anserinus; deep surface attached to capsule and medial meniscus Resists valgus stress; its attachment to the meniscus explains combined injury Fibular (lateral) collateral Lateral femoral epicondyle → lateral surface of the fibular head; cord-like, separated from the capsule by a bursa Resists varus stress; not attached to the lateral meniscus Anterior cruciate (ACL) Anterior part of the intercondylar area of the tibia → back of the lateral wall of the intercondylar fossa of the femur; crosses lateral to the PCL Prevents anterior displacement of the tibia on the femur and hyperextension; taut in extension Posterior cruciate (PCL) Posterior aspect of the intercondylar area of the tibia → medial wall of the intercondylar fossa; stronger than the ACL Prevents posterior displacement of the tibia; taut in flexion Oblique popliteal Expansion of the semimembranosus tendon across the back of the capsule, from medial to lateral Reinforces the posterior capsule 5. Movements, participating muscles and the locking mechanism Movement Muscles Nerve (root value) Extension Quadriceps femoris: rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius Femoral n. (L2–L4) Flexion Semitendinosus, semimembranosus, biceps femoris; gastrocnemius; plantaris; popliteus; sartorius; gracilis Sciatic n. (L5–S2); tibial n. (S1–S2) for gastrocnemius and plantaris; popliteus: tibial n. (L4–S1); sartorius: femoral n. (L2–L3); gracilis: obturator n. (L2–L3) Medial rotation of the flexed leg Semitendinosus, semimembranosus, gracilis, sartorius, popliteus Sciatic n. (L5–S2); obturator n. (L2–L3); femoral n. (L2–L3); tibial n. (L4–S1) Lateral rotation of the flexed leg Biceps femoris Sciatic n. (L5–S2) Locking. In the standing position the knee is locked to spare the extensor muscles. Three factors act together: (1) the articular surfaces change from the small curved posterior parts of the condyles in flexion to the broad flat inferior parts in extension, so the joint surfaces become larger and more stable; (2) during the last part of extension the femur rotates medially on the tibia (the \"screw-home\" movement), which tightens all the ligaments; and (3) the line of gravity passes anterior to the knee joint, which keeps the joint extended. Unlocking is initiated by the popliteus muscle, which laterally rotates the femur on the tibia and so releases the tight ligaments before flexion begins. 6. Radiological anatomy and clinical correlations AP radiograph: femoral and tibial condyles, intercondylar eminence, joint space (medial and lateral compartments should be symmetric), patella superimposed on the distal femur, fibular head. In osteoarthritis the medial compartment is usually the first to narrow. Lateral radiograph: patellofemoral joint space, tibial tuberosity, the infrapatellar fat pad and suprapatellar bursa — a visible effusion or a fat–fluid level indicates an intra-articular fracture or lipohaemarthrosis; the fabella may be present. Sunrise (skyline) view shows the patellofemoral articulation and patellar tracking; MRI is the method of choice for menisci, cruciate and collateral ligaments and cartilage; ultrasound demonstrates effusion, tendons and collateral ligaments; arthroscopy allows direct inspection and treatment. Meniscal tears: vertical, horizontal and bucket-handle patterns; pain, locking, clicking and delayed swelling; MRI is diagnostic. Ligament injuries: the ACL is injured by non-contact pivoting and by valgus force; Lachman's, anterior drawer and pivot shift tests; the PCL by a direct blow on a flexed knee (dashboard injury) with a positive posterior drawer and sag sign. Unhappy triad: combined tear of the tibial collateral ligament, medial meniscus and ACL, after a valgus blow to the lateral knee. Collateral ligament injuries: the tibial collateral ligament is injured by a valgus force, the fibular collateral ligament by a varus force. Must-memorise Compound joint: tibiofemoral (modified hinge) + patellofemoral (plane); single articular cavity. Medial meniscus is attached to the capsule and tibial collateral ligament (less mobile); lateral meniscus is mobile and related to the popliteus tendon. ACL prevents anterior tibial displacement and hyperextension; PCL prevents posterior displacement and is stronger. Tibial collateral ligament is attached to the medial meniscus; the fibular collateral ligament is cord-like and separated from the capsule by a bursa. Locking = shape change + medial rotation of the femur on the tibia + line of gravity anterior to the joint; unlocking = popliteus. Knee innervation: femoral, obturator, tibial and common fibular nerves. Clinical / radiological correlation Suprapatellar effusion: the suprapatellar bursa communicates with the joint, so fluid collects above the patella — best seen on the lateral film and by ultrasound. ACL rupture: a pop is heard, haemarthrosis develops within hours; MRI confirms; reconstruction is indicated in active patients. Meniscal tear: delayed effusion, joint-line tenderness, locking; bucket-handle fragments displace into the intercondylar notch. Osteoarthritis: joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts, often medial first. Patellar dislocation is usually lateral, because the pull of the quadriceps has a valgus component and the medial patellofemoral ligament is the main restraint. Likely follow-up questions Why is the knee most stable in extension? — The flat femoral surfaces contact the plateau, the ligaments are tightened by medial femoral rotation and the joint surfaces are largest. What is the screw-home mechanism? — The terminal medial rotation of the femur on the tibia during extension, which locks the joint; it reverses at the start of flexion (popliteus). Which meniscus is more often torn and why? — The medial, because it is fixed to the capsule and tibial collateral ligament and is therefore less mobile. Where do the cruciate ligaments attach? — Intercondylar area of the tibia below; intercondylar fossa of the femur above; they cross in the sagittal plane and are intracapsular but extrasynovial. What does the tibial collateral ligament have to do with meniscal injury? — Its deep fibres attach to the medial meniscus, so valgus injury may tear both (part of the unhappy triad)."},{"id":"t-II-7","p":"II","n":7,"t":"The ankle (talocrural) joint: gross and radiological anatomy, movements and participating muscles.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The ankle joint ( articulatio talocruralis ) is a synovial hinge joint between the trochlea of the talus and the ankle mortise formed by the distal end of the tibia, the medial malleolus and the lateral malleolus of the fibula; the fibula is held to the tibia by the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis. It allows dorsiflexion and plantarflexion and is stabilised medially by the strong deltoid ligament and laterally by the anterior and posterior talofibular and calcaneofibular ligaments. Because the talar trochlea is wider anteriorly, the joint is tightest and most stable in dorsiflexion and loosest in plantarflexion, which explains the frequency of inversion sprains. 1. Type, articular surfaces and the ankle mortise Tibial surface: the smooth inferior surface of the distal tibia (the plafond) forms the roof of the mortise; the medial malleolus forms its short medial wall and bears the medial malleolar facet. Fibular surface: the lateral malleolus, which extends further inferiorly than the medial, forms the long lateral wall of the mortise and bears the lateral malleolar facet. Talar surface: the trochlea (superior articular surface) is shaped like a half-cylinder tipped onto its flat side: convex from front to back, slightly concave from side to side , and wider anteriorly than posteriorly; the medial and lateral malleolar surfaces are on its sides. All articular surfaces are covered by hyaline cartilage. The socket is a bracket-shaped mortise ; the joint is a hinge because the talus can move essentially only around a transverse axis, while the mortise holds it from the sides. The joint capsule is attached near the articular margins; it is thin anteriorly and posteriorly and is reinforced by the ligaments on the sides. 2. Ligaments Ligament Attachments Function / note Medial (deltoid) ligament — tibionavicular part Medial malleolus → tuberosity of navicular and adjacent spring ligament Limits eversion; part of the strong deltoid complex Deltoid — tibiocalcaneal part Medial malleolus → sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus Limits eversion and plantarflexion Deltoid — posterior tibiotalar part Medial malleolus → medial side and medial tubercle of the talus Limits plantarflexion and lateral shift Deltoid — anterior tibiotalar part Deep to the tibionavicular and tibiocalcaneal parts → medial surface of the talus Deep reinforcement of the medial capsule Anterior talofibular (ATFL) Anterior margin of the lateral malleolus → adjacent lateral talus Weakest and most commonly torn ligament of the ankle Posterior talofibular (PTFL) Malleolar fossa → posterior process of the talus Strong; the last to tear in severe sprains Calcaneofibular Malleolar fossa → tubercle on the lateral surface of the calcaneus Limits inversion and assists subtalar stability Distal tibiofibular (syndesmotic) ligaments Anterior and posterior tibiofibular and interosseous ligaments between the distal tibia and fibula Hold the mortise together; slight widening in dorsiflexion 3. Movements and participating muscles The talocrural joint is essentially a hinge: it produces dorsiflexion (the foot is lifted towards the shin) and plantarflexion (the foot is pointed). Inversion and eversion are not movements of this joint — they occur at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints (see II.8), although the long tendons that cross the ankle produce them. Movement Muscles Nerve (root value) Dorsiflexion Tibialis anterior; extensor hallucis longus; extensor digitorum longus; fibularis tertius Deep fibular n.: tibialis anterior (L4–L5), extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius (L5–S1) Plantarflexion Gastrocnemius; soleus; plantaris; tibialis posterior; flexor hallucis longus; flexor digitorum longus; fibularis longus and brevis Tibial n.: gastrocnemius, soleus and plantaris (S1–S2), tibialis posterior (L4–L5), flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus (S2–S3); superficial fibular n.: fibularis longus and brevis (L5–S2) The calf muscles (gastrocnemius and soleus, with plantaris) form the triceps surae and insert through the calcaneal tendon onto the posterior surface of the calcaneus. Tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus lie deep to them and their tendons pass behind the medial malleolus under the flexor retinaculum to enter the sole. 4. Stability and biomechanics Shape: the trochlea is wider anteriorly than posteriorly, so in dorsiflexion the wide part is wedged in the mortise and the joint is congruent and stable ; in plantarflexion the narrow posterior part is in the mortise and the joint is loose — the loose-packed position is plantarflexion, the close-packed position is dorsiflexion. Ligaments: the deltoid ligament is much stronger than the lateral ligament, which is why forced inversion tears the lateral ligament while forced eversion usually fractures the medial malleolus instead of tearing the deltoid. Syndesmosis: the distal tibiofibular ligaments maintain the mortise; a tear allows the fibula to move away from the tibia ( diastasis ) and destroys the hinge. Load: the talus transmits the whole body weight to the foot; in standing the load passes from the tibial plafond into the talus and then divides between the calcaneus and the forefoot. The axis of the joint runs obliquely, downwards and laterally, so that dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are accompanied by a small amount of rotation — one reason the ankle is not a perfect hinge. 5. Radiological anatomy AP radiograph: the tibial plafond, the medial and lateral malleoli, the talar trochlea and the clear joint space; the distal tibiofibular joint is assessed for overlap of the two bones. Lateral radiograph: the talus, calcaneus and the posterior process of the talus; the joint line and the talar dome; the calcaneal tendon and the fat planes. Mortise view: taken with the leg internally rotated about 15–20° so that the mortise is seen without the fibula superimposed; the medial clear space , the tibiofibular clear space and the joint line should be uniform — a widened clear space indicates a syndesmotic or deltoid injury. Stress views (forced inversion or external rotation) show abnormal talar tilt or widening if the ligaments are torn; CT is used for complex fractures and osteochondral lesions; MRI shows ligaments, tendons, marrow oedema and cartilage; ultrasound examines tendons (calcaneal, fibular) and effusions. The Ottawa ankle rules guide the decision to radiograph: pain near the malleoli plus bone tenderness or inability to bear weight; a similar rule applies to the foot (base of the fifth metatarsal, navicular). 6. Clinical correlations Inversion sprain: the most common ankle injury; the anterior talofibular ligament is torn first, then the calcaneofibular ligament; the joint is stable in dorsiflexion and unstable in plantarflexion. Bimalleolar (Pott's) fracture: fracture of both malleoli with subluxation of the talus; trimalleolar fracture adds the posterior tibial margin (posterior malleolus). Danis–Weber classification of ankle fractures is based on the level of the fibular fracture relative to the syndesmosis: type A below, type B at the level, type C above it — type C is unstable because the syndesmosis is torn. Maisonneuve fracture: tear of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis with a high fracture of the proximal fibula; the ankle is unstable although the fibular fracture is far proximally. Calcaneal tendon rupture: a sudden snap, a palpable gap, inability to stand on tiptoe, positive Thompson test. Tarsal tunnel syndrome: compression of the tibial nerve and its branches behind the medial malleolus under the flexor retinaculum. Must-memorise Hinge joint: talar trochlea + tibial plafond + medial and lateral malleoli (ankle mortise). Trochlea wider anteriorly → tight in dorsiflexion, loose in plantarflexion; close-packed position = dorsiflexion. Deltoid ligament = 4 parts: tibionavicular, tibiocalcaneal, posterior tibiotalar, anterior tibiotalar; strong, limits eversion. Lateral ligament = anterior talofibular, posterior talofibular, calcaneofibular; ATFL is the most commonly injured. Dorsiflexors: deep fibular nerve (L4–S1). Plantarflexors: tibial nerve (S1–S3) except fibularis longus and brevis (superficial fibular n.). Clinical / radiological correlation Mortise view is the key film for the syndesmosis; asymmetry of the clear spaces means instability. Ankle sprain vs. fracture: localised tenderness over the ATFL or the malleoli, and the Ottawa rules decide on imaging. Diastasis of the distal tibiofibular joint requires a syndesmotic screw to restore the mortise. Osteochondral lesion of the talar dome after inversion trauma — MRI is required. Likely follow-up questions Why is the ankle more stable in dorsiflexion? — The wider anterior part of the talar trochlea becomes wedged in the mortise, the joint surfaces are congruent and the ligaments are taut. Where do inversion and eversion take place? — Not at the talocrural joint but at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints; the ankle only dorsiflexes and plantarflexes. Which ligament is torn first in a sprain? — The anterior talofibular ligament. Which bones form the mortise? — Tibia (roof and medial wall), fibula (lateral wall), held together by the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis."},{"id":"t-II-8","p":"II","n":8,"t":"Anatomy of the intertarsal joints and surgical lines of the foot. Movements of the foot: participating muscles.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The intertarsal joints are the numerous synovial joints between the tarsal bones: the subtalar joint, the talocalcaneonavicular joint and the calcaneocuboid joint (the talonavicular part of the former, together with the calcaneocuboid joint, forms the transverse tarsal joint), and the cuneonavicular, intercuneiform and cuneocuboid joints; the cubonavicular connection is fibrous. They allow inversion and eversion , and pronation and supination of the forefoot. Two surgical lines are defined on them: Chopart's line , through the transverse tarsal (talonavicular and calcaneocuboid) joints, and Lisfranc's line , through the tarsometatarsal joints. Inversion is produced mainly by tibialis anterior and posterior, eversion by fibularis longus, brevis and tertius. 1. Overview of the joints of the foot Joint Bones Type Movement Subtalar (talocalcaneal) Posterior calcaneal facet of the talus + posterior talar facet of the calcaneus Plane (gliding) Gliding and rotation — inversion/eversion Talocalcaneonavicular Head of talus + anterior and middle facets of calcaneus + plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament + navicular Complex synovial Gliding and rotation — inversion/eversion, pronation/supination Calcaneocuboid Anterior facet of calcaneus + posterior facet of cuboid Plane (saddle-like) Sliding and rotation — inversion/eversion Talonavicular Head of talus + posterior surface of navicular Ovoid synovial Part of the transverse tarsal joint Cuneonavicular, intercuneiform, cuneocuboid Navicular + three cuneiforms; cuneiforms to each other and to the cuboid Plane Limited gliding only Cubonavicular Cuboid + navicular Fibrous (normally) None Tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) Medial cuneiform + metatarsal I, intermediate cuneiform + II, lateral cuneiform + III, cuboid + IV and V Plane Limited sliding Metatarsophalangeal Heads of metatarsals + bases of proximal phalanges; sesamoids under metatarsal I Condyloid Flexion, extension, slight abduction/adduction Interphalangeal Between the phalanges of each toe Hinge Flexion and extension 2. The subtalar joint The subtalar joint ( articulatio subtalaris ) is the posterior articulation between talus and calcaneus: the large posterior calcaneal facet on the inferior surface of the talus and the corresponding posterior talar facet on the superior surface of the calcaneus. Its cavity is enclosed by a synovial membrane and a fibrous capsule. It allows gliding and rotation which, together with the talocalcaneonavicular joint, produce inversion and eversion . It is stabilised by lateral, medial and posterior talocalcaneal ligaments and, most importantly, by the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament , which lies in the tarsal sinus (formed by the calcaneal sulcus and the sulcus tali) and separates the posterior from the anterior compartment of the subtalar complex. 3. Talocalcaneonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints — the transverse tarsal joint The talocalcaneonavicular joint is a complex joint in which the head of the talus articulates with the calcaneus below (anterior and middle calcaneal facets on the sustentaculum tali), with the plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament inferiorly and with the navicular in front. Its capsule is reinforced posteriorly by the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament, superiorly by the talonavicular ligament and inferiorly by the spring ligament. The spring ligament spans the gap between the sustentaculum tali and the navicular, supports the head of the talus, takes part in the joint and resists depression of the medial arch. The calcaneocuboid joint is a synovial joint between the anterior surface of the calcaneus and the posterior surface of the cuboid. It is reinforced by the bifurcate ligament (Y-shaped, from the anterior calcaneus to the cuboid and navicular), the long plantar ligament and the short (plantar calcaneocuboid) plantar ligament . The talonavicular part of the talocalcaneonavicular joint and the calcaneocuboid joint together form the transverse tarsal (Chopart) joint — some descriptions name the whole talocalcaneonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints, which is functionally equivalent. It is the main site of inversion and eversion of the forefoot on the hindfoot in addition to the subtalar joint; the axes of its two components are nearly parallel, so they work as one functional joint. 4. The other intertarsal, tarsometatarsal and toe joints Cuneonavicular, intercuneiform and cuneocuboid joints are plane synovial joints with only limited gliding; the cubonavicular connection is fibrous and normally immobile. Tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) joints are plane joints between the three cuneiforms and the cuboid behind and the bases of the five metatarsals in front. The base of the second metatarsal is recessed between the medial and lateral cuneiforms — it is the keystone of the tarsometatarsal complex — and is tied to the medial cuneiform by the strong Lisfranc ligament . The bases of metatarsals II–V also articulate with each other (intermetatarsal joints). Metatarsophalangeal joints are condyloid: each has collateral and plantar ligaments, and the heads of the metatarsals are joined by the deep transverse metatarsal ligaments . Two sesamoid bones lie in the tendons of flexor hallucis brevis under the head of metatarsal I. Interphalangeal joints are hinges; the great toe has one, the other toes have two. The tendons of the long and short flexors run in fibrous digital sheaths in the toes, and the extensor tendons expand into extensor hoods over the proximal phalanges. 5. Surgical lines of the foot Line / amputation Level and course Surgical note Chopart's line (transverse tarsal amputation) Through the transverse tarsal joint: talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints Removes the forefoot and leaves the talus and calcaneus; the pull of the intact triceps surae is unopposed, so the stump tends to equinus unless the calcaneal tendon is lengthened Lisfranc's line (tarsometatarsal amputation) Through the tarsometatarsal joints, between the cuneiforms/cuboid and the metatarsal bases Leaves the tarsus intact; a smaller, more distal amputation than Chopart Syme's amputation Through the ankle joint, removing the malleoli and the whole foot Weight-bearing through the distal tibia and heel pad Pirogoff's amputation Oblique transcalcaneal amputation with the calcaneal tuberosity rotated into the tibial stump Historical; uses the heel as a weight-bearing stump These lines are surgically important because the Lisfranc joint complex is also the site of a characteristic fracture-dislocation: the forefoot is displaced laterally or dorsally while the base of metatarsal II remains tethered by the Lisfranc ligament. 6. Movements of the foot and the participating muscles Inversion turns the sole inwards, eversion turns it outwards; these are the compound result of subtalar and transverse tarsal motion. Pronation rotates the front of the foot laterally relative to the back (eversion + abduction + dorsiflexion of the forefoot), and supination is the reverse. The range of inversion–supination of the forefoot is about 60°, roughly twice that of eversion–pronation (about 30°). Movement Muscles Nerve (root value) Inversion Tibialis anterior; tibialis posterior; assisted by flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus and extensor hallucis longus Deep fibular n. (L4–L5); tibial n. (L4–L5); tibial n. (S2–S3) for the long flexors; extensor hallucis longus: deep fibular n. (L5–S1) Eversion Fibularis longus; fibularis brevis; fibularis tertius; extensor digitorum longus assists Superficial fibular n. (L5–S2); deep fibular n. (L5–S1) for fibularis tertius and extensor digitorum longus Supination / pronation of the forefoot Combined action of invertors/evertors with the intrinsic muscles As above Flexion of the toes Flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus; the short flexors (flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, flexor digiti minimi brevis) and the other intrinsic muscles Tibial n. (S2–S3); medial and lateral plantar nerves (S1–S3) Extension of the toes Extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus; extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis; lumbricals assist through the extensor hoods Deep fibular n. (L5–S1; brevis: S1–S2); lateral plantar n. for lumbricals 2–4 Must-memorise Subtalar joint = the posterior talocalcaneal articulation; the talocalcaneonavicular joint is the anterior part of the same complex; talonavicular + calcaneocuboid = transverse tarsal (Chopart) joint. Interosseous talocalcaneal ligament lies in the tarsal sinus; spring ligament = plantar calcaneonavicular; short plantar = plantar calcaneocuboid. Chopart's line = transverse tarsal joint; Lisfranc's line = tarsometatarsal joints. Base of metatarsal II is the keystone of the Lisfranc complex, held by the Lisfranc ligament. Main invertors: tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior. Main evertors: fibularis longus, brevis and tertius. Clinical / radiological correlation Lisfranc injury: a fracture-dislocation of the tarsometatarsal joint complex after a twisting injury or a fall from a horse; the AP film shows lateral displacement of the metatarsals, and the medial border of metatarsal II should align with the medial border of the intermediate cuneiform. Chopart dislocation: a rare divergent dislocation of the transverse tarsal joint with marked swelling and deformity of the midfoot. Clubfoot (talipes equinovarus): hindfoot equinus and varus with forefoot adduction — an imbalance of the same muscles that invert the foot. Rupture of the spring ligament leads to acquired flat foot with talonavicular subluxation; the arch collapses. Tarsal coalition (calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal) causes a rigid flat foot with pain and restricted subtalar movement. Likely follow-up questions Where exactly does inversion occur? — Predominantly at the subtalar joint, with a contribution from the transverse tarsal (talonavicular and calcaneocuboid) joints. Which ligament supports the head of the talus? — The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament; it also completes the socket of the talocalcaneonavicular joint. What is the surgical importance of Chopart's and Lisfranc's lines? — They are the levels of transverse tarsal and tarsometatarsal amputation, and the sites of characteristic dislocations. Which muscles evert the foot? — Fibularis longus and brevis (superficial fibular nerve), assisted by fibularis tertius and extensor digitorum longus (deep fibular nerve)."},{"id":"t-II-9","p":"II","n":9,"t":"The anatomy of the foot arches. Radiological anatomy of the foot.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The bones of the foot do not lie in a flat plane but are arranged into a medial longitudinal , a lateral longitudinal and a transverse arch ( arcus pedis ), which absorb and distribute the forces of body weight during standing and walking. The arches are maintained by three groups of structures: the shape of the bones (wedges and keystones), the ligaments — plantar calcaneonavicular (spring), short and long plantar ligaments and the plantar aponeurosis ( aponeurosis plantaris ) — and the muscles , chiefly tibialis posterior, tibialis anterior and fibularis longus, together with the short muscles of the sole. On radiographs the talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, cuneiforms, metatarsals and phalanges are identified, and the longitudinal arch is assessed on a weight-bearing lateral film. 1. Architecture of the three arches Arch Bony components Keystone Form Medial longitudinal Calcaneus → talus → navicular → three cuneiforms → metatarsals I–III Talus High arch; the medial border of the foot is raised well above the ground Lateral longitudinal Calcaneus → cuboid → metatarsals IV–V Cuboid Low arch; the lateral border of the foot rests almost on the ground Transverse Cuboid + three cuneiforms + bases of the metatarsals, in a coronal plane Intermediate cuneiform Highest at the level of the talar head; disappears near the metatarsal heads The arch has two pillars : the posterior pillar is the calcaneal tuberosity (heel), the anterior pillars are the heads of the metatarsals. The summit of the medial arch lies at the level of the talus and navicular. The medial longitudinal arch is the highest and most elastic and is the one described as \"the arch of the foot\"; the lateral arch is flatter and transmits more of the load. It is important that the arches are not rigid: under body weight they flatten slightly, storing elastic energy and protecting the vessels and nerves of the sole. 2. Static (ligamentous) support Structure Attachments Role for the arch Plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament Sustentaculum tali → navicular; supports the head of the talus Chief ligament of the medial arch; resists depression of the talar head Long plantar ligament Calcaneus (between tuberosity and tubercle) → broad ridge on the cuboid and the bases of the metatarsals Strongest ligament of the sole; supports the lateral arch and the calcaneocuboid joint Short plantar ligament (plantar calcaneocuboid) Calcaneal tubercle → inferior surface of the cuboid Supports the lateral arch and the calcaneocuboid joint Plantar aponeurosis Medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity → digital bands to the toes, connected by the superficial transverse metatarsal ligaments Supports the longitudinal arch; the windlass mechanism — extension of the toes at toe-off winds it around the metatarsal heads and raises the arch Interosseous and dorsal ligaments of the tarsus; deltoid and lateral ankle ligaments Between the tarsal bones and between leg and foot Hold the bones together so the arch cannot flatten 3. Dynamic (muscular) support Muscles act as adjustable ties that protect the ligaments during loading; they are the reason the arch can recover its shape after being flattened. The table lists the muscles by their arch-supporting action. Muscle Action relevant to the arches Nerve (root value) Tibialis posterior Inverts and plantarflexes the foot; the chief dynamic support of the medial arch, lifting the navicular Tibial n. (L4–L5) Tibialis anterior Dorsiflexes and inverts; dynamic support of the medial arch through its insertion on the medial cuneiform and metatarsal I Deep fibular n. (L4–L5) Fibularis longus Everts and plantarflexes; crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and metatarsal I and supports mainly the transverse and lateral arches (the \"stirrup\" with the tibialis muscles) Superficial fibular n. (L5–S2) Flexor hallucis longus; flexor digitorum longus Flex the toes and assist in supporting the medial arch Tibial n. (S2–S3) Abductor hallucis; flexor hallucis brevis; flexor digitorum brevis Short muscles of the first and third layers; support the medial arch from below Medial plantar n. (S1–S3) Adductor hallucis (transverse head) Draws the metatarsal heads together and supports the transverse arch Lateral plantar n. (S2–S3) Abductor digiti minimi; flexor digiti minimi brevis Support the lateral arch Lateral plantar n. (S1–S3) 4. Function and biomechanics Weight distribution: in standing, about half the load passes through the calcaneal tuberosity and the rest through the heads of the metatarsals, mainly the first; the arches act as springs that spread the peak pressure over time. Elastic energy: the arch is flattened at mid-stance and recoils at toe-off, contributing to the efficiency of gait. Protection: the concavity of the sole keeps the plantar vessels and nerves away from the ground. Windlass mechanism: at toe-off the metatarsophalangeal joints are extended, the plantar aponeurosis winds around the metatarsal heads, the arch rises and the calcaneus is pulled forwards; this stabilises the foot for push-off. Adaptation: on uneven ground the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints allow the forefoot to twist relative to the hindfoot (supination/pronation), so the whole sole can stay in contact. 5. Radiological anatomy of the foot Bones: 7 tarsals (talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, medial, intermediate and lateral cuneiforms), 5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges — 26 bones per foot. The metatarsals are numbered I–V from medial to lateral; the first is the shortest and thickest, the second the longest. AP radiograph: the talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, cuneiforms, metatarsals and phalanges; the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joint spaces; the base of the fifth metatarsal (a common fracture site) and the sesamoids under the head of metatarsal I. Oblique (medial) view: profiles the lateral metatarsals and the cuboid; the Lisfranc alignment is checked — the medial border of metatarsal II should line up with the medial border of the intermediate cuneiform, and metatarsal III with the lateral cuneiform. Lateral (weight-bearing) view: the only view on which the longitudinal arch can be assessed; it also shows the sinus tarsi, the calcaneal tuberosity, the talar dome and the subtalar joint. CT for complex fractures (calcaneus, Lisfranc), MRI for stress fractures, plantar fascia and ligaments, ultrasound for the plantar fascia, tendons and effusions. Development: at birth usually only the talus and calcaneus are ossified; the other tarsal bones and the metatarsal and phalangeal epiphyses ossify postnatally, the navicular usually being last — a useful point in paediatric radiology. 6. Clinical correlations Pes planus (flat foot): loss of the medial longitudinal arch, congenital or acquired (rupture of tibialis posterior tendon or of the spring ligament); the talar head drops and the heel goes into valgus. Pes cavus: abnormally high medial arch, often with claw toes, in neurological disease (e.g. peroneal muscular atrophy). Plantar fasciitis: degeneration of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal attachment with heel pain and a calcaneal spur; MRI or ultrasound shows thickening. Stress (march) fracture of a metatarsal (commonly the second or third) in soldiers, runners and dancers. Tarsal coalition and accessory navicular are causes of a painful flat foot in adolescents. Fracture of the calcaneus after a fall from height flattens the arch and must be assessed with CT; associated lumbar and lower limb injuries are common. Must-memorise Three arches: medial longitudinal (calcaneus–talus–navicular–cuneiforms–MT I–III), lateral longitudinal (calcaneus–cuboid–MT IV–V), transverse (cuboid, cuneiforms, metatarsal bases). Static support: spring ligament, short and long plantar ligaments, plantar aponeurosis. Dynamic support: tibialis posterior (chief, medial arch), tibialis anterior, fibularis longus (transverse arch), long flexors and the short muscles of the sole. Keystones: talus (medial arch), cuboid (lateral arch), intermediate cuneiform (transverse arch). 26 bones per foot: 7 tarsals + 5 metatarsals + 14 phalanges. Windlass mechanism = plantar aponeurosis tightened by toe extension at toe-off. Clinical / radiological correlation Weight-bearing lateral film is essential to judge the arch; a non-weight-bearing film can make a flat foot look normal. Plantar fasciitis: heel pain on first steps in the morning, tenderness at the medial calcaneal tuberosity, thickened fascia on imaging. Rupture of the tibialis posterior tendon produces an acquired flat foot in an adult, with a \"too many toes\" sign and loss of the medial arch. Accessory navicular (os naviculare) is a common normal variant causing medial foot pain, especially in adolescents. Likely follow-up questions Which muscle is the main dynamic support of the medial arch? — Tibialis posterior, assisted by tibialis anterior and the long flexors. Which structures support the transverse arch? — Fibularis longus tendon crossing the sole, the deep transverse metatarsal ligaments and the transverse head of adductor hallucis, with the intermediate cuneiform as keystone. What happens to the arches in a fracture of the calcaneus? — The arch is flattened; the calcaneus loses height and the talus becomes more horizontal; CT is needed. How do you assess the arch radiologically? — On a weight-bearing lateral film, by the height of the medial arch above the ground and by the relationship of the talus, navicular and metatarsals."},{"id":"t-II-10","p":"II","n":10,"t":"Arteries of the lower limb, anastomoses between the branches of the femoral artery.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The lower limb is supplied by the femoral artery ( arteria femoralis ), the continuation of the external iliac artery beneath the inguinal ligament. It descends through the femoral triangle and the adductor canal, passes through the adductor hiatus as the popliteal artery , and ends behind the knee by dividing into the anterior tibial and posterior tibial arteries; the posterior tibial gives the fibular artery and continues into the sole as the medial and lateral plantar arteries. The superior gluteal , inferior gluteal and obturator arteries from the internal iliac reinforce the proximal supply. Anastomoses around the hip (cruciate and trochanteric), the knee (genicular network) and the ankle protect the limb when a main artery is occluded. 1. Plan of the arterial tree Abdominal aorta → common iliac a. → external iliac a. → (under the inguinal ligament) femoral a. → adductor canal → adductor hiatus → popliteal a. → anterior tibial a. and posterior tibial a. → (anterior tibial) dorsalis pedis a. → deep plantar a. → deep plantar arch ; (posterior tibial) fibular a. and medial/lateral plantar aa. Three arteries enter the thigh from the pelvis: the femoral artery (external iliac), and the obturator and inferior gluteal arteries (internal iliac). The superior gluteal artery (internal iliac) enters the gluteal region through the suprapiriform foramen above the piriformis; the inferior gluteal artery passes through the infrapiriform foramen below it. 2. Femoral artery: course and branches The femoral artery begins where the external iliac artery passes under the inguinal ligament. In the femoral triangle it lies midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis, where its pulse is easily palpated; the femoral vein is posteromedial and the femoral nerve lateral to it. It runs vertically through the triangle, enters the adductor canal ( canalis adductorius ) deep to the sartorius, and leaves the thigh through the adductor hiatus ( hiatus adductorius ) in the adductor magnus muscle as the popliteal artery. Four superficial branches arise in the femoral triangle: superficial epigastric , superficial circumflex iliac , superficial external pudendal and deep external pudendal arteries — supplying the lower abdominal wall, upper thigh and perineum. Deep artery of the thigh ( arteria profunda femoris ) — the largest branch, arising from the lateral side of the femoral artery; the main supply of the thigh. Descending genicular artery — arises in the adductor canal and contributes to the anastomosis around the knee. 3. Deep artery of the thigh (profunda femoris) The deep artery of the thigh passes posteriorly between the pectineus and adductor longus, then between the adductor longus and adductor brevis, and then between the adductor longus and adductor magnus; it perforates the adductor magnus distally to anastomose with branches of the popliteal artery. It has two circumflex femoral branches and three perforating branches. Lateral circumflex femoral artery ( a. circumflexa femoris lateralis ) — passes deep to the sartorius and rectus femoris; gives ascending (to the femoral neck, joining the medial circumflex), transverse and descending branches. Medial circumflex femoral artery ( a. circumflexa femoris medialis ) — passes medially between the pectineus and iliopsoas, then between the obturator externus and adductor brevis; its acetabular branch enters the hip joint through the acetabular notch and anastomoses with the obturator artery, and its terminal branches ascend to the trochanteric fossa and supply the femoral head and neck. Perforating arteries (first, second and third) — pierce the adductor magnus near the linea aspera and supply the posterior compartment of the thigh; their ascending and descending branches form a longitudinal channel that links the hip and knee anastomoses. 4. Popliteal artery and the genicular branches The popliteal artery is the deepest neurovascular structure in the popliteal fossa and is therefore difficult to palpate. It enters the fossa on the upper medial side under the semimembranosus, descends obliquely with the tibial nerve, and ends at the lower border of the popliteus muscle by dividing into the anterior tibial and posterior tibial arteries. Its branches are: Two sural arteries — to the gastrocnemius, soleus and plantaris. Superior lateral, superior medial, inferior lateral and inferior medial genicular arteries ( aa. genus ) — for the knee joint and its anastomotic network. Middle genicular artery — to the cruciate ligaments and interior of the knee joint. 5. Arteries of the leg and foot Artery Course Main branches Territory Anterior tibial (a. tibialis anterior) Passes forward through the aperture in the upper part of the interosseous membrane; descends in the anterior compartment; becomes the dorsalis pedis at the ankle. Recurrent branch, anterior medial and lateral malleolar arteries, dorsalis pedis Anterior compartment of the leg, dorsum of the foot Posterior tibial (a. tibialis posterior) Descends in the deep part of the posterior compartment; passes through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus; divides into medial and lateral plantar arteries midway between the medial malleolus and the heel. Circumflex fibular, fibular, medial and lateral plantar Posterior and lateral compartments of the leg, sole of the foot Fibular (a. fibularis) Descends along the medial crest of the posterior surface of the fibula; ends in a calcaneal network. Perforating branches to the lateral compartment, a perforating branch to the anterior tibial artery, calcaneal branches Posterior and lateral compartments of the leg, lateral heel Dorsalis pedis (a. dorsalis pedis) Continuation of the anterior tibial artery at the ankle; runs over talus, navicular and intermediate cuneiform to the first interosseous space, where it becomes the deep plantar artery. Lateral and medial tarsal, arcuate, first dorsal metatarsal, deep plantar Dorsum of the foot, deep plantar arch Deep plantar arch Formed by the lateral plantar artery joining the deep plantar artery of the dorsalis pedis between the bases of metatarsals I and II. Four plantar metatarsal arteries, digital branches, three perforating arteries Toes and sole of the foot 6. Anastomoses between the branches of the femoral artery Because the limb is long and passes two major joints, the main artery is bridged at three levels. The femoral artery is linked to the internal iliac system at the hip, and to its own popliteal continuation at the knee; the genicular network is the classic example of a collateral circulation in the body. Anastomotic network Site Contributing arteries Cruciate anastomosis Posterior upper thigh, below the ischial tuberosity Medial circumflex femoral (transverse branch), lateral circumflex femoral (transverse branch), inferior gluteal artery, first perforating artery from the deep artery of the thigh Trochanteric anastomosis Around the trochanters and femoral neck Ascending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral , medial circumflex femoral , superior gluteal and inferior gluteal arteries (with the acetabular branch of the obturator artery for the head of the femur) Genicular network (knee) Around the knee joint Descending genicular (femoral), descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral , the four genicular arteries of the popliteal, the circumflex fibular (posterior tibial) and the recurrent branch of the anterior tibial artery Perforating channel of the thigh Posterior compartment, along the linea aspera Ascending and descending branches of the three perforating arteries ; links the cruciate anastomosis above with the popliteal artery below Malleolar network Around the ankle Anterior medial and lateral malleolar branches of the anterior tibial, posterior medial and lateral malleolar and calcaneal branches of the posterior tibial and fibular arteries, perforating branches of the fibular artery Must-memorise Sequence: external iliac → femoral → (adductor hiatus) popliteal → anterior tibial + posterior tibial → fibular, dorsalis pedis, plantar arteries. Femoral artery in the triangle: four superficial branches + profunda femoris + (in the canal) descending genicular. Profunda femoris: lateral circumflex femoral, medial circumflex femoral, three perforating arteries. Cruciate anastomosis = M edial circumflex femoral, L ateral circumflex femoral, I nferior gluteal, 1 st perforating. \"M-L-I-1\" Knee network = descending genicular + descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral + four genicular arteries + circumflex fibular + recurrent branch of the anterior tibial. Two pulses of the foot: dorsalis pedis (between the EHL and EDL tendons) and posterior tibial (midway between the medial malleolus and the heel). Clinical / radiological correlation Femoral access: the femoral artery is palpated just below the inguinal ligament; it is the standard route for angiography, angioplasty and catheterisation of the contralateral limb, abdominal and thoracic vessels. Popliteal aneurysm: the popliteal artery is aneurysmal when its diameter exceeds 7 mm; because it tends to thrombose and embolise rather than rupture, the danger is distal ischaemia, and duplex Doppler is the first-line diagnosis. Knee dislocation and distal femoral fracture may injure the popliteal artery, which is tethered by the adductor hiatus above and the soleus arch below; the limb is checked for distal pulses and Doppler signals. Peripheral arterial disease: occlusion of the femoral or popliteal artery causes claudication and loss of the distal pulses; the genicular and cruciate anastomoses may keep the limb viable after gradual occlusion. Compartment syndrome of the leg follows tibial or fibular arterial injury and swelling inside a closed fascial compartment; the deep fibular and tibial nerves are the most sensitive to ischaemia. Likely follow-up questions Where exactly does the femoral artery become the popliteal artery? — At the adductor hiatus, as it passes through the adductor magnus muscle. Which arteries form the cruciate anastomosis and why is it called cruciate? — The transverse branches of the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries cross between the inferior gluteal and first perforating arteries, giving a cross-like arrangement. How is the head of the femur supplied? — Mainly by the medial circumflex femoral artery, with contributions from the lateral circumflex femoral and the acetabular branch of the obturator artery through the ligament of the head. Which artery pierces the interosseous membrane and at which end? — The anterior tibial artery, through the aperture in the upper part of the membrane. What is the clinical value of the genicular anastomosis? — It preserves the distal limb after ligation or slow occlusion of the femoral or popliteal artery, and it explains the warm knee despite an occluded vessel."},{"id":"t-II-11","p":"II","n":11,"t":"Venous and lymphatic drainage of the lower limb; clinical significance of the perforating veins.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer Blood returns from the lower limb through two connected systems: a superficial system in the subcutaneous tissue — the great saphenous vein ( vena saphena magna ) and the small saphenous vein ( vena saphena parva ), both arising from the dorsal venous arch — and a deep system that follows the arteries (anterior and posterior tibial, popliteal, femoral, external iliac veins). Superficial blood is conducted into the deep veins by perforating veins ; incompetent valves in these veins, or in the saphenous junctions, produce varicose veins . Lymph from the limb drains to the superficial and deep inguinal nodes (and from the lateral leg and foot to the popliteal nodes ), then to the external iliac nodes . 1. General plan The deep veins accompany the arteries as paired venae comitantes (tibial and fibular veins), which unite to form the popliteal vein and then the femoral vein. The superficial veins lie in the subcutaneous tissue, are not accompanied by arteries, and drain into the deep veins through the saphenous openings and perforating veins. Both systems contain valves that direct flow proximally and inward; the calf muscle pump (gastrocnemius, soleus) and the venous valves are the two mechanisms of venous return. Direction of flow: capillaries of skin/subcutis → superficial veins → perforating veins → deep veins → iliac veins → inferior vena cava. 2. Superficial veins All superficial veins begin from the dorsal venous arch over the metatarsals, which receives the dorsal digital veins and communicates with the plantar veins through perforating vessels. Vein Origin Course Termination Great saphenous vein (v. saphena magna) Medial side of the dorsal venous arch Passes anterior to the medial malleolus, ascends on the medial side of the leg (with the saphenous nerve), crosses posterior to the medial condyle of the femur, and ascends on the medial thigh Femoral vein at the saphenous opening ( hiatus saphenus ) Small saphenous vein (v. saphena parva) Lateral side of the dorsal venous arch Passes posterior to the lateral malleolus with the sural nerve, ascends in the midline of the back of the leg Pierces the deep fascia in the popliteal fossa and joins the popliteal vein At the saphenous opening the great saphenous vein receives the superficial epigastric , superficial circumflex iliac and superficial external pudendal veins, which drain the lower abdominal wall and perineum; these are the communications between the caval and portal territories of the body wall. 3. Deep veins Anterior tibial and posterior tibial veins (with the fibular vein) are paired venae comitantes of the corresponding arteries in the leg. They unite behind the knee to form the popliteal vein ( vena poplitea ), which lies superficial to the popliteal artery and passes proximally through the adductor hiatus. Above the adductor hiatus the popliteal vein becomes the femoral vein ( vena femoralis ), which traverses the adductor canal and the femoral triangle in the intermediate compartment of the femoral sheath, and becomes the external iliac vein under the inguinal ligament. Deep tributaries include the deep vein of the thigh ( v. profunda femoris ), the circumflex femoral veins, and the gluteal and obturator veins , which drain to the internal iliac system. 4. Perforating veins and their clinical significance Perforating veins ( vv. perforantes ) pierce the deep fascia and connect the superficial with the deep venous system. Their valves normally allow flow only from superficial to deep, so that the muscle pump can empty the subcutaneous plexus. They are found in the thigh (mid-thigh perforators), in the leg (communicating with the posterior tibial and fibular veins) and around the ankle and foot. Feature Normal state When the valves fail Flow direction Superficial → deep, one way, because of valves Deep → superficial reflux during muscle contraction Pressure in superficial veins Low, kept low by the muscle pump High (ambulatory venous hypertension) Effect on skin Normal nutrition of skin and subcutis Oedema, brown haemosiderin pigmentation, venous eczema, ulceration above the medial malleolus Clinical name — Varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency The typical sites of valvular incompetence are the junction of the great saphenous vein with the femoral vein, the perforating veins of the mid-thigh, and the junction of the small saphenous vein with the popliteal vein. Incompetent perforators are the target of perforator ligation and of endoscopic subfascial surgery. 5. Lymphatic drainage Lymphatic vessels of the lower limb follow the veins: superficial vessels accompany the great saphenous vein to the superficial inguinal nodes , while those of the lateral foot and leg accompany the small saphenous vein to the popliteal nodes ; deep vessels accompany the deep vessels to the deep inguinal nodes . Node group Location Afferents Efferents Popliteal nodes Deep, behind the knee, around the popliteal vessels Deep structures of the leg and foot; superficial vessels accompanying the small saphenous vein Deep inguinal and superficial inguinal nodes Superficial inguinal nodes About ten nodes in the superficial fascia below the inguinal ligament, along the terminal great saphenous vein Superficial tissues of the lower limb; gluteal region; lower abdominal wall; perineum Deep inguinal nodes and external iliac nodes Deep inguinal nodes Up to three nodes medial to the femoral vein, in the femoral canal region Deep lymphatics of the limb accompanying the femoral vessels; glans penis or clitoris External iliac nodes External iliac nodes Along the external iliac vessels in the abdomen Inguinal nodes; deep lymphatics of the pelvis Common iliac and para-aortic nodes Internal iliac nodes Along the internal iliac vessels Deep gluteal lymphatics; pelvic viscera Common iliac nodes Must-memorise Superficial veins: great saphenous (medial arch → anterior to medial malleolus → medial thigh → femoral vein) and small saphenous (lateral arch → behind lateral malleolus → popliteal vein). Deep veins follow arteries: tibial → popliteal → femoral → external iliac. Perforating veins connect superficial to deep; their valves are the key to varicose veins. Lymph: limb → superficial/deep inguinal nodes → external iliac nodes; the lateral foot and leg also drain to the popliteal nodes. Great saphenous vein is the longest vein of the body and the preferred conduit for coronary and peripheral bypass grafts. Clinical / radiological correlation Varicose veins are dilated, tortuous superficial veins produced by incompetent valves; they are commoner in women and aggravated by pregnancy and standing; complications are pigmentation, eczema and venous ulceration. Deep vein thrombosis (DVT): Virchow triad — venous stasis, injury of the vessel wall, hypercoagulable states. Calf-vein thrombi may propagate into the femoral and iliac veins and embolise to the pulmonary arteries. DVT is diagnosed mainly by duplex Doppler sonography (compression ultrasound) and prevented by anticoagulant injections and graduated compression stockings. The saphenous opening is the site of the great saphenous termination and of superficial inguinal lymphadenopathy; a varicose saphenous vein here may be the first sign of an underlying pelvic or abdominal mass. Lymphoedema of the limb follows obstruction or removal of inguinal and iliac nodes; the popliteal nodes are impalpable and enlarged only in deep infection or malignancy of the leg. Likely follow-up questions Where does the great saphenous vein end and what does it pierce? — It ends in the femoral vein at the saphenous opening, piercing the cribriform fascia of the fascia lata. Why is the small saphenous vein at risk during surgery on the back of the leg? — It lies in the superficial fascia with the sural nerve, which may be injured by stripping. What is the direction of flow in a normal perforating vein and why? — From superficial to deep, because its valves face the deep system. Where do the lymphatics of the little toe drain? — Along the small saphenous vein to the popliteal nodes, and thence to the inguinal nodes. Which veins are used for coronary bypass and why? — The great saphenous vein; its length, calibre and superficial position allow long grafts."},{"id":"t-II-12","p":"II","n":12,"t":"Branching system of the lumbar plexus.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The lumbar plexus ( plexus lumbalis ) is formed by the anterior rami of spinal nerves L1–L3 and most of L4 , with a contribution from T12 (subcostal nerve). It lies in the substance of the psoas major muscle and gives six named branches: iliohypogastric (L1), ilio-inguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1–L2), lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2–L3), femoral (L2–L4) and obturator (L2–L4). The rest of the L4 root joins L5 as the lumbosacral trunk and passes to the sacral plexus. 1. Formation and position Roots: anterior rami of T12 (small contribution), L1, L2, L3 and the greater part of L4. The remainder of L4 and all of L5 form the lumbosacral trunk. Site: the plexus is formed in the substance of the psoas major muscle, anterior to the transverse processes of the lumbar vertebrae, on the posterior abdominal wall. Relation to psoas: branches emerge from the muscle in three directions — anteriorly the genitofemoral nerve, medially the obturator nerve, and laterally the iliohypogastric, ilio-inguinal, femoral and lateral cutaneous nerves of the thigh. Sympathetic fibres reach the plexus through grey rami communicantes from the lumbar sympathetic trunk; they are distributed with the branches to vessels, sweat glands and erector pili muscles. 2. Branches: course and distribution Iliohypogastric nerve (L1) — arises together with the ilio-inguinal nerve from the L1 root; runs across the quadratus lumborum and pierces the transversus abdominis, then continues between the transversus and internal oblique. It supplies both muscles and ends as a lateral cutaneous branch (skin over the posterolateral gluteal region) and an anterior cutaneous branch (skin above the pubis). Ilio-inguinal nerve (L1) — runs below and roughly parallel to the iliohypogastric nerve, crosses the iliacus, enters the inguinal canal, and emerges through the superficial inguinal ring. It supplies the internal oblique and transversus abdominis and the skin of the upper medial thigh and of the root of the penis and anterior scrotum (or the mons pubis and labium majus). Genitofemoral nerve (L1, L2) — pierces the anterior surface of the psoas major and divides into two branches. The genital branch passes through the inguinal canal and supplies the cremaster muscle (in men) and the skin of the anterior scrotum or mons pubis and labium majus. The femoral branch passes under the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral artery and supplies skin over the upper central part of the anterior thigh. Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2, L3) — emerges from the lateral border of the psoas, runs across the iliacus and leaves the abdomen either through the gap under the inguinal ligament just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine or through the ligament itself. It is purely sensory to the lateral side of the thigh , as far as the knee. Femoral nerve (L2–L4) — formed within the psoas, it runs in the groove between the psoas and iliacus and reaches the thigh under the inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral artery and outside the femoral sheath. In the abdomen it supplies the iliacus and pectineus ; in the thigh it supplies the sartorius , the four heads of the quadriceps femoris and the articularis genus. Its sensory branches supply the anterior thigh, the anteromedial knee, the medial side of the leg and the medial side of the foot (through its terminal saphenous nerve ). Obturator nerve (L2–L4) — emerges from the medial border of the psoas, descends along the lateral pelvic wall and leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal . Its anterior division supplies the adductor longus, adductor brevis and gracilis, and often gives a branch to the pectineus (which is mainly supplied by the femoral nerve); its posterior division supplies the obturator externus and the adductor part of the adductor magnus. It carries sensation from the medial side of the thigh and gives a branch to the knee joint. 3. Summary table of the lumbar plexus Branch Roots Exit from the abdomen Motor supply Cutaneous supply Iliohypogastric L1 Pierces transversus abdominis Transversus abdominis, internal oblique Posterolateral gluteal skin; skin above the pubis Ilio-inguinal L1 Superficial inguinal ring Transversus abdominis, internal oblique Upper medial thigh; root of penis and anterior scrotum or mons pubis and labium majus Genitofemoral L1, L2 Genital branch: inguinal canal; femoral branch: under inguinal ligament Cremaster (genital branch) Anterior scrotum or mons pubis and labium majus; upper anterior thigh Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh L2, L3 Under inguinal ligament near the ASIS None Lateral thigh to the knee Femoral L2–L4 Under inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral artery Iliacus, pectineus, sartorius, quadriceps femoris, articularis genus Anterior thigh, anteromedial knee, medial leg and medial foot (saphenous nerve) Obturator L2–L4 Obturator canal Obturator externus, gracilis, adductors longus, brevis and magnus (adductor part); occasionally pectineus Medial thigh; knee joint 4. Lumbosacral trunk and the low lumbar contributions The part of the anterior ramus of L4 that does not enter the lumbar plexus descends over the ala of the sacrum and joins the anterior ramus of L5 to form the lumbosacral trunk ( truncus lumbosacralis ). The trunk crosses the pelvic brim medial to the psoas and enters the pelvis, where it joins the sacral plexus. This anatomical continuity explains why an L4 or L5 lesion may produce signs in both the lumbar and the sacral territories, and why the lumbosacral trunk is the most medial structure at risk in pelvic fractures. Must-memorise Roots: L1–L3 + most of L4 (+ T12); the rest of L4 joins L5 as the lumbosacral trunk. Six branches: iliohypogastric (L1), ilio-inguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1–L2), lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh (L2–L3), femoral (L2–L4), obturator (L2–L4). Relation to psoas: anterior — genitofemoral; medial — obturator; lateral — iliohypogastric, ilio-inguinal, femoral, lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh. The femoral nerve is the only branch that supplies an entire compartment of the thigh (anterior). The obturator nerve is the motor nerve of the medial compartment. Clinical / radiological correlation Femoral neuropathy (pelvic or retroperitoneal tumour, haematoma, diabetes, hip surgery): weakness of knee extension, wasting of the quadriceps, loss of the knee jerk (L3–L4) and sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial leg. Obturator neuropathy (pelvic surgery, obturator hernia, lithotomy position): weak thigh adduction, an unstable, wide-based gait, and sensory loss on the medial thigh. Meralgia paraesthetica is entrapment of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh under the inguinal ligament, producing burning pain and numbness of the lateral thigh; it is common in obesity, pregnancy and tight belts. Ilio-inguinal nerve injury is a classic cause of chronic groin pain after open inguinal hernia repair; the nerve may be divided deliberately during mesh repair. Lumbar disc herniation compresses a root rather than a peripheral nerve, producing a radicular pattern with pain in the corresponding dermatome and weakness in the corresponding myotome. Likely follow-up questions Which is the thickest branch of the lumbar plexus? — The femoral nerve, the largest branch of the plexus. Which branch has no motor fibres in the lower limb? — The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is purely sensory. Through which opening does the obturator nerve leave the pelvis? — The obturator canal, in the obturator membrane. Why does a femoral nerve lesion not abolish hip flexion? — Hip flexion is also produced by the psoas major (direct branches of L1–L3), the adductors (obturator nerve, L2–L4) and the tensor fasciae latae (superior gluteal nerve, L4–S1), so a femoral nerve lesion weakens the movement but does not abolish it. How is the lumbar plexus tested clinically? — Knee extension (femoral, L3–L4) and thigh adduction against resistance (obturator, L2–L4), with the corresponding sensory areas and the patellar reflex."},{"id":"t-II-13","p":"II","n":13,"t":"Branching system of the sacral plexus. The branches of the tibial and common fibular (peroneal) nerves.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The sacral plexus ( plexus sacralis ) is formed by the lumbosacral trunk (L4, L5) and the anterior rami of S1–S4 . It lies on the anterior surface of the piriformis and gives the sciatic nerve (L4–S3), the superior gluteal (L4–S1), inferior gluteal (L5–S2), pudendal (S2–S4), posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh (S1–S3), the nerves to the obturator internus, gemelli, quadratus femoris and piriformis, and the perforating cutaneous nerve. The sciatic nerve divides into the tibial nerve (L4–S3, ventral divisions) and the common fibular nerve (L4–S2, dorsal divisions); the tibial nerve supplies the posterior thigh, all muscles of the posterior compartment of the leg and the sole, while the common fibular supplies the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg and the dorsum of the foot. 1. Formation and position Roots: lumbosacral trunk (anterior ramus of L4 plus L5) and the anterior rami of S1, S2, S3 and part of S4. The anterior ramus of S4 contributes only a ventral division. Site: formed on the anterior surface of the piriformis muscle, on the posterolateral wall of the pelvis; sacral roots reach it through the anterior sacral foramina. Exits: most branches leave the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen ; the superior gluteal nerve and vessels pass through its suprapiriform part, the others through the infrapiriform part. A few nerves (nerve to piriformis, nerves to levator ani and coccygeus) remain within the pelvis, and the pudendal nerve and nerve to obturator internus re-enter through the lesser sciatic foramen after looping around the ischial spine. Visceral fibres: the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4) arise from the same roots and carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the pelvic viscera. 2. Branches of the sacral plexus Branch Roots Route Motor supply Cutaneous supply Superior gluteal L4–S1 Above the piriformis Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fasciae latae None Inferior gluteal L5–S2 Below the piriformis Gluteus maximus None Nerve to piriformis S1, S2 Within the pelvis Piriformis None Nerve to obturator internus and superior gemellus L5–S2 Below the piriformis, then through the lesser sciatic foramen Obturator internus, superior gemellus None Nerve to quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus L4–S1 Below the piriformis, deep to the sciatic nerve Quadratus femoris, inferior gemellus None Sciatic L4–S3 Below the piriformis, through the gluteal region into the posterior thigh Posterior compartment of thigh; all muscles of the leg and foot Lateral leg and foot; sole and dorsum of the foot Posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh S1–S3 Below the piriformis, deep to gluteus maximus None Gluteal fold, upper medial thigh, posterior thigh and upper posterior leg Perforating cutaneous S2, S3 Pierces the sacrotuberous ligament None Skin over the medial part of the gluteal fold Pudendal S2–S4 Below the piriformis, around the ischial spine, through the lesser sciatic foramen Perineal muscles, external anal and urethral sphincters Perineal skin, penis and clitoris 3. The sciatic nerve and its two divisions The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve of the body. It forms on the piriformis, leaves the pelvis through the infrapiriform foramen, crosses the posterior surfaces of the obturator internus, the gemelli and the quadratus femoris, and reaches the posterior thigh at the lower border of the quadratus femoris. It is enclosed in a common connective-tissue sheath and divides into its two parts at a variable level, often in the lower third of the thigh, but the two divisions remain functionally separate throughout their course. The tibial part (L4–S3; ventral divisions) supplies the hamstrings except the short head of biceps femoris, the hamstring part of adductor magnus, and all muscles of the posterior leg and the sole. The common fibular part (L4–S2; dorsal divisions) supplies the short head of biceps femoris, the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg, the extensor digitorum brevis and, partly, the first two dorsal interossei. 4. Branches of the tibial nerve In the popliteal fossa: muscular branches to the gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris (these arise between the two heads of gastrocnemius) and the popliteus ; the sural nerve arises here between the heads of gastrocnemius and is joined later by a communicating branch from the common fibular nerve. In the leg: branches to the deep group — tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus — arising deep to the soleus; the medial calcaneal branches arise near the ankle and supply the heel. At the ankle: the nerve passes through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus (lateral to the posterior tibial artery) and divides into the medial plantar and lateral plantar nerves. Medial plantar nerve: supplies the abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis and first lumbrical; its cutaneous branches supply the medial two-thirds of the sole and the medial three and a half digits. Lateral plantar nerve: supplies the quadratus plantae, the second to fourth lumbricals, adductor hallucis, flexor digiti minimi brevis and all the interossei; its cutaneous branches supply the lateral third of the sole and the lateral one and a half digits. 5. Branches of the common fibular nerve Course: the common fibular nerve leaves the popliteal fossa along the medial border of the biceps femoris tendon, winds around the neck of the fibula deep to the fibres of the fibularis longus, and divides into the superficial and deep fibular nerves. Superficial fibular nerve: passes into the lateral compartment and supplies fibularis longus and fibularis brevis ; it pierces the deep fascia of the lower leg and supplies skin of the lower lateral leg and most of the dorsum of the foot and toes (except the first web space). Deep fibular nerve: passes into the anterior compartment and supplies tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius ; it continues with the anterior tibial artery and enters the dorsum of the foot lateral to the dorsalis pedis artery, where it supplies extensor digitorum brevis , partially the first two dorsal interossei, and the skin of the first web space and adjacent sides of toes I and II. 6. Comparison of the two sciatic divisions Feature Medial (tibial) part Lateral (common fibular) part Roots L4–S3 (ventral divisions) L4–S2 (dorsal divisions) Muscles in the thigh Hamstrings except short head of biceps; hamstring part of adductor magnus Short head of biceps femoris Leg and foot Posterior compartment of leg, all intrinsic muscles of the sole Anterior and lateral compartments of leg; extensor digitorum brevis; contributes to first two dorsal interossei Skin Posterolateral leg, lateral side of the foot and sole, heel (with medial calcaneal) Anterolateral leg and dorsum of the foot; the first web space through the deep fibular branch Final branches Medial and lateral plantar nerves Superficial and deep fibular nerves Typical lesion Weak plantarflexion and toe flexion; sole sensation lost Foot drop, loss of dorsiflexion and eversion, loss of dorsum sensation Must-memorise Sacral plexus = lumbosacral trunk (L4, L5) + S1–S4. Superior gluteal nerve is the only nerve passing above the piriformis; everything else named passes below. Sciatic = tibial part (L4–S3) + common fibular part (L4–S2). Tibial nerve supplies all of the posterior leg and all of the sole (medial and lateral plantar nerves). Deep fibular: anterior compartment, extensor digitorum brevis, first web space. Superficial fibular: lateral compartment, dorsum of the foot. Pudendal nerve (S2–S4) is the nerve of the perineum and leaves the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen. Clinical / radiological correlation Common fibular nerve palsy at the fibular neck (fracture, torn ligament, plaster cast, prolonged crossing of the legs, lithotomy position) causes foot drop and a steppage gait, with sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg. Tarsal tunnel syndrome is compression of the tibial nerve behind the medial malleolus, producing burning pain in the sole and, if severe, weakness of the intrinsic foot muscles. Sciatica is radicular pain along the L5 or S1 root, commonly due to a lumbar disc herniation; the straight-leg-raising test is positive. Piriformis syndrome is compression of the sciatic nerve by the piriformis and causes buttock pain radiating down the posterior thigh. Gluteal intramuscular injection must be given in the upper lateral quadrant to avoid the sciatic nerve, which lies in the lower medial part of the gluteal region. Likely follow-up questions Where does the sciatic nerve enter the thigh and where does it divide? — It passes through the infrapiriform foramen, crosses the posterior gluteal muscles and enters the posterior thigh at the lower border of the quadratus femoris; it divides into tibial and common fibular nerves in the lower thigh. Why is the common fibular nerve the most frequently injured nerve of the lower limb? — It lies superficially against the neck of the fibula, where it is exposed to external pressure and to fractures. What is the nerve supply of the short head of the biceps femoris? — The common fibular part of the sciatic nerve. Which muscles are tested for S1 function? — Gastrocnemius and soleus (plantarflexion; ankle jerk) and the gluteus maximus, with the lateral border of the foot for sensation. Which nerve supplies the skin of the heel? — The medial calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve, with the sural nerve for the lateral side."},{"id":"t-II-14","p":"II","n":14,"t":"Sensory innervation of the skin of the lower limb.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The skin of the lower limb has two superimposed patterns of innervation. The segmental (radicular) pattern is the dermatome : L1–S3 for the limb, with S4 and S5 for the perineum. The peripheral pattern is the territory of the cutaneous branches of the lumbar and sacral plexuses — iliohypogastric, ilio-inguinal, genitofemoral, lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, femoral (through its saphenous branch), obturator, posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, perforating cutaneous, and the tibial and common fibular branches. Clinically the two patterns are distinguished by the shape of the sensory loss: a dermatome is a transverse band, a peripheral nerve territory follows the course of a named nerve. 1. Segmental innervation: the dermatomes A dermatome is the area of skin supplied by the somatic sensory fibres of one spinal nerve. In the lower limb the dermatomes are arranged as oblique bands that spiral down the limb: L1–L3 pass down the anterior and medial thigh, L4–L5 cross the knee and leg, and S1–S3 run down the posterior thigh and leg onto the lateral foot. Because adjacent dermatomes overlap, only a limited autonomous zone of each dermatome can be tested reliably. Level Autonomous zone (reliable test area) Main territory of the dermatome L1 Skin over the inguinal ligament and the groin Lower abdomen and groin; upper medial thigh L2 Lateral side of the thigh Anterolateral thigh L3 Lower medial side of the thigh, above the knee Anterior and medial thigh; knee L4 Medial side of the great toe (digit I) Medial side of the leg and the medial malleolus L5 Medial side of digit II Lateral leg and the dorsum of the foot S1 Little toe (digit V) Lateral side of the foot and sole, and the heel S2 Back of the thigh Posterior thigh and calf; posterior aspect of the leg S3 Skin over the gluteal fold Buttock and perineum near the anus S4, S5 Perianal skin Perineum (tested separately, not on the limb) 2. Peripheral innervation: the cutaneous nerves The peripheral pattern is built from the branches of the lumbar and sacral plexuses. On the anterior thigh the femoral nerve, lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, obturator nerve, ilio-inguinal and genitofemoral nerves meet; the posterior thigh is the territory of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh; the leg receives the saphenous, sural, superficial fibular and deep fibular nerves; and the foot adds the calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve and the medial and lateral plantar nerves. Cutaneous nerve Roots Skin area supplied Iliohypogastric L1 Posterolateral gluteal skin and the skin above the pubis Ilio-inguinal L1 Upper medial thigh; root of the penis and anterior scrotum, or mons pubis and labium majus Genitofemoral (femoral branch) L1, L2 Upper central part of the anterior thigh Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh L2, L3 Lateral side of the thigh, down to the knee Femoral — anterior cutaneous branches L2–L4 Anterior thigh and the anteromedial side of the knee Femoral — saphenous nerve L2–L4 Medial side of the leg and the medial side of the foot as far as the ball of the great toe Obturator L2–L4 Medial side of the upper thigh Posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh S1–S3 Gluteal fold, upper medial thigh, posterior thigh and the upper half of the back of the leg Perforating cutaneous S2, S3 Skin over the medial part of the gluteal fold Clunial nerves (posterior rami) — Skin of the buttock, above and over the gluteus maximus Sural nerve (tibial with a communicating fibular branch) S1, S2 Lower posterolateral leg, lateral side of the foot and little toe Superficial fibular L5–S1 Lower lateral leg and the greater part of the dorsum of the foot and toes Deep fibular L5 Adjacent sides of toes I and II (the first web space) Tibial — medial calcaneal branches S1, S2 Medial side and sole of the heel Medial plantar S1–S3 Medial two-thirds of the sole and the medial three and a half digits Lateral plantar S2, S3 Lateral third of the sole and the lateral one and a half digits 3. How the two patterns are tested Sensation: light touch with cotton wool, pinprick, and temperature, compared side to side; the autonomous zones listed above are used because adjacent dermatomes overlap. Radicular pattern: loss confined to a dermatomal band, often with pain in the same segment, aggravated by coughing or straining; the straight-leg-raising test is positive in lumbar root compression. Peripheral pattern: loss in the territory of one named nerve, which may be smaller or larger than a single dermatome and often has a sharp boundary on the limb. Corresponding motor and reflex tests make the segmental diagnosis: hip flexion L1–L2; knee extension L3–L4 (patellar reflex); knee flexion L5–S2; plantarflexion S1–S2 (calcaneal reflex); toe adduction S2–S3. 4. Clinical correlations Meralgia paraesthetica — compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2–L3) under the inguinal ligament gives burning pain and numbness of the lateral thigh with no motor loss. L4 versus femoral nerve — an L4 root lesion affects the medial leg and great toe, weakens knee extension and tibialis anterior, and reduces the patellar reflex; a femoral neuropathy adds weakness of hip flexion and an absent patellar reflex with a smaller sensory field. L5 versus common fibular nerve — an L5 root lesion also weakens hip abduction (gluteus medius) and foot inversion (tibialis posterior), which are spared in a common fibular palsy; both cause weakness of dorsiflexion and eversion. S1 versus tibial nerve — an S1 root lesion reduces the ankle jerk and gives numbness of the little toe and lateral foot; a tibial nerve lesion adds weakness of the intrinsic foot muscles and loss of sole sensation. Referred pain — the hip joint refers pain to the knee through the femoral and obturator nerves, so hip disease may present as knee pain. Must-memorise Autonomous zones: L1 groin, L2 lateral thigh, L3 lower medial thigh, L4 medial great toe, L5 medial digit II, S1 little toe, S2 back of thigh, S3 gluteal fold, S4–S5 perianal skin. Leg and foot nerve map: saphenous medial, sural lateral, superficial fibular dorsum, deep fibular first web space, tibial branches sole and heel. Segmental reflex map: patellar reflex L3–L4; calcaneal reflex S1–S2. Radicular loss = band; peripheral loss = named nerve territory. Clinical / radiological correlation Herpes zoster of a lower thoracic or lumbar dermatome produces a painful vesicular rash in a band, and post-herpetic neuralgia may persist in the same segment. Diabetes mellitus produces a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy in a stocking distribution, beginning in the toes and sole, unlike a single dermatome or a single nerve territory. The sural nerve is harvested for nerve biopsy and for nerve grafting because its loss produces only a small area of numbness on the lateral foot. Compartment syndrome of the leg first produces pain and paraesthesia in the territory of the deep fibular nerve (first web space), before motor signs appear. Spinal cord lesions may leave a sensory level; a conus medullaris or cauda equina lesion produces saddle anaesthesia over S3–S5 with perianal numbness. Likely follow-up questions Which dermatomes overlap the knee? — L3, L4 and L5; the patellar reflex tests L3–L4. Which nerve supplies the medial side of the foot? — The saphenous nerve (femoral), while the lateral side of the foot is supplied by the sural nerve. What is a dermatome and what is an autonomous zone? — A dermatome is the skin supplied by one spinal nerve; the autonomous zone is the part of it with least overlap from neighbouring segments. Why does an L5 radiculopathy not equal a common fibular palsy? — The L5 root also supplies the gluteus medius and tibialis posterior through other nerves, which remain normal in a fibular palsy."},{"id":"t-II-15","p":"II","n":15,"t":"Muscles of the hip. Supra- and infrapiriform foramina (hiatuses).","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The gluteal region contains the muscles that move the hip joint: a superficial group — gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae — which abduct and extend the hip, and a deep group — piriformis, obturator internus, superior gemellus, inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris — which laterally rotate it. The iliopsoas is the powerful flexor of the hip. The greater sciatic foramen is divided by the piriformis into a suprapiriform foramen , which transmits the superior gluteal nerve and vessels, and an infrapiriform foramen , which transmits the sciatic nerve, the inferior gluteal nerve and vessels, the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels, the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, and the small nerves to the gemelli and quadratus femoris. 1. Groups and general actions Superficial abductors and extensors: gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, gluteus maximus and tensor fasciae latae. They abduct and extend the femur and stabilise the pelvis on the stance leg. Deep lateral rotators: piriformis, obturator internus, superior gemellus, inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris. They laterally rotate the extended femur and abduct the flexed femur (except the quadratus femoris, which is a pure lateral rotator). Flexor: the iliopsoas (psoas major and iliacus), which passes from the posterior abdominal wall through the gap under the inguinal ligament to the lesser trochanter. The gluteal nerves and vessels lie in the plane between the superficial and deep groups, which is why surgical approaches to the hip must respect this plane. 2. Muscle table Muscle Origin Insertion Action Innervation Piriformis (m. piriformis) Anterior surface of the sacrum between the anterior sacral foramina Medial side of the superior border of the greater trochanter Laterally rotates the extended femur; abducts the flexed femur Nerve to piriformis (S1, S2) Obturator internus Anterolateral wall of the true pelvis and the deep surface of the obturator membrane Medial side of the greater trochanter Laterally rotates the extended femur; abducts the flexed femur Nerve to obturator internus (L5–S2) Gemellus superior External surface of the ischial spine With the obturator internus tendon into the medial side of the greater trochanter Laterally rotates the extended femur; abducts the flexed femur Nerve to obturator internus (L5–S2) Gemellus inferior Upper aspect of the ischial tuberosity With the obturator internus tendon into the medial side of the greater trochanter Laterally rotates the extended femur; abducts the flexed femur Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4–S1) Quadratus femoris Lateral aspect of the ischium just anterior to the ischial tuberosity Quadrate tubercle on the intertrochanteric crest Laterally rotates the femur at the hip joint Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4–S1) Gluteus minimus External surface of the ilium between the inferior and anterior gluteal lines Linear facet on the anterolateral aspect of the greater trochanter Abducts the femur; holds the pelvis level over the stance leg; medially rotates the thigh Superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1) Gluteus medius External surface of the ilium between the anterior and posterior gluteal lines Elongate facet on the lateral surface of the greater trochanter Abducts the femur; holds the pelvis level over the stance leg; medially rotates the thigh Superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1) Gluteus maximus Fascia over gluteus medius, ilium behind the posterior gluteal line, dorsal sacrum, coccyx and sacrotuberous ligament Posterior aspect of the iliotibial tract and the gluteal tuberosity Extends and laterally rotates the thigh; stabilises the extended knee through the iliotibial tract Inferior gluteal nerve (L5–S2) Tensor fasciae latae Anterior superior iliac spine and the anterior iliac crest Superior and anterior aspects of the iliotibial tract Abducts, flexes and medially rotates the thigh; stabilises the knee in extension Superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1) Iliopsoas (psoas major + iliacus) Posterior abdominal wall: lumbar vertebrae and discs (psoas major); iliac fossa (iliacus) Lesser trochanter of the femur by a common tendon Powerful flexor of the thigh at the hip; contributes to lateral rotation Psoas major: anterior rami L1–L3; iliacus: femoral nerve (L2, L3) 3. The greater sciatic foramen and the two piriform foramina The greater sciatic foramen is bounded by the greater sciatic notch of the ilium, the sacrotuberous ligament , the sacrospinous ligament and the lateral border of the sacrum. The piriformis crosses the foramen and divides it into two passages: Passage Contents Suprapiriform foramen (above the piriformis) Superior gluteal nerve and superior gluteal artery and vein — the only structures that leave the pelvis above the muscle Infrapiriform foramen (below the piriformis) Sciatic nerve ; inferior gluteal nerve and inferior gluteal artery and vein; pudendal nerve and internal pudendal artery and vein (these turn around the ischial spine into the lesser sciatic foramen); posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh ; nerve to obturator internus and superior gemellus ; nerve to quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus The lesser sciatic foramen , between the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments and the ischium, transmits the tendon of obturator internus , the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels , and the nerve to obturator internus (the pudendal nerve, internal pudendal vessels and nerve to obturator internus loop around the ischial spine from the greater foramen). The perforating cutaneous nerve is the only nerve reaching the gluteal region that does not use the greater sciatic foramen: it pierces the sacrotuberous ligament. 4. Vessels of the gluteal region Superior gluteal artery (from the posterior trunk of the internal iliac) — divides into superficial and deep branches; anastomoses with the lateral and medial circumflex femoral arteries and the inferior gluteal artery. Inferior gluteal artery (from the anterior trunk of the internal iliac) — descends into the posterior thigh and anastomoses with the perforating arteries of the deep artery of the thigh and with the first perforating artery in the cruciate anastomosis. The gluteal veins drain to the internal iliac veins; superficial gluteal veins drain anteriorly into the femoral vein. Must-memorise Suprapiriform foramen = superior gluteal nerve + superior gluteal vessels only. Infrapiriform foramen = sciatic nerve, inferior gluteal nerve and vessels, pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels, posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, nerves to the gemelli and quadratus femoris. Medius, minimus and tensor fasciae latae: superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1). Gluteus maximus: inferior gluteal nerve (L5–S2). Deep rotators: piriformis (S1, S2); obturator internus and superior gemellus (L5–S2); inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris (L4–S1). Safe site for gluteal intramuscular injection: upper lateral quadrant of the buttock. Clinical / radiological correlation Trendelenburg sign: if the gluteus medius and minimus (superior gluteal nerve) are weak, the pelvis drops on the unsupported side during single-leg stance, and the trunk lurches to the weak side (Trendelenburg gait). Superior gluteal nerve injury may follow a posterior approach to the hip (total hip arthroplasty, intramedullary nailing) or a pelvic fracture, producing abduction weakness and a positive Trendelenburg test. Gluteal intramuscular injection into the lower medial quadrant risks the sciatic nerve and the inferior gluteal vessels; the upper lateral quadrant is used. Piriformis syndrome — the sciatic nerve may be compressed by a tight piriformis, giving deep buttock pain and sciatic-like radiation. Ischial bursitis and tenderness over the ischial tuberosity relate to the hamstring origins, which sit just below the gluteus maximus. Likely follow-up questions Which muscle forms the landmark of the greater sciatic foramen? — The piriformis; it divides the foramen into supra- and infrapiriform parts. Which two muscles act as abductors and medial rotators of the hip? — Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus (with tensor fasciae latae). Why do the deep rotators abduct a flexed femur? — Their line of pull changes when the femur is flexed, so the lateral rotators become abductors. Which nerve would be injured in a badly placed gluteal injection into the lower medial quadrant? — The sciatic nerve, with resulting weakness of the hamstrings and all leg and foot muscles. How does gluteus maximus differ from the other gluteal muscles? — It is the only muscle supplied by the inferior gluteal nerve and the only powerful extensor of the hip."},{"id":"t-II-16","p":"II","n":16,"t":"Subinguinal hiatus. Femoral canal.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The subinguinal hiatus ( hiatus subinguinalis ) is the gap between the inguinal ligament above and the anterior margin of the pelvic bone and pectineal line below, through which structures pass between the abdomen and the anterior thigh. It is divided by the iliopectineal arch into a lateral lacuna musculorum (iliopsoas, femoral nerve, lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh) and a medial lacuna vasorum , which is filled by the femoral sheath . The sheath has three compartments: the femoral artery laterally, the femoral vein in the middle, and the femoral canal medially, which contains lymphatics and whose upper opening — the femoral ring — is the site of femoral hernia . 1. Boundaries of the subinguinal hiatus Boundary Structure Anterior Inguinal ligament, from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle Posterior Pectineal line of the pubis and the pectineal ligament, with the pectineus muscle and the superior ramus of the pubis Medial Lacunar ligament (the reflected medial end of the inguinal ligament) and the pubic tubercle Lateral Anterior superior iliac spine and the iliac bone Subdivision The iliopectineal arch (a thickening of the iliopsoas fascia passing from the inguinal ligament to the iliopectineal eminence) divides the gap into the lacuna musculorum laterally and the lacuna vasorum medially 2. Lacuna musculorum The lacuna musculorum ( lacuna musculorum ) lies lateral to the iliopectineal arch and is the lateral half of the subinguinal hiatus. It transmits: Iliopsoas (psoas major and iliacus) — the muscle fills the lacuna and glides over the pelvic brim on its way to the lesser trochanter. Femoral nerve — lies in the groove between the psoas and iliacus, lateral to the femoral artery, and is not enclosed by the femoral sheath. Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh — passes through the lateral part of the gap, just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine, or through the inguinal ligament itself. The lateral part of the lacuna is therefore the route of the great flexor of the hip and of the motor nerve of the anterior thigh; a haematoma or tumour spreading here produces a femoral neuropathy with weak knee extension and absent knee jerk rather than a groin lump. 3. Lacuna vasorum and the femoral sheath The lacuna vasorum is the medial part of the subinguinal hiatus. It is occupied by the femoral sheath ( vagina femoralis ), a funnel-shaped sleeve of fascia continuous above with the transversalis fascia and the iliac fascia; below, it blends with the connective tissue of the vessels. It has three compartments: Compartment of the femoral sheath Contents Lateral Femoral artery Intermediate Femoral vein Medial — femoral canal Lymphatic vessels and one or more deep inguinal lymph nodes (the node of Cloquet or Rosenmüller) The femoral nerve lies lateral to the sheath, in the lacuna musculorum; it is therefore never inside the femoral sheath. The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve crosses the artery superficially. 4. The femoral canal and the femoral ring The femoral canal ( canalis femoralis ) is the medial, conical compartment of the femoral sheath. Its base opens inferiorly into the thigh at the saphenous opening region, and its upper opening — the femoral ring ( anulus femoralis ) — faces the abdominal cavity and is the only weak point of the lower anterior abdominal wall at this level. Boundary of the femoral ring Structure Anterior Inguinal ligament, with the medial part of the lacuna Posterior Pectineal ligament (Cooper's ligament) and the pectineus muscle Medial Lacunar ligament (Gimbernat's ligament) Lateral Femoral vein Contents Lymphatics and a deep inguinal lymph node; fat and loose connective tissue The ring is closed on its abdominal side only by peritoneum and the connective tissue of the femoral septum, which is why it gives way under raised intra-abdominal pressure. 5. Clinical significance Femoral hernia — abdominal viscera pass through the femoral ring, along the femoral canal and emerge through the saphenous opening into the medial thigh. The hernial sac appears below and lateral to the pubic tubercle (an inguinal hernia appears above and medial to it). The neck of the femoral canal is narrow, so femoral hernias are prone to irreducibility and strangulation ; they are more frequent in women (wider pelvis) and in middle-aged and elderly people, and are usually acquired, not congenital. Surgical approach: reduction is usually obtained through an incision over the groin, opening the anterior wall of the canal; the lacunar ligament may have to be divided to release the constriction, with care for the femoral vein laterally. Vascular access: the femoral artery and vein are reached just below the inguinal ligament; from medial to lateral the structures are femoral canal, femoral vein, femoral artery and femoral nerve, so the nerve is avoided by puncturing at or medial to the artery (venous access is medial to the artery) — never lateral to it. The femoral canal is medial to the vein, so a large medial haematoma after puncture is avoided by keeping the needle on the artery. Must-memorise Subinguinal hiatus = inguinal ligament above + pelvic bone/pectineal line below; divided by the iliopectineal arch. Lacuna musculorum: iliopsoas, femoral nerve, lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh. Lacuna vasorum: femoral sheath — artery (lateral), vein (intermediate), femoral canal (medial). Femoral ring: anterior inguinal ligament, posterior pectineal ligament, medial lacunar ligament, lateral femoral vein. Femoral hernia: below and lateral to the pubic tubercle; inguinal hernia: above and medial. Clinical / radiological correlation Femoral hernia versus inguinal hernia — the relation to the pubic tubercle is the key bedside test; femoral hernias have a much higher risk of strangulation and are therefore repaired early even if small. Femoral vein puncture is used for central venous access, pacing wires and catheterisation of the right heart and iliac veins; the artery is lateral and the femoral nerve further lateral still. Femoral neuropathy from a retroperitoneal or groin haematoma compresses the nerve in the lacuna musculorum but leaves the femoral vessels and their pulses normal. Radiology: ultrasound distinguishes a femoral hernia from an inguinal one; CT shows the neck of the sac medial to the femoral vein and below the inguinal ligament. Likely follow-up questions What divides the subinguinal hiatus into two lacunae? — The iliopectineal arch, running from the inguinal ligament to the iliopectineal eminence. Why is the femoral nerve not compressed by a femoral hernia? — It lies lateral to the femoral sheath, in the lacuna musculorum, so the hernia in the femoral canal cannot involve it directly. What is the clinical landmark of the femoral ring on the surface? — The pubic tubercle; a femoral hernia lies below and lateral to it, an inguinal hernia above and medial to it. What does the femoral canal contain in the normal subject? — Lymphatic vessels and deep inguinal lymph nodes embedded in fat. Which structure lies immediately lateral to the femoral ring? — The femoral vein, which is at risk when the lacunar ligament is divided during hernia repair."},{"id":"t-II-17","p":"II","n":17,"t":"Femoral triangle, adductor canal, popliteal fossa.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The femoral triangle ( trigonum femorale ) is the wedge-shaped depression below the inguinal ligament bounded by the sartorius laterally and the adductor longus medially, with the inguinal ligament as its base; from lateral to medial it contains the femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein and femoral canal . Its apex leads into the adductor canal , the subsartorial tunnel bounded by the vastus medialis, adductor longus and adductor magnus, which transmits the femoral vessels and the saphenous nerve to the adductor hiatus . The popliteal fossa is the diamond-shaped space behind the knee containing, from superficial to deep, the tibial nerve, popliteal vein and popliteal artery , with the common fibular nerve along its lateral border. 1. Femoral triangle Feature Structure Base Inguinal ligament Lateral border Medial border of the sartorius muscle Medial border Medial border of the adductor longus muscle Apex Where sartorius crosses adductor longus; continuous with the adductor canal Floor Laterally the iliopsoas , medially the pectineus , inferiorly the adductor longus Roof Fascia lata (with the cribriform fascia over the saphenous opening), superficial fascia and skin; superficial inguinal nodes and the great saphenous vein lie in the roof Contents from lateral to medial: femoral nerve and its branches; femoral artery; femoral vein; femoral canal with lymphatics. The femoral nerve is lateral to, and outside, the femoral sheath. Femoral artery branches in the triangle: superficial epigastric, superficial circumflex iliac, superficial external pudendal, deep external pudendal arteries, and the deep artery of the thigh (profunda femoris). Femoral nerve branches in the triangle: anterior cutaneous branches of the thigh, and the saphenous nerve which enters the adductor canal. Clinical landmark: the femoral artery is palpable just below the inguinal ligament, midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis. 2. Adductor canal (subsartorial canal) The adductor canal ( canalis adductorius ) begins at the apex of the femoral triangle and ends at the adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus. It is a musculo-aponeurotic tunnel: Boundary Structure Anterior (roof) Sartorius muscle and the strong vastoadductor membrane ( membrana vastoadductoria ) Lateral Vastus medialis Posterior/medial Proximally the adductor longus , distally the adductor magnus Contents Femoral artery , femoral vein (posterolateral to the artery), the saphenous nerve , and the nerve to vastus medialis ; the posterior division of the obturator nerve may also enter End Adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus — the artery and vein pass through it and become the popliteal vessels The canal is the route by which the femoral vessels reach the back of the knee; its narrow distal end and the fibrous vastoadductor membrane explain why the femoral artery is relatively fixed at the adductor hiatus and is torn in fractures and dislocations of the distal femur and knee. 3. Popliteal fossa Feature Structure Superomedial border Distal ends of semitendinosus and semimembranosus Superolateral border Distal end of biceps femoris Inferomedial border Medial head of gastrocnemius Inferolateral border Plantaris and the lateral head of gastrocnemius Floor Capsule of the knee joint, popliteal surface of the femur and posterior tibia, and the popliteus muscle below Roof Deep fascia, continuous with the fascia lata above and the deep fascia of the leg below; the small saphenous vein and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh pass through it Position Contents of the popliteal fossa Most superficial (superior side) Tibial nerve — enters under the margin of biceps femoris and leaves deep to the plantaris; the common fibular nerve runs along the lateral border under the biceps tendon and winds round the fibular neck Intermediate Popliteal vein — lies between nerve and artery, superficial to the artery Deepest Popliteal artery — difficult to palpate, gives its genicular and sural branches and divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries at the lower border of the popliteus Other Popliteal lymph nodes, fat, small genicular vessels, articular branches of the tibial and common fibular nerves 4. Comparison of the three topographies Region Anterior/lateral boundary Medial/posterior boundary Main contents Femoral triangle Sartorius; iliopsoas floor Adductor longus; pectineus floor Femoral nerve, artery, vein, canal, lymphatics; femoral sheath Adductor canal Sartorius and vastoadductor membrane; vastus medialis Adductor longus then adductor magnus Femoral artery and vein, saphenous nerve, nerve to vastus medialis Popliteal fossa Biceps femoris above, plantaris and lateral head of gastrocnemius below Semitendinosus and semimembranosus above, medial head of gastrocnemius below Tibial nerve, popliteal vein, popliteal artery, common fibular nerve, lymph nodes Must-memorise Femoral triangle boundaries: inguinal ligament (base), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial). Contents of the femoral triangle, lateral to medial: nerve, artery, vein, lymphatics (\"NAVL\"). Adductor canal contents: femoral artery, femoral vein, saphenous nerve, nerve to vastus medialis. Adductor hiatus: an opening in the adductor magnus where the femoral vessels become the popliteal vessels. Popliteal fossa contents, superficial to deep: tibial nerve → popliteal vein → popliteal artery (\"NVA\"); the common fibular nerve is lateral. Clinical / radiological correlation Femoral pulse and access: palpation at the midpoint of the inguinal ligament; the artery is the route for arterial cannulation, angiography and angioplasty. Femoral hernia emerges through the femoral canal at the medial end of the femoral triangle, below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Saphenous nerve entrapment in the adductor canal causes medial knee and leg pain without motor deficit; the nerve is also at risk during varicose vein stripping. Popliteal artery aneurysm (diameter exceeding 7 mm) is diagnosed by duplex Doppler and treated surgically because of thromboembolism and distal ischaemia. Baker's cyst (synovial cyst of the knee) presents as a popliteal swelling and must be distinguished from a popliteal aneurysm and from popliteal lymphadenopathy by ultrasound. Knee dislocation can stretch or tear the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus, and the common fibular nerve along the fibular neck. Likely follow-up questions What forms the apex of the femoral triangle and what does it lead into? — The crossing of the sartorius and adductor longus; it continues as the adductor canal. Where does the femoral artery become the popliteal artery? — In the adductor hiatus, at the lower end of the adductor canal. Which structures pass through the roof of the popliteal fossa? — The small saphenous vein and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. Why is the popliteal artery the deepest structure of the fossa? — It lies directly on the floor (capsule and popliteus), which is why its pulse is difficult to feel, especially in a muscular or obese patient. Which nerve is at risk in a distal femoral fracture? — The popliteal artery, and the common fibular nerve at the fibular neck after knee injury."},{"id":"t-II-18","p":"II","n":18,"t":"Topography of the medial and lateral malleolar regions.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The malleolar regions are the retromalleolar parts of the ankle region, one behind each malleolus. In the medial malleolar region all structures pass deep to the flexor retinaculum in the tarsal tunnel : from medial to lateral the tendons of tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus , the posterior tibial artery and veins , the tibial nerve , and the tendon of flexor hallucis longus . In the lateral malleolar region the tendons of fibularis longus and fibularis brevis pass behind the lateral malleolus under the fibular retinacula , with the sural nerve and small saphenous vein superficial to them. The malleolar regions are the site of the main tendinous, vascular and nervous passages between the leg and the foot. 1. Bony and general features Medial malleolus of the tibia and lateral malleolus of the fibula form the ankle mortise with the trochlea of the talus; the distal tibia and fibula are united by the tibiofibular syndesmosis . The posterior surface of the medial malleolus bears a broad groove for the tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus tendons; the posterior surface of the talus and the sustentaculum tali carry the groove for the flexor hallucis longus. The medial surface of the lateral malleolus has the malleolar fossa for the posterior tibiofibular ligament; the lateral surface of the calcaneus bears the fibular trochlea over which the fibular tendons glide. Both regions are crossed by the cutaneous branches of the superficial veins and nerves of the foot, and the deep fascia of the leg thickens here into the retinacula. 2. Medial malleolar region and the tarsal tunnel The tarsal tunnel is the fibro-osseous space behind the medial malleolus: its walls are the medial malleolus, the medial and posterior surfaces of the talus, the medial surface of the calcaneus and the sustentaculum tali, and its roof is the flexor retinaculum , which extends from the medial malleolus to the medial and inferior surface of the calcaneus. Order Structure (medial → lateral) Note 1 Tendon of tibialis posterior Most medial and most anterior; in its own compartment on the posterior surface of the medial malleolus; inserts mainly on the navicular tuberosity 2 Tendon of flexor digitorum longus Immediately lateral to tibialis posterior; its tendon crosses superficial to the tibialis posterior tendon near the ankle 3 Posterior tibial artery and veins The artery is the one palpable structure here: its pulse is felt midway between the medial malleolus and the calcaneus 4 Tibial nerve Lies immediately lateral/posterior to the artery; gives medial calcaneal branches and then divides into medial and lateral plantar nerves 5 Tendon of flexor hallucis longus Most lateral and most posterior, lying in the groove on the talus and sustentaculum tali; its tendon enters the sole superficial to the flexor digitorum longus tendon \"Tom, Dick, And Harry\" — T ibialis posterior, flexor D igitorum longus, posterior tibial A rtery (with veins), tibial N erve, flexor H allucis longus — the order of the structures in the tarsal tunnel. Each tendon has a synovial sheath; septa from the retinaculum separate the sheaths. Superficial to the flexor retinaculum, in the subcutaneous tissue, pass the great saphenous vein and the saphenous nerve in front of the medial malleolus, and the medial calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve over the heel. 3. Lateral malleolar region Behind the lateral malleolus the tendons of the lateral compartment of the leg pass through a groove and under two retinacula: Superior fibular retinaculum — extends between the lateral malleolus and the calcaneus. Inferior fibular retinaculum — attaches to the lateral surface of the calcaneus around the fibular trochlea and blends above with the inferior extensor retinaculum. Fibularis brevis — its tendon lies anterior to that of fibularis longus at the malleolus and is held in the upper compartment at the fibular trochlea. Fibularis longus — lies posterior to the brevis tendon, passes below the fibular trochlea, enters the groove on the cuboid and crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and base of metatarsal I. Superficial to the retinacula run the sural nerve and the small saphenous vein , posterior to the lateral malleolus. Anterior to the lateral malleolus are the tendons of the extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius and the sinus tarsi with the cervical ligament and interosseous talocalcaneal ligament. 4. Comparison of the two regions Feature Medial malleolar region Lateral malleolar region Bony groove Posterior surface of the medial malleolus (tibia) Posterior surface of the lateral malleolus (fibula) Deep fascia Flexor retinaculum Superior and inferior fibular retinacula Tendons Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus Fibularis longus, fibularis brevis; anteriorly extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius Vessels Posterior tibial artery and veins (in the tunnel); great saphenous vein superficially Small saphenous vein superficially; fibular arteries from the fibular artery Nerves Tibial nerve (in the tunnel), medial calcaneal branches, saphenous nerve superficially Sural nerve superficially Clinical landmark Posterior tibial pulse midway between the malleolus and the heel Site of inversion sprains and of fibular tendon dislocation Must-memorise Tarsal tunnel boundaries: bony groove (medial malleolus, talus, calcaneus, sustentaculum tali) + flexor retinaculum. Order in the tunnel: tibialis posterior → flexor digitorum longus → posterior tibial artery → tibial nerve → flexor hallucis longus . Lateral region: fibularis brevis anterior, fibularis longus posterior at the lateral malleolus. Superficial to the retinacula: great saphenous vein and saphenous nerve medially, small saphenous vein and sural nerve laterally. Posterior tibial pulse = midway between the medial malleolus and the calcaneus; dorsalis pedis pulse = dorsum of the foot between the EHL and EDL tendons. Clinical / radiological correlation Tarsal tunnel syndrome — compression of the tibial nerve behind the medial malleolus (post-traumatic fibrosis, ganglion, varicose veins, tenosynovitis) causes burning pain in the sole, worse at night, with sensory loss in the plantar territory and later weakness of the intrinsic foot muscles. Ankle sprain is usually an inversion injury: the anterior talofibular ligament is injured first, then the calcaneofibular ligament; the deltoid (medial) ligament is much stronger and usually survives. Malleolar fractures are classified by their relation to the tibiofibular syndesmosis (Danis–Weber); a medial malleolar fracture is commonly associated with a fibular fracture or a syndesmotic injury. Dislocation of the fibular tendons at the lateral malleolus follows rupture of the superior fibular retinaculum and causes a snapping sensation behind the lateral malleolus. Great saphenous vein cut-down is performed anterior to the medial malleolus, the most accessible part of the vein in an emergency. Likely follow-up questions What is the tarsal tunnel and what forms its roof? — The fibro-osseous space behind the medial malleolus, roofed by the flexor retinaculum. Which tendon lies in a separate groove on the talus and sustentaculum tali? — The flexor hallucis longus tendon, the most posterolateral structure in the tunnel. Which nerve supplies the heel? — The medial calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve (and the sural nerve laterally). Which structures cross the lateral malleolar region superficially? — The small saphenous vein and the sural nerve, posterior to the malleolus. Why is the medial malleolus more prone to pressure necrosis from a cast? — It is subcutaneous and overlies the tarsal tunnel, so pressure here compresses the tibial nerve and vessels."},{"id":"t-II-19","p":"II","n":19,"t":"Dorsum of the foot: muscles, fasciae, tendons, tendon sheaths, vessels and nerves.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The dorsum of the foot is covered by thin skin and loose superficial fascia containing the dorsal venous arch , the great and small saphenous veins and the cutaneous nerves (saphenous, superficial fibular, deep fibular, sural). Its deep fascia forms the superior and inferior extensor retinacula , under which the long extensor tendons and their synovial sheaths pass: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius . The intrinsic muscles are the extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis , both supplied by the deep fibular nerve . The arterial supply is the dorsalis pedis artery , with the deep fibular nerve lateral to it; the lateral part of the dorsum is supplied by the superficial fibular nerve. 1. Skin, superficial fascia and superficial veins Skin is thin, mobile and hair-bearing on the dorsum; it is loosely attached by a thin layer of superficial fascia, so dorsalis pedis pulsation and tendon contours are visible. Dorsal venous arch ( arcus venosus dorsalis pedis ) lies over the metatarsals and receives the dorsal digital veins; the great saphenous vein arises from its medial end and the small saphenous vein from its lateral end. Cutaneous nerves of the dorsum: the saphenous nerve (medial side), superficial fibular nerve (most of the dorsum and toes), deep fibular nerve (first web space) and sural nerve (lateral side and little toe). 2. Deep fascia and retinacula The deep fascia of the dorsum is continuous above with the deep fascia of the leg and below with the dorsal fascia of the toes. It is thickened into two extensor retinacula : Superior extensor retinaculum — a transverse thickening of deep fascia just above the ankle joint, attached to the anterior borders of the tibia and fibula. Inferior extensor retinaculum — Y-shaped; its base attaches to the lateral side of the upper surface of the calcaneus, one arm crosses medially to attach to the medial malleolus, and the other wraps around the medial side of the foot to the plantar aponeurosis. Fibular retinacula — superior and inferior, holding the fibularis longus and brevis tendons on the lateral side of the foot. The retinacula prevent bowstringing of the extensor tendons during dorsiflexion. 3. Muscles and tendons of the dorsum Muscle Origin Insertion Action Innervation Tibialis anterior (m. tibialis anterior) Lateral surface of the tibia and adjacent interosseous membrane Medial and inferior surfaces of the medial cuneiform and base of metatarsal I Dorsiflexes and inverts the foot; supports the medial arch Deep fibular nerve (L4, L5) Extensor hallucis longus Middle half of the medial surface of the fibula and interosseous membrane Base of the distal phalanx of the great toe Extends the great toe; dorsiflexes the foot Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1) Extensor digitorum longus Proximal half of the medial surface of the fibula and lateral tibial condyle Dorsal digital expansions into the bases of the distal and middle phalanges of toes II–V Extends toes II–V; dorsiflexes the foot Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1) Fibularis tertius Distal part of the medial surface of the fibula Dorsomedial surface of the base of metatarsal V Dorsiflexes and everts the foot Deep fibular nerve (L5, S1) Extensor digitorum brevis Superolateral surface of the calcaneus lateral to the tarsal sinus Lateral sides of the tendons of extensor digitorum longus to toes II–IV Extends toes II–IV Deep fibular nerve (S1, S2) Extensor hallucis brevis With the extensor digitorum brevis on the calcaneus Base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe Extends the metatarsophalangeal joint of the great toe Deep fibular nerve (S1, S2) The long extensor tendons pass beneath the extensor retinacula in a characteristic order from medial to lateral: tibialis anterior → extensor hallucis longus → dorsalis pedis artery → extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius . The tendons continue into the digits as the extensor hoods (dorsal digital expansions), which receive the insertions of the intrinsic muscles and distribute their pull. 4. Tendon sheaths Each long extensor tendon has a synovial sheath where it passes under the extensor retinacula; the sheaths extend a short distance into the dorsum of the foot. The sheath of the extensor hallucis longus and that of the tibialis anterior extend furthest distally and are the ones most commonly involved in tenosynovitis . The sheaths are separate from each other, so infection spreads along a single tendon rather than across the dorsum. 5. Vessels of the dorsum Dorsalis pedis artery ( arteria dorsalis pedis ) — continuation of the anterior tibial artery as it crosses the ankle joint; passes over the talus, navicular and intermediate cuneiform, and between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle becomes the deep plantar artery joining the deep plantar arch. Branches: medial and lateral tarsal arteries, the arcuate artery (giving three dorsal metatarsal arteries), the first dorsal metatarsal artery and the deep plantar artery. The pulse of the dorsalis pedis is felt against the tarsal bones between the tendons of the extensor hallucis longus and the extensor digitorum longus to the second toe. Dorsal metatarsal arteries anastomose with the plantar metatarsal arteries through perforating branches , so the dorsum and sole form one functional arterial unit. 6. Nerves of the dorsum Nerve Roots Area and branches Deep fibular L5 Lies lateral to the dorsalis pedis artery; supplies extensor digitorum brevis and partly the first two dorsal interossei; sensory to the first web space and adjacent sides of toes I and II Superficial fibular L5–S1 Pierces the deep fascia of the lower leg; supplies most of the skin of the dorsum and the dorsal digital nerves to the toes, except the first web space and the lateral side of the little toe Saphenous L2–L4 Medial side of the ankle and the medial side of the proximal foot Sural S1, S2 Lateral side of the foot and the dorsolateral surface of the little toe Must-memorise Dorsum muscles: extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis — both deep fibular nerve (S1, S2) ; they are the only intrinsic foot muscles not supplied by the tibial nerve. Order under the extensor retinacula: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, dorsalis pedis artery, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius. Dorsalis pedis branches: tarsal, arcuate, first dorsal metatarsal, deep plantar. Cutaneous map: saphenous medial, sural lateral, superficial fibular most of the dorsum, deep fibular first web space. Arterial pulses of the foot: dorsalis pedis on the dorsum, posterior tibial behind the medial malleolus. Clinical / radiological correlation Absent dorsalis pedis pulse may be normal when the artery is replaced by a large perforating branch of the fibular artery; the posterior tibial pulse and Doppler are then used to assess the foot. Laceration of the dorsum of the foot may divide extensor tendons and the dorsalis pedis artery; the tendons retract and must be repaired, and the artery is ligated if necessary because of the rich collateral supply. Foot drop (deep fibular nerve palsy) abolishes active dorsiflexion and extension of the toes, and the dorsalis pedis pulse remains palpable. The dorsal venous arch is a common site for peripheral venous cannulation in children and in patients with difficult veins. Tenosynovitis of the extensor sheaths presents as a painful, swollen dorsum with pain on passive flexion of the toes; it may follow a penetrating injury or gonococcal infection. Likely follow-up questions Which muscle is the only intrinsic muscle of the dorsum supplied by the deep fibular nerve and why does it matter clinically? — Extensor digitorum brevis; it is a landmark for the deep fibular nerve and is tested by extending toes II–IV against resistance. What forms the extensor hood and what is its function? — The expansion of the extensor tendons over the proximal phalanges, receiving intrinsic muscle insertions and distributing force so that the toe flexes at the metatarsophalangeal joint while extending at the interphalangeal joints. Where is the dorsalis pedis artery palpated? — On the dorsum, lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon and medial to the tendon to the second toe. Which nerve supplies the skin between the first and second toes? — The deep fibular nerve. What is the venous drainage of the dorsum? — The dorsal venous arch, with the great saphenous vein medially and the small saphenous vein laterally."},{"id":"t-II-20","p":"II","n":20,"t":"Sole (planta) of the foot: muscles, tendons, fasciae, compartments, vessels and nerves.","u":"part-2.html","x":"Core answer The sole ( planta pedis ) is covered by thick, keratinised skin and a strong plantar aponeurosis ; its deep fascia and intermuscular septa divide the soft tissues into medial, central and lateral compartments. The intrinsic muscles are arranged in four layers from plantar to dorsal — abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis and abductor digiti minimi; quadratus plantae and the four lumbricals; flexor hallucis brevis, adductor hallucis and flexor digiti minimi brevis; and the dorsal and plantar interossei. The long tendons of tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus and fibularis longus enter the sole and are guided by synovial sheaths. The sole is supplied by the medial and lateral plantar arteries (from the posterior tibial) — the lateral plantar artery forming the deep plantar arch with the deep plantar artery of the dorsalis pedis — and by the medial and lateral plantar nerves (from the tibial nerve). 1. Skin, superficial fascia and plantar aponeurosis Skin: very thick, especially over the heel and the metatarsal heads, firmly bound to the underlying aponeurosis by fibrous septa; it is hairless and richly innervated, and the thick stratum corneum protects the weight-bearing surface. Superficial fascia: contains a dense fat pad over the heel and the ball of the foot, divided into loculi by fibrous septa that act as shock absorbers. Plantar aponeurosis ( aponeurosis plantaris ) — the thickened deep fascia of the sole; it is anchored to the medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity and extends forward as longitudinal fibres that diverge into digital bands for the toes; distally the bands are joined by transverse fibres (superficial transverse metatarsal ligaments). It holds the skin of the sole, protects the vessels and nerves, and supports the longitudinal arch. Medial and lateral plantar septa pass from the margins of the aponeurosis to the bones, dividing the sole into compartments. 2. Fascial compartments of the sole The deep fascia and the two intermuscular septa divide the sole into three compartments: Compartment Boundaries Contents Medial Between the medial plantar septum and the first metatarsal Abductor hallucis, flexor hallucis brevis, tendon of flexor hallucis longus, medial plantar nerve and vessels Central Between the medial and lateral plantar septa Flexor digitorum brevis, quadratus plantae, lumbricals, tendons of flexor digitorum longus, lateral plantar nerve and vessels, deep plantar arch Lateral Lateral to the lateral plantar septum Abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, tendon of fibularis longus The interossei occupy the spaces between the metatarsals, deep to the central compartment, and are the deepest structures of the sole. 3. The four muscle layers Layer Muscle Origin Insertion Action Innervation 1 (superficial) Abductor hallucis Medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity, flexor retinaculum and plantar aponeurosis Medial side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe Abducts and flexes the great toe Medial plantar nerve (S1–S3) Flexor digitorum brevis Medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity and plantar aponeurosis Sides of the middle phalanges of toes II–V Flexes toes II–V at the proximal interphalangeal joints Medial plantar nerve (S1–S3) Abductor digiti minimi Medial and lateral processes of the calcaneal tuberosity and the band to metatarsal V Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the little toe Abducts the little toe Lateral plantar nerve (S1–S3) 2 Quadratus plantae Medial surface of the calcaneus and the lateral process of the calcaneal tuberosity Lateral side of the tendon of flexor digitorum longus Assists flexion of toes II–V; corrects the line of pull of the long flexor Lateral plantar nerve (S1–S3) Four lumbricals Tendons of flexor digitorum longus (first: medial side; others: adjacent sides) Medial free margins of the extensor hoods of toes II–V Flex the metatarsophalangeal and extend the interphalangeal joints First: medial plantar nerve; second to fourth: lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3) 3 Flexor hallucis brevis Plantar surface of the cuboid and lateral cuneiform; tendon of tibialis posterior Lateral and medial sides of the base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe (with sesamoids) Flexes the metatarsophalangeal joint of the great toe Medial plantar nerve (S1, S2) Adductor hallucis Transverse head: plantar ligaments of the lateral three toes; oblique head: bases of metatarsals II–IV and sheath of fibularis longus Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe Adducts the great toe Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3) Flexor digiti minimi brevis Base of metatarsal V and the sheath of fibularis longus Lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the little toe Flexes the little toe Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3) 4 (deepest) Dorsal interossei (four) Sides of adjacent metatarsals Extensor hoods and bases of the proximal phalanges of toes II–IV Abduct toes II–IV from the axis of the second toe Lateral plantar nerve; first two also partly deep fibular nerve (S2, S3) Plantar interossei (three) Medial sides of metatarsals III–V Extensor hoods and bases of the proximal phalanges of toes III–V Adduct toes III–V towards the axis of the second toe Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3) 4. Long tendons crossing the sole and their sheaths Tibialis posterior — its tendon enters the sole behind the medial malleolus and inserts mainly on the navicular tuberosity and medial cuneiform; it inverts the foot and supports the medial arch. Flexor digitorum longus — divides into four tendons for the lateral four toes; each tendon passes through the split of the corresponding flexor digitorum brevis tendon (the chiasma tendinum) and inserts on the distal phalanx. Flexor hallucis longus — the strongest flexor of the great toe; its tendon runs in the tarsal tunnel, crosses the sole and passes between the two sesamoid bones of the flexor hallucis brevis to the distal phalanx. Fibularis longus — its tendon crosses the sole in a fibrous tunnel deep to the long plantar ligament to reach the medial cuneiform and base of metatarsal I; it everts the foot and supports the transverse arch. Sheaths: the flexor tendons are surrounded by synovial sheaths in the tarsal tunnel and in the digits (digital fibrous sheaths), which are separate from the sheaths of the dorsum — hence a deep-space infection of the sole tends to remain plantar. 5. Vessels of the sole Posterior tibial artery enters the sole through the tarsal tunnel, gives a small medial plantar artery and a much larger lateral plantar artery . Lateral plantar artery passes anterolaterally between the quadratus plantae and flexor digitorum brevis, reaches the base of metatarsal V, and curves medially as the deep plantar arch on the metatarsal bases and interossei. The arch is completed between metatarsals I and II by the deep plantar artery (terminal branch of the dorsalis pedis) and gives four plantar metatarsal arteries , digital branches and three perforating arteries to the dorsum. Medial plantar artery supplies muscles and skin of the medial side of the sole and ends by joining the digital branch to the medial side of the great toe. The pulse of the posterior tibial artery is felt in the tarsal tunnel and, more distally, between the medial malleolus and the heel. 6. Nerves of the sole Nerve Roots Motor supply Cutaneous supply Medial plantar S1–S3 Abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, first lumbrical Medial two-thirds of the sole and the medial three and a half digits Lateral plantar S2, S3 Quadratus plantae, second to fourth lumbricals, adductor hallucis, flexor digiti minimi brevis, all interossei Lateral third of the sole and the lateral one and a half digits Medial calcaneal (from the tibial nerve) S1, S2 None Medial side and sole of the heel 7. Arches of the foot Medial longitudinal arch — calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuneiforms and metatarsals I–III; the higher arch, supported by the plantar aponeurosis, the plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament , and the tendons of tibialis posterior and tibialis anterior. Lateral longitudinal arch — calcaneus, cuboid and metatarsals IV–V; flatter and more rigid, supported by the long and short plantar ligaments and the plantar aponeurosis. Transverse arch — formed by the cuneiforms, cuboid and metatarsal bases; supported by the fibularis longus tendon, which crosses the sole obliquely. The intrinsic muscles act as dynamic supports of the arches during standing and walking, especially the first layer and the flexor hallucis brevis. Must-memorise Four layers, plantar to dorsal: (1) abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, abductor digiti minimi; (2) quadratus plantae, four lumbricals; (3) flexor hallucis brevis, adductor hallucis, flexor digiti minimi brevis; (4) dorsal and plantar interossei. Medial plantar nerve supplies four muscles: abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, first lumbrical. Lateral plantar nerve supplies everything else, including all interossei. Deep plantar arch = lateral plantar artery + deep plantar artery of the dorsalis pedis, between metatarsals I and II. Long tendons entering the sole: tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, fibularis longus. Three compartments: medial, central, lateral; the interossei lie deepest between the metatarsals. Clinical / radiological correlation Plantar fasciitis — degeneration of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal insertion; heel pain on first steps in the morning, thickening of the aponeurosis on MRI, and sometimes a calcaneal spur. Flat foot (pes planus) — loss of the medial longitudinal arch, often from dysfunction of the tibialis posterior tendon in adults; the arch fails to recover on toe-standing. Morton's neuroma — enlargement of a common plantar nerve, usually in the third interspace, with pain on walking and a sensation of a pebble under the ball of the foot. Foot compartment syndrome after crush injury produces tense swelling, pain on passive toe movement and sensory loss in the sole; it requires emergency fasciotomy. Diabetic foot — sensory neuropathy removes protective sensation, so pressure at the metatarsal heads and heel produces painless plantar ulcers; the plantar arterial supply is assessed by the posterior tibial and dorsalis pedis pulses. Likely follow-up questions Which muscles form the first layer of the sole and how are they arranged? — From medial to lateral: abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, abductor digiti minimi. Which muscle is supplied by the medial plantar nerve but belongs to the second layer? — The first lumbrical. What crosses the sole to reach the medial side of the foot? — The tendon of fibularis longus, which supports the transverse arch. Which arteries form the deep plantar arch and where does it lie? — The lateral plantar artery and the deep plantar branch of the dorsalis pedis; it lies on the bases of the metatarsals and the interossei, deep in the central compartment. Why is the skin of the sole both thick and highly sensitive? — It is a weight-bearing friction surface with a thick keratin layer and fibrous septa binding it to the aponeurosis, but it is also richly innervated for protective pain sensation and position sense. Which ligaments support the medial longitudinal arch? — The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament, the long and short plantar ligaments and the plantar aponeurosis, assisted by tibialis posterior and tibialis anterior."},{"id":"t-III-1","p":"III","n":1,"t":"The vertebral column: gross anatomy, syndesmology and X-ray anatomy.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The vertebral column is the segmented axial skeleton of the trunk: about 33 vertebrae — 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral and 3–4 coccygeal — united by intervertebral symphyses (discs) , zygapophysial synovial joints and a continuous system of ligaments, and arranged in four curvatures (cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, lumbar lordosis, sacral kyphosis). A typical vertebra is a weight-bearing body plus an arch that encloses the vertebral foramen. The syndesmology — discs, zygapophysial joints, anterior and posterior longitudinal, flaval, interspinous, supraspinous and intertransverse ligaments — explains why the column is stiff in the thorax and mobile in the cervical and lumbar regions, and the same elements form the checklist of its X-ray anatomy : alignment, body height, disc spaces, pedicles, intervertebral foramina and facet joints. 1. Number, regions and curvatures The column extends from the skull to the coccyx and is the axis of support of the trunk: it transmits body weight through the sacro-iliac joints to the pelvis and lower limbs, carries and positions the head, and gives attachment to the muscles that move the trunk and limbs. The vertebrae are segmental , and each region carries the imprint of its function — mobility in the neck, a rib-bearing stability in the thorax, weight transmission in the lumbar region. Table 1 — Regional characteristics of the vertebrae Region Number Distinguishing features Cervical 7 Small, square body; foramen transversarium in each transverse process (transmits the vertebral artery, C6–C1); bifid spinous process ; triangular vertebral foramen; laterally raised uncinate processes (C3–C7). CI (atlas) lacks a body and CII (axis) bears the dens; CVII is the vertebra prominens . Thoracic 12 Heart-shaped body with superior and inferior costal demifacets ; a facet on the transverse process for the tubercle of its own rib; long spinous process running obliquely downwards; circular vertebral foramen and a strong arch. TX (often TIX) has a single complete costal facet; TXI and TXII bear single facets and have no transverse costal facets. Lumbar 5 Largest vertebrae; broad cylindrical body; triangular foramen; transverse processes are costal processes ( processus costales ); mammillary processes on the superior articular processes and accessory processes behind the costal processes; LV has massive transverse processes for the iliolumbar ligament. Sacrum 5 (fused) Base (with promontory and superior articular processes), apex, ala with the auricular surface, four pairs of anterior and posterior sacral foramina for the anterior and posterior rami of S1–S4, sacral canal, median, intermediate and lateral sacral crests. Coccyx 3–4 (up to 5) Rudimentary and usually fused; no vertebral arches, therefore no vertebral canal; provides attachment for muscles and ligaments and takes part in supporting the body when sitting. Seen from the side the column shows four curvatures. The cervical lordosis and lumbar lordosis are secondary curvatures (concave posteriorly): they appear after birth when the infant lifts the head and begins to stand. The thoracic kyphosis and sacral kyphosis are primary curvatures (concave anteriorly) and are retained from the C-shaped embryonic spine. The secondary curvatures bring the line of gravity into a vertical line, so the upright posture is maintained with the least muscular effort, and the alternating curves increase elasticity and spread the load. Abnormal curvatures are hyperlordosis , hyperkyphosis , scoliosis (a lateral curvature in the frontal plane, usually with rotation of the vertebrae) and gibbus (an angular kyphotic prominence). 2. The typical vertebra: parts and their roles Every vertebra is built on the same plan and each element is named in the Terminologia Anatomica; knowing the role of each part is what allows the student to read a radiograph later. Table 2 — Parts of a typical vertebra Part Morphology and function Vertebral body ( corpus vertebrae ) Anterior weight-bearing block, linked to its neighbours by intervertebral discs and the longitudinal ligaments. Its size increases from CII to LV as the load increases; CI has no body (it becomes the dens of CII). Vertebral arch ( arcus vertebrae ) Two pedicles (pillars attached to the body) and two laminae meeting in the midline; forms the lateral and posterior walls of the vertebral foramen. Vertebral foramen and canal ( foramen vertebrale ) All foramina together form the vertebral canal from CI to SV; it contains the spinal cord and meninges, an extradural space with fat and a venous plexus, and the proximal parts of the spinal nerves. Spinous process ( processus spinosus ) Midline posterior lever for muscles, ligaments and fascia; long and oblique in the thorax, bifid in the neck, short and stout in the lumbar region. Transverse process ( processus transversus ) Lateral lever containing the fused costal element ; in the thorax it carries a facet for the tubercle of its own rib. Articular processes Superior and inferior processes bearing the zygapophysial facets; their orientation determines what movement each region allows. Vertebral notches & intervertebral foramen Each pedicle is notched above and below. The inferior notch of the vertebra above plus the superior notch of the vertebra below form the intervertebral foramen , bordered anteriorly by the disc and adjacent bodies and posteriorly by the zygapophysial joint; it transmits the spinal nerve and vessels. The 31 pairs of spinal nerves are 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal. 3. Syndesmology: the joints of the column A typical vertebra has six joints with its neighbours: four synovial (two above, two below) and two symphyses (one above, one below), each symphysis containing an intervertebral disc ( discus intervertebralis ). Movement between two vertebrae is small, but the effects are additive along the column. The intervertebral disc fills the gap between the hyaline cartilage plates covering the vertebral bodies. Its outer part, the annulus fibrosus , is fibrocartilage arranged in concentric lamellae and is continuous with the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments; its centre, the nucleus pulposus , is mucoid in the young, rich in water and proteoglycans, and becomes more fibrous with age. Discs extend from C2 to the sacrum (including L5–S1), are thinnest in the thoracic and thickest in the lumbar region, and are avascular — they are nourished by diffusion and carry nerve fibres only peripherally. Table 3 — Joints and connections of the vertebral column Joint Type Surfaces Movement and notes Intervertebral symphysis Secondary cartilaginous (symphysis) Vertebral bodies with hyaline plates and an intervening disc Small movements that summate; disc herniation compresses the nerve root in the foramen. Zygapophysial joint ( articulatio zygapophysialis ) Plane synovial Superior and inferior articular processes Strong but lax capsule, no significant accessory ligaments; range set by facet orientation. Uncovertebral joint (joint of Luschka) Cleft without capsule or synovium Uncinate process of C3–C7 and the body above Appears after the first year of life; arthrosis in adulthood may cause pain. Lumbosacral joint Symphysis plus zygapophysial joints LV and the sacrum Reinforced by the iliolumbar ligament from the transverse process of LV to the ilium. Sacrococcygeal joint Symphysis Sacrum and coccyx Reinforced by small ligaments; slight movement. Sacro-iliac joint Synovial with very strong ligaments Auricular surfaces of the sacrum and ilium Anterior, posterior and interosseous sacro-iliac ligaments; transmits weight to the lower limb. 4. The ligaments of the column and the three clinical columns The ligaments are the passive restraints of the column and are the structures the examiner wants named with their attachments and their main action. Table 4 — Ligaments of the vertebral column Ligament Attachments Action Anterior longitudinal (lig. longitudinale anterius) Anterior surfaces of the vertebral bodies and discs, from the base of the skull to the anterior surface of the sacrum Chief restraint of extension ; broad and strong. Posterior longitudinal (lig. longitudinale posterius) Posterior surfaces of the bodies, within the vertebral canal; its upper part is the tectorial membrane Chief restraint of flexion ; weaker and narrower laterally, which is why discs herniate posterolaterally. Ligamenta flava Between the laminae of adjacent vertebrae; predominantly elastic Resist separation of the laminae in flexion and help return the column to the upright position. Interspinous ligaments Between adjacent spinous processes, blending with the supraspinous ligament and the ligamenta flava Limit flexion. Supraspinous ligament Along the tips of the spinous processes, CVII to the sacrum Limit flexion; continuous above with the ligamentum nuchae. Ligamentum nuchae Triangular sheet from the external occipital protuberance and foramen magnum to the spinous process of CVII, attached deeply to the posterior tubercle of CI and the cervical spinous processes Supports the head, resists flexion and facilitates return to the anatomical position. Intertransverse ligaments Between adjacent transverse processes Limit lateral flexion to the opposite side. For trauma the column is divided into three clinical columns : anterior (vertebral bodies and anterior longitudinal ligament), middle (vertebral bodies and posterior longitudinal ligament) and posterior (ligamenta flava, interspinous and supraspinous ligaments, plus the ligamentum nuchae in the neck). Injury of one column is usually stable; injury of two or more is usually unstable and needs fixation. At the craniocervical junction the ligaments are so critical that disruption risks brainstem and cord injury. 5. Movements and their regional distribution The column flexes, extends, laterally flexes, rotates and circumducts. The range in each region follows the orientation of the zygapophysial facets: Cervical: facets almost horizontal — maximum mobility, and the first two vertebrae are specialised (III.2) so that nodding occurs at the atlanto-occipital and rotation at the atlanto-axial joints. Thoracic: facets approximately in the coronal plane and the ribs splint the region — flexion and extension are limited, rotation is relatively free. Lumbar: facets approximately sagittal and interlocking — flexion and extension are the main movements, rotation is restricted. Extension is limited by the anterior longitudinal ligament and by the approximation of spinous processes; flexion by the posterior longitudinal ligament, ligamenta flava, interspinous and supraspinous ligaments and the capsules. Rotation is checked by the alar ligaments at the top of the column and by the orientation of the facets below. 6. X-ray anatomy of the vertebral column Plain radiographs remain the first examination, and the examiner expects a systematic reading : alignment, bone (body height, endplates, pedicles, processes), cartilage (disc spaces), and soft tissue. CT best shows fractures and bone destruction; MRI shows the cord, dural sac, subarachnoid space, discs and nuclei pulposi, the ligamenta flava and the extradural space. Table 5 — What each projection displays Projection Structures assessed on the film Anteroposterior Vertebral bodies and pedicles (paired oval densities flanking the midline spinous process), the interpedicular distance, transverse and spinous processes; in the thorax the ribs and the orientation of the spinous processes; in the cervical region an open-mouth view shows the dens and the lateral masses of the atlas. Lateral Alignment of the anterior and posterior surfaces of the bodies, vertebral body height and shape , endplates, intervertebral disc spaces , intervertebral foramina (bounded by the pedicles), facet joints and the spinous processes. Oblique (lumbar) The pars interarticularis , seen as the neck of the “Scottie dog”: transverse process = nose, pedicle = eye, superior articular process = ear, inferior articular process = front leg; a break in the neck is spondylolysis. CT / MRI Fracture lines, bone destruction and metastases; the spinal cord, cauda equina, dural sac, subarachnoid CSF, nuclei pulposi, ligamenta flava and extradural venous plexus; in the sacrum the cartilaginous plates between the fused vertebrae remain visible. Nuclear medicine Increased uptake (for example labelled glucose or bone tracers) marks metastases, which are common in the vertebrae. Must-memorise Counts: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 3–4 coccygeal ; 31 pairs of spinal nerves (8 C, 12 T, 5 L, 5 S, 1 Co). A typical vertebra has six joints with its neighbours: 4 zygapophysial (synovial) + 2 symphyses (discs). Anterior longitudinal ligament limits extension; posterior longitudinal ligament limits flexion ; ligamenta flava are elastic. Discs run from C2 to the sacrum , are thinnest in the thorax and thickest in the lumbar region , and are avascular. Curvatures: cervical and lumbar lordoses are secondary ; thoracic and sacral kyphoses are primary . Facet orientation — cervical horizontal, thoracic coronal, lumbar sagittal — predicts the movement of each region. Clinical / radiological correlation Disc herniation: an annular tear allows nuclear material to protrude, usually posterolaterally, into the intervertebral foramen, compressing the spinal nerve root; in the cervical region protrusions often ossify into disc-osteophyte bars. Diagnosis is by MRI, and the level must be identified before discectomy/laminectomy. Vertebral fractures: the three-column concept decides stability. Wedge (crush) fractures of osteoporotic vertebrae occur in the thoracolumbar region and are treated by vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty — cement is injected through the pedicle under X-ray guidance. Spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis: a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis (most often at LIV–LV) lets the body slip forwards. Spina bifida: failure of fusion of the vertebral arches. Occulta (defect of LV or SI, asymptomatic, sometimes a tuft of hair) and the severe form with meningocele or myelomeningocele and neurological deficit. Metastases are common in vertebral bodies and may cause collapse or extrude into the canal; sacralisation of LV and lumbarisation of SI alter the counting of levels; fused cervical vertebrae occur in Klippel–Feil syndrome. Likely follow-up questions Why does the vertebral body increase in size from CII to LV? — The load increases caudally, and the trabeculae and body adapt to it. Why do discs herniate posterolaterally? — The posterior longitudinal ligament is narrow and weak laterally, and the annulus is thinnest behind. How do you identify LV on an MRI? — By the iliolumbar ligament running from its transverse process to the iliac crest. Where is a lumbar puncture performed and what does the needle traverse? — Between the spinous processes of the lumbar vertebrae (the arches do not overlap here): supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, ligamentum flavum, extradural space, dura and arachnoid, into the subarachnoid space. What is the pars interarticularis? — The segment of the lamina between the superior and inferior zygapophysial processes; its fracture is seen on the oblique film as a break in the neck of the “Scottie dog”."},{"id":"t-III-2","p":"III","n":2,"t":"Functional anatomy of the atlantooccipital and atlantoaxial joints.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer Two joint complexes connect the skull to the cervical spine. The atlanto-occipital joints are paired biaxial ellipsoid (condyloid) synovial joints between the convex occipital condyles and the slightly concave superior articular facets of the atlas; they produce nodding (flexion and extension). The atlanto-axial joints are the median atlanto-axial joint — a pivot between the dens and the anterior arch of the atlas, held by the transverse ligament — plus two lateral plane joints ; together they act as a single pivot for rotation of the head . Stability depends on the transverse, alar, apical and cruciform ligaments and on the tectorial membrane, which is why their injury can be fatal. 1. Osteology of the atlas and the axis The first two cervical vertebrae are so modified for the head that they are examined as a separate topic. The atlas (CI) is a ring with no body — the body of CI fuses with that of CII during development and becomes the dens — so there is no intervertebral disc between CI and CII. The axis (CII) carries the tooth-like dens upwards from its body. Table 1 — The atlas and the axis Feature Atlas (CI) Axis (CII) Body Absent Present, bearing the dens (odontoid process) with an anterior and a posterior articular facet Arches Anterior arch (anterior tubercle, facet for the dens = dental fovea) and posterior arch (groove for the vertebral artery, posterior tubercle) Vertebral arch with pedicles and laminae; vertebral foramen Lateral masses Superior articular facet — elongated, bean-shaped, concave, often divided — for the occipital condyle; inferior facet circular and flat Superior articular facet for the inferior facet of the atlas; inferior articular facet for CIII Transverse process Large, protruding further laterally than in other cervical vertebrae — levers for rotator muscles; foramen transversarium Transverse process with foramen transversarium Spinous process Replaced by the posterior tubercle Spinous process, often bifid Special features Oval attachment facets on the medial surfaces of the lateral masses for the transverse ligament; impressions on the dens side for the alar ligaments Dens: anterior facet for the anterior arch of the atlas; superolateral circular impressions for the alar ligaments 2. The atlanto-occipital joints The two atlanto-occipital joints ( articulatio atlantooccipitalis ) are formed by the convex occipital condyles and the slightly concave superior articular facets of the atlas . Each is a biaxial ellipsoid (condyloid) synovial joint with its own capsule. They are reinforced by the anterior and posterior atlanto-occipital membranes , which are the upward continuations of the anterior longitudinal ligament and of the ligamentum flavum respectively. Function. Because the condyles are convex and the facets concave, movement is essentially flexion and extension — the nodding movement of the head — with a small amount of lateral flexion; rotation is negligible here because the capsule and the alar ligaments are tight. The two joints move together and share the movement with the cervical intervertebral joints, so nodding looks like a movement of the whole neck even though its specific joint is the atlanto-occipital. 3. The atlanto-axial joints: three joints, one pivot The atlanto-axial complex consists of one median and two lateral joints. In the median atlanto-axial joint ( articulatio atlantoaxialis mediana ) the dens articulates anteriorly with the dental fovea of the anterior arch of the atlas and posteriorly with the transverse ligament of the atlas ; two synovial cavities are therefore present. The lateral atlanto-axial joints are plane synovial joints between the inferior articular facets of the atlas and the superior articular facets of the axis. Together the three joints behave as a pivot : the atlas, carrying the skull, rotates around the vertical axis of the dens. The dens is the pivot and the transverse ligament is the restraint that keeps it in place against the anterior arch. Table 2 — The craniovertebral joints Joint Type Articular surfaces Movement Atlanto-occipital (paired) Biaxial ellipsoid (condyloid) synovial Occipital condyle + superior articular facet of the atlas Flexion and extension (nodding), slight lateral flexion; no significant rotation Median atlanto-axial Pivot synovial, two cavities Dens + dental fovea of the anterior arch (anteriorly) and transverse ligament (posteriorly) Rotation of the atlas (and head) around the dens Lateral atlanto-axial (paired) Plane synovial Inferior articular facet of the atlas + superior articular facet of the axis Small gliding accompanying rotation; some lateral flexion 4. The ligaments of the craniovertebral region These ligaments are the real answer to “why is the craniovertebral junction stable?”; each must be named with its attachments and its restraining action. Table 3 — Ligaments of the craniovertebral junction Ligament Attachments Action Anterior atlanto-occipital membrane Anterior margin of the foramen magnum to the anterior arch of the atlas; continuation of the anterior longitudinal ligament Limits extension Posterior atlanto-occipital membrane Posterior margin of the foramen magnum to the posterior arch of the atlas; continuation of the ligamentum flavum Limits flexion; the vertebral artery pierces it Transverse ligament of the atlas Runs between the oval tubercles on the medial surfaces of the lateral masses of the atlas, passing behind the dens Holds the dens against the anterior arch ; prevents anterior displacement of the atlas — the key stabiliser Cruciform ligament Transverse ligament plus superior and inferior longitudinal bands (the latter to the occipital bone and to the body of the axis) Completes the ring around the dens Alar ligaments (paired) Superolateral surfaces of the dens to the medial surfaces of the occipital condyles Check excessive rotation and lateral flexion of the head Apical ligament of the dens Apex of the dens to the anterior margin of the foramen magnum Minor midline restraint Tectorial membrane Broad upper continuation of the posterior longitudinal ligament, from the body of CII to the intracranial aspect of the base of the skull; covers all the ligaments above from behind Limits flexion; protects the cord 5. Functional anatomy: how the movement is shared The functional division of labour is the point of the topic: the atlanto-occipital joints are for nodding, the atlanto-axial joints for turning . Flexion and extension of the head occur largely at the atlanto-occipital joints, and rotation of the head occurs as the atlas and the attached skull rotate on the axis. In practice the two complexes work together — turning the head is accompanied by slight extension and lateral flexion, and nodding is accompanied by small rotations — and they are assisted by the suboccipital muscles (rectus capitis posterior major and minor, obliquus capitis superior and inferior), which extend and rotate the head and are all supplied by the posterior ramus of C1 . Stability is therefore bought by bone shape plus ligaments, not by muscles: the transverse ligament resists anterior translation of the atlas, the alar ligaments limit rotation, the capsules and membranes limit the extremes of flexion and extension, and the tectorial membrane lies between these ligaments and the spinal cord. This is why a ligamentous injury here is far more serious than a sprain elsewhere in the spine. 6. Radiology and clinical anatomy The routine film is the open-mouth anteroposterior (odontoid) view , which shows the dens between the lateral masses of the atlas and the symmetry of the two lateral atlanto-axial joints, together with the lateral view , on which the relationship between the anterior arch of the atlas and the dens (the atlantodental interval) is measured — widening indicates failure of the transverse ligament. CT defines fractures; MRI shows the ligaments, the tectorial membrane and the spinal cord; flexion–extension films demonstrate instability. Must-memorise Atlanto-occipital = biaxial ellipsoid synovial joint, paired, between occipital condyles and the superior facets of the atlas; nodding . Atlanto-axial = median pivot (dens + anterior arch + transverse ligament) + two lateral plane joints ; rotation . The transverse ligament holds the dens; the alar ligaments limit rotation; the apical ligament is minor; the tectorial membrane is the upper posterior longitudinal ligament. No intervertebral disc between CI and CII; the body of CI becomes the dens of CII. Suboccipital muscles = posterior ramus of C1. Clinical / radiological correlation Jefferson (burst) fracture of the atlas: axial loading (diving into shallow water, head hitting the roof of a car) breaks the closed ring at more than one site — seen on CT. Hangman's fracture: hyperextension with distraction fractures the pedicles of CII with spondylolisthesis of C2 on C3. Dens fracture: hyperextension or flexion may fracture the tip or base of the dens or the body of the axis; the dens fragment can move with the atlas and compress the cord. Transverse ligament failure (trauma, rheumatoid arthritis, laxity syndromes) allows atlanto-axial subluxation with a widened atlantodental interval; the cord and brainstem are at risk. Craniocervical instability carries a high risk of cord injury: quadriplegia, respiratory compromise from phrenic paralysis ( C3–C5 ) and hypotension from interruption of the sympathetic outflow. Likely follow-up questions Why is there no disc between CI and CII? — The body of CI has fused with that of CII to form the dens. Which ligament would you test in a patient with suspected atlanto-axial instability? — The transverse ligament, indirectly by the atlantodental interval on the lateral film; MRI shows it directly. Which joint produces “yes” and which produces “no”? — Atlanto-occipital: nodding; atlanto-axial: rotation. What are the contents of the suboccipital triangle and its boundaries? — Rectus capitis posterior major and obliquus capitis superior and inferior bound it; it contains the vertebral artery and the posterior ramus of C1. Why is the open-mouth view needed? — The dens lies behind the anterior arch of the atlas and is obscured by the mandible and maxilla on the standard AP film."},{"id":"t-III-3","p":"III","n":3,"t":"The bones and joints of the thorax. The movements of respiration.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The bony thorax is built of 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs ( costae ) with costal cartilages ( cartilagines costales ) and the sternum ( sternum ) . The superior thoracic aperture (TI, rib I, manubrium) is open to the neck; the inferior thoracic aperture (TXII, ribs XI and XII, costal margin, xiphoid process) is closed by the diaphragm. Ribs articulate with the vertebrae at the costovertebral joints (joint of the head of the rib and costotransverse joint), with the sternum at the sternocostal joints , and with each other at the interchondral joints . Respiration changes thoracic volume in three dimensions: vertically by descent of the diaphragm, anteroposteriorly by the pump-handle movement of the ribs and sternum, and transversely by the bucket-handle movement of the rib shafts. 1. The bones of the thorax: the ribs There are 12 pairs of ribs , each ending anteriorly in a costal cartilage . The upper seven are true ribs because their cartilages reach the sternum; ribs VIII–X are false ribs whose cartilages join the cartilage of the rib above; ribs XI and XII are floating ribs with no anterior connection. The costal cartilages give the wall its mobility and elasticity. A typical rib has a curved shaft with an anterior and a posterior end. The posterior end carries a head (with two articular facets separated by a crest), a neck and a tubercle ; the tubercle has an articular part with an oval facet for the transverse process and a rough non-articular part for ligaments. The shaft bends at the angle and bears the costal groove on its inferior, internal surface, which protects the intercostal vessels and nerve. Table 1 — Typical and atypical ribs Rib Distinguishing features III–IX (typical) Head with two facets; neck; tubercle with an articular facet; angle; costal groove. I Short, flat and horizontal, with broad superior and inferior surfaces, sloping down to the manubrium. The head has a single facet for TI. The superior surface shows the scalene tubercle between two grooves — a groove for the subclavian vein anteriorly and for the subclavian artery posteriorly. II Flat like rib I but about twice as long; articulates in the typical way; its cartilage is the landmark of the sternal angle. X Head with a single facet for its own vertebra. XI and XII No neck and no tubercle; articulate only with the bodies of their own vertebrae; short, little curved, pointed anteriorly. 2. The sternum and the thoracic apertures The adult sternum has three parts. The manubrium bears the jugular (suprasternal) notch , the clavicular fossae, a facet for the first costal cartilage and a demifacet for the upper half of the second. The body has a demifacet for the lower half of the second cartilage, facets for the third to sixth cartilages and a demifacet for the seventh; its anterior surface shows transverse ridges, the fused sternebrae . The xiphoid process is variable in shape, begins as cartilage, ossifies in the adult and carries demifacets for the seventh costal cartilages. The junction of the manubrium and the body is the manubriosternal symphysis , felt as the sternal angle . It lies at the level of the intervertebral disc between TIV and TV and is the most useful landmark of the thorax: the second costal cartilage articulates here, the plane separates the superior from the inferior mediastinum, marks the superior border of the pericardium, the end of the ascending aorta and beginning of the arch, the end of the arch and beginning of the thoracic aorta, and the bifurcation of the trachea. Because rib I is hidden below the clavicle, ribs are counted from rib II at the sternal angle. The superior thoracic aperture is bounded by vertebra TI, the first ribs and the manubrium and is open to the neck. The inferior thoracic aperture is bounded by vertebra TXII, ribs XII and XI, the costal margin and the xiphoid process, and is closed by the diaphragm . The angle between the two costal margins at the xiphoid is the infrasternal (subcostal) angle . 3. The joints of the thoracic cage Each rib is joined at its two ends and its cartilage is joined to its neighbours, so the cage is a deformable but continuous unit. Two synovial joints make up the costovertebral articulation of a typical rib: the joint of the head and the costotransverse joint. Table 2 — Joints of the thoracic cage Joint Type Surfaces, ligaments and movement Joint of the head of the rib (articulatio capitis costae) Synovial, divided into two compartments The two facets of the head articulate with the superior costal facet of its own vertebra and the inferior facet of the vertebra above. An intra-articular ligament attaches the crest of the head to the disc and divides the joint; the radiate ligament reinforces the capsule. Ribs I, X, XI and XII have a single facet and therefore one cavity. Costotransverse joint Plane synovial Facet of the tubercle of the rib and the costal facet of the transverse process; thin capsule. Reinforced by the costotransverse (medial), lateral costotransverse and superior costotransverse ligaments. Only slight gliding. Sternocostal joints Rib I: fibrocartilaginous synchondrosis (not synovial); ribs II–VII: synovial Rib I is fixed. The synovial joints have thin capsules reinforced by the sternocostal (radiate) ligaments; the joint of the second cartilage is divided into two compartments by an intra-articular ligament at the manubriosternal junction. Costochondral joints Primary cartilaginous (synchondrosis) Immovable junction between the bony rib and its cartilage. Interchondral joints Usually synovial, plane Between the costal cartilages of adjacent ribs, mainly VII–X (occasionally V–VI); reinforced by interchondral ligaments; they give indirect anchorage to the sternum and a smooth costal margin. Manubriosternal joint Symphysis Only slight angular movement during respiration; projects posteriorly to TIV/TV. Xiphisternal joint Symphysis Slight movement; often ossifies with age. The costovertebral joints allow the neck of each rib to rotate around its own long axis (mainly in the upper ribs) or to ascend and descend relative to the vertebral column (mainly in the lower ribs). The combined movements of all ribs are what alters the volume of the thoracic cavity in breathing. 4. Movements of respiration: pump-handle and bucket-handle Breathing changes three diameters of the thorax. The vertical diameter increases when the diaphragm contracts and its domes flatten. The anteroposterior and transverse diameters change when the ribs are elevated, by two different mechanisms that the examiner always asks for by name. Pump-handle movement. The anterior ends of the ribs are lower than their posterior ends. When the ribs are elevated, their anterior ends and the sternum move upwards and forwards , the angle between the manubrium and the body of the sternum may open slightly, and the anteroposterior diameter of the thorax increases — the ribs work like the handle of a pump. Depression reverses the movement, carrying the sternum downwards and backwards. Bucket-handle movement. The shafts of the ribs are curved so that their middles lie lower than their two ends. When the shafts are elevated, their middles move laterally , increasing the transverse diameter as the handles of a bucket move outwards when lifted. Table 3 — The three dimensions of the thorax in respiration Movement Mechanism Dimension increased Diaphragmatic descent Contraction of the muscle fibres flattens the domes and lowers the central tendon Vertical Pump handle Elevation of the anterior rib ends and of the sternum (mainly upper ribs) Anteroposterior Bucket handle Lateral elevation of the rib shafts (mainly lower ribs) Transverse Quiet inspiration : diaphragm contracts and domes descend → external (and intercartilaginous internal) intercostals elevate the ribs → thoracic volume rises → intrathoracic pressure falls → air enters and venous return improves. Quiet expiration is passive: elastic recoil of lung and chest wall. 5. The muscles of respiration Inspiration. The diaphragm is the chief muscle of quiet inspiration, assisted by the external intercostals and the intercartilaginous part of the internal intercostals, which elevate the ribs. In forced inspiration the scalene muscles fix and elevate the upper ribs, with sternocleidomastoid , levatores costarum , serratus posterior superior , pectoralis major and minor and serratus anterior . Expiration. Quiet expiration needs no muscle. Forced expiration uses the internal intercostals (interosseous part), transversus thoracis , subcostales , serratus posterior inferior and the abdominal wall muscles , which raise intra-abdominal pressure and push the relaxed diaphragm back into the thorax. Fixation. Muscles of the neck and abdomen can fix the upper or lower ribs, so that the action of the intercostals produces movement instead of a futile pull on a mobile cage. In deep inspiration the transverse and anteroposterior diameters, and the infrasternal angle, all increase; the downward movement of the diaphragm adds the vertical component. The ribs are also the site of attachment of muscles of the upper limb, which is why a shoulder girdle fixed by its muscles can assist forced inspiration. 6. Radiology and clinical anatomy of the thoracic cage Rib fracture. A single fracture is painful but of little consequence; fractures of several ribs in two places produce a flail segment , which moves paradoxically (inwards during inspiration) and may require assisted ventilation. Cervical rib. An accessory rib articulating with CVII, present in about 1 % of people; its fibrous band may compress the lower brachial plexus and produce thoracic outlet syndrome . Sternal puncture reaches the marrow cavity of the body of the sternum for bone marrow aspiration; median sternotomy and lateral thoracotomy are the standard surgical approaches to the chest. Chest drainage is placed in the fourth or fifth intercostal space between the anterior and midaxillary lines, immediately above the superior border of the rib below, because the neurovascular bundle lies against the inferior border of the rib above (III.5). Chest radiograph. Ribs are counted from rib II at the sternal angle; costal cartilage calcification increases with age; the sternal angle marks the level of the aortic arch and tracheal bifurcation. Must-memorise 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs , 3 parts of the sternum; true ribs I–VII, false VIII–XII, floating XI–XII. Superior aperture: TI, rib I, manubrium. Inferior aperture: TXII, ribs XI–XII, costal margin, xiphoid — closed by the diaphragm. Sternal angle = manubriosternal symphysis = level of the TIV/TV disc and of the second costal cartilage . Rib I is a synchondrosis and is immobile; ribs II–VII are synovial; the second joint is divided by an intra-articular ligament. Pump handle = anteroposterior diameter; bucket handle = transverse diameter; diaphragm = vertical diameter. Clinical / radiological correlation Flail chest: a segment of the wall moves paradoxically, preventing full expansion on the injured side; ventilation may need support. Fracture of rib I implies severe violence and raises suspicion of injury to the subclavian vessels and brachial plexus, which cross its superior surface. Thoracic outlet syndrome: compression of the brachial plexus (and subclavian vessels) between the scalenes, rib I and a cervical rib or band. Sternal marrow aspiration is possible because the sternum is subcutaneous; the procedure carries a small risk to the internal thoracic vessels and the heart. Costochondral pain (Tietze) and sternocostal strains are common causes of chest-wall pain that must be distinguished from cardiac pain. Likely follow-up questions Why is the first rib not palpable? — It lies below the clavicle, embedded in the tissues at the base of the neck; rib II at the sternal angle is the reference for counting. What are the attachments of the intra-articular ligament of the head of the rib? — From the crest of the head to the intervertebral disc, dividing the joint into two synovial cavities. Which ribs move mainly with pump-handle and which with bucket-handle? — The upper ribs and sternum dominate the pump-handle (AP) movement, the lower ribs the bucket-handle (transverse) movement. Which muscle is the chief muscle of quiet inspiration? — The diaphragm; the external intercostals assist. What happens to the infrasternal angle in deep inspiration? — It increases as the costal margins rise and the thorax widens."},{"id":"t-III-4","p":"III","n":4,"t":"The diaphragm.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The diaphragm ( diaphragma ) is a thin musculotendinous sheet that fills the inferior thoracic aperture and separates the thoracic from the abdominal cavity. It has a central central tendon and three groups of muscular fibres — sternal, costal and lumbar — the lumbar part consisting of the two crura and the median, medial and lateral arcuate ligaments . It has three large openings: the caval opening at TVIII , the oesophageal hiatus at TX and the aortic hiatus at TXII . It is the chief muscle of inspiration, is supplied almost entirely by the phrenic nerves (C3–C5) , and its paralysis or herniation is of major clinical importance. 1. Form, position and domes The diaphragm has two asymmetric domes . On the right the dome is produced by the liver , with contributions from the right kidney and suprarenal gland; on the left by the fundus of the stomach and spleen , with contributions from the left kidney and suprarenal gland. In normal expiration a reasonable estimate places the right dome at the level of rib V and the left dome at the fifth intercostal space — a useful guide when percussing the thorax. The domes are flattened centrally where the fibrous pericardium is attached to the central tendon, and they ascend and descend with breathing. In the median plane the diaphragm slopes inferiorly from its anterior attachment to the xiphoid (approximately TVIII/IX) to its posterior attachment at the median arcuate ligament, crossing anteriorly to the aorta at about TXII. The peripheral attachments of the diaphragm are the xiphoid process , the costal margin , the ends of ribs XI and XII , the ligaments across the posterior abdominal wall and the lumbar vertebrae . 2. The parts of the diaphragm Three muscular parts converge on the central tendon; the lumbar part is the most complex and is examined in most detail. Table 1 — Parts and attachments of the diaphragm Part Attachments Note Costal part Inner surfaces and lower margins of the costal arch of ribs VII–XII Largest part; fibres arch upwards to the central tendon Sternal part Posterior surface of the xiphoid process Small; leaves the sternocostal triangle between it and the costal part Lumbar part — crura Right crus: bodies of LI–LIII and the intervening discs. Left crus: bodies of LI–LII and the intervening disc Blend with the anterior longitudinal ligament; the right crus surrounds the oesophageal hiatus Lumbar part — arcuate ligaments Median: tendinous arch joining the two crura across the aorta. Medial: fascia over psoas major, from the side of LI/LII to the transverse process of LI. Lateral: thickening of the fascia over quadratus lumborum, from the transverse process of LI to rib XII Supply the posterior attachments and enclose the lumbocostal (Bochdalek) triangle between the costal and lumbar parts Central tendon Tendinous aponeurosis into which all muscle fibres insert The pericardium is attached to its middle part; it contains the caval opening 3. The openings of the diaphragm The three large openings — the caval opening ( foramen venae cavae ), the oesophageal hiatus ( hiatus oesophageus ) and the aortic hiatus ( hiatus aorticus ) — are defined by their vertebral level and by what passes through them, a classic oral examination question. Table 2 — Openings of the diaphragm Opening Level Contents and note Caval opening TVIII , in the central tendon Inferior vena cava and the right phrenic nerve . The opening is in the tendinous part, so contraction does not compress the vein. Oesophageal hiatus TX , in the muscular part (right crus), just left of the midline Oesophagus with the anterior and posterior vagal trunks , oesophageal branches of the left gastric artery and vein and a few lymphatics. Aortic hiatus TXII , posterior to the diaphragm between the crura and behind the median arcuate ligament, just left of the midline Aorta , thoracic duct and sometimes the azygos vein. Because it is a bony-tendinous tunnel behind the diaphragm, the aorta is not compressed during contraction. Smaller structures pass through or around the diaphragm: the sympathetic trunks pass posterior to the medial arcuate ligaments; the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves penetrate the crura; the hemi-azygos vein passes through the left crus; the left phrenic nerve pierces the muscular part just anterior to the central tendon; the superior epigastric vessels and the musculophrenic vessels and intercostal nerves cross at various points. 4. Innervation The phrenic nerves (C3, C4, C5) provide the entire motor supply and sensory fibres to the central part of the diaphragm, including the pericardium and the mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura. They descend through the thorax between the mediastinal pleura and the pericardium, pierce the diaphragm and branch on its abdominal surface . The right phrenic nerve accompanies the inferior vena cava through the caval opening; the left passes through the muscle on its own. The peripheral part of the diaphragm receives additional sensory fibres from the lower intercostal nerves . This double sensory supply explains the two patterns of referred pain: irritation of the central diaphragm is felt in the shoulder region (C3–C5 dermatomes), whereas irritation of the peripheral diaphragm is felt over the lower thoracic or abdominal wall. 5. Blood supply and venous drainage The diaphragm is supplied from above and below — a mnemonic advantage when asked about collateral circulation. Table 3 — Arteries of the diaphragm Artery Origin Note Inferior phrenic arteries Abdominal aorta (occasionally from the coeliac trunk) Chief blood supply , from below Superior phrenic arteries Lower thoracic aorta Small, from above Pericardiacophrenic arteries Internal thoracic artery Run with the phrenic nerves Musculophrenic arteries Internal thoracic artery Along the costal margin Small branches of the intercostal arteries also contribute. The veins parallel the arteries: the inferior phrenic veins drain to the inferior vena cava , the superior phrenic veins to the azygos and hemi-azygos veins, and the pericardiacophrenic veins to the internal thoracic and brachiocephalic veins. Lymphatics drain to diaphragmatic nodes situated behind the xiphoid and where the phrenic nerves pierce the muscle, and these interconnect with parasternal, brachiocephalic and lateral aortic nodes. 6. Function, radiology and clinical anatomy Contraction of the muscle fibres flattens the domes and draws the central tendon inferiorly . This increases the vertical diameter of the thorax, raises thoracic volume and lowers intrathoracic pressure, so that air enters the lungs and venous return to the heart is enhanced. The same contraction raises intra-abdominal pressure and thus assists coughing, micturition, defecation and parturition — the abdominal press . On a chest radiograph the domes are seen as curved shadows; the right is normally higher than the left because of the liver, and the costophrenic angles should be sharp. On coronal CT the crura, the arcuate ligaments and the three openings are identifiable. Must-memorise Parts: sternal, costal, lumbar (crura + median, medial and lateral arcuate ligaments) converging on the central tendon . Openings: TVIII = caval (IVC + right phrenic), TX = oesophageal (oesophagus + vagal trunks + left gastric branches), TXII = aortic (aorta + thoracic duct). Right crus: LI–LIII ; left crus: LI–LII ; the right crus forms the oesophageal hiatus. Motor and central sensory supply: phrenic nerve, C3–C5 ; peripheral sensory: lower intercostal nerves. Chief artery: inferior phrenic from the abdominal aorta; also superior phrenic, pericardiacophrenic and musculophrenic. Domes in expiration: right at rib V , left at the fifth intercostal space . Clinical / radiological correlation Diaphragmatic paralysis after phrenic nerve palsy: the affected dome rises, and on screening it moves paradoxically. Causes include malignant infiltration by lung cancer (never to be missed), postviral neuropathy, trauma, iatrogenic injury and cervical spondylosis compressing the C3–C5 roots. Unilateral palsy is often asymptomatic; bilateral palsy causes severe respiratory distress. Surgical plication may be performed. Hiatal hernia: a lax oesophageal hiatus allows the fundus of the stomach to herniate into the posterior mediastinum, producing reflux, sometimes ulceration and anaemia; diagnosed by barium studies, endoscopy or CT. Congenital hernias occur where the embryonic components failed to fuse: Morgagni hernia between the xiphoid and the right costal margin, and Bochdalek hernia through the left posterolateral defect. Bowel in the thorax compresses the lungs and may cause pulmonary hypoplasia. Small diaphragmatic defects may allow fluid but not bowel to pass, so ascites and pleural effusion can communicate. Referred pain: central diaphragmatic irritation (for example blood from a ruptured spleen) is referred to the shoulder tip through the phrenic nerve (C3–C5). Likely follow-up questions Why is the aortic opening not compressed during contraction? — It lies behind the diaphragm, between the crura, so the contracting muscle does not narrow it. Where does the oesophagus pierce the diaphragm and what passes with it? — At TX, through the right crus, with the vagal trunks, oesophageal branches of the left gastric vessels and lymphatics. What is the nerve supply of the diaphragm and what is its segmental value? — Phrenic nerve, C3–C5; the periphery also receives lower intercostal nerves. What are the boundaries of the lumbocostal triangle? — Between the costal and lumbar parts, over the upper part of quadratus lumborum; a site of congenital hernia. Which structures pass through the crura? — The greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves, and the hemi-azygos vein through the left crus."},{"id":"t-III-5","p":"III","n":5,"t":"The muscles and layers of the thoracic wall. The intercostal space.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer From the surface inwards the thoracic wall consists of skin, superficial fascia (with the breast), deep pectoral fascia, a muscular layer, endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura . The muscular layer contains the three intercostal muscles (external, internal, innermost), the subcostales and transversus thoracis , reinforced by the levatores costarum, serratus posterior and the pectoral and serratus anterior muscles. Each intercostal space contains the three muscle layers and, in the costal groove between the internal and innermost layers , the intercostal vein, artery and nerve in that order from above downwards — the nerve, lying most inferiorly, is the structure most at risk. The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of T1–T11 , with the subcostal nerve (T12) below rib XII. 1. The layers of the thoracic wall The wall is built in layers, and surgical planes and spread of infection follow them. The superficial pectoral fascia surrounds the breast, separated from the pectoralis major by the retromammary space . The deep pectoral fascia has three parts: the fascia over pectoralis major, the clavipectoral fascia enclosing pectoralis minor and subclavius, and the thoracic fascia covering the external intercostals. Between the muscle layers lie tissue spaces — the subpectoral space (which contains the interpectoral lymph nodes) and the retromammary space — and laterally the pectoral and clavipectoral fasciae merge into the axillary fascia . Table 1 — Layers of the thoracic wall from superficial to deep # Layer Structures and note 1 Skin and subcutaneous tissue Cutaneous nerves of the trunk are segmental (intercostal nerves) except over the upper chest, supplied by supraclavicular nerves from the cervical plexus. 2 Superficial fascia and breast Contains the mammary gland in the female; the retromammary space allows the breast to move on the deep fascia. 3 Deep pectoral fascia Pectoral fascia, clavipectoral fascia (around pectoralis minor and subclavius) and thoracic fascia over the external intercostals. 4 Muscular layer Pectoralis major and minor, subclavius, serratus anterior anteriorly; the three intercostal layers, subcostales, transversus thoracis, levatores costarum and serratus posterior. 5 Endothoracic fascia Loose connective tissue with fat between the muscles and the pleura; its thickened dome over the apex of the pleura is the suprapleural membrane . 6 Parietal pleura Costal, mediastinal, diaphragmatic and cervical parts; the costal pleura is supplied by intercostal nerves. 2. The muscles of the thoracic wall The intercostal muscles ( musculi intercostales ) fill and support the spaces; the other muscles either cross several ribs or connect the ribs to the sternum or vertebral column. All of them can alter the position of the ribs and therefore act as respiratory muscles. Table 2 — Muscles of the thoracic wall Muscle Origin Insertion Action Innervation External intercostal (11 pairs) Inferior margin (lateral edge of the costal groove) of the rib above Superior margin of the rib below; fibres run obliquely anteroinferiorly Elevates the ribs — most active in inspiration ; supports the intercostal space Intercostal nerves T1–T11 Internal intercostal (11 pairs) Lateral edge of the costal groove of the rib above Superior margin of the rib below, deep to the external intercostal; fibres run obliquely posteroinferiorly Depresses the ribs — most active in expiration ; supports the space Intercostal nerves T1–T11 Innermost intercostal Medial edge of the costal groove of the rib above Internal aspect of the superior margin of the rib below Same action as the internal intercostals; the neurovascular bundle lies superficial to it Intercostal nerves T1–T11 Subcostales Internal surface of the lower ribs near the angle Internal surface of the second or third rib below May depress the ribs Related intercostal nerves Transversus thoracis Posterior surface of the xiphoid and lower body of the sternum and adjacent costal cartilages Lower borders of the costal cartilages of ribs III–VI Depresses the costal cartilages (expiration) Related intercostal nerves Levatores costarum (short and long) Transverse processes of CVII–TXI Rib below, near its tubercle (long: the rib two below) Elevates the ribs; extends and rotates the thoracic spine Posterior rami of spinal nerves (C8–T11) Serratus posterior superior Ligamentum nuchae; spinous processes CVII–TIII Superior borders of ribs II–IV Elevates the upper ribs Anterior rami T2–T5 Serratus posterior inferior Spinous processes T11–L2 Inferior borders of the lower ribs near the angles Depresses the lower ribs Anterior rami T9–T12 Pectoralis major Medial half of the clavicle, sternum, cartilages of ribs I–VII, aponeurosis of external oblique Lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus Adducts, flexes and medially rotates the humerus; accessory inspiration Lateral and medial pectoral nerves Pectoralis minor Anterior surfaces of ribs III–V Coracoid process of the scapula Depresses the shoulder tip, protracts the scapula; accessory inspiration Medial pectoral nerve (C8–T1) Subclavius Rib I at the costochondral junction Groove on the inferior surface of the middle third of the clavicle Steadies the clavicle at the sternoclavicular joint Nerve to subclavius (C5–C6) Serratus anterior Outer surfaces of ribs I–IX Anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula Protracts and rotates the scapula; elevates the ribs when the scapula is fixed Long thoracic nerve (C5–C7) The scalene muscles act on ribs I and II and are accessory inspiratory muscles, supplied by anterior rami of the cervical nerves (the anterior scalene receives C4–C6). The external intercostal membrane replaces the muscle anteriorly between the costal cartilages, and the internal intercostal membrane replaces the internal muscle posteriorly between the angles and the vertebral column. 3. The intercostal space: contents and the neurovascular order There are eleven intercostal spaces ( spatia intercostalia ). Each contains the three muscle layers and, in the costal groove of the rib above , the intercostal vessels and nerve. Three points are examined every year: The neurovascular bundle lies in the plane between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles — the neurovascular plane. In the groove the order from above downwards is vein, artery, nerve (VAN); the vein is highest, the nerve lowest. The nerve is the structure most at risk when an object perforates the upper part of a space, because it lies inferior to the artery and is often not fully protected by the groove. Table 3 — Contents of the costal groove Structure Position Course and note Intercostal vein Most superior in the groove Posterior intercostal vein drains backwards to the azygos or hemi-azygos system; anterior intercostal vein drains to the internal thoracic vein. Intercostal artery Intermediate Posterior and anterior intercostal arteries anastomose in the space. Intercostal nerve Most inferior, often outside the protection of the groove Anterior ramus of T1–T11; gives a lateral cutaneous branch, a collateral branch and an anterior cutaneous branch. Small collateral branches of the nerve and vessels run along the superior border of the rib below . Because of this arrangement, any needle or drain should be placed close to the superior border of the rib below the space being entered. 4. Vessels of the thoracic wall Blood supply forms a basket around the chest: posterior and anterior intercostal arteries anastomose within each space. Posterior intercostal arteries. The upper two on each side arise from the supreme intercostal artery (a branch of the costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery); the remaining nine arise from the posterior surface of the thoracic aorta . Because the aorta lies on the left, the right-sided vessels cross the midline anterior to the vertebral bodies and are longer than the left. Anterior intercostal arteries. Those of the upper six spaces arise from the internal thoracic artery , those of the lower spaces from its terminal musculophrenic branch. Each space usually receives two branches — one below the upper rib and one above the lower rib, where it meets a collateral branch of the posterior artery. Internal thoracic artery. Arises from the subclavian artery, descends behind the costal cartilages of the upper six ribs about 1 cm lateral to the sternum, and ends at about the sixth intercostal space by dividing into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. Its perforating branches pass forwards with the anterior cutaneous nerves to supply the skin and breast. Veins. Posterior intercostal veins drain to the azygos and hemi-azygos systems; anterior intercostal veins drain to the internal thoracic veins . The upper posterior intercostal veins on the left may join as the left superior intercostal vein (to the left brachiocephalic vein); on the right as the right superior intercostal vein (to the azygos vein). Lymphatics. Drain to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels, to intercostal nodes near the heads and necks of the ribs, and to diaphragmatic nodes ; parasternal nodes drain to the bronchomediastinal trunks, upper intercostal nodes also to the bronchomediastinal trunks, and lower intercostal nodes to the thoracic duct. 5. Nerves of the thoracic wall The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of spinal nerves T1–T11 and lie in the intercostal spaces; the anterior ramus of T12 is the subcostal nerve and runs below rib XII. Each typical intercostal nerve gives a large lateral cutaneous branch (which divides into anterior and posterior branches for the overlying skin), a collateral branch near the lower rib, and ends as an anterior cutaneous branch that emerges parasternally or on the anterior abdominal wall. In the thorax the intercostal nerves carry somatic motor fibres to the muscles of the wall, somatic sensory fibres from the skin and parietal pleura, and postganglionic sympathetic fibres to the periphery; they communicate with the sympathetic trunk through rami communicantes. Atypical nerves must be named: T1 contributes to the brachial plexus; the lateral cutaneous branch of T2 is the intercostobrachial nerve supplying the medial surface of the upper arm; the lower intercostal nerves supply the muscles, skin and peritoneum of the abdominal wall; and the skin of the upper chest is supplied by supraclavicular nerves from the cervical plexus. 6. Clinical and radiological notes Chest drain (thoracostomy) insertion: in the fourth or fifth intercostal space between the anterior and midaxillary lines, entering just above the superior border of the rib below the space, to avoid the neurovascular bundle; complications include pneumothorax and bleeding. Intercostal nerve block: local anaesthetic placed near the subcostal groove produces analgesia for chest trauma, thoracotomy, mastectomy or upper abdominal surgery; the pleura and intercostal vessels may be punctured. Surgical access: median sternotomy gives access to the heart, great vessels and anterior mediastinum; lateral thoracotomy and video-assisted thoracoscopy enter through intercostal spaces and divide thoracic wall muscles. Internal thoracic artery is used as a graft in coronary artery bypass surgery because of its rich anastomoses and resistance to atherosclerosis. Rib fracture and flail chest (III.3): the intercostal muscles normally splint each space, and their loss of continuity allows paradoxical movement. Must-memorise Three intercostal layers from outside in: external, internal, innermost ; the neurovascular bundle lies between the internal and innermost . Order in the costal groove from above downwards: vein – artery – nerve ; the nerve is the most at risk. External intercostals = inspiration; internal intercostals = expiration; both supplied by T1–T11 . Posterior intercostal arteries: ribs 1–2 from the supreme intercostal ; the rest from the thoracic aorta . Anterior intercostals from the internal thoracic and musculophrenic . Intercostal nerves = anterior rami T1–T11 ; subcostal = T12 ; intercostobrachial = lateral cutaneous branch of T2 . Clinical / radiological correlation Penetrating chest injury in the upper part of an intercostal space can divide the intercostal nerve but spare the artery and vein, producing an anaesthetic band and weakness of the local muscles. Pneumothorax: air in the pleural cavity collapses the lung; a tension pneumothorax shifts the mediastinum to the opposite side and kinks the great vessels — a medical emergency treated by needle decompression and then a chest drain. Pleural effusion collects first in the costodiaphragmatic recess (III.7), which is why a drain is placed in the lower intercostal spaces. Breast surgery may injure the long thoracic nerve on the medial axillary wall (serratus anterior paralysis, “winged scapula”) or the nerve to latissimus dorsi. Rib resection for access is used in older patients to avoid rib fracture; the intercostal bundle must be protected at the inferior border of each rib. Likely follow-up questions Why is the neurovascular bundle placed in the costal groove? — The groove protects it from pressure and from injury by objects passing through the space. Where exactly do you insert a chest drain and why? — Fourth or fifth intercostal space in the mid-axillary line, above the superior border of the rib below, because the bundle lies under the inferior border of the rib above. Which structures replace the external and internal intercostal muscles at the ends of the space? — The external intercostal membrane anteriorly, the internal intercostal membrane posteriorly. What is the difference between the innervation of levatores costarum and of serratus posterior superior? — Levatores costarum: posterior rami; serratus posterior superior: anterior rami (T2–T5). Which artery supplies the anterior intercostal spaces below the sixth rib? — The musculophrenic artery, a terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery."},{"id":"t-III-6","p":"III","n":6,"t":"The muscles of the posterior abdominal wall and the deep muscles of the back.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The posterior abdominal wall is built on the lumbar vertebrae, the sacrum, ribs XI and XII and the iliac fossae, and is filled by the psoas major, psoas minor, iliacus and quadratus lumborum muscles, with the diaphragm above and the thoracolumbar fascia behind them. The deep (intrinsic) muscles of the back are arranged in layers — spinotransversales (splenius), erector spinae (iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis), transversospinales (semispinalis, multifidus, rotatores), segmental muscles (interspinales, intertransversarii, levatores costarum) and the suboccipital group — and every one of them is supplied by the posterior rami of spinal nerves . They extend, laterally flex and rotate the vertebral column and head and maintain posture. 1. Bony and fascial framework The midline of the posterior abdominal wall is formed by the bodies of the five lumbar vertebrae and their intervertebral discs; the discs complete the boundary, and the prominence of the region is due to the lumbar lordosis. Below them the upper margin of the sacrum continues the midline, and laterally the ilia join the sacrum at the sacro-iliac joints, their iliac fossae giving origin to iliacus. Superiorly, ribs XI and XII complete the bony frame; rib XII gives attachment to many muscles and ligaments. The diaphragm closes the superior boundary of the region. Two fascial sheets dominate the region. The psoas fascia forms a sheath around the psoas major and is attached to the lumbar vertebrae and discs; the thoracolumbar fascia covers the deep muscles of the back and has three layers in the lumbar region. 2. Muscles of the posterior abdominal wall Table 1 — Muscles of the posterior abdominal wall Muscle Origin Insertion Action Innervation Psoas major Lateral surfaces of the bodies of TXII and LI–LV, the intervening discs, and the transverse processes of the lumbar vertebrae Lesser trochanter of the femur (joining iliacus as iliopsoas) Flexes the thigh at the hip when the trunk is fixed; flexes the trunk against gravity when supine Anterior rami of L1–L3 Psoas minor (sometimes absent) Lateral surfaces of the bodies of TXII and LI and the intervening disc Pectineal line of the pelvic brim and iliopubic eminence Weak flexor of the lumbar vertebral column Anterior ramus of L1 Quadratus lumborum Transverse process of LV, iliolumbar ligament and adjoining iliac crest Transverse processes of LI–LIV and the inferior border of rib XII Depresses and stabilises rib XII; lateral bending of the trunk, and extension of the lumbar spine when acting together Anterior rami of T12 and L1–L4 Iliacus Upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa, anterior sacro-iliac and iliolumbar ligaments, upper lateral surface of the sacrum Lesser trochanter of the femur (with psoas major) Flexes the thigh at the hip; flexes the trunk against gravity Femoral nerve (L2–L4) Diaphragm Xiphoid, costal margin, ribs XI–XII, arcuate ligaments, lumbar vertebrae Central tendon Chief muscle of inspiration; abdominal press Phrenic nerves (C3–C5) Because the psoas major takes origin from the intervertebral discs, infection of a disc (for example tuberculous or salmonella discitis) can spread into the muscle sheath and track downwards to appear as a mass below the inguinal ligament — the classical psoas abscess . 3. The thoracolumbar fascia In the lumbar region the thoracolumbar fascia ( fascia thoracolumbalis ) consists of three layers. The posterior layer is thick, attached to the spinous processes of the lumbar and sacral vertebrae and to the supraspinous ligament, and extends laterally to cover the erector spinae . The middle layer is attached medially to the tips of the lumbar transverse processes and intertransverse ligaments, inferiorly to the iliac crest and superiorly to the lower border of rib XII. The anterior layer covers the anterior surface of quadratus lumborum , is attached medially to the lumbar transverse processes, inferiorly to the iliac crest, and superiorly forms the lateral arcuate ligament for the diaphragm. The posterior and middle layers unite at the lateral margin of the erector spinae; at the lateral border of quadratus lumborum the anterior layer joins them and forms the aponeurotic origin of transversus abdominis . In the thoracic region the same fascia covers the deep muscles and separates them from the superficial and intermediate groups, attaching medially to the thoracic spinous processes and laterally to the angles of the ribs. The intrinsic back muscles therefore occupy an osseofibrous compartment bounded by this fascia, the vertebral arches and the spinous and transverse processes. 4. Classification of the deep (intrinsic) muscles of the back The back muscles are divided into extrinsic and intrinsic groups. The extrinsic muscles (trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, levator scapulae — and the intermediate serratus posterior superior and inferior) move the upper limb or the ribs and are innervated by anterior rami of spinal nerves, except trapezius (accessory nerve). The intrinsic muscles ( musculi dorsi proprii ) develop in the back, are supplied by posterior rami , and move the vertebral column and head. Table 2 — Layers of the intrinsic back muscles Group Muscles Innervation Main action Spinotransversales Splenius capitis (ligamentum nuchae, CVII–TIV to the mastoid process and occipital bone) and splenius cervicis (TIII–TVI to transverse processes of CI–CIII) Posterior rami of middle and lower cervical nerves Together extend the neck and head; individually rotate the head to the same side Erector spinae Iliocostalis (angles of the ribs), longissimus (transverse processes, to the mastoid process) and spinalis (spinous processes); arise from a common tendon on the sacrum, lumbar spinous processes and iliac crest Posterior rami of spinal nerves Primary extensors of the column and head; unilateral contraction produces lateral flexion Transversospinales Semispinalis (thoracis, cervicis, capitis; spans four to six vertebrae), multifidus (two to four vertebrae; best developed in the lumbar region) and rotatores (short and long; best developed in the thoracic region) Posterior rami of spinal nerves Extend the column; unilateral contraction rotates the trunk to the opposite side Segmental muscles Interspinales , intertransversarii and levatores costarum (short and long, from transverse processes CVII–TXI to the rib below or the rib two below) Posterior rami (anterior rami for anterior cervical and lateral lumbar intertransversarii) Stabilise adjoining vertebrae; levatores costarum elevate the ribs Suboccipital Rectus capitis posterior major and minor, obliquus capitis superior and inferior Posterior ramus of C1 Extend and rotate the head at the atlanto-occipital and atlanto-axial joints The semispinalis capitis is exceptional in that its unilateral contraction pulls the head posteriorly and turns the chin towards the contracting side — the same pattern as the upper erector spinae. The multifidus is the best-developed transversospinal muscle in the lumbar region and is important for segmental stability, which is why it is a target of rehabilitation after low back pain. 5. The suboccipital muscles and the suboccipital triangle Table 3 — Suboccipital muscles Muscle Origin Insertion Action Rectus capitis posterior major Spinous process of CII Lateral part of the occipital bone below the inferior nuchal line Extends the head; rotates the face to the same side Rectus capitis posterior minor Posterior tubercle of CI Medial part of the occipital bone below the inferior nuchal line Extends the head Obliquus capitis superior Transverse process of CI Occipital bone between the nuchal lines Extends the head and bends it to the same side Obliquus capitis inferior Spinous process of CII Transverse process of CI Rotates the face to the same side The three muscles rectus capitis posterior major, obliquus capitis superior and obliquus capitis inferior bound the suboccipital triangle , which contains the vertebral artery and the posterior ramus of C1 . All four muscles are supplied by the posterior ramus of C1 and act at the atlanto-occipital and atlanto-axial joints (III.2). 6. Clinical and functional notes Posterior rami lesions weaken the intrinsic muscles on one side and produce localised back pain; the posterior rami are also the route for facet-joint pain. Lumbar triangle (of Petit) between the lateral border of latissimus dorsi, the posterior border of external oblique and the iliac crest is a weak point and a site for auscultation, percussion and renal examination. Psoas abscess may present as a mass below the inguinal ligament; the psoas sheath is also a route for the spread of disc infection. Scoliosis and posture depend on balanced action of the erector spinae and transversospinales; muscle imbalance (for example in muscular dystrophy) produces a structural curve. Renal and lumbar examination uses the same muscles and fascia as landmarks: the lumbar region is examined for tenderness and percussion tenderness over the kidneys, and the deep muscles must be relaxed for the examination. Must-memorise Posterior abdominal wall muscles: psoas major (L1–L3), psoas minor (L1), quadratus lumborum (T12, L1–L4), iliacus (femoral n., L2–L4) . Almost all intrinsic back muscles are innervated by posterior rami ; the exceptions are the anterior cervical and lateral lumbar intertransversarii, supplied by anterior rami. Extrinsic back muscles (except trapezius) are also supplied by anterior rami. Erector spinae = iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis ; transversospinales = semispinalis, multifidus, rotatores . Suboccipital muscles: posterior ramus of C1 ; the triangle contains the vertebral artery and the posterior ramus of C1. Thoracolumbar fascia: posterior layer over erector spinae, middle layer on lumbar transverse processes, anterior layer over quadratus lumborum (forms the lateral arcuate ligament). Clinical / radiological correlation Lumbar disc herniation compresses the descending root in the intervertebral foramen; the deep muscles and fascia are divided in the surgical approach, which is why postoperative muscle weakness and pain are common. Psoas sign: inflammation of the psoas (abscess, appendicitis) produces pain on extension of the hip, because the psoas is stretched. Quadratus lumborum spasm produces deep loin pain and is a common cause of “low back pain” after prolonged sitting; it also stabilises the twelfth rib in inspiration. Posterior abdominal wall injuries may damage the lumbar plexus or the ureters, which lie on the psoas major. CT of the lumbar region shows the psoas, quadratus lumborum, erector spinae and the three layers of the thoracolumbar fascia as separate densities. Likely follow-up questions Why is the psoas sheath clinically important? — It extends from the lumbar vertebrae and discs to the thigh, so disc infection can spread along it and present below the inguinal ligament. Which muscles are supplied by the femoral nerve? — Iliacus (and the other flexors of the hip in the anterior thigh); the psoas major is supplied by the lumbar plexus anterior rami L1–L3. What separates the intrinsic from the extrinsic back muscles? — The posterior layer of the thoracolumbar fascia; the intrinsic group is innervated by posterior rami. Which is the deepest muscle of the back? — Rotatores (and the segmental intertransversarii/interspinales), lying deepest of the transversospinales. Where does quadratus lumborum attach and what does it do? — From the iliac crest, iliolumbar ligament and transverse process of LV to rib XII and the transverse processes of LI–LIV; it depresses rib XII, stabilises the trunk and produces lateral bending."},{"id":"t-III-7","p":"III","n":7,"t":"The surface projections of thoracic organs. Topography of the thoracic cavity, the divisions of the mediastinum.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The thoracic cavity is divided into a right and a left pleural cavity and the mediastinum ( mediastinum ) between them. A transverse plane through the sternal angle and the TIV/TV intervertebral disc divides the mediastinum into a superior and an inferior mediastinum, and the inferior part is subdivided by the pericardial sac into anterior, middle and posterior mediastinum. The same palpable landmarks — jugular notch, sternal angle, costal margin, ribs, midclavicular and midaxillary lines — allow the heart (apex in the fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line), the valves, the lungs and pleura and the mediastinal contents to be projected onto the chest wall, which is the basis of percussion, auscultation and safe chest drainage. 1. Topography of the thoracic cavity The thorax is an irregular cylinder with a narrow superior thoracic aperture open to the neck and a large inferior thoracic aperture closed by the diaphragm. Inside, the wall encloses three compartments : the two pleural cavities , each around a lung, and the mediastinum . The pleural cavities are completely separated from each other by the mediastinum, so disease on one side does not automatically cross to the other, and the mediastinum can be entered surgically without opening the pleura. Both pleural cavities extend above rib I into the root of the neck, because the apices of the lungs project there. The mediastinum is a thick, flexible, longitudinally oriented partition in the median sagittal plane. It contains the heart in its pericardial sac, the great vessels, the trachea and oesophagus, the thymus, nerves and lymphatics. It also works as a conduit : the oesophagus, vagus nerves and thoracic duct pass through it between neck and abdomen, the phrenic nerves pass through it to the diaphragm, and the trachea, thoracic aorta and superior vena cava run within it to and from thoracic organs. The lungs occupy the pleural cavities; each lung has an apex, three surfaces (costal, mediastinal and diaphragmatic) and a hilum where the root enters, with the mediastinal pleura reflecting onto the organ there. 2. Surface landmarks and reference lines Projection of thoracic organs is only possible because a few bony points are palpable. The jugular notch lies at the top of the manubrium; the sternal angle is felt as a transverse ridge at the level of the TIV/TV disc; the xiphoid process , the costal margin and the infrasternal angle are palpable below; the second costal cartilage is identified immediately lateral to the sternal angle and is the starting point for counting ribs; the midclavicular line is drawn vertically through the middle of the clavicle, and the midaxillary line vertically through the middle of the axilla. The vertical reference lines used in the thorax are the anterior median, sternal, parasternal, midclavicular, anterior axillary, midaxillary and posterior axillary lines (plus the scapular and posterior median lines behind). The sternal angle is the single most tested plane. At the level of the TIV/TV disc it marks: the articulation of the second costal cartilage with the sternum; the boundary between the superior and inferior mediastinum ; the superior border of the pericardium ; the end of the ascending aorta and beginning of the arch ; the end of the arch and beginning of the thoracic aorta ; and the bifurcation of the trachea , just superior to the pulmonary trunk. 3. The divisions of the mediastinum Table 1 — Divisions of the mediastinum: boundaries and contents Compartment Boundaries Main contents Superior mediastinum Above: oblique plane from the jugular notch to the superior border of TI. Below: transverse plane from the sternal angle to the TIV/TV disc. Anterior: manubrium. Posterior: bodies of the upper four thoracic vertebrae. Lateral: mediastinal pleura. Thymus ; right and left brachiocephalic veins and the superior vena cava ; left superior intercostal vein; arch of the aorta with its three branches (brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries); trachea ; oesophagus ; phrenic and vagus nerves; left recurrent laryngeal nerve; thoracic duct and lymphatics. Anterior mediastinum Anterior: body of the sternum. Posterior: pericardial sac. Above: TIV/V plane. Below: diaphragm. Lateral: mediastinal pleura. Fat and loose connective tissue, lymph nodes, mediastinal branches of the internal thoracic vessels, sternopericardial ligaments , and the inferior extension of the thymus (especially in children). It contains no major organ. Middle mediastinum Centred on the pericardial sac; bounded anteriorly and posteriorly by it, above by the TIV/V plane and below by the diaphragm. Pericardium and heart , the origins of the great vessels (ascending aorta, pulmonary trunk, superior vena cava, pulmonary veins), the phrenic nerves on the sides of the pericardium, and smaller vessels and nodes. Posterior mediastinum Anterior: pericardial sac and diaphragm. Posterior: bodies of the middle and lower thoracic vertebrae. Above: TIV/V plane. Below: diaphragm. Lateral: mediastinal pleura. Oesophagus with its nerve plexus; thoracic aorta and its branches; azygos system of veins; thoracic duct and lymph nodes; sympathetic trunks and thoracic splanchnic nerves . 4. Projection of the heart and great vessels The heart is projected onto the anterior chest wall using the sternal angle, the costal cartilages and the midclavicular line. The upper limit reaches the third costal cartilage on the right of the sternum and the second intercostal space on the left . The right margin runs from the right third costal cartilage to near the right sixth costal cartilage (formed by the right atrium and superior vena cava); the left margin descends laterally from the second intercostal space to the apex , which lies in the fifth intercostal space at (or near) the midclavicular line ; the lower margin runs from the sternal end of the right sixth costal cartilage to the apex. In Szeged teaching the apex is placed 9 cm to the left of the sternal midline in the fifth intercostal space, the superior vena cava enters the right atrium at the sternal end of the right third costal cartilage, and the two ends of the projected coronary sulcus are placed 3 cm left of the sternal end of the left third cartilage and 2 cm right of the sternal end of the right sixth cartilage. Table 2 — Surface projection of thoracic organs Structure Surface projection Apex of the heart Fifth intercostal space, in the midclavicular line, about 9 cm to the left of the sternal midline. Heart borders Upper limit: right third costal cartilage and left second intercostal space. Right margin: right third to right sixth costal cartilage. Left margin: left second intercostal space to the apex. Lower margin: sternal end of the right sixth costal cartilage to the apex. Aortic valve Anatomical projection: left sternal border at the third rib. Best heard at the medial end of the right second intercostal space (downstream). Pulmonary valve Anatomical projection: left sternal border at the third costal cartilage. Best heard at the medial end of the left second intercostal space. Tricuspid valve Anatomical projection: behind the sternum at the fifth costal cartilage. Best heard just to the left of the lower sternum near the fifth intercostal space. Mitral valve Anatomical projection: left fourth to fifth costal cartilage. Best heard at the apex, left fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line. Aortic arch Begins and ends at the TIV/V plane; may reach as high as the midlevel of the manubrium. Superior vena cava Formed behind the lower border of the costal cartilage of the right first rib; enters the right atrium at the sternal end of the right third costal cartilage. Lung apices 1–2 cm above the superior border of the clavicle; the pleura projects above the first costal cartilage but not above the neck of rib I. Inferior border of the lung Quiet respiration: rib VI in the midclavicular line, rib VIII in the midaxillary line, vertebra TX posteriorly. On the left it begins about two fingerbreadths lateral to the xiphoid. Inferior border of the pleura Rib VIII in the midclavicular line, rib X in the midaxillary line, vertebra TXII posteriorly; the costodiaphragmatic recess lies between this line and the lung border. Oblique fissure Begins posteriorly at the spine of TIV, crosses the fourth and fifth intercostal spaces and reaches rib VI laterally; anteriorly it follows the contour of rib VI. Horizontal fissure (right lung) Follows the contour of rib IV and its costal cartilage anteriorly; crosses rib V in the midaxillary line. Two recesses must be understood rather than memorised. The costodiaphragmatic recess is the space between the inferior margin of the lung and the inferior margin of the pleural cavity; it is deepest after forced expiration and shallowest after deep inspiration, and it is where pleural fluid first collects. The costomediastinal recess lies anteriorly where costal and mediastinal pleura are opposed; the largest is on the left over the heart. 5. Radiological topography On a postero-anterior chest radiograph the mediastinal shadow is formed by the heart and great vessels, and each border has a named cause. On the lateral film the retrosternal and retrocardiac spaces are seen, and the diaphragm, sternum and vertebral column form the frame. Table 3 — Structures forming the mediastinal contours Projection Structure Right border (PA film) Superior vena cava (upper part) Right atrium (lower part) Right hemidiaphragm below Left border (PA film) Aortic arch (upper, rounded “knuckle”) Pulmonary trunk and left pulmonary artery; left auricle Left ventricle, ending at the apex Hila Formed by the pulmonary arteries and veins and the bronchi; the left hilum normally sits slightly higher than the right. Lateral film Retrosternal space anteriorly, retrocardiac space posteriorly, both hemidiaphragms, sternum and thoracic vertebral bodies. CT At the level of the aortic arch: arch, trachea, oesophagus, superior vena cava; lower down: ascending aorta, pulmonary trunk, left atrium, descending aorta, azygos vein and oesophagus. 6. Clinical use of the projections Auscultation sites are not the anatomical projections: heart sounds are carried downstream with the blood, so each valve is heard where its jet strikes the chest wall — aortic right second intercostal space, pulmonary left second, tricuspid left lower sternal border, mitral at the apex. Pleural effusion collects in the costodiaphragmatic recess and blunts the costophrenic angle; it is aspirated or drained posteriorly and inferiorly where the recess is deepest. Pneumothorax removes the lung from the chest wall; the apex and the lateral chest wall are the first to show it because the lung does not normally fill these extremes. Mediastinal widening on the chest film suggests lymphadenopathy, aortic disease or tumour; the divisions of the mediastinum localise the lesion. Pericardial and cardiac borders are used to assess chamber enlargement, and the position of the apex indicates the cardiac silhouette in the fifth intercostal space. Must-memorise TIV/TV plane = sternal angle = boundary of superior and inferior mediastinum = superior border of the pericardium = aortic arch begins/ends = tracheal bifurcation. Superior mediastinum: thymus, brachiocephalic veins, SVC, aortic arch and three branches, trachea, oesophagus, phrenic and vagus nerves, thoracic duct. Middle mediastinum = pericardium, heart, roots of the great vessels, phrenic nerves. Posterior mediastinum = oesophagus, thoracic aorta, azygos system, thoracic duct, sympathetic trunks, splanchnic nerves. Heart apex: fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line , about 9 cm from the sternal midline. Lung border VI–VIII–X (midclavicular – midaxillary – posterior); pleural border VIII–X–XII . Lung apex 1–2 cm above the clavicle ; pleura above the first costal cartilage. Clinical / radiological correlation Thoracocentesis and chest drainage are performed in the lower intercostal spaces (above the rib below) so as to enter the costodiaphragmatic recess without injuring the liver, spleen or diaphragm. Auscultation is the practical test of the surface projections: the valve heard best in each point is downstream of the valve itself, which is why the pulmonary valve is heard at the left second space. Mediastinal masses are localised by compartment: anterior (thymoma, thyroid, teratoma), middle (lymphadenopathy, aneurysm), posterior (neurogenic tumour, oesophageal lesion). Pleural tapping should never be performed below the eighth rib in the midclavicular line or below the tenth rib in the midaxillary line, because the pleural cavity ends there and the needle would enter the abdomen. Cardiac enlargement on the PA film changes the left and lower borders; the apex is displaced laterally and downwards in left ventricular enlargement. Likely follow-up questions What separates the superior from the inferior mediastinum? — The plane through the sternal angle and the intervertebral disc between TIV and TV. Which structures lie in the anterior mediastinum? — Thymus (inferior extension), fat, lymph nodes, mediastinal branches of the internal thoracic vessels and sternopericardial ligaments — no major organ. Where do you find the apex of the heart? — Fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line, in Szeged teaching 9 cm to the left of the sternal midline. Why does pleural fluid collect where it does? — Because the lung does not fill the costodiaphragmatic recess, which is deepest after forced expiration. Which fissure separates the middle from the superior lobe of the right lung and where is it projected? — The horizontal fissure, along the contour of rib IV anteriorly."},{"id":"t-III-8","p":"III","n":8,"t":"The anatomy, blood supply and lymphatic drainage of the female breast.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The female breast ( mamma ) is a modified sweat gland lying in the superficial fascia over the pectoralis major, with its base extending from rib II to rib VI and from the sternum to the midaxillary line ; an axillary tail projects into the axilla. It consists of 15–20 lobes drained by lactiferous ducts that open on the nipple, supported by the suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments and separated from the deep fascia by the retromammary space . Its blood supply is from the internal thoracic (medial), the axillary branches (lateral) and the second to fourth posterior intercostal arteries ; its lymph drains mainly to the axillary nodes (about 75 %) , the parasternal nodes for the medial part, and to intercostal and interpectoral nodes. The terminal duct lobular unit is the functional unit and the commonest site of origin of carcinoma. 1. Extent, position and form The breast lies in the pectoral region , in the superficial fascia anterior to the pectoralis major and the anterior thoracic wall, resting also on serratus anterior and the external oblique. Its base extends vertically from rib II to rib VI and transversely from the sternum to as far laterally as the midaxillary line . A layer of loose connective tissue, the retromammary space , separates it from the deep fascia and gives it a degree of movement over the underlying muscles — a plane the surgeon uses. The axillary process (axillary tail) extends superolaterally around the lower margin of pectoralis major into the axilla, may perforate the deep fascia, and can reach as high as the apex of the axilla; it can be mistaken for an axillary mass. The size and shape of the breast depend mainly on fat and on the state of the gland. In non-lactating women fat predominates; during lactation glandular tissue becomes abundant. The nipple ( papilla mammaria ) is surrounded by the pigmented areola , which bears the openings of the areolar glands (Montgomery). In men the breast is rudimentary, consisting only of small ducts that normally do not extend beyond the areola, but carcinoma can still occur. 2. Structure of the gland The mammary gland is a branching tubulo-alveolar gland. Its lobes and lobules are embedded in a firm fibrofatty stroma, and the stroma condenses in places into the suspensory ligaments of the breast (Cooper's ligaments) , which run from the gland to the dermis and support the breast. Table 1 — Structure of the female breast Component Description and note Lobes and lobules 15–20 lobes, each drained by its own duct and made of lobules containing acini; embedded in fibrofatty stroma. Lactiferous ducts and sinuses Each lobe drains by a lactiferous duct that opens independently on the nipple; just proximal to the opening each duct dilates as the lactiferous sinus . Terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU) Terminal duct plus its clustered acini — the functional and pathological unit; proliferates under hormonal stimulation (pregnancy) and is the commonest site of origin of carcinoma . Suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments Condensations of stroma continuous with the dermis; support the breast. Carcinoma traction on them causes skin retraction and pitting . Retromammary space Loose areolar plane between the breast and the deep fascia over pectoralis major; allows movement and is the plane of surgical dissection. Axillary process (tail) Superolateral extension of glandular tissue into the axilla; clinically palpable as a mass if enlarged. Nipple and areola Nipple raised, with the ducts opening on it; areola pigmented, bearing sebaceous areolar glands; both are richly supplied by the fourth intercostal nerve. Note also the mammary ridge : in the embryo, glands develop along the line from axilla to groin, and remnants may persist as an accessory nipple (polythelia) , usually along this line. 3. Blood supply and venous drainage The breast sits on the boundary between the thorax and the upper limb, so it is supplied from both directions and from the chest wall. Table 2 — Arterial supply of the breast Artery Origin Region supplied Internal thoracic artery (mammary branches) Subclavian artery, descending behind the costal cartilages Medial part of the breast; its perforating branches reach the gland through the intercostal spaces. Lateral thoracic artery Axillary artery Lateral part of the breast, along the lower border of pectoralis minor. Thoraco-acromial artery (pectoral branch) Axillary artery Upper part of the gland and pectoral muscles. Superior thoracic and subscapular arteries Axillary artery Small lateral contributions. Posterior intercostal arteries (2nd–4th) Supreme intercostal artery (2nd); thoracic aorta (3rd–4th) Lateral and inferior parts through lateral cutaneous and perforating branches. The venous drainage parallels the arteries: superficial veins drain to the internal thoracic , axillary and intercostal veins , and the deep veins of the gland follow the corresponding arteries. The rich anastomoses between these territories explain why the gland can survive on one pedicle after the others are divided. 4. Innervation The sensory supply of the breast comes from the anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of the second to sixth intercostal nerves ; the nipple is innervated by the fourth intercostal nerve . The skin of the upper part of the breast may also be supplied by supraclavicular nerves from the cervical plexus. The motor nerves that matter surgically are not for the gland at all but run close to it: the long thoracic nerve (C5–C7, to serratus anterior) on the medial wall of the axilla, the thoracodorsal nerve (to latissimus dorsi), the pectoral nerves (to pectoralis major and minor) and the intercostobrachial nerve (lateral cutaneous branch of T2). 5. Lymphatic drainage Lymphatic drainage is the most examined part of the topic because it determines the spread of carcinoma. The breast has a superficial subcutaneous plexus and deep vessels; the direction of flow follows the quadrant. Main route (about 75 % of the lymph): lateral and superior part of the breast → axillary nodes (pectoral/anterior group → central → apical) → subclavian trunk → thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct. Table 3 — Four-quadrant lymphatic drainage of the breast Quadrant Primary route Clinical note Upper lateral (upper outer) Axillary nodes — pectoral group, then central and apical groups Commonest site of carcinoma and the commonest route of axillary metastasis. Lower lateral (lower outer) Axillary nodes, partly to the subscapular group Also drains to the axilla; may spread along the lateral thoracic vessels. Upper medial (upper inner) Parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels; some lymph to the apical axillary group Parasternal metastasis without axillary involvement is possible. Lower medial (lower inner) Parasternal nodes, and through the diaphragm to subdiaphragmatic and abdominal nodes Explains abdominal spread of medial tumours; parasternal route allows spread across the midline to the opposite breast. In addition, some drainage passes through vessels that follow the lateral branches of the posterior intercostal arteries to intercostal nodes near the heads and necks of the ribs, and lymph may reach the interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor (part of the axillary system). The sentinel lymph node is the first node to receive lymph from a tumour and therefore the first site of metastasis; it is identified by radiolabelled colloid mapping and removed for histological examination. Table 4 — Levels of the axillary lymph nodes Level Position relative to pectoralis minor Node groups I Lateral to the lateral border Pectoral (anterior), subscapular (posterior) and humeral (lateral) groups II Along the muscle Central and interpectoral groups III Medial to the medial border Apical (subclavicular) group, which drains to the subclavian trunk 6. Clinical and radiological anatomy Carcinoma of the breast arises most often in the terminal duct lobular unit. Tumour traction on Cooper's ligaments produces skin retraction and pitting ; obstruction of subcutaneous lymphatics produces the orange-peel (peau d'orange) appearance; extensive dermal infiltration may give the hard, woody skin of cancer en cuirasse . Spread is by lymphatics, by veins and by direct invasion. Staging requires the size and site of the primary tumour, the number and sites of involved nodes and the organs of spread; CT, bone scanning and PET-CT are used to look for lung, liver and bone metastases. Mastectomy removes breast tissue from the medial axillary wall, where the long thoracic nerve lies; injury paralyses serratus anterior and produces a winged scapula . The nerve to latissimus dorsi may also be damaged. Mammography shows the Cooper's ligaments as a fibrous network; invasive ductal carcinoma appears as an irregular radiodense mass that distorts the architecture of the surrounding fat. Male breast cancer is rare but occurs, because the rudimentary ducts are the same tissue; accessory nipples occur along the mammary ridge. Must-memorise Base: rib II to rib VI , from the sternum to the midaxillary line ; lies in the superficial fascia with the retromammary space behind it and the axillary tail reaching into the axilla. Structure: 15–20 lobes → lactiferous ducts → lactiferous sinuses → nipple ; Cooper's ligaments support it; the TDLU is the unit of origin of carcinoma. Arteries: internal thoracic (medial), lateral thoracic and thoraco-acromial (lateral), 2nd–4th posterior intercostals . Nerves: 2nd–6th intercostal nerves ; the nipple is the 4th intercostal . Lymph: about 75 % to the axillary nodes ; medial quadrants to the parasternal nodes ; intercostal and interpectoral nodes also receive lymph; axillary nodes are grouped I–III around pectoralis minor. Clinical / radiological correlation Peau d'orange = subcutaneous lymphatic obstruction; retraction = shortening of Cooper's ligaments by tumour; both are signs of advanced local disease. Nipple discharge and a subareolar mass point to a ductal lesion; the lactiferous sinuses are the site where such lesions are felt. Sentinel node biopsy avoids unnecessary axillary dissection and its lymphoedema; the first node to take up the radiolabelled colloid is removed and examined. Axillary tail tumours may be missed on breast examination because they lie in the axilla; they can also mimic enlarged axillary nodes. Breast surgery injury: long thoracic nerve → winged scapula; thoracodorsal nerve → weakness of extension, adduction and medial rotation of the humerus; intercostobrachial nerve → numbness of the medial arm. Likely follow-up questions What are the limits of the base of the breast? — Rib II to rib VI vertically; from the sternum to the midaxillary line transversely. Why is the retromammary space important? — It is a loose plane that allows the breast to move and is used for surgical dissection between the gland and the pectoral fascia. Where does the lymph from the medial quadrants drain? — Mostly to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels; the lower medial part may drain through the diaphragm to abdominal nodes. Which nerve supplies the nipple? — The fourth intercostal nerve (anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of the 2nd–6th intercostal nerves supply the breast). Which nerve is at risk during mastectomy on the medial axillary wall, and what happens if it is injured? — The long thoracic nerve; serratus anterior paralysis with a winged scapula."},{"id":"t-III-9","p":"III","n":9,"t":"The definition and structures of the superior mediastinum. Topography, developmental stages and histology of the thymus.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The mediastinum is the median region of the thorax between the two pleural cavities; the transverse plane through the sternal angle and the intervertebral disc TIV/V divides it into a superior and an inferior mediastinum. The superior mediastinum contains the thymus , the right and left brachiocephalic veins , the superior vena cava , the arch of the aorta with its three branches, the trachea , the oesophagus , the thoracic duct and the phrenic and vagus nerves . The thymus ( thymus ) is the most anterior structure of this compartment – a bilobed, endodermally derived, encapsulated primary lymphoid organ in which T lymphocytes are educated; histologically it has a dark cortex and a pale medulla containing Hassall's corpuscles , and it undergoes fatty involution after puberty. 1. The mediastinum and the definition of its superior part The mediastinum ( mediastinum ) extends from the posterior surface of the sternum anteriorly to the thoracic vertebrae posteriorly, and from the superior thoracic aperture to the diaphragm. A horizontal plane through the sternal angle and the disc between vertebrae TIV and TV separates the superior from the inferior mediastinum; the inferior part is further subdivided by the pericardium into the anterior , middle (pericardium and heart) and posterior mediastina. The same plane is a landmark for rib II, the superior limit of the pericardium, the beginning and the end of the arch of the aorta, the bifurcation of the trachea and the entry of the superior vena cava into the pericardial sac. Border of the superior mediastinum Structure forming it Anterior Posterior surface of the manubrium of the sternum Posterior Bodies of the first four thoracic vertebrae Superior Oblique plane from the jugular notch upwards and backwards to the superior border of vertebra TI ; it is continuous with the neck Inferior Transverse plane from the sternal angle to the intervertebral disc TIV/V ; it is continuous with the inferior mediastinum Lateral Mediastinal part of the parietal pleura on each side 2. Structures of the superior mediastinum Structure Position and key points Thymus Most anterior; retrosternal, anterior to the great veins, the arch of the aorta and the pericardium; two lobes. Right and left brachiocephalic veins Immediately posterior to the thymus; the left crosses the midline to join the right and form the superior vena cava, posterior to the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage. Left superior intercostal vein Crosses the left side of the aortic arch, lateral to the left vagus nerve and medial to the left phrenic nerve, to enter the left brachiocephalic vein. Superior vena cava Begins at the level of the right first costal cartilage and enters the right atrium at the level of the right third costal cartilage; its lower half lies inside the pericardial sac, i.e. in the middle mediastinum. Arch of the aorta with its three branches Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery and left subclavian artery arise from the superior border of the arch; all three are crossed anteriorly by the left brachiocephalic vein. Ligamentum arteriosum Fibrous remnant of the foetal ductus arteriosus ; connects the pulmonary trunk to the arch of the aorta. Trachea Midline, anterior to the oesophagus; bifurcates into the two main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (TIV/V). Oesophagus Posterior to the trachea; crossed on its left by the arch of the aorta and then continues into the posterior mediastinum. Phrenic nerves Derived mainly from C4 , also C3 and C5; descend lateral to the vagus nerves and then along the sides of the pericardial sac. Vagus nerves and left recurrent laryngeal nerve Right vagus descends lateral to the trachea; the left vagus crosses the left side of the aortic arch and gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve at the inferior margin of the arch, lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum. Thoracic duct Enters the superior mediastinum from below slightly left of the midline, ascends posterior to the arch of the aorta and the beginning of the left subclavian artery, between the oesophagus and the left mediastinal pleura. From anterior to posterior the organs are layered: thymus → brachiocephalic veins and superior vena cava → arch of the aorta and its branches → trachea → oesophagus ; the phrenic and vagus nerves run vertically on each side of this central mass, and the thoracic duct lies furthest posteriorly. 3. Topography of the thymus In the adult the thymus is a bilobed, asymmetrical organ lying in the superior mediastinum immediately posterior to the manubrium of the sternum , in front of the left brachiocephalic vein, the arch of the aorta and the pericardium. Its upper poles may reach into the neck as high as the thyroid gland, while its lower part typically extends into the anterior mediastinum over the pericardial sac. Laterally it is related to the mediastinal pleura and to the phrenic nerves . It is a large organ in the child, begins to atrophy after puberty and in the elderly is barely identifiable, being represented by lobulated fatty tissue in which small islands of thymic tissue persist. Arteries: small branches from the internal thoracic arteries ; smaller twigs from the pericardiacophrenic and inferior thyroid vessels may contribute. Veins: drain chiefly into the left brachiocephalic vein , sometimes into the internal thoracic veins. Lymphatics: to the parasternal , tracheobronchial and root-of-neck lymph nodes; the thymus has only efferent lymphatic vessels. Nerves: derived from the vagus and sympathetic plexuses accompanying the vessels. 4. Development of the thymus The thymus is an endodermal organ. Its epithelial primordium arises from the third pharyngeal pouch on each side; the parathyroid glands develop from the same pharyngeal pouch region, which explains why ectopic parathyroid tissue is a common finding inside the thymus. The developmental stages are: Epithelial primordium: endodermal cells of the third pouch proliferate and form a paired, initially hollow epithelial cord that soon becomes solid and loses its connection with the pharyngeal wall. Descent: the primordia migrate caudally and medially with the developing heart and great vessels into the superior mediastinum , where the two lobes come to lie behind the manubrium of the sternum; the thymus thus has a cervical origin and a thoracic position . Colonisation by lymphocytes: lymphoid progenitor cells from the bone marrow enter the epithelial primordium and proliferate, converting it into a lymphoid organ; the epithelial cells become the epithelial reticular cells ( epithelioreticulocyti ) which form the stroma and present self-antigens. Compartmentalisation: the organ is divided into an outer dark cortex and an inner pale medulla ; a capsule and connective-tissue septa grow in from the surrounding mesenchyme and divide the cortex into incomplete lobules (pseudolobules) that share a common medulla. Hassall's corpuscles ( corpuscula thymica ) differentiate in the medulla from concentric layers of epithelial reticular cells; their number and size increase with age. The thymic stroma is unique: it is a reticular epithelium and not ordinary reticular connective tissue – apart from the developing tooth, no other organ has this type of stroma. A small population of myoid cells expressing skeletal-muscle proteins is also present; their function is unknown. 5. Histology of the thymus 5.1 General architecture Capsule ( capsula thymi ): dense irregular collagenous connective tissue; septa ( septa corticalia ) penetrate the parenchyma and produce incomplete lobules , each with its own cortex but a medulla continuous with the neighbouring lobules. Cortex ( cortex thymi ): dark on H&E because of densely packed thymocytes with large nuclei and scanty cytoplasm; mitoses are numerous in the subcapsular zone, and the cells mature as they move inwards. There are no lymphoid nodules in the thymus – the cortex is uniformly dark. Corticomedullary junction: blurred boundary containing numerous vessels and high endothelial venules ; this is where progenitors enter and mature T cells leave. Medulla ( medulla thymi ): pale, because lymphocytes are fewer and epithelial reticular cells more abundant; contains mature T cells, dendritic cells, macrophages and Hassall's corpuscles . Larger vessels and the only (efferent) lymphatic vessels lie in the septa; there are no afferent lymphatics – antigens do not reach the thymic parenchyma through lymph. 5.2 Epithelial reticular cells (six types) Type Location Main function Type I Subcapsular and perivascular Seals the cortex from the capsule and septa; forms the outer cellular layer of the blood-thymus barrier . Type II Midcortex Surrounds islands of thymocytes; presents self-antigens with MHC I and MHC II in positive selection . Type III Corticomedullary junction Also presents self-antigens/MHC to thymocytes; participates in positive selection. Type IV Medulla at the corticomedullary junction Helps type III cells to isolate cortex from medulla. Type V Medulla Forms the architectural framework of the medulla. Type VI Medulla Forms Hassall's corpuscles ; releases thymic stromal lymphopoietin and promotes clonal deletion. 6. Blood-thymus barrier, T-cell education and involution The blood-thymus barrier exists only in the cortex . It prevents circulating macromolecules and antigens from reaching developing T cells, so that the thymocytes meet only the self-antigens presented by epithelial reticular cells. It has five layers : (1) capillary endothelium with tight junctions, (2) capillary basal lamina, (3) a thin perivascular connective-tissue space containing macrophages, (4) the basal lamina of the reticular epithelium, and (5) the processes of type I epithelial reticular cells joined by occluding junctions. The medulla has no such barrier – T cells there have already been selected. Education of T cells begins when bone-marrow progenitors enter the cortex as double-negative thymocytes (CD2 positive, CD4 and CD8 negative). They express a pre-T-cell receptor, proliferate and become double-positive (CD4+ CD8+). They are then tested in the cortex by epithelial reticular cells ( positive selection ): those that fail to recognise self-MHC–self-peptide complexes – about 90% – undergo apoptosis and are phagocytosed by cortical macrophages. Survivors become single-positive (CD4+ or CD8+) and move to the medulla, where dendritic cells and medullary epithelial cells challenge them with self-antigens; autoreactive cells are deleted ( negative selection ). In total well over 90% of thymocytes die; the survivors leave as naive T cells through the high endothelial venules and efferent lymphatics and seed the secondary lymphoid organs. Involution: the thymus is relatively largest in the neonate and child; after puberty rising sex-hormone levels cause progressive atrophy, with adipose replacement of the cortex. It never disappears completely – islands of thymic tissue and epithelial reticular cells persist, and the organ can be reactivated in youth. Accelerated involution follows chronic illness, infection, radiation and stress. Must-memorise Superior mediastinum: from the sternal angle/TIV-V disc upwards to the thoracic inlet; boundaries = manubrium, upper four thoracic vertebrae, mediastinal pleura. Contents: thymus, brachiocephalic veins, SVC, arch of aorta + three branches, trachea, oesophagus, thoracic duct, phrenic and vagus nerves, left recurrent laryngeal nerve, ligamentum arteriosum . Thymus = most anterior organ of the superior mediastinum; endoderm of the 3rd pharyngeal pouch ; stroma = reticular epithelium; only efferent lymphatics. Cortex: dark, positive selection, blood-thymus barrier. Medulla: pale, negative selection, Hassall's corpuscles . Clinical / radiological correlation DiGeorge syndrome: congenital absence (or hypoplasia) of the thymus → failure to generate T cells → severe cell-mediated immunodeficiency; because the parathyroid glands are also missing, hypocalcaemia with calcium tetany may be the presenting feature. Ectopic parathyroid glands may be found inside the thymus (shared third pharyngeal pouch), a potential cause of persistently raised parathyroid hormone after neck exploration. In infants and children the normal thymus may appear as a broadening of the superior mediastinum on a chest radiograph; in the elderly only a retrosternal fat pad is left. During central venous cannulation the left brachiocephalic vein – the vessel lying immediately behind the thymus – is less protected in infants because it may rise above the manubrium. Likely follow-up questions Why is the plane TIV/V so important? It runs through the sternal angle and separates the superior and inferior mediastinum; it marks rib II, the top of the pericardium, the tracheal bifurcation and the beginning/end of the aortic arch. What is the difference between cortex and medulla of the thymus? Cortex: dense thymocytes, mitoses, positive selection, blood-thymus barrier, no Hassall's corpuscles. Medulla: paler, more epithelium, Hassall's corpuscles, negative selection, no barrier. Why does the thymus have no afferent lymphatics? Antigens must not reach developing T cells; the blood-thymus barrier and the absence of afferent vessels keep the cortex antigen-free."},{"id":"t-III-10","p":"III","n":10,"t":"Histology of the red bone marrow. Embryonic/foetal haematopoiesis. Haematopoietic stem cells and their regulation. The structure and development of erythrocytes and thrombocytes.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer Red bone marrow ( medulla ossium rubra ) is a highly vascular, cell-rich connective tissue occupying the marrow cavities of long bones and the intertrabecular spaces of spongy bone; after birth it is the only normal site of haematopoiesis . Prenatal blood formation passes through mesoblastic (yolk sac), hepatic , splenic and myeloid (bone-marrow) phases. Every blood cell descends from a pluripotential haematopoietic stem cell , whose proliferation and differentiation are controlled by colony-stimulating factors, interleukins, erythropoietin and thrombopoietin . Erythrocytes develop through the erythroblastic series with progressive haemoglobinisation and extrusion of the nucleus; platelets are fragments of the cytoplasm of megakaryocytes , released into the marrow sinusoids as proplatelets. 1. Histology of the red bone marrow Bone marrow is a gelatinous, extensively vascular, cell-rich tissue filling the spaces between bone trabeculae and the medullary cavity of long bones. In infants and young individuals all marrow is haematopoietically active and therefore red; as a person approaches 20 years of age much of the diaphyseal marrow accumulates fat and becomes inactive yellow marrow ( medulla ossium flava ). Vascular compartment: nutrient arteries enter through nutrient canals and break up into a network of wide sinusoids ; these drain into a central longitudinal vein , which empties into veins leaving through the nutrient canals. The marrow veins are smaller than their arteries, and the high hydrostatic pressure keeps the thin-walled sinusoids patent. Haematopoietic compartment: between the sinusoids lie the haematopoietic cords (islands) , usually composed of a single cell lineage at various stages of maturation. Red-cell precursors and megakaryocytes lie mainly in the intertrabecular spaces, white-cell precursors mainly in the paratrabecular spaces. Sinusoid wall: a discontinuous endothelium resting on a basal lamina, a fine mesh of reticular fibres (type III collagen) and adventitial reticular cells , whose processes wrap the sinusoid and enclose the haematopoietic cords. Adventitial reticular cells regulate how much marrow volume is available for haematopoiesis: when they accumulate lipid they enlarge and convert red into yellow marrow. Reticular cells also secrete growth factors (e.g. stem cell factor). Macrophages in the cords phagocytose extruded nuclei and discarded cytoplasm and supply iron to the erythroid line (they become siderophages when loaded with haemosiderin). Mature blood cells cross the sinusoidal endothelium into the bloodstream. In the adult, active red marrow persists in the axial skeleton (vertebrae, sternum, ribs, skull, pelvis) and in the proximal ends of the humerus and femur . 2. Embryonic and foetal haematopoiesis Prenatal haematopoiesis begins around the 14th day of development and passes through four phases, each replacing the previous one. Phase Time of onset Site and products Mesoblastic Around day 14 Yolk-sac blood islands . Peripheral cells become endothelium (vessels); the central cells become erythroblasts producing primitive, nucleated red cells. Hepatic End of the 5th week Liver . Red cells are still nucleated; leucocytes begin to be formed from the 8th week. Splenic 4th month Spleen (together with the liver); haematopoiesis continues in both organs until birth. Myeloid (bone marrow) About the 6th month Red bone marrow ; it becomes progressively more important towards term. After birth all haematopoiesis is medullary, although the liver and spleen can resume blood formation if the marrow fails. Postnatally the marrow produces an enormous number of cells – on the order of one billion blood cells per day – so that the circulating population of each cell line is continuously renewed. 3. Haematopoietic stem cells and progenitors Stem cells are the least differentiated haematopoietic cells. The pluripotential haematopoietic stem cell (PHSC) forms about 0.1% of the nucleated cells of the marrow, resembles a small lymphocyte and only rarely enters the cell cycle. Although stem cells look alike, their membrane markers differ and allow them to be recognised. The PHSC gives rise to multipotential stem cells , which in turn produce progenitor cells (colony-forming units, CFU) and finally precursor cells recognisable by light microscopy. Progenitor cells cannot be identified histologically; precursor cells cannot divide indefinitely but are named after their line. Level Cell Comments Stem cell PHSC 0.1% of nucleated marrow cells; self-renewing; membrane markers only. Multipotent CFU-GEMM and CFU-Ly Myeloid and lymphoid lines respectively. Progenitors BFU-E → CFU-E (erythroid); CFU-Meg (platelets); CFU-Eo, CFU-Ba; CFU-GM → CFU-G and CFU-M; CFU-LyT, CFU-LyB Oligopotent or unipotent; no distinctive light-microscopic features. Precursors Proerythroblast; megakaryoblast; myeloblast; promonocyte; naive T and B lymphocytes First morphologically identifiable cell of each line; the myeloblast is shared by all three granulocyte lines. 4. Regulation of haematopoiesis Haematopoietic growth factors ( colony-stimulating factors ) are glycoproteins acting as endocrine hormones, paracrine signals or through cell-to-cell contact. Stem cell factor (steel factor) is expressed on the plasma membrane of marrow stromal cells: the stem, multipotent and lymphoid progenitor cells must touch it to be activated. Haematopoietic cells that are not contacted by growth factors enter apoptosis and are removed by macrophages. Factor Main source Principal action Stem cell factor Bone-marrow stromal cells Promotes survival and proliferation of PHSC, CFU-GEMM and CFU-Ly; membrane-bound contact required. GM-CSF T cells; endothelial cells Mitosis and differentiation of CFU-GM; facilitates granulocyte activity. G-CSF Macrophages; endothelial cells Mitosis and differentiation of CFU-G; activates neutrophils. M-CSF Macrophages; endothelial cells Mitosis and differentiation of CFU-M. IL-3 Activated T and B cells With IL-1 and IL-6, promotes proliferation of PHSC, CFU-GEMM, CFU-Ly and most unipotential precursors. IL-7 Stromal cells Differentiation of CFU-Ly; enhances NK-cell differentiation. Erythropoietin Peritubular capillary endothelial cells of the kidney; hepatocytes Differentiation of CFU-E, mitosis of BFU-E; released when the red-cell count falls. Thrombopoietin Not established Proliferation and differentiation of CFU-Meg and megakaryoblasts. 5. Erythropoiesis Erythropoiesis produces about 2.5 × 10 11 red cells daily. When the red-cell count is low, the peritubular capillary endothelial cells of the kidney and hepatocytes release erythropoietin , which – together with stem cell factor, IL-3, IL-9 and GM-CSF – drives CFU-GEMM towards BFU-E and then CFU-E . As the CFU-E pool is formed, erythropoietin production falls and the low level of the hormone induces CFU-E to become proerythroblasts . Cell Size (μm) Nucleus, nucleoli, mitosis Cytoplasm Proerythroblast 14–19 Round, fine chromatin; 3–5 nucleoli; mitotic Grey-blue, basophilic Basophilic erythroblast 12–17 Coarser chromatin; 1–2 nucleoli; mitotic Strongly basophilic Polychromatophilic erythroblast 12–15 Very coarse chromatin; no nucleoli; mitotic Pinkish in a bluish background; haemoglobin appears Orthochromatophilic erythroblast 8–12 Small, dense, eccentric or being extruded; no mitosis Pink; much haemoglobin Reticulocyte 7–8 No nucleus Bluish reticulum with cresyl blue (residual ribosomes) Erythrocyte about 7.5 No nucleus, no organelles Pink; haemoglobin only Maturation is accompanied by progressive haemoglobinisation of the cytoplasm, condensation of chromatin and a fall in the number of nucleoli. The nucleus is extruded at the orthochromatophilic stage and is phagocytosed by a marrow macrophage; the cell leaves the marrow as a reticulocyte and matures in the blood within a day or two. Mature erythrocytes are biconcave, contain no nucleus or organelles and live about 120 days ; they are destroyed by macrophages of the spleen. 6. Thrombopoiesis and the structure of platelets Platelets arise from CFU-Meg , which produce megakaryoblasts . These enlarge by endomitosis – repeated nuclear division without cytoplasmic division – and become giant, polyploid megakaryocytes (up to 64 N; among the largest cells of the body, about 35–150 μm), regulated by thrombopoietin . Megakaryocytes lie next to the marrow sinusoids and push their cytoplasm into the sinusoidal lumen; the cytoplasm fragments along demarcation channels and releases proplatelets , which disperse into individual platelets in the circulation. Platelets are disc-shaped cell fragments 2–4 μm in diameter, without a nucleus; they survive about two weeks . On Romanovsky staining they show a pale periphery – the hyalomere – and a darker central granulomere . Hyalomere: a ring of microtubules plus actin and myosin, and two channel systems – the surface-opening tubular system (increases surface area) and the dense tubular system (calcium sequestration). Granulomere: mitochondria, glycogen, peroxisomes and three granule types – alpha granules (fibrinogen, platelet-derived growth factor, thromboplastin, coagulation factors), delta granules (ADP, ATP, calcium, serotonin, histamine) and gamma granules (lysosomes). Function: adhesion to exposed collagen, aggregation and formation of the platelet plug, then participation in the coagulation cascade. Must-memorise Red marrow = haematopoietic; yellow marrow = fat-storing but can revert to red marrow. Prenatal phases in order: mesoblastic (yolk sac, day 14) → hepatic (end of week 5) → splenic (month 4) → myeloid (month 6). Erythroid sequence: proerythroblast → basophilic → polychromatophilic → orthochromatophilic → reticulocyte → erythrocyte; the nucleus is extruded at the orthochromatophilic stage. Platelets = fragments of megakaryocyte cytoplasm; megakaryocyte enlargement is by endomitosis ; regulated by thrombopoietin . Red cells: erythropoietin (kidney/liver). Neutrophils: G-CSF. Monocytes: M-CSF. Clinical / radiological correlation Myelofibrosis: excessive fibrous tissue replaces the marrow space → anaemia, reduced platelet formation and, later, extramedullary haematopoiesis in the spleen and liver with abdominal pain; diagnosis requires a bone-marrow biopsy (trephine). Marrow failure after irradiation, benzene exposure or cytotoxic chemotherapy suppresses all three lines, producing anaemia, infection and bleeding; the marrow can be examined by aspiration or trephine biopsy. Erythropoietin deficiency in chronic kidney disease is a cause of normocytic anaemia, and recombinant erythropoietin is used therapeutically. In adults, an active red marrow reserve exists in the axial skeleton and the proximal humerus and femur – the basis of choosing sternum or posterior iliac crest for marrow aspiration. Likely follow-up questions Why is the marrow sinusoid wall special? Discontinuous endothelium, incomplete basal lamina and a reticular-fibre mesh allow maturing cells to enter the blood. How do you recognise an orthochromatophilic erythroblast? Small, dense, eccentric nucleus about to be extruded, pink cytoplasm and no mitoses. What happens to haematopoiesis after birth? It is confined to the red marrow; liver and spleen can resume it only if the marrow fails."},{"id":"t-III-11","p":"III","n":11,"t":"White blood cells: types, and their morphological properties and development. The mononuclear phagocytic system.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer White blood cells ( leukocytes , leucocyti ) are the nucleated formed elements of blood; they use the circulation only to reach the tissues. They are classified into granulocytes – neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils, which have specific granules and lobed nuclei – and agranulocytes – lymphocytes and monocytes, which lack specific granules. All descend from the pluripotential haematopoietic stem cell: the granulocytes pass through myeloblast → promyelocyte → myelocyte → metamyelocyte → band cell → mature cell, while monocytes pass through promonocyte and enter the tissues as macrophages , the cells of the mononuclear phagocytic system . 1. Classification and general properties Leukocytes number about 6,500–10,000 per mm³ of blood. They have no function within the bloodstream : they leave the capillaries and venules by diapedesis (migration between endothelial cells), enter the connective tissue compartment and act there. Two large groups are distinguished: Granulocytes ( granulocyti , polymorphonuclear leukocytes): contain specific granules and a segmented nucleus; examples are neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils. They are terminally differentiated and cannot divide. Agranulocytes ( agranulocyti , mononuclear cells): no specific granules; lymphocytes and monocytes. The granules are of two kinds: azurophilic granules , which are lysosomes present in all granulocytes, and specific granules , whose staining defines the cell type. On Romanovsky-type stains (Wright, Giemsa) neutrophils are pink, eosinophils bright orange-red and basophils dark blue-black. 2. Morphology of the five leukocyte types Cell Number (% of WBC) Diameter in smear Nucleus Granules and function Neutrophil 3,500–7,000 (60–70%) 9–12 μm 3–4 lobes connected by thin chromatin threads; in females a small \"drumstick\" (Barr body) Small pale-pink specific granules plus azurophilic and tertiary granules. Phagocytosis and killing of bacteria; forms pus. Eosinophil 150–400 (2–4%) 10–14 μm Two lobes (sausage-shaped) Large orange-red specific granules (major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein in the internum; histaminase, peroxidase in the externum). Kills parasites; phagocytoses antigen–antibody complexes. Basophil 50–100 (less than 1%) 8–10 μm S-shaped Dark blue-black specific granules (histamine, heparin, chemotactic factors, chondroitin sulphate); high-affinity IgE receptors. Mediates inflammatory and immediate hypersensitivity reactions, like a mast cell. Lymphocyte 1,500–2,500 (20–25%) 8–10 μm (small) Round, acentric, occupies most of the cell No specific granules, a few azurophilic granules. T cells (about 80% of lymphocytes) → cell-mediated immunity; B cells (about 15%) → humoral immunity and plasma cells; null cells (about 5%) = stem cells and NK cells. Monocyte 200–800 (3–8%) 12–15 μm Kidney-shaped, eccentric Abundant lysosomes, small Golgi in the nuclear indentation; largest blood cell. Leaves the blood after a few days and becomes a macrophage. Neutrophil ultrastructure: specific granules contain type IV collagenase, phospholipase A2, lactoferrin, lysozyme, phagocytin, alkaline phosphatase and vitamin B12-binding protein; azurophilic granules contain hydrolytic and oxidative enzymes; tertiary granules contain gelatinase, which digests the basal lamina to ease diapedesis. Life spans: neutrophil under 1 week; eosinophil under 2 weeks; lymphocyte from months to years; monocyte a few days in blood but several months in connective tissue. 3. Development of the granulocytes All three granulocyte lines share the first two morphologically recognisable precursors – the myeloblast and the promyelocyte – neither of which possesses specific granules. The cell in which specific granules first appear is the myelocyte , and only from this stage onwards can the line (neutrophilic, eosinophilic or basophilic) be identified. The neutrophilic series is the model: Stage Size (μm) Nucleus, nucleoli, mitosis Granules Myeloblast 12–14 Round, fine chromatin; 2–3 nucleoli; mitotic None Promyelocyte 16–24 Round to oval, coarser chromatin; 1–2 nucleoli; mitotic Azurophilic only Myelocyte 10–12 Flattened, acentric; 0–1 nucleolus; mitotic Azurophilic + specific Metamyelocyte 10–12 Kidney-shaped; no nucleoli; no mitosis Azurophilic + specific Band (stab) cell 9–12 Horseshoe-shaped; no mitosis Azurophilic + specific Neutrophil 9–12 Multilobed; no mitosis Azurophilic + specific Granulocytopoiesis depends on CFU-GM , which gives rise to CFU-G (neutrophils) and CFU-M (monocytes); eosinophils and basophils arise from CFU-Eo and CFU-Ba. IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-α induce the release of G-CSF, GM-CSF and IL-5, which stimulate the corresponding lines. Neutrophils are attracted to sites of acute inflammation by chemotactic factors; integrins bind ICAM-1 and ICAM-2 on endothelium, and after phagocytosis the respiratory burst (NADPH oxidase → superoxide → hydrogen peroxide → hypochlorous acid) kills the ingested bacteria. 4. Monocytopoiesis and the mononuclear phagocytic system Monocytopoiesis starts from the bipotential CFU-GM , which gives the unipotent CFU-M ; its precursor cells are the promonocyte , which matures into the monocyte that enters the circulation. A few days after release the monocyte leaves the bloodstream, enters the connective tissue and differentiates into a macrophage . The mononuclear phagocytic system (monocyte–macrophage system, MPS) comprises all cells derived from these marrow precursors: blood monocytes, connective-tissue macrophages and the specialised macrophages of individual organs. It is a system of phagocytes and antigen-presenting cells . Resident (fixed) macrophages: Kupffer cells of the liver, dust cells of the lung, microglia of the central nervous system, and the dendritic (Langerhans) cells of the epidermis; splenic and lymph-node macrophages; bone-marrow macrophages. Transient (free, elicited) macrophages: monocytes that enter an area of inflammation, act and then die or migrate away. Fused macrophages: foreign-body giant cells (when the material is too large for one cell) and osteoclasts of bone. Functions of macrophages: Phagocytosis of invading microorganisms, dead and disrupted cells and debris. Secretion of signalling molecules such as TNF-α and IL-1, which initiate inflammation and immune responses. Antigen presentation: antigens are broken down into epitopes, loaded on MHC II molecules and presented to T helper cells. Iron recycling after erythrophagocytosis (haemosiderin in siderophages) and removal of extruded nuclei in the bone marrow. 5. Lymphocytes and lymphopoiesis Lymphopoiesis begins with CFU-Ly , giving CFU-LyT (T cells) and CFU-LyB (B cells). B cells acquire immunocompetence in the bone marrow and then seed the B-dependent zones of secondary lymphoid organs; T-cell progenitors leave the marrow, enter the thymic cortex and become immunocompetent there (see III.9). Mature lymphocytes recirculate between blood, lymph and connective tissue; only about 2% of the body's lymphocytes are in the peripheral blood. Must-memorise Five leukocytes: neutrophil, eosinophil, basophil (granulocytes); lymphocyte, monocyte (agranulocytes). Differential count: neutrophils 60–70%, lymphocytes 20–25%, monocytes 3–8%, eosinophils 2–4%, basophils <1%. Nuclei: neutrophil multilobed, eosinophil bilobed, basophil S-shaped, lymphocyte round and acentric, monocyte kidney-shaped. Granulocyte sequence: myeloblast → promyelocyte → myelocyte (specific granules appear) → metamyelocyte → band → mature. MPS = monocytes + macrophages + specialised organ macrophages; derived from a common bone-marrow precursor. Clinical / radiological correlation Leukocyte adhesion deficiency I: defective selectin ligands or β2-integrins prevent margination and diapedesis, so leukocytes cannot reach inflamed tissue; recurrent bacterial infections occur despite a normal white-cell count. Hereditary NADPH oxidase deficiency: neutrophils phagocytose bacteria but cannot mount a respiratory burst, so they cannot kill them – recurrent bacterial infections in childhood. Pus consists of dead neutrophils, killed bacteria and extracellular fluid; the type of inflammatory exudate (purulent vs. chronic with lymphocytes, plasma cells and fibroblasts) reflects the stage of inflammation. Autoimmune disease may follow failure of selection; HIV destroys CD4+ T helper cells and thus cripples cell-mediated and most humoral responses. Likely follow-up questions What distinguishes a myeloblast from a promyelocyte? Both lack specific granules and are mitotic; the promyelocyte is larger and has azurophilic granules, coarser chromatin and fewer nucleoli. Which cell type is the largest leukocyte in the circulation? The monocyte – kidney-shaped nucleus, grey-blue cytoplasm; it becomes a macrophage in the tissues. Name four resident macrophage populations. Kupffer cells (liver), dust cells (lung), microglia (CNS), splenic/lymph-node macrophages (and epidermal dendritic cells)."},{"id":"t-III-12","p":"III","n":12,"t":"Histology of the lymphatic follicles and lymph nodes.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer Lymphoid (lymphatic) follicles ( noduli lymphoidei ) are nodular accumulations of B lymphocytes supported by reticular tissue. A primary follicle is a resting nodule without a germinal centre; a secondary follicle has a pale germinal centre (dark zone of proliferating centroblasts, light zone of centrocytes), a dense mantle of naive B cells and a peripheral marginal zone . Lymph nodes ( nodi lymphoidei ) are small, encapsulated, bean-shaped organs interposed in the course of lymphatic vessels: lymph enters through afferent vessels at the convex surface, percolates through the subcapsular, trabecular and medullary sinuses , and leaves through efferent vessels at the hilum , while lymphocytes enter the node through high endothelial venules of the paracortex. 1. Organisation of lymphoid tissue Lymphoid tissue is classified by its arrangement and by its capsule. By arrangement there is diffuse lymphoid tissue (mainly T lymphocytes scattered among the stroma) and nodular (follicular) lymphoid tissue (mainly B lymphocytes in discrete nodules). By encapsulation the organs are encapsulated (lymph nodes, spleen), partially encapsulated (tonsils) and non-encapsulated ( MALT – mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, e.g. Peyer's patches, BALT). The framework everywhere is reticular connective tissue – type III collagen fibres with reticular cells – except in the thymus, where the stroma is a reticular epithelium. Arrangement Main cell Where seen Diffuse T lymphocytes Thymic cortex, lymph-node paracortex , splenic periarterial lymphatic sheath (PALS) , tonsillar interfollicular tissue Nodular (follicular) B lymphocytes Lymph-node cortex , splenic white pulp, tonsils, MALT (Peyer's patches) 2. The lymphoid follicle (nodule) A follicle is a spherical cluster of B lymphocytes, reticular cells, follicular dendritic cells, macrophages and a few T helper cells. It has no capsule. The primary follicle is round, uniformly dark and non-activated: it has no germinal centre. When B cells are activated by antigen at the border of cortex and paracortex, they migrate into the primary follicle, begin rapid mitosis and create a pale germinal centre – the follicle becomes secondary . Compartment Cell population Functional meaning Dark zone of the germinal centre ( zona densa ) Densely packed centroblasts (spherical, up to 30 μm, several nucleoli close to the nuclear membrane) Proliferation (division roughly every 6 hours) with somatic hypermutation of the immunoglobulin variable-region genes. Light zone of the germinal centre ( zona lucida ) Centrocytes (cleaved nucleus), follicular dendritic cells , T helper cells Positive selection : centrocytes must receive a rescue signal from follicular dendritic cells, otherwise they undergo apoptosis; this is affinity maturation . Mantle (corona) Naive B lymphocytes Pushed to the periphery by the enlarging germinal centre; forms a cap towards the subcapsular sinus. Marginal zone Plasmoblasts, memory B cells, macrophages, dendritic cells Peripheral rim; first contact with blood- and lymph-borne antigens; usually indistinct on routine sections unless the organ is reactive. Follicular dendritic cells are not bone-marrow derived; they reside in the follicle, hold antigen–antibody complexes on their surface and deliver the survival signal to centrocytes (positive selection). After selection, 20% of centrocytes leave the germinal centre and become plasma cells or memory cells ; the rest return to the centroblast stage. Macrophages in the germinal centre remove apoptotic bodies – the \"starry sky\" of tingible-body macrophages on a slide. The number of secondary follicles reflects the activity of the organ: many germinal centres indicate an ongoing immune (inflammatory) reaction. 3. General structure of a lymph node Lymph nodes are small, usually bean-shaped organs (a few millimetres up to about 3 cm; a healthy node is soft and often not palpable) with a convex surface pierced by afferent vessels and a concave hilum where vessels enter and leave. There are several hundred nodes in the body, arranged in chains and groups (cervical, axillary, inguinal, mediastinal, para-aortic, pelvic). Region Structure and cells Capsule and trabeculae Dense collagenous (type I) connective tissue; trabeculae radiate inwards and divide the cortex into incomplete compartments. Deeper stroma is reticular connective tissue (type III collagen + reticular cells). Cortex Immediately beneath the capsule; contains B-cell follicles (primary and secondary) and the subcapsular sinus . Paracortex T-cell zone ; diffuse lymphocytes, dendritic cells, macrophages; contains the high endothelial venules (HEV) through which lymphocytes enter. Medulla Medullary sinuses and medullary cords ; cords contain plasma cells, B cells, macrophages and reticular cells. Hilum Concave area: arteries enter, veins and one or two efferent lymphatic vessels leave. 4. Lymph flow through the node afferent lymph vessels → subcapsular (marginal) sinus → trabecular (paratrabecular) sinuses → medullary sinuses → efferent lymph vessels at the hilum Afferent vessels pierce the capsule of the convex surface and open into the subcapsular sinus; their valves prevent lymph from flowing back out of the node. Efferent vessels at the hilum also possess valves, preventing lymph from re-entering the node. The sinuses are lined by simple squamous endothelium and spanned by a network of stellate reticular cells (littoral cells) that slow the flow, giving macrophages a scaffold on which to catch particulate matter. Macrophages in the sinuses remove approximately 99% of the antigen reaching the node by phagocytosis; the remainder reaches the follicles and is presented to B cells. Lymph flow is driven by the contraction of surrounding skeletal muscle and by vessel-wall smooth muscle, since it has no pump of its own. 5. Blood supply and lymphocyte traffic Arteries enter at the hilum and reach the parenchyma along the trabeculae; they break up into capillary beds in the cortex and pass through the paracortex as post-capillary venules . In the paracortex these venules acquire a cuboidal endothelium and become high endothelial venules : B and T lymphocytes leave the blood here, B cells migrate to the cortex and T cells remain in the paracortex. Lymphocytes leave the node with lymph through the efferent vessels or return to the blood, and recirculate between blood, lymph and lymphoid tissue throughout life. Plasma cells formed in the cortex enter the medullary cords; most of them leave through the efferent lymph and settle in the bone marrow, while some secrete antibody locally until they die. Must-memorise Primary follicle = no germinal centre; secondary follicle = germinal centre + mantle + marginal zone. Dark zone = centroblasts + mutation; light zone = centrocytes + follicular dendritic cells + positive selection. Lymph node: cortex = B cells; paracortex = T cells + HEV; medulla = cords and sinuses. Lymph route: afferent → subcapsular → trabecular → medullary sinuses → efferent at the hilum. FDC are not bone-marrow derived; all other lymph-node macrophages/APCs are. Clinical / radiological correlation Lymphadenopathy: inflammation makes a node enlarge, become tender and hard; a node enlarged by tumour is usually painless. Enlargement is caused by germinal-centre formation and lymphocyte proliferation. Sentinel node: the first node draining a region or organ; it is examined surgically (biopsy/blue dye/isotope) because it is the first to receive metastatic cells. Lymphogenic metastasis is typical of carcinomas; the sinuses act as a \"sieve\" that traps released tumour cells. Chains of connected nodes explain how infection or tumour spreads from one region to another; obstruction of lymphatics produces lymphoedema . Likely follow-up questions How do you distinguish a lymph node from a tonsil on a slide? The node is completely encapsulated with a hilum, afferent and efferent vessels, and a cortex–paracortex–medulla arrangement; the tonsil is partly covered by epithelium with crypts and has no afferent lymphatics. Where are T and B cells in the node? B cells in cortical follicles, T cells in the paracortex (around the HEV). What are the functions of the node? Filtration of lymph, phagocytosis of particulate antigen, and initiation of humoral and cell-mediated immune responses."},{"id":"t-III-13","p":"III","n":13,"t":"The functional anatomy and histology of the tonsils.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The tonsils ( tonsillae ) are partially encapsulated collections of lymphoid tissue lying beneath the epithelium at the entrance of the pharynx, where – together with the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine and lingual tonsils – they form Waldeyer's tonsillar ring . Their function is to sample inhaled and ingested antigens; the crypts trap debris and microorganisms, while the lymphoid nodules behind the epithelium mount the immune response. The palatine tonsil lies between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches, is covered by stratified squamous non-keratinised epithelium and possesses only efferent lymphatics, so it does not filter lymph. 1. Definition and classification Tonsils are partially encapsulated lymphoid organs: their free surface is covered by epithelium continuous with that of the pharynx, while only the deep surface is separated from the underlying tissues by a fibrous capsule. They consist of a stroma of reticular connective tissue containing numerous lymphoid nodules , many with germinal centres, with T lymphocytes scattered in the interfollicular tissue. Tonsil Paired or unpaired Location Palatine ( tonsilla palatina ) Paired Lateral wall of the oropharynx, in the tonsillar fossa between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches ; visible through the open mouth when the tongue is depressed. Pharyngeal ( tonsilla pharyngealis ) Unpaired Midline roof/arch of the nasopharynx ; when enlarged and inflamed it is called the adenoid . Lingual ( tonsilla lingualis ) Unpaired Dorsal aspect of the posterior one-third (pharyngeal part) of the tongue. Tubal ( tonsilla tubaria ) Paired Mucosa around the nasopharyngeal opening of the pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube; the smallest tonsil. 2. Waldeyer's tonsillar ring – functional anatomy Waldeyer's tonsillar ring ( anulus lymphoideus pharyngis ) is a nearly complete ring of lymphoid tissue surrounding the openings of the nasal and oral cavities into the pharynx. It guards the entrance of both the respiratory and the digestive tracts, so that antigens in air and food are sampled before they pass further. The ring is formed by the pharyngeal tonsil above, the palatine and tubal tonsils laterally, the lingual tonsil below and small lymphoid nodules in the mucosa of the soft palate and pharyngeal wall. Pharyngeal tonsil: on the roof of the nasopharynx; covered by respiratory epithelium (pseudostratified ciliated columnar) with pleats rather than true crypts and a thin capsule; its enlargement (adenoid) blocks the nasal airway. Palatine tonsils: the largest and most frequently examined; wedged between the two palatal arches, resting on the superior constrictor of the pharynx, with the glossopharyngeal nerve passing close to its deep surface (hence the gag reflex). Lingual tonsils: a collection of nodules at the root of the tongue; the ducts of the posterior mucous lingual glands open into the floor of its crypts. Tubal tonsil: a small nodular mass at the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube; part of the defence of the middle ear. Tonsils develop from the endoderm of the pharyngeal wall, as does the thymus, and they combine features of the thymus (endodermal origin, only draining lymphatics) with those of a lymph node (numerous B-cell nodules). They diminish with age and are at their most prominent in childhood. Their mucosal defence is mediated mainly by IgA , which is secreted onto the epithelial surface as a dimer stabilised by the secretory component produced by the epithelial cells. 3. Histology of the palatine tonsil Surface epithelium: stratified squamous non-keratinised epithelium, continuous with the pharyngeal mucosa; it is permeated by lymphocytes (\"fibrillated\" or reticulated appearance) and contains antigen-presenting cells. Crypts ( cryptae tonsillae ): about a dozen deep, branching pits lined by the same epithelium; their lumina contain desquamated epithelium, food debris, cell detritus and colonies of microorganisms. The entrance to a crypt is the fossula . Parenchyma: numerous lymphoid nodules , many with pale germinal centres, lying immediately beneath the epithelium; T lymphocytes occupy the interfollicular tissue. Each nodule is structurally identical to a lymph-node follicle. Capsule and trabeculae: the deep aspect is separated from the surrounding muscle by a dense fibrous capsule that sends trabeculae into the organ; this capsule is the plane of surgical cleavage in tonsillectomy and a barrier against the spread of infection. Lymphatic vessels: the tonsil contains only efferent lymphatic vessels; it has no afferent lymphatics and therefore does not act as a lymph filter. 4. Histological comparison of the tonsils Tonsil Epithelium Crypts Capsule Palatine Stratified squamous non-keratinised About a dozen deep crypts Thick fibrous capsule Pharyngeal Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) Shallow pleats/fossules, not true crypts Thin capsule Lingual Stratified squamous non-keratinised Numerous crypts; mucous gland ducts open at their floor Thin capsule Tubal Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) Small crypts Thin, poorly defined 5. Blood supply, innervation and lymphatic drainage Feature Detail Arteries Chiefly the tonsillar branch of the facial artery , which pierces the superior constrictor; additional contributions from the ascending palatine, ascending pharyngeal, maxillary and lingual arteries. Veins A venous network around the tonsil (paratonsillar veins) drains into the pharyngeal venous plexus and then into the facial and internal jugular veins. Nerves Sensory supply of the oropharynx – including the palatine tonsil – is from the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) via the pharyngeal plexus; this is also the afferent limb of the gag reflex. Lymphatics Efferent only : vessels pass through the pharyngeal wall to the jugulodigastric nodes (deep cervical group) at the level where the facial vein joins the internal jugular vein. Must-memorise Four tonsils: palatine and tubal paired ; pharyngeal and lingual unpaired ; together they form Waldeyer's ring . Epithelium: stratified squamous for palatine and lingual; respiratory for pharyngeal and tubal. Only efferent lymphatics → tonsils are not lymph filters. Palatine tonsil: between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches; main artery = tonsillar branch of the facial artery; sensory nerve = glossopharyngeal (IX); drainage = jugulodigastric nodes. Clinical / radiological correlation Acute tonsillitis (angina): bacteria penetrate the crypts; the crypt lumina fill with pus and appear as white dots on the surface of the swollen palatine tonsil. Peritonsillar spread: infection can extend through the capsule into the loose paratonsillar tissue, and the tonsillar region is a common site of abscess formation. Adenoid hypertrophy: enlargement of the pharyngeal tonsil causes mouth breathing, a characteristic \"adenoid face\" in children, snoring and recurrent middle-ear inflammation; it may require adenoidectomy . Tonsillectomy is facilitated by the fibrous capsule, which allows the tonsil to be shelled out; the tonsillar branch of the facial artery must be controlled. Likely follow-up questions Why is the palatine tonsil a common site of infection? Deep crypts retain debris and microorganisms, and the epithelium is thin and infiltrated by lymphocytes – ideal for antigen contact and also for microbial invasion. Which tonsils are covered by respiratory epithelium? Pharyngeal and tubal. What is the difference between a tonsil and a lymph node? A tonsil is only partially encapsulated, has an epithelial surface with crypts and only efferent lymphatics; the node is fully encapsulated with afferent and efferent vessels and no epithelium."},{"id":"t-III-14","p":"III","n":14,"t":"The spleen: anatomy, blood supply and histology.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The spleen ( splen , lien ) is the largest lymphoid organ, lying in the left hypochondrium against the diaphragm at the level of ribs IX–X. It is covered by peritoneum except at the hilum and is suspended by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments . Its blood supply is the splenic artery from the coeliac trunk; its parenchyma consists of white pulp (T cells in the periarterial lymphatic sheath and B cells in lymphoid nodules), a marginal zone and red pulp (splenic cords of Billroth and sinusoids). It filters the blood, destroys aged erythrocytes and mounts immune responses against blood-borne antigens. 1. Gross anatomy The spleen is a soft, vascular, wedge-shaped organ in the left upper quadrant (left hypochondrium) of the abdomen, lying against the diaphragm at the level of ribs IX to X . It is surrounded by viscera and is normally not palpable. It has: a convex diaphragmatic surface facing posterosuperiorly; a concave visceral surface facing anteroinferiorly, bearing impressions for the stomach (gastric impression), the left kidney (renal impression) and the left colic flexure (colic impression); an upper pole and a lower pole ; a medial hilum , the only area not covered by visceral peritoneum, where the splenic vessels enter; a dense, thin capsule of collagenous connective tissue covered by peritoneum. The spleen is connected to the greater curvature of the stomach by the gastrosplenic ligament , which contains the short gastric and gastro-omental vessels, and to the left kidney by the splenorenal ligament , which contains the splenic vessels; both ligaments are parts of the greater omentum. Occasionally the tail of the pancreas reaches the hilum. In an adult the organ weighs roughly 50–250 g. 2. Development and functions The spleen develops as part of the vascular system in the part of the dorsal mesentery that suspends the developing stomach from the body wall; it therefore belongs to the foregut region and is supplied by the coeliac trunk. It is a site of haematopoiesis in the foetus , and in the adult it can resume blood formation if the marrow fails ( extramedullary haematopoiesis ). Its functions are: Filtration of blood and destruction of senescent erythrocytes (about 120 days old) and old platelets by macrophages of the red pulp. Immune surveillance : T and B cells are activated in the white pulp against blood-borne antigens, and plasma cells and antibodies are produced. Haematopoiesis in the foetus and in marrow failure. Blood reservoir : the human spleen has only isolated myofibroblasts in its capsule and cannot contract to expel blood as the spleen of some animals does. 3. Blood supply and venous drainage The splenic artery is the largest branch of the coeliac trunk . It runs a tortuous course to the left along the superior border of the pancreas , travels in the splenorenal ligament and divides into branches that enter the hilum. Along the way it gives: short gastric arteries , which pass through the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus of the stomach; the left gastro-omental artery , which runs along the greater curvature of the stomach; numerous small branches to the neck, body and tail of the pancreas. At the hilum the artery divides into segmental branches ; inside the organ the vessels continue as trabecular arteries within the connective-tissue trabeculae, then as sheathed arteries in the white pulp, central arterioles and penicillar arteries . Venous blood is collected by red-pulp veins into trabecular veins ; these unite at the hilum to form the splenic vein , which runs behind the body of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein . Only efferent lymphatics leave the spleen. 4. Histology Capsule and trabeculae: dense collagenous connective tissue with elastic fibres, covered by mesothelium (peritoneum); trabeculae carry the trabecular arteries and veins into the parenchyma. The stroma is reticular connective tissue (type III collagen). White pulp ( pulpa alba ): lymphoid tissue arranged around the arteries, in two parts. The periarterial lymphatic sheath (PALS) is a cylinder of T lymphocytes around a central arteriole ; within or beside it a lymphoid nodule of B lymphocytes may develop, sometimes with a pale germinal centre (if the nodule is active the central arteriole appears eccentric). Marginal zone: the interface between white and red pulp; contains macrophages, interdigitating dendritic cells, plasma cells, T and B cells and small marginal sinuses. It is the first site where arriving blood contacts the splenic parenchyma and where antigens are captured. Red pulp ( pulpa rubra ): composed of splenic cords of Billroth – reticular fibres covered by stellated reticular cells, containing erythrocytes, macrophages, plasma cells, granulocytes, monocytes and platelets – separated by wide splenic sinusoids with a special endothelium (fusiform \"stave\" cells arranged along the long axis), discontinuous basal lamina and annular reticular fibres. Compartment Main cells Key morphology and role White pulp T lymphocytes (PALS), B lymphocytes (nodule), macrophages, dendritic cells Lymphoid tissue arranged around the central arteriole; immune response to blood-borne antigens. Marginal zone Macrophages, interdigitating dendritic cells, plasma cells, T and B cells Interface between white and red pulp, with small marginal sinuses; first site of antigen contact and capture. Red pulp Macrophages, erythrocytes, platelets, plasma cells, granulocytes A network of cords of Billroth and wide sinusoids; filtration of blood and phagocytosis of aged cells. 5. Open and closed circulation splenic artery → trabecular arteries → sheathed artery (in the PALS) → central arteriole → penicillar arteries (pulp arteriole → sheathed arteriole with the macrophage sheath of Schweigger–Seidel → terminal arterial capillary) → splenic sinusoids → red-pulp veins → trabecular veins → splenic vein Type Path of blood Functional meaning Closed circulation The terminal arterial capillary opens directly into a splenic sinusoid; blood remains inside the vessels. Rapid passage of blood through the red pulp; less contact with macrophages. Open circulation The capillary opens into the extravascular space of the red pulp (the cords of Billroth); blood then seeps into the sinusoids through the gaps between their endothelial cells. Slows the flow and exposes erythrocytes and platelets to the macrophages; generally considered to predominate in humans . Old red cells lose sialic acid and cannot pass through the sinusoid wall, so they are phagocytosed. Both routes exist in the human spleen. The structure of the sinusoid wall – wide intercellular gaps, a discontinuous basal lamina – is what makes the red pulp a filter: rigid, aged or damaged erythrocytes cannot squeeze through and are removed. Must-memorise Spleen: left hypochondrium, ribs IX–X, largest lymphoid organ; peritoneum covers everything except the hilum. Ligaments: gastrosplenic (short gastric and gastro-omental vessels) and splenorenal (splenic vessels). White pulp = PALS (T cells) around the central arteriole + lymphoid nodule (B cells) + marginal zone; red pulp = cords of Billroth + sinusoids. Vascular sequence: splenic artery → trabecular → sheathed → central arteriole → penicillar → sinusoids → pulp veins → trabecular veins → splenic vein → portal vein. Open versus closed circulation; open predominates in humans. Clinical / radiological correlation Splenomegaly: the spleen enlarges in infection, portal hypertension, haemolytic anaemias, leukaemia and lymphoma; it is detected by deep palpation of the left hypochondrium and confirmed by ultrasound or CT. Splenic rupture: the capsule is thin and the organ highly vascular, so blunt trauma to the left lower ribs – often with rib fractures – causes profuse intraperitoneal bleeding; rupture must always be suspected after blunt abdominal injury. Splenectomy may be required after rupture; the organ is removed with its vessels at the hilum, and the patient must thereafter be protected against blood-borne infection. Hypersplenism – an overactive spleen – removes excessive numbers of blood cells, producing cytopenias that improve after splenectomy. Likely follow-up questions What are the differences between the spleen and a lymph node? The spleen filters blood (not lymph), has no cortex/paracortex/medulla but white pulp, marginal zone and red pulp, has only efferent lymphatics, and its T zone is the PALS around a central arteriole. Where are old red blood cells destroyed? In the red pulp cords of the spleen, by macrophages; the sinusoid wall lets only deformable young cells through. Why is the spleen vulnerable in trauma? It has a thin capsule, is highly vascular and lies under the left lower ribs."},{"id":"t-III-15","p":"III","n":15,"t":"Upper airways: functional anatomy of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses. (without structures of the skull)","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The two nasal cavities ( cavitas nasi ) are the uppermost part of the respiratory tract: wedge-shaped spaces held open by bone and cartilage, divided by the nasal septum and opening posteriorly through the choanae into the nasopharynx. Their lateral wall carries the three conchae , which create the inferior, middle and superior meatuses plus the spheno-ethmoidal recess ; each meatus receives a defined set of sinus openings. They warm, humidify and filter inspired air, give the sense of smell , help in resonance of the voice, and their mucosa and paranasal sinuses are common sites of disease. Sinus drainage is the classic examination table: frontal, anterior ethmoidal and maxillary sinuses open into the middle meatus , posterior ethmoidal cells into the superior meatus , the sphenoidal sinus into the spheno-ethmoidal recess , and the nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus . 1. Boundaries, regions and general form Each nasal cavity is an elongated wedge with a wide base below (on the hard palate) and a narrow apex above and behind. It has a roof , a floor , a medial wall (the septum) and a lateral wall . Anteriorly each cavity opens onto the face through the naris (nostril) and posteriorly through the choana into the nasopharynx. The cavities are separated from the anterior cranial fossa above, the oral cavity below and the orbits laterally, which explains the direction in which sinus and nasal infection spreads. Three regions are described in each cavity: Nasal vestibule ( vestibulum nasi ) — a small dilated space just inside the naris, lined by skin with coarse hairs ( vibrissae ) that trap large particles. Respiratory region — the largest part, with a thick, richly vascular mucosa that conditions the air. Olfactory region — a small area at the apex, in the roof and upper part of the septum and superior concha, lined by olfactory epithelium. The nasal septum is partly cartilaginous (anteriorly), partly bony (perpendicular plate of the ethmoid and the vomer), with contributions from the nasal crests of the maxilla and palatine bones. Deviation of the septum is a frequent, functionally significant finding. 2. The lateral wall: conchae, meatuses and their openings Three curved shelves project medially and inferiorly from the lateral wall — the superior , middle and inferior conchae ( conchae nasales ). They divide the cavity into four air channels, increasing the surface of contact between mucosa and inspired air and creating turbulent, slow flow that favours conditioning of the air. The inferior concha is a separate bone; the superior and middle conchae are parts of the ethmoidal labyrinth. Table 1 — Air channels of the lateral nasal wall and their openings Channel Boundaries Structures opening into it Spheno-ethmoidal recess (recessus sphenoethmoidalis) Between the superior concha and the roof of the cavity Sphenoidal sinus (on the posterior wall of the recess, high on the anterior wall of the sinus) Superior meatus (meatus nasi superior) Between the middle and superior conchae Posterior ethmoidal cells Middle meatus (meatus nasi medius) Between the inferior and middle conchae Frontal sinus via the frontonasal duct and ethmoidal infundibulum (front end of the semilunar hiatus); anterior ethmoidal cells into the infundibulum/frontonasal duct; ethmoidal bulla receives the middle ethmoidal cells; maxillary sinus in the centre of the semilunar hiatus ( hiatus semilunaris ) Inferior meatus (meatus nasi inferior) Between the inferior concha and the floor of the cavity Nasolacrimal duct (ductus nasolacrimalis) — drains tears from the conjunctival sac The middle meatus is the busiest region: the semilunar hiatus is a curved groove under the ethmoidal bulla , and the ethmoidal infundibulum runs upwards from its anterior end to become the frontonasal duct . Because the maxillary ostium lies near the top of the maxillary sinus and opens into the middle meatus, the sinus cannot drain by gravity — the basis of its susceptibility to infection. The middle meatus also contains the openings of the anterior ethmoidal cells, which drain into the infundibulum. 3. The paranasal sinuses Four groups of air-filled, mucosa-lined paranasal sinuses ( sinus paranasales ) develop as outgrowths of the nasal cavity that erode into the surrounding bones during childhood and early adulthood. All of them are lined by ciliated, mucus-secreting respiratory mucosa, all open into the nasal cavity, and all are innervated by branches of the trigeminal nerve [V]. Table 2 — The paranasal sinuses: drainage, relations and innervation Sinus Form and position Opening Important relations / innervation Frontal (sinus frontalis) Paired, triangular, variable in size; part of the frontal bone above the nose; base vertical near the midline Middle meatus via frontonasal duct and ethmoidal infundibulum at the front end of the semilunar hiatus Roof and anterior wall of the sinus lie in the forehead; floor is the orbital roof so infection may spread to the orbit. Innervation: supra-orbital nerve [V1] . Ethmoidal cells (cellulae ethmoidales) Anterior, middle and posterior groups inside the ethmoidal labyrinth, between orbit and nasal cavity Anterior cells: infundibulum/frontonasal duct; middle cells: ethmoidal bulla ; posterior cells: superior meatus Separated from the orbit by the thin orbital plate ; they may erode into frontal, maxillary, lacrimal, sphenoid and palatine bones. Innervation: anterior and posterior ethmoidal nerves [V1] and orbital branches of [V2]. Maxillary (sinus maxillaris) The largest sinus; pyramidal; fills the body of the maxilla, apex pointing laterally Middle meatus , at the top of the base in the centre of the semilunar hiatus Roof = orbit; floor = roots of upper molar and premolar teeth ; anterior wall = face (canine fossa); posterior wall = infratemporal fossa. Innervation: infra-orbital and alveolar branches [V2] . Sphenoidal (sinus sphenoidalis) Paired, inside the body of the sphenoid; apertures high on the anterior wall Spheno-ethmoidal recess Above: cranial cavity, pituitary gland and optic chiasm; lateral: cavernous sinus ; below and in front: nasal cavity. Innervation: posterior ethmoidal [V1] and [V2] branches from the pterygopalatine ganglion. Only thin plates of bone separate the sinuses from dangerous neighbours, and this determines the complications: frontal sinusitis may produce an orbital abscess or a subdural empyema; ethmoidal infection may spread into the orbit; sphenoidal disease may involve the cavernous sinus; maxillary infection may spread through the floor to the teeth or, from a decayed tooth, in the opposite direction. The subarachnoid space and the hypophysis lie immediately above the roof, which is why the transsphenoidal route is used for pituitary surgery. 4. Mucosa, olfaction and respiratory protection The respiratory region is covered by respiratory epithelium : pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium rich in goblet cells , resting on a lamina propria containing seromucous glands, lymphoid aggregates and large, thin-walled venous channels known as swell bodies . Engorgement of these venous sinusoids alternates from side to side roughly every 20–30 minutes, slightly obstructing one cavity and directing most airflow to the other; this contributes to hydration of the mucosa. The cilia carry the mucus blanket, with entrapped particles, posteriorly to be swallowed — the mucociliary escalator . The nasal mucosa is the first site of contact for inhaled allergens, and its lymphoid tissue is the basis of local immunity. The olfactory region is yellowish and small. Its epithelium contains bipolar olfactory cells (cranial nerve I), supporting ( sustentacular ) cells that provide insulation and pigment, and regenerative basal cells. The axons of olfactory cells pass through the foramina of the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb. The lamina propria here contains Bowman's glands , whose watery secretion dissolves odorous molecules and flushes them away. The nose also functions as a resonating chamber for the voice, and the trigeminal mucosa triggers protective reflexes — sneezing, nasal congestion, and reflex apnoea on strong chemical stimulation. 5. Blood supply and innervation The blood supply comes from both carotid systems, with a rich submucosal anastomosis: External carotid origin: terminal branches of the maxillary artery (sphenopalatine, greater and lesser palatine) and of the facial artery (superior labial, lateral nasal). Internal carotid origin: anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries from the ophthalmic artery. The anteroinferior part of the septum — Kiesselbach's area — is the meeting point of anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial branches, and is the usual source of epistaxis. Venous blood drains by way of the facial and ophthalmic veins and the pterygoid venous plexus , which communicates with the cavernous sinus; retrograde flow through valveless veins is the route by which nasal or facial sepsis can reach the intracranial venous sinuses. General sensation is carried by the trigeminal nerve : anterior regions by the ophthalmic (V1) division and posterior regions by the maxillary (V2) division (nasopalatine, greater and lesser palatine, infra-orbital nerves). Smell is cranial nerve I . Secretion is parasympathetic from the greater petrosal nerve (VII) , which joins maxillary branches in the pterygopalatine fossa and relays in the pterygopalatine ganglion ; sympathetic fibres arise from the T1 segment, relay in the superior cervical ganglion and travel with vessels and V2 branches. Must-memorise Three conchae and four channels: inferior meatus, middle meatus, superior meatus and the spheno-ethmoidal recess. Middle meatus = frontal (frontonasal duct), anterior ethmoidal cells, ethmoidal bulla (middle cells), maxillary sinus. Superior meatus = posterior ethmoidal cells; spheno-ethmoidal recess = sphenoidal sinus; inferior meatus = nasolacrimal duct. Maxillary sinus is the largest and its ostium sits high above the floor, so it drains poorly — the commonest sinus to become infected. Nerve supply of the sinuses is the same as that of the corresponding nasal mucosa: V1 for frontal and ethmoidal, V2 for maxillary, V1/V2 for sphenoidal. Kiesselbach's area on the anteroinferior septum is the site of most nosebleeds. Clinical / radiological correlation Epistaxis: bleeding from Kiesselbach's area, usually from an anterior ethmoidal or sphenopalatine branch; managed by pressure over the alae, vasoconstrictor packing, cautery or arterial ligation in severe cases. Persistent posterior bleeding can be dangerous, especially in older patients. Acute sinusitis: pain, tenderness and discharge; the maxillary sinus is most often affected. Purulent material cannot drain from the high ostium and pools in the floor, which is why the upright position does not empty it and why the roots of the upper molars and premolars, which lie immediately below the floor, may be involved in dental infection. Complications of sinusitis: orbital cellulitis or abscess (ethmoidal and frontal), cavernous sinus thrombosis (sphenoidal, ethmoidal), subdural empyema, osteomyelitis of the frontal bone. CSF rhinorrhoea: after fracture of the cribriform plate or of the sphenoid; clear fluid drips from the nose and the infection may ascend to the meninges. Imaging: on CT the air-filled sinuses are black (low density), the mucosal lining and fluid are grey; the osteomeatal complex of the middle meatus is the key region in endoscopic sinus surgery. Likely follow-up questions Which sinus drains into the inferior meatus? — None; the inferior meatus receives only the nasolacrimal duct. Why does the nose heat and humidify air so efficiently? — The extensive contact surface of the conchae, the rich submucosal venous sinusoids (counter-current warming), seromucous glands, and slow, turbulent airflow. Where would you look on a radiograph for the maxillary sinus and what bone forms its roof? — The maxilla; its roof is the floor of the orbit. Which sinus lies next to the pituitary gland? — The sphenoidal sinus, approached through its roof for transsphenoidal pituitary surgery. How do nasal contents reach the intracranial venous sinuses? — Through valveless veins draining to the pterygoid plexus and from there to the cavernous sinus, or by direct spread through the thin ethmoidal roof."},{"id":"t-III-16","p":"III","n":16,"t":"The cartilages, ligaments and cavity of the larynx. The anatomy of the hyoid bone. Laryngoscopic image.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The larynx ( larynx ) is a musculocartilaginous tube in the anterior neck, suspended from the hyoid bone above and continuous with the trachea below, which guards the airway, produces the voice and closes during swallowing. Its skeleton is built of three unpaired cartilages (thyroid, cricoid, epiglottis) and three paired cartilages (arytenoid, corniculate, cuneiform), united by synovial joints (cricothyroid and cricoarytenoid) and by the fibro-elastic membrane — the conus elasticus with its thickened upper margin, the vocal ligament , and the quadrangular membrane with its thickened lower margin, the vestibular ligament . The cavity is divided by two pairs of folds into vestibule , middle chamber (with the laryngeal ventricle) and infraglottic cavity ; the narrowest point is the rima glottidis . On indirect laryngoscopy the typical picture is a pearly-white pair of vocal folds forming an anteriorward-pointing V, with the ventricular (false) folds pink and lateral to them and the piriform fossae alongside. 1. Position, extent and gross relations The larynx lies in the anterior part of the neck, its superior border at the level of the hyoid bone (about C3) and its inferior border at the cricoid cartilage (about C6) , where it continues as the trachea. Behind it is the laryngopharynx; in front, it is covered by the infrahyoid (strap) muscles, the deep cervical fascia and the thyroid gland, whose isthmus lies over the first tracheal rings. Laterally run the carotid sheaths with the thyroid lobes; in front of the lower part the skin and subcutaneous tissue form the median cricothyroid ligament , an emergency airway landmark. It moves upwards and forwards on swallowing and is highly mobile in the neck. Palpable landmarks are the laryngeal prominence (Adam's apple), the superior thyroid notch , the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, the cricothyroid space (median cricothyroid ligament) and the rounded arch of the cricoid cartilage. 2. The laryngeal cartilages Table 1 — The cartilages of the larynx Cartilage Type Features Thyroid (cartilago thyroidea) Hyaline; may ossify with age The largest laryngeal cartilage. Two laminae fused anteriorly at the midline, forming the laryngeal prominence ; the angle between the laminae is about 90° in men and about 120° in women . Above the prominence is the superior thyroid notch , below it the fainter inferior thyroid notch . The posterior border of each lamina ends in a superior horn (linked to the hyoid by the lateral thyrohyoid ligament) and an inferior horn (synovial joint with the cricoid). The outer surface bears the oblique line between the superior and inferior thyroid tubercles, for the attachment of sternothyroid, thyrohyoid and the inferior constrictor. Cricoid (cartilago cricoidea) Hyaline The only laryngeal cartilage that completely encircles the airway — a signet ring: narrow arch in front, broad lamina behind. Its upper border is at the level of C6. On the lamina are facets for the arytenoid cartilages and, on each side, for the inferior horn of the thyroid; the arch gives attachment to the cricothyroid muscle and to the cricotracheal ligament below. Epiglottis (epiglottis) Elastic cartilage Leaf-shaped, standing posterosuperiorly behind the tongue and above the laryngeal inlet. Its stem is fixed to the thyroid cartilage at the angle, midway between the laryngeal prominence and the inferior notch, by the thyro-epiglottic ligament . The lower half of its posterior surface is raised as the epiglottic tubercle . Its lingual surface is covered by stratified squamous epithelium; it is flexible and springs back after swallowing. Arytenoid (cartilago arytenoidea) Hyaline; vocal process elastic Paired, pyramid-shaped, with three surfaces and a concave base that articulates with the cricoid lamina. The vocal process projects anteriorly and carries the vocal ligament; the muscular process projects laterally for the posterior and lateral crico-arytenoid muscles. The apex articulates with the corniculate cartilage. Corniculate (cartilago corniculata) Elastic Small cones on the apices of the arytenoid cartilages; their apices project posteromedially and form the corniculate tubercles at the posterior margin of the laryngeal inlet. Cuneiform (cartilago cuneiformis) Elastic Small club-shaped cartilages in the ary-epiglottic folds, anterior to the corniculate cartilages; they produce the cuneiform tubercles of the inlet. 3. Joints, membranes and ligaments Two pairs of synovial joints move the cartilages relative to each other and are the mechanical basis of phonation: Cricothyroid joints — between the inferior horns of the thyroid and the sides of the cricoid. They allow the thyroid cartilage to tilt forwards and downwards on the cricoid, which lengthens and tightens the vocal ligaments. Crico-arytenoid joints — between the bases of the arytenoid cartilages and the sloping facets of the cricoid lamina. They allow the arytenoids to slide towards and away from each other and to rotate, so that the vocal processes pivot medially (adduction) or laterally (abduction). Table 2 — Membranes and ligaments of the larynx Structure Attachments Note Thyrohyoid membrane (membrana thyrohyoidea) Upper border of the thyroid cartilage to the hyoid bone; thickened in the midline as the median thyrohyoid ligament and posterolaterally as the lateral thyrohyoid ligaments Extrinsic membrane suspending the larynx. It is pierced on each side by the internal laryngeal nerve and the superior laryngeal artery and vein . Hyo-epiglottic ligament Apex of the epiglottis to the body of the hyoid Attaches the epiglottis to the hyoid and allows it to fold down over the inlet during swallowing. Cricotracheal ligament Lower border of the cricoid to the first tracheal cartilage Extrinsic; the level where the trachea begins (C6). Conus elasticus (cricovocal membrane) (membrana fibroelastica laryngis) Arch of the cricoid, spreading upwards; its free upper margin attaches anteriorly to the thyroid angle and posteriorly to the vocal processes of the arytenoids The free margin is thickened as the vocal ligament (true vocal cord). Anteriorly the conus is thickened as the median cricothyroid ligament , the site for emergency cricothyrotomy. Quadrangular membrane Lateral margin of the epiglottis, corniculate and arytenoid cartilages Its free lower margin is thickened as the vestibular ligament (false vocal cord), which lies lateral and superior to the vocal ligament. Because the vestibular ligament attaches to the anterolateral surface of the arytenoid while the vocal ligament attaches to the vocal process, the vestibular ligament lies lateral to the vocal ligament when seen from above. The gap between them opens into the laryngeal ventricle . 4. The cavity of the larynx The cavity is tubular and lined by mucosa; its shape is determined by the fibro-elastic membrane and the cartilages. It is described in three parts separated by two pairs of folds. The laryngeal inlet ( aditus laryngis ) opens posterosuperiorly into the pharynx. Its anterior border is the mucosa over the epiglottis; its lateral borders are the ary-epiglottic folds ; its posterior border is the interarytenoid notch, with the corniculate tubercles on either side. The inferior opening , in contrast, is horizontal, completely encircled by the cricoid and continuous with the trachea. Table 3 — Divisions of the laryngeal cavity Space Boundaries Contains / note Vestibule From the laryngeal inlet above to the vestibular folds below Encloses the vestibular ligaments and soft tissue of the quadrangular membrane. Middle chamber Between the vestibular folds above and the vocal folds below Very narrow; on each side the mucosa bulges laterally through the gap between the two ligaments to form the laryngeal ventricle , whose tubular upward extension is the saccule . The saccule contains mucous glands that lubricate the vocal folds. Rima vestibuli The triangular opening between the two vestibular folds Apex anterior; base is the posterior wall of the laryngeal cavity. It can be narrowed by the arytenoid movements during effort closure. Rima glottidis The opening between the two vocal folds, lateral walls formed by the vocal folds and the mucosa over the arytenoids The narrowest part of the airway ; its shape changes from a rhomboid (abducted, during inspiration) to a slit (adducted, during phonation). Infraglottic cavity Between the vocal folds and the inferior opening of the larynx Continuous with the tracheal lumen; its walls are supported by the cricoid cartilage. 5. The hyoid bone The hyoid bone ( os hyoideum ) is a small U-shaped bone lying horizontally in the upper neck at about the level of C3 , where it can be palpated between the mandible and the thyroid cartilage and moved from side to side. It has a body anteriorly and two pairs of horns: the greater horns , which project posteriorly and continue the line of the body, and the lesser horns , which project upwards from the junction of body and greater horn. It has no direct articulation with any other bone; it is suspended by muscles and by the stylohyoid ligament from the styloid process, and it provides attachment for the tongue, the larynx and the pharynx — it lies at the interface of the floor of the mouth, the larynx and the pharynx. Table 4 — Attachments of the hyoid bone Group Attachments Above (suprahyoid) Digastric (intermediate tendon), stylohyoid (near the lesser horn), mylohyoid (body), geniohyoid (body), and the hyo-epiglottic ligament. Below (infrahyoid) Sternohyoid, omohyoid (superior belly) and thyrohyoid, plus the thyrohyoid membrane of the larynx. Posterior / lateral Middle and inferior constrictors of the pharynx, and the stylohyoid ligament . 6. The laryngoscopic image Indirect laryngoscopy uses a small warmed mirror held in the oropharynx; the image is inverted , so the anterior structures appear at the top. Direct laryngoscopy is performed with a curved blade whose tip is placed in the vallecula to lift the epiglottis forwards; it requires an unconscious patient or one in whom the gag reflex is abolished. Flexible fibreoptic endoscopy through the nose gives a direct, upright view and allows assessment of movement. The normal image shows, from anterior to posterior: the epiglottis with its free margin, the paired ary-epiglottic folds , the piriform fossae laterally, and the arytenoid and corniculate tubercles posteriorly. The vestibular folds (false cords) are pink and lie lateral to the pearly-white, sharp-edged vocal folds , which meet anteriorly at the anterior commissure and diverge posteriorly. During quiet inspiration the rima glottidis is triangular, widening into a rhomboid only on forced inspiration; on phonation of a high-pitched 'ee' the vocal folds adduct, lengthen and become tense; during a Valsalva manoeuvre they close completely, and the false folds are apposed. The examiner looks for symmetry, mobility, mucosal irregularity, nodules, oedema or a mass. Must-memorise Cartilages: unpaired — thyroid, cricoid, epiglottis ; paired — arytenoid, corniculate, cuneiform . Cricoid is the only complete ring; the epiglottis is elastic cartilage. Vocal ligament = thickened free margin of the conus elasticus ; vestibular ligament = thickened free margin of the quadrangular membrane . Thyrohyoid membrane transmits the internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal vessels . Median cricothyroid ligament — the emergency airway, between the inferior thyroid notch and the cricoid arch. Cavity: vestibule → rima vestibuli → middle chamber (ventricle, saccule) → rima glottidis → infraglottic cavity. Hyoid at C3, no direct joint, suspended by the stylohyoid ligament; greater horn and lower body from the 3rd pharyngeal arch, lesser horn and upper body from the 2nd arch. Clinical / radiological correlation Acute epiglottitis (mainly in children): rapid swelling of the epiglottis with stridor and a muffled voice; the lateral neck radiograph shows the thumb sign . The airway must not be manipulated without facilities for immediate intubation. Cricothyrotomy: the median cricothyroid ligament is perforated when the airway is obstructed above the vocal folds; only skin, superficial tissue and small vessels lie between it and the skin. Laryngeal trauma: the thyroid and cricoid cartilages are injured by blunt blows; the airway may be disrupted, and the cricothyroid and crico-arytenoid joints may be dislocated, producing an immobile vocal fold. Laryngoscopy in the examination setting: the larynx is inspected for hoarseness, suspected tumour, a foreign body, or vocal fold palsy; on phonation a paralysed fold lies paramedian and does not move. Laryngomalacia in infants: floppy epiglottis and ary-epiglottic folds causing inspiratory stridor and a 'floppy' image. Likely follow-up questions Which laryngeal cartilage is a complete ring and what is its vertebral level? — The cricoid, at about C6, where the trachea begins. Which joint tenses the vocal folds and how? — The cricothyroid joint: the thyroid cartilage tilts forwards and downwards, lengthening the vocal ligaments. Name the structures pierced by the thyrohyoid membrane. — The internal laryngeal nerve and the superior laryngeal artery and vein. What separates the true from the false vocal fold? — The gap between the vocal and vestibular ligaments, which opens laterally into the laryngeal ventricle and saccule. Why is the laryngeal inlet safe during swallowing? — The larynx moves up and forwards, the epiglottis is folded down over the inlet, and the rima glottidis is closed; the bolus passes through the piriform fossae into the oesophagus. Which part of the laryngeal cavity is the narrowest? — The rima glottidis, between the vocal folds."},{"id":"t-III-17","p":"III","n":17,"t":"The muscles, blood supply and innervation of the larynx. The histology of the larynx.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The muscles of the larynx are divided into extrinsic muscles, which move the whole larynx (suprahyoid, infrahyoid and pharyngeal muscles), and intrinsic muscles, which act on the cricothyroid and crico-arytenoid joints and on the vocal ligaments. The intrinsic group has one abductor — the posterior crico-arytenoid — several adductors (lateral crico-arytenoid, transverse and oblique arytenoids, thyro-arytenoid) and two tensors (cricothyroid and vocalis). All intrinsic muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve except the cricothyroid , which is supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve . Blood comes from the superior and inferior laryngeal arteries ; the mucosa is respiratory epithelium except over the vocal folds and the upper (superior) part of the epiglottis, where it is non-keratinized stratified squamous. 1. Classification Extrinsic muscles attach the larynx to the hyoid bone, the mandible, the sternum and the pharynx; they move the whole larynx up or down and so participate in swallowing and in phonation. Intrinsic muscles have both attachments on the laryngeal cartilages and fibro-elastic membrane; they alter the tension of the vocal ligaments, open and close the rima glottidis, narrow the vestibule and close the laryngeal inlet. They achieve this by moving the cricothyroid and crico-arytenoid joints, by pulling directly on the vocal folds, and by forcing the soft tissues of the quadrangular membrane towards the midline. 2. The intrinsic muscles Table 1 — Intrinsic muscles of the larynx Muscle Origin Insertion Action Nerve Cricothyroid (m. cricothyroideus) Anterolateral surface of the arch of the cricoid Oblique part: inferior horn of the thyroid; straight part: lower border of the thyroid lamina Tilts the thyroid cartilage forwards and downwards at the cricothyroid joint — lengthens and tenses the vocal folds (raises pitch) External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve Posterior crico-arytenoid (m. cricoarytenoideus posterior) Depression on the posterior surface of the cricoid lamina Posterior surface of the muscular process of the arytenoid Abducts and externally rotates the arytenoid — the only opener of the rima glottidis Recurrent laryngeal nerve Lateral crico-arytenoid (m. cricoarytenoideus lateralis) Upper border of the arch of the cricoid Anterior surface of the muscular process of the arytenoid Internally rotates the arytenoid — adducts the vocal folds and closes the rima glottidis Recurrent laryngeal nerve Transverse arytenoid (m. arytenoideus transversus) Lateral border of the posterior surface of one arytenoid Same border of the opposite arytenoid Approximates the two arytenoids — adduction ; the only unpaired intrinsic muscle Recurrent laryngeal nerve Oblique arytenoid (m. arytenoideus obliquus) Posterior surface of the muscular process of the arytenoid Apex of the opposite arytenoid; some fibres continue into the ary-epiglottic fold Sphincter of the laryngeal inlet — pulls the arytenoids together and narrows the inlet Recurrent laryngeal nerve Thyro-arytenoid (m. thyroarytenoideus) Thyroid angle and adjacent cricothyroid ligament Anterolateral surface of the arytenoid; some fibres pass into the ary-epiglottic fold to the epiglottis Sphincter of the vestibule and inlet ; relaxes the vocal fold Recurrent laryngeal nerve Vocalis (m. vocalis) Lateral surface of the vocal process of the arytenoid Vocal ligament and thyroid angle Fine adjustment of tension in the vocal fold — sharpens the edge of the fold Recurrent laryngeal nerve The functional grouping is the answer the examiner wants: abduction — posterior crico-arytenoid only; adduction — lateral crico-arytenoid, transverse arytenoid, oblique arytenoid, thyro-arytenoid; tension — cricothyroid (gross) and vocalis (fine); closure of the inlet — oblique arytenoid and thyro-arytenoid. 3. The extrinsic muscles Table 2 — Extrinsic muscles acting on the larynx and hyoid Group Muscles Action Nerve (root) Suprahyoid Digastric (anterior and posterior bellies), stylohyoid, mylohyoid, geniohyoid Elevate the hyoid and, with it, the larynx in swallowing; with the hyoid fixed they open the mouth Anterior belly of digastric and mylohyoid: nerve to mylohyoid from V3 ; posterior belly and stylohyoid: facial [VII] ; geniohyoid: C1 carried on the hypoglossal nerve Infrahyoid (strap muscles) Sternohyoid, omohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid Depress the hyoid and larynx after swallowing; thyrohyoid elevates the larynx when the hyoid is fixed Sternohyoid, omohyoid, sternothyroid: ansa cervicalis (C1–C3) ; thyrohyoid: C1 carried on the hypoglossal nerve Pharyngeal Stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus, superior and inferior constrictors Elevate and suspend the larynx and pharynx during swallowing Stylopharyngeus: glossopharyngeal [IX] ; palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus and constrictors: pharyngeal plexus from vagus [X] 4. Innervation All motor and sensory innervation of the larynx comes from the vagus nerve [X] through two branches on each side. Superior laryngeal nerve arises from the inferior vagal ganglion high in the neck and divides just above the level of the superior horn of the hyoid. Its internal branch pierces the thyrohyoid membrane with the superior laryngeal artery and is mainly sensory to the laryngeal cavity down to the vocal folds , including the laryngeal surface of the epiglottis. Its external branch descends on the inferior constrictor and supplies the cricothyroid . Recurrent laryngeal nerve is sensory below the vocal folds and motor to all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid . The left recurrent nerve arises in the thorax and hooks under the arch of the aorta lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum; the right arises in the root of the neck and hooks under the right subclavian artery. Both ascend in the groove between the oesophagus and the trachea and enter the larynx deep to the margin of the inferior constrictor. This asymmetry matters clinically: a mass in the aortopulmonary window paralyses the left cord; a right apical lung tumour (Pancoast tumour) paralyses the right. The vocal folds also receive autonomic fibres along blood vessels and proprioceptive fibres that are of little clinical significance. 5. Blood supply and lymphatic drainage Table 3 — Vessels and lymphatics of the larynx Structure Origin / destination Note Superior laryngeal artery Superior thyroid artery from the external carotid Enters with the internal laryngeal nerve through the thyrohyoid membrane; supplies the upper half of the larynx. Inferior laryngeal artery Inferior thyroid artery from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian Ascends with the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheo-oesophageal groove; supplies the lower half. Superior laryngeal veins Superior thyroid vein → internal jugular vein Above the vocal folds. Inferior laryngeal veins Inferior thyroid vein → left brachiocephalic vein Below the vocal folds. Lymphatics above the vocal folds Along the superior laryngeal artery to deep cervical nodes at the carotid bifurcation Rich lymphatic network. Lymphatics below the vocal folds To deep nodes of the inferior thyroid chain and to the prelaryngeal and pretracheal nodes Carcinoma below the folds may spread early to the pretracheal nodes. The vocal folds themselves are sparsely supplied with lymphatics and vessels. Because of this, early glottic carcinoma gives hoarseness early but metastasises to lymph nodes late, whereas supraglottic and subglottic tumours drain freely and present with nodal spread. 6. Histology of the larynx The laryngeal mucosa shows two types of epithelium, and the transition between them is a favourite examination point. Site Epithelium Special features Laryngeal surface of the epiglottis and upper part; vocal folds , ary-epiglottic folds, margins of the inlet Stratified squamous non-keratinised Mechanical protection from contact and from the airstream; the vocal fold needs a sharp, resilient edge. Rest of the laryngeal cavity (vestibule, ventricle, infraglottic space) Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) Goblet cells and underlying seromucous glands; the cilia sweep mucus towards the pharynx. Vocal fold (coronal section) Layered structure Epithelium → superficial lamina propria ( Reinke's space , loose, poor in vessels) → intermediate layer (elastic fibres) and deep layer (collagen), which together form the vocal ligament → vocalis muscle. This layered architecture is the basis of the mucosal wave of phonation. Saccule Respiratory epithelium Numerous mucous glands in its wall; secretion lubricates the vocal folds. Cartilages Hyaline: thyroid, cricoid, most of the arytenoids. Elastic: epiglottis, corniculate, cuneiform, vocal process Hyaline cartilage may ossify with age, which is visible radiologically; the elastic cartilages keep their flexibility. Joints Synovial (cricothyroid, crico-arytenoid) Fibrous capsule reinforced by ligaments; prone to post-traumatic fibrosis. Must-memorise Abduction: posterior crico-arytenoid (only). Adduction: lateral crico-arytenoid, transverse and oblique arytenoids, thyro-arytenoid. Tension: cricothyroid (coarse, superior laryngeal nerve) and vocalis (fine, recurrent laryngeal nerve). All intrinsic muscles are recurrent laryngeal except cricothyroid = external branch of superior laryngeal nerve. Sensory: internal laryngeal nerve above the folds, recurrent laryngeal nerve below the folds. Transverse arytenoid is the only unpaired intrinsic muscle; the only abductor is the posterior crico-arytenoid. The inferior laryngeal artery and the recurrent laryngeal nerve ascend together in the tracheo-oesophageal groove — at risk in thyroidectomy. Clinical / radiological correlation Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury: the vocal fold takes the cadaveric position (half-way between abduction and adduction) and the voice is hoarse; bilateral injury causes stridor and may require tracheostomy. Causes include thyroid and parathyroid surgery, oesophageal and lung cancer, aortic aneurysm and enlarged mediastinal nodes. External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve injury (for example during ligation of the superior thyroid vessels): loss of high-pitched phonation and fatigue of the voice, while the vocal fold remains mobile. Laryngeal carcinoma: hoarseness, stridor or dysphagia; glottic tumours are often diagnosed early and spread late, while supraglottic tumours spread to deep cervical nodes. Reinke's oedema: fluid in the superficial lamina propria of the vocal folds in smokers, giving a deep, husky voice and polypoid folds. Vocal fold nodules: bilateral fibrotic thickenings from vocal abuse, typically near the junction of the anterior and middle thirds of the fold. Likely follow-up questions Which is the only abductor muscle of the vocal folds? — The posterior crico-arytenoid; its palsy leaves the fold in the paramedian position. By what route does the internal laryngeal nerve enter the larynx, and with what vessel? — Through the thyrohyoid membrane, with the superior laryngeal artery. Why is the vocal fold covered by stratified squamous epithelium and not respiratory epithelium? — It is exposed to contact and friction and must withstand mechanical stress to produce a clean vibrating edge. Compare the lymphatics above and below the vocal folds. — Above: deep cervical nodes; below: inferior thyroid, prelaryngeal and pretracheal nodes. How does the layered structure of the vocal fold produce the mucosal wave? — Loose Reinke's space lets the epithelium slide over the stiff ligament while the vocalis adjusts tension and shape."},{"id":"t-III-18","p":"III","n":18,"t":"The anatomy of the trachea. Organization of the bronchial system. The histological properties of the trachea, the bronchi and bronchioli. Functional structure and cell types of the alveolus.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The trachea is a flexible tube about 12 cm long that begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage (about C6 ) and bifurcates at vertebral level T4/T5 , at the sternal angle , into the right and left main bronchi . Its wall is kept open by C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings whose open ends face posteriorly and are joined by the trachealis muscle . The bronchial tree continues as lobar and segmental bronchi , then bronchioles , terminal and respiratory bronchioles , alveolar ducts , alveolar sacs and alveoli ; as the branches narrow, cartilage and glands disappear, the epithelium flattens, and smooth muscle and elastic tissue become relatively more abundant. The alveolus is lined by type I pneumocytes (about 95 % of the surface area) and type II pneumocytes (which produce surfactant ) and contains alveolar macrophages ; the blood–air (alveolocapillary) barrier consists of endothelium, fused basal laminae, type I pneumocyte and surfactant. 1. The trachea: extent, relations and structure The trachea ( trachea ) begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, at about the level of C6 , and ends at its bifurcation into the two main bronchi at vertebral level T4/T5 — a level that can be marked on the body surface by the sternal angle , the plane separating the superior from the inferior mediastinum. It is about 12 cm long. The carina is the hook-shaped ridge of the last tracheal cartilage that projects backwards in the midline between the origins of the two main bronchi; it is the landmark used at bronchoscopy. Table 1 — Relations of the trachea Direction Structures Anterior Skin and superficial fascia, deep cervical fascia, the infrahyoid (strap) muscles and the isthmus of the thyroid gland over the second to fourth rings; below, the manubrium and thymic remnants. This is the surgical and percutaneous access line (tracheostomy). Posterior The oesophagus , with the recurrent laryngeal nerves running in the tracheo-oesophageal groove; the membranous posterior wall lies directly against it, which is why the trachea is mobile during swallowing. Lateral The thyroid lobes , the carotid sheath and its contents, the cervical sympathetic chain; lower down the arch of the aorta (left), the azygos vein and the superior vena cava (right), and the mediastinal pleura on each side. The wall is built in three layers. The mucosa consists of respiratory epithelium, a fibro-elastic lamina propria and a thick elastic sheet; the submucosa contains seromucous glands and lymphoid tissue; the adventitia carries the C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, whose perichondria are linked by fibro-elastic tissue. The open ends of the rings face posteriorly and are joined by the trachealis (smooth) muscle in the membranous wall; its contraction reduces the tracheal lumen and speeds airflow during coughing. 2. Organisation of the bronchial system The trachea bifurcates into two main (primary) bronchi , each of which enters the root and hilum of a lung. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, so inhaled foreign bodies lodge on the right more often. The right main bronchus gives its lobar branch to the superior lobe while still in the root (the eparterial bronchus , superior to the right pulmonary artery); on the left the superior lobe bronchus arises within the lung. Each lobar (secondary) bronchus supplies one lobe: three on the right, two on the left . Each then divides into segmental (tertiary) bronchi , each supplying one of the ten bronchopulmonary segments of that lung. Beyond that lie the bronchioles and the respiratory portion. Table 2 — Generations of the bronchial tree Level Number Cartilage Glands Epithelium Key feature Trachea 1 C-shaped hyaline rings Present Pseudostratified ciliated columnar Trachealis muscle posteriorly Main (primary) bronchi 2 C-shaped rings, becoming plates inside the lung Present Respiratory Right wider and more vertical; eparterial bronchus on the right Lobar (secondary) bronchi 3 right, 2 left Irregular plates of hyaline cartilage around the whole circumference Present Respiratory One per lobe; smooth muscle in two helical layers Segmental (tertiary) bronchi 10 per lung Plates, decreasing in size Present, fewer Respiratory One per bronchopulmonary segment; the smallest surgically independent unit Bronchioles many None None Simple ciliated columnar to cuboidal; Clara (club) cells Diameter < 1 mm; serves a pulmonary lobule; relatively thick smooth muscle Terminal bronchioles many None None Simple cuboidal; many Clara cells, no goblet cells < 0.5 mm; the last purely conducting segment; serves a lung acinus Respiratory bronchioles many None None Cuboidal with Clara cells and pneumocytes Alveoli open into their walls — first gas exchange Alveolar ducts and sacs many None None Squamous (type I and type II pneumocytes) No wall of their own; elastic fibres keep them patent Three general rules govern the transition from the hilum to the periphery: the amount of cartilage and glandular tissue decreases ; the proportion of goblet cells and the thickness of the epithelium decrease ; and the relative amount of smooth muscle and elastic tissue increases . The total cross-sectional area of the airways increases at each generation, so airflow velocity falls, which favours gas exchange. 3. Histology of the trachea, bronchi and bronchioli The respiratory epithelium is pseudostratified ciliated columnar and contains six cell types: ciliated columnar cells, goblet cells (about one third of the population, storing mucinogen in their theca), short regenerative basal cells , brush cells with long microvilli, serous cells, and DNES (neuroendocrine) cells that respond to hypoxia and form part of the pulmonary neuroepithelial bodies. The cilia drive the mucus blanket towards the larynx — the mucociliary escalator . In the trachea the C-rings lie in the adventitia; in secondary and tertiary bronchi the cartilage is broken into plates that no longer have open arms facing posteriorly, so the smooth muscle migrates inwards and forms two helical layers between the lamina propria and the submucosa. Bronchioles lose cartilage and glands altogether; their patency depends on elastic fibres radiating from the adventitia and on a relatively thick smooth-muscle coat. Clara (club) cells secrete a surfactant-like material that helps to keep the bronchiolar lumen open, detoxify inhaled substances and act as stem cells. 4. The alveolus: cell types, surfactant and the air–blood barrier Alveoli are the terminal air spaces; there are about 300 million in the two lungs, each about 200 μm in diameter, giving a total surface of roughly 140 m² for gas exchange. Neighbouring alveoli share an interalveolar septum which contains the continuous capillary network, elastic and reticular fibres and connective tissue cells; the openings between adjacent alveoli ( alveolar pores ) allow collateral ventilation, and a smooth muscle sphincter encircles the mouth of each alveolus. Cell type Features and function Type I pneumocyte (squamous alveolar cell) Highly attenuated cell forming more than 95 % of the alveolar surface ; the attenuated cytoplasm is only about 80 nm thick and the organelles are gathered around the nucleus. Adjacent type I cells are joined by occluding junctions that prevent fluid leaking into the alveolar airspace. It cannot divide. Type II pneumocyte (septal cell) Rounded, cuboidal cell, more numerous than the type I cell but covering only about 5 % of the surface . Its lamellar bodies store and release pulmonary surfactant — dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine with apoproteins SP-A, SP-B, SP-C and SP-D. Surfactant lowers surface tension and keeps the alveoli patent during expiration. The type II cell is also the stem cell of the alveolar epithelium and can give rise to both cell types. Alveolar macrophage (dust cell) Derived from blood monocytes; lies in the septum or migrates into the alveolar lumen. It phagocytoses inhaled particles and micro-organisms and removes used surfactant; it leaves the lung by lymphatics or by climbing the bronchial tree to be swallowed or expectorated. Capillary endothelium Continuous non-fenestrated endothelium of the pulmonary capillaries; the first layer of the barrier and the site where gas exchange begins. The blood–air (alveolocapillary) barrier is the thinnest possible arrangement of tissues, in four layers: 1 capillary endothelium → 2 fused basal laminae of endothelium and type I pneumocyte → 3 attenuated type I pneumocyte → 4 surfactant lining the alveolar airspace → alveolar air. Elastic fibres are essential to the passive recoil of expiration; their destruction in emphysema is the reason the lungs lose elasticity and the small airways collapse. Must-memorise Trachea: from C6 to T4/T5 (sternal angle), about 12 cm; carina at the bifurcation; C-rings with trachealis posteriorly. Bronchial tree: main → lobar (3 right / 2 left) → segmental (10 per lung) → bronchiole (< 1 mm) → terminal bronchiole (< 0.5 mm) → respiratory bronchiole → alveolar duct → sac → alveolus. Cartilage: rings in the trachea, plates in the bronchi, absent in bronchioles. Glands: present down to the bronchi only. Type I: 95 % of surface, 80 nm thick, occluding junctions. Type II: 5 % of surface, lamellar bodies, surfactant (dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine), stem cell. Blood–air barrier: endothelium → fused basal laminae → type I pneumocyte → surfactant. About 300 million alveoli, each about 200 μm, total surface about 140 m². Clinical / radiological correlation Foreign body aspiration: the right main bronchus is wider and more vertical, so the object usually lodges on the right, producing unilateral wheeze and air trapping. Tracheostomy is performed through the second and third tracheal rings, dividing the thyroid isthmus if necessary; the cricoid and cricothyroid region are avoided because stenosis and vocal damage are likely. Asthma: bronchospasm of the abundant smooth muscle of the bronchioles plus mucosal oedema and mucus plugging; the response to sympathomimetic bronchodilators is possible because the smooth muscle bears sympathetic receptors. Emphysema: destruction of alveolar walls and elastic tissue reduces both the surface for gas exchange and the elastic recoil, so small airways close during expiration (air trapping). Respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn: immaturity of type II pneumocytes with insufficient surfactant, alveolar collapse, ground-glass lung fields and hyaline membranes. Squamous metaplasia of the respiratory epithelium in chronic smokers: ciliated cells are lost and goblet cells increase, so mucus cannot be cleared and infection follows. Likely follow-up questions At what vertebral level does the trachea bifurcate? — T4/T5, which corresponds on the surface to the sternal angle; the carina lies at this level. How does the histology of a bronchus differ from that of a bronchiole? — The bronchus has cartilage plates and glands; the bronchiole has neither but has a relatively thicker smooth-muscle coat and Clara cells. What are the cell types of the alveolar region and their roles? — Type I (gas exchange), type II (surfactant and regeneration), macrophage (clearance). Why do the alveoli not collapse at end-expiration? — Surfactant lowers surface tension, elastic fibres maintain radial traction, and the alveolar pores allow collateral ventilation. What happens to the bronchial wall after chronic irritation? — Metaplasia of the epithelium with goblet-cell hyperplasia, loss of cilia and hypertrophy of mucous glands, so clearance fails."},{"id":"t-III-19","p":"III","n":19,"t":"Development of the respiratory system.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The respiratory system develops as an endodermal outgrowth of the ventral wall of the foregut — the respiratory (lung) diverticulum — which appears in the 4th week and grows into the surrounding splanchnic mesoderm. The diverticulum forms the laryngotracheal groove , which deepens and is separated from the oesophagus by the tracheo-oesophageal septum , giving rise to the larynx, trachea and bronchial tree. The endoderm gives the epithelium and glands ; the splanchnic mesoderm gives the cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue and blood vessels . Further growth proceeds through five stages — embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, terminal saccular and alveolar — whose approximate weeks must be known, and failure at any step produces a characteristic malformation: tracheo-oesophageal fistula, laryngeal web, pulmonary hypoplasia or respiratory distress syndrome. 1. The lung bud and the separation of the airway from the gut In the 4th week a median respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) appears on the ventral aspect of the foregut, just caudal to the pharyngeal pouches. It grows caudally and ventrally into the splanchnic (visceral) mesoderm of the foregut. As the diverticulum elongates, its cranial end forms the laryngotracheal groove , whose lips fuse to create the laryngotracheal tube . The tube is separated from the dorsal part of the foregut by the tracheo-oesophageal septum , so that the ventral derivative becomes the airway and the dorsal one becomes the oesophagus. Two rules must be stated at the start of any answer: the epithelium and the glands of the whole respiratory tract derive from endoderm , while the cartilages, muscles, connective tissue and blood vessels derive from the surrounding splanchnic mesoderm . This is why the respiratory epithelium is endodermal but the laryngeal cartilage is mesodermal in origin. The distal end of the tube then divides into two bronchial buds , which grow laterally into the pericardioperitoneal canals and become surrounded by the pleural cavities. The right bud divides into three, the left into two, giving the lobar pattern. Subsequent branching is a reciprocal interaction between the endodermal epithelium and the mesenchyme, which determines the number and pattern of the airways. 2. Development of the larynx The larynx develops around the laryngeal inlet , from the region between the 4th and 6th pharyngeal arches. Its cartilages come from the branchial arch cartilages: the thyroid cartilage mainly from the 4th arch , the cricoid, arytenoid and corniculate from the 6th arch , and the epiglottis from the hypopharyngeal eminence at the junction of the 3rd and 4th arches; the elastic cartilages retain their elasticity for life. The laryngeal muscles are derived from the mesoderm of the 4th and 6th arches , and this explains their innervation: the 4th-arch muscle is the cricothyroid, supplied by the superior laryngeal nerve , while the 6th-arch muscles are those supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve . The laryngeal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalises . Failure of recanalisation produces a laryngeal web or atresia; incomplete separation of the larynx from the pharynx may give a laryngeal cleft. The larynx also descends in the neck during childhood, which is why the infant larynx is high and the epiglottis lies close to the soft palate, allowing simultaneous breathing and feeding. 3. The five stages of lung development Table 1 — Stages of lung development (approximate weeks) Stage Approximate weeks Main events Embryonic 4–7 Lung bud forms and separates from the foregut; laryngotracheal tube, trachea and the two main bronchi appear; the lobar pattern is established. Pseudoglandular 5–17 The conducting airways develop down to the level of the terminal bronchioles ; the lungs look like a gland under the microscope. No alveoli and no gas exchange, so a fetus born in this period cannot survive. Canalicular 16–25 Respiratory bronchioles and the first alveolar ducts appear; the capillary network grows into the mesenchyme and the blood–air barrier becomes thin. Type II pneumocytes begin to secrete surfactant and the fetus becomes potentially viable towards the end of this stage. Terminal saccular (saccular) 24–term Terminal sacs and increasing numbers of type II cells; surfactant production rises steeply in the last weeks; true alveoli are not yet present. Alveolar late fetal (about 36 weeks) to childhood (about 8 years) The terminal sacs are divided by secondary septa into true alveoli ; the number of alveoli increases after birth and the lung surface area grows enormously. Alveolar multiplication is largely complete by about 8 years of age. 4. Maturation, surfactant and the fetal circulation Surfactant is produced by type II pneumocytes : it is stored in lamellar bodies and released into the alveolar lining, where it lowers surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse at end-expiration. Production begins in the canalicular stage and increases greatly in the last weeks of pregnancy; glucocorticoids accelerate its maturation. The lungs are not functionally mature until there is enough surfactant and enough surface area, which is why prematurity is the main risk factor for respiratory distress syndrome. The lower respiratory tract is fluid-filled in the fetus; the fluid derives from the pulmonary epithelium and amniotic fluid is a small component. Fetal breathing movements are important for the growth of the respiratory muscles and lungs. At birth the first breath must overcome the surface tension of the fluid-filled airways; with the following breaths, the fluid is absorbed, the pulmonary vascular resistance falls, the pulmonary circulation increases and the ductus arteriosus closes functionally within the first days. 5. Anatomical basis of congenital malformations Tracheo-oesophageal fistula with oesophageal atresia: failure of the tracheo-oesophageal septum to close or an abnormal separation of the two tubes; the commonest pattern is a blind upper oesophageal pouch with a fistula between the distal oesophagus and the trachea. The newborn presents with choking, cyanosis on feeding and an air-filled stomach. Part of the VACTERL association. Laryngeal web or atresia: incomplete recanalisation; presents with stridor or aphonia. Bronchial atresia or stenosis: abnormal branching, with distal overinflation or bronchiectasis. Pulmonary hypoplasia: reduced airway branching and small lung volume (e.g. in congenital diaphragmatic hernia, oligohydramnios, bilateral renal agenesis); it is the degree of hypoplasia that determines survival in diaphragmatic hernia. Respiratory distress syndrome: deficiency of surfactant in preterm neonates; hyaline membranes, atelectasis and ground-glass lung fields on radiography; treated by antenatal steroids and exogenous surfactant. Congenital pulmonary airway malformation and pulmonary sequestration: abnormal overgrowth or an isolated mass of lung tissue with an anomalous systemic arterial supply, often from the aorta. Must-memorise Respiratory diverticulum appears in the 4th week from the ventral foregut ; endoderm gives epithelium and glands, splanchnic mesoderm gives cartilage, muscle, connective tissue and vessels. Tracheo-oesophageal septum separates the respiratory tube from the oesophagus; its failure gives tracheo-oesophageal fistula and oesophageal atresia. Laryngeal cartilages: thyroid from the 4th arch; cricoid, arytenoid and corniculate from the 6th; epiglottis from the hypopharyngeal eminence. Cricothyroid = 4th arch = superior laryngeal nerve; the rest = 6th arch = recurrent laryngeal nerve. Stages and weeks: embryonic 4–7; pseudoglandular 5–17; canalicular 16–25; saccular 24–term; alveolar from about 36 weeks to about 8 years. Surfactant from type II pneumocytes; the fetus becomes viable at the end of the canalicular stage and surfactant matures in the last weeks. Clinical / radiological correlation Oesophageal atresia with fistula: the prenatal ultrasound may show a small stomach bubble, and polyhydramnios is common; after birth a nasogastric tube coils in the upper pouch on the chest radiograph. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek): abdominal contents in the thorax compress both lungs and cause pulmonary hypoplasia; the heart may be displaced. Preterm lung on a radiograph: low volume, fine reticulogranular (ground-glass) opacity and air bronchograms, with hyaline membranes on histology. Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn: failure of the normal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance; the ductus arteriosus may remain patent and blood is shunted away from the lungs. Laryngeal atresia with the fetal lungs full of fluid: on prenatal ultrasound the lungs are large and echogenic and the diaphragm may be inverted. Likely follow-up questions From what germ layers does the respiratory system develop and what does each give? — Endoderm: epithelium and glands; splanchnic mesoderm: cartilage, muscle, connective tissue and blood vessels. How does the larynx separate from the pharynx? — The laryngotracheal groove deepens, its lips fuse, and the tracheo-oesophageal septum divides the tube from the foregut. Why does a baby born at 22 weeks not survive? — The canalicular stage is incomplete: not enough respiratory bronchioles, too thick a barrier and virtually no surfactant. Which structure in the lung needs the longest to mature? — The alveolar region; alveolar multiplication continues to about 8 years of age. What is the embryological explanation of the innervation of the cricothyroid muscle? — It derives from the 4th pharyngeal arch mesoderm, supplied by the superior laryngeal nerve; the remaining laryngeal muscles are 6th-arch and are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve."},{"id":"t-III-20","p":"III","n":20,"t":"The gross anatomy and histology of the lungs, the bronchopulmonary segments and pleura. The innervation of the pleura.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer Each lung has the shape of a half-cone with a base on the diaphragm, an apex above rib I, a costal and a mediastinal surface and three borders; the right lung has three lobes and an oblique and a horizontal fissure, the left lung two lobes, an oblique fissure and a lingula . Each lung is divided into ten bronchopulmonary segments (some fused on the left), the smallest functionally independent and resectable unit, each supplied by its own segmental bronchus. The lung and the inner surface of the thoracic wall are covered by pleura — visceral and parietal (costal, diaphragmatic, mediastinal, cervical) — with the costomediastinal and costodiaphragmatic recesses in between. The parietal pleura is somatically innervated : costal and cervical parts and the periphery of the diaphragmatic part by intercostal nerves , the mediastinal part and the centre of the diaphragmatic part by the phrenic nerve ; the visceral pleura is autonomic and insensitive. 1. Form, surfaces and borders The lungs lie on either side of the mediastinum, within the pleural cavities. Each is a half-cone with a base ( basis pulmonis ) resting on the diaphragm, an apex ( apex pulmonis ) projecting above rib I into the root of the neck, a costal surface against the ribs and intercostal spaces, and a mediastinal surface against the mediastinum anteriorly and the vertebral column posteriorly. The mediastinal surface bears the hilum , a comma-shaped area where the root enters. There are three borders: the inferior , which is sharp and separates the base from the costal surface; the anterior , sharp and overlapping the pericardium (with the cardiac notch on the left); and the posterior , smooth and rounded, fitting against the vertebral column. The right lung is a little larger than the left because the heart bulges to the left. 2. Lobes and fissures Table 1 — Lobes and fissures of the lungs Right lung Left lung Lobes Three: superior , middle , inferior Two: superior (with the lingula ) and inferior Fissures Oblique (between superior and middle above, inferior below) and horizontal (between superior and middle) Oblique only Approximate surface marking of the oblique fissure A curved line from about the level of the T IV spinous process, crossing the fifth intercostal space laterally and following rib VI anteriorly A similar but slightly more oblique line, again from about the T III/T IV level to rib VI anteriorly Horizontal fissure Follows the fourth intercostal space from the sternum until it meets the oblique fissure at about rib V Absent Mediastinal surface impressions Heart, IVC, SVC, azygos vein, oesophagus Heart (deep cardiac impression), aortic arch, thoracic aorta, oesophagus The fissures are invaginations of visceral pleura, so the lobes are almost completely separated and freely movable against each other. They determine where each lobe is heard with the stethoscope and how a lobe is resected. 3. The root and the hilum The root of the lung ( radix pulmonis ) is a short tubular collection of structures joining the lung to the mediastinum, covered by a sleeve of mediastinal pleura that reflects on the lung as visceral pleura; the area enclosed by this reflection is the hilum . The root contains a pulmonary artery , two pulmonary veins , a main bronchus , bronchial vessels, nerves and lymphatics. A thin fold of pleura, the pulmonary ligament , hangs from the root below the hilum and stabilises the inferior lobe. Generally the pulmonary artery is superior , the pulmonary veins inferior and the bronchus posterior and central . On the right the superior lobar bronchus branches within the root and lies above the pulmonary artery (eparterial), whereas on the left the artery lies above the bronchus. The classical mnemonic for the arrangement of the main structures is RALS : R ight — pulmonary A rtery anterior to the bronchus; L eft — pulmonary A rtery S uperior to the bronchus. The phrenic nerve passes anterior to the root and the vagus nerve posterior to it — a fact that explains which nerve is injured in different mediastinal operations. 4. Bronchopulmonary segments A bronchopulmonary segment is the area of lung supplied by a segmental bronchus with its accompanying pulmonary artery branch; the pulmonary veins run intersegmentally, between segments. Each segment is shaped like an irregular cone with its apex at the origin of the segmental bronchus and its base at the lung surface. A segment is the smallest functionally independent region of the lung and the smallest area that can be removed without damaging adjacent tissue. There are ten in each lung , but some fuse on the left. Table 2 — Bronchopulmonary segments (S I–S X) No. Right lung Left lung I Apical (superior lobe) Apicoposterior (superior lobe) — the apical and posterior segments are fused II Posterior (superior lobe) III Anterior (superior lobe) Anterior (superior lobe) IV Lateral (middle lobe) Superior lingular (superior lobe) V Medial (middle lobe) Inferior lingular (superior lobe) VI Superior (inferior lobe) Superior (inferior lobe) VII Medial basal (inferior lobe) Medial basal (inferior lobe) — small, often fused with segment VIII VIII Anterior basal (inferior lobe) Anterior basal (inferior lobe) IX Lateral basal (inferior lobe) Lateral basal (inferior lobe) X Posterior basal (inferior lobe) Posterior basal (inferior lobe) All ten segments of the right lung are separate. On the left, S I and S II fuse into the apicoposterior segment and S VII is small and frequently fused with S VIII; the lingular segments (S IV and S V) correspond to the middle lobe of the right lung. The segments are used for segmentectomy (removal of one or more segments), lobectomy and pneumonectomy . 5. The pleura and its recesses The pleura is a serous membrane of flat mesothelial cells on a supporting connective tissue layer. The visceral pleura covers the lung, dips into the fissures and is continuous with the parietal pleura at the hilum. The parietal pleura lines the wall and is named according to the part it covers: Costal part — on the ribs and intercostal spaces. Diaphragmatic part — on the diaphragm. Mediastinal part — on the mediastinum, reflected around the root of the lung. Cervical pleura (pleural cupola) — the dome extending above rib I into the root of the neck, covered by the suprapleural membrane , which is attached to the medial margin of rib I and the transverse process of C VII. Table 3 — Inferior margins of the lung and of the pleural cavity, and the recesses Line Inferior margin of the lung Inferior margin of the pleural cavity Midclavicular line Rib VI Rib VIII Midaxillary line Rib VIII Rib X Par vertebral / posterior Vertebra T X Vertebra T XII The gap between the two margins is the costodiaphragmatic recess , the largest and clinically most important recess; it is deepest after forced expiration and shallowest after forced inspiration, and it is the space into which fluid collects and from which it is aspirated. The costomediastinal recess lies anteriorly, where costal pleura meets mediastinal pleura; the largest is on the left, over the heart. The cervical pleura may extend as much as 3–4 cm above the first costal cartilage but never above the neck of rib I. 6. Innervation of the pleura Part of the pleura Nerve Type of sensation Costal and cervical parietal pleura Intercostal nerves Somatic; pain is felt in the corresponding thoracic dermatomes. Peripheral part of the diaphragmatic parietal pleura Intercostal nerves (lower ones) Somatic; pain referred to the lower thoracic or upper abdominal wall. Mediastinal parietal pleura and the central part of the diaphragmatic pleura Phrenic nerve (C3–C5) Somatic; pain referred to the C3–C5 dermatomes — the side of the neck and the supraclavicular region, the tip of the shoulder. Visceral pleura Autonomic (visceral afferents accompanying the bronchial vessels) Insensitive; local irritation produces no pain. This double innervation is the anatomical basis of two common clinical patterns. Irritation of the central diaphragm or mediastinal pleura (for example by blood after a splenic rupture, or by a subphrenic abscess) gives referred pain at the shoulder tip , while irritation of the costal pleura gives sharp, stabbing pain over the chest wall that is worse on inspiration — pleuritic pain. 7. Histology of the lungs and pleura The pleura is a thin serous membrane: a single layer of flat mesothelial cells on a layer of collagen and elastic fibres with vessels, lymphatics and nerves. The visceral pleura is firmly adherent to the lung and carries a rich lymphatic network; the parietal pleura is thicker and is attached to the endothoracic fascia. Between the two lies only a film of serous fluid, which allows frictionless sliding during respiration and holds the two layers together by surface tension. The lung parenchyma consists of the branching airways with their accompanying vessels and the alveolar tissue. Intrapulmonary bronchi retain cartilage plates and seromucous glands; their smooth muscle forms two helical layers. Bronchioles have no cartilage and no glands but a relatively thick smooth muscle coat and Clara (club) cells. The alveoli are lined by type I and type II pneumocytes, contain alveolar macrophages and lie in a capillary-rich interalveolar septum with elastic fibres. Lymphoid tissue ( BALT ) lies along the airways, and a network of lymphatics follows the bronchi and the pleura, connecting the two systems at the hilum. Must-memorise Right lung: 3 lobes, oblique + horizontal fissures, 10 separate segments. Left lung: 2 lobes, oblique fissure, lingula, 10 segments but S I+II and often S VII+VIII fused. Root contents: one pulmonary artery, two pulmonary veins, one main bronchus, bronchial vessels, nerves and lymphatics. RALS : right artery anterior, left artery superior to the bronchus. Phrenic nerve anterior to the root, vagus posterior to it. Inferior lung margin: rib VI (midclavicular), rib VIII (midaxillary), T X posterior. Pleural margin: rib VIII, rib X, T XII. Parietal pleura: costal and cervical plus peripheral diaphragmatic = intercostal nerves; mediastinal and central diaphragmatic = phrenic nerve (C3–C5). Visceral pleura = autonomic, painless. Costodiaphragmatic recess is deepest after forced expiration — the site of fluid accumulation and aspiration. Clinical / radiological correlation Pneumothorax: air in the pleural cavity separates the two layers, elastic recoil collapses the lung and the surface tension mechanism is lost; a tension pneumothorax pushes the mediastinum to the opposite side and is an emergency. Pleural effusion: fluid collects in the costodiaphragmatic recess, so the first radiological sign is blunting of the costophrenic angle; a large effusion collapses the lung and may need drainage. Thoracocentesis and chest drain: fluid is aspirated in the region of the costodiaphragmatic recess, and a chest drain is inserted in the 'safe triangle' of the axilla (anterior border of latissimus dorsi, lateral border of pectoralis major, below the axillary vessels). The needle is passed over the upper border of the lower rib to avoid the intercostal nerve and vessels, which lie in the costal groove. Pleuritic pain from the costal pleura is sharp and inspiratory; referred shoulder-tip pain from the central diaphragm or mediastinal pleura is due to phrenic (C3–C5) afferents. Surgical resection: the segmental architecture allows segmentectomy, lobectomy or pneumonectomy; the bronchopulmonary segments are also the basis of the radiological localisation of pneumonia and of lung cancer staging. Mesothelioma: malignant tumour of the pleural mesothelium, strongly associated with asbestos exposure; it produces a pleural effusion and thickens the pleura. Likely follow-up questions How do you define a bronchopulmonary segment? — The area supplied by a segmental bronchus and its pulmonary artery branch, with the pulmonary veins running intersegmentally; the smallest resectable unit of the lung. Which segments form the middle lobe? — S IV (lateral) and S V (medial) on the right; on the left they are the superior and inferior lingular segments. Where would you insert a chest drain and why there? — In the safe triangle of the axilla, over the costodiaphragmatic recess and above the upper border of the lower rib, avoiding the intercostal vessels and nerve. What are the differences between visceral and parietal pleura? — Nerve supply (autonomic vs somatic), blood supply (bronchial vs systemic intercostal vessels), lymphatic drainage and pain sensitivity. Why is the right lung more liable to aspirated material? — The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical. How is the apex of the lung protected? — By the cervical pleura reinforced by the suprapleural membrane attached to rib I and vertebra C VII."},{"id":"t-III-21","p":"III","n":21,"t":"The blood supply, innervation and lymphatic drainage of the lungs.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The lung has two circulations . The pulmonary arteries and veins form the functional system: deoxygenated blood reaches the lung through the right and left pulmonary arteries and oxygenated blood leaves through two pulmonary veins on each side. The bronchial arteries , arising from the thoracic aorta or from a posterior intercostal artery, form the nutritive system for the bronchial walls, glands, large vessels and the visceral pleura; the bronchial veins drain partly into the azygos and hemi-azygos systems and partly into the pulmonary veins. The lung is innervated by the anterior and posterior pulmonary plexuses formed by vagal and sympathetic branches — vagal fibres constrict the bronchioles, sympathetic fibres dilate them. Lymphatics drain from superficial (subpleural) and deep (peribronchial) networks through bronchopulmonary and tracheobronchial to paratracheal nodes, and then by the bronchomediastinal trunks into the thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct. 1. The dual blood supply The clue to the whole topic is that the lung is the only organ receiving blood from two separate arterial systems, with different functions and different pressures. The pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure, high-flow system for gas exchange; the bronchial circulation is a small-volume, systemic-pressure system that keeps the airway walls and supporting tissues alive. The two systems anastomose at the level of the bronchial wall and of the visceral pleura, and this anastomosis becomes clinically important in chronic inflammation, when the bronchial arteries hypertrophy and can bleed profusely. Table 1 — Pulmonary versus bronchial circulation Feature Pulmonary vessels Bronchial vessels Function Gas exchange (functional) Nutrition of lung tissue (nutritive) Artery origin Right ventricle, through the pulmonary trunk Thoracic aorta, or the third right posterior intercostal artery on the right Blood carried Deoxygenated, then oxygenated Oxygenated, at systemic pressure Pressure Low (low-resistance circuit) Systemic, low flow Veins Two pulmonary veins per side into the left atrium Bronchial veins into the azygos / hemi-azygos systems, and some into the pulmonary veins Supplies Alveolar capillary bed Bronchial walls, glands, large vessels, visceral pleura, vasa vasorum and nervorum 2. Arterial supply: the bronchial arteries The bronchial arteries arise proximally from the aorta and pass on the posterior surfaces of the bronchi, dividing with them into the lung. They ramify within the lung and supply the bronchial walls, glands, walls of the large vessels and the visceral pleura. The pattern is not symmetrical: Right bronchial artery is normally a single vessel arising from the third right posterior intercostal artery ; occasionally it arises from the upper left bronchial artery. Two left bronchial arteries typically arise directly from the anterior surface of the thoracic aorta — a superior one at about vertebral level T V and an inferior one below the left main bronchus. The bronchial veins drain the proximal part of the bronchial tree: the right bronchial veins end in the azygos vein , the left bronchial veins in the accessory hemi-azygos vein or in the superior intercostal vein. A substantial fraction of the blood from the peripheral lung, however, drains through the pulmonary veins into the left atrium, so that a small amount of desaturated blood bypasses the alveoli — the physiological right-to-left shunt . 3. Innervation of the lungs The nerves of the lung form the anterior pulmonary plexus and the posterior pulmonary plexus , two interconnected networks lying respectively anterior and posterior to the tracheal bifurcation and to the main bronchi; the anterior plexus is the smaller. Their fibres come from the vagus nerves (parasympathetic and visceral afferent) and from the sympathetic trunks (sympathetic and visceral afferent). Branches of the plexuses follow the bronchi and the pulmonary vessels into the lung as the peribronchial plexus . Fibre type Source Effect Parasympathetic efferent Vagus nerve [X] Constriction of the bronchioles , increased glandular secretion, perhaps vasodilatation; also slows the heart through cardiac branches. Sympathetic efferent Upper thoracic sympathetic ganglia, T1–T2 Dilatation of the bronchioles , inhibition of glandular secretion, vasoconstriction; also accelerates the heart. Visceral afferents — vagal Vagus nerve (cell bodies in the inferior vagal ganglion) Reflex control: stretch reception, the cough reflex and the pulmonary chemoreflex; these fibres do not transmit pain. Visceral afferents — sympathetic Marching with sympathetic efferents to the upper thoracic segments Convey the sensation of pain from the lung and pleura, which is referred to the corresponding thoracic dermatomes. Some of the parasympathetic discharges of the lung are protective: the Hering–Breuer type of vagal reflex controls the depth of breathing, and bronchoconstriction together with mucus secretion is part of the reaction to inhaled irritants. 4. Lymphatic drainage The lung contains two communicating lymphatic networks: a superficial (subpleural) plexus draining the visceral pleura and the peripheral parenchyma, and a deep (peribronchial) plexus draining the bronchi and the lung substance. Both flow toward the hilum. Table 2 — Lymph nodes of the lung and mediastinum, in order of flow Group Position and note Intrapulmonary (pulmonary) nodes Within the lung substance, along the segmental and lobar bronchi; small and often not identified separately. Bronchopulmonary nodes (hilar nodes) At the hilum, around the roots of the lobar and main bronchi. Tracheobronchial nodes (superior and inferior) Around the tracheal bifurcation and the main bronchi; the superior group lies above the bronchi, the inferior group below the main bronchi. Paratracheal nodes Along the sides of the trachea, extending upwards into the superior mediastinum. Bronchomediastinal trunks Formed by efferents of the paratracheal, parasternal and brachiocephalic nodes; they end in the thoracic duct on the left or the right lymphatic duct / deep veins at the base of the neck on the right. In practice, tumour cells from a lung primary follow this chain and produce the hilar and mediastinal nodal enlargement that determines staging; a supraclavicular or even a left-sided node may enlarge from a right-sided tumour because the draining trunks cross the midline in the mediastinum. Must-memorise Two circulations: pulmonary (functional, low pressure) and bronchial (nutritive, systemic pressure). Right bronchial artery: single, from the third right posterior intercostal artery. Left bronchial arteries: two, from the thoracic aorta. Bronchial veins: right into the azygos, left into the accessory hemi-azygos or superior intercostal vein; some blood drains into the pulmonary veins (right-to-left shunt). Innervation: anterior and posterior pulmonary plexuses; vagus constricts bronchioles, sympathetic dilates. Lymph flow: superficial and deep plexuses → bronchopulmonary → tracheobronchial → paratracheal → bronchomediastinal trunks → thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct. Lung pain travels with sympathetic afferents; vagal afferents are reflex, not pain. Clinical / radiological correlation Haemoptysis comes from the bronchial arteries, which are at systemic pressure and hypertrophy in chronic inflammation (bronchiectasis, tuberculosis, fungal cavities). In massive haemoptysis, bronchial artery embolisation is the treatment of choice. Pulmonary embolism: thromboemboli carried by the pulmonary arteries obstruct the low-pressure circuit, causing sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic pain and hypoxaemia; a large saddle embolus at the bifurcation may be fatal. Lung cancer staging: spread to bronchopulmonary, tracheobronchial and paratracheal nodes determines operability; CT and endobronchial ultrasound-guided biopsy (EBUS) are used to sample these nodes. Bronchial asthma: the therapeutic effects of β-agonists and anticholinergics follow directly from the autonomic supply described above. Pleural disease: because the visceral pleura is insensitive, pain in pleurisy arises from the parietal pleura, which is somatically innervated. Likely follow-up questions Where do the bronchial arteries arise? — Two left arteries from the thoracic aorta; a single right artery usually from the third right posterior intercostal artery. What is a physiological right-to-left shunt in the lung? — Drainage of some bronchial venous blood into the pulmonary veins, so that a small volume of desaturated blood reaches the left atrium. Which nerve constricts the bronchioles and which dilates them? — Vagus constricts; sympathetic dilates. Trace the lymph from the right lower lobe to the venous system. — Bronchopulmonary → tracheobronchial → paratracheal → right bronchomediastinal trunk → right lymphatic duct (or directly into a deep vein). Why is the visceral pleura insensitive to pain? — It is supplied only by autonomic visceral afferents, which do not carry pain."},{"id":"t-III-22","p":"III","n":22,"t":"The anatomy of the pulmonary circulation, topography and branches of the pulmonary trunk.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The pulmonary trunk ( truncus pulmonalis ) arises from the conus arteriosus of the right ventricle at the pulmonary valve, inside the pericardial sac, and ascends, lying first anterior and then to the left of the ascending aorta, with which it shares a common sheath of serous pericardium. It divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries at about the level of the T V–T VI intervertebral disc, close to the left border of the sternum at the level of the third left costal cartilage and just below the bifurcation of the trachea; the ligamentum arteriosum connects the bifurcation to the aortic arch. The right pulmonary artery is longer and passes behind the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava and in front of the right main bronchus; the left is shorter, passes in front of the descending aorta and behind the superior pulmonary vein. Each side has two pulmonary veins draining into the left atrium. This is a low-pressure, low-resistance, high-flow circuit, and its obstruction by embolism or disease is the basis of pulmonary hypertension. 1. Functional anatomy of the pulmonary circulation The pulmonary circulation carries the whole cardiac output from the right ventricle to the alveolar capillaries and returns it to the left atrium. It is a low-pressure, low-resistance system: the walls of the pulmonary arteries are thin, the capillary bed is enormous, and the flow is distributed so that the alveoli are evenly perfused. The functional consequences are that the right ventricle is a thin-walled pump for a low pressure, that the circuit is easily obstructed, and that it immediately receives everything injected into the systemic venous circulation. The circuit runs as follows. right ventricle → pulmonary valve → pulmonary trunk → right and left pulmonary arteries → lobar and segmental arteries → alveolar capillaries → venules → two pulmonary veins on each side → left atrium . 2. The pulmonary trunk: origin, course and relations The pulmonary trunk begins at the pulmonary valve , at the orifice of the conus arteriosus of the right ventricle slightly anterior to the aortic orifice, and is enclosed within the pericardial sac as the most anterior of the great vessels at its origin. It ascends for a few centimetres, moving posteriorly and to the left ; it lies initially anterior and then to the left of the ascending aorta, and both are surrounded by the same visceral layer of serous pericardium. Its normal calibre is about that of the aorta at its origin but its wall is distinctly thinner. Its important relations are: Right and posterior: the ascending aorta. Posterior (above): the left atrium and the left auricle, with the left coronary artery and the circumflex branch passing between the trunk and the auricle. Anterior: the sternum, the left pleura and lung and, at its origin, the right ventricular outflow tract. Left: the left auricle and, beyond the pericardium, the left phrenic nerve which descends anterior to the root of the left lung. Bifurcation: connected to the concavity of the aortic arch by the ligamentum arteriosum , the remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch lateral to the ligament, and the region between the pulmonary artery and the aorta is the aortopulmonary window where enlarged nodes may compress that nerve. The bifurcation occurs slightly inferior to the tracheal bifurcation and to its left, at about the level of the T V–T VI intervertebral disc ; on a chest radiograph the left border of the pulmonary trunk forms a small convexity below the aortic knuckle (the pulmonary bay), and the main pulmonary artery is seen on CT leaving the right ventricle anterior to the ascending aorta. 3. The right and left pulmonary arteries Table 1 — Comparison of the right and left pulmonary arteries Right pulmonary artery Left pulmonary artery Length Longer; runs almost horizontally across the mediastinum Shorter; it is almost immediately in the root of the lung Course and relations Passes posterior to the ascending aorta and to the superior vena cava and the upper right pulmonary vein, and anterior to the right main bronchus , slightly below the tracheal bifurcation Passes anterior to the descending aorta and posterior to the superior pulmonary vein , above the left main bronchus; its origin is tethered to the aortic arch by the ligamentum arteriosum Branches in the lung Gives a large branch to the superior lobe; the main vessel continues through the hilum, gives a second (recurrent) branch to the superior lobe, then divides to supply the middle and inferior lobes Branches within the lung to the superior lobe (including the lingula) and to the inferior lobe Note The superior lobar bronchus lies above the artery (eparterial bronchus) The artery lies above the main bronchus 4. The pulmonary veins On each side a superior and an inferior pulmonary vein carry oxygenated blood from the lungs. They begin at the hilum, traverse the root of the lung anterior and inferior to the artery and bronchus, and drain into the left atrium ; the two left veins and the two right veins enter separately, and a rare common trunk may occur. The veins have no valves, receive some bronchial venous blood, and their tributaries run intersegmentally, between bronchopulmonary segments — the anatomical reason why a segmental resection can be performed along the intersegmental planes almost without bleeding. 5. Comparison with the systemic circulation and with the bronchial circulation Feature Pulmonary circulation Systemic circulation Pump Right ventricle (thin wall) Left ventricle (thick wall) Pressure Low — low resistance High — high resistance Function Gas exchange Delivery of oxygen and nutrients Blood carried by arteries Deoxygenated Oxygenated Arterial wall Thin, distensible Thick, muscular Effect of obstruction Pulmonary hypertension, right heart strain Ischaemia of the supplied tissue Additional supply Bronchial arteries (nutritive) — Total pulmonary blood flow equals the cardiac output, so the pulmonary circulation is the whole output of the right heart in series with the systemic circulation. The bronchial circulation, by contrast, receives only a small fraction of the cardiac output but at systemic pressure. Must-memorise Pulmonary trunk: from the right ventricular conus arteriosus, inside the pericardial sac, anterior then to the left of the ascending aorta. Bifurcation: just below and to the left of the tracheal bifurcation, at about the T V–T VI disc, near the left border of the sternum at the third left costal cartilage. Ligamentum arteriosum connects the bifurcation to the aortic arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch lateral to it. Right pulmonary artery: longer, behind the ascending aorta and SVC, in front of the right main bronchus. Left: shorter, in front of the descending aorta, behind the superior pulmonary vein. Two pulmonary veins on each side; no valves; they open into the left atrium. Pulmonary circulation = low pressure, low resistance, high flow; carries deoxygenated blood in its arteries. Clinical / radiological correlation Pulmonary thromboembolism: the commonest acute disease of the pulmonary circulation; emboli from the deep veins of the legs lodge at the bifurcation (saddle embolus) or in the lobar arteries, producing acute dyspnoea, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis and hypoxaemia. CT pulmonary angiography is the diagnostic method of choice. Pulmonary hypertension: chronic elevation of pulmonary arterial pressure causes hypertrophy of the right ventricle and eventually right heart failure; causes include chronic lung disease, left heart disease, recurrent embolism and idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy as a sign of mediastinal disease: a mass in the aortopulmonary window (enlarged nodes, aneurysm) compresses the nerve where it passes under the aortic arch, and the patient presents with hoarseness. Patent ductus arteriosus: failure of the ductus arteriosus to close leaves a communication between the aortic arch and the pulmonary trunk; blood shunts from the aorta to the pulmonary artery, causing a continuous murmur and pulmonary overcirculation. Tetralogy of Fallot: pulmonary stenosis with an overriding aorta, VSD and right ventricular hypertrophy; reduced pulmonary blood flow causes cyanosis. Pulmonary artery catheterisation (Swan–Ganz) uses the right ventricular outflow tract and the pulmonary trunk to measure pressures in the right heart and the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure; the pulmonary artery is also ligated during pneumonectomy. Likely follow-up questions At what level does the pulmonary trunk bifurcate and what is its relation to the tracheal bifurcation? — About the T V–T VI disc, inferior to and to the left of the tracheal bifurcation. What is the ligamentum arteriosum and what is its clinical importance? — The remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus between the left pulmonary artery/pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch; it may remain patent, and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch beside it. Which structure passes in front of the right main bronchus, and which behind it? — The right pulmonary artery passes in front; the oesophagus and vagus pass behind; the azygos arch crosses above. Why do pulmonary arteries have thin walls? — They work at low pressure and low resistance; the thin wall reduces the work of the right ventricle. Why do pulmonary veins not have valves? — Flow into the left atrium is driven by the low downstream pressure and the respiratory pump; the atria act as a suction pump during ventricular systole."},{"id":"t-III-23","p":"III","n":23,"t":"The tributaries of the superior vena cava. The azygos-hemiazygos system. The lymphatic drainage of the thoracic cavity.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The superior vena cava is formed behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage by the union of the two brachiocephalic veins , descends vertically and ends at the lower edge of the right third costal cartilage in the right atrium; its lower half lies inside the pericardial sac and it receives the azygos vein immediately before entering the pericardium. The azygos system consists of the azygos vein on the right and the hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins on the left; they drain the thoracic wall (posterior intercostal veins) and thoracic viscera, and form an important collateral pathway between the superior and inferior venae cavae. The thoracic duct is the principal lymphatic channel of the body: it begins in the abdomen, enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus, ascends between the aorta and the azygos vein, crosses to the left at about T V and ends in the left venous angle; thoracic lymph nodes (parasternal, intercostal, diaphragmatic, posterior mediastinal and tracheobronchial) drain into the bronchomediastinal trunks and hence into the thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct. 1. The superior vena cava The superior vena cava ( vena cava superior ) is a short, vertically oriented valveless trunk. It begins behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage where the right and left brachiocephalic veins unite, and terminates at the lower edge of the right third costal cartilage , where it opens into the right atrium. Its lower half lies within the pericardial sac, and therefore in the middle mediastinum; the upper half belongs to the superior mediastinum. The azygos vein joins it immediately before it enters the pericardial sac, and it also receives pericardial and mediastinal veins. On a chest radiograph it forms the right upper border of the mediastinal shadow. Its relations are: the right pleura and lung laterally, the ascending aorta and the right pulmonary artery medially, the right phrenic nerve and the pericardiophrenic vessels lying on its right side within the fibrous pericardium, and the tracheobronchial nodes medially. 2. The brachiocephalic veins and the tributaries of the superior vena cava Each brachiocephalic vein is formed behind the sternoclavicular joint by the union of the internal jugular and subclavian veins. The left brachiocephalic vein is much longer than the right because it crosses the midline anterior to the branches of the aortic arch; the right is short and vertical. The venous drainage of the superior vena cava territory is best remembered in groups. Table 1 — Tributaries of the superior vena cava and of the brachiocephalic veins Vessel Tributaries / origin Notes Right and left brachiocephalic veins Internal jugular vein; subclavian vein; vertebral vein; internal thoracic vein; thymic veins; pericardiacophrenic veins; mediastinal, oesophageal and bronchial veins; inferior thyroid vein Formed behind the sternoclavicular joints; the left is longer and crosses anterior to the aortic arch branches. Left superior intercostal vein Second and third (sometimes the fourth) left posterior intercostal veins; left bronchial veins; sometimes the left pericardiacophrenic vein Passes over the left side of the aortic arch, lateral to the left vagus and medial to the left phrenic nerve, and ends in the left brachiocephalic vein . Azygos vein Posterior intercostal veins, hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins, oesophageal, mediastinal, pericardial and right bronchial veins The largest tributary of the superior vena cava, entering it at about vertebral level T IV. Pericardial and mediastinal veins Small vessels of the pericardium and the mediastinal connective tissue Direct, small tributaries; may open into the azygos system instead. 3. The azygos and hemi-azygos system The azygos system is a longitudinal venous pathway on each side of the vertebral column that drains the body wall and moves blood upwards to the superior vena cava; it anastomoses freely with the inferior vena cava and with the vertebral venous plexuses, and is therefore an important collateral route when the inferior vena cava is obstructed. There is considerable individual variation in its origin, course and termination. Table 2 — The veins of the azygos system Vein Origin and course Tributaries and termination Azygos vein (v. azygos) Arises at about the level of L I–L II at the junction of the right ascending lumbar and right subcostal veins; enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus or through or behind the right crus; ascends in the posterior mediastinum, usually to the right of the thoracic duct, and arches anteriorly over the root of the right lung at about T IV Right superior intercostal vein (second to fourth intercostal veins); fifth to eleventh right posterior intercostal veins; hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins; oesophageal, mediastinal, pericardial and right bronchial veins. Ends in the superior vena cava just before the latter enters the pericardial sac. Hemi-azygos vein (inferior hemi-azygos) Arises from the junction of the left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins; often connected to the left renal vein ; enters the thorax through the left crus or the aortic hiatus; ascends on the left to about T IX and crosses the vertebral column behind the aorta, oesophagus and thoracic duct Lowest four or five left posterior intercostal veins; oesophageal and mediastinal veins. Ends in the azygos vein . Accessory hemi-azygos vein (superior hemi-azygos) Descends on the left side from the upper posterior mediastinum to about T VIII Fourth to eighth left posterior intercostal veins, sometimes left bronchial veins; communicates above with the left superior intercostal vein. Crosses to the azygos vein , or ends in the hemi-azygos, or both. The system communicates above with the brachiocephalic veins through the superior intercostal veins and below with the inferior vena cava through the ascending lumbar and subcostal veins. It also communicates with the vertebral venous plexus and, through oesophageal veins, with the portal system — the anatomical basis of oesophageal varices in portal hypertension. 4. The thoracic duct and the right lymphatic duct The thoracic duct is the principal lymphatic channel of the body. It begins in the abdomen as a confluence of lymph trunks, sometimes dilated into a cisterna chyli , and extends from about vertebra L II to the root of the neck. It enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus posterior to the aorta, ascends through the posterior mediastinum between the thoracic aorta on the left and the azygos vein on the right , posterior to the oesophagus and the diaphragm and anterior to the vertebral bodies. At about vertebral level T V it moves to the left of the midline, enters the superior mediastinum, passes posterior to the aortic arch and between the oesophagus and the left mediastinal pleura, and ends — after being joined by the left jugular and left subclavian trunks — at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins (the left venous angle). The thoracic duct receives: the abdominal confluence of lymph trunks; descending thoracic lymph trunks draining the lower six or seven intercostal spaces on both sides; upper intercostal trunks draining the upper left intercostal spaces; ducts from the posterior mediastinal and posterior diaphragmatic nodes; and, at its termination, the left bronchomediastinal trunk, the left jugular trunk and the left subclavian trunk. The right lymphatic duct is much smaller and drains the right head and neck, right upper limb and right thorax into the right venous angle. 5. Lymphatic drainage of the thoracic cavity Table 3 — Lymphatic drainage of the thoracic wall and viscera Region First-order nodes Second-order pathway Anterior thoracic wall Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes along the internal thoracic vessels To the bronchomediastinal trunk, then the thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct; some efferents to the brachiocephalic nodes. Posterior thoracic wall Intercostal nodes in the posterior intercostal spaces near the vertebral column Upper intercostal nodes to the bronchomediastinal trunks; upper left intercostal trunks directly to the thoracic duct; lower intercostal nodes to the thoracic duct. Diaphragm and pleura Anterior (superior phrenic) nodes and posterior (inferior phrenic) nodes; pericardiacophrenic nodes Superior phrenic to the bronchomediastinal trunk; inferior phrenic to the lateral aortic and lumbar trunks in the abdomen. Lungs and bronchi Bronchopulmonary → tracheobronchial → paratracheal nodes Bronchomediastinal trunk → thoracic duct (left) or right lymphatic duct (right). Oesophagus Para-oesophageal nodes Upper half drains cranially to deep cervical nodes; lower half to the posterior mediastinal nodes and the celiac nodes of the abdomen. Heart and pericardium Anterior mediastinal and brachiocephalic nodes To the bronchomediastinal trunks; the heart has a crossed drainage pattern, the left chambers draining towards the right venous junction and vice versa. Must-memorise SVC: from the union of the brachiocephalic veins behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage to the right atrium at the lower edge of the right third costal cartilage; lower half in the pericardium. Azygos: arises at L I–L II, enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus, arches over the right lung root at T IV into the SVC. Hemi-azygos crosses to the azygos at about T IX; accessory hemi-azygos crosses at about T VIII. Thoracic duct: L II to the left venous angle; enters through the aortic hiatus, between aorta and azygos, and crosses to the left at about T V. Left superior intercostal vein runs between the left vagus and the left phrenic nerve to the left brachiocephalic vein. Node groups of the thorax: parasternal, intercostal, diaphragmatic, posterior mediastinal, tracheobronchial. Clinical / radiological correlation Superior vena cava obstruction: facial and upper limb swelling, distended neck veins and a collateral venous pattern over the chest; causes include bronchial carcinoma, mediastinal fibrosis, thrombosis of central lines and aortic aneurysm. The azygos system provides the main collateral route, so the level of obstruction can often be deduced from the collateral network. Central venous access: catheters inserted through the subclavian, internal jugular or axillary veins pass through the brachiocephalic vein into the SVC; the tip should lie in the distal SVC or the right atrium. Chylothorax: injury to the thoracic duct (thoracic surgery, oesophageal or aortic operations, trauma, malignancy) produces a milky pleural effusion with loss of lymphocytes and protein; ligation or embolisation of the duct may be required. Azygos continuation of the inferior vena cava: a congenital anomaly in which the prerenal IVC continues as the azygos vein; it is important to recognise before cardiac surgery or catheterisation. Portal hypertension: oesophageal veins draining into the azygos system dilate and may bleed, because the azygos is the collateral route between the portal and systemic circulations. Supraclavicular (Virchow) node: enlargement of the left supraclavicular node may be the first sign of an abdominal malignancy, because it lies at the termination of the thoracic duct. Likely follow-up questions Which veins unite to form the superior vena cava and where? — The right and left brachiocephalic veins, behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage. What is the importance of the azygos system? — It drains the thoracic wall and viscera and provides a collateral pathway between the superior and inferior venae cavae. At what level does the thoracic duct cross the midline and why does this matter? — At about T V; the change of side explains why left-sided thoracic operations risk the duct and why a left chylothorax may follow oesophageal surgery. Where does the left superior intercostal vein run? — Over the left side of the aortic arch, lateral to the left vagus and medial to the left phrenic nerve. Where would you expect enlarged nodes in a patient with a right lower lobe carcinoma? — Hilar (bronchopulmonary), tracheobronchial and paratracheal nodes, then the bronchomediastinal trunk."},{"id":"t-III-24","p":"III","n":24,"t":"The topography of the vagus nerve in the thoracic cavity. The anatomy and function of the phrenic nerve.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The vagus nerve [X] enters the thorax through the superior thoracic aperture and descends in the superior and posterior mediastinum on each side. The right vagus crosses the right subclavian artery (giving the right recurrent laryngeal nerve) and then the right lateral surface of the trachea and the posterior aspect of the root of the right lung; the left vagus crosses the left side of the aortic arch (giving the left recurrent laryngeal nerve lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum) and the posterior aspect of the root of the left lung. Both then form the oesophageal plexus , which condenses into an anterior vagal trunk (mainly left) and a posterior vagal trunk (mainly right) that pass through the oesophageal hiatus into the abdomen. The vagus supplies parasympathetic fibres and visceral afferents to the thoracic viscera. The phrenic nerve (C3–C5, mainly C4) descends on the anterior scalene muscle, enters the thorax between the subclavian vein and artery, passes anterior to the root of the lung on the side of the pericardium, and is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm ; it also carries sensation from the pericardium and the mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura. 1. Origin and cervical course of the vagus The vagus nerve leaves the medulla through the jugular foramen together with the glossopharyngeal and accessory nerves, and descends in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and the internal carotid (then common carotid) artery, posterolateral to them. It gives the auricular branch, the pharyngeal branches, the superior laryngeal nerve and the cardiac branches in the neck. It then passes through the superior thoracic aperture into the superior mediastinum, where its course differs on the two sides. 2. Topography of the right and left vagus nerves in the thorax Table 1 — Course of the vagus nerve in the thorax Right vagus nerve Left vagus nerve Entry and first relation Enters the superior mediastinum between the right brachiocephalic vein and the brachiocephalic trunk; crosses the right subclavian artery , around which the right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks in the root of the neck Enters posterior to the left brachiocephalic vein and between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, lying deep to the mediastinal pleura Course in the superior mediastinum Descends posteriorly toward the trachea, crossing its lateral surface, then passes posterior to the root of the right lung Crosses the left side of the aortic arch , where it gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve at the inferior margin of the arch just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum; the nerve then passes posterior to the root of the left lung Relation to the oesophagus Approaches the oesophagus and is crossed just before it by the arch of the azygos vein Reaches the oesophagus in the posterior mediastinum Termination Forms the oesophageal plexus; its fibres contribute mainly to the posterior vagal trunk Forms the oesophageal plexus; its fibres contribute mainly to the anterior vagal trunk The oesophageal plexus lies on the surface of the oesophagus and contains mixed fibres of both nerves. Just above the diaphragm its fibres condense into the anterior vagal trunk (mainly from the left vagus) and the posterior vagal trunk (mainly from the right vagus), both of which continue through the oesophageal hiatus into the abdomen to supply the stomach, liver, coeliac plexus and intestine as far as the distal transverse colon. 3. Branches and functions of the vagus in the thorax Branch Destination Function Recurrent laryngeal nerve Right: hooks under the right subclavian artery; left: hooks under the aortic arch lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum. Both ascend in the tracheo-oesophageal groove to the larynx Motor to all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid; sensory to the larynx below the vocal folds Cardiac branches Cardiac plexus (superficial and deep) with sympathetic cardiac nerves Parasympathetic: slowing of the heart rate, reduced atrial contractility; visceral afferents of the cardiac reflexes Pulmonary branches Anterior and posterior pulmonary plexuses Bronchoconstriction, glandular secretion, reflex afferents Oesophageal branches and oesophageal plexus Wall of the oesophagus Motor to the striated muscle of the upper oesophagus (branchial efferents) and parasympathetic supply to its smooth muscle; afferents Pericardial branches Pericardium Sensory (visceral afferent) fibres In summary, the vagus is the parasympathetic nerve of the thoracic viscera : it slows the heart, constricts the bronchi, increases glandular secretion and carries the visceral afferents that mediate reflex control of the heart, lungs and oesophagus. Its recurrent laryngeal branch is branchial motor to the muscles of the larynx and its superior laryngeal branch supplies the cricothyroid. 4. The phrenic nerve: origin and course The phrenic nerve is a somatic nerve formed from the anterior rami of C3, C4 and C5 , mainly C4 . It forms on the surface of the anterior scalene muscle in the neck, descends within the prevertebral fascia deep to the sternocleidomastoid, and enters the thorax through the superior thoracic aperture between the subclavian vein and the subclavian artery . From there it descends anterior to the root of the lung on each side, accompanied by the pericardiacophrenic vessels , and runs on the side of the fibrous pericardium to reach the diaphragm. Right phrenic nerve: lies lateral to the right vagus and lateral and slightly posterior to the beginning of the right brachiocephalic vein; descends along the right side of the SVC, enters the middle mediastinum, passes along the right side of the pericardial sac within the fibrous pericardium and anterior to the root of the right lung ; it leaves the thorax by passing through the diaphragm with the inferior vena cava at the caval opening (T VIII). Left phrenic nerve: lies lateral to the left vagus and lateral and slightly posterior to the left brachiocephalic vein; descends across the left lateral surface of the aortic arch , passing superficially to the left vagus and the left superior intercostal vein; then follows the left side of the pericardium, anterior to the root of the left lung , and pierces the diaphragm near the apex of the heart. The key differential is therefore simple: the phrenic nerve is anterior to the lung root, the vagus is posterior to it. 5. Function of the phrenic nerve and its clinical anatomy The phrenic nerve provides the entire motor innervation of the diaphragm , including the crura, and supplies somatic sensory fibres to the mediastinal pleura, the fibrous and parietal serous pericardium, the central part of the diaphragmatic pleura and the peritoneum on the central part of the diaphragm. The peripheral part of the diaphragm receives additional sensory fibres from the lower intercostal nerves . This double sensory supply explains the two patterns of referred pain: central diaphragmatic irritation produces shoulder-tip pain (C3–C5 dermatomes), while peripheral irritation produces pain over the lower thorax or upper abdomen. Feature Vagus nerve Phrenic nerve Root values Cranial nerve X (nuclei in the medulla) C3–C5 (mainly C4) Relation to the lung root Posterior to the root Anterior to the root Nature Mixed: parasympathetic, branchial motor and visceral afferent Somatic motor and somatic sensory Main thoracic targets Heart, lungs, oesophagus, larynx Diaphragm, pericardium, mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura Effect of lesion Hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal), arrhythmia, loss of cough reflex Paralysis of the hemidiaphragm, which rises and moves paradoxically Must-memorise Vagus: jugular foramen, carotid sheath, then superior and posterior mediastinum; right hooks the right subclavian artery, left hooks the aortic arch. Right vagus is crossed by the arch of the azygos vein just before it reaches the oesophagus. Oesophageal plexus → anterior vagal trunk (mainly left) and posterior vagal trunk (mainly right). Phrenic nerve: C3–C5, mainly C4; formed on the anterior scalene; enters the thorax between subclavian vein and artery. Phrenic is anterior , vagus is posterior to the root of the lung. Right phrenic passes through the caval opening with the IVC; left pierces the diaphragm near the apex of the heart. Phrenic = sole motor supply of the diaphragm; sensory to the pericardium, mediastinal pleura and central diaphragm. Clinical / radiological correlation Phrenic nerve palsy: the ipsilateral hemidiaphragm is elevated on the chest radiograph and moves paradoxically on fluoroscopy; unilateral palsy is often asymptomatic, bilateral palsy causes severe respiratory distress. Causes include lung cancer infiltration, operative trauma (cardiac surgery, mediastinal dissection), trauma and neuropathy. Surgical plication may be needed. Phrenic nerve in cardiac surgery: the nerve runs on the fibrous pericardium and may be damaged by cold cardioplegia or by pericardial traction; postoperative diaphragmatic weakness delays weaning from ventilation. Hiccup is a reflex contraction of the diaphragm triggered by irritation of the phrenic or vagal afferents (gastric distension, pericarditis, subdiaphragmatic abscess). Referred pain: irritation of the central diaphragm, gallbladder or subphrenic abscess may be felt at the shoulder tip because the phrenic nerve is a C3–C5 nerve. Vagal injury: recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy (hoarseness) from mediastinal tumour, aortic aneurysm or thyroid surgery; bilateral vagotomy abolishes the cough reflex and slows the heart. Aortopulmonary window mass between the pulmonary artery and the aorta compresses the left recurrent laryngeal nerve and produces hoarseness — a valuable clinical sign of mediastinal disease. Likely follow-up questions What is the relation of the vagus and phrenic nerves to the root of the lung? — Vagus posterior, phrenic anterior. Where does the left recurrent laryngeal nerve arise and what is its relation? — From the left vagus at the inferior margin of the aortic arch, lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum; it hooks under the arch. What are the root values of the phrenic nerve? — C3, C4, C5, mainly C4 (the 'C3-4-5 keeps the diaphragm alive' mnemonic). How does the right phrenic nerve leave the thorax? — Through the caval opening with the inferior vena cava at T VIII. Why is a subphrenic abscess felt at the shoulder? — Irritation of the central diaphragmatic peritoneum and pleura is carried by the phrenic nerve to the C3–C5 segments, whose dermatomes include the shoulder region. What are the anterior and posterior vagal trunks derived from? — Mainly from the left and right vagus nerves respectively."},{"id":"t-III-25","p":"III","n":25,"t":"Organization of the thoracic part of the sympathetic chain.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The sympathetic trunk ( truncus sympathicus ) is a paired paravertebral chain of ganglia running from the skull base to the coccyx, and its thoracic part lies in the posterior mediastinum , under the parietal pleura. In the thorax it consists of about 11 to 12 ganglia , numbered with the thoracic spinal nerves, connected to those nerves by white rami communicantes (preganglionic, from T1 to L2) and grey rami communicantes (postganglionic). Each ganglion gives medial branches : the upper five give postganglionic cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves to the cardiac and pulmonary plexuses, and the lower seven give the preganglionic greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves , which pass through the crura of the diaphragm to the coeliac, aorticorenal and renal plexuses. It is continuous above with the cervical and below with the lumbar trunk, and it carries both sympathetic efferents and visceral afferents, including the pain fibres of the heart. 1. General organisation and position The sympathetic trunks are two parallel cords of ganglionated nerve tissue, one on each side of the vertebral column, extending from the base of the skull to the coccyx. In the neck they lie behind the carotid sheath; in the thorax they are the most lateral of the posterior mediastinal structures, lying on the heads of the ribs in the upper part of the thorax and becoming progressively more medial below, until they rest on the lateral aspect of the vertebral bodies. In the superior part of the posterior mediastinum they lie anterior to the necks of the ribs ; throughout their course they are covered by the parietal pleura . They leave the thorax by passing behind the diaphragm under the medial arcuate ligament or through the crura of the diaphragm, and continue as the lumbar sympathetic trunks. The thoracic part contains 11 or 12 ganglia — 11 or 12 because the first thoracic ganglion may be fused with the inferior cervical ganglion to form the stellate (cervicothoracic) ganglion , and the lowest thoracic ganglion may be fused with the first lumbar. The ganglia are numbered with the thoracic spinal nerve with which they are associated (T1 to T12). 2. Rami communicantes: white and grey Each thoracic sympathetic ganglion is connected to the corresponding anterior ramus of the thoracic spinal nerve by two small roots whose difference is a classic examination question. Table 1 — White and grey rami communicantes White ramus communicans Grey ramus communicans Fibre type Myelinated preganglionic sympathetic fibres (and visceral afferents) Unmyelinated postganglionic sympathetic fibres Presence Only between T1 and L2 , the segments with sympathetic outflow; present in the thorax at every thoracic level T1–T12 At every spinal nerve level, including those without a white ramus (below L2 and above T1) Direction of flow From the spinal nerve to the ganglion From the ganglion to the spinal nerve Destination Ganglion — relay (synapse) or passage upwards or downwards in the trunk Blood vessels, sweat glands and arrector pili of the skin (vasomotor, sudomotor, pilomotor) Note Carry the visceral afferents too, so pain from the heart and thoracic viscera enters the cord at these levels Do not carry afferents Preganglionic fibres enter the trunk through the white rami and may (a) synapse in the ganglion of the same segment, (b) ascend in the trunk to synapse in a higher cervical ganglion, or (c) descend to a lower thoracic or lumbar ganglion. Postganglionic fibres leave through the grey rami to the somatic distribution, or through the medial branches to the viscera. 3. Branches of the thoracic sympathetic ganglia The medial branches are of two functionally different types. Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves — small postganglionic branches from the upper five thoracic ganglia ; they join the cardiac plexus and the pulmonary plexuses and supply the heart, lungs and thoracic vessels. They contain visceral afferents as well. They are called 'splanchnic' in the sense of viscera, but they are postganglionic . Thoracic splanchnic nerves — the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves , formed by branches of the lower seven ganglia. These are large and are composed mainly of preganglionic fibres, together with visceral afferents; they pass through the crura of the diaphragm to relay in the abdominal prevertebral ganglia. The name is a warning against assuming that every splanchnic nerve is postganglionic. Table 2 — The three thoracic splanchnic nerves Nerve Ganglia of origin Course and destination Greater splanchnic nerve Usually the fifth to ninth or tenth thoracic ganglia Descends across the vertebral bodies, moving medially; passes through the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the coeliac ganglion in the coeliac plexus Lesser splanchnic nerve Usually the ninth and tenth , or tenth and eleventh, thoracic ganglia Descends medially, passes through the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the aorticorenal ganglion Least splanchnic nerve (lowest splanchnic nerve) Usually the twelfth thoracic ganglion Descends, passes through the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the renal plexus The lateral branches are the grey rami communicantes, and small branches also pass to the thoracic aorta and to the oesophagus. The trunk itself has no other lateral branches of importance in the thorax. 4. Functions of the thoracic sympathetic outflow The sympathetic outflow to the trunk and limbs arises from the T1 to L2 segments; the thoracic chain distributes it to three groups of targets: Thoracic viscera: acceleration of the heart, increased contractility and conduction velocity, dilatation of the bronchioles, inhibition of bronchial secretion, and vasoconstriction of the thoracic vessels; the coronary arteries are influenced through the sympathetic nerves. Body wall: vasoconstriction of the skin and muscle vessels (the basis of thermoregulation), sudomotor secretion of sweat glands and pilomotor contraction of the arrector pili; these reach the wall through the grey rami and the somatic nerve branches. Abdominal viscera through the splanchnic nerves: vasoconstriction of the splanchnic bed, inhibition of gastrointestinal motility and secretion, relaxation of the sphincters, glycogenolysis and lipolysis, release of catecholamines from the adrenal medulla, and reduced renal blood flow with renin release. Because the sympathetic outflow is segmentally organised, the effects on the body wall are also segmental: a sympathetic block or a lesion produces a band of dry, warm skin with loss of sweating, and this is used diagnostically in sympathectomy. 5. Visceral afferents and referred pain The thoracic sympathetic trunk also carries visceral afferent fibres, which travel from the thoracic viscera through the sympathetic chain and the white rami communicantes to the cell bodies in the upper thoracic dorsal root ganglia . These are the fibres that conduct pain from the heart, aorta and lungs. Their segmental level explains the segmental pattern of referred cardiac pain: ischaemia of the heart is felt over the anterior chest, the left shoulder and the inner side of the left arm, in the dermatomes of the upper thoracic segments, because the visceral afferents enter the same segments as the somatic afferents of those dermatomes. Pain from the oesophagus and from the thoracic aorta is referred in the same manner. By contrast, the vagal afferents of the thoracic viscera are involved in reflex control and do not transmit pain. Must-memorise Thoracic chain: 11–12 ganglia (T1–T12); T1 may fuse with the inferior cervical ganglion to form the stellate ganglion. Position: anterior to the necks of the ribs above, on the lateral aspect of the vertebral bodies below, covered by the parietal pleura. White rami: T1–L2, preganglionic, myelinated. Grey rami: all levels, postganglionic, unmyelinated. Upper five thoracic ganglia: postganglionic cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves to the cardiac and pulmonary plexuses. Lower seven thoracic ganglia: greater splanchnic (T5–T9/T10 → coeliac ganglion), lesser (T9–T11 → aorticorenal ganglion), least (T12 → renal plexus); all preganglionic, all pass through the crura of the diaphragm. Cardiac pain is referred to T1–T5 dermatomes; the afferents travel with the sympathetic nerves, not the vagus. Clinical / radiological correlation Horner syndrome: interruption of the sympathetic pathway anywhere from the hypothalamus to the T1 level produces ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis and enophthalmos . In the thorax it may be caused by a Pancoast tumour of the lung apex invading the sympathetic chain at T1. Sympathectomy and sympathectomy-like procedures: used for palmar and axillary hyperhidrosis, Raynaud phenomenon, refractory angina (cardiac sympathectomy) and some forms of complex regional pain syndrome; compensatory sweating is the commonest side effect. Stellate ganglion block: interrupts the sympathetic supply of the head, neck and upper limb and is used in complex regional pain syndrome, Raynaud syndrome and hyperhidrosis; the needle is placed at the level of the C6 transverse process. Coeliac plexus (splanchnic) block: used to relieve the pain of upper abdominal malignancy, especially pancreatic cancer, by interrupting the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves before they relay. Referred cardiac pain: myocardial ischaemia produces pain in the chest, left shoulder and left arm because the visceral afferents enter the upper thoracic segments, where they converge with somatic afferents. Autonomic dysreflexia in high spinal cord injury: an unbalanced sympathetic discharge below the lesion causes severe hypertension and headache. Likely follow-up questions Why are there white rami only from T1 to L2? — Because the sympathetic preganglionic neurons are located only in the intermediolateral cell columns of T1–L2; above and below these levels the trunk contains postganglionic fibres distributed through grey rami. Where do the greater splanchnic nerves synapse? — In the coeliac ganglion, having arisen from T5 to T9/T10 and passed through the crus of the diaphragm. What is the difference between the cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves and the thoracic splanchnic nerves? — Cardiopulmonary: small, postganglionic, from the upper five ganglia. Splanchnic: large, preganglionic, from the lower seven ganglia, to the abdominal prevertebral ganglia. Where does the thoracic sympathetic trunk leave the thorax? — Posterior to the diaphragm, under the medial arcuate ligament or through the crura, continuing as the lumbar trunk. Which structures cover the sympathetic trunk in the thorax? — The parietal pleura; this is why pleural disease may irritate the trunk, and why the trunk is encountered when the pleura is stripped. Why is a Pancoast tumour associated with Horner syndrome? — It invades the sympathetic trunk at the T1 level in the apex of the thorax, interrupting the oculosympathetic pathway."},{"id":"t-III-26","p":"III","n":26,"t":"General embryology I: fertilization, implantation, cleavage, gastrulation, development of the neuroectoderm. Differentiation of intraembryonic mesoderm.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer Fertilisation is the fusion of a spermatozoon with a secondary oocyte in the ampulla of the uterine tube ; it restores the diploid chromosome number (46) and determines the sex of the embryo. Cleavage converts the zygote into a morula (day 3) and then into a blastocyst (day 4–5), which implants in the endometrium from about day 6 and is completely embedded by about day 11–12. In the third week gastrulation through the primitive streak turns the bilaminar disc into a trilaminar disc ( ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm ) and creates the notochord , and the induced neuroectoderm folds into the neural tube between about day 18 and the end of the fourth week. The intraembryonic mesoderm then separates into paraxial, intermediate and lateral plate mesoderm — the somites, the urogenital primordia, and the serous membranes, gut wall and body wall. 1. The timetable of the first four weeks The prenatal period runs from fertilisation to birth and is divided into blastogenesis (the first two weeks), the embryonic period (fertilisation to the end of the eighth week, when the primordia of all organ systems are laid down) and the fetal period (from the beginning of the ninth week, when the organs grow and mature). Two clinical rules follow from this division: in blastogenesis the insult is usually all or nothing (miscarriage or an intact embryo), while the embryonic period is the phase most sensitive to teratogens, because the organ primordia are being formed. Table 1 — Day-by-day and week-by-week timetable Stage Timing Key event Fertilisation day 0 Fusion of spermatozoon and secondary oocyte in the ampulla of the uterine tube; the zygote is diploid (46 chromosomes). Cleavage days 1–3 Repeated mitotic divisions without growth of the conceptus; blastomeres become progressively smaller; the zona pellucida stays intact. Morula day 3 Cluster of 8–16 blastomeres; reaches the uterine cavity on day 4. Blastocyst days 4–5 Cavitation creates the blastocoele; outer trophoblast and inner embryoblast (inner cell mass) appear. Hatching (denudation) days 5–6 The zona pellucida is lost, so the trophoblast can contact the uterine mucosa. Implantation days 6–12 Attachment, adhesion and invasion of the endometrium; completed around day 11–12. Bilaminar disc week 2 Epiblast and hypoblast; amniotic cavity dorsally, primary yolk sac ventrally, extraembryonic mesoderm around them. Gastrulation week 3 (about day 14–16) Primitive streak and node; migration of epiblast cells produces ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm; notochord appears. Neurulation days 18–27 Neural plate (about day 18), fusion of the neural folds begins about day 21 at the cervical level, neuropores close by the end of week 4. Mesoderm differentiation weeks 3–4 Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites; intermediate and lateral plate mesoderm separate; intraembryonic coelom forms. 2. Fertilisation Fertilisation ( fertilisatio ) is the fusion of a haploid spermatozoon with a haploid secondary oocyte to form a diploid zygote ( zygota ). It normally takes place in the ampulla of the uterine tube , the dilated continuation of the infundibulum, within about a day of ovulation. Oocyte: a secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase of meiosis II , surrounded by the zona pellucida and the corona radiata of follicular cells. Capacitation: in the female genital tract the spermatozoon must be capacitated by factors of the tubal secretion before its acrosome can react; without capacitation it cannot fertilise the oocyte. Penetration and acrosome reaction: the sperm passes through the corona radiata; the acrosome reaction releases hydrolytic enzymes that digest a path through the zona pellucida, and the sperm membrane fuses with the oolemma. Completion of meiosis: the oocyte completes meiosis II and emits the second polar body ; the male and female pronuclei approach each other and fuse. Block to polyspermy: the cortical reaction immediately changes the zona pellucida (zona reaction), so that no further sperm can enter. Result: the zygote has 46 chromosomes; the sex is determined at fertilisation by the sperm (X or Y), the oocyte always contributing an X. 3. Cleavage, the blastocyst and implantation Cleavage is a series of mitotic divisions in which the total mass of the conceptus does not increase — the daughter cells ( blastomeres ) become smaller at each division. The intact zona pellucida prevents the conceptus from adhering prematurely to the tubal wall. By day 3 there are 8–16 blastomeres forming the morula ( morula ), which reaches the uterine cavity on day 4. Fluid then enters and cavitation produces the blastocyst ( blastocystis ): an outer layer of trophoblast and an eccentric inner cell mass, the embryoblast , with the fluid-filled blastocoele between them. The trophoblast gives the chorion and the fetal part of the placenta and induces the maternal part; the embryoblast gives the embryo, the amnion, the yolk sac and the allantois. Table 2 — The trophoblast and the maternal decidua at the implantation site Layer Morphology Function Cytotrophoblast (Langhans cells) Polyhedral cells with pale, basophilic, glycogen-rich cytoplasm and a large spherical, euchromatic nucleus; mitotically active. Stem cells that regenerate the trophoblast; they are added to the overlying syncytium. Syncytiotrophoblast Multinucleated, mitotically inactive syncytium with small dark nuclei, numerous mitochondria, abundant rough and smooth endoplasmic reticulum and surface microvilli. Invasive layer that erodes the endometrium and opens maternal vessels; transfers gases and nutrients; secretes hCG and most pregnancy hormones. Embryoblast Inner cell mass of pluripotent cells. Gives the three germ layers and the extraembryonic structures derived from the epiblast. Decidua Altered endometrium with decidual cells, enlarged glands and sinusoids. Maternal part of the placenta; histiotrophic nutrition before the placenta takes over. Implantation ( implantatio , nidation) begins on about day 6, when the blastocyst loses the zona pellucida during hatching , and the trophoblast attaches to the uterine epithelium through adhesion molecules (L-selectin, integrins). The syncytiotrophoblast then penetrates the endometrium; the maternal vessels are opened, and the expanding lacunae fill with maternal blood. The tissues become embedded in the vascularised endometrium by about day 11–12. The most common site is the posterior wall of the uterine body (about two-thirds of cases), the anterior wall accounting for about one-third. Nutrition is first histiotrophic (from decidual tissue), then hemotrophic once the utero-placental circulation works. 4. Gastrulation and the notochord At the beginning of the third week the human embryo is a bilaminar disc : the dorsal epiblast (adjacent to the amniotic cavity) and the ventral hypoblast (facing the yolk sac). Gastrulation begins with the appearance, in the caudal midline of the epiblast, of the primitive streak ( linea primitiva ), whose cranial end carries the primitive node . Epiblast cells proliferate, migrate to the streak and ingress: Gastrulation : epiblast → primitive streak and node → endoderm (replaces the hypoblast) + intraembryonic mesoderm (between ectoderm and endoderm) → remaining epiblast = ectoderm. The disc is now trilaminar. Notochord ( notochorda ): a rod of cells growing cranially from the primitive node (notochordal process → notochordal plate → definitive notochord). It is the primary inducer of the neural plate, patterns the paraxial mesoderm into somites, and later becomes the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs. Oropharyngeal and cloacal membranes: small bilaminar areas where ectoderm and endoderm remain in contact without intervening mesoderm; they give the future mouth and the anal membrane. Allantois: a small finger-like diverticulum of the hindgut that grows into the connecting stalk; its intra-embryonic remains become the urachus and its vessels become the umbilical vessels. Table 3 — The three germ layers and their main derivatives Germ layer Main derivatives Ectoderm Surface ectoderm: epidermis and its appendages (hair, nails, sweat and sebaceous glands), lens, inner ear, anterior pituitary, tooth enamel. Neuroectoderm: neural tube and neural crest (see Table 4). Mesoderm Dermis, skeletal, smooth and cardiac muscle, bone, cartilage and connective tissue, blood and blood vessels, heart, kidneys, gonads and genital ducts, spleen, serous membranes, adrenal cortex. Endoderm Epithelium of the digestive tube and its glands (liver, pancreas), respiratory epithelium and glands, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, palatine tonsil, tympanic cavity and auditory tube, urinary bladder and urethra. 5. Neurulation and the development of the neuroectoderm The neuroectoderm is the part of the ectoderm induced by the notochord. Its development is neurulation : first the ectoderm thickens into the neural plate ( lamina neuralis ) on about day 18, then its margins rise into neural folds while the median neural groove deepens. The folds fuse in the midline, beginning on about day 21 at the cervical level, and fusion spreads both cranially and caudally. The two open ends are the rostral (anterior) neuropore , which closes on about day 24, and the caudal (posterior) neuropore , which closes two to three days later; the neural tube is complete by the end of the fourth week. Neurulation : neural plate → neural groove → neural folds → fusion starts at the cervical level → spreads cranially and caudally → rostral neuropore closes → caudal neuropore closes → neural tube. Neural tube ( tubus neuralis ): the cranial part dilates into the three primary brain vesicles ( prosencephalon, mesencephalon, rhombencephalon ) by the end of the fourth week and into five secondary vesicles (telencephalon, diencephalon, mesencephalon, metencephalon, myelencephalon) by the end of the fifth week. The caudal part becomes the spinal cord ; the lumen of the tube persists as the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord. Neural crest ( crista neuralis ): cells set free from the margins of the neural folds as the tube closes. They migrate widely and form all neurons outside the CNS (spinal and autonomic ganglia), the adrenal medulla, Schwann cells, both leptomeninges (pia and arachnoid), melanocytes, Merkel cells, and the ectomesenchyme of the head and neck; the cardiac neural crest forms the aortopulmonary septum. Surface ectoderm : as the tube sinks below the surface, the remaining ectoderm closes over it and becomes the epidermis, together with the lens and the inner ear. Table 4 — Neural tube and neural crest derivatives Structure Derivative Neural tube — cranial part Brain (cerebral hemispheres, diencephalon, midbrain, pons, cerebellum, medulla) and the ventricular system; retina and optic nerve, posterior pituitary and pineal gland arise from the diencephalon. Neural tube — caudal part Spinal cord and its central canal; the anterior horn motor neurons for the axial and limb muscles. Neural crest Spinal ganglia and autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, leptomeninges, melanocytes and Merkel cells, ectomesenchyme of the head and neck, aortopulmonary septum. 6. Differentiation of the intraembryonic mesoderm The intraembryonic mesoderm is at first a sheet on either side of the notochord. It becomes arranged in three longitudinal bands, each with a different fate: Paraxial mesoderm ( mesoderma paraxialis ): thickens beside the neural tube and becomes segmented into somites ( somiti ), which are added craniocaudally from about the end of the third week. Each somite divides into a sclerotome (vertebrae and ribs), a myotome (skeletal muscle) and a dermatome (dermis of the back). The unsegmented paraxial mesoderm of the head and neck forms the muscles attached to the branchial skeleton. Intermediate mesoderm ( mesoderma intermedium ): the urogenital band — nephric primordia (pronephros, mesonephros, metanephros), gonads and genital ducts. Lateral plate mesoderm ( mesoderma laminae lateralis ): splits into a somatic (parietal) layer applied to the ectoderm and a splanchnic (visceral) layer applied to the endoderm, with the intraembryonic coelom between them. The somatic layer+ectoderm build the body wall and the parietal serous membranes; the splanchnic layer+endoderm build the gut wall and the visceral serous membranes; the coelom becomes the pericardial, pleural and peritoneal cavities. Table 5 — The three divisions of the intraembryonic mesoderm Division Immediate derivatives Adult structures Paraxial Somites → sclerotome, myotome, dermatome Vertebrae, ribs, axial skeletal muscle, dermis of the trunk. Intermediate Nephrotomes, gonadal ridge, genital ducts Kidneys and ureters, gonads, uterus, uterine tube and epididymis/ductus deferens. Lateral plate — somatic Parietal layer of the serous membranes; limb mesenchyme Body wall (with ectoderm), parietal pleura, pericardium and peritoneum, limb skeleton and dermis. Lateral plate — splanchnic Visceral layer of the serous membranes Wall of the gut and its smooth muscle and connective tissue, visceral pleura, pericardium and peritoneum, blood cells and vessels. Must-memorise Timeline: fertilisation day 0 → morula day 3 → blastocyst day 4–5 → implantation days 6–12 → gastrulation week 3 → neural plate about day 18 → neural folds fuse about day 21 → neural tube closed by the end of week 4. Fertilisation normally in the ampulla of the uterine tube; the zygote is diploid (46) and the sex is fixed by the sperm. Trophoblast → cytotrophoblast (Langhans cells, mitotic stem cells) + syncytiotrophoblast (invasive, hCG); embryoblast → embryo, amnion, yolk sac, allantois. Gastrulation: primitive streak and node are the only source of the three germ layers; the notochord induces the neural plate and the somites and becomes the nucleus pulposus. Neural tube → CNS (three brain vesicles by week 4, five by week 5; spinal cord); neural crest → PNS neurons, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, leptomeninges, melanocytes, head ectomesenchyme. Mesoderm: paraxial → somites → sclerotome (bone), myotome (muscle), dermatome (dermis); intermediate → urogenital; lateral plate → serous membranes, gut wall, coelom. Clinical / radiological correlation Ectopic pregnancy ( graviditas extrauterina ): implantation outside the uterine cavity, most often in the uterine tube, less often on the ovary, on the peritoneum or in the cervix. It threatens the mother through bleeding and tubal rupture, and must be excluded in any woman of fertile age with sudden acute abdominal pain. Placenta praevia: if the blastocyst implants near the cervical os, the placenta forms in the lower uterine segment and obstructs the birth canal; it is a contraindication to vaginal delivery. Teratogens produce malformations mainly in the embryonic period (weeks 3–8); radiation, infections and drugs acting during blastogenesis often follow the all-or-nothing rule. Neural tube defects: failure of the neuropores to close — anencephaly when the rostral neuropore remains open, spina bifida when the caudal neuropore fails; periconceptional folate reduces the risk. Twinning: dizygotic twins arise from two zygotes and are always dichorionic and diamniotic; monozygotic twins arise from one zygote and the number of amnions and chorions depends on how early the split occurs. Likely follow-up questions Where and when does fertilisation occur, and what does it restore? — Ampulla of the uterine tube, within about 24 hours of ovulation; it restores the diploid chromosome number and determines sex. What is capacitation and what happens without it? — Conditioning of the sperm in the female tract that enables the acrosome reaction; without it the sperm cannot penetrate the zona pellucida. What blocks polyspermy? — The cortical reaction (zona reaction). Difference between cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast? — Mitotic stem cells with glycogen versus a mitotically inactive, invasive, hormone-producing syncytium. What induces the neural plate, and what is the fate of the notochord? — The notochord; it persists as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs. Which germ layer gives the skeleton, which the epidermis, which the gut epithelium? — Mesoderm, ectoderm and endoderm respectively. What is a somite and what are its three parts? — A segment of the paraxial mesoderm dividing into sclerotome, myotome and dermatome."},{"id":"t-III-27","p":"III","n":27,"t":"General embryology II: development of the amnion, yolk sacs, extraembryonic mesoderm. Formation of the placenta.","u":"part-3.html","x":"Core answer The extraembryonic mesoderm splits into a somatic layer (lining the trophoblast and the amnion) and a splanchnic layer (covering the yolk sac), and the space between them becomes the chorionic cavity . The amnion is a thin membrane enclosing the embryo in amniotic fluid, and the yolk sac provides early nutrition, the first blood cells and the primordial germ cells. The chorion — syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast and extraembryonic mesenchyme — forms the chorionic villi, which pass from primary to secondary to tertiary ; over the decidua basalis they become the chorion frondosum and form the fetal part of the placenta , while the decidua basalis forms the maternal part. The term placenta is a disc of 15–25 cm diameter and 400–600 g with 15–30 cotyledons, and it is hemochorionic : maternal blood bathes the villi but never mixes with fetal blood, which is separated from it by the placental barrier . The umbilical cord connects fetus and placenta and contains two umbilical arteries, one umbilical vein and Wharton's jelly. 1. Timeline of the extraembryonic structures Most of the extraembryonic apparatus is already present by the end of the fourth week; only the villi, the cotyledons and the cord then grow and mature. Table 1 — Timetable of the fetal membranes and placenta Timing Event days 7–8 The amniotic cavity appears as a cleft in the embryoblast; amnioblasts form the amniotic epithelium. days 8–9 Trophoblastic lacunae appear in the syncytiotrophoblast and fill with maternal blood. week 2 Primary yolk sac, then the definitive (secondary) yolk sac; extraembryonic mesoderm and the chorionic cavity (about day 13); secondary villi at the end of the week. week 3 From about day 21 the tertiary villi contain vessels, the first fetoplacental circulation starts, and the villi of the chorion frondosum grow into the decidua basalis. week 4 Body folding encloses the embryo in the amnion; the yolk sac and allantois are compressed into the connecting stalk , the future umbilical cord. weeks 6–10 Physiological herniation : a loop of midgut enters the base of the umbilical cord and later returns to the abdomen. week 8 Villi facing the capsular decidua disappear ( chorion laeve ); villi facing the basal decidua enlarge ( chorion frondosum ). 4th month The placenta is fully developed; it is delivered in the third stage of labour. 2. Extraembryonic mesoderm and body folding The extraembryonic mesoderm ( mesoderma extraembryonicum ) is a loose population of cells derived from the epiblast that fills the space between the trophoblast externally and the amnion and yolk sac internally. Cavities appear in it and coalesce into the chorionic cavity (extraembryonic coelom), which is completed on about day 13. The mesoderm is thus split into two layers: Somatic (parietal) extraembryonic mesoderm: applied to the inner surface of the trophoblast and to the amnion; with the trophoblast it forms the chorion , and it later carries the vessels of the chorionic plate. Splanchnic (visceral) extraembryonic mesoderm: covers the yolk sac and, with the endoderm, forms its wall; it is the site where the first blood islands and vessels appear. Body folding follows in the third and fourth weeks. The flat trilaminar disc grows faster than its margins, so cranial, caudal and lateral folds turn it ventrally. The amnion expands with it, the yolk sac is constricted at its stalk into the narrow vitelline duct , and the proximal yolk sac plus the allantois are pushed into the connecting stalk that suspends the embryo in the chorionic cavity. The intraembryonic coelom becomes the body cavities, the amnion fuses with the chorion to form the amniochorion , and the fetal membranes are complete. 3. The amnion, amniotic fluid and the yolk sacs 3.1 Amnion The amnion is the innermost fetal membrane. It arises from the embryoblast on about day 7–8, when a slit appears within the embryoblast (the epiblast): the cells lining the slit become amnioblasts , the cavity comes to lie between the embryoblast and the trophoblast, and the membrane around it is the amnion. Its wall is a simple cuboidal to squamous amniotic epithelium on a thin extraembryonic mesodermal layer. As the amniotic cavity enlarges it encloses the whole embryo, covers the fetal surface of the placenta and forms the outer coat of the umbilical cord. The fluid it contains ( amniotic fluid ) is at first a secretion of the amniotic epithelium and later is mainly fetal urine; it permits free movement and symmetrical growth, prevents adhesion of the embryo to the membranes, cushions it against mechanical injury and maintains a constant temperature. Too little fluid ( oligohydramnios ) or too much ( polyhydramnios ) is an important obstetric warning sign, and amniotic fluid cells are sampled at amniocentesis for prenatal diagnosis. 3.2 Yolk sacs Primary yolk sac ( saccus vitellinus primarius ): the cavity bordered by the hypoblast above and by Heuser's membrane below, in the second week. Secondary (definitive) yolk sac ( saccus vitellinus ): appears when the extraembryonic coelom splits the extraembryonic mesoderm; it is the smaller, definitive sac attached to the midgut by the vitelline duct. Functions: early nutrition (before the placenta works), the site of the first blood islands and of early blood formation, the route by which primordial germ cells reach the gonadal ridges, and the source of the allantois (whose intra-embryonic part becomes the urachus and whose vessels become the umbilical vessels). Fate: the yolk sac shrinks and is incorporated into the umbilical cord as a small remnant. Persistence of the vitelline duct gives a Meckel's diverticulum of the ileum. 4. Chorion, decidua and the chorionic villi The chorion ( chorion ) is the outermost fetal membrane. It has three layers: the outer syncytiotrophoblast , the cytotrophoblast and the extraembryonic mesenchyme with the vessels of the embryo. Its cavity is the chorionic cavity, and its outer surface bears the chorionic villi ( villi chorionici ), which grow into the surrounding decidua. Two parts are distinguished by the number of villi: the chorion frondosum (bushy chorion over the embryo, with long branched villi; the future fetal placenta) and the chorion laeve (smooth chorion, whose villi disappear after the eighth week). Table 2 — The three generations of chorionic villi Villus Appears Structure Function Primary during implantation Cytotrophoblast covered by syncytiotrophoblast; no mesodermal core. Erode the uterine mucosa and anchor the conceptus; early nutrition. Secondary end of week 2 Extraembryonic mesenchyme grows into the primary villus; still no vessels. Nutrition by diffusion between cells. Tertiary from about day 21 Vessels appear in the mesenchymal core (vascularisation), coinciding with the start of the heart tube. True fetoplacental exchange; the beginning of the hemotrophic stage. The uterine mucosa of pregnancy is the decidua ( decidua ): endometrium altered by progesterone and by the implanting embryo, with enlarged glands, sinusoids and decidual cells that store glycogen and lipid. It is named according to its relation to the embryo. Table 3 — The three parts of the decidua Part Position Fate Decidua basalis Between the embryo and the myometrium. Becomes the maternal part of the placenta (decidual plate). Decidua capsularis Over the embryo, between it and the uterine lumen. Thins and fuses with the chorion laeve; contributes to the fetal membranes but not to the placenta. Decidua parietalis The rest of the endometrium, away from the implantation site. Degenerates and is shed after delivery. 5. The placenta: architecture, circulation and barrier The placenta is a temporary organ of mixed origin, part fetal and part maternal. The mature organ is a disc of 15–25 cm diameter, 2–3 cm thickness and 400–600 g weight. Its fetal surface is smooth, covered by amnion, with the fetal vessels converging towards the insertion of the umbilical cord; its maternal surface is rough and granular and is divided into 15–30 cotyledons . Fetal part: the chorionic plate (extraembryonic mesenchyme) covered by amniotic epithelium, from which the tertiary villi grow towards the decidua. The villi are either anchoring villi (fixed to the decidual plate) or free villi (floating in maternal blood). The villous core contains loose connective tissue, capillaries, fibroblasts, myofibroblasts and Hofbauer cells (placental macrophages). Maternal part: the decidual plate , from which placental septa grow towards the fetal part. The septa do not reach the chorionic plate, so they do not completely divide the intervillous space; they mark out the cotyledons. Intervillous space ( spatium intervillosum ): contains about 150 mL of maternal blood, renewed by the spiral arteries at about 500 mL per minute. The maternal circulation is open (blood flows freely around the villi) while the fetal circulation is closed (umbilical arteries → villous capillaries → umbilical vein). Because maternal blood directly contacts the trophoblast, the human placenta is hemochorionic . Functions: exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide, transfer of nutrients, removal of urea, uric acid, creatinine and bilirubin, an endocrine function (hCG and human chorionic somatomammotropin from the syncytiotrophoblast, progesterone and oestrogens from the trophoblast, relaxin from the decidual cells) and a barrier function. The placenta takes over progesterone production from the corpus luteum from the second trimester onwards. Table 4 — Stages of the placental barrier Layer Early pregnancy At term 1. Fetal capillary Continuous endothelium Endothelium, its basal lamina fused with that of the trophoblast 2. Connective tissue of the villus Present, with Hofbauer cells Reduced to a thin layer 3. Cytotrophoblast Continuous layer with its basal lamina Discontinuous or absent in the second half of pregnancy 4. Syncytiotrophoblast Present, with microvilli Present; the definitive layer Number of layers 6 2–3 The barrier lets small molecules pass, uses facilitated diffusion for glucose, active transport for ions and receptor-mediated endocytosis for maternal antibodies (IgG), but holds back most macromolecules and cells. Many drugs, alcohol, some viruses and other teratogens nevertheless cross it, which is why the placental barrier is only a relative protection. 6. The umbilical cord The umbilical cord ( funiculus umbilicalis ) runs from the umbilicus of the fetus through the amniotic cavity to the fetal surface of the placenta. It is covered externally by a simple squamous to cuboidal amniotic epithelium . Its core is Wharton's jelly , a mucous connective tissue rich in hyaluronic acid with fibroblasts and macrophages, which protects the vessels from compression. At term the cord is about 2 cm wide and 50 cm long , roughly matching the length of the fetus. Table 5 — Contents of the umbilical cord Structure Origin and characteristics Fate / function Umbilical arteries (2) Branches of the internal iliac arteries; no elastic membranes in the wall; short transverse anastomoses (Hyrtl) may link them. Carry deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta. Umbilical vein (1) The single, non-paired vein of the cord. Carries oxygenated blood to the fetus; part goes to the liver through the portal vein, part bypasses it through the ductus venosus into the inferior vena cava. Wharton's jelly Mucous connective tissue. Supports and protects the vessels. Remnants (variable) The omphaloenteric duct (yolk-sac remnant) and the allantoic duct . Normally obliterate; persistence of the vitelline duct gives Meckel's diverticulum. Must-memorise Chorionic cavity completed about day 13; amniotic cavity about day 7–8; tertiary villi from about day 21; placenta fully developed by the fourth month. Villi: primary (trophoblast only) → secondary (mesenchymal core) → tertiary (vessels). Anchoring = fixed to the decidual plate; free = floating in the intervillous space. Chorion frondosum + decidua basalis = placenta; chorion laeve = smooth chorion facing the capsular decidua. Placenta: disc 15–25 cm, 2–3 cm thick, 400–600 g, 15–30 cotyledons; intervillous space about 150 mL; hemochorionic. Placental barrier: 6 layers early, 2–3 at term (cytotrophoblast becomes discontinuous; syncytiotrophoblast and capillary endothelium remain). Cord: 2 arteries, 1 vein, Wharton's jelly , amniotic epithelium; about 2 cm wide and 50 cm long. hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast maintains the corpus luteum; the placenta takes over progesterone in the second trimester. Clinical / radiological correlation Placenta praevia: implantation near the internal os; the placenta covers or approaches the birth canal and is a contraindication to vaginal delivery. Placenta accreta, increta, percreta: the villi attach to the myometrium, invade it, or pass through the whole uterine wall; a major cause of postpartum haemorrhage, usually with previous caesarean section. Placental abruption: premature separation of the placenta, with vaginal bleeding and painful uterine contractions. Gestational trophoblastic disease (hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma) arises from trophoblast cells; hCG in blood or urine diagnoses and monitors it. Amniocentesis and cell-free fetal DNA: sampling of amniotic fluid or of free fetal DNA in maternal blood allows prenatal karyotyping, Rh determination and mutation analysis. Cord abnormalities: a single umbilical artery is associated with other anomalies; true knots and cord prolapse compromise the fetal circulation. Likely follow-up questions Which structures form the placenta, and what are its two parts? — Chorion frondosum (fetal part: chorionic plate and tertiary villi) and decidua basalis (maternal part: decidual plate and septa). How do primary, secondary and tertiary villi differ? — By the presence of a mesodermal core (secondary) and then of vessels (tertiary). Why do maternal and fetal blood not mix? — The placental barrier; the placenta is hemochorionic but the two circulations are separate. Name the layers of the placental barrier and say what happens to them at term. — Syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast and its basal lamina, villous connective tissue, capillary endothelium and its basal lamina; at term the cytotrophoblast becomes discontinuous and some basal laminae fuse, leaving 2–3 layers. What does the umbilical cord contain and where do its vessels come from? — Two umbilical arteries (internal iliac) and one umbilical vein, Wharton's jelly and amniotic epithelium. What is the function of the ductus venosus? — It bypasses the hepatic sinusoids, carrying oxygenated umbilical blood to the inferior vena cava. What remains of the yolk sac and allantois in the adult? — Meckel's diverticulum (vitelline duct) and the urachus (median umbilical ligament) respectively."},{"id":"t-IV-1","p":"IV","n":1,"t":"Middle of the body of humerus: muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (two cross-sections).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer Both cross-sections pass through the middle third of the arm, where the humerus and the medial and lateral intermuscular septa divide the limb into an anterior (flexor) compartment — biceps brachii, brachialis and coracobrachialis, supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve — and a posterior (extensor) compartment — triceps brachii, supplied by the radial nerve . The neurovascular bundle (brachial artery and veins, median nerve) lies on the medial side; the radial nerve and profunda brachii artery lie in the radial groove on the posterior surface of the humerus. The two sections differ in one decisive point: at the level of the deltoid tuberosity the deltoid is still present laterally and the ulnar nerve is still in the anterior compartment , while below the deltoid insertion the ulnar nerve has already pierced the medial intermuscular septum into the posterior compartment. 1. The two levels and the bone Level A is taken through the deltoid tuberosity , in the upper part of the middle third of the humerus: the deltoid is inserting on the lateral surface of the bone, so the muscle is part of the section. Level B is taken a few centimetres more distally, below the insertion of the deltoid , where the muscle is no longer present and the anterior compartment is covered only by the biceps. In both planes the humerus is a ring of compact bone around a medullary cavity, roughly triangular to cylindrical, with the radial groove ( sulcus n. radialis ) running diagonally on its posterior surface and the nutrient foramen on its anteromedial surface; the bone is the main septum between the two compartments. Table 1 — The two cross-sections compared Structure Level A — deltoid tuberosity Level B — below the deltoid insertion Deltoid Present, covering the lateral surface of the humerus and the upper part of the anterior compartment Absent Ulnar nerve Still in the anterior compartment, on the medial side In the posterior compartment, anterior to the medial head of the triceps, after piercing the medial intermuscular septum Radial nerve In the radial groove with the profunda brachii artery, deep to the lateral head of the triceps Near the distal end of the groove, moving laterally towards the lateral intermuscular septum Median nerve Lateral or anterolateral to the brachial artery Medial to the brachial artery Basilic vein In the superficial fascia on the medial side Has pierced the deep fascia and lies medial to the brachial artery Anterior muscles Biceps brachii, brachialis, inserting coracobrachialis Biceps brachii and brachialis only 2. From the skin to the bone Skin and superficial fascia: thin, mobile skin over an areolar layer containing the two large subcutaneous veins and the cutaneous nerves of the arm. Cephalic vein ( v. cephalica ): lateral, in the groove between the deltoid and the biceps; it is the most constant vein for peripheral access in this region. Basilic vein ( v. basilica ): medial; in the distal half of the arm it pierces the deep fascia and runs medial to the brachial artery, while proximally it is still subcutaneous. Cutaneous nerves: the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm (axillary nerve) over the deltoid; the inferior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm (radial nerve) pierce the lateral head of the triceps; the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm and the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm (medial cord) run on the medial side. Deep fascia (brachial fascia) completely encloses the arm and sends the medial and lateral intermuscular septa to the humerus. These septa, with the bone, complete the two compartments; the medial septum is pierced by the ulnar nerve and the superior ulnar collateral artery, the lateral septum by the radial nerve and the radial collateral artery. Neurovascular bundle: the brachial artery with its paired brachial veins and the median nerve lie in the medial part of the anterior compartment, covered by the fascia and crossed by the basilic vein. 3. The compartments and their muscles Table 2 — Compartments of the middle arm Compartment Muscles Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior (flexor) Coracobrachialis ( m. coracobrachialis ), inserting on the medial midshaft; biceps brachii ( m. biceps brachii ), both heads; brachialis ( m. brachialis ) on the anterior surface of the humerus and septa Musculocutaneous n. (C5–C7 as a compartment; biceps brachii and brachialis C5–C6, coracobrachialis C5–C7; the nerve passes through coracobrachialis and between biceps and brachialis) Brachial a. and its muscular branches Flexors of the elbow and of the shoulder; the biceps is also a powerful supinator. Brachialis is the main flexor of the elbow. Posterior (extensor) Triceps brachii ( m. triceps brachii ): long head from the infraglenoid tubercle, lateral and medial heads from the posterior surface of the humerus above and below the radial groove Radial n. (C6–C8) Profunda brachii a. (deep brachial artery, a branch of the brachial artery) Extends the elbow; the long head also extends and adducts the shoulder. The radial nerve runs between the lateral and medial heads in the radial groove. Deltoid (level A only) Deltoid ( m. deltoideus ), inserting on the deltoid tuberosity Axillary n. (C5–C6) Posterior circumflex humeral a. and deltoid branches of the profunda brachii Abducts the shoulder; it is a muscle of the scapular region and is only caught by the proximal plane. 4. Contents: where each vessel and nerve lies Contents of the two sections Level A: the brachial artery with its paired venae comitantes lies on the medial side of the humerus, applied to the medial head of the triceps and brachialis; the median nerve is lateral or anterolateral to it; the ulnar nerve is still in the anterior compartment on the medial side, next to the superior ulnar collateral artery; the radial nerve and the profunda brachii lie deep on the posterior surface in the radial groove, between the lateral and medial heads of the triceps; the basilic vein is in the superficial fascia medially ( cephalic vein laterally); the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery are already inside the deltoid. Level B: the brachial artery is still medial, with the median nerve medial to it ; the ulnar nerve has passed through the medial intermuscular septum and lies in the posterior compartment, anterior to the medial head of the triceps; the radial nerve is approaching the lateral intermuscular septum, still with the profunda brachii; the basilic vein is now deep to the deep fascia, medial to the brachial artery; the cephalic vein runs in the superficial fascia over the lateral border of the biceps. In both planes the medial side is the \"danger side\": skin and superficial fascia with the medial cutaneous nerves → deep fascia → basilic vein → the neurovascular bundle applied to the medial surface of the humerus. At level A the bundle is anterior, with the ulnar nerve its most medial element; at level B the ulnar nerve has moved behind the septum, leaving the brachial vessels and median nerve as the medial contents. 5. Imaging of the section The level is easily identified on axial images: the humerus lies in the middle of a rounded muscular mass, the brachial vessels and median nerve form the medial \"danger point\", and the radial nerve is seen against the posterior surface of the bone just lateral to the midline. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Compact bone of the humerus Dense white ring, high attenuation Black rim (signal void) Medullary cavity (fatty marrow in the adult) Dark grey centre Bright centre (fat signal) Muscle bellies (biceps, brachialis, triceps) Intermediate grey Intermediate signal; darker than fat, brighter than tendon Intermuscular septa and fascia Thin faint lines Thin dark lines Brachial artery and brachial veins Soft-tissue density; bright after contrast Dark flow voids on spin echo; bright on contrast-enhanced or gradient-echo images Median, ulnar and radial nerves Soft-tissue density, seen best after contrast Intermediate-signal rounded structures; fascicular \"honeycomb\" on high-resolution MRI Subcutaneous fat and skin Dark fat, thin skin line Bright fat, thin dark skin line Must-memorise The arm has two compartments : anterior = biceps brachii, brachialis, coracobrachialis (musculocutaneous n.); posterior = triceps brachii (radial n.). The humerus, medial and lateral intermuscular septa and brachial fascia bound them. Radial nerve + profunda brachii in the radial groove between the lateral and medial heads of the triceps — the danger zone of a midshaft humeral fracture (wrist drop). Median nerve stays with the brachial artery throughout the arm: lateral to it proximally, medial to it distally; no major branch in the arm. Ulnar nerve pierces the medial intermuscular septum in the middle of the arm; that is the difference between the two cross-sections. Deltoid is cut only at the higher level; axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery are its neurovascular pedicle. The brachial artery can be compressed against the medial side of the humerus; it is palpable along its whole course. Clinical / radiological correlation Midshaft fracture of the humerus: the radial nerve is tethered against the bone in the radial groove, so it is the nerve most often injured — weakness of the wrist and finger extensors (wrist drop) and sensory loss on the dorsum of the hand. Surgical-neck fracture of the humerus: the axillary nerve is damaged against the surgical neck — deltoid paralysis and a small patch of anaesthesia over the deltoid. Brachial artery injury: penetrating trauma in the medial bicipital groove causes severe bleeding and distal ischaemia; the artery can be compressed against the humerus to control haemorrhage. Compartment syndrome of the arm: after crush injury or prolonged pressure; pain on passive stretch of the flexors, tenseness of the compartment, and a risk of Volkmann-type contracture if decompression is delayed. Venous access: the basilic vein is used for peripherally inserted central catheters and the cephalic vein for pacemaker leads; both are superficial at this level. Likely follow-up questions What separates the two compartments of the arm? — The humerus plus the medial and lateral intermuscular septa of the brachial fascia. Where exactly is the radial nerve at mid-arm, and what accompanies it? — In the radial groove on the posterior surface of the humerus, deep to the lateral head of the triceps, with the profunda brachii artery. Why does the ulnar nerve change compartments? — It pierces the medial intermuscular septum in the middle of the arm to reach the posterior compartment and the groove behind the medial epicondyle. Which muscles of the anterior compartment are supplied by the radial nerve? — None as a main supply; the radial nerve contributes to the lateral part of brachialis (C7) but the compartment is musculocutaneous. What would you see on the two sections that tells them apart? — The deltoid laterally and the position of the ulnar nerve (anterior at level A, posterior at level B)."},{"id":"t-IV-2","p":"IV","n":2,"t":"Cubital fossa: muscular compartments, vessels and nerves (two cross-sections).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer The cubital fossa is the triangular space anterior to the elbow, bounded by the brachioradialis laterally and the pronator teres medially, with the interepicondylar line as its base, the brachialis and supinator as its floor and the bicipital aponeurosis as the roof of its distal part. Its contents, from lateral to medial, are the tendon of biceps brachii , the brachial artery and the median nerve ; the radial nerve lies lateral to the fossa between brachialis and brachioradialis and divides here into superficial and deep branches, while the ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle and never enters the fossa. The first cross-section lies at the base of the fossa (through the elbow joint); the second lies distally at the bicipital aponeurosis, where the brachial artery has just divided into the radial and ulnar arteries. 1. Definition, boundaries and the two levels The fossa is a depression anterior to the elbow joint, a transition zone between the arm and the forearm. Its base is the imaginary line between the medial and lateral epicondyles; its lateral border is the brachioradialis, its medial border the pronator teres; the two borders meet inferiorly at the apex . The floor is formed mainly by the brachialis, laterally by the supinator and the capsule of the elbow joint; the roof is skin, superficial fascia and, distally, the bicipital aponeurosis ( aponeurosis musculi bicipitis brachii ). Table 1 — The two cross-sections compared Feature Level A — base of the fossa (interepicondylar line) Level B — at the bicipital aponeurosis Bones cut Distal humerus (trochlea, capitulum), the olecranon of the ulna behind it and the head of the radius laterally with the anular ligament Proximal radius (neck and tuberosity) and proximal ulna (coronoid region) with the proximal radio-ulnar joint; the supinator begins to wrap the radius Key structure in the roof Skin and superficial fascia; the median cubital vein is above the deep fascia Bicipital aponeurosis , separating the median cubital vein from the brachial artery and median nerve Brachial artery Single trunk, medial to the biceps tendon Divided into radial and ulnar arteries at or near the neck of the radius Median nerve Medial to the brachial artery, above the joint Passing between the two heads of pronator teres Radial nerve Lateral to the fossa, between brachialis and brachioradialis Divided into a superficial branch (deep to brachioradialis) and a deep branch entering the supinator Ulnar nerve Posterior to the medial epicondyle, outside the fossa Passing between the heads of the flexor carpi ulnaris 2. From the skin to the floor Skin and superficial fascia: the most important vessel here is the median cubital vein ( v. mediana cubiti ), which runs diagonally across the roof from the cephalic vein on the lateral side to the basilic vein on the medial side. Its pattern is variable, which matters for venepuncture. Cutaneous nerves: the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm (continuation of the musculocutaneous nerve) laterally and the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm medially. Deep fascia and roof: the bicipital aponeurosis is a flat fibrous expansion from the medial side of the biceps tendon to the deep fascia of the forearm; its sharp medial edge is palpable. It lies between the median cubital vein superficially and the brachial artery plus median nerve deeply, and protects the artery from a venepuncture needle. The deep fascia also bridges the two epicondyles, completing the roof. Floor: brachialis (with the biceps tendon descending in front of it), the supinator laterally and the anterior capsule of the elbow joint; the radial nerve separates the brachialis from the brachioradialis on the lateral side. 3. Muscular compartments at the elbow At this level the compartments are meeting: the arm's anterior and posterior compartments end, and the forearm's anterior and posterior compartments begin. The section therefore cuts the distal ends of the upper-arm muscles and the proximal origins of the forearm muscles. Table 2 — Muscle groups and their neurovascular supply at the elbow Compartment / group Muscles Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior arm (distal) Biceps brachii (tendon), brachialis Musculocutaneous n. (C5–C6) Brachial a. Biceps tendon is the most lateral content of the fossa; brachialis is the floor. Anterior forearm — superficial group Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor digitorum superficialis (and flexor carpi ulnaris medially) Median n. (C6–T1); flexor carpi ulnaris by the ulnar n. (C7–T1) Ulnar a. and radial a. as they arise Common origin from the medial epicondyle; pronator teres forms the medial border of the fossa. Posterior forearm — superficial group Brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, anconeus; supinator in the deep layer Radial n. (C5–C7) and its deep branch/posterior interosseous n. (C6–C8) Radial recurrent a., interosseous arteries Brachioradialis forms the lateral border; the radial nerve lies between it and the brachialis. Posterior arm (distal) Triceps brachii (tendon into the olecranon) Radial n. (C6–C8) Profunda brachii a. Lies behind the elbow joint and does not enter the fossa. 4. Contents: where each vessel and nerve lies Contents of the two sections Level A — from lateral to medial: tendon of the biceps brachii → brachial artery → median nerve . The radial nerve is just lateral to the fossa, in the interval between the brachialis and the brachioradialis; the ulnar nerve is behind the medial epicondyle in the cubital tunnel and is not a content of the fossa. The brachial artery and median nerve lie on the brachialis and are crossed by the bicipital aponeurosis distally. Level B: the brachial artery divides into the radial artery (lateral, deep to the brachioradialis) and the ulnar artery (medial, deep to the pronator teres); the median nerve passes between the humeral and ulnar heads of the pronator teres; the radial nerve gives its superficial branch , which runs into the forearm deep to the brachioradialis, and its deep branch , which passes between the two heads of the supinator; the ulnar nerve enters the forearm between the heads of the flexor carpi ulnaris. The median cubital vein lies in the superficial fascia above the bicipital aponeurosis, so a venepuncture at this site is separated from the brachial artery by the aponeurosis. 5. Imaging of the section On axial MRI the cubital fossa is read as a triangle between the brachioradialis and the pronator teres, with the brachialis as its floor; the median nerve and brachial artery are the two round structures in front of the brachialis, and the biceps tendon is the flat structure at the lateral corner. Radiographs show the bony base of the fossa and are used for fractures and for the fat-pad signs of an elbow effusion. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Humerus, radius and ulna (cortex) Dense white Black cortical rim Medullary bone and marrow Dark centre Bright fatty marrow Joint space and capsule of the elbow Soft-tissue density Intermediate; effusion is bright on T2 Biceps tendon and bicipital aponeurosis Intermediate density Uniformly dark band Brachial, radial and ulnar arteries Bright after intravenous contrast Flow voids; bright with contrast Median and radial nerves Soft-tissue density Intermediate-signal round structures; the radial nerve is seen between brachialis and brachioradialis Median cubital vein Superficial soft-tissue density Superficial dark/bright vessel in the subcutaneous fat Must-memorise Boundaries: base = interepicondylar line; lateral = brachioradialis; medial = pronator teres; floor = brachialis and supinator; roof = skin, fascia and the bicipital aponeurosis. Contents lateral → medial: biceps tendon → brachial artery → median nerve . The radial nerve divides here into superficial and deep branches; the deep branch enters the supinator and becomes the posterior interosseous nerve. The ulnar nerve does not enter the fossa ; it passes posterior to the medial epicondyle. The brachial artery divides into radial and ulnar arteries at the apex of the fossa, near the neck of the radius. The bicipital aponeurosis separates the median cubital vein (superficial) from the brachial artery and median nerve (deep). Clinical / radiological correlation Blood pressure: the stethoscope is placed over the brachial artery in the cubital fossa, medial to the biceps tendon, while the cuff compresses the artery against the humerus. Venepuncture and dialysis fistula: the median cubital vein is the favourite site for venepuncture; surgical anastomoses here or at the wrist (radial artery to cephalic vein) create a high-flow fistula for dialysis. Supracondylar fracture of the humerus: the distal fragment may injure the brachial artery and the median nerve, producing acute forearm ischaemia and, if untreated, a Volkmann ischaemic contracture. Cubital tunnel syndrome: the ulnar nerve is compressed behind the medial epicondyle; numbness of the little finger and weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles result. Radial nerve injury at the elbow: a fracture of the proximal radius or a lesion in the supinator produces finger drop, but wrist extension is largely preserved because the extensor carpi radialis longus is supplied by the radial nerve above the supinator. Likely follow-up questions Name the contents of the cubital fossa from lateral to medial. — Biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve. What forms the roof and what does it protect? — The bicipital aponeurosis; it separates the median cubital vein from the brachial artery and median nerve. Where does the radial nerve lie in relation to the fossa, and what does it do here? — Between the brachialis and brachioradialis; it divides into superficial and deep branches. Why is the ulnar nerve not a content of the fossa? — It runs posterior to the medial epicondyle in the cubital tunnel. Where does the brachial artery end? — At the apex of the fossa, near the neck of the radius, where it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. Which structures cross the fossa superficially? — The median cubital vein, the medial and lateral cutaneous nerves of the forearm, and the bicipital aponeurosis."},{"id":"t-IV-3","p":"IV","n":3,"t":"Upper part of the forearm (supinator's level): bones, muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer This section lies just distal to the cubital fossa, at the level of the radial tuberosity and the supinator . The radius lies laterally and the ulna medially, joined by the upper part of the interosseous membrane , which with the lateral intermuscular septum and the deep fascia separates the anterior (flexor) from the posterior (extensor) compartment. Anteriorly lie the superficial flexors (pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris) and the beginning intermediate and deep layers; posteriorly lie the brachioradialis, the radial wrist extensors, the superficial extensors and, deepest of all, the supinator wrapped around the radius. The key relations are the median nerve between the two heads of pronator teres , the deep branch of the radial nerve between the two layers of the supinator , and the ulnar nerve between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris . 1. The level and the bones The plane passes through the proximal forearm at the height of the radial tuberosity. Both bones are separate here and triangular in cross-section, with anterior, posterior and interosseous borders and anterior, posterior and lateral (radius) or medial (ulna) surfaces. The radius is the smaller, laterally placed bone: its head and neck are just above the plane, its radial tuberosity projecting medially receives the biceps tendon, and the anterior oblique line gives origin to the radial head of flexor digitorum superficialis. The ulna is the larger, medially placed bone; its coronoid region with the radial notch lies just above the plane, and the supinator crest on its posterolateral surface gives origin to the deep layer of the supinator. The interosseous membrane ( membrana interossea ) stretches between the interosseous borders; its free upper margin lies just below the radial tuberosity and its collagen fibres pass inferiorly from the radius to the ulna. It is the main septum between the compartments and transfers force from the hand to the radius and thence to the humerus. Table 1 — Bony framework and septa at this level Structure Cross-sectional appearance and role Radius (proximal shaft) Small, lateral, roughly round to triangular; the radial tuberosity projects medially, and the anterior oblique line gives origin to the radial head of flexor digitorum superficialis. Ulna (proximal shaft) Larger, medial, triangular; its sharp posterior border is palpable subcutaneously and the supinator crest lies posterolaterally. Interosseous membrane Thin fibrous sheet between the interosseous borders; separates anterior and posterior compartments, gives origin to deep muscles, and is crossed by the anterior interosseous vessels. Lateral intermuscular septum Runs from the anterior border of the radius to the deep fascia and separates the brachioradialis from the flexor carpi radialis. Antebrachial fascia Encloses the forearm; posteriorly it attaches along the posterior border of the ulna, completing the posterior compartment. 2. From the skin to the bone Skin and superficial fascia: the cephalic vein lies anterolaterally, the basilic vein posteromedially; the two are connected above by the median cubital vein of the cubital fossa. Cutaneous nerves: the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm (from the musculocutaneous nerve) laterally, the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm medially, and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm (radial nerve) behind. Deep fascia (antebrachial fascia): thick and tight, reinforced proximally by the bicipital aponeurosis on the medial side and sending the lateral intermuscular septum towards the radius. Muscular surface: the most superficial muscles in the section are the pronator teres medially, the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus anteriorly, the brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus laterally, and the extensor carpi ulnaris and anconeus medially behind. 3. The compartments and their muscles Table 2 — Compartments of the proximal forearm Compartment Muscles (layer) Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior (flexor) Superficial: pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris Median n. (C6–C8); flexor carpi ulnaris by the ulnar n. (C7–T1) Ulnar a., radial a. Common origin from the medial epicondyle; pronator teres rotates the radius over the ulna. Intermediate: flexor digitorum superficialis (humero-ulnar and radial heads) Median n. (C8–T1) Ulnar a. passes deep to it The median nerve and ulnar artery pass between its two heads. Deep: flexor digitorum profundus (lateral part), flexor pollicis longus Anterior interosseous n. (from median, C7–T1); medial part of flexor digitorum profundus by the ulnar n. Anterior interosseous a. Deep to the superficial and intermediate layers, on the interosseous membrane. Posterior (extensor) Superficial: brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, anconeus Radial n. (C5–C7) for brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus; deep branch and posterior interosseous n. (C7–C8) for most of the rest; anconeus by the radial n. (C6–C8) Radial recurrent a., posterior interosseous a. Brachioradialis forms the lateral border of the cubital fossa and is an accessory elbow flexor. Deep: supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis Posterior interosseous n. (C6–C8) Posterior interosseous a. Supinator wraps the proximal radius; its two layers are split by the deep branch of the radial nerve. 4. Contents: where each vessel and nerve lies Contents of the section Median nerve: enters the forearm between the humeral and ulnar heads of the pronator teres , then passes between the humero-ulnar and radial heads of the flexor digitorum superficialis; it runs down the middle of the anterior compartment deep to the superficial flexors. It gives the anterior interosseous nerve , which runs on the anterior surface of the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous artery. Ulnar nerve: enters the forearm between the humeral and ulnar heads of the flexor carpi ulnaris ; it runs on the medial side between the flexor carpi ulnaris and the flexor digitorum profundus. Radial nerve: lateral to the fossa it divides into a superficial branch , which runs deep to the brachioradialis with the radial artery, and a deep branch , which passes between the superficial and deep layers of the supinator and then continues as the posterior interosseous nerve in the posterior compartment. Brachial artery divides at the neck of the radius into the radial artery (lateral, deep to the brachioradialis) and the ulnar artery (medial, deep to the pronator teres). The ulnar artery gives the common interosseous artery , which divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries; the posterior interosseous artery passes dorsally over the upper margin of the interosseous membrane. 5. Imaging of the section On axial MRI the two bones are easy to identify: the radius is round and lateral, the ulna is triangular and medial, and the thin dark line between them is the interosseous membrane. The muscle groups are read around them: the superficial flexors form the anterior mass, the brachioradialis and radial extensors the anterolateral mass, and the extensor group the posterior mass. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Radius and ulna (cortex) Dense white rings Black rims Marrow of the two bones Dark centre Bright fatty marrow Interosseous membrane Thin faint line Thin dark band between the bones Muscle bellies Intermediate grey Intermediate signal with visible fascial septa Supinator and its two layers Muscle density wrapped around the radius Intermediate muscle signal; the deep branch of the radial nerve may be visible between the layers Radial, ulnar and interosseous arteries Bright after contrast Flow voids; small round structures near the bones Median, ulnar and radial nerves Soft-tissue density Intermediate-signal round/oval structures in the fascial planes Must-memorise Two compartments separated by the radius, ulna, interosseous membrane , lateral intermuscular septum and deep fascia. Three nerve gates at this level: median nerve between the heads of pronator teres ; ulnar nerve between the heads of flexor carpi ulnaris ; deep branch of the radial nerve between the layers of the supinator . Anterior compartment: median nerve and its anterior interosseous branch, except flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (ulnar nerve). Posterior compartment: entirely radial nerve — superficial branch is sensory, deep branch is motor and becomes the posterior interosseous nerve. Brachial artery divides at the neck of the radius: radial artery lateral, ulnar artery medial; the ulnar artery gives the common interosseous artery. Clinical / radiological correlation Posterior interosseous nerve entrapment (in the supinator or at its proximal fibrous edge): weakness of thumb and finger extension with finger drop, but wrist extension is largely spared because the extensor carpi radialis longus is supplied by the radial nerve above the supinator; there is no sensory loss. Monteggia fracture-dislocation (fracture of the proximal ulna with dislocation of the radial head): the radial nerve or its deep branch may be injured, and the radial head must always be checked. Supracondylar and medial epicondyle fractures put the median nerve and brachial artery, or the ulnar nerve, at risk respectively. Pronator teres syndrome: median nerve compression in the proximal forearm causes aching pain, weakness of the thenar muscles and sensory loss in the median distribution, but no carpal tunnel findings. Likely follow-up questions Name the boundaries between the anterior and posterior compartments of the forearm. — Radius, ulna, interosseous membrane, lateral intermuscular septum and deep fascia. Where does the median nerve enter the forearm? — Between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres. Where does the deep branch of the radial nerve enter the posterior compartment? — Between the two layers of the supinator, and it continues as the posterior interosseous nerve. Which muscles of the anterior compartment are not supplied by the median nerve? — Flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (ulnar nerve). Which arteries arise from the brachial artery at this level? — The radial and ulnar arteries; the ulnar gives the common interosseous artery."},{"id":"t-IV-4","p":"IV","n":4,"t":"Middle part of the forearm: bones, muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer At mid-forearm the radius (lateral) and ulna (medial) are both narrow shafts joined by the interosseous membrane , which separates the anterior (flexor) from the posterior (extensor) compartment. The anterior compartment contains the four superficial flexors, the flexor digitorum superficialis (intermediate layer) and the flexor digitorum profundus plus flexor pollicis longus (deep layer); the posterior compartment contains the brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, anconeus and the deep extensors. Four neurovascular planes dominate the picture: the median nerve between flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus; the ulnar nerve and artery between flexor carpi ulnaris and flexor digitorum profundus; the radial artery with the superficial branch of the radial nerve between brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis; and the anterior interosseous nerve and artery on the interosseous membrane. 1. The level and the bones The section passes through the middle third of the forearm, where the two bones are at their narrowest and are almost parallel. The radius is lateral and roughly cylindrical; the ulna is medial and triangular with a sharp subcutaneous posterior border. The interosseous membrane spans between them and is the most important landmark of the section, because almost every deep vessel and nerve lies in relation to it or to the bones. The radial artery turns laterally, the ulnar artery medially, and the median nerve occupies the midline of the anterior compartment. Table 1 — Bony framework and septa at mid-forearm Structure Appearance and role Radius Lateral, cylindrical shaft; gives origin to the flexor pollicis longus and receives the insertion of the pronator teres; the radial artery runs on its lateral side in the distal part of the section. Ulna Medial, triangular shaft with a palpable posterior border; gives origin to flexor digitorum profundus and the extensors on its posterior surface. Interosseous membrane Separates the compartments, gives origin to deep flexors and extensors, transmits the anterior interosseous vessels, and its distal third has an aperture for a perforating vessel. Lateral intermuscular septum From the anterior border of the radius to the deep fascia; separates the brachioradialis from the flexor carpi radialis. Deep (antebrachial) fascia Encloses both compartments; attached posteriorly to the posterior border of the ulna. 2. From the skin to the bone Skin and superficial fascia: the cephalic vein runs laterally, the basilic vein medially, in the subcutaneous fat. Cutaneous nerves: the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm and the superficial branch of the radial nerve laterally, the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm medially, the posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm behind. Deep fascia: gives attachment to the lateral intermuscular septum and separates the superficial muscles from the subcutaneous tissue. Muscles immediately under the fascia: anteriorly the flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor digitorum superficialis with flexor carpi ulnaris medially; laterally the brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus; posteriorly the extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris. 3. The compartments and their muscles Table 2 — Compartments of the middle forearm Compartment Muscles Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior (flexor) Superficial and intermediate: pronator teres (ending), flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor digitorum superficialis, flexor carpi ulnaris Median n. (C6–T1); flexor carpi ulnaris by the ulnar n. (C7–T1) Radial a., ulnar a. Flexors of the wrist and fingers; the flexor digitorum superficialis lies superficial to the profundus here. Deep: flexor digitorum profundus (lateral part), flexor pollicis longus Anterior interosseous n. (C7–T1); medial part of flexor digitorum profundus by the ulnar n. Anterior interosseous a. Rest on the interosseous membrane; their tendons begin to form distally. Posterior (extensor) Superficial: brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, anconeus (ending) Radial n. (C5–C7) for the first two; posterior interosseous n. (C7–C8) for the others Radial recurrent a., posterior interosseous a. Extensors of the wrist and fingers; the extensor digitorum gives four tendons distally. Deep: supinator (ending), abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis Posterior interosseous n. (C7–C8) Posterior interosseous a. Lateral to the extensor digitorum in this plane; the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis will emerge between the extensor digitorum and extensor carpi radialis brevis. 4. Contents: where each vessel and nerve lies Contents of the section Median nerve: in the midline of the anterior compartment, deep to the flexor digitorum superficialis and superficial to the flexor digitorum profundus ; its branch, the anterior interosseous nerve , runs deeper on the interosseous membrane together with the anterior interosseous artery. Ulnar nerve and ulnar artery: on the medial side, in the fascial plane between the flexor carpi ulnaris and the flexor digitorum profundus ; the nerve lies immediately medial to the artery. Radial artery: on the lateral side, in the interval between the brachioradialis and the flexor carpi radialis (its proximal half is deep to the brachioradialis). The superficial branch of the radial nerve lies close to it, and in the middle third it is related to the lateral side of the artery. Posterior interosseous nerve and artery: in the posterior compartment, between the extensor digitorum and the deep extensors (extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis), running on the interosseous membrane. Deep veins accompany these arteries; the superficial venous channels (cephalic and basilic veins) lie in the subcutaneous layer. 5. Imaging of the section At mid-forearm the axial image is dominated by the two small bones and four muscle masses. The interosseous membrane is a reliable midline landmark, and each of the four neurovascular planes can be followed on consecutive slices, which is why MRI is used for nerve entrapment and for compartment pathology. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Radius and ulna (cortex) Dense white rings Black rims Marrow Dark centre Bright fatty marrow Interosseous membrane Thin faint line Thin dark band Flexor and extensor muscle masses Intermediate grey, grouped by fascia Intermediate signal; the intermuscular planes are dark lines Radial and ulnar arteries Small rounded densities, bright with contrast Flow voids in the expected planes Median, ulnar, radial, interosseous nerves Soft-tissue density, not separable without contrast Small intermediate-signal oval structures; high-resolution MRI shows the fascicular pattern Subcutaneous fat and superficial veins Dark fat; veins density Bright fat; dark/bright vessels superficially Must-memorise Four planes to learn: median nerve between flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus; ulnar nerve and artery between flexor carpi ulnaris and profundus; radial artery with the superficial radial nerve between brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis; anterior interosseous nerve and artery on the interosseous membrane. The posterior interosseous nerve and artery lie between the extensor digitorum and the deep extensors. Anterior compartment = median nerve (plus ulnar nerve to flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of the profundus); posterior compartment = radial nerve entirely. At mid-forearm the radius is lateral and small, the ulna medial and triangular; the interosseous membrane is the midline structure. Clinical / radiological correlation Compartment syndrome of the forearm: after a crush injury or a supracondylar fracture; the deep flexor compartment is especially vulnerable. Pain on passive extension of the fingers, a tense forearm and paraesthesia are the warning signs; a late result is the Volkmann ischaemic contracture. Anterior interosseous nerve syndrome: weakness of the flexor pollicis longus and of the lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus — the patient cannot flex the tip of the thumb or index finger, and there is no sensory loss. Laceration of the radial or ulnar artery is common in glass injuries because both are relatively superficial in the distal forearm; the dual supply of the hand usually allows ligation of one without loss of the hand. Nerve entrapment must be localised by the pattern of weakness: pronator teres for the median nerve, the cubital tunnel above for the ulnar nerve, the supinator for the posterior interosseous nerve. Likely follow-up questions Where does the median nerve lie at mid-forearm? — Deep to the flexor digitorum superficialis and superficial to the flexor digitorum profundus, in the midline. What is the relation of the ulnar nerve to the ulnar artery? — The nerve is immediately medial to the artery in the plane between flexor carpi ulnaris and flexor digitorum profundus. What does the anterior interosseous nerve supply? — Flexor pollicis longus, the lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus and pronator quadratus. Which vessels does the posterior interosseous artery accompany? — The posterior interosseous nerve, in the posterior compartment. What is the danger of a crush injury at this level? — Acute compartment syndrome of the forearm and, if unrelieved, Volkmann ischaemic contracture."},{"id":"t-IV-5","p":"IV","n":5,"t":"Lower part of the forearm (pronator quadratus' level): bones, muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer In the lower forearm the two bones are broad and close together, and the pronator quadratus fills the anterior interval between the distal radius and ulna. The muscle bellies have largely become tendons : anteriorly the flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus tendons (eight in all), the flexor pollicis longus tendon, the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus tendons and, medially, the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon; posteriorly the tendons of the brachioradialis, the radial wrist extensors, the thumb extensors, the extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris, with the extensor pollicis longus turning around the dorsal tubercle of the radius. The median nerve comes to lie superficially between the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus tendons, ready to enter the carpal tunnel; the ulnar nerve and artery lie medially, and the radial artery lies laterally, between the brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis tendons. 1. The level and the bones The plane passes through the distal quarter of the forearm. The radius is now broad and flattened anteroposteriorly, with the dorsal tubercle (Lister's tubercle) on its posterior surface and the ulnar notch on its medial side; the ulna ends in its small head and styloid process. The distal radio-ulnar joint is present, and the pronator quadratus spans the anterior surfaces of both bones just proximal to the joint. The interosseous membrane remains as a thin sheet between them; distally its aperture transmits the anterior interosseous vessels to the posterior compartment. Table 1 — Bony framework at this level Structure Appearance and relations Distal radius Broad, flattened; lateral surface continues to the radial styloid; dorsal tubercle on the posterior surface; ulnar notch medially for the head of the ulna. Distal ulna Small head with the styloid process; articulates with the ulnar notch of the radius through the distal radio-ulnar joint. Pronator quadratus Flat, square muscle on the anterior surfaces of both bones; the deepest structure of the anterior compartment at this level. Interosseous membrane Thin and partly replaced by the tendons; its distal aperture transmits the anterior interosseous vessels. Flexor and extensor retinacula Not yet reached at this plane, but their proximal attachments (radius, ulna, carpal bones) are close below. 2. From the skin to the bone Skin and superficial fascia: thin here; the cephalic vein lies laterally, the basilic vein medially, and the dorsal venous network of the hand begins just distally. Cutaneous nerves: the superficial branch of the radial nerve on the dorsolateral side, the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve anteriorly, and the dorsal cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve posteromedially. Deep fascia: strong, holding the tendons against the bones; the tendons are surrounded by synovial sheaths which begin at about this level. Tendons under the fascia: anteriorly the flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor digitorum superficialis, flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus and flexor carpi ulnaris tendons; posteriorly the brachioradialis and radial wrist extensor tendons, the thumb extensors, the extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris tendons. 3. The compartments and their contents Table 2 — Compartments and tendons of the lower forearm Compartment Muscle or tendon Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior (flexor) Pronator quadratus (muscle) Anterior interosseous n. (C7–C8) Anterior interosseous a. The deepest structure, immediately on the two bones; the radial artery lies anterior to it. Flexor digitorum superficialis (4 tendons) and profundus (4 tendons), flexor pollicis longus tendon Median n. for superficialis and the lateral profundus; ulnar n. for the medial profundus; anterior interosseous n. for flexor pollicis longus — The eight digital flexor tendons and the flexor pollicis longus tendon pass through the carpal tunnel; superficialis tendons are superficial to profundus tendons. Flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris tendons Median n. (FCR, palmaris longus); ulnar n. (FCU) Radial a. lateral to FCR; ulnar a. with FCU Palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum; flexor carpi radialis has its own compartment. Posterior (extensor) Brachioradialis tendon; extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis tendons; abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons; extensor pollicis longus tendon; extensor indicis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris tendons Radial n. (brachioradialis, ECRL); posterior interosseous n. (all the others) Posterior interosseous a. The extensor pollicis longus tendon hooks around the dorsal tubercle of the radius; the tendons pass under the extensor retinaculum in six compartments. 4. Contents: where each vessel and nerve lies Contents of the section Median nerve: now superficial, lying between the tendons of the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus , immediately deep to the deep fascia; it is about to pass deep to the flexor retinaculum into the carpal tunnel. Radial artery: on the lateral side between the brachioradialis tendon (lateral) and the flexor carpi radialis tendon (medial), directly anterior to the pronator quadratus and the distal radius; here the radial pulse is palpated. Ulnar nerve and ulnar artery: medial, under the anterolateral lip of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon; the nerve is immediately medial to the artery and both pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum into the palm. Anterior interosseous nerve and artery: on the interosseous membrane, deep between the flexor pollicis longus and the pronator quadratus; the nerve ends in the pronator quadratus. Posterior interosseous nerve and artery: in the posterior compartment between the extensor digitorum and the other extensors, descending towards the wrist. 5. Imaging of the section This plane is the transition from muscle to tendon and is a favourite for ultrasound, because tendons are seen as fibrillar structures with a characteristic echotexture and their sheaths can be followed into the carpal tunnel and the extensor compartments. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Distal radius and ulna (cortex) Dense white Black rim Marrow Dark centre Bright fatty marrow Pronator quadratus Muscle density between the bones anteriorly Intermediate muscle signal; easily separated from the dark tendons Tendons (flexor and extensor) Intermediate to slightly dense round/oval dots Uniformly dark round/oval structures; synovial fluid around them is bright on T2 Radial and ulnar arteries Bright after contrast Flow voids; the radial artery is lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon Median and ulnar nerves Soft-tissue density Intermediate-signal oval structures; the median nerve is superficial between the two tendons Subcutaneous fat and veins Dark fat; vein density Bright fat, dark/bright superficial vessels Must-memorise Pronator quadratus is the deepest anterior structure between the distal radius and ulna; the radial artery lies anterior to it. Radial artery is between the brachioradialis tendon and the flexor carpi radialis tendon — the site of the radial pulse. Median nerve becomes superficial between the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus tendons. Ulnar nerve and artery run under the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon, superficial to the flexor retinaculum. The eight digital flexor tendons and the flexor pollicis longus tendon are the contents of the carpal tunnel below this plane; the flexor carpi radialis has its own compartment and the palmaris longus has no sheath. Clinical / radiological correlation Colles' fracture: fracture of the distal radius with dorsal displacement of the distal fragment; the classic deformity follows a fall on the outstretched hand, and the distal radio-ulnar joint and the median nerve must be assessed. Radial pulse: felt against the distal radius, lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon; it is the standard site for pulse rate and for arterial blood gas sampling. Ulnar artery and nerve at the wrist: the artery is superficial and vulnerable to laceration; compression of the ulnar nerve here or in the ulnar tunnel gives numbness of the little finger and weakness of the intrinsic muscles. Carpal tunnel syndrome begins distal to this plane, but the median nerve is already superficial here, which is why the diagnosis is made by the distribution of symptoms in the hand. Likely follow-up questions What lies between the distal radius and ulna on the anterior side? — The pronator quadratus muscle, innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve. Where do you feel the radial pulse and what lies beneath it? — Between the brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis tendons, over the pronator quadratus and distal radius. How does the median nerve reach the carpal tunnel? — It becomes superficial between the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus tendons and then passes deep to the flexor retinaculum. Which tendons pass through the carpal tunnel at the next plane? — Four flexor digitorum superficialis, four flexor digitorum profundus and one flexor pollicis longus tendon, with the median nerve. Which structures lie superficial to the flexor retinaculum? — The ulnar nerve and artery, and the tendon of palmaris longus."},{"id":"t-IV-6","p":"IV","n":6,"t":"Wrist: carpal tunnel, bones, tendinous sheaths, tendons, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer The wrist section passes through the carpal arch , which is converted into the carpal tunnel by the flexor retinaculum . The arch is bounded medially by the pisiform and the hook of the hamate and laterally by the tubercles of the scaphoid and trapezium ; the floor is formed by the distal carpal row. Through the tunnel pass the nine flexor tendons (four flexor digitorum superficialis, four flexor digitorum profundus, one flexor pollicis longus) and the median nerve , which lies anterior (superficial) to the tendons. Outside the tunnel, the ulnar nerve and artery run superficial to the retinaculum in the ulnar (Guyon's) canal, the flexor carpi radialis tendon has its own compartment, the palmaris longus tendon lies superficial to the retinaculum, and the radial artery crosses dorsally. The extensor tendons are held by the extensor retinaculum in six compartments with their own synovial sheaths. 1. The level and the bones The section lies at the level of the wrist joint, immediately proximal to the metacarpals. The distal ends of the radius and ulna lie just above the plane, and the section cuts the two rows of carpal bones ( ossa carpi ), eight in all, arranged in a proximal and a distal row. The four bony landmarks that bound the carpal arch are the key to the section: the scaphoid tubercle and the trapezium tubercle laterally, and the pisiform with the hook of the hamate medially. Table 1 — The carpal bones at this level Row Bone Key features and relations Proximal Scaphoid Lateral bone of the proximal row; its tubercle is a lateral attachment of the flexor retinaculum; it articulates with the radius and is the commonest carpal bone to fracture. Lunate Articulates with the radius and the capitate; its blood supply is precarious (Kienböck's disease). Triquetrum Medial, articulates with the hamate and the articular disc of the wrist. Pisiform Sesamoid bone in the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris; forms the medial wall of the carpal tunnel. Distal Trapezium Articulates with metacarpal I; its tubercle is a lateral attachment of the flexor retinaculum. Trapezoid Articulates with metacarpal II; part of the floor of the carpal arch. Capitate Largest carpal bone, central; it forms the keystone of the carpal arch and part of the floor of the carpal tunnel. Hamate Its hook is the medial attachment of the flexor retinaculum and the lateral wall of the ulnar canal. 2. From the skin to the tunnel Skin and superficial fascia: thin, mobile; the dorsal venous network, the cephalic vein laterally and the basilic vein medially lie in the subcutaneous layer. Cutaneous nerves: the superficial branch of the radial nerve dorsolaterally, the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve over the thenar region, and the palmar and dorsal cutaneous branches of the ulnar nerve medially. Deep fascia: the antebrachial fascia is thickened here into the palmar carpal ligament anteriorly (superficial to the flexor retinaculum) and into the extensor retinaculum dorsally. The extensor retinaculum is attached laterally to the radius and medially to the ulna and triquetrum. Flexor retinaculum ( retinaculum flexorum ): a strong fibrous band between the lateral and medial walls of the carpal arch; it holds the flexor tendons against the carpal bones, prevents bowing and forms the roof of the carpal tunnel. 3. Compartments, tendons and their sheaths Table 2 — The flexor side of the wrist Compartment / canal Contents Synovial sheath Notes Carpal tunnel Four flexor digitorum superficialis tendons, four flexor digitorum profundus tendons, one flexor pollicis longus tendon, median nerve FDS and FDP tendons share one common sheath; FPL has a separate sheath Median nerve lies anterior to the tendons; the flexor retinaculum is the roof. Flexor carpi radialis compartment Flexor carpi radialis tendon Own sheath Passes through a separate fibrous compartment lateral to the tunnel, in the groove of the trapezium. Ulnar (Guyon's) canal Ulnar nerve and ulnar artery None Superficial to the flexor retinaculum, between the pisiform and the hook of the hamate; roofed by the palmar carpal ligament and the palmaris brevis. Superficial to the retinaculum Palmaris longus tendon None Passes into the palmar aponeurosis; the tendon is absent in a minority of people. Dorsal, lateral Radial artery — Passes dorsally around the lateral side of the wrist, over the scaphoid and trapezium, to reach the first web space. Table 3 — The six extensor compartments (from lateral to medial) No. Tendons Notes 1 Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis Site of de Quervain's tenosynovitis; the tendons form the lateral border of the anatomical snuffbox. 2 Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis Extensors and abductors of the wrist; the longus tendon lies superficial to the brevis. 3 Extensor pollicis longus Hooks around the dorsal tubercle of the radius; forms the medial border of the anatomical snuffbox. 4 Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis Share one compartment and one sheath; the extensor digitorum has four tendons. 5 Extensor digiti minimi Its own compartment and sheath; extends the little finger. 6 Extensor carpi ulnaris Extends and adducts the wrist; runs in the groove on the back of the ulna. 4. Contents of the carpal tunnel Carpal tunnel — walls and contents Lateral wall: tubercles of the scaphoid and trapezium. Medial wall: pisiform and hook of the hamate. Floor: the carpal arch formed by the distal carpal row (capitate, trapezoid, hamate) and the proximal row. Roof: the flexor retinaculum. Contents: the median nerve plus the nine flexor tendons — four of flexor digitorum superficialis, four of flexor digitorum profundus and one of flexor pollicis longus. The median nerve lies anterior (superficial) to the tendons , immediately under the flexor retinaculum — hence its vulnerability. The ulnar nerve and artery do not enter the tunnel : they lie superficially in the ulnar canal on the lateral side of the pisiform. The flexor carpi radialis tendon has its own lateral compartment and is outside the tunnel; the palmaris longus tendon passes superficial to the retinaculum. 5. Imaging of the section Axial MRI and high-resolution ultrasound are the examinations of choice for the carpal tunnel; CT is used for the bony arch and for fractures. The normal median nerve is a flattened oval structure anterior to the tendon bundle, and its swelling and flattening within the tunnel are the morphological signs of compression. Table 4 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Carpal bones (cortex) Dense white; the hook of the hamate and the pisiform are easy landmarks Black cortical rims with bright fatty marrow inside Flexor retinaculum Thin faint band bridging the arch Thin dark band as the roof of the tunnel Flexor tendons Rounded densities closely packed under the retinaculum Uniformly dark round/oval structures; tenosynovial fluid bright on T2 Median nerve Soft-tissue density; not separable without contrast Flattened intermediate-signal oval structure anterior to the tendons Ulnar nerve and artery Small medial densities superficial to the retinaculum Intermediate oval (nerve) and flow void (artery) in Guyon's canal Extensor tendons (six compartments) Small dense dots on the dorsum Dark dots in their sheaths under the extensor retinaculum Subcutaneous fat and superficial veins Dark fat, vein density Bright fat, dark/bright superficial vessels Must-memorise Carpal tunnel = carpal arch + flexor retinaculum; lateral wall scaphoid & trapezium, medial wall pisiform & hook of hamate. Contents: 9 flexor tendons + median nerve ; the median nerve is the most superficial structure in the tunnel. Outside the tunnel: ulnar nerve and artery (Guyon's canal), flexor carpi radialis (own compartment), palmaris longus (superficial), radial artery (dorsal). Six extensor compartments in order: APL+EPB, ECRL+ECRB, EPL, ED+EI, EDM, ECU. FDS and FDP share one synovial sheath; FPL has a separate one. Proximal carpal row: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform; distal row: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate. Clinical / radiological correlation Carpal tunnel syndrome: compression of the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum causes pain and pins-and-needles in the median distribution (thumb, index, middle and lateral half of the ring finger), sparing the thenar skin because its cutaneous branch leaves the nerve proximal to the retinaculum; thenar wasting and weakness appear later. Tapping over the retinaculum reproduces the symptoms (Tinel's sign), and severe cases are decompressed by dividing the retinaculum. Ulnar tunnel (Guyon's canal) syndrome: compression of the ulnar nerve at the wrist causes numbness of the little finger and weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles. De Quervain's tenosynovitis affects the first extensor compartment (abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis); pain on thumb movement and a positive Finkelstein test. Scaphoid fracture: the blood supply enters distally and runs retrogradely, so a proximal-pole fracture risks avascular necrosis; tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox after a fall on the hand is the clinical clue. Kienböck's disease: avascular necrosis of the lunate with pain, stiffness and later arthritis. Likely follow-up questions What forms the carpal tunnel and what are its walls? — The carpal arch and the flexor retinaculum; scaphoid/trapezium laterally, pisiform/hook of the hamate medially. Name the contents of the carpal tunnel. — Nine flexor tendons and the median nerve. Why is the median nerve at risk in the tunnel? — It lies most superficially, immediately under the retinaculum, in a confined space. Which structures pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum? — Ulnar nerve and artery, and the palmaris longus tendon. Which tendons occupy the first and third extensor compartments? — Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first; extensor pollicis longus in the third. Where is the anatomical snuffbox and what bounds it? — Over the scaphoid and trapezium; APL and EPB laterally, EPL medially, with the radial artery in its floor."},{"id":"t-IV-7","p":"IV","n":7,"t":"Upper part of the thigh (femoral triangle's level): muscular compartments, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer The section passes through the upper third of the thigh, a few centimetres below the inguinal ligament, where the femoral vessels and nerve lie in the femoral triangle and the muscle is divided into anterior (quadriceps and sartorius, femoral nerve), medial (pectineus, adductors, gracilis, obturator externus, obturator nerve) and posterior (hamstrings, sciatic nerve) compartments. The femoral triangle has the inguinal ligament as its base, the sartorius as its lateral border and the adductor longus as its medial border; its floor is formed by the iliopsoas, pectineus and adductor longus. Its contents from lateral to medial are the femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein and the femoral canal with lymphatics , the artery and vein enclosed in the femoral sheath while the nerve lies outside it. 1. The level and the bones The plane lies below the inguinal ligament, so the hip joint and the pelvic bones are above the level of the section; the only bone cut is the femur , through its proximal shaft, whose posterior surface bears the gluteal tuberosity and the beginning of the linea aspera. The femur lies in the centre of the section, surrounded by the quadriceps anteriorly, the adductors medially and the hamstrings posteriorly. Table 1 — The framework of the section Structure Appearance and role Femur (proximal shaft) The only bone in the plane; the greater trochanter may appear at the lateral edge if the section is slightly higher, and the lesser trochanter posteromedially. Fascia lata The deep fascia of the thigh; sends intermuscular septa to the linea aspera which separate the three compartments; its lateral thickening is the iliotibial tract. Saphenous opening (fossa ovalis) An aperture in the fascia lata just below the medial end of the inguinal ligament, closed by the cribriform fascia, through which the great saphenous vein reaches the femoral vein. Inguinal ligament Immediately above the section; it is the base of the femoral triangle and the boundary between abdomen and thigh. 2. From the skin to the deep fascia Skin and superficial fascia: thick skin; the fatty superficial layer contains the superficial inguinal lymph nodes (arranged in a horizontal group below the inguinal ligament and a vertical group along the great saphenous vein) and the great saphenous vein ( v. saphena magna ), which ascends on the medial side of the thigh to pierce the cribriform fascia at the saphenous opening and drain into the femoral vein. Cutaneous nerves: the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2–L3) laterally; the anterior cutaneous branches of the femoral nerve anteriorly; the obturator nerve branches medially; the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh behind; the ilioinguinal nerve and the femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve supply the skin just below the inguinal ligament. Deep fascia: the fascia lata , a strong stocking that sends the lateral, medial and posterior intermuscular septa towards the linea aspera of the femur; laterally it is reinforced by the iliotibial tract , which receives the insertion of the tensor fasciae latae and the gluteus maximus. Femoral sheath: a funnel-shaped sleeve of fascia (from the transversalis and iliac fasciae) around the femoral artery, vein and canal in the femoral triangle; the femoral nerve lies lateral to it and outside it. 3. The three compartments and their muscles Table 2 — Compartments of the upper thigh Compartment Muscles Nerve (roots) Artery Notes Anterior Sartorius , rectus femoris , vastus lateralis , vastus intermedius , vastus medialis (the quadriceps; the vastus medialis becomes prominent more distally); the iliopsoas forms the lateral floor of the femoral triangle above Femoral n. (L2–L4) Lateral circumflex femoral a. and branches of the profunda femoris a. Sartorius crosses the femoral triangle as its lateral border; the rectus femoris is the most superficial muscle of the compartment. Medial Pectineus , adductor longus , adductor brevis , gracilis , adductor magnus , obturator externus Obturator n. (L2–L4); pectineus also receives a femoral branch, adductor magnus also a tibial (sciatic) branch Obturator a., deep femoral a. and its perforating branches Adductor longus is the medial border of the femoral triangle; gracilis is the most superficial and most medial muscle of the thigh. Posterior Biceps femoris (long and short heads), semitendinosus , semimembranosus Sciatic n. (L4–S3): tibial part for most of the hamstrings, common fibular part for the short head of biceps femoris Perforating branches of the profunda femoris a. The sciatic nerve lies on the adductor magnus, deep to the long head of biceps femoris; the hamstrings flex the knee and extend the hip. 4. Contents of the femoral triangle The femoral triangle — boundaries and contents Boundaries: base = inguinal ligament ; lateral = medial margin of sartorius ; medial = medial margin of adductor longus ; floor = iliopsoas laterally, pectineus medially and adductor longus inferomedially; roof = fascia lata, superficial fascia and skin. Contents, lateral to medial: femoral nerve (outside the femoral sheath) → femoral artery → femoral vein → femoral canal with lymphatics. Femoral sheath: three compartments — lateral for the artery, intermediate for the vein, medial for the femoral canal. Femoral canal and ring: the most medial compartment; its abdominal opening, the femoral ring , is bounded anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, posteriorly by the pectineus and pectineal ligament, medially by the lacunar ligament and laterally by the femoral vein. It is the weak point for a femoral hernia. Surface landmarks: the femoral artery is palpable just below the inguinal ligament, midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis; the great saphenous vein joins the femoral vein at the saphenous opening, medial to the artery. 5. Imaging of the section Axial MRI and CT are used to assess the femoral vessels, the lymph nodes and the muscles; ultrasound is the first-line method for the femoral vessels and for the great saphenous vein, and fluoroscopy with contrast is used during femoral catheterisation. Table 3 — What the structures look like on CT and MRI Structure CT MRI (T1-weighted) Femur (cortex) Dense white ring Black cortical rim Marrow Dark centre Bright fatty marrow Muscle compartments (quadriceps, adductors, hamstrings) Intermediate grey, separated by thin fascial lines Intermediate signal; fat between muscles is bright Femoral artery and vein Bright after contrast; the artery lies lateral to the vein Flow voids; bright with contrast; the vein is larger and medial Femoral nerve Soft-tissue density lateral to the artery Flattened intermediate-signal structure lateral to the artery, outside the sheath Lymph nodes in the femoral canal Small soft-tissue nodules medial to the vein Intermediate-signal nodules in the fatty canal Subcutaneous fat, great saphenous vein Dark fat, vein density medially Bright fat; dark/bright superficial vein Must-memorise Three compartments: anterior (quadriceps + sartorius, femoral n.), medial (adductors + gracilis + pectineus + obturator externus, obturator n.), posterior (hamstrings, sciatic n.). The fascia lata and its septa to the linea aspera separate them. Femoral triangle: base inguinal ligament, lateral sartorius, medial adductor longus, floor iliopsoas/pectineus/adductor longus. Contents lateral to medial: femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, femoral canal ; the nerve is outside the femoral sheath. Femoral ring: inguinal ligament anteriorly, pectineus/pectineal ligament posteriorly, lacunar ligament medially, femoral vein laterally — the site of femoral hernia. Great saphenous vein enters the femoral vein through the saphenous opening; superficial inguinal nodes lie in the superficial fascia below the inguinal ligament. Clinical / radiological correlation Femoral hernia: the bowel passes through the femoral ring into the femoral canal, producing a swelling below and lateral to the pubic tubercle; it is more common in women and has a high risk of strangulation because the ring is narrow. Vascular access: the common femoral artery and vein are punctured just below the inguinal ligament for angiography, angioplasty and central catheters; the artery is located midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis. Femoral nerve block: the nerve lies lateral to the artery and outside the femoral sheath, which is why a local anaesthetic injected lateral to the artery produces anaesthesia of the anterior thigh and of the saphenous territory. Great saphenous vein: it is used as a venous conduit for coronary and peripheral bypass grafts, and its junction with the femoral vein (the saphenofemoral junction) is a landmark in varicose vein surgery. Compartment syndrome of the thigh: after severe trauma; pain on passive stretching of the quadriceps and a tense thigh are the signs, and the femoral vessels are assessed by Doppler. Likely follow-up questions Name the boundaries of the femoral triangle. — Inguinal ligament (base), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial); floor iliopsoas, pectineus and adductor longus. Name its contents from lateral to medial. — Femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, femoral canal with lymphatics. What is the femoral sheath and which structures are inside it? — A fascial sleeve around the femoral artery, vein and canal; the femoral nerve is outside. What are the boundaries of the femoral ring? — Inguinal ligament anteriorly, pectineus and pectineal ligament posteriorly, lacunar ligament medially, femoral vein laterally. Which compartment contains the adductor longus and which nerve supplies it? — The medial compartment; the obturator nerve. Where is the sciatic nerve in the upper thigh? — Posteriorly, between the hamstrings and the adductor magnus, deep to the long head of biceps femoris."},{"id":"t-IV-8","p":"IV","n":8,"t":"Lower part of the thigh (adductor canal's level): muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section through the distal third of the thigh cuts the shaft of the femur and shows the three compartments of the thigh: an anterior compartment (the quadriceps femoris with sartorius), a medial compartment (adductor magnus, gracilis and the ending of the adductor longus) and a posterior compartment (the hamstrings with the sciatic nerve , already splitting into its tibial and common fibular divisions). The defining feature of this level is the adductor canal ( canalis adductorius ), the subsartorial space in which the femoral artery , femoral vein , saphenous nerve and nerve to vastus medialis run obliquely towards the adductor hiatus. 1. Level of the section, bones and orientation The plane is placed in the distal (lower) third of the thigh , above the knee joint. Only one bone is cut — the femur ( femur ) — and it appears as a closed ring of thick compact cortex around a medullary cavity, roughly round to oval, positioned slightly anterior to the mid-point of the section because the hamstrings bulk large behind it. The tibia, fibula and patella are not in the section at this level. A CT or MRI slice is displayed in the anatomical position as seen from below (inferior view): the top of the image is anterior, the bottom is posterior, and the right side of the image is the patient's left side. The section is asymmetrical : the anterior compartment is broad and rounded, the medial compartment forms a thick wedge on the medial side, and the posterior compartment bulges behind the femur. Because the femur is here a straight shaft without condyles, the muscles still surround the bone on all sides; this is the last level at which this is true before the popliteal fossa opens up. 2. Superficial to deep: skin, fascia, septa Skin and subcutaneous fat form a continuous ring, thinnest anteriorly and medially, thickest posterolaterally. Superficial fascia contains, on the medial side, the great saphenous vein ( v. saphena magna ) with its accompanying saphenous nerve branches; posteriorly lie the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh and small veins draining to the popliteal region. Deep fascia ( fascia lata ) is a stout ring that sends two intermuscular septa to the femur: the lateral intermuscular septum (between vastus lateralis and biceps femoris) and the medial intermuscular septum (between vastus medialis and the adductors). A third, thinner posterior septum separates the adductors from the hamstrings. These septa, together with the femur and the septa of the thigh's own fascial sheath, define the three compartments. The deep fascia is not uniformly tense: over the adductor canal it is reinforced by the sartorius muscle and by the vastoadductor membrane ( septum vastoadductorium ), which closes the canal anteriorly and is pierced by the saphenous nerve and the descending genicular artery. 3. The three compartments and their muscles Compartment Muscles present at this level Nerve (root value) Artery Note Anterior (extensor) Vastus medialis (large, anteromedial), vastus lateralis (anterolateral), vastus intermedius (directly on the anterior surface of the femur) and rectus femoris (superficial and central, becoming tendinous); sartorius crosses obliquely over the anteromedial surface Femoral n. (L2–L4); sartorius femoral n. (L2–L3) Branches of the femoral artery and of the deep artery of the thigh The quadriceps tendon begins to form here; the vastus medialis is the most medial muscle of the compartment and its lower fibres head for the patella Medial (adductor) Adductor magnus (large, deep, posteromedial, reaching the linea aspera and medial supracondylar line), gracilis (most medial and superficial), adductor longus (tendinous part only at this level); pectineus and adductor brevis lie too proximal to appear Obturator n. (L2–L4); the hamstring part of adductor magnus is supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic n. Branches of the obturator and deep arteries of the thigh; perforating arteries pierce adductor magnus The adductor magnus forms the posterior wall (floor) of the adductor canal and contains the adductor hiatus Posterior (flexor) Biceps femoris — long head lateral and superficial, short head deep and medial to it; semitendinosus (medial and superficial); semimembranosus (medial, deep to semitendinosus) Sciatic n.: tibial division (L5–S2) for semitendinosus, semimembranosus and the long head of biceps; common fibular division (L5–S2) for the short head Perforating branches of the deep artery of the thigh The sciatic nerve lies between the hamstrings, posterior to adductor magnus; at this distal level it is usually already split into tibial and common fibular nerves 4. The adductor canal: walls and contents The adductor canal (subsartorial canal, Hunter's canal) is a musculo-aponeurotic tunnel that continues the apex of the femoral triangle downwards and medially into the popliteal fossa. In the cross-section it is a small triangular space tucked between the vastus medialis and the adductors, immediately anteromedial to the femur. Wall Formed by Roof (anteromedial) Sartorius muscle reinforced by the vastoadductor membrane , which stretches between the vastus medialis and the adductor magnus Lateral wall Vastus medialis Posterior wall (floor) Adductor longus above, adductor magnus below Apex / distal opening Adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus, through which the femoral vessels leave the thigh The contents of the canal, from anterior to posterior, are: Saphenous nerve ( n. saphenus ) — crossing in front of the artery; it leaves the canal by piercing the vastoadductor membrane with the descending genicular artery, becomes subcutaneous at the medial side of the knee, and continues down the medial leg to the medial border of the foot. Nerve to vastus medialis — a branch of the femoral nerve that runs in the canal before entering the muscle. Femoral artery ( a. femoralis ) — the most important content; in the distal canal it lies anterior to the femoral vein. Femoral vein ( v. femoralis ) — behind the artery here (in the femoral triangle it was medial to it). Descending genicular artery and small nerve and lymphatic twigs. 5. What to point to on the image Structure Expected position in the section Femur Central, slightly anterior; white cortical ring, grey medullary cavity Vastus intermedius Thin muscle sheet plastered on the anterior surface of the femur Rectus femoris Most anterior muscle, superficial and central; its tendon appears as a dark (low-signal) band Vastus lateralis / medialis Large muscle masses anterolaterally and anteromedially Adductor canal Small fat-containing triangle anteromedial to the femur, between vastus medialis and adductor magnus, containing two rounded vessels (artery anterior, vein posterior) and two small nerve dots Adductor magnus Broad posteromedial muscle mass, the floor of the canal Gracilis Thin, most medial muscle, forming the medial contour of the thigh Hamstrings Posterior compartment: biceps femoris laterally, semitendinosus and semimembranosus medially Sciatic nerve, or tibial + common fibular nerves In the fat between the hamstrings and the adductor magnus, posteromedial to the femur Great saphenous vein Small rounded structure in the subcutaneous fat of the medial side Femoral artery → adductor canal → adductor hiatus → popliteal artery behind the knee. Femoral vein → adductor hiatus → popliteal vein. Must-memorise Three compartments at this level: anterior (quadriceps + sartorius, femoral n.), medial (adductors, obturator n.), posterior (hamstrings, sciatic n.). Adductor canal roof = sartorius + vastoadductor membrane; lateral wall = vastus medialis; floor = adductor longus and adductor magnus. Canal contents: femoral artery, femoral vein, saphenous nerve, nerve to vastus medialis (plus the descending genicular artery). In the canal the vein lies behind the artery and the saphenous nerve crosses in front of the artery. The saphenous nerve leaves through the vastoadductor membrane — it does not pass through the adductor hiatus. Clinical / radiological correlation Vascular access and the canal: the femoral artery is palpated and punctured in the femoral triangle, where it is superficial; this is the standard route for coronary and peripheral angiography. The adductor canal protects the vessel more distally, which is why the femoral artery is not palpable in the lower thigh. Saphenous nerve entrapment in the canal or at the vastoadductor membrane causes medial knee and leg pain; the nerve is also the target of a saphenous nerve block for medial knee surgery. Adductor canal syndrome — compression of the femoral artery in the canal, typically by the adductor magnus or vastus medialis, producing exercise-related medial thigh pain in young patients. Femoral shaft fracture at this level can tear the profunda femoris branches and, rarely, injure the femoral vessels as they pass through the canal; the thigh compartments are closed by the deep fascia, so bleeding can produce a tense, painful thigh. Sciatic nerve injury in the posterior compartment (penetrating trauma, posterior hip operations) paralyses the hamstrings and all muscles below the knee because both the tibial and common fibular divisions are involved. Likely follow-up questions Where does the adductor canal begin and end? — Begins at the apex of the femoral triangle and ends at the adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus. Why can the femoral artery not be felt in the lower thigh? — It lies deep in the adductor canal, covered by sartorius and the vastoadductor membrane. Which structures change their relative position between the femoral triangle and the adductor canal? — The femoral vein moves from medial to posterior relative to the artery, and the saphenous nerve moves to the front of the artery. What exactly passes through the adductor hiatus, and what does not? — The femoral artery and femoral vein pass through it to become the popliteal vessels; the saphenous nerve leaves the canal by piercing the vastoadductor membrane above the hiatus and does not pass through it."},{"id":"t-IV-9","p":"IV","n":9,"t":"Popliteal fossa: bones, muscular compartments, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section through the knee at the level of the popliteal fossa cuts the distal femur (condylar part) and the knee joint in front and opens behind into a fat-filled diamond bounded above by the hamstrings and below by the heads of the gastrocnemius, floored by the posterior surface of the femur, the knee capsule and the popliteus, and roofed by the deep fascia. Through this space pass, from superficial to deep, the tibial nerve , the popliteal vein and the popliteal artery , together with the common fibular nerve laterally and the small saphenous vein in the roof. 1. Level of the section, bones and orientation The plane passes through the distal femur at the level of the condyles : the section shows the medial and lateral femoral condyles as two large, rounded areas of trabecular bone joined anteriorly by the trochlear groove, with the intercondylar fossa between them posteriorly. Anteriorly the patella and the quadriceps tendon / patellar ligament are cut; the knee joint cavity and its capsule lie immediately behind the patella. A slightly more distal plane cuts the tibial plateau (medial and lateral tibial condyles) instead of the femoral condyles; the relations of the popliteal fossa are unchanged. The fibula appears only on lower planes, at the level of its head and neck, lateral to the lateral tibial condyle. Orientation: axial images are viewed from below — anterior is at the top, and the right of the image is the patient's left side. 2. Boundaries of the popliteal fossa The fossa ( fossa poplitea ) is a diamond-shaped space behind the knee joint, wider above than below. Its walls are all muscular; its floor is skeletal and capsular; its roof is fascial. Boundary Structure Superomedial wall Distal ends (tendons) of semitendinosus and semimembranosus Superolateral wall Distal end (tendon) of biceps femoris Inferomedial wall Medial head of gastrocnemius Inferolateral wall Lateral head of gastrocnemius and the plantaris Floor Posterior surface of the distal femur , the capsule of the knee joint with the oblique popliteal ligament, the posterior surface of the proximal tibia , and inferiorly the popliteus muscle Roof Deep fascia , continuous above with the fascia lata and below with the deep fascia of the leg; then superficial fascia and skin 3. Muscles and compartments cut at this level Region Muscles / tendons seen in or around the fossa wall Nerve (root value) Anterior compartment of the thigh (above, anteriorly) Quadriceps tendon being inserted into the patella; vastus medialis and lateralis fibres at the upper margin of the section Femoral n. (L2–L4) Posterior compartment of the thigh (superomedial and superolateral walls) Semitendinosus , semimembranosus , biceps femoris (its tendon also gives a slip to the fibular collateral ligament) Sciatic n. — tibial division (L5–S2) for the hamstrings, common fibular division (L5–S2) for the short head of biceps Posterior compartment of the leg, superficial group (inferior walls) Gastrocnemius medial and lateral heads, plantaris (its long slender tendon runs down between the heads) Tibial n. (S1, S2) Posterior compartment of the leg, deep group (floor) Popliteus — the fleshy floor of the lower part of the fossa Tibial n. (L4–S1) 4. Contents of the fossa and their arrangement The contents are described in two directions, and both must be reproduced: superficial to deep and medial to lateral . Structure Position in the fossa Note Tibial nerve Most superficial of the neurovascular structures; enters from above under the margin of the biceps femoris and descends vertically near the midline The larger terminal division of the sciatic nerve; leaves the fossa deep to the margin of the plantaris to enter the posterior compartment of the leg. Supplies all muscles of the posterior leg and the sole. Common fibular nerve Most lateral ; runs along the medial border of the biceps femoris tendon and leaves over the lower lateral margin of the fossa Winds round the neck of the fibula , where it is superficial and vulnerable, then divides into superficial and deep fibular nerves. Popliteal vein Between the tibial nerve and the popliteal artery; superficial to the artery Becomes the femoral vein by passing through the adductor hiatus. Popliteal artery Deepest structure; enters at the upper medial side under the semimembranosus and runs obliquely downwards and laterally, ending just lateral to the midline of the leg Continuation of the femoral artery from the adductor hiatus; gives muscular branches, sural arteries and the genicular arteries (superior medial, superior lateral, middle, inferior medial, inferior lateral), then divides into the anterior tibial and posterior tibial arteries. Small saphenous vein In the superficial fascia of the roof, ascending on the back of the leg from the lateral side of the foot Pierces the roof and joins the popliteal vein. Posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh Passes through the roof Then descends with the small saphenous vein to supply the upper half of the back of the leg. Popliteal fat and lymph nodes Fill the space around the vessels; superficial and deep groups of nodes Receive lymph from the knee and the deep tissues of the leg. Superficial → deep: tibial nerve → popliteal vein → popliteal artery. Upper fossa, medial → lateral: semimembranosus / semitendinosus → popliteal artery → popliteal vein → tibial nerve → common fibular nerve → biceps femoris. 5. What to point to on the image Structure Appearance / position Femoral condyles, patella White cortical outline with grey trabecular interior; the condyles are the largest bony structures of the section Quadriceps tendon / patellar ligament Uniform low-signal (dark) band anterior to the patella or patella-to-tibia Knee joint cavity and capsule Thin, low-signal boundary behind the patella; a small amount of fluid is bright on T2-weighted MRI Popliteal fossa fat Bright on both T1- and T2-weighted MRI, very dark on CT; outlines the vessels and nerves Popliteal artery and vein Two rounded flow-voids (dark on spin-echo MRI) or contrast-filled lumina (bright on CT angiography) in the middle of the fossa Tibial nerve Small rounded structure with a fascicular (honeycomb) internal pattern, superficial to the vessels Small saphenous vein Superficial to the deep fascia, in the subcutaneous fat of the roof Must-memorise Diamond of the fossa: superiorly semitendinosus + semimembranosus medially, biceps femoris laterally; inferiorly the two heads of gastrocnemius (+ plantaris). Floor = femur + knee capsule + tibia + popliteus; roof = deep fascia (+ skin, small saphenous vein, posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh). Contents, superficial to deep: tibial nerve → popliteal vein → popliteal artery ; the common fibular nerve is the most lateral content. The popliteal artery is the deepest structure and therefore the hardest to palpate. The common fibular nerve is superficial at the neck of the fibula — the classic site of compression injury. Clinical / radiological correlation Baker's cyst (popliteal cyst): a synovial cyst communicating with the knee joint, appearing on MRI as a fluid-filled (bright on T2) loculation between the medial head of gastrocnemius and the semimembranosus tendon — a common differential of a popliteal mass. Popliteal artery aneurysm: abnormal dilatation of the artery, often bilateral and associated with aneurysms elsewhere; it tends to thrombose and embolise to the distal leg rather than rupture. Duplex Doppler is the first-line test; it must be distinguished from a Baker's cyst. Knee dislocation and distal femoral fracture: the popliteal artery is tethered above at the adductor hiatus and below at the soleal arch, so it is injured in fracture-dislocations; the pulse may be present initially, so vascular assessment is mandatory. Common fibular nerve palsy: compression or injury at the fibular neck causes foot drop and loss of eversion (superficial fibular n.) plus loss of dorsiflexion and sensory loss in the first web space (deep fibular n.). Popliteal (Baker's) cyst rupture produces calf pain and swelling that mimics deep vein thrombosis — a classic clinical trap. Likely follow-up questions Which structure in the popliteal fossa is most superficial? — The tibial nerve; the popliteal artery is the deepest. How does the popliteal artery end? — It divides in the posterior compartment of the leg into the anterior tibial artery (which passes through the interosseous membrane) and the posterior tibial artery (which gives the fibular artery). What pierces the roof of the popliteal fossa? — The small saphenous vein and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. Why is the common fibular nerve more often injured than the tibial nerve? — It is superficial and fixed against the neck of the fibula, where it lies just under the skin and deep fascia. Which muscles form the floor of the fossa? — Popliteus is the fleshy floor; the capsule, the femur and the tibia complete it."},{"id":"t-IV-10","p":"IV","n":10,"t":"Middle part of the leg: bones, muscular compartments, muscles, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section through the middle of the leg cuts the triangular shaft of the tibia anteromedially and the slender fibula laterally, joined by the interosseous membrane ; the deep fascia and two intermuscular septa divide the limb into three compartments — anterior (extensor), lateral (fibular) and posterior (flexor), the posterior one being further split by the transverse intermuscular septum into superficial and deep parts. The anterior compartment contains the deep fibular nerve and anterior tibial vessels, the lateral compartment the superficial fibular nerve, and the posterior compartment the tibial nerve with the posterior tibial and fibular vessels. 1. Level of the section, bones and orientation The plane passes through the shafts of both leg bones. The tibia ( tibia ) is triangular in cross-section , with an anterior border, a medial border and an interosseous border, and corresponding anterior (lateral), medial and posterior surfaces; its anterior and medial borders and its whole medial surface are subcutaneous and therefore palpable through the skin — this flat medial face gives the section its characteristic medial contour. The fibula ( fibula ) is a thin bone with a small medullary cavity, positioned laterally and slightly posterior to the tibia. The interosseous membrane ( membrana interossea cruris ) stretches between the interosseous borders of the two bones and completes the separation of the anterior from the posterior compartment; it also gives origin to muscle fibres. Orientation: axial images viewed from below — anterior at the top, the patient's left on the right side of the image. The section is clearly triangular , with the apex anterior (the shin) and the base posterior (the calf). 2. Superficial to deep Skin: thin over the shin and anterior surface, thicker and more mobile posteriorly. Superficial fascia: medially contains the great saphenous vein with the saphenous nerve ; posterolaterally the small saphenous vein with the sural nerve (formed by the medial sural cutaneous branch of the tibial nerve and the communicating fibular branch). Deep fascia of the leg ( fascia cruris ): a tight sleeve that is fused to the periosteum of the anterior and medial borders of the tibia, so the anterior and posterior compartments cannot communicate across the shin. Intermuscular septa: the anterior and posterior intermuscular septa pass from the deep fascia to the fibula and separate the lateral compartment from the anterior and posterior compartments. A transverse intermuscular septum between soleus and the deep flexors separates the superficial and deep parts of the posterior compartment. The tight deep fascia and the unyielding bone are the anatomical reason why swelling inside a compartment raises tissue pressure — the basis of compartment syndrome . 3. The compartments and their muscles Compartment Muscles at this level Nerve (root value) Vessels Action of the group Anterior (extensor) Tibialis anterior (applied to the lateral surface of the tibia), extensor hallucis longus , extensor digitorum longus , and often fibularis tertius inferolaterally Deep fibular n. (L4, L5) Anterior tibial artery with its venae comitantes Dorsiflexion of the ankle, extension of the toes, inversion Lateral (fibular) Fibularis longus (more superficial, upper part) and fibularis brevis (deep to it, lower part) Superficial fibular n. (L5, S1) Muscular branches only Eversion and plantar flexion of the foot Posterior, superficial (superficial flexors) Soleus (deep, arising from the soleal line and the head and neck of the fibula), gastrocnemius (two bellies superficial to soleus), plantaris (long slender tendon) Tibial n. (S1, S2) Muscular branches; sural arteries Plantar flexion of the ankle (gastrocnemius also flexes the knee) Posterior, deep (deep flexors) Tibialis posterior (on the interosseous membrane and adjacent bones), flexor digitorum longus , flexor hallucis longus Tibial n. — tibialis posterior (L4, L5); flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus (L5–S2) Posterior tibial artery and fibular artery Plantar flexion, inversion, flexion of the toes; support of the arches 4. Where the vessels and nerves lie Deep fibular nerve and anterior tibial artery lie in the anterior compartment , on (or just lateral to) the interosseous membrane, between the tibialis anterior medially and the extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus laterally. The nerve is lateral to the artery. Superficial fibular nerve runs down the lateral compartment between the two fibular muscles, then pierces the deep fascia in the lower leg to become cutaneous. Tibial nerve descends in the deep posterior compartment , between the soleus (superficial to it) and the tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus (deep to it); it lies just lateral to the posterior tibial artery. Posterior tibial artery runs on the superficial surfaces of the tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus, medial to the tibial nerve, and reaches the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus. Fibular (peroneal) artery arises from the posterior tibial artery and descends laterally along the posterior surface of the fibula, in the ridge that separates the tibialis posterior from the flexor hallucis longus; it supplies both compartments and gives perforating branches to the lateral and anterior compartments. Great saphenous vein and saphenous nerve lie in the superficial fascia on the medial side; the small saphenous vein and sural nerve lie posteriorly in the superficial fascia, superficial to the gastrocnemius. 5. What to point to on the image Structure Appearance / position on the slice Tibia Large triangular bone at the anteromedial corner; thick cortex, grey marrow; its medial and anterior faces lie right under the skin Fibula Small round bone at the lateral side, slightly posterior Interosseous membrane Fine low-signal line joining the two bones, separating anterior from deep posterior compartment Tibialis anterior Muscle immediately lateral to the tibia, the most anterior muscle of the leg Anterior compartment Triangular space between tibia, interosseous membrane and the anterior septum; contains the anterior tibial vessels and deep fibular nerve near the membrane Lateral compartment Small space between fibula, anterior septum and posterior septum, with the two fibular muscles Soleus and gastrocnemius Large muscle masses filling the posterior half of the section; the two gastrocnemius bellies are superficial, the soleus lies deep to them Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus Deep muscles applied to the posterior surfaces of the tibia, interosseous membrane and fibula Posterior tibial and fibular vessels Small rounded flow-voids in the deep posterior compartment fat, with the tibial nerve beside them Great and small saphenous veins Punctate structures in the superficial fat, medial and posterior respectively Must-memorise Three compartments, four muscle groups: anterior (deep fibular n.), lateral (superficial fibular n.), posterior superficial and posterior deep (tibial n.). Anterior compartment: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius — all deep fibular n. (L4, L5) . Lateral compartment: fibularis longus and brevis — superficial fibular n. (L5, S1) . Posterior superficial: gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris — tibial n. (S1, S2) ; deep: tibialis posterior (L4, L5), flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus (L5–S2). The tibia is subcutaneous on its medial surface — hence the shin is a site of direct trauma and of open fractures. Clinical / radiological correlation Compartment syndrome of the leg: after tibial fracture, crush injury or prolonged exercise, bleeding or oedema raises the pressure inside a closed compartment; the anterior compartment is most often affected and the deep fibular nerve is the most sensitive structure, so loss of dorsiflexion and of sensation in the first web space are early signs. Pain on passive stretch is the cardinal sign; the deep fibular nerve and anterior tibial artery are at risk of irreversible damage. Deep fibular nerve lesion: foot drop with steppage gait and sensory loss in the first web space. Superficial fibular nerve lesion: loss of eversion; inversion injuries may stretch it over the fibula. Tibial fracture: the tibia is subcutaneous, so a fracture may be open; the fibula, intact, acts as a splint and may delay displacement. Deep vein thrombosis: the soleal and gastrocnemius veins are the commonest origin of calf DVT; on ultrasound the deep fascia and septa are the landmarks separating the compartments. Fibular artery perforators: the fibular (peroneal) artery is the usual pedicle of the fibular free flap used in reconstructive surgery. Likely follow-up questions Which structures pass through the interosseous membrane? — The anterior tibial artery passes through its upper part; the perforating branch of the fibular artery may pass near its lower part; the deep fibular nerve does not cross it (it enters the anterior compartment behind the fibula). Why does the leg have a lateral compartment separate from the anterior one? — The anterior and posterior intermuscular septa run from the deep fascia to the fibula, isolating the fibular muscles. What separates the superficial from the deep posterior compartment? — The transverse intermuscular septum, with the deep transverse fascia. Where is the posterior tibial artery palpable? — Behind the medial malleolus, in the tarsal tunnel, midway between the medial malleolus and the calcaneus."},{"id":"t-IV-11","p":"IV","n":11,"t":"Foot (tarsal sinus' level): bones, muscles, tendinous sheaths, tendons, vessels and nerves (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section through the foot at the level of the tarsal sinus ( sinus tarsi ) cuts the talus above and the calcaneus below, with the fat-filled sinus between them containing the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament ; the navicular and cuboid lie immediately in front. Dorsally the long extensor tendons with their synovial sheaths and the dorsalis pedis artery with the deep fibular nerve are seen; medially and plantarly the tendons of tibialis posterior , flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus enter the sole through the tarsal tunnel with the posterior tibial vessels and tibial nerve ; laterally the fibularis longus and brevis tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus. 1. Level of the section and the bones cut The plane passes through the talocalcaneal region . The talus ( talus ) is cut through its body and neck, and the calcaneus ( calcaneus ) through its body and anterior part including the sustentaculum tali medially. Between the two bones, and opening on the lateral side of the foot, is the tarsal sinus — formed by the calcaneal sulcus on the superior surface of the calcaneus and the sulcus tali on the inferior surface of the talus. It is a wide gap filled with fat and fibrous tissue that is visible when the skeleton is viewed from the lateral side. The interosseous talocalcaneal ligament lies inside the sinus and is a major stabiliser of the subtalar joint; the bifurcate (calcaneonavicular) ligament lies just in front of it. Immediately anterior to this plane lie the navicular (medially, boat-shaped, with a prominent tuberosity for the tibialis posterior tendon) and the cuboid (laterally, with a groove for the fibularis longus tendon); on slightly proximal planes the medial and lateral malleoli of the tibia and fibula are cut. Above the section, the ankle joint; below it, the sole. Orientation as for all axial images: anterior (dorsum) at the top, the patient's left on the right of the picture. 2. Superficial to deep, and dorsal versus plantar Dorsum: thin skin and loose subcutaneous tissue, then the inferior extensor retinaculum holding down the long extensor tendons in their synovial sheaths, then the extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis muscles lying on the lateral part of the calcaneus and the cuboid. Plantar surface: thick keratinised skin firmly bound to the plantar aponeurosis (a thickening of the deep fascia anchored to the medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity), then the first layer of intrinsic muscles — abductor hallucis medially, flexor digitorum brevis centrally, abductor digiti minimi laterally. Medial side: the flexor retinaculum completes the tarsal tunnel over the groove between the medial malleolus, the talus and the calcaneus; septa from the retinaculum divide the tunnel into separate channels for each tendon and for the vessels and nerve. Lateral side: the fibularis longus and brevis tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus, held by the superior and inferior fibular retinacula , and reach the lateral side of the calcaneus and the cuboid. 3. Muscles and tendons crossing this level Group Tendon / muscle and where it lies Muscle action Nerve (root value) Anterior compartment of the leg (dorsal) Tibialis anterior tendon (medial, inserting on the medial cuneiform and base of metatarsal I); extensor hallucis longus (central); extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius (lateral) Dorsiflexion; inversion (tibialis anterior) or eversion (fibularis tertius); extension of the toes Deep fibular n. (L4, L5) Posterior compartment, deep (medial and plantar) Tibialis posterior tendon (most medial, on the tuberosity of the navicular); flexor digitorum longus ; flexor hallucis longus (most lateral, in the groove on the talus and under the sustentaculum tali) Plantar flexion and inversion; flexion of the toes; arch support Tibial n.: tibialis posterior (L4, L5); flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus (L5–S2) Lateral compartment of the leg Fibularis longus and fibularis brevis tendons posterior to the lateral malleolus Eversion and plantar flexion Superficial fibular n. (L5, S1) Dorsum of the foot (intrinsic) Extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis on the lateral calcaneus and cuboid Extension of the toes Deep fibular n. (L5, S1) Sole, first layer (intrinsic) Abductor hallucis (medial), flexor digitorum brevis (central), abductor digiti minimi (lateral) Flexion and abduction of the toes; support of the arches Medial plantar n. (S1, S2) for abductor hallucis and flexor digitorum brevis; lateral plantar n. (S1–S3) for abductor digiti minimi Sole, second layer (intrinsic) Quadratus plantae and the four lumbricals attached to the flexor digitorum longus tendons Redirect and augment toe flexion Lateral plantar n. (S1–S3) for quadratus plantae; first lumbrical medial plantar n. (S2, S3); others lateral plantar n. 4. Tendinous (synovial) sheaths of the foot Where tendons change direction they are wrapped in synovial sheaths and held by retinacula. At the tarsal-sinus level the following sheaths are cut or lie just distal to the plane, and each must be named: Tendon Sheath and retinaculum Note Tibialis anterior Synovial sheath deep to the superior and inferior extensor retinacula Most medial of the dorsal tendons Extensor hallucis longus Its own sheath deep to the extensor retinacula Dorsalis pedis artery lies lateral to it Extensor digitorum longus (+ fibularis tertius ) Common sheath deep to the extensor retinacula Divides into the four tendon slips on the dorsum Fibularis longus and fibularis brevis Common synovial sheath behind the lateral malleolus, deep to the superior and inferior fibular retinacula The longus tendon then grooves the cuboid and crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and metatarsal I Tibialis posterior Sheath in the tarsal tunnel , in a compartment of its own behind the medial malleolus Inserts on the navicular tuberosity and adjacent bones Flexor digitorum longus Sheath in the tarsal tunnel, medial to the vessels Its tendons become the core of the digital flexor apparatus Flexor hallucis longus Long sheath in the tarsal tunnel, in the groove on the posterior talus and the undersurface of the sustentaculum tali; continues into the sole Most lateral tendon of the tunnel, next to the tibial nerve Flexor tendons of the toes Fibrous digital sheaths from the metatarsophalangeal joints to the distal phalanges, lined by synovial sheaths Hold the tendons to the bone and prevent bowing when the toes flex 5. Vessels and nerves at this level Dorsalis pedis artery ( a. dorsalis pedis ) — continuation of the anterior tibial artery, on the dorsum between the extensor hallucis longus tendon (medial to it) and the extensor digitorum longus (lateral), with the deep fibular nerve just lateral to it. Posterior tibial artery and venae comitantes, with the tibial nerve , passing through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus; the artery divides into medial and lateral plantar arteries , and the nerve into medial and lateral plantar nerves , as they enter the sole. Medial plantar nerve and artery run in the sole between the abductor hallucis and the flexor digitorum brevis; the lateral plantar nerve and artery run between the flexor digitorum brevis and the quadratus plantae towards the lateral side. Sural nerve and small saphenous vein posteriorly and laterally; saphenous nerve and great saphenous vein on the medial side of the dorsum. Calcaneal branches of the tibial and sural nerves supply the heel skin. 6. What to point to on the image Structure Appearance / position on the slice Talus Large bone mass in the upper central part, with its articular surfaces; cortex white, interior spongy grey Calcaneus Large bone below and lateral to the talus, with the sustentaculum tali projecting medially Tarsal sinus Fat-filled (dark on CT, bright on T1/T2 MRI) gap between talus and calcaneus, with the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament as a low-signal band across it Navicular and cuboid Bones at the anterior edge of the section, medial and lateral respectively Extensor tendons and sheaths Small dark (low-signal) rounded structures on the dorsum, each surrounded by a thin layer of bright synovial fluid Flexor tendons in the tarsal tunnel Three round low-signal tendons on the posteromedial aspect, with the posterior tibial artery and the tibial nerve between them Plantar muscles and aponeurosis Muscle compartment below the bones; the plantar aponeurosis is a thick low-signal band on the plantar surface Dorsalis pedis artery Small bright (contrast CT angiography) or flow-void structure on the dorsum, lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon Must-memorise Tarsal sinus = calcaneal sulcus + sulcus tali; contains fat and the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament ; opens laterally. Tendon order in the tarsal tunnel from medial to lateral: tibialis posterior → flexor digitorum longus → posterior tibial artery and tibial nerve → flexor hallucis longus. Tarsal tunnel contents: three flexor tendons + posterior tibial artery + tibial nerve; the roof is the flexor retinaculum . Dorsalis pedis artery lies lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon, with the deep fibular nerve lateral to the artery. Medial plantar n. (S1, S2) supplies abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis (medial head) and the first lumbrical; lateral plantar n. (S1–S3) supplies the rest of the sole. Clinical / radiological correlation Tarsal tunnel syndrome: compression of the tibial nerve under the flexor retinaculum (flat foot, ganglia, tenosynovitis, fracture) causes burning pain and tingling of the sole with sparing of the heel skin (which is supplied by calcaneal branches given off before the tunnel) — a useful localising sign. Sinus tarsi syndrome: post-traumatic pain over the lateral side of the hindfoot with instability; MRI shows ill-defined high signal in the fat of the tarsal sinus and thickening of the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament. Tarsal coalition: congenital fibrous, cartilaginous or bony union (talocalcaneal or calcaneonavicular) presenting in adolescence with a rigid flat foot; the tarsal sinus and the region just in front of it are the sites to examine on CT and MRI. Plantar fasciitis: degeneration of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal attachment — the commonest cause of heel pain; the aponeurosis is thick and low-signal on MRI. Subtalar joint and inversion injuries: the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament is part of the complex that is strained in a severe inversion sprain; the anterior talofibular and calcaneofibular ligaments are injured first. Dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable on the dorsum between the extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus tendons, and is used to assess the distal arterial supply of the foot. Likely follow-up questions What forms the tarsal sinus, and what lies inside it? — The calcaneal sulcus and the sulcus tali; fat and the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament. Name the contents of the tarsal tunnel in order. — Tendon of tibialis posterior, tendon of flexor digitorum longus, posterior tibial artery and veins, tibial nerve, tendon of flexor hallucis longus. Which tendons have synovial sheaths at the ankle? — All the long tendons that pass under a retinaculum: the three extensor tendons, the two fibular tendons, and the three flexor tendons; the sheaths allow the tendons to glide without friction. Which nerve supplies most of the sole? — The lateral plantar nerve (S1–S3); the medial plantar nerve supplies the medial side and the first lumbrical. How do the arches of the foot relate to this section? — The section is at the summit of the medial longitudinal arch (talus, calcaneus, navicular); the arch is maintained by the interosseous and plantar ligaments, the plantar aponeurosis and the tibialis posterior tendon."},{"id":"t-IV-12","p":"IV","n":12,"t":"Trunk (level of the primary branches of the aortic arch); (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer This is an axial CT level through the upper thorax in the superior mediastinum , at the height of the third thoracic vertebra, where the three great arteries of the arch — the brachiocephalic trunk , the left common carotid artery and the left subclavian artery — lie side by side in front of the trachea. In front of them are the thymus and the two brachiocephalic veins (the left crossing the midline), behind them the trachea , the oesophagus and the thoracic duct , with the phrenic and vagus nerves in the fat at the sides. 1. Level of the section and what the plane cuts The plane lies in the superior mediastinum , whose inferior boundary is the transverse plane from the sternal angle to the intervertebral disc between TIV and TV ; above, it opens obliquely into the neck at the thoracic inlet. Bone cut: the manubrium of the sternum ; alongside it the medial ends of the clavicles and the first ribs , with the second and third ribs cut further back, and posteriorly the body of the third thoracic vertebra with the spinal cord in its canal. Lateral to the mediastinum the section cuts the superior lobes of both lungs , covered by mediastinal pleura. The thymus occupies the retrosternal space. It is large in the child, atrophies after puberty and is largely replaced by fat in the adult, so on an adult CT it appears as a fatty retrosternal triangle rather than a gland. 2. Order of structures from anterior to posterior Skin and subcutaneous tissue of the anterior chest wall. Manubrium of the sternum (and the clavicles and costal cartilages at the sides). Thymus (or its fatty remnant) in the retrosternal space. Right and left brachiocephalic veins — the left brachiocephalic vein crosses the midline in front of all three arterial branches. Brachiocephalic trunk , then left common carotid artery , then left subclavian artery — from right to left in one row; at their origin the brachiocephalic trunk is slightly the most anterior. Trachea — midline, immediately behind the arterial row. Oesophagus — behind the trachea. Thoracic duct — posterior to the oesophagus, close to its left border, between the oesophagus and the left mediastinal pleura. Body of vertebra TIII , with the spinal cord behind it in the vertebral canal. 3. Structures seen at this level Structure Where it lies in the section Key relation / note Manubrium of sternum Midline, most anterior Its posterior surface is the anterior wall of the superior mediastinum Thymus Retrosternal fat/soft tissue between sternum and the great veins Most anterior component of the superior mediastinum; ectopic parathyroid tissue may be found in it Right brachiocephalic vein Right of the midline, anterior to the brachiocephalic trunk Formed by the internal jugular and subclavian veins; joins the left brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC Left brachiocephalic vein Crossing the midline obliquely, anterior to all three branches The key landmark of this level: it lies in front of the arterial row and separates that row from the thymus Brachiocephalic trunk Right of the midline, in front of the trachea Largest of the three; divides at the upper edge of the right sternoclavicular joint into right common carotid and right subclavian arteries Left common carotid artery Left of the brachiocephalic trunk, in front of the trachea Arises to the left and slightly posterior to the brachiocephalic trunk Left subclavian artery Leftmost of the three arteries Arises to the left of and slightly posterior to the left common carotid artery Trachea Midline, behind the arteries Air-filled lumen, thin wall; palpable in the jugular notch Oesophagus Midline, behind the trachea, immediately in front of the vertebral column Collapsed soft-tissue tube; its position shifts with swallowing Thoracic duct Posterior to the oesophagus, slightly left of the midline Ascends behind the arch of the aorta and the initial part of the left subclavian artery Right phrenic nerve Lateral and slightly posterior to the beginning of the right brachiocephalic vein, and lateral to the right vagus Descends along the lateral aspect of the SVC; roots C3–C5 Right vagus nerve Between the right brachiocephalic vein and the brachiocephalic trunk Crosses the lateral surface of the trachea to reach the oesophagus Left phrenic nerve Lateral to the left vagus nerve Lies lateral and slightly posterior to the beginning of the left brachiocephalic vein, then crosses the left side of the arch Left vagus nerve Between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, behind the left brachiocephalic vein Crosses the left side of the arch and gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve Left recurrent laryngeal nerve Between the trachea and the oesophagus on the left Ascends in the tracheo-oesophageal groove to the larynx Sympathetic trunks Posterolateral, on the heads of the ribs beside the vertebrae Part of the autonomic chain, not part of the mediastinum proper Superior lobes of the lungs Lateral to the mediastinum on both sides Air-filled (black) with visible vessels 4. How the CT looks Structure Appearance on an axial CT Bone (sternum, vertebra, ribs) High attenuation (white) cortex with a grey trabecular interior Lung Very low attenuation (black), with thin white branching vessels Mediastinal fat Low attenuation, dark grey; outlines every vessel and nerve dot Muscle Intermediate soft-tissue density, similar to the vessels on a non-contrast scan Aorta and its branches, veins Intermediate on a non-contrast scan; bright after intravenous contrast, so the three arteries and the veins are easily separated Trachea and bronchi Air-filled black lumen with a thin soft-tissue wall Oesophagus Collapsed soft-tissue ring, may contain a little air or fluid Thymus Soft-tissue density in the child; fatty (dark) in the adult Nerves and thoracic duct Not separately resolved on routine CT; seen as small dots in the fat on high-resolution MRI Must-memorise Level: superior mediastinum, vertebra TIII ; cut bone = manubrium of the sternum. Anterior to posterior: sternum → thymus → brachiocephalic veins → brachiocephalic trunk + left common carotid + left subclavian → trachea → oesophagus → thoracic duct → vertebra TIII . The three arteries lie in one row, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian . All three are crossed anteriorly by the left brachiocephalic vein . The thoracic duct is posterior to the oesophagus, slightly to the left . Clinical / radiological correlation Central venous catheterisation: the internal jugular and subclavian veins are punctured here and the catheter passes through the brachiocephalic veins into the distal SVC or right atrium, so the venepuncture is guided by exactly this level's anatomy. Tracheal compression: enlargement of the thyroid or a mediastinal mass at this level narrows the trachea, which is why the section is used to assess goitre and superior mediastinal masses. Thymic and parathyroid tissue: the thymus develops from the third pharyngeal pouch, so ectopic parathyroid glands may be found within it, sometimes in the superior mediastinum. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: its position between the trachea and oesophagus explains hoarseness in apical lung tumours and mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and its paralysis is visible on CT as a widened vocal cord. Coarctation of the aorta lies just distal to the left subclavian artery, i.e. immediately below this level; rib notching and a collateral circulation may be seen on the chest radiograph. Lymphadenopathy at this level (paratracheal nodes) is a common site of spread from lung cancer and is assessed on this slice. Likely follow-up questions What are the three primary branches of the aortic arch, in order? — Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, left subclavian artery. Which structure lies immediately in front of the three branches? — The left brachiocephalic vein (with the thymus and manubrium in front of that). Where does the superior mediastinum end? — At the plane from the sternal angle to the intervertebral disc between TIV and TV. Why is the left brachiocephalic vein at risk in infants? — In infants and young children it may rise above the superior border of the manubrium and is therefore less protected. What is the relation of the phrenic nerves to the mediastinal pleura? — They run immediately deep to the mediastinal pleura, between it and the fibrous pericardium, which is why they are at risk in pleural and pericardial disease."},{"id":"t-IV-13","p":"IV","n":13,"t":"Trunk (aortic arch's level); (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer This axial CT level passes through the arch of the aorta in the superior mediastinum at the height of the fourth thoracic vertebra. The arch occupies the middle of the section, with the superior vena cava on its right and the trachea and the oesophagus behind it; the thymus lies in front, the arch of the azygos vein is at the right of the trachea, and the thoracic duct passes behind the oesophagus close to its left border. The phrenic and vagus nerves accompany the lateral aspect of the arch. 1. Level of the section and what the plane cuts The plane is in the superior mediastinum at the level of the fourth thoracic vertebra — the level at which the arch of the aorta ends on the left side (the arch ends at vertebral level TIV/V ). Bone cut: the manubrium of the sternum anteriorly, the bodies of the upper thoracic vertebrae posteriorly, ribs laterally. Lung: the superior lobes on both sides, with the mediastinal pleura applied to the mediastinum. At this level the left brachiocephalic vein has already joined the right one, so the superior vena cava has formed on the right, while the arterial branches have already arisen from the top of the arch higher up — so here we see the arch itself , not its three branches. The thymus (or its fatty remnant, and the lower part that extends over the pericardial sac) lies anteriorly. 2. Order of structures from anterior to posterior Skin and subcutaneous tissue , then the manubrium of the sternum . Thymus in the retrosternal space. Arch of the aorta — the large central structure, curving up, back and to the left; the superior vena cava lies to its right. Trachea — behind the arch, in the midline. Oesophagus — behind the trachea, immediately in front of the vertebral column. Thoracic duct — posterior to the arch of the aorta, behind the oesophagus, slightly to the left. Body of vertebra TIV , with the spinal cord in the vertebral canal behind it. Two structures are best described as lying at the sides of this anterior-to-posterior sequence: the arch of the azygos vein , which comes forward over the root of the right lung to join the superior vena cava on the right, and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve , which hooks under the arch on the left and ascends in the groove between the trachea and oesophagus. 3. Structures seen at this level Structure Where it lies in the section Key relation / note Manubrium of sternum Midline, anterior Anterior boundary of the mediastinum; the thymus is between it and the arch Thymus Retrosternal, anterior to the arch Atrophies after puberty; still identifiable as fatty lobules in the adult Arch of the aorta Central, crossing the section obliquely; begins anterior and ends posterior on the left Courses upward, backward and to the left; it is initially anterior and finally lateral to the trachea Superior vena cava Right of the arch, anterolateral to the trachea Its lower half is inside the pericardial sac; it receives the azygos vein just before entering the pericardium Trachea Midline, behind the arch Air-filled; the arch crosses its left side Oesophagus Behind the trachea, in front of the vertebral body The arch of the aorta crosses it, producing one of its four normal constrictions Arch of the azygos vein On the right, lateral to the trachea and the oesophagus Arches over the root of the right lung to join the SVC; it crosses the right vagus nerve just before that nerve reaches the oesophagus Thoracic duct Posterior to the oesophagus, slightly left of midline Continues through the superior mediastinum behind the arch of the aorta and the initial part of the left subclavian artery Left vagus nerve On the left side of the arch Crosses the left side of the arch, deep to the mediastinal pleura, and gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve at the inferior margin of the arch, just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum Left recurrent laryngeal nerve Hooking under the arch on the left, then ascending between trachea and oesophagus Visible as a small nodule in the aortopulmonary window; compression here causes hoarseness Right vagus nerve Right of the trachea Crosses the lateral surface of the trachea and passes behind the root of the right lung Phrenic nerves Right phrenic lateral to the SVC; left phrenic lateral to the left vagus, crossing the left side of the arch Both lie within the fibrous pericardium in the middle mediastinum; roots C3–C5 Left superior intercostal vein On the left side of the arch, between the left vagus (medial to it) and the left phrenic (lateral to it) Drains the second and third posterior intercostal veins into the left brachiocephalic vein Ligamentum arteriosum Between the pulmonary trunk and the concavity of the arch, just distal to the left subclavian artery Remnant of the ductus arteriosus; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks beneath it Superior lobes of the lungs Lateral on both sides Air-filled (black) with vessels 4. Relations that must be recited The arch is anterior and then lateral to the trachea as it curves backwards; it passes over the left main bronchus and ends on the left side of vertebra TIV/V as the descending aorta. The arch reaches as high as the mid-level of the manubrium of the sternum. In front of the arch: thymus, manubrium, and (higher up) the left brachiocephalic vein. Behind the arch: trachea, oesophagus, thoracic duct, deep cardiac plexus and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. To the right of the arch: superior vena cava and the right vagus; the right pulmonary artery passes below the arch. To the left of the arch: left phrenic nerve, left vagus, left superior intercostal vein and the mediastinal pleura. Below the arch: the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk, the left main bronchus, the ligamentum arteriosum and the left atrium. 5. How the CT looks Structure Appearance on an axial CT Arch of the aorta Large contrast-filled (bright) ring or ellipse, depending on the exact plane; the curve of the arch means it may appear as an elongated band rather than a circle Superior vena cava Round bright structure to the right of the arch Trachea Air-filled black circle behind the arch, with a thin wall Oesophagus Small collapsed soft-tissue ring in front of the vertebral body, often with a little air Azygos arch Small bright dot to the right of the trachea; the arch itself is best seen on slightly lower or coronal reformats Thymus Soft-tissue triangle behind the manubrium in the young; fat density in the adult Nerves and thoracic duct Not individually resolvable on routine CT; small soft-tissue dots within the mediastinal fat on high-resolution MRI Lung and pleura Black lung with a hair-thin pleural line against the mediastinum Must-memorise Level: superior mediastinum, vertebra TIV ; the arch ends at the TIV/V level on the left. Anterior to posterior: sternum → thymus → arch of aorta (+ SVC on its right) → trachea → oesophagus → thoracic duct → vertebra TIV . The SVC is on the right of the arch; the azygos arch is at the right of the trachea. The left vagus crosses the left side of the arch, giving the left recurrent laryngeal nerve under the arch just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum. The left phrenic is lateral to the left vagus and passes over the left surface of the arch. Clinical / radiological correlation Aortic dissection: an intimal flap in the arch divides a true and a false lumen (seen as a linear filling defect on contrast CT); involvement of the arch threatens the coronary and cerebral arteries. Coarctation of the aorta lies just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery, that is, at the very beginning of the descending aorta immediately below this level. Aortopulmonary window: the space between the aortic arch and the pulmonary artery contains the left recurrent laryngeal nerve; lymph node enlargement there (for example from lung cancer) compresses the nerve and causes left vocal cord palsy and hoarseness. Right-sided aortic arch: the arch passes to the right of the trachea and over the right main bronchus; it may be asymptomatic or associated with congenital heart disease and an aberrant left subclavian artery. Aberrant right subclavian artery: arises from the distal arch and passes behind the oesophagus, forming a vascular ring that can cause dysphagia. Aortic trauma: deceleration injuries occur at the fixed points of the aorta — the aortic valve, the ligamentum arteriosum and the diaphragmatic hiatus — so the region just below the arch is a classic site of traumatic rupture. Likely follow-up questions What is in front of, behind, above and below the arch? — In front: thymus and manubrium; behind: trachea, oesophagus, thoracic duct and deep cardiac plexus; above: the three primary branches; below: the pulmonary trunk bifurcation and the left main bronchus. Which nerve hooks under the arch, and which under the right subclavian artery? — The left recurrent laryngeal nerve under the arch; the right recurrent laryngeal nerve under the right subclavian artery. Where does the SVC begin and end? — Begins behind the lower edge of the right first costal cartilage (junction of the brachiocephalic veins) and ends at the lower edge of the right third costal cartilage, where it enters the right atrium. How many constrictions has the oesophagus, and which of them is at this level? — Four; the second is where the arch of the aorta crosses it in the superior mediastinum."},{"id":"t-IV-14","p":"IV","n":14,"t":"Trunk (pulmonary trunk's level); (one cross-section).","u":"part-4.html","x":"Core answer This axial CT level passes through the pulmonary trunk just below the aortic arch, at the upper border of the middle mediastinum. Anteriorly the right ventricular outflow tract continues into the pulmonary trunk, which lies in front of and to the left of the ascending aorta ; the superior vena cava is on the right of the ascending aorta, and behind them lie the tracheal bifurcation (or the two main bronchi), the left atrium and the oesophagus , with the descending thoracic aorta on the left and the azygos vein on the right of the vertebral column. 1. Level of the section and what the plane cuts The plane lies in the middle mediastinum , immediately below the plane of the aortic arch, at the level at which the pulmonary trunk is still a single vessel before it divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries (the division occurs lower, just inferior to the level of the intervertebral disc between TIV and TV ). The ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk are both inside the pericardial sac , wrapped in a common visceral layer of serous pericardium; the transverse pericardial sinus lies between the arterial reflection anteriorly and the venous reflection (SVC and pulmonary veins) posteriorly. Anteriorly the section cuts the body of the sternum and the costal cartilages; the lower pole of the thymus may still be present over the pericardium. Posteriorly the section cuts the bodies of the upper thoracic vertebrae (about TIV–TV ), the oesophagus , the descending thoracic aorta and the azygos vein . At this plane the trachea is at or just above its bifurcation, so the section may show the carina or the right and left main bronchi ; the left atrium and the entrance of the pulmonary veins lie behind the aortic root. 2. Order of structures from anterior to posterior Skin, subcutaneous tissue and the body of the sternum (with costal cartilages at the sides). Lower pole of the thymus and the fibrous pericardium over the heart. Right ventricular outflow tract ( conus arteriosus ) — the most anterior cardiovascular structure, giving rise to the pulmonary trunk. Pulmonary trunk , anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta; the ascending aorta lies behind it and to its right. Superior vena cava — to the right of the ascending aorta, its lower half inside the pericardial sac. Tracheal bifurcation / right and left main bronchi — immediately behind the great arteries. Left atrium with the entry of the pulmonary veins , behind the aortic root; the oblique pericardial sinus lies behind the atrium. Oesophagus — behind the left atrium, directly in front of the vertebral column. Descending thoracic aorta on the left and azygos vein on the right, both anterolateral to the vertebral column; the thoracic duct between the oesophagus and the vertebral column, slightly to the left. Body of the vertebra (about TIV–TV) with the spinal cord behind it. 3. Structures seen at this level Structure Where it lies in the section Key relation / note Right ventricular outflow tract Most anterior cardiac structure, just behind the sternum and pericardium Gives rise to the pulmonary trunk at the pulmonary orifice, which lies slightly anterior to the aortic orifice Pulmonary trunk Anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta Ascends posteriorly and to the left; divides into right and left pulmonary arteries below this level; joined to the arch by the ligamentum arteriosum Ascending aorta Behind and to the right of the pulmonary trunk Begins at the aortic orifice at the base of the left ventricle and becomes the arch at the level of the second right costal cartilage Superior vena cava Right of the ascending aorta, entering the right atrium below Receives the azygos vein just before entering the pericardial sac Right pulmonary artery Passing to the right, behind the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava Its course behind these two vessels is a favourite examination point Left pulmonary artery Passing to the left, below the arch of the aorta and in front of the descending aorta Connected to the arch by the ligamentum arteriosum Trachea / carina / main bronchi Midline, behind the great arteries The trachea divides at or just below the sternal-angle-to-TIV/V plane; the left main bronchus passes below the arch Left atrium and pulmonary veins Posterior to the aortic root, in the midline Forms the posterior surface of the heart; the oblique pericardial sinus is behind it Oesophagus Behind the left atrium, in front of the vertebrae Compressed against the left main bronchus and then by the left atrium Descending thoracic aorta Left of the vertebral bodies Begins at the end of the arch on the left side at the TIV/V level Azygos vein Right of the vertebral bodies, behind the oesophagus Ascends to arch over the root of the right lung and join the SVC Thoracic duct Between the oesophagus and the vertebral column, slightly left of midline Crosses to the left side at about the TIV/V level Phrenic nerves Right phrenic lateral to the SVC; left phrenic lateral to the left pulmonary artery and left vagus Both lie within the fibrous pericardium and continue to the diaphragm Vagus nerves Right vagus behind the root of the right lung; left vagus behind the root of the left lung Both reach the oesophagus and form the oesophageal plexus Thymus Anterior to the pericardium, behind the sternum The lower pole of the thymus normally extends over the pericardial sac into the anterior mediastinum 4. Relations that must be recited Pulmonary trunk and ascending aorta are enclosed together in a common sheath of visceral serous pericardium — the reason the transverse pericardial sinus can be entered between them and the atria. The pulmonary trunk is anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta; the right pulmonary artery then passes behind the ascending aorta and the SVC, while the left pulmonary artery passes below the arch and in front of the descending aorta. The trachea divides at or just below the plane from the sternal angle to the intervertebral disc TIV/V; the oesophagus continues into the posterior mediastinum. The descending aorta lies to the left of the vertebral column and the azygos vein to its right; the thoracic duct lies between the oesophagus and the vertebrae. The left atrium is the most posterior chamber of the heart and lies directly in front of the oesophagus — which is why left atrial enlargement may compress the oesophagus and cause dysphagia. 5. How the CT looks Structure Appearance on an axial CT Pulmonary trunk and ascending aorta Two adjacent contrast-filled bright circles or ellipses in the middle of the section; the trunk is anterior and to the left, the aorta behind and to the right Right ventricular outflow tract Anterior, muscular-walled chamber continuous with the pulmonary trunk, immediately behind the sternum Superior vena cava Bright round structure to the right of the ascending aorta Main bronchi Two air-filled black circles (or the carina as a ridge) behind the great arteries Left atrium Broad soft-tissue chamber behind the aortic root, outlined by contrast in the pulmonary veins Oesophagus Small collapsed ring in front of the vertebral body, behind the atrium Descending aorta and azygos vein Contrast-filled circles: aorta on the left of the vertebrae, azygos on the right Pericardium Thin low-attenuation line around the heart and the roots of the great vessels; a small pericardial effusion appears as fluid density in the sac Lung and pleura Black lung with a thin pleural line; the heart border is the mediastinal pleura Must-memorise Level: middle mediastinum , through the pulmonary trunk just below the aortic arch. Anterior to posterior: sternum → thymus → right ventricular outflow tract → pulmonary trunk + ascending aorta → SVC (right) → carina / main bronchi → left atrium → oesophagus → descending aorta (left) / azygos vein (right) → vertebra . The pulmonary trunk is anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta; the right pulmonary artery passes behind the ascending aorta and SVC. The left atrium is the most posterior cardiac chamber, directly in front of the oesophagus. The transverse pericardial sinus lies between the arterial reflection (aorta and pulmonary trunk) and the venous reflection (SVC and pulmonary veins). Clinical / radiological correlation Pulmonary embolism: the pulmonary trunk and the main pulmonary arteries are the structures examined on a contrast-enhanced CT pulmonary angiogram; a filling defect in a contrast-filled artery is the diagnostic sign. Pericardial effusion and tamponade: fluid collects in the pericardial cavity, which is visible around the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk on this slice; the fibrous pericardium limits acute distension. Left atrial enlargement: the atrium is directly in front of the oesophagus, so enlargement may compress it (dysphagia) and is also the anatomical basis of the oblique and transverse pericardial sinuses used in cardiac surgery. Cardiac catheterisation and central lines: a catheter passed from the SVC through the right atrium and right ventricle enters the pulmonary trunk — the route used for right heart catheterisation and pulmonary angiography. Congenital anomalies of the outflow tract: pulmonary stenosis and tetralogy of Fallot involve the right ventricular outflow tract and pulmonary trunk seen in this section. Mediastinal masses: the pretracheal and subcarinal lymph node stations of this level drain the lungs and are staged on this slice. Likely follow-up questions Where does the pulmonary trunk begin and where does it divide? — It begins at the pulmonary orifice of the right ventricle, anterior to the aortic orifice, and divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries just inferior to the level of the TIV/V intervertebral disc, to the left of the midline. What are the relations of the right pulmonary artery? — It passes to the right, posterior to the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava, to enter the right lung. What are the relations of the left pulmonary artery? — It passes inferiorly to the arch of the aorta and anteriorly to the descending aorta, to enter the left lung. What is the transverse pericardial sinus, and what bounds it? — It is the passage between the two reflections of serous pericardium: the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk lie in front of it, the superior vena cava and the pulmonary veins behind it, and the left atrium below it. Why is the oesophagus closely related to the left atrium? — The atrium forms the posterior surface of the heart in front of the oesophagus, so left atrial enlargement can compress the oesophagus."},{"id":"t-V-1","p":"V","n":1,"t":"Kidney (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A coronal section of the kidney ( ren ) stained with haematoxylin and eosin: a compound tubular gland in which the cortex contains renal corpuscles with glomeruli and two kinds of convoluted tubule, while the medulla contains parallel straight tubules, collecting ducts and vasa recta. The round glomerulus inside Bowman's capsule is the structure that identifies the organ at first glance. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, low-power overview, cut in the coronal (frontal) plane through the hilum so that cortex, medulla and a papilla with its minor calyx are all visible. Set the slide up at low power first: the examiner expects the plane of section and the direction of the layers before any cytology. 2. Low-power architecture From the surface inwards: a thin fibrous capsule ; the cortex ( cortex renalis ), which looks granular because it is packed with round renal corpuscles ( corpuscula renalia ); the medulla ( medulla renalis ), made of parallel straight tubules and ducts running towards the papilla; and at the hilum a papilla opening into a minor calyx lined by urothelium. Conical medullary rays (pars radiata) straddle cortex and medulla: they are bundles of straight tubules that appear to radiate out from the medulla, and the tissue between them is the convoluted part containing the corpuscles. The cortex also dips between pyramids as the renal columns (of Bertini). 3. High-power key features Renal corpuscle: a tuft of glomerular capillaries inside a clear urinary space, bounded by the thin squamous parietal sheet of Bowman's capsule. The afferent and efferent arterioles enter at the vascular pole; the proximal tubule leaves at the tubular pole. The proximal tubule ( tubulus contortus proximalis ) is lined by cuboidal cells with strongly eosinophilic cytoplasm and a brush border that narrows the lumen to a star shape. The distal tubule ( tubulus contortus distalis ) has cuboidal cells of medium eosinophilia, no brush border and a wider, clearly open lumen; its wall beside the vascular pole thickens into the macula densa . Thin limbs are simple squamous and pale. Collecting ducts ( ductus colligens ) have the widest pale lumina, with principal cells and scattered darker intercalated cells. In the medulla the straight tubules run in parallel, separated by a fine interstitium containing the vasa recta. Structure Epithelium Light-microscopic signature Function Renal corpuscle Parietal squamous sheet + glomerular capillaries Round tuft in a clear space, capsule around it Filtration of plasma Proximal tubule Simple cuboidal with brush border Most eosinophilic tubule, narrow star-shaped lumen Bulk reabsorption Distal tubule Simple cuboidal, no brush border Paler, wider lumen, macula densa near the corpuscle Ion transport, tubular feedback Thin limb Simple squamous Flattened nuclei, low pale profile Water permeability Collecting duct Simple cuboidal to columnar Largest pale lumina; principal and intercalated cells Final concentration of urine 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: nuclei are basophilic blue-violet (haematoxylin), cytoplasm and basement membranes eosinophilic pink. The proximal tubules are the most eosinophilic structures on the slide; the glomerular tuft is moderately eosinophilic with many blue nuclei; the medullary interstitium is pale. If further stains are asked for: PAS is positive in the brush border and in the glomerular and tubular basement membranes, silver impregnation blackens the reticular fibres of the interstitium, and immunohistochemistry can mark renin in the juxtaglomerular cells. How to identify it at the exam Round glomerulus inside a clear space inside a thin capsule — no other organ has this. Side-by-side eosinophilic proximal and paler distal tubules in the cortex. Medulla = parallel straight tubules and wide collecting ducts, no glomeruli. Papilla lined by urothelium gives you the cortex-to-medulla direction. Differential diagnosis Liver: uniform lobules with sinusoids, no corpuscles, no tubules. Thyroid gland: follicles filled with colloid, not tubular profiles. Pancreas: serous acini plus islets, no glomeruli. Ureter or urinary bladder: urothelium over a thick muscular wall, no cortex. Clinical / functional correlation Renal biopsy is reported on HE together with PAS (basement membranes), silver (interstitium) and immunofluorescence; one slide lets glomeruli, tubules and vessels be judged together. Proximal tubules are the segment most vulnerable to ischaemia and nephrotoxins, hence acute tubular necrosis in shock and drug toxicity. Juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole secrete renin and the macula densa senses distal sodium chloride; these two structures are the anatomical basis of the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system."},{"id":"t-V-2","p":"V","n":2,"t":"Jejunum (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section of the jejunum ( jejunum ) stained with HE: a small intestine in which the mucosa carries villi and crypts lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells, the submucosa contains no glands, and the muscular layer has an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. Villi plus crypts together, and the absence of submucosal glands and of large lymphoid aggregates, identify it as jejunum rather than duodenum or ileum. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, transverse section through the wall of the intestinal tube. Four layers are expected in the answer: mucosa (epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae), submucosa , tunica muscularis and serosa . Villi fill the lumen and may be cut longitudinally, obliquely or transversely, so at low power they look like an irregular fringe. 2. Low-power architecture Lumen containing desquamated cells and mucus; then the mucosa, which is dominated by finger-like villi ( villi intestinales ) whose cores are lamina propria. Below the villi, the epithelium invaginates into the crypts of Lieberkühn ( cryptae intestinales ). A thin layer of smooth muscle, the muscularis mucosae ( lamina muscularis mucosae ), separates mucosa from the loose submucosa , which carries vessels but no gland mass. The tunica muscularis shows the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers with the myenteric plexus between them, and the outer surface is covered by serosa. 3. High-power key features The epithelium is simple columnar : tall enterocytes with an eosinophilic striated border (brush border) and oval basal nuclei, among which sit goblet cells — pale, cup-shaped, with the nucleus squeezed to the base and the mucin granules washed out during processing. At the base of the crypts there are Paneth cells with eosinophilic apical granules, plus stem cells and scattered enteroendocrine cells. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with lymphocytes, plasma cells, eosinophils, capillaries and a central lacteal in each villus. Each villus core also contains smooth muscle fibres continuous with the muscularis mucosae. Layer Tissue Key cells and structures Function Mucosa: epithelium Simple columnar Enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells in crypt bases Digestion and absorption; mucus barrier Mucosa: lamina propria Loose connective tissue Lymphocytes, plasma cells, capillaries, lacteal Immune defence; transport Muscularis mucosae Smooth muscle Thin layer at the crypt bases Movement of villi and crypts Submucosa Loose connective tissue Vessels, Meissner's plexus; no glands in jejunum Support and blood supply Tunica muscularis Smooth muscle Inner circular, outer longitudinal, Auerbach's plexus Peristalsis and segmentation 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: enterocyte cytoplasm is eosinophilic, nuclei basophilic; goblet-cell mucin is pale and may look empty. To prove the mucus say PAS stains goblet-cell granules and the brush border magenta/purple-red , and Alcian blue stains acid mucins blue-green. Paneth cell granules are strongly eosinophilic; collagen of the submucosa is eosinophilic and would be blue/green with a trichrome. How to identify it at the exam Villi and crypts present together — the signature of the small intestine. Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells; no villus goblet-cell-free sheet of stomach type. No glands in the submucosa (contrast duodenum) and no large Peyer's patches (contrast ileum). Four-layered wall with a clear muscularis mucosae and a serosa. Differential diagnosis Duodenum: Brunner's mucous glands occupy the submucosa. Ileum: large lymphatic nodules (Peyer's patches) in the lamina propria and submucosa. Colon: crypts but no villi, and far more goblet cells. Stomach: gastric pits and glands, no villi. Clinical / functional correlation Coeliac disease is diagnosed on oriented small-intestinal biopsies: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes. The jejunum is the main site of absorption, so resection causes malabsorption and short-bowel syndrome. Goblet cells are the source of the mucus barrier; their number and the mucin staining are used in reporting intestinal metaplasia."},{"id":"t-V-3","p":"V","n":3,"t":"Unicellular gland (PAS + H)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A PAS–haematoxylin preparation of an epithelium containing unicellular glands: the goblet cells ( exocrinocyti caliciformes ) appear as goblet-shaped cells whose mucin granules are strongly PAS-positive magenta , while the nuclei are counterstained blue with haematoxylin. A single mucus-secreting cell sitting inside an epithelium is the definition of a unicellular exocrine gland. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Cellular level inside an epithelium — the slide is often a high-power view of intestinal or respiratory mucosa (jejunal villus, colonic crypt, tracheal mucosa). The cut may follow the long axis of the goblet cells (goblet shape visible) or graze the surface (only round magenta caps visible). 2. Low-power architecture The epithelium is a ribbon of cells with scattered, intensely magenta dots or cups at regular intervals. Beware of three other magenta lines that belong to the method, not to the gland: the basement membrane beneath the epithelium, reticular fibres , and the apical brush border are all PAS-positive. Mucus lying free in the lumen may also be magenta. On the section of intestine you can orient yourself by the villi or crypts; in the respiratory tract by the underlying cartilage or glands. 3. High-power key features Goblet cell: narrow base resting on the basement membrane, and a swollen apical theca filled with mucinogen granules. The nucleus is oval to triangular, compressed basally by the granules, with dense chromatin. The granules are PAS-positive magenta ; in HE they are washed out and the cell looks empty and pale. Secretion is merocrine (exocytosis of granules); after discharge the cell is short and its theca collapses. Neighbouring cells are enterocytes with a striated border, or ciliated and basal cells in the respiratory epithelium. Goblet cells are never grouped into acini and never possess a duct of their own: each cell opens directly onto the surface. Cell Shape Nucleus Staining Product Goblet cell Cup-shaped, swollen apex Basal, compressed, oval/triangular PAS-positive magenta granules; pale on HE Mucin Enterocyte Tall columnar Basal, oval Eosinophilic cytoplasm; PAS-positive brush border Absorption Ciliated cell Tall columnar Basal, oval Eosinophilic, with an apical fringe of cilia Mucus transport Basal cell Pyramidal, does not reach the lumen Basal, oval Weakly basophilic Stem cell for the epithelium 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud The PAS reaction (periodic acid + Schiff reagent) oxidises vicinal diols of polysaccharides to aldehydes, and the Schiff reagent then gives a magenta/purple-red product. It marks glycogen, neutral glycosaminoglycans, mucin, glycoproteins, basement membranes, reticular fibres and the walls of fungi. The diastase (amylase) control is the elegant point: if the staining disappears after diastase, the material is glycogen; if it persists, it is another polysaccharide such as mucin. Haematoxylin counterstains nuclei blue. Acid mucins would also stain with Alcian blue (blue-green). How to identify it at the exam Single goblet-shaped magenta cells standing in a row of non-staining epithelial cells. The magenta material is inside the apical part of the cell, not in a duct or acinus. Nucleus squeezed to the base — a gland cell that secretes directly to the surface. Diastase control separates mucin from glycogen staining. Differential diagnosis Multicellular mucous gland (sublingual, submandibular mucous tubules): magenta cells grouped into acini/tubules with ducts and interlobular septa. Gastric surface mucous cells: PAS-positive but form a continuous simple columnar sheet, not individual goblet cells. Basement membrane or brush border: a thin continuous magenta line, not a cell-shaped drop. Paneth cells: eosinophilic apical granules that are not PAS-positive mucin. Clinical / functional correlation Goblet-cell hyperplasia is a response to chronic irritation (chronic bronchitis, asthma) and thickens the mucus layer. Goblet cells normally absent from the oesophagus; their appearance there marks intestinal metaplasia ( Barrett oesophagus ) and is demonstrated with PAS or Alcian blue. In cystic fibrosis the mucus is abnormally viscid, so the mucociliary and intestinal mucus barriers fail."},{"id":"t-V-4","p":"V","n":4,"t":"Trachea (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section of the trachea ( trachea ) stained with HE: respiratory epithelium (ciliated pseudostratified columnar with goblet cells) resting on a very thick basement membrane, a lamina propria with seromucous glands, and dorsally open horseshoe-shaped pieces of hyaline cartilage. The cartilage is what separates trachea from every other tubular organ in the slide set. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, transverse section through the wall of the airway; the lumen is round to oval. Three concentric layers are named: mucosa , fibromusculocartilaginous layer and adventitia . Because the cartilage is a series of separate C-shaped pieces, one section may show one large plate or the gap of the membranous wall with the trachealis muscle. 2. Low-power architecture The epithelium is thrown into longitudinal folds, so the luminal border is wavy. Beneath it lies a pale lamina propria, then the cartilage: glassy, slightly basophilic plates with a perichondrium, occupying roughly the outer third of the wall. Between the mucosa and the cartilage you find seromucous tracheal glands — pale, clustered acini with ducts running towards the lumen. On the dorsal (membranous) side the cartilage is replaced by a band of smooth muscle, the trachealis muscle ( musculus trachealis ), and elastic fibres. The outer adventitia is loose connective tissue. 3. High-power key features The epithelium is ciliated pseudostratified columnar : nuclei lie at several levels, giving a false impression of stratification. Cell types: ciliated cells with an apical fringe of cilia, goblet cells with pale mucin, basal cells resting on the basement membrane, and occasional brush and neuroendocrine cells. The basement membrane is strikingly thick (2–4 µm) and eosinophilic. The glands are tubuloalveolar seromucous: mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes , with ducts lined by cuboidal to columnar cells. The cartilage is hyaline : chondrocytes in lacunae, often in isogenous groups, with a perichondrium and no vessels inside the matrix. The trachealis is a compact bundle of smooth muscle with elongated nuclei. Layer Tissue Key cells and structures Function Epithelium Ciliated pseudostratified columnar Ciliated, goblet, basal, brush and endocrine cells Mucociliary clearance Basement membrane Thick basal lamina Prominent eosinophilic band (2–4 µm) Mechanical support Lamina propria Loose connective tissue Lymphocytes, plasma cells, elastic fibres, glands Immune defence, elasticity Fibromusculocartilaginous layer Hyaline cartilage + smooth muscle 15–20 C-shaped cartilages; trachealis dorsally Keeps the lumen patent Adventitia Loose connective tissue Vessels, nerves, lymphatics Attachment to mediastinum 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: cartilage matrix is pale to slightly basophilic , nuclei blue; cytoplasm of ciliated cells eosinophilic; the thick basement membrane and the serous demilunes are strongly eosinophilic ; mucous cells are pale. PAS stains the goblet-cell mucin and the basement membrane magenta; Alcian blue marks acid mucin of the glands; orcein (red-brown) shows the elastic fibre layer of the lamina propria; and the cartilage would be metachromatic with toluidine blue. How to identify it at the exam Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium on a very thick basement membrane. Hyaline cartilage plate in the wall, with a perichondrium. Seromucous glands with serous demilunes between mucosa and cartilage. Dorsal membranous wall = smooth trachealis muscle, no cartilage. Differential diagnosis Oesophagus: non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, no cartilage, has a muscularis mucosae. Bronchus: cartilage in small irregular plates, a complete ring of smooth muscle between mucosa and cartilage, fewer glands. Larynx: elastic cartilage and stratified squamous epithelium over the vocal fold. Skin: keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with appendages. Clinical / functional correlation Prolonged intubation causes fibrotic narrowing — post-intubation tracheal stenosis with stridor. Primary ciliary dyskinesia (dynein defect) causes failed mucociliary clearance, recurrent infection and often situs inversus (Kartagener syndrome). Smoking and chronic tracheitis reduce cilia, increase goblet cells and enlarge the tracheal glands — the morphological basis of chronic bronchitis."},{"id":"t-V-5","p":"V","n":5,"t":"Oesophagus (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section of the oesophagus ( oesophagus ) stained with HE: a thick non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium over a lamina propria, a well-developed muscularis mucosae, a submucosa with small mucous glands, and a muscular layer whose upper third is skeletal and lower third smooth muscle. The combination of stratified squamous epithelium with a muscularis mucosae and submucosal glands is diagnostic. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, transverse section of this collapsed muscular tube; the lumen is irregular and star-shaped because of longitudinal mucosal folds. Four layers: mucosa, submucosa, tunica muscularis and adventitia (serosa only in the short abdominal segment). 2. Low-power architecture The epithelium is pale pink and thick, with connective tissue papillae indenting its base. Beneath it is the lamina propria, then the muscularis mucosae — a distinctly thicker longitudinal smooth muscle layer than elsewhere in the digestive tract, which helps to identify the oesophagus. The submucosa is loose and contains small mucous glands (glandulae submucosae oesophagei) and blood vessels. The tunica muscularis consists of an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer with Auerbach's myenteric plexus between them; the outer border is adventitia in the thorax. 3. High-power key features The epithelium is non-keratinized stratified squamous : a basal layer of dividing columnar cells, a thick polygonal (prickle) layer with prominent desmosomes, and flattened superficial cells that retain their nuclei and look clear because they are rich in glycogen. No keratin layer and no goblet cells are present, but lymphocytes and melanocytes may lie between the basal cells. The submucosal mucous glands are small and branched, with pale mucous acini; their ducts open through the mucosa. Near the lower end, oesophageal cardiac glands may appear in the lamina propria. The muscular layer is striated in its upper third (non-cardiac visceral striated muscle), mixed in the middle third, and smooth in the lower third. Layer Tissue Key features Function Epithelium Stratified squamous, non-keratinized No keratin, glycogen-rich surface cells, CT papillae Protection from abrasion Lamina propria Loose connective tissue Cardiac glands near the lower end, lymphocytes Mucus, immune defence Muscularis mucosae Smooth muscle, longitudinal Unusually thick and conspicuous Mucosal folds Submucosa Loose connective tissue Small mucous glands, Meissner's plexus, vessels Lubrication, blood supply Tunica muscularis Skeletal then smooth muscle Inner circular, outer longitudinal; Auerbach's plexus Peristalsis 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: the epithelium is eosinophilic with basophilic nuclei; the superficial cell layers are pale due to glycogen; the submucosal glands are pale mucinous. With PAS the glycogen of the superficial cells and the mucin of the submucosal glands react, but the pattern is diffuse rather than the crisp magenta cup of a goblet cell. A trichrome makes the collagen of the lamina propria green/blue, separating it from the pink epithelium and muscle. Silver stains the reticular fibre of the lamina propria. How to identify it at the exam Thick non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium without keratin and without goblet cells. Conspicuous muscularis mucosae and small submucosal mucous glands. Star-shaped lumen with mucosal folds. Two muscle layers of skeletal, mixed or smooth type depending on the level. Differential diagnosis Trachea: ciliated pseudostratified epithelium, cartilage plates, no muscularis mucosae. Stomach (cardia): simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits; no stratified squamous sheet. Vagina: similar stratified squamous epithelium, but a fibromuscular wall without muscularis mucosae and without glands. Thin skin: keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with appendages. Clinical / functional correlation Gastro-oesophageal reflux produces basal-zone hyperplasia, elongation of the CT papillae and eosinophils; long-standing reflux can cause intestinal metaplasia (Barrett oesophagus). Oesophageal varices form from the submucosal venous plexus in portal hypertension, which is why they bleed so profusely. Squamous cell carcinoma arises from this epithelium, and the loss of myenteric neurons is the basis of achalasia."},{"id":"t-V-6","p":"V","n":6,"t":"Finger pad (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A vertical section through the pad of a fingertip — thick, glabrous skin stained with HE: a very thick keratinized epidermis with a prominent stratum corneum and a visible stratum lucidum, dermal papillae carrying Meissner corpuscles, eccrine sweat ducts spiralling to the surface, and Pacinian corpuscles deep in the subcutaneous tissue. There are no hair follicles and no sebaceous glands. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, vertical section through skin plus subcutaneous tissue; identify the free surface and orient the slide with the epidermis upwards. The section runs across the skin crests and grooves (cristae et sulci cutis) of the finger pad, so the surface is wavy — the anatomical basis of fingerprints. 2. Low-power architecture From the surface down: a massive, homogeneous, strongly eosinophilic stratum corneum ; the cellular epidermis; the dermis , divided into a papillary layer (loose connective tissue with the dermal papillae) and a reticular layer (dense irregular connective tissue); and the subcutaneous tissue dominated by adipose cells. Coiled eccrine glands lie in the deep dermis and subcutis, and their ducts run as spirals through the epidermis. Nerves and vessels run in the dermis, with Pacinian corpuscles at the dermis–subcutis junction. 3. High-power key features Epidermis, from base to surface: stratum basale (a single layer of columnar keratinocytes on a corrugated basement membrane, with melanocytes and Merkel cells), stratum spinosum (several layers of polygonal cells with spiny processes and tonofibrils), stratum granulosum (basophilic keratohyalin granules, lamellar granules), stratum lucidum (a thin, strongly eosinophilic line of dead cells containing eleidin, visible only in thick skin), and the stratum corneum (dead anucleate keratinized scales, much thicker than the whole living epidermis). In each dermal papilla there is a capillary loop and often a Meissner corpuscle — an oval, lamellated tactile corpuscle lying just under the epidermis. The eccrine secretory coil is lined by clear and dark cells with myoepithelial cells; its duct has two layers of cuboidal cells and spirals through the epidermis to a sweat pore. The Pacinian corpuscle is a large onion-like lamellated structure in the deep dermis/subcutis. Layer / structure Distinguishing feature on this slide Stratum corneum Very thick, eosinophilic, anucleate; thicker than the living epidermis Stratum lucidum Thin pale eosinophilic line; only in thick skin Stratum granulosum Basophilic keratohyalin granules in flattened cells Stratum spinosum Several layers of prickle cells with tonofibrils Stratum basale Single row of columnar keratinocytes; melanocytes and Merkel cells Papillary dermis Dermal papillae with capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles Eccrine sweat gland Coiled secretory part deep; two-layered duct spiralling to the surface Pacinian corpuscle Large lamellated \"onion\" in deep dermis or subcutis 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: keratin and the stratum lucidum are strongly eosinophilic ; keratohyalin granules are basophilic ; nuclei of the living layers are blue; collagen of the dermis is pink and elastic fibres are poorly seen. To show the elastic network use orcein (red-brown) or resorcin-fuchsin (brown-purple to blue-black); PAS outlines the basement membrane and the glycogen of the sweat-gland clear cells; silver impregnation demonstrates the nerve endings and Meissner corpuscles; Sudan staining of a frozen section shows the lipid of the subcutaneous adipocytes. How to identify it at the exam Thick skin: stratum corneum thicker than the living epidermis, and a visible stratum lucidum. No hair follicles, no sebaceous glands, no apocrine glands — only eccrine sweat glands. Sweat ducts spiralling through the epidermis to pores on the crests. Meissner corpuscles in the dermal papillae; Pacinian corpuscles deep. Differential diagnosis Thin (hairy) skin: thin stratum corneum, no stratum lucidum, hair follicles and sebaceous glands present. Lip: shows a transition from keratinized skin to non-keratinized mucosa with labial glands. Oesophagus or vagina: non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, no skin appendages. Sole skin: the same thick type in principle, so argue the site from the overall architecture and, if present, the Meissner corpuscle density. Clinical / functional correlation Dermatoglyphs (fingerprints) are formed by the epidermal crests and are individually stable — the basis of forensic identification. Meissner corpuscles detect fine touch and low-frequency vibration; Pacinian corpuscles detect deep pressure and higher-frequency vibration. Their loss is part of diabetic peripheral neuropathy . Blocked eccrine ducts cause miliaria (prickly heat); the thick epidermis regenerates from the stratum basale after superficial burns."},{"id":"t-V-7","p":"V","n":7,"t":"Submandibular gland (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A section of the submandibular gland ( glandula submandibularis ) stained with HE: a mixed seromucous salivary gland, predominantly serous, in which basophilic serous acini and pale mucous tubules are capped by serous demilunes , and the secretion drains through intercalated, striated and interlobular ducts. The combination of mixed acini, demilunes and striated ducts is the classic examination picture. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, section of a lobulated gland: the parenchyma is divided into lobules by connective tissue septa, with interlobular ducts running in those septa. The cut is random, so acini, tubules and ducts of several orders are all visible in one field. 2. Low-power architecture The gland is tubuloacinar and compound : secretory end pieces of two tinctorial types (dark blue serous acini and pale mucous tubules) are packed into lobules; the pale rounded spaces between acini are adipose cells. In the septa, large interlobular ducts have a visible lumen. Within the lobule, ducts of smaller calibre are seen between the acini. Capsule surrounds the whole gland. There are no islets of Langerhans and no follicular structures. 3. High-power key features Serous acinus: pyramidal cells arranged around a small central lumen; basal cytoplasm strongly basophilic because of rough endoplasmic reticulum, apical cytoplasm packed with eosinophilic zymogen granules ; round nucleus in the basal third. Mucous tubule: pale, foamy cells with flattened basal nuclei and clear apical mucin. Serous demilune: a crescent of serous cells applied to the end of a mucous tubule (largely a fixation artefact of two cell types that are adjacent in life, but a standard examinable feature). Ducts, in order: intercalated duct — small, low cuboidal, pale; striated duct — columnar cells with basal striations produced by mitochondria and basal membrane infoldings; interlobular duct — taller cuboidal to columnar epithelium in the septa. Myoepithelial cells sit between the secretory cells and their basement membrane and squeeze the secretion out. Component Cells Light-microscopic appearance Role Serous acinus Serous acinar cells Basal basophilia, apical eosinophilic granules Protein-rich saliva Mucous tubule Mucous cells Pale foamy cytoplasm, flattened basal nucleus Mucin secretion Serous demilune Serous cells Eosinophilic crescent capping a mucous tubule Adds protein to mucus Intercalated duct Low cuboidal cells Small pale duct inside the lobule First duct segment Striated duct Columnar cells Basal striations, eosinophilic cytoplasm Modifies saliva, resorbs sodium Interlobular duct Cuboidal to columnar Lumen in the connective tissue septum Conducts secretion 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: serous cells are biphasic — basophilic base, eosinophilic apex — while mucous cells are pale and nearly unstained, and this contrast alone gives the diagnosis. PAS stains mucous cells and demilunes magenta and is the stain to ask for if dense mucous cells are suspected; Alcian blue marks acid mucin blue-green; mucicarmine marks mucin red. In a trichrome the collagen septa are blue/green while acinar cytoplasm remains red. How to identify it at the exam Two acinar populations in one lobule: dark-blue serous acini and pale mucous tubules. Serous demilunes capping mucous tubules. Striated ducts: pink columnar ducts with basal striations. Lobules separated by septa with interlobular ducts and adipose tissue. Differential diagnosis Parotid gland: pure serous acini, abundant adipose tissue, no demilunes, no mucous tubules. Sublingual gland: predominantly mucous units with poorly developed striated ducts. Pancreas: serous acini but with islets of Langerhans and no striated ducts. Tracheal glands: the same seromucous design, but inside the wall of the airway with cartilage nearby. Clinical / functional correlation Saliva is modified as it passes along the striated duct by sodium reabsorption and potassium/bicarbonate secretion, which is why striated ducts are the most metabolically active duct segment. Sialolithiasis most often obstructs the submandibular duct (Wharton's duct) because its secretion is the most mucinous and its course runs upwards. Inflammatory and immune sialadenitis ( Sjögren syndrome ) destroys acini with a lymphocytic infiltrate; mixed tumours (pleomorphic adenoma) arise from duct and myoepithelial cells."},{"id":"t-V-8","p":"V","n":8,"t":"Tendon (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A longitudinal section of a tendon ( tendo ) stained with HE: dense regular connective tissue ( textus connectivus compactus regularis ) made of parallel bundles of strongly eosinophilic type I collagen fibres, with rows of flattened, spindle-shaped fibrocyte nuclei between the bundles and no cells or vessels inside them. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue and organ level; the section is longitudinal, so the bundles run parallel across the field. Some slides include the myotendinous junction (muscle fibres of the free end) or the osteotendinous junction; state which end you see, because the crimp and the branching pattern change there. 2. Low-power architecture Long, parallel, wavy bundles of collagen fill almost the whole field. Between the bundles run thin septa of loose connective tissue, the endotenon ( endotenonium ), carrying blood vessels and nerves; the surface of the tendon is wrapped by a thin epitenon and, where present, a paratenon. There is no epithelium, no gland and no cartilage. 3. High-power key features Collagen bundles: type I collagen fibres, 1–10 µm thick and of indefinite length, grouped into bundles; they run parallel to the line of pull and show a gentle wave (crimp) that straightens under tension. Cells: fibrocytes/tenocytes squeezed between bundles, with elongated, flattened, sharp-pointed (spindle) nuclei arranged in rows; cytoplasm is scanty and the cells never lie in lacunae. Ground substance is rich in dermatan sulfate and contains some elastic fibres. At the point of attachment to bone the tendon fibres continue as Sharpey fibres into the bone matrix. Component Appearance and staining Function Type I collagen bundles Parallel, wavy, strongly eosinophilic Transmit muscle force; high tensile strength Fibrocytes / tenocytes Flattened nuclei with pointed ends, in rows Synthesis and maintenance of the matrix Endotenon Loose connective tissue between bundles, with vessels Nutritive and sliding spaces Epitenon / paratenon Thin connective tissue sheath on the surface Low-friction gliding Elastic fibres Scanty, orcein-positive Restores the crimp after stretch 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: bundles are intensely eosinophilic and nuclei basophilic; no striation and no sarcoplasm. Ask for a collagen stain to prove the type: picrosirius red stains collagen red, and in polarised light type I collagen appears red to yellow while type III appears green; aniline blue stains collagen blue; orcein would show only the scanty elastic fibres red-brown. Reticulin (silver) is present only around the endotenon vessels, not around the bundles. How to identify it at the exam Parallel eosinophilic collagen bundles running across the whole field. Thin, sharp-ended fibrocyte nuclei in rows between bundles. No cells inside the bundles, no striation, no lacunae. Endotenon septa with vessels at regular intervals. Differential diagnosis Smooth muscle (longitudinal): spindle cells with central cigar-shaped nuclei and real cytoplasm; bundles not wavy; no endotenon. Peripheral nerve (longitudinal): wavy fibres with myelin sheaths and perineurium, not homogeneous eosinophilic bundles. Fibrocartilage: chondrocytes in lacunae arranged in rows between coarse collagen bundles. Dense irregular connective tissue (dermis): bundles run in all directions. Clinical / functional correlation Tendons are hypovascular , so healing after rupture is slow and scar-dominated; the Achilles tendon ruptures in its relatively avascular zone. Tendinopathy is a degenerative process with disorganised collagen, not a pure inflammation; the crimp pattern and fibre alignment are the histological markers of normal function. The Golgi tendon organ at the myotendinous junction senses tension and inhibits the muscle through the tendon reflex, protecting the tendon from overload."},{"id":"t-V-9","p":"V","n":9,"t":"Adipose tissue (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer White adipose tissue ( textus adiposus albus ) stained with HE: tightly packed unilocular adipocytes ( adipocyti uniloculares ) whose single large lipid droplet has been dissolved out during paraffin processing, leaving clear, round spaces limited by a thin eosinophilic rim of cytoplasm with a flattened peripheral nucleus — the \"chicken-wire\" pattern. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, taken from subcutaneous fat, mesentery, renal sinus or epicardium. The section may be a thin sheet (spread preparation of peritoneum) or a block with lobules separated by septa. Conventional HE cannot show the lipid itself, so identify the tissue from the shape of the spaces and the position of the nuclei. 2. Low-power architecture A mosaic of polygonal to round clear spaces of fairly uniform size, separated by a delicate network of eosinophilic lines (basement membranes and fine collagen of the septa) and by thin connective tissue septa carrying vessels. At the edge of the fat lobule the adipocytes are smaller and merge into loose connective tissue. 3. High-power key features Unilocular adipocyte: one huge lipid droplet fills the cell; the nucleus is flattened and pushed to the periphery like a signet ring; the cytoplasm is reduced to a thin eosinophilic rim with a few organelles; the cell is surrounded by an external lamina and reticular fibres. Between the cells run capillaries. Brown adipose tissue ( textus adiposus fuscus ) is the contrast to quote: multilocular adipocytes with many small droplets, a central rounded nucleus, abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria (hence brown), rich blood supply and sympathetic innervation; present in the fetus and neonate (interscapular, mediastinal, perirenal). Feature White adipose tissue Brown adipose tissue Adipocyte Unilocular, one large droplet Multilocular, many small droplets Nucleus Flattened, peripheral Round, central Cytoplasm Thin rim, few organelles Abundant, eosinophilic, many mitochondria Vascularity Moderate Very rich Function Energy store, insulation, cushioning, endocrine (leptin) Non-shivering thermogenesis 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: the lipid is not stained at all — it is dissolved by alcohol and xylene, leaving empty spaces; only the thin cytoplasm, basement membrane and nuclei are coloured, with nuclei basophilic and the rims eosinophilic . To demonstrate the fat you must say the magic sentence: a frozen section stained with Sudan III/IV or Oil Red O shows red droplets. Osmium tetroxide blackens the lipid, and a trichrome shows the collagen of the septa. How to identify it at the exam Empty, round, closely packed vacuoles with a \"chicken-wire\" pattern. Nuclei flattened at the very periphery of the spaces. No fibres crossing the spaces; delicate capillaries between adipocytes. To prove lipid, ask for a frozen section + Sudan or Oil Red O. Differential diagnosis Loose connective tissue: abundant fibres and many cell types between the spaces; fat cells are only a minority. Mucous connective tissue (Wharton's jelly): sparse cells, abundant gelatinous matrix, not a uniform vacuole sheet. Processing artefacts: irregular cracks or clefting rather than uniform round spaces with marginal nuclei. Brown adipose tissue: multilocular cells with central nuclei. Clinical / functional correlation Adipose tissue is an endocrine organ (leptin) and contributes to the pro-inflammatory state of obesity. Benign tumours are lipomas (mature unilocular adipocytes); malignant liposarcomas are rare and show lipoblasts. After long-bone fracture, fat droplets may enter torn venules and cause fat embolism ; sudden death in a patient with recent trauma should raise this possibility."},{"id":"t-V-10","p":"V","n":10,"t":"Adipose tissue (frozen section, Sudan","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A frozen section of adipose tissue stained with a fat-soluble dye (Sudan III/IV or Oil Red O): because the tissue has never touched alcohol or xylene, the lipid remains in place and stains bright red , proving that the clear spaces of the HE slide are indeed fat and the cells are adipocytes. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level; the crucial preparation point is the frozen section ( sectio congelata ). Routine paraffin processing dissolves fat in the clearing agents, so lipid histochemistry needs material snap-frozen and cut on a cryostat, with water-based reagents only. 2. Low-power architecture The same \"chicken-wire\" sheet of adipocytes as in HE, but every cell now contains a brightly red globule. The background of connective tissue septa, capillaries and nuclei is counterstained, so the tissue architecture remains readable. Fat lobules, septa and vessels match the HE slide one-to-one. 3. High-power key features Unilocular adipocyte: a single large spherical red droplet occupying almost the whole cell, with a thin rim of counterstained cytoplasm and a flattened nucleus at the margin; droplet size is fairly uniform. In brown adipose tissue the dye reveals many small red droplets (multilocular pattern) with a central nucleus. Lipid in other cells (steatotic hepatocytes, foam cells, sebaceous gland cells, the lipid of the adrenal cortex) stains the same way, so always confirm which organ you are in. Method Colour of lipid What it proves / comment Sudan III / IV Red Fat-soluble dye dissolved into the lipid; standard frozen-section method Sudan black B Blue-black Also used for myelin and for phospholipid Oil Red O Red Common alternative for neutral lipid Osmium tetroxide Black Reduces to black metallic osmium in unsaturated lipid (also myelin) HE on paraffin Not stained (dissolved) Shows empty spaces only; the negative control for the argument 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud The chemistry to quote: the dye is more soluble in the lipid droplet than in its own solvent, so it partitions into the fat. Therefore the section must not contact lipid solvents (alcohol, xylene, chloroform) at any step. Nuclei are counterstained blue (haematoxylin) or red (carmine), and this gives the red-on-blue picture. Emphasise that this is the method that turns the negative evidence of HE into positive evidence of lipid, and that the same reasoning explains why frozen sections are used for enzyme histochemistry. How to identify it at the exam Bright red globules filling cells that look empty on HE. Frozen section, water-based staining — no alcohol or xylene anywhere in the protocol. Blue nuclei at the periphery of the red droplets, matching the adipocyte shape. The next step in the answer: compare with the HE slide of the same tissue. Differential diagnosis HE adipose tissue: identical architecture but empty spaces; do not call the spaces \"fat\" on HE alone. Sebaceous gland: lipid also stains red but the cells form alveoli with holocrine secretion. Steatotic liver or foam cells: red droplets inside hepatocytes or macrophages, not one droplet per cell in a fatty sheet. Ice-crystal artefact: irregular clefts that also fail to stain uniformly. Clinical / functional correlation Intraoperative frozen section is used for rapid diagnosis: fat necrosis, lipoma versus liposarcoma, and the detection of fat in lymph nodes. Oil Red O on a renal biopsy demonstrates lipid droplets in tubular cells in nephrotic-range proteinuria. The same cryostat technique preserves enzymes for histochemistry and is the reason frozen sections are kept for research and for lipid storage disorders."},{"id":"t-V-11","p":"V","n":11,"t":"Hyaline cartilage (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Hyaline cartilage ( cartilago hyalina ) stained with HE: a smooth, glassy, basophilic matrix containing chondrocytes in lacunae, often in isogenous groups, with a perichondrium on the free surfaces (absent from articular surfaces). The matrix protein fibres are \"masked\", so no fibres can be seen — that is the meaning of hyaline. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level; the commonest examples are a tracheal ring, a rib cartilage or the articular surface of a long bone. Decide first whether a perichondrium is present: it is present over the trachea and ribs, but absent on articular surfaces, which are nourished by synovial fluid. 2. Low-power architecture A homogeneous, pale to moderately basophilic matrix with round or oval clear spaces (lacunae) containing cells. Around each lacuna the matrix is darker (capsular matrix), around each isogenous group it is still fairly dark (territorial matrix), and between the groups it is palest (interterritorial matrix). A perichondrium, when present, is a distinct eosinophilic band on the surface with an outer fibrous and an inner chondrogenic layer. There are no vessels and no nerves inside the cartilage. 3. High-power key features Chondrocytes: round to oval cells with a small, dark nucleus, a pale cytoplasm often containing lipid droplets and glycogen; they sit singly or in isogenous groups (two to four cells derived from one chondroblast division) inside lacunae. At the surface, flattened chondroblasts lie in the inner layer of the perichondrium. Matrix: type II collagen fibrils, aggrecan and hyaluronate give the glassy basophilia; fibrils are invisible because their refractive index matches the matrix. In older cartilage the matrix may calcify and become more deeply basophilic. Growth is by interstitial (division of chondrocytes) and appositional (from the perichondrium) mechanisms. Zone Matrix Cells Staining Capsular matrix Immediately around each lacuna, richest in sulfated GAGs Single chondrocyte Deeply basophilic Territorial matrix Around isogenous groups Groups of 2–4 chondrocytes Basophilic Interterritorial matrix Between the groups, mostly type II collagen Few cells Pale, weakly basophilic Perichondrium Outer fibrous + inner chondrogenic layer Fibroblasts, chondroblasts Eosinophilic 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: the matrix is basophilic because of the sulfated glycosaminoglycans, and this is the single most useful staining statement. PAS is positive in the matrix and outlines the capsule; Alcian blue stains acid GAGs blue-green; toluidine blue shows metachromasia (violet around the lacunae). The collagen is type II and needs immunohistochemistry to be demonstrated, precisely because it is masked. Calcified areas take haematoxylin more strongly. How to identify it at the exam Glassy, homogeneous basophilic matrix with no visible fibres. Chondrocytes in lacunae, often in isogenous groups. Perichondrium on a free surface; absent on articular surfaces. No blood vessels inside the tissue. Differential diagnosis Elastic cartilage: looks similar on HE, but an orcein stain reveals a branching elastic network. Fibrocartilage: coarse eosinophilic collagen bundles with chondrocytes in rows, no perichondrium. Bone: eosinophilic matrix, osteons and canaliculi; lacunae are not round and empty in decalcified bone. Tracheal gland adenoma or thyroid colloid: no lacunae and no chondrocytes. Clinical / functional correlation Articular hyaline cartilage has no perichondrium and no vessels , so it heals poorly; microfissures progress to osteoarthritis . Trauma or degeneration can detach cartilage fragments into the joint cavity ( joint mouse ), which are kept alive by synovial fluid. Hyaline cartilage calcifies with age (rib and laryngeal cartilages) and is the source of chondrosarcoma ; it is the template for endochondral ossification."},{"id":"t-V-12","p":"V","n":12,"t":"Elastic cartilage (orcein + H)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Elastic cartilage ( cartilago elastica ) stained with orcein and haematoxylin: the same chondrocytes in lacunae and the same perichondrium as hyaline cartilage, but the matrix contains a conspicuous, branching network of elastic fibres stained by orcein, which is the only way to tell it from hyaline cartilage. It is found in the auricle, the epiglottis and the auditory tube. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level from the auricle, epiglottis or the cartilaginous part of the auditory tube. Perichondrium is present on both surfaces. The orcein preparation is combined with a nuclear counterstain (haematoxylin), so both cells and elastic fibres can be assessed in the same field. 2. Low-power architecture At low power the cartilage looks similar to hyaline cartilage, except that a dark, branching network runs through the matrix — brown to black with orcein. The perichondrium is visible at the border. In the epiglottis, one side is covered by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and the other by respiratory epithelium, so orientation may be needed. 3. High-power key features Elastic fibres: fine, wavy, branching fibres which anastomose to form a three-dimensional network throughout the interterritorial matrix; they are absent from the immediate capsular region around the lacunae in many areas. Chondrocytes: as in hyaline cartilage — round to oval cells in lacunae, isogenous groups, small dark nuclei, lipid and glycogen in the cytoplasm. Matrix: type II collagen masked as in hyaline cartilage, but with elastin added; the tissue is therefore compression-resistant AND flexible. Perichondrium is present, with the outer fibrous and inner chondrogenic layers. Feature Hyaline cartilage Elastic cartilage Fibrocartilage Matrix fibres Type II collagen only (masked) Type II collagen + elastic network Predominantly type I collagen bundles Cells Chondrocytes, isogenous groups Chondrocytes, isogenous groups Chondrocytes in rows; fibroblasts at margins Perichondrium Present (absent on articular surfaces) Present Absent or indistinct Elastic stain Negative Strongly positive network Negative or scanty Location Trachea, bronchi, ribs, joints, larynx Auricle, epiglottis, auditory tube Intervertebral disc, symphysis, menisci 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud Orcein stains elastic fibres and membranes red-brown ; the nuclear counterstain is haematoxylin (blue nuclei). Alternative elastic stains to mention: resorcin-fuchsin (brown-purple to blue-black), aldehyde fuchsin (purple) and Verhoeff's ferric haematoxylin (black-violet to black, but it also stains nuclei, so it must be differentiated carefully). On plain HE the elastic fibres are invisible and the slide is indistinguishable from hyaline cartilage — this is exactly why the slide is issued with orcein. How to identify it at the exam A brown to black branching network of fibres running through cartilage matrix. Chondrocytes in lacunae and a perichondrium — it is still cartilage. The orcein stain is the discriminator against hyaline cartilage. Site: auricle, epiglottis or auditory tube. Differential diagnosis Hyaline cartilage: no elastic network on orcein; the commonest confusion if the stain is not considered. Elastic connective tissue (aorta, ligamentum flavum): elastic fibres without lacunae or chondrocytes. Fibrocartilage: coarse eosinophilic collagen bundles and rows of chondrocytes. Auricle skin: contains elastic fibres in the dermis but also epidermis and appendages. Clinical / functional correlation Elastic cartilage combines resilience with flexibility, which is why the auricle and epiglottis spring back into shape; it is the material used for auricular and nasal reconstruction. Relapsing polychondritis is an autoimmune inflammation of cartilage that classically affects the auricle and can destroy its elastic framework. Epiglottic oedema (epiglottitis) rapidly obstructs the airway because the loose connective tissue over elastic cartilage swells freely."},{"id":"t-V-13","p":"V","n":13,"t":"Fibrocartilage (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Fibrocartilage ( cartilago fibrosa ) stained with HE: coarse, strongly eosinophilic bundles of type I collagen in parallel or crossing arrays, with oval chondrocytes lying in lacunae in rows between the bundles, very little basophilic matrix and no distinct perichondrium. It is the structural transition between dense connective tissue and hyaline cartilage. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level from the annulus fibrosus of an intervertebral disc ( annulus fibrosus disci intervertebralis ), the interpubic disc, a meniscus or a fibrocartilaginous articular surface. In the annulus the fibrocartilage lamellae alternate with thin layers of hyaline cartilage and the collagen bundles of successive lamellae cross obliquely. 2. Low-power architecture The field is dominated by thick, wavy, deeply eosinophilic bundle systems that resemble a tendon but are interrupted by rows of round/oval cells. There is no perichondrium, no capsule and no epithelium; the tissue blends at its edges into dense regular connective tissue or into hyaline cartilage. Blood vessels are confined to the margins. 3. High-power key features Collagen: bundles of type I collagen predominate and are strongly eosinophilic , running in parallel arrays with a felt-like interweaving; type II collagen is present but scanty. Cells: chondrocytes are oval and lie in lacunae, single or in small isogenous groups, characteristically arranged in axial rows between the bundles; true fibroblasts appear at the margins. Matrix: the proteoglycan versican predominates over aggrecan, so there is little basophilia and only weak Alcian-blue staining. Because collagen dominates the whole volume, a perichondrium cannot be distinguished. Structure Appearance Why it matters for identification Type I collagen bundles Coarse, wavy, strongly eosinophilic Dominant component; gives tensile strength Chondrocytes Oval cells in lacunae, in rows or small groups Proves it is cartilage, not tendon Matrix Pale, little basophilia Weak Alcian blue; versican-rich Perichondrium Absent or not distinguishable Separates it from hyaline cartilage Marginal zone Transition to dense connective tissue Explains the \"hybrid\" appearance 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: intensely eosinophilic bundles with basophilic nuclei — easy to mistake for tendon. AZAN or Masson's trichrome colours the collagen bundles blue; picrosirius red confirms type I collagen (red-yellow in polarised light); Alcian blue is weak and patchy because the GAG content is low; type II collagen immunohistochemistry confirms the cartilaginous component. Orcein shows only scattered elastic fibres. How to identify it at the exam Strongly eosinophilic collagen bundles plus chondrocytes in lacunae arranged in rows. No perichondrium and very little basophilic matrix. Hybrid look: half tendon, half cartilage. Site: intervertebral disc, symphysis, meniscus, tendon insertion. Differential diagnosis Tendon: parallel collagen bundles but spindle-shaped fibrocyte nuclei with sharp poles, never lacunae or rows of round cells. Hyaline cartilage: glassy basophilic matrix, perichondrium, isogenous groups. Elastic cartilage: elastic network and perichondrium on orcein. Bone: lamellar or trabecular eosinophilic matrix with osteocytes and canaliculi. Clinical / functional correlation Fibrocartilage is built for combined compression and tension, which is why the annulus fibrosus and the menisci are made of it; tears in the annulus let the nucleus pulposus herniate. The peripheral red zone of a meniscus has vessels and some healing capacity, while the inner white zone (pure fibrocartilage) does not — the anatomical basis of meniscal surgery. The interpubic fibrocartilage softens under relaxin in pregnancy to widen the birth canal."},{"id":"t-V-14","p":"V","n":14,"t":"Bone (ground section)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A ground section of compact lamellar bone ( os compactum ): an undecalcified slice of the diaphysis ground thin and mounted, showing osteons ( osteona ) as concentric lamellae around central Haversian canals, interstitial lamellae between them, Volkmann canals and empty osteocyte lacunae with their canaliculi. No cells survive the grinding, so the slide shows the architecture of the matrix only. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, transverse section through the compact cortex of a long bone (the tibial diaphysis is the classic block). Hard tissue cannot be cut on a routine microtome, so it is either ground thin without decalcification — the present slide — or decalcified and sectioned as soft tissue for HE. 2. Low-power architecture A solid, homogeneous matrix perforated by many small circular holes. Around each hole a set of concentric rings: this is an osteon . Between neighbouring osteons lie angular patches, the interstitial lamellae , the remnants of older generations of osteons. At the periosteal and endosteal surfaces, circumferential lamellae run parallel to the surface. Some canals run across the field, linking adjacent osteons — the Volkmann canals . 3. High-power key features Central (Haversian) canal: contains a capillary, a venule, a nerve and loose connective tissue; the osteon is the cylindrical unit of bone built around it. Concentric lamellae: 3–7 µm thick layers of mineralised matrix in which collagen fibres change direction from lamella to lamella, giving the \"plywood\" strength. Osteocyte lacunae: small oval spaces between the lamellae, appearing empty or dark in a ground section, with fine canaliculi radiating towards the canal. Volkmann canals run transversely and connect neighbouring osteon canals. Cement lines mark the outer limit of each osteon. Structure Appearance on a ground section Function Osteon (Haversian system) Concentric rings around a central hole Structural unit of compact bone Haversian canal Central hole with vessels Blood supply of osteocytes Concentric lamellae 3–7 µm rings of matrix Resist bending and torsion Interstitial lamellae Angular patches between osteons Remnants of remodelled osteons Volkmann canal Transverse canal crossing the lamellae Links osteon canals Osteocyte lacunae + canaliculi Empty oval spaces with fine radiating channels House osteocytes; lacuno-canalicular network 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud A ground section is not decalcified: in the simplest preparation it is mounted unstained and the lacunae and canaliculi appear dark against a translucent matrix, with the canals filled by debris. Many teaching sets stain the ground section with basic fuchsin , which colours the mineralised matrix red-pink and makes the canals and lamellae stand out. Von Kossa or alizarin red demonstrate calcium, and microradiography maps the mineral density. The decalcified HE slide is a different preparation: there the osteocytes are visible in their lacunae. How to identify it at the exam Concentric lamellae around central canals — osteons and nothing else. Empty osteocyte lacunae and fine canaliculi, no cells with nuclei. Interstitial and circumferential lamellae complete the architecture. Say the technique: undecalcified ground section, calcified matrix retained. Differential diagnosis Decalcified bone (HE): osteocytes visible in lacunae, eosinophilic matrix, marrow spaces, cutting cones. Calcified hyaline cartilage: chondrocytes in lacunae, no osteons or Haversian canals. Fibrocartilage: coarse collagen bundles and rows of chondrocytes. Tooth ground section: dentinal tubules and enamel, not osteons. Clinical / functional correlation Osteons are constantly renewed: in decalcified bone the remodelling front appears as a cutting cone of osteoclasts followed by a closing cone of osteoblasts. The orientation of lamellae and the density of osteons adapt to load; loss of trabeculae and thinning of the cortex is osteoporosis , while chaotic remodelling with mosaic lamellae is Paget disease . Ground sections are the standard technique for hard tissues in histology and forensic anthropology, and they preserve the mineral phase that ordinary processing dissolves."},{"id":"t-V-15","p":"V","n":15,"t":"Endochondral ossification (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A longitudinal section through a developing long bone or fetal rib stained with HE: a cartilaginous model is replaced by bone in an ordered sequence, so from the epiphysis towards the diaphysis the epiphyseal growth cartilage shows resting, proliferating, hypertrophic, calcified and eroded zones before the ossification zone, while a periosteal bone collar forms around the shaft and trabeculae are lined by osteoblasts and resorbed by osteoclasts. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, longitudinal section along the axis of a fetal long bone or a fetal rib; the epiphyseal growth cartilage ( cartilago epiphysialis ) is the line to follow, and the direction of growth is always from the epiphysis towards the diaphysis. The perichondrium becomes periosteum at the level of the bone collar, and if a rib is used, intercostal muscle and nerve appear beside it. 2. Low-power architecture At one end there is a cap of hyaline cartilage. Towards the shaft the cells line up in columns, the matrix darkens, and then the cartilage is replaced by a network of eosinophilic trabeculae with marrow spaces between them. On the outside of the shaft, deep to the periosteum, a rim of bone — the periosteal (subperiosteal) collar — has formed by intramembranous ossification. This collar cuts off cartilage nutrition and is the first step of endochondral ossification. 3. High-power key features The six zones, in order: resting (reserve) cartilage with scattered chondrocytes; proliferating zone with chondroblasts dividing into columns (isogenous lines); hypertrophic zone where chondrocytes enlarge several-fold, become pale and glycogen-rich, and the matrix between them narrows; calcified zone where the matrix becomes deeply basophilic; erosion zone where vessels, chondroclasts and osteoprogenitor cells invade the empty lacunae; and ossification zone where osteoblasts deposit osteoid on the remnants of calcified cartilage, forming primary trabeculae. Osteoclasts sit in Howship lacunae on the trabecular surfaces, and between the trabeculae the marrow space is established. Trabeculae therefore have a blue calcified cartilage core with a pink bone surface — a point examiners love. Zone Cells Matrix Event Resting Scattered chondrocytes Hyaline, basophilic Reserve of cartilage Proliferating Dividing chondroblasts in columns Basophilic Interstitial growth in length Hypertrophic Enlarged pale chondrocytes Narrow septa Preparation for calcification Calcified Degenerating chondrocytes Deeply basophilic Temporary mineralisation Erosion Chondroclasts, vessels, osteoprogenitor cells Lacunae opened Invasion of the cartilage Ossification Osteoblasts, osteoclasts Osteoid on cartilage cores Trabeculae and marrow space 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: cartilage (and especially calcified cartilage) is basophilic , whereas osteoid and mineralised bone are eosinophilic — so the alternating blue and pink layers of each trabecula are directly readable. PAS demonstrates the glycogen of hypertrophic chondrocytes; Alcian blue marks the cartilage GAGs; the osteoclasts can be highlighted by TRAP histochemistry. In trichrome preparations bone collagen is blue while calcified cartilage remains a different shade. How to identify it at the exam Bands of cartilage changing in a fixed order from the epiphysis to the diaphysis. Trabeculae with a basophilic cartilage core and an eosinophilic bone surface. Periosteal bone collar and periosteum on the shaft. Osteoblasts lining trabeculae and osteoclasts in Howship lacunae. Differential diagnosis Intramembranous ossification: bone trabeculae directly in condensed mesenchyme, no cartilage zones (skull vault, maxilla). Adult long bone: only osteons and marrow, no growth cartilage. Pure hyaline cartilage: no vessels, no trabeculae, no marrow spaces. Fracture callus: disorganised mixture of cartilage and woven bone at a fracture site, without the ordered zones. Clinical / functional correlation Rickets and osteomalacia are failures of mineralisation: the growth plate widens and osteoid accumulates unmineralised, so the bone bends. Achondroplasia impairs endochondral ossification (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3), giving short limbs with a normal skull. The hypertrophic-calcified zone is mechanically the weakest, which is why Salter–Harris type I fractures separate the epiphysis through it; closure of the plate ends growth in length."},{"id":"t-V-16","p":"V","n":16,"t":"Smooth muscle (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Smooth muscle ( textus muscularis nonstriatus ) stained with HE: bundles or sheets of spindle-shaped leiomyocytes ( leiomyocyti ) with medium-eosinophilic cytoplasm and a single central, elongated cigar-shaped nucleus; there is no cross-striation, and in cross-section nuclei are seen only in the widest cell profiles. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, taken from the wall of a hollow organ (intestine, ureter, uterus) or a blood vessel. Because smooth muscle bundles run in different directions, most slides show both longitudinal and transverse profiles in one field: look for the plane where the cells are long, and for the plane where the cells are round. 2. Low-power architecture Eosinophilic bundles with no visible striation and no regular parallel arrangement across the whole field. In the gut wall the bundles form layers; in the uterus they interlace. Connective tissue (endomysium) separates the bundles and carries small vessels, but it is far less prominent than the perimysium of skeletal muscle. 3. High-power key features Longitudinal profile: elongated spindle cells, roughly 5–10 µm wide and 20–50 µm long, whose tapered ends interdigitate with the broad parts of neighbouring cells; the nucleus is central, oval and blunt-ended (\"cigar-shaped\"), with a pale perinuclear endoplasm at its poles containing mitochondria and Golgi. Cross-section: irregular round or oval profiles of widely varying diameter, closely apposed; a nucleus is visible only in the largest profiles, and most profiles show nucleus-free homogeneous eosinophilic sarcoplasm. Myofilaments do not form myofibrils; they insert into dense bodies , and gap junctions spread the contraction from cell to cell. There are no T-tubules and no striation. Feature Smooth muscle Skeletal muscle Cardiac muscle Unit Leiomyocyte Muscle fibre (syncytium) Cardiomyocyte Shape Spindle with tapered ends Long unbranched cylinder Branched cylinder Nuclei One, central, cigar-shaped Many, peripheral One, central, perinuclear halo Striation Absent Present Present Control Autonomic, involuntary Somatic, voluntary Autonomic, involuntary 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: cytoplasm has medium eosinophilia — less intense than skeletal muscle — with basophilic nuclei. Iron haematoxylin reveals the fine longitudinal striations of the myofilament bundles and the dense bodies (it also blackens nuclei). PAS shows glycogen; immunohistochemistry for desmin or smooth-muscle actin is the modern way to prove the phenotype; a reticulin (silver) stain outlines the external lamina around each cell. How to identify it at the exam Spindle cells with central cigar-shaped nuclei and no cross-striation. Cross-section: variable-diameter round profiles, nuclei only in the widest. Bundles interlacing rather than running in one strict direction. Describe both planes of section — longitudinal and transverse — in the same answer. Differential diagnosis Skeletal muscle (longitudinal): cross-striation and peripheral nuclei. Cardiac muscle: central nuclei but branching cells and intercalated discs. Tendon: parallel collagen bundles with sharp-ended nuclei and no cytoplasm. Myofibroblasts or dense connective tissue: collagen dominates and the nuclei are more slender and wavy. Clinical / functional correlation The smooth muscle of vessel media and of hollow organs maintains resting tone, and its contraction is involuntary, slow, weak and tireless. Leiomyoma of the uterus is the commonest benign smooth-muscle tumour; leiomyosarcoma is malignant. Bronchial smooth-muscle contraction narrows the airway in asthma, and uterine leiomyocytes hypertrophy enormously in pregnancy — the same cell type in two very different clinical settings."},{"id":"t-V-17","p":"V","n":17,"t":"Skeletal muscle (cross section, HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section of skeletal muscle stained with HE: fascicles of polygonal muscle fibres ( myofibrae striatae ) of varying diameter, each with eosinophilic sarcoplasm and several flattened nuclei displaced to the periphery under the sarcolemma, with endomysium between the fibres and perimysium between the fascicles. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, cut across the long axis of the muscle fibres. This is the plane that shows fibre size, nuclei position and connective tissue sheaths best; the same block cut longitudinally gives the companion slide V.18. 2. Low-power architecture Skeletal muscle is organised hierarchically: epimysium on the surface of the muscle, perimysium separating fascicles and carrying vessels and nerves, and endomysium — loose connective tissue with capillaries — between individual fibres. In cross-section the fascicles look like islands of closely packed polygonal profiles divided by pink septa. 3. High-power key features Muscle fibres: round to polygonal profiles; diameters vary between fibres (10–100 µm range) because each fibre is an independent syncytium. Sarcoplasm: homogeneously eosinophilic with a fine stippling (myofibrils seen end-on); no cross-striation in this plane. Nuclei: several per fibre, flattened and pushed to the very margin beneath the sarcolemma — the single most important observation, because it excludes cardiac and smooth muscle. Capillaries with erythrocytes lie in the endomysium. An occasional muscle spindle may appear as a small encapsulated group of intrafusal fibres. Structure Appearance in cross-section Function Muscle fibre Polygonal profile, variable diameter, eosinophilic Contractile unit (syncytium) Nuclei Flattened, peripheral, several per fibre Transcriptional control of the syncytium Endomysium Fine pink tissue between fibres Nourishment, force transmission Perimysium Septum between fascicles, with vessels Bundles the fascicles, carries vessels Epimysium Connective tissue on the muscle surface Attachment to fascia 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: sarcoplasm is strongly eosinophilic and nuclei are basophilic ; the endomysial collagen is also eosinophilic but paler and less homogeneous. Iron haematoxylin blackens the nuclei and makes the myofibrillar stippling obvious. For fibre typing, ATPase or NADH histochemistry separates type I, IIA and IIB fibres, and PAS shows the glycogen of fast fibres. How to identify it at the exam Polygonal profiles with nuclei at the periphery, never in the centre. Several nuclei per fibre — it is a syncytium. Fascicles divided by perimysium; endomysium between fibres. No striation visible in this plane, so nuclei position is the discriminator. Differential diagnosis Cardiac muscle (cross-section): central nuclei with perinuclear pale endoplasm, branching, one capillary per cell. Smooth muscle (cross-section): variable-diameter round profiles, no fascicular organisation, nuclei central in the widest profiles. Peripheral nerve (cross-section): rings of myelin around axons, wavy fascicles, no homogeneous sarcoplasm. Dense irregular connective tissue: no polygonal profiles. Clinical / functional correlation Muscle biopsy is read in cross-section to assess fibre types, size variation and capillary density. Denervation produces group atrophy of fibres, and Duchenne dystrophy shows wide variation in fibre size, central nuclei and replacement by fat and fibrous tissue. Each fibre belongs to a single motor unit (one alpha motor neuron), which is why loss of a motor neuron atrophies a scattered group of fibres."},{"id":"t-V-18","p":"V","n":18,"t":"Skeletal muscle (longit. section, HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A longitudinal section of skeletal muscle stained with HE: long, parallel, unbranched muscle fibres with several peripheral nuclei and a distinct cross-striation produced by the sarcomeres, separated by delicate endomysium. Cross-striation plus peripheral nuclei is the definitive identification. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, cut parallel to the axis of the fibres. Several fibres run across the field as long eosinophilic ribbons; if the section is slightly oblique, the striation is still visible but the nuclei may appear within rather than at the border — always look at the best-oriented fibres. 2. Low-power architecture Parallel bundles of fibres with perimysium between the fascicles and endomysium between individual fibres. Near a myotendinous junction the fibres end in tapering tips and the tendon collagen begins; the Golgi tendon organ and the muscle spindle may be found near this region. 3. High-power key features Cross-striation: alternating dark A bands (myosin thick filaments, anisotropic) and light I bands (actin thin filaments, isotropic), with a thin Z line in the middle of each I band; the repeating unit — the sarcomere ( sarcomerum ) — is on average about 2.2–2.8 µm long. The A band contains a lighter H zone and a dark M line in its centre. Nuclei: multiple, oval, flattened, immediately under the sarcolemma at the fibre periphery. Sarcoplasm: filled with myofibrils, with rows of mitochondria between them and glycogen and myoglobin. Fibres are never branched and never joined by intercalated discs. Band / structure Composition Behaviour during contraction A band Myosin thick filaments; darker, anisotropic Constant length I band Actin thin filaments; lighter, isotropic Shortens H zone Centre of the A band, myosin only Narrows M line Middle of the H zone, myosin cross-links Unchanged Z line Middle of the I band, anchors actin Approach each other Sarcomere Z line to Z line, about 2.2–2.8 µm Shortens as a whole 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE shows the cross-striation faintly but reliably in well-fixed, well-oriented fibres: eosinophilic A bands alternate with paler I bands. Iron haematoxylin (Heidenhain) blackens the nuclei and greatly enhances the striation, which is why it is the classical stain for demonstrating the sarcomere. PAS shows glycogen granules, phosphorylase histochemistry and immunohistochemistry for myosin heavy chain allow fibre typing, and the sarcomere itself is measured by electron microscopy. How to identify it at the exam Cross-striation: alternating A and I bands along the fibre. Several nuclei, all peripheral, under the sarcolemma. Fibres long, parallel and unbranched. Iron haematoxylin is the stain that makes striation and nuclei unmistakable. Differential diagnosis Cardiac muscle: branched cells, central nucleus with perinuclear halo, intercalated discs. Smooth muscle: spindle cells with central cigar nuclei and no striation. Tendon: no cells inside the bundles and no striation. Peripheral nerve: wavy fibres and myelin, not homogeneous sarcoplasm with striation. Clinical / functional correlation The striation is the morphological expression of the sliding filament mechanism: actin slides over myosin, the I band and H zone shorten and the A band stays constant. Failure of calcium reuptake after death leaves actin and myosin locked — rigor mortis ; hypocalcaemia increases excitability and causes tetany. In myasthenia gravis antibodies block the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor, so the striated fibre is present but cannot be activated; in rhabdomyolysis, myoglobin released from these fibres can cause acute kidney injury."},{"id":"t-V-19","p":"V","n":19,"t":"Cardiac muscle (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Cardiac muscle ( myocardium ) stained with HE: branching cardiomyocytes ( cardiomyocyti ) joined end to end by intercalated discs , each with a single central nucleus surrounded by a pale perinuclear endoplasm, cross-striation in the sarcoplasm, and a very rich capillary network in the endomysium. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level; in the ventricular wall the cardiomyocytes run in a spiral, so one section usually contains fibres cut longitudinally, obliquely and transversely. The longitudinal fibres are needed to see striation and intercalated discs; the transverse ones show the central nucleus best. 2. Low-power architecture Eosinophilic fibre bundles separated by delicate connective tissue with many capillaries; no thick perimysium comparable with skeletal muscle. In the subendocardium, Purkinje fibres of the conducting system appear as large pale glycogen-rich cells, and epicardial fat and vessels may border the section. 3. High-power key features Cardiomyocyte: a cylinder about 85–100 µm long and 15 µm wide with one or two terminal cytoplasmic processes, so cells form a Y- or X-shaped network. Nucleus: single (exceptionally two), central , oval, dark, surrounded by a clear perinuclear zone ( endoplasm ) where organelles, glycogen, lipid droplets and the age pigment lipofuscin accumulate. Sarcoplasm: strongly eosinophilic, filled with myofibrils, showing cross-striation. Intercalated discs ( disci intercalares ): transverse, straight or step-like dark lines where two cells meet; they contain fascia adherens and desmosomes for mechanical coupling and gap junctions for electrical coupling. Endomysium: loose connective tissue with an extremely dense capillary network — the basis for the heart's dependence on aerobic metabolism. Feature Cardiac muscle Skeletal muscle Cell shape Branched cylinder, Y/X-shaped processes Long unbranched cylinder Nucleus One, central, with perinuclear endoplasm Many, peripheral Cell junctions Intercalated discs with gap junctions None between fibres Striation Present Present Control Own conducting system, modulated by autonomic nerves Voluntary, somatic motor neurons Regeneration None — repaired by scar Limited, via satellite cells 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: sarcoplasm is strongly eosinophilic ; nuclei are basophilic; lipofuscin is a brown, non-staining pigment seen in older hearts. Iron haematoxylin is the classical stain for striation and intercalated discs. PAS demonstrates glycogen, and immunohistochemistry for cardiac troponin or desmin confirms myocardial identity. In a phosphotungstic acid–haematoxylin or semi-thin section, the disc appears as a dark transverse band of stair-like shape. How to identify it at the exam Branching fibres with a central nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo. Intercalated discs — transverse dark lines between cells. Cross-striation present, so it is striated muscle. Very rich capillary network in the endomysium. Differential diagnosis Skeletal muscle: unbranched fibres, many peripheral nuclei, no intercalated discs. Smooth muscle: no striation, central cigar-shaped nuclei, spindle cells. Purkinje fibres of the conducting system: much larger, pale, glycogen-rich cells with fewer myofibrils. Artefactual contraction bands: dark transverse bands after death or injury, not true intercalated discs. Clinical / functional correlation Cardiomyocytes are post-mitotic: after myocardial infarction the necrotic focus is replaced by a collagen scar, which cannot contract, so ejection fraction falls. Lipofuscin accumulates with age (\"wear-and-tear pigment\"); hypertrophy enlarges the nuclei and the fibre diameter. Atrial cardiomyocytes secrete atrial natriuretic peptide, and cardiac troponin released from damaged cells is the standard blood test for myocardial necrosis."},{"id":"t-V-20","p":"V","n":20,"t":"Cardiac muscle (iron hematoxylin)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A myocardium preparation stained with Heidenhain's iron haematoxylin: the dye blackens the nuclei, the rows of mitochondria and the cross-striation, producing a high-contrast image in which the sarcomeres and the step-like intercalated discs stand out clearly, so cardiac muscle can be proved striated and distinguished from smooth muscle. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Tissue level, high-power cytology; longitudinal and transverse fibres are both present in the same section. The purpose of the slide is contrast, not architecture: the examiner shows it when the question is \"what kind of muscle is this and how do you prove it\". 2. Low-power architecture Bundles of fibres with black stippling and black nuclei against a paler background; connective tissue septa and capillaries are much less conspicuous than the muscle itself. The overall arrangement is the same as in the HE slide of myocardium, so orientation is easy. 3. High-power key features Nuclei are black and central, often with the paler perinuclear endoplasm visible as a halo. Between the myofibrils, rows of mitochondria appear as black dots and dashes, giving the sarcoplasm a stippled, striated look. The cross-striation (A and I bands) is sharper than on HE. Intercalated discs are transverse dark lines, straight or step-like, at the junction of neighbouring cells. Branching of the cells and the rich capillary network remain visible, and the central position of the nuclei excludes skeletal muscle. Feature On HE On iron haematoxylin Nuclei Basophilic blue-violet Black, very sharply outlined Cross-striation Faintly visible when well oriented Strong contrast, bands easy to count Mitochondria Not resolved Black rows between myofibrils Intercalated discs Visible as pale or dark transverse lines Sharply black transverse lines Sarcoplasm Strongly eosinophilic Pale to grey with black stippling 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud Heidenhain's ferric (iron) haematoxylin is a mordant dye complex that stains nuclear structures, the striation of skeletal and cardiac muscle, and mitochondria black ; it is used with careful differentiation, so only the structures of interest retain the dye. It can be combined with a counterstain (for example eosin or van Gieson) that colours collagen and cytoplasm. This is the classical method for demonstrating the sarcomere and for showing mitochondrial accumulations, for example the \"ragged red fibres\" of mitochondrial myopathy when combined with a modified Gomori trichrome. How to identify it at the exam Black, sharply outlined central nuclei with perinuclear halos. Black stippling between myofibrils = mitochondria. Cross-striation and step-like intercalated discs are conspicuous. Branching cells with a dense capillary network — cardiac, not skeletal. Differential diagnosis Skeletal muscle with iron haematoxylin: peripheral nuclei, long unbranched fibres, no discs. Smooth muscle with iron haematoxylin: longitudinal striations of myofilament bundles and dense bodies, but no cross-striation and central cigar nuclei. HE myocardium: lower contrast; the striation may be missed if the section is thick. Necrotic myocardium: hypereosinophilic fibres with loss of striation and nuclear lysis — a pathological change, not the normal slide. Clinical / functional correlation Early after infarction, cardiomyocytes show contraction bands — intensely eosinophilic transverse bands produced by hypercontraction — that must not be confused with intercalated discs. Because the myocardium is so dependent on oxidative metabolism, mitochondrial stains are used to demonstrate mitochondrial disease, and the density of mitochondria explains the vulnerability to ischaemia. The intercalated disc is the site where gap junctions allow the contraction wave to spread, so its integrity underlies coordinated systolic function."},{"id":"t-V-21","p":"V","n":21,"t":"Sensory ganglion (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A sensory ganglion ( ganglion sensorium ), typically a spinal ganglion, stained with HE: clusters of large, round pseudounipolar neurons ( neuronum pseudounipolare ) with pale vesicular nuclei and prominent nucleoli, each surrounded by a ring of satellite glial cells , with bundles of nerve fibres running between the nerve cell bodies and a connective tissue capsule around the organ. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level: the section cuts through the ganglion along the course of the posterior root, so the fibrous capsule, the clusters of neurons and the entering/leaving nerve fibres are all visible. The neurons are more concentrated towards the periphery of the ganglion, with the fibre bundles running through the centre. 2. Low-power architecture A nodular organ bounded by a dense connective tissue capsule that continues into the epineurium of the nerve and, towards the central nervous system, into the dura mater. Inside, small round neuron bodies form groups separated by wavy nerve fibres and delicate connective tissue. There is no cortex, no medulla and no ependymal canal — the architecture is a mixture of neurons and fibres, not layers. 3. High-power key features Pseudounipolar neuron: large, spherical perikaryon with a single process that bifurcates into a peripheral and a central branch (not visible in the section). The nucleus is large, pale and vesicular with a very prominent, dark nucleolus — the \"owl-eye\" appearance. The cytoplasm contains clumped basophilic Nissl substance , mostly at the periphery, and sometimes lipofuscin. Satellite glial cells form a complete single-layered capsule around each neuron and are separated from it by a basal lamina. Between the neurons, nerve fibres run in bundles with Schwann cells, and no synapses are present inside a sensory ganglion. Cell / structure Light-microscopic appearance Function Pseudounipolar neuron Large round cell, pale nucleus, prominent nucleolus, peripheral Nissl substance Carries somatosensory information to the CNS Satellite glial cells Small dark nuclei in a ring around each neuron Support and insulation of the perikaryon Nerve fibres Wavy bundles between the neurons Peripheral and central processes Capsule Dense collagenous connective tissue Mechanical protection Bipolar neurons Found in the cochlear and vestibular ganglia Hearing and balance 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: the neuronal cytoplasm is pale and the Nissl clumps are basophilic, so the large round cells with pale nuclei and dark nucleoli are obvious; satellite cells show small dark nuclei. Cresyl violet or Nissl staining makes the Nissl substance and nucleolus deep purple and is the classic way to demonstrate chromatolysis. Silver impregnation shows the T-shaped process and the capsule of fibres; S100 or GFAP immunohistochemistry marks the satellite cells, and the myelin is pale and unstained on HE. How to identify it at the exam Large round neurons with pale nuclei and prominent nucleoli, grouped in clusters. A complete ring of small satellite-cell nuclei around each neuron body. Nerve fibre bundles between the neurons; a connective tissue capsule around the organ. No synapses and no neuropil-rich grey matter with dendrites — this is not CNS. Differential diagnosis Autonomic (sympathetic or parasympathetic) ganglion: multipolar neurons of irregular shape, several dendrites, satellite cells not forming a complete capsule, and synapses present. Spinal cord grey matter: anterior horn motor neurons are multipolar and embedded in neuropil with a central canal nearby. Peripheral nerve: only fibres and sheaths, no neuron bodies. Adrenal medulla: chromaffin cells in nests, no pseudounipolar neurons. Clinical / functional correlation Varicella-zoster virus becomes latent in sensory ganglia and reactivates as shingles in the corresponding dermatome. Compression or inflammation of a dorsal root ganglion produces radicular pain and sensory loss; the ganglion has no blood–brain barrier, which is why some viruses and drugs reach it easily. Chromatolysis — dispersion of Nissl substance with a pale swollen perikaryon — is the histological response to axon injury, and it is demonstrated with Nissl staining."},{"id":"t-V-22","p":"V","n":22,"t":"Spinal cord (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A transverse section of the spinal cord ( medulla spinalis ) stained with HE: a central, H-shaped mass of grey matter ( substantia grisea ) containing multipolar motor neurons in the anterior horns, surrounded by a peripheral ring of white matter funiculi, with the central canal lined by ependyma in the midline. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, transverse (horizontal) section through a spinal segment. The shape of the grey matter tells you the approximate level: the anterior horns are largest where the plexuses of the limbs arise (cervical and lumbar enlargements), and the amount of white matter increases cranially. The anterior median fissure and the posterior median sulcus orient the section. 2. Low-power architecture A butterfly- or H-shaped central region of grey matter surrounding the central canal , and a broad peripheral region of white matter divided into anterior, lateral and posterior funiculi . The anterior horns are broad and rounded, the posterior horns slender and reaching the surface. Pia mater and vessels are seen along the surface, and the anterior median fissure contains loose connective tissue and vessels. 3. High-power key features Anterior horn: large multipolar alpha and gamma motor neurons — the largest cells in the cord (roughly 80–100 µm) — with abundant Nissl substance, a pale nucleus and a prominent nucleolus; their axons leave through the anterior root. Lateral horn: visceromotor neurons at the segments C8–L3 and S2–S4. Posterior horn: smaller neurons and interneurons with many incoming sensory fibres. Central canal: lined by a single layer of cuboidal-to-columnar ependyma and filled with cerebrospinal fluid. White matter: bundles of myelinated axons with oligodendrocytes; in HE both grey and white matter look pink, differing only in intensity and cellularity. Region Contents Function Anterior horn Multipolar alpha and gamma motor neurons Somatic motor output to skeletal muscle Lateral horn Visceromotor neurons (C8–L3, S2–S4) Autonomic output Posterior horn Sensory relay neurons and interneurons Processing of somatosensory input Central canal Cuboidal to columnar ependyma Contains cerebrospinal fluid White matter funiculi Myelinated ascending and descending tracts Long-distance conduction 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: both grey and white matter appear pink, the grey matter is more cellular and the white matter more homogeneous; this is the point examiners like, because \"grey and white\" are macroscopic terms, not staining colours. Nissl (cresyl violet) stains the neuronal perikarya purple and leaves the white matter pale; Luxol fast blue stains myelin blue; silver impregnation shows axons; GFAP immunohistochemistry shows astrocytes and the glial scar. How to identify it at the exam H-shaped or butterfly-shaped central grey matter with a central canal. Large multipolar motor neurons concentrated in the broad anterior horns. Peripheral white matter funiculi; anterior median fissure. No folia, no six-layered cortex — those belong to cerebellum and cerebrum. Differential diagnosis Medulla oblongata: no regular H-shaped grey matter; instead nuclei such as the inferior olive and decussating tracts. Cerebellum: leaf-like folia with a three-layered cortex. Cerebral cortex: a six-layered ribbon on the surface with white matter beneath, no central canal. Sensory ganglion: clusters of round pseudounipolar neurons, not an H-shaped grey matter. Clinical / functional correlation Loss of anterior horn cells causes lower motor neuron signs — weakness, atrophy and fasciculation — as in poliomyelitis and in the lower motor neuron component of ALS . Expansion of the central canal ( syringomyelia ) first damages the crossing spinothalamic fibres, producing a cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation. Lumbar puncture is performed below the termination of the cord, and the level of the lesion can be inferred from the segmental pattern of sensory and motor loss."},{"id":"t-V-23","p":"V","n":23,"t":"Cerebral cortex (Cresyl-violet)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer A Cresyl-violet (\"Nissl\") preparation of the cerebral cortex ( cortex cerebri ): the basic dye stains neuronal nuclei, nucleoli and the Nissl substance purple-blue, so the six laminae of the neocortex and the pyramidal cells — including the giant Betz cells of the motor cortex — are clearly displayed, while the white matter beneath stays pale. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level, a strip of cortex cut perpendicular to the pial surface, usually including a gyrus with its overlying pia and the underlying white matter. The grey matter of the cerebral cortex is only 2–5 mm thick, so orientation from pia to white matter is essential and easy: pia at the surface, then the layers, then the paler white matter. 2. Low-power architecture A band of grey matter with six ill-defined horizontal layers, limited externally by the pia and internally by the white matter. In Cresyl-violet preparations the neuronal perikarya appear as purple dots, most densely packed in the granular layers; the molecular layer at the surface is pale, and the white matter is almost unstained because myelin does not take up the dye. 3. High-power key features The six laminae, from the surface: I molecular (few cells, many tangential fibres); II external granular (small neurons); III external pyramidal (pyramidal cells, larger towards the depth); IV internal granular (densely packed small granular cells; thickest in sensory areas); V internal pyramidal (large pyramidal cells, including the Betz cells up to about 100 µm in the motor cortex, the origin of the corticospinal tract); VI multiform (mixed fusiform and pyramidal cells merging into the white matter). Pyramidal cells are triangular with an apical dendrite pointing to the surface and basal dendrites; granular cells are small and round. Nissl substance in the perikarya is the purple cytoplasmic material, and the nucleolus is a prominent dark dot inside the pale nucleus. Lamina Name Predominant cells / character I Molecular layer Few cells; tangential fibres and apical dendrites II External granular layer Small granular and pyramidal cells III External pyramidal layer Pyramidal cells; association and commissural output IV Internal granular layer Densely packed granular cells; main input layer V Internal pyramidal layer Large pyramidal and Betz cells; main output layer VI Multiform layer Mixed spindle, oval and pyramidal cells; borders white matter 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud Cresyl violet is a basic (cationic) dye that binds RNA and DNA, so it stains the nucleus, the nucleolus and the rough endoplasmic reticulum (the Nissl substance) purple-blue; it is one of the standard Nissl stains alongside toluidine blue and thionine. The technique is the logical way to map neuronal cell bodies and their layers, and the pale white matter is the internal control. In the combined Luxol-blue–cresyl-violet preparation, myelin is blue while neurons are purple, so grey and white matter are shown in different colours in one slide. Chromatolysis after axon injury, with dispersion of Nissl substance, is demonstrated with the same stain. How to identify it at the exam Six layers of purple neuronal perikarya arranged in horizontal laminae. Triangular pyramidal cells with apical dendrites pointing to the pia. Pale white matter beneath the cortex; pia on the surface. Cresyl violet = nuclei, nucleoli and Nissl substance purple; myelin pale. Differential diagnosis Cerebellar cortex: three layers, with a single row of huge pear-shaped Purkinje cells and an extremely dense granular layer. Spinal cord: H-shaped grey matter and a central canal, no cortical layers. Hippocampus (allocortex): three or four layers, no six-layered neocortex. Sensory ganglion: clustered large pseudounipolar neurons with satellite capsules. Clinical / functional correlation Cytoarchitecture is the basis of the Brodmann map : the thickness and cellularity of the laminae differ between motor, sensory and association areas, and the motor cortex is identified by its giant Betz cells in layer V. Hypoxic-ischaemic injury produces cortical laminar necrosis in the metabolically most active layers, and Betz cell loss explains the corticospinal signs of motor neuron disease. The cortex depends almost entirely on oxidative metabolism, so blood flow interruption causes irreversible neuronal death within minutes."},{"id":"t-V-24","p":"V","n":24,"t":"Cerebellum (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer Cerebellar cortex ( cortex cerebellaris ) in HE: a three-layered cortex on leaf-like folia — an outer pale molecular layer , a middle Purkinje cell layer of large pear-shaped neurons in a single row, and an inner, intensely basophilic granular layer packed with tiny granule cells — over a core of branching white matter. 1. Level of organisation and plane of section Organ level. In a transverse section the folia look like leaves or fingers, each with a white-matter core and a three-layered cortex on both sides; in a sagittal section the branching white matter forms the arbor vitae . The pia follows the surface into the fissures between the folia. 2. Low-power architecture Alternating leaf-like profiles: pale molecular layer outside, a thin darker line (Purkinje cells) at the interface, an intensely dark granular layer inside, then pale white matter. Because the granular layer is so basophilic, the cortex stands out as a dark ribbon outlining every folium — the easiest low-power recognition feature in the whole nervous system set. 3. High-power key features Molecular layer: pale, cell-sparse, with the richly branched flattened dendritic trees of Purkinje cells, parallel fibres, and scattered stellate and basket cells near the deeper part. Purkinje cell layer: one row of very large, pear- or bottle-shaped cells — about 50–70 µm by 35 µm — with a pale nucleus and a very prominent nucleolus; their axons are the only output of the cerebellar cortex and project to the cerebellar nuclei. Granular layer: enormously densely packed small granule cells with dark nuclei (the darkest layer in the brain), plus Golgi cells and cerebellar glomeruli. White matter: myelinated axons with oligodendrocytes. Bergmann glia (specialised astrocytes) radiate through the Purkinje layer. Layer Cells and fibres Appearance on HE Output Molecular Stellate and basket cells; parallel and climbing fibres; Purkinje dendrites Pale, few nuclei Modulates Purkinje cells Purkinje cell layer Purkinje cells in a single row; Bergmann glia Large pale cells with dark nucleoli in one line Only output of the cerebellar cortex Granular Granule cells (excitatory), Golgi cells, glomeruli Intensely basophilic, dense nuclei Parallel fibres to Purkinje cells White matter Myelinated axons; cerebellar nuclei deeper Pale, homogeneous Afferent and efferent tracts 4. Staining behaviour — how to say it aloud HE: the granular layer is strongly basophilic because of the dense nuclei; the molecular layer is pale and eosinophilic; the white matter is pale. Cresyl violet/Nissl makes the Purkinje cells and granule cells stand out and is the routine stain for counting them; Luxol fast blue colours the white matter blue; silver impregnation shows the basket-cell axons and the climbing fibres; calbindin immunohistochemistry specifically labels Purkinje cells. How to identify it at the exam Three cortical layers: pale molecular, one row of large Purkinje cells, dark granular layer. Leaf-like folia with a white-matter core; arbor vitae on sagittal sections. Purkinje cells are the largest and palest neurons in a single line at the layer interface. No six-layered cortex, no central canal, no H-shaped grey matter. Differential diagnosis Cerebral cortex (neocortex): six layers, pyramidal cells, no dense granular layer of this type. Hippocampus: tightly packed dentate gyrus and a single curved pyramidal layer, not folia. Spinal cord: H-shaped grey matter and a central canal. Cerebellar nuclei: islands of grey matter inside the white matter without cortical lamination. Clinical / functional correlation Purkinje cells are the most vulnerable neurons of the cerebellum: alcohol, phenytoin and paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration all cause their loss and produce ataxia and dysmetria. Medulloblastoma arises from the granule-cell lineage of the external granular layer, which is why it is a tumour of childhood. The cerebellum coordinates the timing and accuracy of movement and maintains equilibrium — the functional counterpart of its three-layered cortex and arbor vitae."},{"id":"t-V-25","p":"V","n":25,"t":"Vegetative ganglion (Ag)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a whole autonomic (vegetative) ganglion : a peripheral collection of multipolar postganglionic neuron bodies with their satellite glial cells, embedded in bundles of preganglionic and postganglionic nerve fibres and enclosed in a connective tissue capsule. Prepared by silver impregnation (Ag) , it is recognised by multipolar neurons, each with a pale nucleus and one prominent dark nucleolus, scattered among nerve fibres with an argyrophilic network and relatively incomplete satellite-cell sheaths. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level: a whole small ganglion — a paravertebral ganglion of the sympathetic trunk, a prevertebral ganglion beside the aorta (coeliac, mesenteric), or an intramural ganglion of the enteric nervous system in the wall of the gut. The section may be cut in any plane. The capsule is continuous with the epineurium of the nerve roots that enter and leave the nodule, so a short piece of nerve trunk is usually attached to one pole. The slide code Ag stands for ganglion autonomicum . 2. Low-power architecture From outside in there is a capsule of dense irregular collagenous connective tissue, then thin septa of loose connective tissue carrying blood vessels, then the neuronal parenchyma. The neuron bodies are pale, rounded islands of unequal size; between them run broad wavy fascicles of nerve fibres. Compared with a sensory ganglion the stroma is abundant and the neurons look scattered , not nested. Table 1 — The elements of an autonomic ganglion Element Low power High power Function Capsule and septa Dense pink rim with thin pink septa entering the organ Collagen bundles, elongated fibroblast nuclei Protection; continuous with the epineurium Neuron bodies Pale islands of unequal size Multipolar, eccentric pale nucleus, dark nucleolus, lipofuscin Postganglionic visceromotor neuron Nerve fibres Wavy pale fascicles between the neurons Myelinated fibres: empty myelin rings around a coloured axon; unmyelinated fibres: small groups of dots inside one Schwann cell Preganglionic inflow, postganglionic outflow Satellite cells Not resolvable Thin, often incomplete glial ring around the perikaryon Metabolic support of the neuron Stroma and vessels Loose and vascular Loose collagen, capillaries, occasional adipocytes Vasa nervorum 3. High-power key features Neuron body: large, oval to stellate; the nucleus is eccentric (off-centre), pale and vesicular, with one prominent dark nucleolus — the classic \"owl's eye\". Cytoplasm: pale and finely granular from Nissl (tigroid) substance , which is denser towards the periphery; yellow-brown lipofuscin granules may be stacked at one pole, more with age. Processes: several short dendrites leave the body and radiate into the surrounding tissue; the single axon is thin and cannot be followed. Multipolarity itself is the criterion that excludes a pseudounipolar sensory neuron. Satellite (amphicyte) cells: a thin glial sheath surrounds the perikaryon, but it is less complete than in a sensory ganglion, which is why the autonomic neuron looks relatively bare. Two populations of fibres: preganglionic fibres are myelinated and therefore appear as pale, optically empty rings around a coloured axonal dot; postganglionic fibres are unmyelinated and are seen only as clusters of fine dots in the Schwann cell cytoplasm. 4. Reading the colours In a silver preparation the neurons, their processes and the neurofibrils are black to dark brown against a pale background: the nucleolus and the argyrophilic network are the darkest points, the neuropil and connective tissue stay pale, and myelin is only weakly argyrophilic. A haematoxylin counterstain, where used, colours nuclei blue. There is no epithelium, no keratin, no cartilage and no muscle except the thin media of intraganglionic vessels — so every large pale cell with a big nucleolus on this slide is a ganglion cell, and every capsule is connective tissue, never a perineurium-delimited fascicle. How to identify it at the exam A cluster of multipolar neuron bodies with an eccentric nucleus and a single dark nucleolus, lying in abundant nerve-fibre bundles and loose connective tissue. The neurons are not in tight nests; there is plenty of stroma between them — the opposite of a spinal ganglion. A distinct capsule plus attached nerve bundles proves it is a ganglion and not a piece of central grey matter. No lymphoid tissue, no epithelium, no cartilage: the differential list is short. Differential diagnosis Sensory (spinal) ganglion: neurons are pseudounipolar and round, with a central nucleus and abundant Nissl substance; bodies sit in tight nests separated by fibre bundles, and satellite cells form a complete ring. Autonomic neurons are multipolar, eccentric, scattered. Spinal cord grey matter: multipolar neurons are present, but there is no capsule, no attached nerve fascicles and no perineuronal satellite capsule; neuropil and white matter are visible instead. Peripheral nerve in cross section: contains no neuron bodies at all, only fascicles bounded by perineurium. Adrenal medulla or paraganglion: cords of secretory cells of neural crest origin, without large multipolar neurons; needs special stains to prove. Clinical and functional note The ganglion is the synaptic relay of the autonomic pathway: the preganglionic (myelinated) axon releases acetylcholine on the postganglionic (mostly unmyelinated) neuron, which then releases noradrenaline (sympathetic) or acetylcholine (parasympathetic). Because transmission here is nicotinic, ganglion-blocking drugs interrupt the whole visceromotor pathway distal to the ganglion. Hirschsprung disease is congenital absence of intramural ganglion cells in a distal segment of colon: the aganglionic segment cannot relax, so the colon above it dilates into a megacolon."},{"id":"t-V-26","p":"V","n":26,"t":"Peripheral nerve (longit. section, HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a peripheral nerve cut longitudinally and stained with HE: parallel, slightly wavy fascicles of nerve fibres separated by connective tissue sheaths — epineurium around the whole nerve, perineurium around each fascicle and endoneurium between the individual fibres. In HE the axoplasm is eosinophilic while the lipid myelin is dissolved during preparation, so the nerve reads as bundles of pink wavy ribbons separated by pale clefts and dotted with elongated blue nuclei. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level. A peripheral nerve is a cord of fascicles , each a bundle of nerve fibres (axons with their Schwann sheaths), wrapped in three named connective tissue sheaths. A longitudinal section displays the fibres along their length, so internodes, nodes and the wavy course of the bundles are seen in profile; a peripheral nerve is usually mixed (somatic motor, somatic and visceral sensory, and autonomic fibres), and axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites in a routine section. 2. Low-power architecture Outside is the epineurium , a dense irregular collagenous envelope that sends septa between the fascicles; between the fascicles lie loose connective tissue, adipose tissue and the vasa nervorum . Each fascicle is bounded by a thin, sharply defined perineurium . The fascicles differ in calibre, interweave and branch, which gives the whole nerve its wavy, rope-like appearance. The nerve is often accompanied by an artery and a vein in a neurovascular bundle. Table 1 — The three connective tissue sheaths of a peripheral nerve Sheath Position Composition Role Epineurium (epineurium) Around the whole nerve; septa between fascicles Dense irregular collagen with adipose tissue; carries vasa nervorum and nervi nervorum Mechanical protection, allows the fascicles to glide Perineurium (perineurium) Around each fascicle Outer fibrous collagen layer plus concentric lamellae of flattened perineurial fibroblasts joined by tight junctions, resting on a basal lamina Blood-nerve barrier : diffusion barrier between perineurial and endoneurial tissue fluid; also the main tensile sheath Endoneurium (endoneurium) Between the single fibres inside a fascicle Reticular (type III collagen) connective tissue with capillaries Support and nutrition of individual fibres 3. High-power key features Nerve fibres are closely packed, wavy eosinophilic bands. Because the myelin lipids are extracted, the myelin appears as pale, often optically empty clefts between darker axonal ribbons — do not mistake these for oedema. Nodes of Ranvier are transverse constrictions interrupting the myelin at intervals along the fibre; Schmidt–Lantermann incisures are oblique pale notches within a single internode. Nuclei: elongated Schwann cell nuclei lie on the surface of the fibres and are aligned with them; perineurial fibroblast nuclei form one or two rows at the border of each fascicle; endothelial nuclei of capillaries and scattered adipocytes complete the picture. Artefact: after poor fixation the myelin may retract into globes (\"myelin ovoids\"), which must not be read as degeneration. 4. Reading the colours HE leaves the myelin unstained, so the colour code is simple: nuclei blue-violet, axoplasm and collagen pink, adipose vacuoles empty. This is precisely why a longitudinal HE nerve cannot show myelin directly — the companion slide with osmium tetroxide is the one on which the myelin is black. How to identify it at the exam Parallel, wavy bundles of eosinophilic fibres with a perineurium outlining each fascicle — the single most reliable feature. Nuclei are elongated and aligned along the fibres (Schwann cells, perineurial fibroblasts), never central and cigar-shaped as in smooth muscle. No epithelium, no muscle, no glands, no lumen; the only \"spaces\" are dissolved myelin and adipose. Vasa nervorum in the epineurium confirm that this is a nerve trunk and not a tendon. Differential diagnosis Smooth muscle, longitudinal section: spindle cells with a single central cigar-shaped nucleus and homogeneous eosinophilic cytoplasm; there is no perineurium and no fascicular architecture, and the cells form one continuous sheet. Tendon: dense regular collagen bundles are strongly eosinophilic, but only rows of fibroblast nuclei lie between them; there are no axons, no endoneurium and no perineurium, and the bundles are straight rather than wavy. Skeletal muscle, longitudinal section: cross-striations and multiple peripheral nuclei; the fibres are much thicker and unbranched. Peripheral nerve, cross section: same organ, but the fascicles are round and the fibres are dots, not ribbons. Clinical and functional note After transection the distal stump undergoes Wallerian degeneration ; surviving Schwann cells form longitudinal cords ( Büngner's bands ) that guide the regenerating sprouts, whose tangled front is the Perroncito apparatus — both are named in the nervous tissue lectures. Regeneration fails when the endoneurial tubes are not aligned, which is why a sharp cut repairs better than a crush and why a neuroma may form at the proximal stump. Compression neuropathies (carpal tunnel) damage the perineurial blood-nerve barrier and cause endoneurial oedema, so the nerve swells inside an unyielding tunnel."},{"id":"t-V-27","p":"V","n":27,"t":"Peripheral nerve (cross section, HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a peripheral nerve in transverse section , HE: a rounded cord divided by epineurial septa into fascicles , and inside each fascicle each myelinated fibre appears as a pale myelin ring around a coloured axonal dot — the classic \"honeycomb\" or \"target\" picture — with the fascicle bordered by the concentric lamellae of the perineurium . 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse plane. The plane of section is the reason the same organ looks completely different from V.26: instead of ribbons there are thousands of small circular fibre profiles cut across. The cord is polyfascicular ; the number and size of fascicles vary from nerve to nerve and along the same nerve. 2. Low-power architecture The nerve is round to oval, its surface formed by the epineurium ; connective tissue septa radiate inwards and separate fascicles of very different calibre; adipose tissue and vessels lie in the epineurial spaces. Each fascicle has a distinct, thin, eosinophilic rim — the perineurium. A companion artery or vein may lie beside the nerve in the same connective tissue sheath. Table 1 — What you see in a transverse nerve section Structure Light-microscopic appearance Note Epineurium Dense pink envelope and pink septa between fascicles; adipose vacuoles and vessels in the gaps Carries vasa nervorum Perineurium Narrow rim of several concentric layers of flattened cells with elongated nuclei, plus collagen Blood-nerve barrier; the fascicle's outer limit Myelinated fibre Central axonal dot surrounded by a pale, optically empty myelin space and a thin Schwann sheath Myelin dissolved by routine processing Unmyelinated fibre Small groups of fine dots inside one Schwann cell, without a myelin space Mostly autonomic postganglionic fibres Endoneurium Pale reticular connective tissue between the fibre profiles Contains the capillary bed 3. High-power key features Fibre profiles: a central pink axonal dot, a wide pale halo (the dissolved myelin), then the thin Schwann sheath. Fibres of large diameter have proportionally thicker myelin. Perineurium: several concentric lamellae of extremely flattened fibroblasts with elongated nuclei, joined by tight junctions and resting on basal laminae — the \" pars epithelioidea \" of the perineurium. Endoneurium: delicate collagen between the fibres, with occasional endoneurial capillaries and mast cells. Artefact: the empty myelin spaces can mimic severe endoneurial oedema; true oedema widens the whole endoneurial space and separates the fibres. 4. Reading the colours The blue nuclei belong to Schwann cells, perineurial fibroblasts, endothelial cells and the sparse cells of the endoneurium; the pink is axoplasm and collagen. Nothing is black, because HE does not preserve myelin — the black myelin ring appears only after osmium tetroxide (V.29). How to identify it at the exam A dot-in-a-ring (\"target\") pattern repeated over a whole fascicle: that is a myelinated nerve in cross section. A multi-layered cellular perineurium delimiting each fascicle — no other tissue in this series has this structure. No lumen, no epithelium, no cartilage: only connective tissue sheaths and fibre profiles. Compare directly with V.26: the same three sheaths, but the fibres are cut across. Differential diagnosis Artery: has a true lumen with an endothelial lining and a muscular media; the \"rings\" are not individual fibres but one elastic membrane. Vein: lumen, thin irregular wall, no fascicular architecture. Adipose tissue: round empty spaces, but each is one large vacuole with a nucleus squeezed at the margin; no perineurium, no axonal dots. Spinal cord white matter: myelinated fibres too, but no perineurium and no epineurium — it belongs to the CNS, and glial nuclei (oligodendrocytes) replace Schwann cells. Clinical and functional note The perineurium is the anatomical basis of the blood-nerve barrier ; its tight junctions keep proteins and circulating antibodies out of the endoneurial space. Inflammatory demyelinating neuropathies (Guillain–Barré syndrome, diphtheria) attack the Schwann cell and myelin; the nerve conduction slows while the axon remains intact. Nerve biopsy is reported on transverse sections, where the density of myelinated fibres per fascicle can be measured."},{"id":"t-V-28","p":"V","n":28,"t":"Peripheral nerve (longit. section, Os)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a peripheral nerve, longitudinal, treated with osmium tetroxide : the unsaturated lipids of the myelin sheath reduce osmium tetroxide to black reduced osmium , so the myelin segments are black, the axon inside them is pale, and the nodes of Ranvier appear as pale gaps between consecutive black internodes. There is no counterstain, so the image is essentially a black-on-yellow image of lipid. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, longitudinal plane, demonstrated with a lipid (osmium) method rather than a routine stain. Osmium tetroxide is both a fixative and a stain: it is reduced by unsaturated lipids to black osmium dioxide, so myelin and fat droplets become black while protein structures remain pale yellow-brown. It is the classical way to demonstrate myelin sheaths and fat. 2. Low-power architecture The nerve is a cord of parallel fascicles in which each fibre is not uniformly dark but segmented : black internodes alternate with short pale gaps, so the fascicles look beaded or cross-striped. Between the fibres, connective tissue is pale because it contains little lipid; the epineurium may contain black adipose droplets. Perineurium and epineurium are therefore seen as pale bands framing the dark fascicles. Table 1 — Osmium tetroxide: what turns black and why Structure Colour after Os Reason Myelin sheath Black Lipid-rich (about 80 % lipid) and reduces osmium tetroxide Axon Pale, visible as a lighter core Protein-rich, little lipid Node of Ranvier Pale transverse gap Bare axolemma between two Schwann cells; no myelin Schmidt–Lantermann incisures Oblique pale notches within a black internode Clefts where the myelin lamellae are separated by Schwann cytoplasm Adipose tissue Black droplets Triglyceride lipid Collagen, nuclei Pale yellow-brown to unstained Not lipid; nuclei need a counterstain to be seen 3. High-power key features Each fibre is a chain of black cylinders : one cylinder is one internode, i.e. the myelin formed by one Schwann cell. The axon runs as a pale line in the centre of the black cylinder; at the node the black sheath stops on both sides of a narrow pale band. Schmidt–Lantermann clefts are oblique pale lines crossing the black internode: they are normal channels of residual Schwann-cell cytoplasm between the myelin lamellae, not a fixation artefact, and they are diagnostically useful. The nerve may be teased apart, in which case single fibres are seen isolated against a pale background; in a section the fibres stay bundled. 4. Reading the colours Only lipid is black. That single rule explains the whole slide: myelin black, axon pale, connective tissue pale, fat black. It also explains why the nuclei are almost invisible — osmium alone does not stain chromatin, so the slide is read by shape, not by nuclear detail, which is the opposite of what you do on the HE slides. How to identify it at the exam Black segmented bands with pale nodes of Ranvier: myelin stained with osmium. Pale axonal core inside each black cylinder, and pale connective tissue between cylinders. No blue nuclei at all — a monochrome, lipid-based preparation, not HE. If only black droplets are seen without cylinders and nodes, the field is adipose tissue, not nerve. Differential diagnosis Peripheral nerve, HE (V.26): same shape of tissue, but myelin is pale, not black, and blue nuclei are everywhere. Elastic fibres stained with orcein or resorcin-fuchsin: also dark, but they form a wavy network in a vessel wall or a cartilage, never segmented cylinders with nodes. Adipose tissue (osmium or frozen section): black droplets of very different size with no internal pale core and no nodes. Melanin or haemosiderin pigment: brown, granular and intracellular, not a smooth black sheath. Clinical and functional note Myelin is the electrical insulator of the axon; the gaps at the nodes allow saltatory conduction, which is why loss of myelin slows conduction even when the axon survives. Myelin stains are the classical way to show demyelination and remyelination — the internodes become shorter and thinner after repair. Osmium tetroxide is a dangerous vapour and is now used mainly for small blocks and for fat demonstration; paraffin sections of osmicated tissue must be cut with care."},{"id":"t-V-29","p":"V","n":29,"t":"Peripheral nerve (cross section, Os)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a nerve in transverse section treated with osmium: each myelinated fibre is a black myelin ring with a pale axonal centre , so a fascicle looks punctate or \"bicycle-spoke\" patterned; unmyelinated fibres show no black ring, and the perineurium stays pale, which is what outlines each fascicle. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse plane, lipid (osmium) method. Here the myelin is seen as a ring rather than a segment, so the section asks a different question from V.28: not \"where are the nodes\" but \"how many fibres are myelinated and how thick is their sheath\". 2. Low-power architecture A round cord whose fascicles stand out as fields of black rings on a pale background. The perineurium appears as a pale band around each fascicle — it contains no myelin — and the epineurium is a broad pale zone with black adipose droplets and vessel walls. Because the black rings are distributed evenly inside each fascicle, low power alone identifies the organ. Table 1 — Myelinated and unmyelinated fibres in an osmium section Fibre type Appearance at high power Where it comes from / what it does Myelinated somatic motor Thick black ring with pale axonal dot Axons of alpha motor neurons to skeletal muscle Myelinated somatic sensory Black ring, variable thickness depending on fibre calibre Peripheral processes of pseudounipolar spinal ganglion cells Unmyelinated (Remak bundle) Several pale dots together inside one Schwann cell; no black ring Postganglionic autonomic efferents and some visceral afferents Perineurium Pale, cell-rich band delimiting the fascicle Blood-nerve barrier; no myelin, therefore no black ring 3. High-power key features Myelin rings: concentric black annulus; the larger the fibre, the thicker the black ring relative to the pale axonal centre, and the larger the axon itself. Axon: a pale dot in the middle of each ring; on tangentially cut fibres it becomes eccentric or even tangential, so rings must be judged in the plane of section. Unmyelinated fibres: not individually ringed; they appear as small groups of pale dots, the whole group being enclosed by one Schwann cell (Remak bundle). Endoneurium and perineurium are pale: collagen and fibrocytes take up no osmium, which is why the fascicle boundary is a negative image. 4. Reading the colours Black is myelin and lipid, nothing else. Because no counterstain is applied, nuclei and collagen are pale, so the two questions the examiner asks — \"myelinated or unmyelinated?\" and \"where is the perineurium?\" — are answered by the presence and absence of black rings, not by colour of the cells. How to identify it at the exam Black rings with pale centres, all of the same construction, filling a fascicle: osmium-stained nerve in cross section. Pale band separating fascicles = perineurium; pale space between rings = endoneurium. No nuclei visible and no eosinophilia — a monochrome lipid preparation. Glycogen, collagen and cartilage stay pale, so a black structure here is always lipid-rich. Differential diagnosis Peripheral nerve, HE (V.27): the same dot-in-ring picture, but the rings are pale (dissolved myelin) and blue nuclei are visible. Adipose tissue with osmium: black droplets of irregular size without a pale core and without a perineurium. Artery with an elastic stain: concentric dark lamellae, but they surround one large lumen, not thousands of small axons. White matter of the spinal cord or optic nerve: histologically central nervous tissue with oligodendrocyte myelin, no perineurium, no epineurium. Clinical and functional note Counting myelinated fibres and measuring their myelin thickness is the classical way to separate axonal from demyelinating neuropathy. In demyelinating disease the axon survives but the black ring is thin or absent over segments; in axonal loss whole fibres disappear and the fascicle looks empty. The pale perineurial band is not a passive wrapper: it is the diffusion barrier that keeps the endoneurial environment stable, and it hypertrophies in chronic compression."},{"id":"t-V-30","p":"V","n":30,"t":"Astrocyte (GFAP IHC)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is an immunohistochemical preparation of central nervous tissue reacted for GFAP (glial fibrillary acidic protein), the intermediate filament characteristic of astrocytes: the astrocytes and all their processes are covered by the brown diaminobenzidine reaction product, while the background and nuclei carry only a pale counterstain. It demonstrates one cell type, not an organ's architecture. 1. Level of organisation and plane Cellular level, demonstrated by a marker rather than by a histological stain. GFAP is an intermediate filament of neuroglial cells, especially astrocytes; because it is present in the cell body and throughout the processes, an anti-GFAP antibody with a peroxidase detection system makes the whole astrocyte visible. The tissue is brain — fibrous astrocytes of the white matter are the easiest to demonstrate, and vessels and the subpial surface are included in favourable fields. 2. Low-power architecture There is no capsule, no epithelium and no layered wall: the field is a more or less uniform network of brown lines on a pale background. The network is denser in white matter (long straight processes of fibrous astrocytes) and finer in grey matter (protoplasmic astrocytes); around blood vessels the brown processes converge to form a ring, and at the surface of the tissue they form a continuous brown limiting layer. This perivascular and subpial accentuation is the first orientation point at low power. Table 1 — Astrocytes and the ways they are demonstrated Astrocyte type Location Shape of the processes Marker or stain Fibrous astrocyte White matter Few, long, straight and unbranched; end-feet on capillaries GFAP-positive (brown on this slide) Protoplasmic astrocyte Grey matter Shorter, thicker and more branched processes GFAP-positive Marginal astrocyte Subpial surface Flattened processes forming a continuous limiting layer GFAP-positive Perivascular astrocyte Around CNS capillaries End-feet applied to the vascular basal lamina GFAP-positive; forms the glia limitans 3. High-power key features Cell shape: a small brown body with several long radiating processes — a star or spider outline. The processes branch irregularly and end in flattened expansions. Reaction product: the brown precipitate fills the cytoplasm and the processes, so the nucleus appears as a negative pale area inside the brown cell body. Read the shape, not the nucleus. Perivascular end-feet: brown processes converge on capillary walls; this is the astrocytic contribution to the blood-brain barrier . Negative cells: neurons and oligodendrocytes show only the counterstain — they have no GFAP, which is what makes the method specific. 4. Reading the colours Brown = the antigen: GFAP, and therefore astrocytes. Blue = nuclei of all cells, counterstained with haematoxylin. Everything that is brown is astrocytic, and everything that is blue-only is something else. This single sentence is the whole logic of the slide. How to identify it at the exam Brown, star-shaped cells with radiating processes on a pale background: an immunohistochemical preparation, not a routine stain. The marker is GFAP , an intermediate filament of neuroglia — the astrocyte's own cytoskeleton. Perivascular and subpial brown end-feet prove the processes belong to astrocytes, not to neurons. The reaction product is cytoplasmic and follows the processes; a purely nuclear signal would mean a different marker. Differential diagnosis Nissl or cresyl violet preparation: neurons are purple because of their Nissl substance, but no cell processes are stained and astrocytes show only their nuclei — the opposite of this slide. Golgi silver impregnation: one whole neuron is black (perikaryon, dendrites, axon), not a population of small star cells. Oligodendrocytes: small, round, dense nuclei with no GFAP and no radiating processes; they form myelin in the CNS. Microglia: of monocyte origin, ramified but GFAP-negative; they are demonstrated with macrophage markers such as CD68. Ependyma: a cuboidal-to-columnar lining of the ventricles and central canal, not scattered star cells. Clinical and functional note Astrocytes induce the tight junctions of CNS capillaries and cover them with end-feet, so they are part of the blood-brain barrier; they also buffer extracellular potassium and take up neurotransmitters. After injury astrocytes proliferate and become GFAP-rich ( reactive astrogliosis ), forming the glial scar that walls off a lesion but also blocks axonal regeneration. GFAP is used in diagnostic pathology to identify astrocytic tumours, and GFAP mutations cause Alexander disease — which is why the same stain serves the histology class and the tumour board."},{"id":"t-V-31","p":"V","n":31,"t":"Aorta (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the wall of the aorta — the prototype elastic (conducting) artery — cut transversely and stained with HE. Its dominating feature is an extremely thick tunica media built of concentric fenestrated elastic membranes with smooth muscle cells between them; on HE these membranes read as wavy, glassy eosinophilic bands, so the media looks striped. The lumen stays round and open, the intima is thin and the adventitia is thin but carries vasa vasorum. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level: the full thickness of a large vessel wall in cross section. The aorta is the type specimen of an elastic artery ( arteria elastotypica ), the group that also contains its largest branches — subclavian, common carotid, renal and common iliac arteries. The plane of section is transverse, so every coat is seen as a concentric ring around the lumen. 2. Low-power architecture Three coats are visible at once, and their proportions are the diagnosis. The lumen is large, round and not collapsed , because the elastic wall retains its shape after the pressure falls. The intima is only a thin pale line; the media occupies most of the wall and is conspicuously striped; the adventitia is thin, paler and blends into the periaortic fat, with small vessels (vasa vasorum) inside it. In proportion to the very wide lumen the wall looks thin, even though in absolute terms it is a massive structure. Table 1 — The three tunics of the aorta in HE Tunica Components Appearance in HE Function Intima (tunica intima) Endothelium, basal lamina, a wide subendothelial layer containing scattered smooth muscle cells, collagen and elastic fibres; the first fenestrated elastic membrane is its inner limit Thin pale band; flat endothelial nuclei Non-thrombogenic surface, permeability barrier Media (tunica media) 40–70 fenestrated elastic membranes alternating with circular smooth muscle cells; the internal elastic membrane is only the first of the series and the external elastic membrane the last Thickest coat; regular wavy glassy eosinophilic bands with elongated nuclei between them Elastic recoil: stores energy in systole and maintains flow in diastole Adventitia (tunica adventitia) Loose fibroelastic connective tissue, vasa vasorum, nervi vasorum, adipose tissue Thinner than the media, paler, merging with periaortic fat Nutrition of the outer wall, anchoring 3. High-power key features Endothelium: a single layer of flat cells with elongated nuclei; beneath it a wide subendothelial layer, which is where atherosclerosis begins. Elastic membranes: numerous, evenly spaced, wavy, glassy and strongly eosinophilic; their regular periodicity is what makes the media look striped. Smooth muscle cells are squeezed between them, their nuclei elongated circumferentially. Vasa vasorum: small arteries, capillaries and veins in the adventitia and outer media — the inner part of the aortic wall is nourished by diffusion from the lumen. No valves, no clear intimo-medial boundary: unlike a muscular artery, the aorta does not single out one internal elastic membrane; the whole media is elastic. 4. Reading the colours On HE both elastic membranes and collagen are eosinophilic, so colour alone cannot separate them; the discriminator is the regular wavy periodicity of the media. This is exactly why the companion slide is stained with an elastic method: HE shows the architecture, resorcin-fuchsin proves the elastin. How to identify it at the exam A very thick media full of regular wavy eosinophilic bands alternating with smooth muscle: elastic artery. Round, wide, uncollapsed lumen with a thin intima and a thin adventitia containing vasa vasorum. No single internal elastic membrane standing out, and no valves — those belong to other vessels. Many elastic lamellae = aorta or its large branches; a single elastic membrane = muscular artery. Differential diagnosis Muscular (distributing) artery: media of many layers of pure smooth muscle with one thick continuous internal elastic membrane and a prominent external elastic membrane; adventitia relatively thicker; lumen much smaller. Large vein: thin media, thickest adventitia with longitudinal collagen and smooth muscle bundles, irregular collapsed lumen, valves possible. Pulmonary trunk: also elastic, but its media is thinner because pulmonary arterial pressure is far lower than aortic pressure. Umbilical artery: a muscular artery whose media contains no elastic membranes at all, although it is a large vessel. Clinical and functional note The elastic recoil of the media converts the intermittent ejection of the left ventricle into continuous flow during diastole — the \"elastic spring\" or Windkessel effect. Atherosclerosis begins in the intima as lipid accumulation after endothelial injury; dissection is a longitudinal intimal tear that lets blood split the media; an aneurysm is a bulge caused by loss of the load-bearing elastic and muscular elements. Because the inner wall is avascular and fed by diffusion, the vasa vasorum of the outer wall become clinically important in inflammatory aortitis and in the ischaemic outer media."},{"id":"t-V-32","p":"V","n":32,"t":"Aorta (resorcin-fuchsin)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The same aortic wall stained with resorcin-fuchsin , a selective elastic stain that colours elastin brown-purple to blue-black. The outcome is a media of dozens of continuous wavy dark lamellae running around the lumen, with unstained smooth muscle cells between them: this is the definitive histological proof that the vessel is an elastic artery . 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse section, demonstrated with a histochemical method for a single matrix component. Resorcin-fuchsin is one of the classical elastic stains; it is applied to show exactly the element that HE cannot resolve, and it is therefore the natural companion to the HE section of the same vessel. 2. Low-power architecture The whole media turns into a regular series of parallel wavy dark lines, evenly spaced around the lumen; the intima carries a thin dark line at its base, and the adventitia is almost free of dark lines except for the walls of its small vessels. Because the lines are continuous and evenly repeated, low power alone is enough to call the vessel an elastic artery. Table 1 — Elastic stains and what each one gives in this slide set Stain Colour of elastin Effect on nuclei Where it is used Resorcin-fuchsin Brown-purple to blue-black Faint, usually counterstained Aorta (this slide): counts the fenestrated membranes Orcein Red-brown Pale unless counterstained Lung with haematoxylin (V.45); artery and vein (V.34) Verhoeff's iron haematoxylin Black-violet to black Also stains nuclei black — a trap Alternative elastic stain, needs differentiation HE Not demonstrated Blue General architecture only (V.31) 3. High-power key features Each dark wavy line is one fenestrated elastic membrane ( membrana elastica fenestrata ); small discontinuities along the line are its fenestrae. Between the membranes lie the smooth muscle cells , unstained or only faintly counterstained, their elongated nuclei lying parallel to the lumen. The internal elastic membrane is the first dark lamella of the series and the external elastic membrane the last; there is no sharp change of structure across either, which is typical of elastic arteries. The adventitia shows scattered dark elastic fibres and the elastic laminae of its small vessels; the intima may show a thin dark line. 4. Reading the colours Dark = elastin. Pale = smooth muscle, collagen and ground substance. The stain is therefore a \"negative\" picture of the muscle: the muscle fibres are seen only as the unstained gaps between the lamellae, and their number gives an idea of how much contractile tissue is packed between the elastic sheets. How to identify it at the exam Dozens of parallel, evenly spaced, wavy dark lamellae filling a thick media: elastic stain of an elastic artery, i.e. aorta or its large branches. The number of membranes, not their presence, is the discriminator: many = elastic artery, one or two = muscular artery. The dark bands are continuous around the lumen and give the wall its corrugated (\"accordion\") profile. If the dark material is a fine network in the adventitia only, you are looking at a vein or a small vessel. Differential diagnosis Muscular artery with the same stain: only the internal elastic membrane (and sometimes an external one) is strongly coloured, so the media shows 1–2 lines instead of dozens. Orcein instead of resorcin-fuchsin: the same target, but red-brown rather than blue-black; never read the colour as a different structure. Verhoeff's haematoxylin: elastin is black, but nuclei are black too, which mimics numerous fine lamellae if the section is over-stained. HE section of the same aorta: architecture is identical, but the elastic membranes are merely eosinophilic and cannot be counted reliably. Clinical and functional note Elastin is the element that stores the energy of systole and returns it in diastole; when it fragments with age, systolic pressure rises and diastolic pressure falls. In Marfan syndrome and in cystic medial degeneration the elastic membranes are fragmented and the media is weakened, which predisposes to aortic dilatation and dissection. Elastic stains are used in diagnostic histopathology to demonstrate the integrity of the internal elastic membrane, for example in inflammatory disease of arteries."},{"id":"t-V-33","p":"V","n":33,"t":"Artery, vein (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide shows an artery and its companion vein side by side in transverse section, HE. The artery has a thick wall, a small round uncollapsed lumen and a muscular media , so its wall-to-lumen ratio is high; the vein has a thin wall, a large irregular collapsed lumen, a thin media and an adventitia that is its thickest coat . Both are paired because they share one connective tissue sheath in a neurovascular bundle. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, two vessels in one transverse section. The pair is deliberately chosen: comparing two organs with the same three coats side by side is the fastest way to learn what each coat does, because every difference in the field is a function of pressure. 2. Low-power architecture The two lumens may be of comparable size, but the walls are not. The artery is round, its wall is thick and its lumen relatively small; a wavy eosinophilic internal elastic membrane is often visible inside the media. The vein is flattened or stellate, its lumen often contains blood or a clot, its wall is thin and merges gradually with the surrounding connective tissue, so no sharp outer border exists. Table 1 — Artery versus vein in the same HE section Feature Artery Vein Lumen Round, regular, kept open Irregular, collapsed, folded Wall-to-lumen ratio High Low Thickest coat Media Adventitia Internal elastic membrane Present, single, wavy, prominent Absent or reduced to a fine network Media Many circular layers of smooth muscle Few layers, or flat fascicles with reticular and elastic fibres Adventitia Thin, well delimited Thickest coat, longitudinal collagen; longitudinal smooth muscle bundles in large veins Valves None Paired intimal leaflets in medium veins, especially of the limbs 3. High-power key features Artery: endothelium with flat nuclei, a thin subendothelial layer, the corrugated internal elastic membrane, then many concentric layers of smooth muscle with elongated nuclei; the adventitia is fibrous and clearly narrower than the media. Vein: a very thin intima, a media of only a few muscle layers, and a broad adventitia of collagen bundles running longitudinally; the boundary between media and adventitia is blurred. Orientation of nuclei is a clue: circular muscle cut across shows elongated nuclei, while longitudinal muscle bundles show round nuclei — the vein's adventitial muscle is recognised this way. Valves , when present, are paired folds of intima projecting into the venous lumen. 4. Reading the colours Both walls are pink, so colour is useless here: the diagnosis is made by shape and proportion — round versus collapsed, thick wall versus thin wall, media-thick versus adventitia-thick. That is exactly the order in which the examiner expects the comparison to be spoken. How to identify it at the exam Three-point rule: lumen shape (round vs collapsed), wall-to-lumen ratio (high vs low), which coat is thickest (media vs adventitia). The wavy internal elastic membrane is the artery's signature; the vein has nothing comparable. The vein's wall fades into the surrounding connective tissue, the artery's adventitia is a distinct boundary. Both vessels have three coats — the slide is a comparison, so name both sets, not just the artery. Differential diagnosis Elastic artery (aorta): no single internal elastic membrane; instead the media is striped by dozens of wavy elastic bands (see V.31 and V.32). Large vein: adventitia is enormously thickened and contains longitudinal smooth muscle bundles; medium veins have valves. Lymphatic vessel: even thinner wall, no erythrocytes in the lumen, numerous valves, irregular calibre. Peripheral nerve: no lumen at all, only fascicles and sheaths. Clinical and functional note The artery's thick media resists arterial pressure and regulates peripheral resistance; the vein's thin wall and large lumen make it a low-pressure capacitance vessel holding most of the blood volume. When venous valves become incompetent and the wall dilates, varicose veins result; inflammation of a superficial vein is thrombophlebitis. Arterial occlusion (thrombus, embolus, atherosclerosis) causes ischaemia of the territory with pain and pallor, whereas venous occlusion causes swelling and oedema — the two clinical pictures mirror the two wall structures."},{"id":"t-V-34","p":"V","n":34,"t":"Artery, vein (Orcein)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The same artery and vein stained with orcein , which colours elastic fibres and elastic membranes red-brown . The artery is identified at once by a sharply defined, wavy red-brown internal elastic membrane at the intimo-medial junction (with an external elastic membrane in larger muscular arteries), whereas the vein contains only scanty, fine elastic fibres in its intima and adventitia. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse section, selective elastic stain. Orcein is the elastic stain that preserves the best morphological detail of the vessel wall, so it is the ideal slide on which to point at the internal elastic membrane and name it. 2. Low-power architecture On the arterial side a single bright, continuous, scalloped red-brown line traces the lumen just outside the endothelium: this is the internal elastic membrane, and it immediately marks the vessel as an artery. On the venous side there is no such line — only faint, interrupted red-brown fibres in the intima and in the adventitia. The arterial media also shows a fine red-brown mesh of elastic fibres between the muscle cells. Table 1 — What orcein adds to the artery and to the vein Structure Orcein appearance Artery Vein Internal elastic membrane Thick, continuous, wavy red-brown band Present and prominent Absent or represented only by a thin network External elastic membrane Thinner red-brown band at the media/adventitia border Present in larger muscular arteries Absent Media elastic fibres Fine red-brown mesh between muscle cells Moderate Sparse Adventitial elastic fibres Loose red-brown mesh Present Present, often more conspicuous than in the media Diagnostic conclusion Pattern of the fibres, not their colour Muscular artery Vein 3. High-power key features Internal elastic membrane: thick, continuous, corrugated (scalloped) because the artery was fixed in contraction; small gaps are its fenestrations. Media: smooth muscle cells are unstained or faintly counterstained; the red-brown elastic fibres form a delicate mesh around them, not parallel lamellae — that is the difference from an elastic artery. Vein: elastic fibres are fine, sparse and irregularly scattered; the intimal elastic network never forms a single solid band. Adventitia: elastic fibres are present in both vessels and are more numerous than in the media of the vein, which is why a vein at low power can look \"fuzzy\" at its outer border. 4. Reading the colours Orcein makes elastin red-brown; nuclei and collagen stay pale unless a counterstain is used. Resorcin-fuchsin would make the same structures blue-black, and Verhoeff's haematoxylin black — so the colour identifies the stain , while the pattern (one membrane versus many lamellae versus a sparse mesh) identifies the vessel . How to identify it at the exam One crisp wavy red-brown line at the intimo-medial junction = internal elastic membrane = muscular artery . Almost no elastic material in the wall = vein . Dozens of parallel red-brown lamellae = elastic artery (aorta, large branches). The stain is selective for elastin, so everything red-brown on the slide is elastic tissue — arteries and veins cannot be confused by colour, only by pattern. Differential diagnosis Resorcin-fuchsin on the same vessels: identical pattern, blue-black instead of red-brown; the colour difference must not be read as a structural difference. HE of the same vessels: the internal elastic membrane is present but only weakly eosinophilic and easily missed. Elastic cartilage: also orcein-positive, but the elastic fibres are in a chondromucoid matrix with chondrocytes in lacunae, not in a vessel wall. Nerve: the wavy fascicles of a nerve may catch the eye, but there is no lumen and no elastic membrane. Clinical and functional note The internal elastic membrane is the landmark used to measure intimal thickening in arteriosclerosis: the distance from endothelium to membrane is the intima, and it grows with disease. Loss and fragmentation of elastic fibres reduce the resilience of the vessel and contribute to dilatation and to the formation of varices in veins. Because orcein also stains the elastic fibres of skin and cartilage, the method is used beyond the vascular system, but in this course it is the vascular pattern that matters."},{"id":"t-V-35","p":"V","n":35,"t":"Spermatic cord (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a transverse section of the spermatic cord ( funiculus spermaticus ), HE: a neurovascular bundle running from the deep inguinal ring to the testis. Two structures make the diagnosis — the ductus deferens , a small-lumen tube with an exceptionally thick three-layered smooth muscle wall and a pseudostratified stereociliated epithelium, and the pampiniform plexus , a cluster of thick-walled veins — both wrapped in loose connective tissue and in the coverings of the abdominal wall, including the striated cremaster muscle . 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse section through the cord. The cord is not a viscus with a lumen: it is a bundle of ducts, vessels, nerves and lymphatics held together by connective tissue and covered by the layers it dragged with it during the descent of the testis — external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle with its cremasteric fascia, and internal spermatic fascia. The plane of section is transverse, so every content is cut across and counted. 2. Low-power architecture Outside, if the scrotal skin has been included, there is skin and dartos; otherwise the outermost layer is the thin external spermatic fascia , followed by bundles of cremaster muscle (striated, in cross section) and then the internal spermatic fascia . The interior is loose connective tissue with fat, and within it the contents are seen: one ductus deferens with a tiny, star-shaped lumen and a very thick pink wall; a crowd of veins of different calibre with thick muscular walls; one or two small arteries; nerve bundles; thin-walled lymphatic vessels. Table 1 — The contents of the spermatic cord Content Number Key microscopic features Function Ductus deferens One Narrow stellate lumen; pseudostratified columnar epithelium with stereocilia and basal cells; three muscle layers: internal longitudinal, middle circular, external longitudinal Active transport of sperm Pampiniform plexus Many Muscle-type veins with thick walls, irregular lumens, varying calibre, often congested Countercurrent heat exchange; venous drainage of the testis Testicular artery One Muscular artery with a prominent internal elastic membrane Arterial supply of the testis Artery of the ductus deferens One Small muscular artery Supply of the ductus and epididymis Deferential nerve plexus Bundles Pale nerve fascicles with perineurium Autonomic supply of the ductus Lymphatic vessels Several Very thin endothelial wall, no muscle coat Lymphatic drainage towards the para-aortic nodes Vestigium processus vaginalis Inconstant Small connective tissue/peritoneal remnant Its persistence predisposes to hydrocele 3. High-power key features Ductus deferens: the epithelium is pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia; the lamina propria is rich in elastic fibres; the muscularis is the thickest part of the wall and its three layers are arranged longitudinally–circularly–longitudinally. Pampiniform plexus: many veins with unusually thick muscular walls (they are \"muscle-type\" veins) and irregular, often collapsed lumens containing blood. Cremaster: bundles of striated muscle fibres cut across, with peripheral nuclei — a useful marker that this is the cord and not a vessel bundle elsewhere. Coverings: thin fascial layers of collagen; no epithelium lining the cord itself and no cartilage anywhere. 4. Reading the colours Muscle and collagen are pink, nuclei blue; the elastic fibres of the ductus are not demonstrated by HE, and the epithelium is basophilic. The key is proportion, not colour: a very small lumen in a very thick pink wall is the ductus deferens, and a cluster of thick-walled vessels with a thinnish overall wall is the pampiniform plexus. How to identify it at the exam Ductus deferens: tiny stellate lumen + extremely thick three-layered muscle coat + pseudostratified columnar stereociliated epithelium — nothing else in the body looks like this. Pampiniform plexus: many thick-walled veins of different sizes packed together. Cremaster: striated muscle bundles around the whole bundle prove you are in the spermatic cord. No glands, no cartilage, no respiratory epithelium: the differential list is short. Differential diagnosis Umbilical cord (V.46): amniotic epithelium, abundant Wharton's jelly, two arteries and one vein, no muscular duct with a narrow lumen and no striated cremaster. Epididymis: one highly coiled duct with regular, tall pseudostratified epithelium and long stereocilia, surrounded by a thin smooth muscle coat; there is no thick three-layer muscularis and no pampiniform cluster. A single thick-walled duct without a cord: the ductus deferens alone, without the vessels, nerves and coverings, is not the spermatic cord — always name the pampiniform plexus and the cremaster alongside it. Oesophagus (the other \"thick muscular tube\"): wide lumen, non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, muscularis mucosae and submucosa, two muscle layers with striated muscle in its upper third, and never a pampiniform plexus. Clinical and functional note The pampiniform plexus cools arterial blood on its way to the testis by countercurrent exchange; dilatation of the plexus ( varicocele ) raises testicular temperature and is a recognised cause of male subfertility. Because the cord passes through the inguinal canal, it is the pathway of an indirect inguinal (funicular) hernia , and the persistence of the processus vaginalis explains hydrocele and, in part, cryptorchidism. Torsion of the cord compromises first the thin-walled veins and then the artery, so the testis becomes congested and ischaemic — a surgical emergency."},{"id":"t-V-36","p":"V","n":36,"t":"Blood smear (MGG)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a peripheral blood smear stained with May–Grünwald–Giemsa (MGG) , a Romanovsky-type stain: the acidic dye eosin colours basic structures (haemoglobin, granules) pink-red, while the basic methylene-blue/azure dyes colour acidic structures (nuclear chromatin, RNA) purple-blue. Erythrocytes dominate the field; leucocytes and platelets are the minority to be identified one by one. 1. Level of organisation and plane Cellular level: this is not a section but a smear — a drop of blood spread into a monolayer, air-dried, fixed in methanol and then stained. Morphology is best read in the thin tail (\"feather edge\") of the smear, where the cells lie flat and do not overlap; in the thick body of the smear erythrocytes are piled up and distorted. 2. Low-power architecture The field is a pink sea of erythrocytes with a few purple nucleated cells and tiny lilac platelets scattered between them. Because 60–70 % of leucocytes are neutrophils, the first nucleated cell found is likely to be a neutrophil. The background should be clean; precipitated stain or dirt is an artefact, not a cell. Table 1 — Formed elements of blood as seen in an MGG smear Cell % of WBC Diameter in smear Nucleus Cytoplasm and granules Erythrocyte — 7.2 μm None (anucleate) Pink-red, biconcave with a central pallor; no organelles Platelet — — None Pale blue with a denser granulomere and a paler hyalomere Neutrophil 60–70 9–12 μm 3–4 lobes joined by thin strands Pale pink; fine neutral granules, plus purple azurophil granules Eosinophil 2–4 10–14 μm Bilobed (\"sausage\" or spectacles) Brick-red, coarse, uniform eosinophilic granules Basophil <1 8–10 μm S-shaped, usually hidden by granules Dark blue-black coarse basophilic granules Lymphocyte 20–25 8–10 μm Round, dense, occupying most of the cell Thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm Monocyte 3–8 12–15 μm Kidney- or horseshoe-shaped Abundant grey-blue cytoplasm, often vacuolated 3. High-power key features Erythrocytes: uniform, anucleate, pink-red discs; the paler centre is thinner than the rim. Their shape depends on a spectrin membrane skeleton, which is also why they can deform in capillaries. Platelets: small, anucleate, pale blue fragments with a granular centre; they are not cells and contain no nucleus. Granulocytes: classified by the colour of their specific granules — neutral pink (neutrophil), brick-red (eosinophil), dark blue-black (basophil); the nucleus is lobed and the lobes decrease in number from neutrophil to basophil. Agranulocytes: no specific granules; lymphocyte has a round dense nucleus with a thin cytoplasmic rim, monocyte has an indented nucleus and generous grey-blue cytoplasm. 4. Reading the colours MGG gives a fixed colour code: erythrocytes pink-red, nuclear chromatin purple, myeloid cytoplasm pale pink, lymphocyte cytoplasm pale blue, eosinophilic granules brick-red, basophilic granules dark blue, azurophil (lysosomal) granules purple-red. Learning these seven colours is enough to classify every cell on the slide. How to identify it at the exam Erythrocytes have no nucleus ; every nucleated cell in the field is a leucocyte. Classify each leucocyte in two steps: nucleus shape first, then granule colour . Neutrophil = lobed nucleus + neutral pink granules; eosinophil = bilobed + brick-red granules; basophil = obscured nucleus + dark blue-black granules. Lymphocyte = round dense nucleus with a thin rim; monocyte = largest cell, indented nucleus, abundant grey-blue cytoplasm. Platelets are anucleate fragments, not cells, and must not be counted as white cells. Differential diagnosis Monocyte versus large lymphocyte: the monocyte has a folded or kidney-shaped nucleus, more cytoplasm and vacuoles; the large lymphocyte still has a round nucleus occupying most of the cell. Eosinophil versus neutrophil with toxic granulation: eosinophil granules are larger, uniform and brick-red, and the nucleus has two lobes. Basophil versus stain precipitate: precipitate lies on top of cells and is irregular and structureless, whereas basophil granules belong to one cell and surround its nucleus. Crenated erythrocytes (spiculated artefact of drying) must not be reported as pathological poikilocytosis on a poor smear. Clinical and functional note The differential count is a daily clinical test: neutrophilia points to bacterial infection, eosinophilia to allergy or parasites, lymphocytosis to viral infection, and the appearance of blast cells suggests leukaemia. Erythrocyte morphology reports anaemia: hypochromia and microcytosis in iron deficiency, macrocytes in B12/folate deficiency, sickle cells in haemoglobinopathy. Intra-erythrocytic parasites (malaria, babesia) are diagnosed on exactly this stain, and MGG is also used for bone marrow aspirates, where the same colour rules apply."},{"id":"t-V-37","p":"V","n":37,"t":"Red bone marrow (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is red (haematopoietically active) bone marrow in HE: a highly cellular tissue filling the intertrabecular spaces of spongy bone , supported by reticular connective tissue and penetrated by wide sinusoids, in which the various blood-cell lineages mature side by side with megakaryocytes . Its hallmark is the mixture — several lineages at several stages of maturation in a single field. 1. Level of organisation and plane Tissue and organ level at once: the slide usually contains fragments of spongy bone with the marrow in the spaces between the trabeculae, obtained by aspiration or biopsy, or taken from a long bone. Red marrow is the active form; yellow marrow is the same stroma loaded with adipocytes and haematopoietically silent, and it can be reactivated. In the adult, red marrow persists in the axial skeleton and in the proximal humerus and femur. 2. Low-power architecture Pink bony trabeculae border dark purple cellular fields. The marrow is dark because nuclei are densely packed; the empty round spaces are adipocytes, more numerous with age; the pale channels are sinusoids . Megakaryocytes stand out even at this magnification as the largest single cells of the field, and this mixture of many cell types is itself the diagnostic low-power feature. Table 1 — The components of red bone marrow Component Appearance Function Bone trabeculae Solid eosinophilic matrix with osteocytes in lacunae; no marrow inside the bone itself Mechanical support and a surface for the marrow Erythroid islands (erythrons) Clusters of small cells with dense round nuclei; the cytoplasm becomes steadily pinker as haemoglobin accumulates Erythropoiesis; often centred on a nurse macrophage that supplies iron Myeloid (granulocytic) series Larger cells with paler, indented nuclei and granulated cytoplasm Granulopoiesis Megakaryocytes Very large cells with a multilobated nucleus, lying next to sinusoids Platelet production by shedding cytoplasm Sinusoids Wide thin-walled channels containing erythrocytes Entry of mature cells into the circulation Adventitial reticular cells Stellate cells covering the sinusoids; produce reticular fibres Stroma and haematopoietic microenvironment Macrophages Large free or fixed cells with engulfed material Remove extruded nuclei, recycle iron Adipocytes Empty round spaces, more numerous with age Fat store; their increase converts red to yellow marrow 3. High-power key features Maturation is read in the nucleus: erythroid precursors start with a large pale nucleus and basophilic cytoplasm and end as small cells with a dense pyknotic nucleus and eosinophilic (haemoglobin-rich) cytoplasm before losing the nucleus altogether. Myeloid precursors are larger, their nuclei are indented or horseshoe-shaped and their cytoplasm acquires specific granules as they mature. Megakaryocytes are the largest cells of the marrow — a single cell may exceed several ordinary marrow cells in diameter — with a single hugely lobulated nucleus and granular cytoplasm. Sinusoids are lined by a thin endothelium and are surrounded by reticular fibres; mature cells cross their wall to reach the blood. Plasma cells and lymphocytes are normally present in small numbers and complete the mixed picture. 4. Reading the colours Dense dark nuclei = erythroid cells and lymphocytes; larger, paler, indented nuclei = myeloid cells; pink cytoplasm = haemoglobin synthesis; pink trabeculae = bone; empty circles = fat. The marrow is judged by the cellularity (proportion of cells to fat) and by the relative numbers of the lineages, which is exactly what a haematologist reports. How to identify it at the exam Bone trabeculae plus a densely cellular space filled with several lineages at once = bone marrow. Megakaryocytes are the single most useful confirmatory cell: large, multilobated, sinusoidal. Erythroid islands with dense nuclei and myeloid cells with paler indented nuclei sit together — no lymphoid organ shows this mixture. Adipocytes of all sizes and sinusoids complete the picture; absence of capsule and of epithelium excludes lymphoid organs. Differential diagnosis Yellow bone marrow: dominated by adipocytes with only sparse cells; no erythroid islands, no megakaryocytes. Spleen red pulp: also blood-rich and sinusoidal, but there are cords of Billroth and no bony trabeculae containing a mixed haematopoietic population with megakaryocytes. Bone ground section: shows osteons and lacunae but no cells inside the lacunae and no marrow at all. Lymph node or thymus: encapsulated, lymphoid only, no bone and no megakaryocytes. Clinical and functional note Marrow is sampled from the posterior iliac crest ; the aspirate is smeared (MGG) and the biopsy is decalcified and stained with HE, as on this slide. Marrow cellularity and lineage distribution diagnose aplasia (empty, fatty marrow), leukaemia (sheets of blast cells), myelofibrosis and metastatic carcinoma. When the body needs more blood cells (haemolysis, chronic hypoxia), yellow marrow can reconvert to red marrow, which is why red marrow reappears in the diaphyses of long bones in adults."},{"id":"t-V-38","p":"V","n":38,"t":"Thymus (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the thymus , HE: a lobulated lymphoid organ in which each lobule has a dark cortex packed with lymphocytes and a pale medulla containing Hassall's corpuscles . The medulla of neighbouring lobules is continuous and there are no germinal centres , because the thymus is a primary lymphoid organ where T cells are generated, not a filter for lymph. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, cut so that capsule, septa, cortex and medulla are all visible. The thymus lies in the anterior mediastinum behind the sternum; it is largest in childhood and is gradually replaced by adipose tissue with age, although it never disappears completely. Its stroma is unique: reticular epithelium , an endodermal derivative, replaces the usual reticular connective tissue. 2. Low-power architecture A capsule of dense connective tissue sends septa inwards, but the septa do not traverse the whole organ, so the \"lobules\" are false lobules. Each lobule is basophilic outside (cortex) and eosinophilic inside (medulla) ; because the septa are incomplete, the pale medulla of adjacent lobules joins across the organ. In the medulla, pink whorled Hassall's corpuscles are visible even at low power. Table 1 — The regions of a thymic lobule Region Dominant cells Special structures Function Capsule and septa Collagen, fibroblasts Incomplete septa → pseudolobules; vessels and rare efferent lymphatics; no afferent lymphatics Framework and blood supply Cortex Dense small thymocytes; pale stellate epithelial reticular cells; macrophages Mitoses in the subcapsular zone; blood-thymus barrier around cortical capillaries Proliferation and positive selection Medulla Fewer lymphocytes; conspicuous eosinophilic epithelial reticular cells Hassall's corpuscles (concentric epithelial cells, keratinized, may calcify); macrophages and dendritic cells Negative selection and removal of autoreactive cells Cortico-medullary junction Lymphocytes and endothelium High endothelial venules with cuboidal endothelium Entry of progenitors and exit of mature T cells 3. High-power key features Cortex: densely packed small lymphocytes — so dense that the epithelial framework is hard to see, except as pale stellate cells with large vesicular nuclei between them. Medulla: fewer lymphocytes and much more eosinophilic cytoplasm of the epithelial reticular cells; this is why the medulla is pale. Hassall's corpuscles: concentric lamellae of flattened keratinized epithelial cells with an eosinophilic, onion-like centre that may calcify and become basophilic; their number and size increase with age. No lymphoid nodules with germinal centres and no subcapsular sinus — the thymus is not exposed to lymph and does not filter it. Blood-thymus barrier in the cortex: continuous capillary endothelium with tight junctions, its basal lamina, a thin perivascular connective tissue layer with macrophages, the basal lamina of the reticular epithelium, and the epithelial cell processes. 4. Reading the colours Blue means lymphocytes: the cortex is blue because it is lymphocyte-rich. Pink means epithelium: the medulla is pink because epithelial reticular cells and Hassall's corpuscles predominate. Reading a thymus is therefore reading a blue-outside/pink-inside pattern, and that polarity is the exact opposite of a lymph node, where the cortex is nodular and the medulla is pale for a different reason. How to identify it at the exam Dark cortex, pale medulla, and a medulla that is continuous between lobules. Hassall's corpuscles — concentric pink whorls in the medulla — are the single most specific structure. No germinal centres and no subcapsular sinus: this is a primary lymphoid organ. The stroma is epithelial (cytokeratin-positive), not reticular connective tissue, so no reticular fibre network is seen. Differential diagnosis Lymph node: cortex with nodules and germinal centres , a subcapsular sinus under the capsule, and a medulla of cords and sinuses; no Hassall's corpuscles. Spleen: no cortex and medulla at all; white pulp arranged around central arterioles, red pulp with cords and sinusoids. Palatine tonsil: covered by stratified squamous epithelium with deep crypts on the luminal side. Parathyroid gland: nests and cords of chief cells with no lymphocytes and no lobulation of this kind. Clinical and functional note The thymus is largest in childhood and undergoes involution after puberty, being replaced by fat — which is why an adult mediastinal scan shows mostly adipose tissue. Thymoma is associated with myasthenia gravis , and removal of a hyperplastic or neoplastic thymus can improve the disease. Failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch derivatives (DiGeorge syndrome) causes thymic aplasia with T-cell deficiency, while the blood-thymus barrier explains why circulating antigens do not normally reach developing T cells."},{"id":"t-V-39","p":"V","n":39,"t":"Spleen (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the spleen , HE: an encapsulated organ with a dense capsule and trabeculae , whose parenchyma is white pulp — periarteriolar lymphoid sheath and lymphoid nodules around a central arteriole — embedded in red pulp made of splenic cords of Billroth and wide sinusoids. There is no cortex and no medulla, because the spleen filters blood, not lymph. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, with capsule, trabeculae and both pulps visible. The spleen lies in the peritoneal cavity and is the largest accumulation of lymphoid tissue in the body. Its stroma is reticular connective tissue, its framework comes from the capsule, and its vascular arrangement — open circulation in humans — explains the structure of the red pulp. 2. Low-power architecture A thick capsule of dense collagenous connective tissue with a mesothelial surface sends trabeculae into the organ; trabecular arteries and veins run inside them. Between the trabeculae the parenchyma is a mosaic of basophilic white pulp islands and eosinophilic, blood-filled red pulp . This two-pulp pattern is recognisable at low power and is the first thing to say aloud. Table 1 — White pulp and red pulp of the spleen Region Composition Key cells Capsule and trabeculae Dense collagen (type I) with a mesothelial surface; trabecular arteries and veins inside Fibroblasts, smooth muscle White pulp — PALS Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath around the sheathed artery T lymphocytes, dendritic cells White pulp — nodule and marginal zone Lymphoid nodule with germinal centre and mantle; marginal zone at its edge B lymphocytes, macrophages, antigen-presenting cells Central arteriole Runs eccentrically through the white pulp, not centrally Endothelium, smooth muscle Red pulp — cords of Billroth Anastomosing cords of reticular tissue in the extravascular space Macrophages (erythrophages, siderophages), plasma cells, erythrocytes, granulocytes Red pulp — sinusoids Wide, blood-filled channels lined by elongated fusiform (\"stave\") endothelial cells with gaps; ringed by reticular fibres Endothelial cells 3. High-power key features White pulp: the central arteriole is eccentric in the nodule, so one side of the vessel has more lymphoid tissue; around it the PALS is T-cell territory and the nodule with its germinal centre is B-cell territory. Red pulp: cords of Billroth are packed with erythrocytes and macrophages; the sinusoids between them are wider than ordinary capillaries and are lined by stave-like endothelial cells whose gaps let red cells squeeze through. Erythrophagocytosis: macrophages containing red-cell debris (erythrophages) and haemosiderin (siderophages) are found in the cords — the morphological trace of the spleen's filter function. Trabecular vessels: arteries and veins within the trabeculae are surrounded by dense collagen, which distinguishes them from vessels in the pulp. No subcapsular sinus and no afferent lymphatics: the spleen has only efferent lymphatics at the hilum. 4. Reading the colours Blue = lymphoid tissue (white pulp). Red-pink = blood, sinusoids and cords (red pulp). Deep pink solid bundles = capsule and trabeculae. The contrast between the two pulps is the whole slide: once you can point to white pulp and red pulp, the organ is identified. How to identify it at the exam White pulp and red pulp side by side, with no cortex/medulla arrangement. A central arteriole running eccentrically through a lymphoid nodule — pathognomonic for the spleen. Dense capsule with trabeculae containing trabecular arteries and veins. Red pulp sinusoids lined by stave-like cells and packed with erythrocytes, plus macrophages with ingested red cells. Differential diagnosis Lymph node: cortex with nodules and a subcapsular sinus, paracortex, medullary cords and sinuses; no white/red pulp and no central arteriole in a nodule. Thymus: lobules with dark cortex and pale medulla, Hassall's corpuscles, no germinal centres, no erythrocyte-filled pulp. Palatine tonsil: epithelium with crypts on the surface — epithelium is the giveaway. Haemorrhagic or infarcted tissue: blood everywhere but no organised white pulp around an arteriole. Clinical and functional note The spleen filters blood: it removes aged erythrocytes (about 120 days old), recycles iron, and mounts immune responses against blood-borne antigens. Splenomegaly accompanies portal hypertension, haemolytic and haematological disease; the enlarged organ is fragile and may rupture after relatively minor trauma. After splenectomy the loss of filtering and of antibody response to encapsulated bacteria increases susceptibility to overwhelming sepsis, which is why vaccination is required."},{"id":"t-V-40","p":"V","n":40,"t":"Lymph node (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a lymph node , HE: a bean-shaped, encapsulated organ with afferent lymphatics entering the convex surface, a subcapsular sinus, a cortex containing lymphoid nodules with germinal centres, a paracortex, and a medulla of cords and sinuses , with efferent vessels leaving at the hilum . It is the filter interposed in the course of lymphatic vessels. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, cut through the hilum when possible. The node is embedded along a lymphatic vessel, so lymph enters through several afferent vessels on the convex side, percolates through the sinuses and leaves through efferent vessels at the hilum, where the blood vessels also enter and leave. 2. Low-power architecture The organ is bean-shaped. Under the capsule a pale subcapsular (marginal) sinus runs as a clear band. The outer cortex is studded with basophilic nodules, some with pale germinal centres; the deeper paracortex is diffuse and paler; the medulla at the hilum is pale and irregular, made of cords and sinuses. Trabeculae from the capsule divide the cortex, and the hilum is the indented side where vessels converge. Table 1 — The compartments of a lymph node and the route of lymph Compartment Contents Main cell type Capsule and trabeculae Dense collagen (type I) with reticular stroma (type III) beneath Fibroblasts Subcapsular (marginal) sinus Pale channel just beneath the capsule, receiving afferent lymph Macrophages, reticular cells Cortex — nodules Secondary nodules with a pale germinal centre and a dark mantle zone B lymphocytes Paracortex Diffuse lymphoid tissue between the nodules and the medulla; high endothelial venules T lymphocytes Medullary cords Branched cords of lymphoid tissue continuous with the paracortex Plasma cells, macrophages, lymphocytes Medullary sinuses Irregular pale channels with reticular cells and fibres crossing them Macrophages, dendritic cells Hilum Exit of efferent lymphatics; entry and exit of artery and vein Endothelium 3. High-power key features Germinal centre: pale, with centroblasts, centrocytes and scattered tingible-body macrophages phagocytosing apoptotic debris (the \"starry sky\"); the dark ring around it is the mantle zone of small resting B cells. Sinuses: crossed by reticular cells and fibres, which slow the lymph and give macrophages time to phagocytose antigen — the node's filter mesh. High endothelial venules in the paracortex: cuboidal endothelium that allows lymphocytes to leave the blood and enter the node. Medullary cords are rich in plasma cells, whose eccentric nucleus with a \"clock-face\" chromatin and basophilic cytoplasm marks antibody production. 4. Reading the colours Cortex is blue where lymphocytes are dense; germinal centres are paler because the cells are larger with more cytoplasm; sinuses are pale because they contain lymph and only a loose mesh; the medulla is pale overall. The colour code therefore maps directly onto the path of lymph from the subcapsular sinus to the hilum. How to identify it at the exam Bean shape with a capsule, a subcapsular sinus and a hilum . Cortex outside, medulla inside — the same peripheral-dark, central-pale plan as a thymic lobule (so not a discriminating feature by itself); here the cortex carries lymphoid follicles and the medulla is discontinuous. Lymphoid nodules with germinal centres in the cortex and medullary cords and sinuses centrally. Both afferent and efferent lymphatics (the thymus has only efferent vessels, the spleen none afferent). Differential diagnosis Thymus: lobules with dark cortex and pale medulla, Hassall's corpuscles, no germinal centres and no subcapsular sinus. Spleen: white pulp around a central arteriole inside a red pulp; no cortex/medulla, no subcapsular sinus. Palatine tonsil: surface epithelium with deep crypts; lymphoid tissue directly under the epithelium, capsule only at the base. Appendix or Peyer's patch: lymphoid nodules in the gut wall under an intestinal epithelium, without a capsule. Clinical and functional note The lymph node is an immunological filter: about 99 % of arriving antigen is phagocytosed by macrophages in the sinuses, and the remainder is presented to lymphocytes in the nodules. Sentinel lymph node is the first node draining a tumour and the first to receive metastases; its biopsy is part of cancer staging. Lymphadenopathy in infection is due to germinal-centre formation, and removal of regional nodes (axillary clearance) causes lymphoedema because the drainage route is interrupted."},{"id":"t-V-41","p":"V","n":41,"t":"Palatine tonsil (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the palatine tonsil , HE: a mass of lymphoid tissue covered by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium that invaginates into deep crypts , with numerous lymphoid nodules and a fibrous capsule only on its deep aspect, resting on the pharyngeal muscle. It is a partially encapsulated lymphoid organ guarding the entrance of the digestive tract. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, cut so that both the mucosal surface and the deep capsular side are present. The tonsil is part of Waldeyer's tonsillar ring and lies in the tonsillar fossa between the palatal arches; it is a peripheral lymphoid organ but partially encapsulated and has only efferent lymphatics. 2. Low-power architecture One side of the slide is epithelium, folded into deep crypts whose lumens contain debris; beneath it lies a broad field of lymphoid tissue with nodules, many of them with pale germinal centres. At the base a fibrous capsule separates the tonsil from the deeper connective tissue, where skeletal muscle of the pharynx and seromucous glands are seen. There is no subcapsular sinus and no cortex/medulla distinction. Table 1 — Layers of the palatine tonsil Layer Structure Note Surface epithelium Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium Heavily infiltrated by lymphocytes; antigen-presenting cells lie between the epithelial cells Crypts Deep invaginations of the epithelium Lumens contain cell detritus, desquamated cells and bacterial colonies Lymphoid tissue Secondary nodules with germinal centres and mantle zones; T cells between the nodules No afferent lymphatics — the tonsil is not a lymph filter Capsule Fibrous connective tissue, deep aspect only Separates the tonsil from the peritonsillar tissue and limits the spread of infection Deep structures Skeletal muscle of the pharynx; seromucous glands Muscle and glands identify the palatine rather than the pharyngeal tonsil 3. High-power key features Epithelium: stratified squamous and non-keratinized; numerous lymphocytes migrate through it into the crypt, which is why it looks \"moth-eaten\" in places. Crypt content: amorphous detritus, desquamated epithelial cells, bacteria and neutrophils — the reason tonsillar crypts are a site of repeated infection. Nodules: germinal centres with tingible-body macrophages, and a mantle of small lymphocytes; between nodules the tissue is a T-cell zone. Capsule: fibrous, with trabeculae; only on the deep side, and it is the plane along which the tonsil is removed surgically. 4. Reading the colours The epithelium is pink with blue nuclei and is easy to follow into the crypts; the lymphoid tissue is blue because of its dense nuclei; germinal centres are pale; detritus in the crypt is a mixture of blue and pink debris. The rule to remember: epithelium plus crypts means tonsil , because no other lymphoid organ on the list has an epithelial surface. How to identify it at the exam Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium with deep crypts over lymphoid tissue: palatine tonsil. Capsule only on the deep side , so the luminal surface is free — the definition of a partially encapsulated organ. Lymphoid nodules with germinal centres but no subcapsular sinus and no afferent lymphatics. Skeletal muscle deep to the capsule confirms the pharyngeal location. Differential diagnosis Pharyngeal and tubal tonsils: the surface is respiratory — ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium — not stratified squamous; the pharyngeal tonsil also lacks a thick capsule. Lingual tonsil: a single crypt and no capsule; lymphoid nodules over the root of the tongue. Lymph node: no epithelium and no crypts; it has a subcapsular sinus and both afferent and efferent lymphatics. Thymus: lobules with cortex and medulla and Hassall's corpuscles, no crypts and no epithelium on the surface. Oesophagus: also lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, but it has muscularis mucosae, submucosa and a two-layered muscularis externa, not lymphoid nodules in crypts. Clinical and functional note Acute inflammation of the palatine tonsil (correctly amygdalitis , though \"tonsillitis\" is used clinically) fills the crypts with pus, seen as white spots; a peritonsillar abscess is a feared complication. Enlarged pharyngeal tonsil (\"adenoid vegetation\") obstructs the nasal airway in children and causes mouth breathing, snoring and recurrent middle-ear disease. The tonsil produces mainly IgA , which is stabilised in secretions by the secretory component — an effective mucosal defence at the entrance of the digestive tract."},{"id":"t-V-42","p":"V","n":42,"t":"Larynx (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a section through the larynx , HE: a cavitated organ whose mucosa forms two kinds of folds — the vestibular (false) fold with loose connective tissue and glands, and the vocal (true) fold with stratified squamous epithelium, Reinke's space, the elastic vocal ligament and the vocalis muscle — all supported by a skeleton of hyaline and elastic cartilage and moved by striated laryngeal muscles. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, cut frontally or coronally through the larynx, or sagittally through the epiglottis. The lumen has an hourglass shape: vestibule above, glottis in the middle, infraglottic cavity below. The larynx is the first part of the lower respiratory tract and the organ of voice production. 2. Low-power architecture An air-filled lumen is bounded by mucosa thrown into folds; pieces of cartilage occupy a large part of the section, and bundles of striated skeletal muscle and seromucous glands lie between them. The vocal fold is the wedge-shaped projection with a very loose core, and the vestibular fold lies above it. The mixture of cartilage, striated muscle and glandular mucosa in one slide is what makes the larynx unique in this series. Table 1 — The two mucosal folds of the larynx compared Feature Vestibular (false) fold Vocal (true) fold Epithelium Respiratory: ciliated pseudostratified columnar with goblet cells Non-keratinized stratified squamous on the medial surface Core Loose connective tissue; vestibular ligament; oedematous stroma Reinke's space (very loose connective tissue), then the elastic vocal ligament Muscle None inside the fold Vocalis muscle lateral to the vocal ligament Glands Absent in the fold itself; moistened by Knoll's glands of the laryngeal ventricle Absent in the fold itself; moistened by the saccular glands Function Protective, closes the airway during swallowing; resonance Sound production by vibration of the epithelial edge 3. High-power key features Two epithelia in one organ: respiratory epithelium over most of the larynx, replaced by stratified squamous where mechanical stress is greatest — the medial surface of the vocal folds and the upper part of the laryngeal surface of the epiglottis. Cartilage of two types: hyaline for the thyroid, cricoid and most of the arytenoid cartilages; elastic for the epiglottic cartilage, and elastic for the corniculate and cuneiform sesamoid cartilages. The cricoid is the only cartilage that completely encircles the laryngeal lumen. Glands: seromucous glands of the lamina propria — the epiglottic glands, Knoll's glands in the laryngeal ventricle, and arytenoid glands in the aryepiglottic folds. Striated muscle: the six paired laryngeal muscles; the vocalis regulates tension of the vocal ligament and is the muscle seen in the vocal fold. Loose stroma of Reinke's space is the reason the vocal fold can vibrate and also the reason it accumulates oedema. 4. Reading the colours Hyaline cartilage is basophilic with chondrocytes in groups; elastic cartilage has a similar matrix but elastic fibres in it (visible only with an elastic stain); skeletal muscle is strongly eosinophilic with peripheral nuclei; glands are pale and lobulated. Then ask where the epithelium changes: that transition marks the vocal fold. How to identify it at the exam Cartilage plus striated muscle plus a cavitated mucosa in one section: larynx. Two epithelia — respiratory and stratified squamous — meeting at the vocal fold. Two cartilages — hyaline and elastic — in the same organ. The vocal fold is identified by its stratified squamous epithelium, loose Reinke's space, elastic vocal ligament and vocalis muscle, in that order from the surface. Differential diagnosis Trachea: a single type of epithelium (respiratory) throughout, C-shaped hyaline cartilage, no stratified squamous lining and no mucosal folds of this kind. Oesophagus: stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium everywhere, no cartilage, muscularis mucosae, submucosa and two layers of muscularis externa with skeletal muscle in the upper third. Bronchus: cartilage present only as irregular plates around the whole circumference, respiratory epithelium throughout, no striated muscle. Epiglottis alone: elastic cartilage with two epithelial surfaces (lingual and laryngeal) and taste buds on the lingual side. Clinical and functional note Because Reinke's space is extremely loose, the vocal folds swell readily — vocal nodules and polyps (singer's nodes) arise there, and the resulting mass prevents complete closure of the glottis, producing hoarseness. Most laryngeal carcinomas arise from the stratified squamous epithelium of the vocal fold, which is one reason the epithelial transition zone matters clinically. The vocal folds are moved by the laryngeal muscles supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve; its damage (thyroid surgery, tumour) causes hoarseness, and bilateral damage can obstruct the airway."},{"id":"t-V-43","p":"V","n":43,"t":"Trachea (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the trachea in transverse section, HE: a hollow airway lined by respiratory epithelium resting on a conspicuously thick basement membrane, with seromucous glands in the submucosa and a series of C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings that are open posteriorly and closed there by the trachealis muscle . The C-shape is visible only because the section is transverse. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse plane. The trachea is a 10–12 cm tube with a lumen about 2 cm wide, continuous with the larynx above and dividing into the two main bronchi at the level of the fourth thoracic vertebra. Its wall contains 15–20 pieces of horseshoe-shaped hyaline cartilage, and the plane of section is what allows the horseshoe to be seen as a C. 2. Low-power architecture A lumen lined by folded mucosa; a pale, thick layer of glands; and then the cartilage, seen as a basophilic C-shaped plate with its opening directed posteriorly. Across the opening lies the membranous wall , in which smooth muscle and elastic fibres replace the cartilage. Outside, the adventitia blends with the surrounding connective tissue. Table 1 — The layers of the tracheal wall Layer Structure Key feature Epithelium Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium with goblet cells — respiratory epithelium Goblet cells become more numerous caudally Basement membrane Very thick (2–4 μm) and clearly visible under the epithelium A reliable discriminator from the bronchiole Lamina propria Loose connective tissue with lymphocytes, lymphoid nodules (BALT) and a longitudinal elastic fibre layer Elastic fibres allow expansion and recoil Submucosa — tracheal glands Seromucous tubuloalveolar glands with serous demilunes ; more numerous in the membranous wall; ducts lined by cuboidal to columnar epithelium Some glands lie dorsal to the trachealis muscle Fibromusculocartilaginous layer 15–20 dorsally open C-shaped pieces of hyaline cartilage joined by annular ligaments; trachealis muscle (smooth) closing the posterior gap The C-shape is the signature of the trachea Adventitia Loose connective tissue with blood and lymphatic vessels and nerves Blends with the mediastinal tissue 3. High-power key features Epithelium: ciliated columnar cells, goblet cells and basal cells; the cilia are the proof that this is the respiratory tract and not the oesophagus. Glands: mixed seromucous glands with characteristic serous demilunes capping mucous tubules; they keep the surface moist and trap particles. Cartilage: hyaline, with a perichondrium and chondrocytes in isogenous groups; the annular ligaments between successive rings are dense fibrous tissue. Membranous wall: bundles of smooth muscle (trachealis) with elastic and collagen fibres; contraction narrows the lumen, as in coughing. 4. Trachea versus oesophagus These two tubes lie side by side in the neck and mediastinum and are the commonest mix-up on a transverse section. They are told apart by epithelium first, then by the presence or absence of cartilage and of a muscularis mucosae . Table 2 — Trachea versus oesophagus on a transverse section Feature Trachea Oesophagus Lumen Wide, kept open by cartilage Narrow, collapsed, with longitudinal folds Epithelium Respiratory: ciliated pseudostratified columnar with goblet cells Stratified squamous non-keratinized Basement membrane Very thick Not conspicuous Muscularis mucosae Absent Present — a distinct longitudinal smooth muscle layer Submucosal glands Tracheal seromucous glands, numerous Oesophageal glands in the submucosa Cartilage C-shaped hyaline rings None Muscle coat Trachealis only, posterior Inner circular and outer longitudinal muscularis externa; skeletal muscle in the upper third Adventitia Loose, blends with mediastinum Loose, blends with mediastinum How to identify it at the exam C-shaped hyaline cartilage with a posterior gap filled by smooth muscle: trachea, no alternative. Respiratory epithelium on a thick basement membrane , with goblet cells and cilia. Seromucous glands with serous demilunes in the submucosa. The lumen is held open; there is no muscularis mucosae and no stratified squamous epithelium. Differential diagnosis Oesophagus: stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, a muscularis mucosae, two layers of muscularis externa (skeletal in the upper third) and no cartilage — the differences are tabulated above. Bronchus: cartilage is present as irregular plates around the entire circumference rather than one C-shaped ring, smooth muscle forms a complete layer inside the cartilage, and the lumen is smaller. Bronchiole: no cartilage, no glands and no goblet cells; a thick layer of smooth muscle and a folded mucosa identify it. Larynx: contains stratified squamous epithelium over the vocal folds and striated muscle besides cartilage. Clinical and functional note Prolonged intubation damages the mucosa and causes post-intubation tracheal stenosis with stridor; the narrowest part of the adult airway is the region of the cricoid and the first tracheal rings. Because the right main bronchus is wider and less angled, inhaled foreign bodies and aspirated material enter it more often than the left. The mucociliary apparatus (cilia plus mucus from goblet cells and glands) is the tracheal defence; smoking and repeated inflammation reduce the cilia and increase goblet cells, which is the histological basis of chronic bronchitis."},{"id":"t-V-44","p":"V","n":44,"t":"Lung (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is lung , HE: a spongy organ of alveoli — thin-walled air spaces separated by capillary-rich interalveolar septa — together with bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts, and a pleural surface covered by mesothelium. In the fields where alveoli dominate there is no cartilage and no gland , which is what separates lung from trachea and bronchus. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, a section through the parenchyma that should include at least one airway and, in favourable slides, the pleura. The conducting portion ends at the bronchiole; the respiratory portion begins at the respiratory bronchiole, where gas exchange starts, and continues through alveolar ducts and saccules to the alveoli. 2. Low-power architecture The field is a \"sponge\": empty spaces of very different size. The largest tubes with folded, basophilic walls are bronchioles; the smallest round spaces are alveoli; between them run thin pink septa with capillaries. Where a vessel is cut, erythrocytes mark it. At the edge of the section the pleura appears as a thin line of mesothelium over a narrow layer of connective tissue. Table 1 — Conducting and respiratory parts of the lung Structure Epithelium Wall Gas exchange Intrapulmonary bronchus Respiratory epithelium Irregular cartilage plates, glands and a complete smooth muscle layer No Bronchiole Respiratory epithelium becoming lower, with exocrine bronchiolar (Clara) cells No cartilage, no glands ; prominent spiral smooth muscle No Respiratory bronchiole Cuboidal to low columnar Alveoli bud from the wall Begins here Alveolar duct and sac No continuous wall of its own — a linear sequence of alveoli; simple squamous lining of the alveolar mouths Reticular and elastic fibres; small smooth muscle knobs at the alveolar mouths Yes Alveoli Type I and type II pneumocytes Interalveolar septum with continuous capillaries Yes 3. High-power key features Type I pneumocyte: extremely flattened cell; its nucleus is flat and its cytoplasm thins to a film over the capillary, covering most of the alveolar surface. Type II pneumocyte: cuboidal cell bulging into the alveolar lumen, with vacuolated (lamellar) cytoplasm; it secretes surfactant and is the progenitor of type I cells. Alveolar macrophage (dust cell): large, free or attached cell with phagocytosed particles; it is the lung's scavenger. Interalveolar septum: a continuous capillary sandwiched between type I cells, with reticular and elastic fibres, fibroblasts and Kohn's pores that connect neighbouring alveoli. Pleura: a single mesothelial layer with a thin submesothelial connective tissue, which contains elastic fibres — seen clearly on the orcein slide that follows. 4. The blood-gas barrier Gas exchange happens where the septum is thinnest. The barrier, from air to blood, consists of surfactant, the cytoplasm of the type I pneumocyte , the fused basal laminae of pneumocyte and endothelium, and the endothelial cell of the continuous capillary. Its extreme thinness is the structural reason why oxygen and carbon dioxide equilibrate in a fraction of a second. How to identify it at the exam Empty alveolar spaces of different sizes separated by thin capillary-rich septa : lung parenchyma. An airway with no cartilage and no glands = bronchiole, i.e. inside the lung. Type II pneumocytes (cuboidal, bulging) and alveolar macrophages identify the alveolar wall at high power. Pleura at the edge confirms the organ; anthracotic pigment is a normal finding in adults. Differential diagnosis Trachea or bronchus: cartilage is present — as C-shaped rings in the trachea, as irregular plates in a bronchus — so their presence excludes the purely alveolar part of the lung. Oesophagus: a muscular tube with stratified squamous epithelium; no air spaces at all. Adipose tissue: also \"empty spaces\", but they are round, of uniform size, each with a nucleus at the margin, and there are no septa with capillaries. Emphysema: enlarged air spaces with destroyed septa — a pathological version of the same architecture, not a different organ. Other stains of the same lung: HE shows architecture only; orcein and resorcin-fuchsin show the elastic skeleton (see V.45). Clinical and functional note Surfactant from type II pneumocytes lowers surface tension and keeps alveoli open; its deficiency in premature infants causes infant respiratory distress syndrome, and its destruction in ARDS damages the blood-gas barrier. Emphysema destroys the elastic fibres of the septa, so the alveoli merge and lose their recoil, producing air trapping and a barrel chest. Because the pulmonary circulation receives the whole cardiac output at low pressure, the alveolar capillaries are the site of gas exchange and also the first filter for emboli from the systemic veins."},{"id":"t-V-45","p":"V","n":45,"t":"Lung (orcein + H)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The same lung stained with orcein , which colours elastic fibres red-brown , and haematoxylin , which colours nuclei blue. The point of the slide is the elastic skeleton of the lung: elastic fibres in the interalveolar septa, at the mouths of the alveoli, along the bronchioles, around the vessels and, most obviously, in the pleura , whose continuous elastic layer is a reliable landmark. 1. Level of organisation and plane Tissue level with a selective histochemical stain and a nuclear counterstain. It is the same organ as V.44, but the question it answers is different: not \"what structures are here\" but \"where is the elastic tissue and how is it arranged\". 2. Low-power architecture The alveolar architecture is now drawn in red-brown lines : a delicate mesh outlines every alveolar wall, so the \"sponge\" is visible even where the HE section looked almost empty. The pleura is a continuous red-brown band along the surface. Bronchioles and blood vessels have distinctly red-brown walls, and the cartilage of larger airways shows a red-brown fibrous perichondrium. Table 1 — Where the elastic tissue of the lung is found Site Orcein appearance Functional meaning Interalveolar septa Fine red-brown mesh outlining each alveolus Recoil and patency of the alveoli Alveolar mouths and ducts Condensed red-brown fibres around the openings Keeps the openings patent during expiration Bronchiole wall Dense longitudinal red-brown bundles in the lamina propria Recoil of the airway during expiration Pulmonary vessels Elastic laminae in the vessel wall Pressure buffering in the pulmonary circulation Pleura Continuous red-brown layer under the mesothelium Elastic recoil of the lung surface; the best landmark on the slide 3. High-power key features Elastic fibres are red-brown, of variable thickness, and never intracellular; they branch and anastomose, forming a three-dimensional net around the air spaces. Nuclei are blue from the haematoxylin counterstain, so the cellular detail of V.44 is still available — this is what makes orcein + H more useful than a pure elastic stain. Pleura: mesothelium on the surface, then a continuous elastic lamina, then connective tissue — a layered structure that identifies the organ's surface. Bronchiole: a ring of dense elastic fibres in the lamina propria, just inside the smooth muscle. 4. The three stains you may be given on lung tissue The same lung looks different, and the differences are entirely predictable from what each stain targets. Table 2 — HE, orcein + H and resorcin-fuchsin applied to lung Stain Target What it shows in the lung How to recognise the stain HE Nuclei (blue) and cytoplasm/collagen (pink) General architecture: alveoli, septa, bronchioles, vessels, pleura — but the elastic fibres are not distinguished Pink background, blue nuclei, no dark fibres Orcein + H Elastin (red-brown) and nuclei (blue) The elastic skeleton: a red-brown mesh in the septa, around alveolar mouths, in bronchioles and in the pleura Red-brown fibres on a pale pink background with blue nuclei Resorcin-fuchsin Elastin (brown-purple to blue-black) The same elastic skeleton, but with higher contrast and without cellular detail Blue-black fibres; the colour tells you the stain, the pattern tells you the tissue How to identify it at the exam Red-brown fibres forming a net around air spaces: an elastic stain of lung , not HE. The continuous red-brown pleural band proves the organ is the lung and gives the surface for orientation. Elastic fibres are densest at the alveolar mouths and in the bronchiole wall — exactly where mechanical stress is greatest. Blue nuclei are still visible, so the stain can be orcein + H rather than a pure elastic preparation. Differential diagnosis HE lung (V.44): same architecture, but no red-brown fibres; the septa are simply pink. Elastic aorta (V.32): elastic fibres also demonstrated, but they form dozens of parallel lamellae in a vessel wall, not a mesh around air spaces. Elastic cartilage: orcein-positive too, but the elastic fibres lie in a chondromucoid matrix with chondrocytes in lacunae. Collagen stain or trichrome: colours collagen, not elastin — the pattern would be a blue/green fibrous background instead of a discrete fibre net. Clinical and functional note Elastic fibres are the physical basis of the lung's elastic recoil: they store energy during inspiration and empty the lung passively during expiration. In emphysema the elastic fibres of the septa are destroyed (proteases, smoking, alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency), so the alveoli merge and the lung loses its recoil. Ageing and chronic inflammation fragment pulmonary elastin, which is why the elastic stain is used in research and in the histological assessment of emphysema."},{"id":"t-V-46","p":"V","n":46,"t":"Umbilical cord (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the umbilical cord ( funiculus umbilicalis ) in transverse section, HE: two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein embedded in Wharton's jelly , a mucous connective tissue, and covered by a simple squamous-to-cuboidal amniotic epithelium . There are no nerves and no other structures in a normal cord. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, transverse section. The cord runs from the umbilicus of the fetus through the amniotic cavity to the fetal surface of the placenta; it develops as the body folds push the proximal yolk sac and allantois to its base. A mature cord is about 2 cm wide and about 50 cm long, roughly the length of the fetus. 2. Low-power architecture A thin surface layer of amniotic epithelium encloses a large field of pale, myxoid Wharton's jelly . Inside it, three vessels stand out: two arteries with thick, deeply eosinophilic walls and small irregular lumens, and one vein with a much thinner wall and a large, often collapsed, oval lumen. No cartilage, no muscle bundles and no glands are present. Small remnants of the omphaloenteric or allantoic duct may be seen in some cords. Table 1 — The contents of the umbilical cord Structure Number Wall and lumen Blood and fate Umbilical artery 2 Thick muscular media; the wall contains no elastic membranes ; small irregular lumen Deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta; branches of the internal iliac artery Umbilical vein 1 Thin wall, large lumen, often collapsed Oxygenated blood to the fetus; part drains to the portal vein, part passes through the ductus venosus to the inferior vena cava Wharton's jelly — Mucous connective tissue: sparse fibroblasts and macrophages, fine collagen fibres, abundant ground substance rich in hyaluronic acid Protects the vessels from compression and torsion Amniotic epithelium — Simple squamous to cuboidal cells on the surface Fetal membrane covering Omphaloenteric duct Variable Remnant of the yolk sac stalk Normally obliterates Allantoic duct Variable Remnant of the allantois Normally obliterates 3. High-power key features Umbilical artery: a very thick media of smooth muscle, but the wall is remarkable for a negative feature — it contains no elastic membranes , which is unusual for an artery of this size and is a useful discriminator in favour of the umbilical cord. Umbilical vein: thin media, wide lumen, frequently folded on itself; no valves. Wharton's jelly: pale, myxoid, almost empty-looking matrix with scattered spindle-shaped fibroblasts and macrophages; there are no adipocytes and no dense collagen bundles. Amniotic epithelium: one layer of flat to cuboidal cells; no cilia, no goblet cells, no keratin. 4. Reading the colours Wharton's jelly is pale blue-grey because its ground substance is basophilic and its cells are sparse; the smooth muscle of the vessels is strongly eosinophilic; the amniotic epithelium is a thin pink line with blue nuclei. The contrast between a mucoid, cell-poor stroma and two thick muscular arteries is the picture to memorise. How to identify it at the exam Two arteries and one vein in a myxoid stroma, covered by a single epithelial layer: umbilical cord. Wharton's jelly — mucous connective tissue with almost no cells — replaces ordinary connective tissue. No nerves, no skeletal muscle, no duct with a thick muscle coat: the cord is only vessels plus jelly. The umbilical arteries lack elastic membranes, which distinguishes them from systemic muscular arteries. Differential diagnosis Spermatic cord (V.35): contains the ductus deferens (tiny lumen, very thick three-layered muscle coat, pseudostratified epithelium), a pampiniform venous plexus, striated cremaster muscle and fascial coverings — none of which is present here. Placenta (V.47): chorionic villi with trophoblast and intervillous maternal blood; there is no villus here. Artery and vein slide (V.33): two systemic vessels with internal elastic membranes and a connective tissue stroma, not three vessels in Wharton's jelly. Mucoid connective tissue elsewhere (e.g. vitreous): has no amniotic epithelium and no paired arteries. Clinical and functional note A single umbilical artery (two-vessel cord) is the commonest congenital anomaly of the cord and is associated with other fetal anomalies, so it prompts further investigation. Wharton's jelly cushions the vessels against compression, which is why cord entanglement and even true knots often cause no harm; when compression does occur, the thinner-walled vein is compressed before the arteries. The cord is the route for cord blood collection (a source of stem cells) and for fetal blood sampling; cord prolapse is an obstetric emergency because it compresses the vessels."},{"id":"t-V-47","p":"V","n":47,"t":"Placenta (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is the placenta , HE: a fetal part — the chorionic plate with its chorionic villi — and a maternal part — the decidual plate — with intervillous spaces between the villi filled with maternal blood. Each villus has a core of fetal connective tissue with capillaries and Hofbauer cells and is covered by syncytiotrophoblast , beneath which a cytotrophoblast layer persists during the first half of pregnancy. 1. Level of organisation and plane Organ level, a section through both parts. The human placenta is haemochorionic : maternal blood in the intervillous space is in direct contact with the syncytiotrophoblast, so only the fetal tissues of the villus separate the two circulations. The section should include the chorionic plate, villi and the decidual plate, which together show the whole architecture. 2. Low-power architecture On one side lies the chorionic plate — connective tissue with large fetal vessels, covered by amnion towards the amniotic cavity. From it, branching villi project into the intervillous space , which is filled with maternal blood. On the other side is the decidual plate , from which placental septa grow towards the fetal side but do not reach it, so that they divide the placenta into lobes ( cotyledons ) without completely separating them. Table 1 — Fetal and maternal parts of the placenta Part Components Note Fetal — chorionic plate Connective tissue with fetal vessels; amnion on its cavity side Gives origin to the villi Fetal — villi Outer syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast beneath, myxoid core with capillaries, fibroblasts and Hofbauer cells Free villi float in maternal blood; anchoring villi are fixed to the decidual plate Maternal — decidual plate Decidual cells, uterine glands, fibrinoid Derived from the decidua basalis Intervillous space Maternal blood; no endothelial lining Open maternal circulation — the basis of the haemochorionic placenta Placental septa Extensions of the decidual plate covered by trophoblast Incomplete, so cotyledons communicate 3. High-power key features Syncytiotrophoblast: a continuous multinucleated eosinophilic layer with a brush border on the villus surface; it is the interface with maternal blood and the source of hCG and other placental hormones. Cytotrophoblast (Langhans cells): a single cuboidal layer beneath the syncytium, continuous only in the first half of pregnancy and important for regeneration. Villus core: loose, jelly-like connective tissue with fetal capillaries, fibroblasts, myofibroblasts and Hofbauer cells — specialised macrophages that regulate local immune reactions and vascular growth. Fibrinoid: amorphous, strongly eosinophilic material on and around villi (Langhans', Rohr's and Nitabuch's fibrinoid), which supports the villi and seals damaged surfaces. Maturation: early villi are short and wide with few vessels; at term they are long, thin and richly branched, with capillaries lying directly under the syncytiotrophoblast. 4. The placental barrier From fetal blood to maternal blood the barrier consists, in order, of the endothelium of the fetal capillary , its basal lamina , the connective tissue core of the villus , the basal lamina of the trophoblast , the cytotrophoblast and the syncytiotrophoblast . In the second half of pregnancy the cytotrophoblast becomes discontinuous and the basal laminae fuse, so the barrier becomes thinner and exchange more efficient — at the price of easier transplacental passage of drugs, viruses and antibodies. How to identify it at the exam Villi with a double trophoblast cover, a cellular core and fetal capillaries, floating in maternal blood : placenta. Hofbauer cells in the villus core and fibrinoid on the villus surface are the two specific details. Two parts must be named: the fetal chorionic plate with villi and the maternal decidual plate with septa. The intervillous space has no endothelial lining — that is what \"haemochorionic\" means. Differential diagnosis Umbilical cord (V.46): three vessels in Wharton's jelly with an amniotic epithelial surface; no villi and no intervillous space. Uterine endometrium: glands, spiral arteries and stroma; no trophoblast and no villi. Amnion and chorion laeve: membranes without villi; the amnion is a simple epithelium on connective tissue. Hydatidiform mole: oedematous, avascular villi with trophoblast proliferation — a pathological exaggeration of the normal villous architecture. Clinical and functional note The placenta performs the functions of lung, gut, kidney and endocrine gland for the fetus; its syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG, progesterone, oestrogens and chorionic somatomammotropin. The barrier is selective, not absolute: IgG crosses by receptor-mediated endocytosis (the basis of passive immunity and of Rh sensitisation), and many drugs, viruses and bacteria cross it too. Defective remodelling of the maternal spiral arteries and placental insufficiency underlie pre-eclampsia and intrauterine growth restriction; an abnormally adherent or deeply invasive placenta (praevia, accreta) causes severe obstetric haemorrhage."},{"id":"t-V-48","p":"V","n":48,"t":"Chicken embryo (HE)","u":"part-5.html","x":"Core answer The slide is a transverse section through the body of a chicken embryo at the somite stage, HE: a whole-body cross-section in which the three germ layers are present at once and the axial organs lie in a fixed order — neural tube dorsally in the midline, notochord immediately ventral to it, somites flanking them, the dorsal aortae and the endodermal gut tube below, and the intraembryonic coelom between the somatic and splanchnic layers of the lateral plate mesoderm. 1. Level of organisation and plane Whole-embryo level, transverse plane. The chick embryo is the classical model because it develops as a flat disc on the surface of the yolk, so a single cross-section contains the complete body wall, the neural tube and the gut at the same time. The plane cuts across the body axis, which is why the organs are arranged symmetrically around the midline. 2. Low-power architecture: find the axis first Orientate by finding the neural tube at the midline dorsally; below it lies the notochord , and lateral to these the paired somites . Further ventrally are the paired dorsal aortae and the gut tube lined by endoderm. The coelom is the clear space between the somatic (body-wall) and splanchnic (gut-associated) layers of the lateral plate mesoderm. The surface is covered by a single layer of ectoderm . Table 1 — Structures, germ layers and adult derivatives Structure on the slide Germ layer Adult derivative Surface ectoderm Ectoderm Epidermis and its appendages; also the neural placodes Neural tube Ectoderm (neuroectoderm) Brain and spinal cord; its cavity becomes the ventricular system and central canal Neural crest (at the neural folds) Ectoderm Spinal and autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, melanocytes, part of the skull and face Notochord Axial mesoderm Nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs; the rest disappears Somite Paraxial mesoderm Sclerotome → vertebrae and ribs; myotome → skeletal muscle; dermatome → dermis Dorsal aortae Mesoderm Aorta Gut tube and endoderm Endoderm Epithelium of the digestive tract and of its glands (liver, pancreas); respiratory epithelium Intraembryonic coelom Lateral plate mesoderm Pericardial, pleural and peritoneal cavities 3. High-power key features Neural tube: a thick wall of pseudostratified neuroepithelium around a narrow neural canal . The sulcus limitans divides its wall into a dorsal alar plate (sensory) and a ventral basal plate (motor). Notochord: a solid rod of cells immediately ventral to the neural tube, enclosed in a sheath; it is the embryonic inducer of the neural plate and the axial skeleton. Somite: a compact, rounded mass of paraxial mesoderm lateral to the neural tube, with an epithelium-like surface; it is the segmental unit of the body. Dorsal aortae: small paired vessels lined by endothelium, ventral to the somites and lateral to the gut; blood cells may be visible inside the lumen. Gut tube: a tube lined by endoderm, at first a flat sheet that has folded into a tube; it lies ventral to the notochord. 4. Reading the colours Neuroepithelium and other densely cellular tissues are strongly basophilic because nuclei are packed; mesenchyme is pale and loosely cellular; endoderm forms a neat cuboidal-to-columnar lining; erythrocytes and blood islands may be eosinophilic. The key is not colour but topography : the structures are always in the same order from dorsal to ventral, so the answer is an ordered list, not a search. How to identify it at the exam Find the neural tube at the midline ; the notochord is immediately ventral to it and the somites are lateral to both. All three germ layers must be identifiable: ectoderm on the surface, mesoderm between, endoderm lining the gut tube. The presence of a notochord excludes adult central nervous tissue, and the presence of a gut tube plus somites excludes a placental or cord section. The section is whole-body : skin and body wall, gut and neural axis are present in one field. Differential diagnosis Adult spinal cord section: a neural tube-like structure, but there is no notochord, no somites, no surface ectoderm, and the cord is surrounded by meninges, bone and muscle. Umbilical cord or placenta (V.46, V.47): extraembryonic structures; no neural tube, no notochord, no somites. Another level of the same embryo: more cranial sections show the pharyngeal arches and heart, more caudal ones the tail bud; the axial organs are the constant features. Teratoma: contains all three germ layers but disorganised, without an axis, a neural tube or a notochord. Clinical and functional note Failure of neural tube closure produces anencephaly (cranial) or spina bifida (caudal); folic acid supplementation reduces the risk. Notochord remnants can give rise to chordoma , a tumour of the clivus and sacrum — the notochord itself normally persists only as the nucleus pulposus. Because each somite forms a vertebra, a muscle block and a dermal segment, errors in segmentation produce vertebral and muscular anomalies, and the segmental origin explains the arrangement of spinal nerves, muscles and dermatomes in the adult."}];